(globalThis.TURBOPACK||(globalThis.TURBOPACK=[])).push(["object"==typeof document?document.currentScript:void 0,47115,(e,t,s)=>{t.exports=JSON.parse('{"english-0001":["\\"Which she has tended for forty years\\" is extra information that could be removed without changing the sentence\'s core meaning, so it needs a comma on BOTH sides. The second choice drops the opening comma and the third drops the closing comma. The fourth uses a semicolon, which can only join two complete sentences, and \\"which she has tended for forty years\\" is not a complete sentence.",["Correct: the aside \\"which she has tended for forty years\\" is nonessential, so it needs a comma on both sides.","Only one half of the pair: the opening comma before \\"which\\" is missing.","Only one half of the pair: the closing comma after \\"years\\" is missing, so the aside runs into the verb.","A semicolon must join two complete sentences, and \\"which she has tended...\\" is not one."]],"english-0002":["\\"The letters came out faint and crooked\\" and \\"I didn\'t care\\" are both complete sentences. Two complete sentences need a period, a semicolon, or a comma plus a joining word like \\"but\\" or \\"and\\". The original uses a period, and \\"Still,\\" signals the contrast, so it is correct. Words like \\"still\\" and \\"however\\" are transition words, not joining words, so putting them between commas (the second and third choices) still leaves a comma splice. The last choice is a plain comma splice. Trap: \\"however\\" sounds formal and correct, but it can\'t glue two sentences together.",["Correct: a period separates two complete sentences, and \\"Still,\\" starts the second one.","Comma splice: \\"still\\" is not a conjunction, so a comma can\'t join the two complete sentences.","Comma splice: \\"however\\" is a transition word, not a conjunction, so it can\'t join two sentences with only commas.","Comma splice: two complete sentences joined by a comma alone, and the contrast word \\"still\\" is lost."]],"english-0003":["The word \\"each\\" in \\"each of the forty-four keys\\" already makes clear that the keys were cleaned one at a time. Every other choice adds two more words that mean the same thing, so it says the idea three times. Deleting the phrase leaves a clear, complete sentence: \\"I cleaned each of the forty-four keys with a cotton swab.\\" When DELETE is offered, check whether the sentence still works and still means the same thing without the words.",["Redundant: \\"one at a time\\" and \\"individually\\" mean the same thing, and \\"each\\" already says it.","Redundant: \\"individually\\" and \\"one by one\\" repeat each other and repeat \\"each\\".","Redundant: \\"separately\\" and \\"singly\\" are two words for the same idea that \\"each\\" already covers.","Correct: \\"each of the forty-four keys\\" already tells you they were cleaned one at a time."]],"english-0004":["The seller warns that the typewriter will probably be useless (\\"a doorstop\\"). The narrator carries it home anyway. That is a contrast: she acts despite the warning. \\"Even so\\" means \\"in spite of that,\\" so it fits. \\"Therefore\\" would mean the warning was her reason for taking it, \\"For instance\\" would introduce an example, and \\"Similarly\\" would mean the two ideas match. None of those fit the relationship between the two sentences.",["Correct: the narrator takes the typewriter home despite the seller\'s warning, so a contrast word fits.","Wrong logic: \\"Therefore\\" means the warning caused her to take it home, but the warning argued against it.","Wrong logic: \\"For instance\\" introduces an example, and carrying it home is not an example of the warning.","Wrong logic: \\"Similarly\\" signals a matching idea, but taking it home goes against the warning."]],"english-0005":["Every choice describes something wrong with the typewriter, so all four fit the list. The goal is narrower: show that the machine had gone UNUSED for a long time. Only the dust choice does that, and it says so outright with \\"as if no one had touched the machine in years.\\" A stuck carriage, a dent, and a silent bell show that the machine was broken or damaged, not that it sat untouched. The trap is picking any detail about the machine\'s bad condition instead of the one that matches every word of the goal.",["Shows the machine was broken, but says nothing about how long it had sat unused.","Correct: dust on every surface and \\"no one had touched the machine in years\\" point directly to long disuse.","Shows damage, which could happen at any time; it doesn\'t show the machine sat untouched.","Shows a broken part, but a part can fail for many reasons; it doesn\'t point to years without use."]],"english-0006":["The question asks for an ending that refers back to the FIRST paragraph. In that paragraph, the seller warns that the typewriter will \\"probably end up as a doorstop.\\" The third choice answers that prediction: the machine is now used for writing that matters, so it was \\"never meant to be a doorstop.\\" That ties the end of the essay to its beginning. The bell comes from the third paragraph and the repair videos from the second, so those choices look back to the wrong place. NO CHANGE is a reasonable thought, but it doesn\'t connect to the opening at all.",["A fine ending idea, but it is new to this paragraph; it doesn\'t return to the first paragraph.","Refers back to the third paragraph (the first typed sentence and the bell), not the first.","Correct: it answers the seller\'s warning in the first paragraph that the typewriter would end up as a doorstop.","Refers back to the second paragraph (the repair videos), not the first."]],"english-0007":["The sentence needs to show that the studio belonged to Penn, so it needs the possessive pronoun \\"whose.\\" \\"Who\'s\\" sounds the same but means \\"who is\\" (\\"a painter who is studio\\" is nonsense). \\"Whom\\" is used as an object (\\"the painter whom she admired\\"), and \\"which\\" refers to things, not people. Quick check: if you can\'t swap in \\"who is,\\" don\'t use \\"who\'s.\\"",["\\"Who\'s\\" is a contraction of \\"who is\\"; \\"a painter who is studio\\" makes no sense.","\\"Whom\\" is an object pronoun; it can\'t show ownership of the studio.","Correct: \\"whose\\" is the possessive form, showing the studio belonged to Penn.","\\"Which\\" is for things, not people, and it doesn\'t show possession."]],"english-0008":["The sentence lists three things Varga does: she \\"measures\\" the window, \\"sketches\\" the design, and then does a third thing. Items in a list should have the same form, so the third verb should also be a present-tense verb ending in -s: \\"traces.\\" \\"Then she is tracing,\\" \\"tracing,\\" and \\"to trace\\" each change the form and break the series. Tip: match the first item in the list.",["Breaks the pattern: the first two actions are single present-tense verbs, not \\"she is tracing.\\"","Correct: \\"measures, sketches, and traces\\" are three matching present-tense verbs.","Breaks the pattern: an -ing word doesn\'t match \\"measures\\" and \\"sketches.\\"","Breaks the pattern: \\"to trace\\" doesn\'t match \\"measures\\" and \\"sketches.\\""]],"english-0009":["This sentence is inverted: it starts with a location (\\"Inside a canvas roll that she carries to every job\\") and puts the subject AFTER the verb. Ask what is inside the roll: \\"the brushes.\\" That subject is plural, so the verb must be \\"are.\\" Flip it to check: \\"The brushes Penn gave her are inside a canvas roll.\\" The trap is matching the verb to the singular nouns right in front of it, \\"roll\\" or \\"job.\\" \\"Was\\" and \\"has been\\" are both singular, and \\"was\\" also breaks the present tense of the paragraph.",["Agrees with \\"roll\\" or \\"job,\\" but the subject of this inverted sentence is the plural \\"brushes.\\"","Singular, and it also shifts to the past tense while the paragraph is in the present.","Singular: \\"has been\\" doesn\'t agree with the plural subject \\"brushes.\\"","Correct: the subject comes after the verb here, and it is the plural \\"brushes,\\" so the verb is \\"are.\\""]],"english-0010":["An opening phrase like \\"Working from the top of a ladder\\" describes whatever noun comes right after the comma. The person working on the ladder is Varga, so \\"Varga\\" has to be the subject that follows. In NO CHANGE and the last choice, the lettering or \\"it\\" would be doing the work. The third choice is the sneaky trap: it mentions Varga, but only as a possessive (\\"Varga\'s signs\\"), so the subject is still \\"the lettering.\\" Only the second choice puts Varga in the subject spot.",["Dangling modifier: this says the lettering is working from the top of a ladder.","Correct: \\"Varga\\" comes right after the opening phrase, so she is the one working from the ladder.","Still dangling: \\"Varga\'s\\" is a possessive here, so the subject is still \\"the lettering.\\"","Dangling modifier: \\"it\\" is not the person working from the ladder."]],"english-0011":["The sentence before it makes a general claim: \\"customers notice the difference.\\" The bracketed sentence backs that up with a specific example: people stop to photograph the bakery\'s window almost every day. The sentence after it also depends on it, since \\"that kind of attention\\" refers to the photographing. So the sentence should be kept. The second choice keeps it for a reason that isn\'t true. The third choice is tempting because the bakery is named, but the sentence is really about the reaction to Varga\'s work. The fourth is wrong because the sentence adds a concrete detail, not a repeat.",["Correct: the photographs are a concrete example of customers noticing, and \\"that kind of attention\\" in the next sentence depends on it.","Right decision, wrong reason: the sentence never mentions paint types or why Varga chooses them.","The sentence is about how people react to Varga\'s sign, not about the bakery\'s business, so it stays on focus.","It doesn\'t just repeat the claim; it adds a specific new detail (people photograph the window almost daily)."]],"english-0012":["The sentence describes the canal at a specific moment in the past: \\"When the canal opened in 1825.\\" The paragraph is also in the past tense (\\"began,\\" \\"cleared,\\" \\"were towed\\"). So the verb should be past tense: \\"stretched.\\" \\"Stretches\\" is present, \\"will stretch\\" is future, and \\"has stretched\\" suggests an action continuing up to today, which doesn\'t fit with a clause pinned to 1825.",["Present tense doesn\'t fit an event the sentence places in 1825.","\\"Has stretched\\" describes something continuing up to now, which clashes with \\"When the canal opened in 1825.\\"","Correct: past tense matches \\"opened in 1825\\" and the past-tense verbs around it.","Future tense makes no sense for an event in 1825."]],"english-0013":["Two punctuation decisions are packed in here. First, \\"the New York politician DeWitt Clinton\\": the name tells us WHICH politician, so it is essential and takes no comma (just like \\"the singer Aretha Franklin\\"). Second, \\"who later served as the state\'s governor\\" adds extra information about a person who has already been named, so it is nonessential and needs a comma before it. NO CHANGE gets both right. The third choice is the strongest trap: it looks neatly balanced, but the commas around the name wrongly treat an essential name as an aside.",["Correct: the name is essential (it tells which politician), so no comma before it; the \\"who\\" clause is extra, so a comma before \\"who.\\"","Puts a comma between the title and the essential name, and drops the comma the \\"who\\" clause needs.","Treats the name as extra information, but \\"the New York politician\\" needs the name to say which one.","Drops the comma before \\"who\\"; the clause adds extra information about one named person, so it needs a comma."]],"english-0014":["After \\"The canal\'s\\" the sentence needs a noun that means \\"result\\" or \\"influence.\\" That noun is \\"effect.\\" \\"Affect\\" is normally a verb (\\"The canal affected the towns\\"). \\"Effects\\" is the right word in the wrong number: the verb later in the sentence is \\"was,\\" so the subject must be singular. \\"Affecting\\" is a verb form. Memory trick: Affect = Action (verb), Effect = End result (noun).",["\\"Affect\\" is usually a verb (to influence); the sentence needs a noun meaning \\"result.\\"","Correct: \\"effect\\" is the noun meaning \\"result,\\" and it is singular to match \\"was.\\"","\\"Effects\\" is the right word but plural, so it doesn\'t agree with the verb \\"was.\\"","\\"Affecting\\" is a verb form, not the noun the sentence needs after \\"The canal\'s.\\""]],"english-0015":["A good transition sentence points in two directions. The previous paragraph ends with the canal opening and boats being towed; this paragraph is about economic results (cheaper shipping, a bigger port, growing towns). \\"Once it opened\\" connects to the opening, and \\"changed the economy of the region\\" sets up everything that follows. The first and third choices only look backward at construction and mules. The folk-song choice drifts to the present day. All are true, but only one links the two paragraphs.",["Looks back at the construction only; it doesn\'t lead into the economic changes this paragraph describes.","A true, interesting detail, but it is about the present day, not about the effects described in this paragraph.","Stays on the previous paragraph\'s mules and towpath; it doesn\'t introduce shipping costs or growing cities.","Correct: \\"Once it opened\\" picks up the previous paragraph, and \\"changed the economy\\" introduces this one."]],"english-0016":["\\"A trip that could take weeks\\" is not a complete sentence; it is a noun phrase that describes the wagon journey. A phrase like that attaches to the sentence with a comma, which is what the original does. Turning it into its own sentence (second choice) creates a fragment. A semicolon (third choice) needs a complete sentence on both sides. The last choice adds \\"it was,\\" which makes a complete sentence, but now two sentences are joined by just a comma: a comma splice.",["Correct: \\"a trip that could take weeks\\" renames the journey, and a comma correctly sets it off.","Creates a fragment: \\"A trip that could take weeks\\" has no main verb, so it can\'t stand alone.","A semicolon must join two complete sentences, and \\"a trip that could take weeks\\" is not one.","Comma splice: \\"it was a trip...\\" is a complete sentence joined to another with only a comma."]],"english-0017":["The new sentence talks about \\"her reply,\\" so it has to come after a sentence where a female replies. That happens in the sentence \\"A female perched on a plant answers with a flash of her own.\\" Point D comes right after that sentence and right before the two fireflies keep signaling, so the order makes sense: she answers, her timing tells him she\'s the right species, and they keep signaling. At Points A, B, and C, no female has replied yet. Trap: Point A sits next to the sentence about flash patterns, which is on the same topic but doesn\'t give \\"her\\" anything to refer to.",["No female has been mentioned yet, so \\"her reply\\" has nothing to refer to.","Only the general idea of flash patterns has come up; there is no \\"her\\" or \\"reply\\" yet.","This comes right before the female is introduced, so \\"her reply\\" would come before she replies.","Correct: it follows the sentence where the female answers, so \\"her reply\\" clearly refers to that flash."]],"english-0018":["The subject of this clause is \\"a compound called luciferin,\\" and its verb is \\"combines.\\" No punctuation belongs inside that subject or between the subject and the verb. The name \\"luciferin\\" is essential, since \\"a compound called\\" means nothing without it, so it shouldn\'t be set off with commas. Each wrong choice adds a comma that breaks up the sentence\'s core. When every choice but one adds commas, check whether any of those commas has a real job to do.",["Correct: \\"called luciferin\\" is an essential part of the subject, and no comma belongs between a subject and its verb.","Puts a comma between \\"called\\" and the name it introduces, splitting an essential phrase.","Puts a comma between the subject (\\"a compound called luciferin\\") and its verb \\"combines.\\"","Treats the name as extra information, but \\"a compound called\\" can\'t stand without it."]],"english-0019":["The essay explains a science topic in a clear, informative voice. Only the third choice keeps that tone and also says exactly what the light is: \\"the product of a chemical reaction.\\" The other three use slang or a chatty voice (\\"seriously cool,\\" \\"nope,\\" \\"totally,\\" \\"folks\\") that doesn\'t match the rest of the essay. They also tell the reader less, because \\"chemistry going on\\" is vaguer than \\"a chemical reaction.\\"",["Too casual: \\"honestly\\" and \\"seriously cool\\" sound like chatting, not the essay\'s clear, informative voice.","Too casual: \\"nope\\" and \\"totally a chemistry thing\\" are slang.","Correct: plain, clear, and informative, matching the rest of the essay.","Too casual: addressing readers as \\"folks\\" is chatty and doesn\'t fit the essay."]],"english-0020":["Read what comes right after the bracketed phrase: \\"For this reason, scientists sometimes describe firefly light as \'cold light.\'\\" The reason is that the energy is released as light RATHER THAN HEAT. Without those words, the sentence just says the energy becomes light, and \\"cold light\\" no longer has an explanation. The other choices name things that are either elsewhere in the essay (the enzyme, the beetle fact) or not in it at all (other glowing insects).",["The enzyme is mentioned in the sentence before; \\"rather than heat\\" says nothing about how it works.","Correct: the next sentence says \\"For this reason\\" the light is called cold light, and the reason is that little energy becomes heat.","No other insects are mentioned in this paragraph.","The beetle fact is in the first paragraph and has nothing to do with heat."]],"english-0021":["This paragraph has one focus: some females copy other species\' flash patterns to lure and eat males. The new statement is true and interesting, but it is about larvae warning predators, which is a different topic. So it should not be added. The first choice is tempting, but a warning that you taste bad is honest, not a trick. The fourth choice has the right decision for a wrong reason: the essay says fireflies use light \\"mainly\\" to find mates, so another use doesn\'t contradict it. On add/delete questions, the reason has to be accurate too.",["A warning that larvae taste bad is honest, not a trick, so it isn\'t another example of deception.","The statement is about larvae, not about how adult females learn to imitate signals.","Correct: the paragraph is about females faking signals to catch males; glowing larvae are off that focus.","Right decision, wrong reason: the essay says \\"mainly\\" to find mates, which leaves room for other uses, so there is no contradiction."]],"english-0022":["The children belong to the neighborhood, so the word needs to be possessive. The essay is about one neighborhood (\\"the neighborhood badly lacks\\"), so it is singular: add \'s to get \\"neighborhood\'s.\\" \\"Neighborhoods\\" is just a plural with no ownership, \\"neighborhoods\'\\" means more than one neighborhood, and \\"neighborhoods\'s\\" isn\'t a correct form at all.",["Plural with no apostrophe: it doesn\'t show that the children belong to the neighborhood.","Plural possessive, but the essay is about one neighborhood, not several.","Correct: singular possessive, the children of the one neighborhood the essay describes.","Not a standard form: a plural ending in -s takes only an apostrophe, and the word should be singular anyway."]],"english-0023":["The first sentence gives the opposing view: selling would bring in money right away. The next sentence answers it: a sale brings in money only ONCE, while a garden lasts for decades. That is a contrast, so the transition must signal one; \\"But\\" does. \\"Indeed\\" would mean the writer agrees and is adding emphasis. \\"For example\\" and \\"Likewise\\" also treat the second sentence as support for the first, when it is really a rebuttal. Read the sentence after the transition before choosing.",["\\"Indeed\\" agrees with and builds on the council members\' point, but the writer is arguing against it.","Nothing here is an example of the council members\' argument; it answers that argument.","\\"Likewise\\" signals a similar point, but this sentence pushes back on the previous one.","Correct: the writer turns from the council members\' view (money right away) to a counterpoint (money only once)."]],"english-0024":["The sentence begins with \\"Every spring,\\" which already tells you the plant sale happens once a year. Calling it \\"annual,\\" \\"yearly,\\" or \\"once-a-year\\" says the same thing twice. The repeated idea is easy to miss because it sits at the other end of the sentence. When you see a time word in a choice, check the whole sentence for another time word.",["Redundant: \\"Every spring\\" already tells you the sale happens once a year.","Redundant: \\"yearly\\" repeats \\"Every spring.\\"","Redundant: \\"once-a-year\\" repeats \\"Every spring.\\"","Correct: \\"Every spring\\" at the start of the sentence already says how often the sale happens."]],"english-0025":["The goal is specific: show that a plot would help families SAVE MONEY. Only the third choice mentions money, indirectly but clearly: if families grow vegetables \\"they would otherwise have to buy at the store,\\" they spend less at the store. The tomato-and-pepper choice is the strongest trap because it is the most vivid, but vivid isn\'t the goal. The neighbors choice fits a different point the essay makes later (bringing people together). Match every word of the goal, not just the topic.",["Accurate, but it says nothing about money or cost.","Vivid and specific, but it shows what the garden looks like, not what families would save.","Correct: food \\"they would otherwise have to buy at the store\\" is money they wouldn\'t spend.","Points to the social benefit of the garden, which is a different goal."]],"english-0026":["The essay sets up a decision (\\"the town council will decide what to do with the land\\"), states a position (\\"the best choice is to turn it into a community garden\\"), and gives reasons: food, money, and community. It even answers the opposing view about selling the lot. That is exactly what persuading leaders to choose a use for land looks like, so the answer is Yes. The second choice says Yes for a reason that isn\'t in the essay. The last choice is tempting, but bringing up the other side and rebutting it is a normal part of persuasion.",["Correct: the essay names a decision the council faces and argues for one choice, the garden, over the other.","Right decision, false reason: the essay says the lot has been empty for six years but never explains why.","The essay doesn\'t explain how to grow vegetables; it argues for the garden\'s benefits.","Mentioning the other side and then answering it makes an argument stronger, not weaker."]],"english-0027":["Some words always pair with a certain preposition. \\"Capable\\" takes \\"of\\": \\"capable of reading.\\" The original mixes up two patterns, \\"able to read\\" and \\"capable of reading.\\" \\"In\\" and \\"for\\" are simply the wrong prepositions. There\'s no rule to derive this; it\'s an idiom, so it helps to try each choice in the sentence and listen for the standard phrase.",["Wrong idiom: \\"capable\\" is followed by \\"of,\\" not \\"to\\" (\\"able to read\\" is fine, \\"capable to read\\" is not).","Correct: the standard idiom is \\"capable of\\" + an -ing word.","Wrong preposition: we say \\"capable of,\\" not \\"capable in.\\"","Wrong preposition: we say \\"capable of,\\" not \\"capable for.\\""]],"english-0028":["Look at how the sentence describes the first two versions: \\"in hieroglyphs, the script of temples and tombs\\" and \\"in Demotic, the script of everyday documents.\\" Each is followed by a short phrase in the form \\"the ___ of ___.\\" The third item should follow the same form: \\"in ancient Greek, the language of the kingdom\'s rulers.\\" The other choices give the same information, but as a clause or a verb phrase, which breaks the pattern. The third choice is the closest trap because it also starts with a noun, but it shifts to \\"a language that... spoke.\\"",["Breaks the pattern: the first two items use a short \\"the ___ of ___\\" phrase, not a \\"which was\\" clause.","Correct: \\"the language of the kingdom\'s rulers\\" matches \\"the script of temples and tombs\\" and \\"the script of everyday documents.\\"","Close, but \\"a language that... spoke\\" is a clause, not the \\"the ___ of ___\\" phrase used for the other two items.","Breaks the pattern: a verb phrase (\\"spoken by...\\") doesn\'t match the noun phrases describing the other scripts."]],"english-0029":["The two parts of the sentence are cause and effect: scholars could read Greek, SO the Greek section gave them a way to work out the other two. \\"Because\\" states that cause. \\"Although\\" and \\"Whereas\\" signal contrast, which would mean reading Greek somehow worked against using the Greek section. \\"Unless\\" turns it into a condition that hasn\'t been met, but the sentence says scholars could already read Greek. When the choices are joining words, decide the relationship between the two halves first.",["Wrong logic: \\"Although\\" signals a contrast, but being able to read Greek is the reason the Greek section helped.","Wrong logic: \\"Unless\\" sets a condition, but scholars already could read Greek.","Wrong logic: \\"Whereas\\" sets up a contrast between two things, and there is none here.","Correct: scholars could read Greek, and that is why the Greek section gave them a way in."]],"english-0030":["The paragraph tells events in order: in 1801 the stone was handed over to the British, and by 1802 it was in London. The second event simply follows the first, so a time transition like \\"Soon after\\" is the logical choice. \\"Instead\\" and \\"In contrast\\" both suggest the second event goes against the first, but it doesn\'t: being shipped to England is what you\'d expect after the handover. \\"For example\\" would introduce an example, and there isn\'t one.",["\\"Instead\\" suggests the shipping replaced the handover, but it followed from it.","Being shipped to England is not an example of the surrender terms; it is the next event.","\\"In contrast\\" signals an opposing idea, but the shipping continues the story.","Correct: the stone was handed over in 1801 and shipped soon after, so a time word fits the sequence."]],"english-0031":["The paragraph shows Mr. Okafor\'s love of order (every tool back on its hook) and then, in the bracketed sentence, the narrator\'s own orderly habit of laying parts out in sequence. Deleting it would lose that sign that the narrator picked up the same careful approach. It doesn\'t explain the hook rule, it isn\'t about patience (that was the bolt sentence), and it never says the narrator later worked without Mr. Okafor. The traps each describe something nearby in the paragraph, so check what the bracketed words themselves actually do.",["The sentence never gives a reason for the hook rule; it describes the narrator\'s own habit.","Correct: laying parts out in order is the narrator\'s version of Mr. Okafor\'s tool-on-its-hook orderliness.","Patience belongs to the bolt sentence before it; the towel sentence is about order, not patience.","Nothing says the narrator later worked alone; in fact the essay stresses working with Mr. Okafor."]],"english-0032":["The goal has two parts: unpredictable AND hard to ride. The original shows only the first part. The \\"jumped two gears\\" choice shows only the second part, because a chain that always jumps two gears is predictable. The noise choice shows neither. Only the last choice does both: the chain might do any of three things (\\"each surprise\\"), and the surprises nearly threw the narrator off. On two-part goals, check every choice against each part separately.",["Half the goal: \\"might climb... drop... or stay\\" shows unpredictability, but nothing shows the bike was hard to ride.","Half the goal: nearly being thrown off shows difficulty, but the chain does the same thing every time, so it isn\'t unpredictable.","Misses both parts: noise is neither unpredictable behavior nor a riding difficulty.","Correct: \\"might... or\\" and \\"each surprise\\" show unpredictability, and \\"nearly threw me off the seat\\" shows the riding difficulty."]],"english-0033":["An opening -ing phrase describes whoever comes right after the comma. \\"Leaning against the workbench with his arms folded\\" has to describe Mr. Okafor, because of the word \\"his,\\" so Mr. Okafor must be the subject, as in the original. The tempting trap is \\"I struggled...\\": a person can lean, so it sounds fine, but the narrator would have said \\"my arms,\\" not \\"his arms.\\" The other two choices make \\"twenty minutes\\" or \\"my struggle\\" do the leaning.",["Correct: the opening phrase \\"Leaning... with his arms folded\\" describes Mr. Okafor, and he is the subject right after the comma.","Subtle dangling modifier: it makes \\"I\\" the one leaning \\"with his arms folded\\"; the narrator would say \\"my arms,\\" so \\"his\\" points to Mr. Okafor.","Dangling modifier: it says the twenty minutes were leaning against the workbench.","Dangling modifier: it says the narrator\'s struggle was leaning against the workbench."]],"english-0034":["The paragraph says the narrator spent most of an afternoon adjusting the cable a quarter turn at a time. The next sentence reports the moment it finally worked, so a time word meaning \\"finally\\" fits: \\"At last.\\" \\"Even so\\" would mean the chain worked in spite of the adjusting, \\"Meanwhile\\" would put the success during the adjusting, and \\"For example\\" would present the success as an example of something, which it isn\'t.",["\\"Even so\\" signals contrast, but success after long, careful adjustment isn\'t a contrast; it\'s the payoff.","\\"Meanwhile\\" means at the same time, but the chain worked only after all the adjusting.","Correct: after an afternoon of quarter turns, the chain finally works, so \\"At last\\" fits.","Nothing here is an example of the previous sentence; it\'s the result that came after it."]],"english-0035":["First find the arrangement: in the first paragraph, Mr. Okafor gives the bike on one condition, \\"I had to fix it in his shed, with him.\\" The last choice returns to exactly that: every repair still happens in his shed, side by side. The other choices are good callbacks, but to the wrong thing: the patience lesson and the towel both come from the second paragraph, and the original sentence stresses the narrator working alone. When a conclusion question names a specific earlier idea, go back and find it before choosing.",["Working \\"by myself\\" moves away from the arrangement; Mr. Okafor\'s condition was that the work happen with him.","Echoes the lesson about the seized bolt in the second paragraph, not the arrangement in the first.","Echoes the towel from the second paragraph, not the arrangement Mr. Okafor proposed.","Correct: Mr. Okafor\'s condition was that the narrator fix the bike \\"in his shed, with him,\\" and this sentence says that is still how they work."]],"english-0036":["Ask whether the title is needed to know which poem is meant. Robert Frost wrote many poems, so \\"Robert Frost\'s poem\\" alone doesn\'t say which one. The title \\"Mending Wall\\" is essential information and takes no commas. Paired commas look tidy, which makes them the trap: they would only be right if Frost had written a single poem (compare \\"Frost\'s first book, A Boy\'s Will,\\" where there is only one first book). The one-comma choices are wrong under any reading, because a single comma either splits the subject from its verb or opens an aside that never closes.",["Correct: Frost wrote many poems, so the title is essential to tell which one is meant and takes no commas.","Paired commas mark the title as extra information, which would mean \\"Robert Frost\'s poem\\" already identified it, as if he wrote only one poem.","A lone comma after the title splits the subject from its verb \\"describes.\\"","A lone comma before the title opens an aside that never closes."]],"english-0037":["This sentence is inverted: the verb comes before its subject. Flip it to find the subject: \\"A row of upright stones runs along the top of the wall.\\" The subject is \\"row,\\" which is singular, so the verb is \\"runs.\\" The plural word \\"stones\\" is inside the phrase \\"of upright stones\\" and doesn\'t control the verb. All three alternatives are plural verbs that agree with \\"stones.\\"",["Correct: the sentence is inverted, and the subject after the verb is \\"a row,\\" which is singular.","Agrees with the nearby plural \\"stones\\" instead of the real subject, \\"a row.\\"","Plural verb (and a tense shift); the subject \\"a row\\" is singular.","Plural verb; the subject \\"a row\\" is singular, and the progressive also clashes with the paragraph\'s simple present."]],"english-0038":["The new sentence starts with \\"Without these,\\" so the sentence before it must name something plural that keeps \\"the two faces\\" from drifting apart. The sentence before Point D says the through stones span the wall, \\"tying the two faces together.\\" Taking away what ties the faces together is exactly what would let them drift apart. Point C is the trap: \\"small, irregular stones known as hearting\\" is also plural, but the paragraph says hearting only fills the space; nothing says it ties the faces. Point A comes before the faces exist, and Point B would make the faces hold themselves together.",["At Point A, the two faces haven\'t been introduced yet, so \\"the two faces\\" and \\"these\\" have nothing to point to.","At Point B, \\"these\\" would refer to the faces themselves, making the sentence say the faces hold the faces together.","Tempting, since hearting is plural, but the paragraph never says hearting holds the faces together; it only fills the space.","Correct: \\"these\\" refers to the through stones, which the previous sentence says tie the two faces together, so without them the faces drift apart."]],"english-0039":["Haldane changes her methods to suit a flaky slate that splits if struck too hard. The verb that means \\"adjust something to fit a situation\\" is \\"adapt,\\" and \\"adapt X to Y\\" is the standard phrase. \\"Adopt\\" (sounds alike) means to take up something new, \\"adept\\" is an adjective meaning skilled, and \\"adhere\\" means to stick to something and can\'t be used this way.",["\\"Adopt\\" means to take on something new; here she changes her existing methods to suit the stone.","Correct: \\"adapt... to\\" means adjust to fit, which is what she does for the crumbly slate.","\\"Adept\\" is an adjective meaning skilled, not a verb.","\\"Adhere\\" means to stick to and can\'t take \\"her methods\\" as an object."]],"english-0040":["Haldane admits concrete would be faster, which is a point in concrete\'s favor. Then she rarely recommends it anyway. That is a contrast, so \\"Even so\\" (meaning \\"despite that\\") fits. \\"For this reason\\" is the trap: the following sentences do give her reasons, but \\"this reason\\" would point back to the speed of concrete, and speed is not why she avoids it. \\"In other words\\" and \\"Likewise\\" signal a restatement and a similarity, and neither fits.",["Faster building is a reason to recommend concrete, not a reason to avoid it; this reverses the logic.","\\"She rarely recommends one\\" doesn\'t restate \\"it would be faster\\"; it pushes against it.","Correct: concrete would be faster, but she still rarely recommends it, which is a contrast.","\\"Likewise\\" signals similarity, but the two sentences point in opposite directions."]],"english-0041":["\\"Only one of whom still builds walls full-time\\" is a dependent clause: the word \\"whom\\" ties it back to \\"apprentices,\\" so it can hang off the sentence after a comma. The tempting swap to \\"them\\" turns it into a complete sentence (\\"Only one of them still builds walls full-time.\\"), and joining two complete sentences with only a comma is a comma splice. The semicolon choice has the opposite problem: a semicolon needs a complete sentence after it. \\"Of who\\" uses the subject form after a preposition, where the object form \\"whom\\" belongs.",["Correct: \\"only one of whom still builds walls full-time\\" is a dependent clause, so a comma correctly attaches it.","Comma splice: \\"only one of them still builds walls full-time\\" is a complete sentence, so a comma alone can\'t join it.","A semicolon must be followed by a complete sentence, and \\"only one of whom still builds...\\" is a dependent clause.","Wrong case: after the preposition \\"of,\\" the object form \\"whom\\" is needed."]],"english-0042":["The sentence before says only one of four apprentices still builds walls, which makes a reader wonder about the other three. The bracketed sentence answers that, and \\"She does not seem to mind\\" then responds to it. So keep it. The second \\"Kept\\" choice has the right decision but a made-up reason: the essay never says Haldane stopped training people. The \\"Deleted\\" choices fail because the apprentices are the paragraph\'s topic, and the sentence gives new information (why they quit) rather than just repeating that the work is slow.",["Correct: the sentence answers the question the previous sentence raises: what happened to the other three apprentices.","Right decision, wrong reason: nothing says Haldane has stopped taking apprentices; the sentence is about why three of them quit.","The paragraph is about who will carry on Haldane\'s work, and the apprentices are central to that, so this isn\'t a shift.","The third paragraph says the work is slow, but not that it drove apprentices away; the sentence adds new information."]],"english-0043":["Waiting a few days already means letting a few days pass, so the original says it twice. \\"Waits a few days\\" keeps the full meaning in the fewest words. The other two choices each double up on an idea (\\"go by and pass,\\" \\"patiently waits and is patient\\").",["\\"Waits\\" and \\"lets a few days pass by\\" say the same thing twice.","Correct: says it once, with nothing lost.","\\"Go by\\" and \\"pass\\" mean the same thing.","\\"Patiently waits\\" and \\"is patient\\" repeat each other."]],"english-0044":["The subject is long: \\"The sour flavor that many people associate with sourdough.\\" Its verb is \\"comes.\\" A long subject still doesn\'t get a comma before its verb. The clause beginning with \\"that\\" is essential (it tells which flavor), and clauses beginning with \\"that\\" never take commas. The original adds paired commas, which treat the clause as removable. The second choice is the subtler trap: it drops the first comma but keeps one between the subject and the verb.",["\\"That\\" begins an essential clause, which never takes commas; here the commas wall off words the subject needs.","A comma between a subject and its verb: \\"The sour flavor... comes\\" shouldn\'t be split, however long the subject.","Correct: the clause tells which flavor is meant, so it\'s essential and takes no commas.","A comma right after \\"that\\" breaks the clause apart for no reason."]],"english-0045":["\\"The acids do more than add flavor\\" and \\"they also make the starter too acidic...\\" are both complete sentences, so a comma alone can\'t join them (comma splice). A period fixes it. The \\"which\\" choice sounds smooth, but \\"which\\" would refer to \\"flavor\\" or the whole idea before it, and a flavor can\'t make the starter acidic; the plural verb \\"make\\" also has nothing plural to agree with. \\"Also making...\\" is a fragment with no subject or main verb.",["Comma splice: two complete sentences joined by only a comma.","\\"Which\\" after the comma points back to \\"flavor\\" (or the whole clause), neither of which can \\"make\\"; the plural verb also doesn\'t fit.","\\"Also making the starter too acidic...\\" has no subject or main verb, so it\'s a fragment.","Correct: two complete sentences, separated by a period."]],"english-0046":["Most students learn that \\"affect\\" is the verb and \\"effect\\" is the noun, but \\"effect\\" is also a verb meaning \\"to bring about.\\" \\"Effect a change\\" means \\"make a change happen,\\" which is exactly what the baker does by feeding the starter more often. \\"Affect that change\\" would mean \\"influence a change that\'s already happening,\\" which isn\'t the idea. \\"Inflict\\" is for unpleasant things, and \\"infect\\" is about disease. Here the original is correct, and the choice that sounds safest is the trap.",["Correct: as a verb, \\"effect\\" means to bring about, and the baker brings about a milder loaf.","\\"Affect\\" means to influence. You can\'t influence a change that doesn\'t exist yet; you bring it about.","\\"Inflict\\" means to force something unpleasant on someone, but a milder loaf is what the baker wants.","\\"Infect\\" means to spread disease, which makes no sense here."]],"english-0047":["The sentence mentions two singular things, \\"the starter\\" and \\"a batch of dough,\\" so \\"it\\" could refer to either one. The point is that the dough needs hours to rise, so naming \\"the dough\\" removes the doubt. \\"They\\" has no plural noun to agree with, and \\"this\\" is just as vague as \\"it.\\" When a pronoun could point to two nouns, the fix is usually to name the noun.",["Unclear: \\"it\\" could mean the starter or the dough, and the meaning changes depending on which.","Plural pronoun with no plural noun to refer to (\\"the baker\\" and \\"the starter\\" are singular).","Still unclear: \\"this\\" could point to the starter, the dough, or the whole act of mixing.","Correct: naming the noun makes clear that the dough is what needs hours to rise."]],"english-0048":["The next sentence claims that \\"a starter can outlive the baker who began it, as long as someone keeps feeding it.\\" The best lead-in shows how a starter continues after its baker, and passing it to one\'s children (with feeding instructions) does exactly that. Two traps are close: a starter kept for years shows a long life but not one longer than the baker\'s, and a friend feeding it during a trip matches \\"someone keeps feeding it\\" but only for a vacation. Check the whole claim, not just a word that matches.",["Shows that a starter can live for years, but not that it can outlive its baker.","Someone else feeds the starter, which echoes the next sentence, but only while the baker is away, not after the baker is gone.","This is about pride in the bread; it has nothing to do with a starter outliving its baker.","Correct: handing a starter down to the next generation is how a starter outlives the baker who began it."]],"english-0049":["\\"Each\\" always points to one item at a time, so the owner is a single school: \\"each school\'s.\\" The workers are the subject of the sentence (they \\"could help teach the course\\"), not owners of anything, so \\"workers\\" is a plain plural with no apostrophe. The original makes the school plural; the other two choices add an apostrophe to \\"workers\\" (or drop the one on \\"school\\").",["\\"Each\\" refers to one school at a time, so the possessive is singular: \\"school\'s,\\" not \\"schools\'.\\"","\\"School\'s\\" is right, but \\"workers\'\\" makes the workers own something, and they\'re the subject here, not owners.","Correct: one school (\\"each school\'s\\") owns the workers, and \\"workers\\" is a plain plural subject.","\\"Schools\\" needs an apostrophe to show possession, and \\"worker\'s\\" wrongly makes the workers possessive."]],"english-0050":["Testing a guess means finding out whether it\'s right, and the order of the list (look, guess, test) already shows that testing comes later. So \\"then... afterward\\" and \\"to see whether or not it is right\\" both repeat ideas. But the shortest choice isn\'t automatically best: \\"test\\" alone loses the object, so the reader doesn\'t know what is being tested. \\"Test that guess\\" is complete and has no repetition.",["\\"Then\\" and \\"afterward\\" say the same thing; the order of the list already shows the sequence.","Testing a guess already means checking whether it\'s right, so the added words repeat the idea.","Correct: says exactly what is tested, with nothing repeated.","Shortest, but it drops what is being tested, leaving the reader to guess."]],"english-0051":["Ask whether the words are needed to tell which motto is meant. \\"Its motto\\" (the club\'s motto) can only be one thing, so the quotation is extra information that renames it, and it takes a comma on both sides. The two one-comma choices open an aside without closing it, or close one that was never opened.",["Correct: the club has only one motto, so the quotation just renames it and is set off by a pair of commas.","Only a closing comma: the aside is never opened.","Only an opening comma: the aside is never closed, so it runs into the verb \\"hangs.\\"","No commas treats the quotation as essential, but \\"its motto\\" already identifies it, because a club has only one motto."]],"english-0052":["The paragraph opens with its focus: \\"the course would teach a way of thinking.\\" Everything after that is about guessing, testing, and trying again. A sentence about careers adds a different kind of benefit, so it drifts from the paragraph\'s focus. Don\'t add it. The \\"Yes\\" choices are tempting because the sentence is true and pro-course, but where it would go matters. The other \\"No\\" choice has the right decision but a false reason: \\"can also lead to careers\\" doesn\'t claim the course is only for students headed into the trades.",["A new benefit can help an essay, but this paragraph is about a way of thinking; a career benefit belongs elsewhere, if anywhere.","The sentence says nothing about employers valuing a way of thinking; it\'s about job opportunities.","Right decision, wrong reason: the sentence says repair skills \\"can also\\" lead to careers; it doesn\'t say the course is only for future tradespeople.","Correct: the paragraph is about the habit of guessing and testing, and careers are a different topic."]],"english-0053":["The critics\' point implies that a repair course would take time away from math and reading. The writer answers that the course would strengthen both, and the next sentence backs that up with fractions and wiring diagrams. That\'s a counterargument, so a contrast word fits: \\"However.\\" \\"Indeed\\" is the trap, because it sounds emphatic, but it signals agreement with what came before. \\"As a result\\" and \\"Similarly\\" signal cause and likeness.",["\\"Indeed\\" agrees with and builds on the critics, but the writer is about to push back on them.","The critics\' worry about time doesn\'t cause the course to strengthen math and reading.","\\"Similarly\\" signals a matching point, but the writer\'s point answers the critics rather than matching them.","Correct: the critics fear the course would crowd out math and reading, and the writer counters that it would actually strengthen both."]],"english-0054":["The essay uses plain, serious, reasonable language (\\"Our schools should change that...\\"). The original slips into slang. Two choices go the other way and become grand and dramatic; the last one also promises far more than the essay\'s modest point (\\"will not turn every student into an electrician\\"). The third choice matches the voice and the modest claim.",["\\"Kids,\\" \\"totally,\\" and \\"busted stuff\\" are too casual for this essay\'s clear, serious voice.","Overblown: \\"bestow,\\" \\"priceless,\\" and \\"eternal\\" are far grander than the rest of the essay.","Correct: plain and direct, and its modest claim (\\"can often be fixed\\") matches the essay\'s careful tone.","Too dramatic (\\"shall ever again defeat them\\") and it overclaims, clashing with the modest sentence before it."]],"english-0055":["The phrase \\"as many as three hundred men in a good year\\" is an aside that interrupts \\"the company\'s crews... walked out onto the lake.\\" It opens with a dash, so it has to close with a dash too. Mixing a dash and a comma is the classic trap, because a comma alone looks normal before a verb. Leaving the aside unclosed or using a semicolon both break the sentence.",["Mismatched pair: the aside opens with a dash, so it must close with a dash, not a comma.","Correct: the aside \\"as many as three hundred men in a good year\\" opened with a dash, so a second dash closes it.","With nothing closing the aside, it runs straight into the verb \\"walked.\\"","A semicolon can\'t close an aside, and \\"walked out onto the lake...\\" isn\'t a complete sentence on its own."]],"english-0056":["Use the pointer words. \\"Workers then sawed along these lines\\" needs something just before it that created lines, which is Sentence 3\'s \\"grid of deep grooves.\\" So 3 must come right before 2. \\"Cutting the ice was a careful process\\" is the topic sentence, so it goes first. The blocks can only be floated after they\'re sawed free (4 after 2), and \\"There\\" in Sentence 5 refers to \\"the shore\\" at the end of Sentence 4. That gives 1, 3, 2, 4, 5.",["Sentence 2 says \\"these lines\\" before any lines exist, and the plowing (Sentence 3) would come after the sawing.","Correct: topic sentence, then the plow scores the grooves, then workers saw \\"along these lines,\\" then the blocks travel to shore and up into the icehouse.","Opens with a detail before the topic sentence, and Sentence 1 separates \\"these lines\\" from the grooves it refers to.","Floats \\"the blocks\\" to shore before anyone has sawed them free, and \\"There\\" in Sentence 5 would no longer follow a place."]],"english-0057":["The clause needs to say where the block of ice kept food cold: inside the cabinet. \\"In which\\" does that. \\"In that\\" sounds formal, which makes it tempting, but it means \\"because\\" or \\"in the sense that,\\" not \\"inside which.\\" Plain \\"which\\" leaves the clause without a role for the cabinet, and \\"into which\\" describes motion into something, which doesn\'t fit a block that is sitting there keeping food cold.",["Correct: \\"in which\\" means \\"inside which,\\" and the block of ice sits inside the cabinet.","\\"In that\\" means \\"because\\" (\\"in that it was cold\\"), which garbles the sentence.","Without a preposition, \\"which\\" has no job in its clause; the block doesn\'t do anything to the cabinet.","\\"Into\\" signals motion, but the block is resting inside the cabinet keeping food cold, not moving into it."]],"english-0058":["\\"Delivery drivers worked... and often finished...\\" has one subject doing two things. When \\"and\\" joins two verbs that share a subject (not two complete sentences), no comma goes before it. The comma feels natural because the first part is long, which is what makes it a trap. The semicolon choice is wrong because a semicolon needs a complete sentence on each side.",["\\"Worked... and often finished\\" is one subject with two verbs, so no comma goes before \\"and.\\"","Adds a second stray comma after \\"and.\\"","Correct: one subject (\\"Delivery drivers\\") with two verbs joined by \\"and\\" needs no comma.","A semicolon must join two complete sentences, and \\"and often finished their routes soaked through\\" isn\'t one."]],"english-0059":["The essay is a clear, factual history written in a steady, serious voice. The second choice keeps that voice and gives a specific danger. The original and the last choice use slang and a chatty \\"you.\\" The third choice swings too far the other way into melodrama and overstates the danger.",["\\"No picnic\\" and \\"a guy\\" are too casual for this informative history.","Correct: plain, specific, and serious, matching the rest of the essay.","Overblown (\\"beyond all imagining,\\" \\"poor soul\\") and it overstates (\\"surely doomed\\").","\\"Kind of scary,\\" \\"you,\\" and \\"super fast\\" are chatty and casual."]],"english-0060":["The first sentence says that \\"a cold drink in July depended on work done in January.\\" The goal has two parts: the trade is still remembered, and the detail recalls that sentence. Only the lemonade chilled with lake ice cut the previous winter does both. The iced-tea choice recalls the July drink but says few people know the history. The saws-and-tongs choice shows remembering but echoes a different sentence (the crews with \\"saws and long iron tongs\\"). The original does neither.",["Shows the trade disappearing, not being remembered, and doesn\'t recall the opening image.","Correct: a July drink chilled with ice cut the winter before recalls the first sentence, and the historical society\'s custom shows the town remembers.","Half the goal: the donated tools show the trade is remembered, but they recall the crews in the first paragraph\'s last sentence, not its first sentence.","Half the goal: a cold July drink recalls the first sentence, but \\"few of whom know\\" shows the trade is forgotten."]],"english-0061":["\\"I went the next Saturday\\" and \\"the door was propped open with a bald tire\\" are both complete sentences. Two complete sentences need a period (or a semicolon) between them, not just a comma, so the original is a comma splice. Removing the comma makes it worse: a run-on. The \\"Going there\\" version sounds smooth, but it says the door went there, because the door is the first noun after the opening phrase.",["Comma splice: two complete sentences joined by only a comma.","Run-on: two complete sentences jammed together with no punctuation at all.","Correct: a period separates the two complete sentences.","Dangling modifier: \\"Going there\\" seems to describe the door, which can\'t go anywhere."]],"english-0062":["Find the subject: \\"The shelves along the back wall.\\" The word right before the verb is \\"wall,\\" but \\"along the back wall\\" only tells where the shelves are. The subject is \\"shelves,\\" which is plural, so the verb must be plural: \\"were.\\" \\"Is\\" and \\"has been\\" are singular too, and \\"is\\" also switches the story into the present tense.",["Agreement error: the subject is \\"shelves\\" (plural), so \\"was\\" (singular) doesn\'t fit.","Two errors: \\"is\\" is singular, and present tense clashes with the past-tense story.","Agreement error: \\"has been\\" is singular; \\"shelves\\" needs a plural verb.","Correct: plural \\"were\\" agrees with \\"shelves\\" and keeps the story in the past tense."]],"english-0063":["This paragraph is about the narrator\'s first visit to the shop: Ines hands over gloves, shows the chain tool, and steps back. A fact about how steep Larkin Hill is has nothing to do with that, so the sentence should go. The tempting trap is the first choice: the chain did snap on the hill, but the essay never says steepness caused it, and the sentence sits in the wrong paragraph to explain it. The \\"contradicts\\" choice has the right decision but a false reason.",["Unsupported link: the essay never says the hill\'s steepness made the chain snap, and this paragraph is about the repair shop anyway.","The detail may be vivid, but it has nothing to do with this paragraph about learning repairs from Ines.","Right decision, wrong reason: the first paragraph says nothing about how steep the hill is, so there is nothing to contradict.","Correct: the paragraph is about Ines teaching the narrator, and a fact about a street sign breaks into it."]],"english-0064":["The owner belongs to the bike, so you need a possessive. The possessive of \\"it\\" is \\"its,\\" with no apostrophe. \\"It\'s\\" always means \\"it is\\" or \\"it has.\\" \\"Its\'\\" doesn\'t exist. \\"Their\\" is plural, but the word it replaces is \\"every bike,\\" which is singular.",["\\"It\'s\\" means \\"it is\\"; \\"leave with it is owner\\" makes no sense.","Correct: \\"its\\" is the possessive form for one bike.","\\"Its\'\\" is not a word; the possessive of \\"it\\" never takes an apostrophe.","Agreement error: \\"their\\" is plural, but it refers to \\"every bike,\\" which is singular."]],"english-0065":["The essay is personal but written in clear, standard English (\\"walked the bike home in a sulk,\\" \\"felt as though I had built the whole bike myself\\"). Only the original keeps that voice. It also neatly repeats what Ines did in the second paragraph. The other three choices use slang (\\"totally chilled,\\" \\"bailed,\\" \\"peaced out\\") that clashes with the rest of the essay.",["Correct: the plain, clear wording matches the rest of the essay and echoes Ines stepping back earlier.","Too slangy: \\"totally chilled\\" is much more casual than the rest of the essay.","Too slangy, and \\"bailed on him\\" also suggests abandoning the boy, which isn\'t what happens.","Too slangy: \\"peaced out\\" and \\"do his thing\\" don\'t fit the essay\'s voice."]],"english-0066":["Ask whether the librarians own something. They don\'t: \\"The librarians who started the program hoped...\\" makes them the subject of the verb \\"hoped.\\" A subject that is just plural needs no apostrophe at all. Every other choice adds an apostrophe that signals possession, and nothing follows that they could possess.",["Unneeded possessive: the librarians don\'t own anything in this sentence; they are the subject.","Unneeded possessive (and singular): nothing belongs to a librarian here.","Correct: plain plural \\"librarians\\" is the subject of \\"hoped.\\"","Not a standard form: a plural ending in -s takes only an apostrophe for possession, and no possession is needed here anyway."]],"english-0067":["\\"Return\\" means \\"bring back.\\" Adding \\"back,\\" \\"back again,\\" or a second verb that means the same thing only repeats the idea. The original is already the shortest version that keeps the full meaning, so NO CHANGE is right.",["Correct: \\"returns\\" already means \\"brings back,\\" so nothing else is needed.","Redundant: \\"returns\\" already includes the idea of \\"back.\\"","Doubly redundant: \\"back\\" and \\"again\\" both repeat what \\"returns\\" means.","Redundant: \\"brings them back\\" and \\"returns them\\" say the same thing twice."]],"english-0068":["\\"Last year\\" puts the action in the past, and the same sentence uses \\"added,\\" which is past tense. So the verb should be the simple past, \\"borrowed,\\" which also matches \\"borrowed\\" in the sentence before it. The other choices are future or present forms that clash with \\"Last year.\\"",["Tense error: \\"Last year\\" is in the past, so the future \\"will borrow\\" doesn\'t fit.","Correct: simple past matches \\"Last year\\" and the other past-tense verb, \\"added.\\"","Tense error: present tense clashes with \\"Last year.\\"","Tense error: future perfect describes something finished by a future time, not last year."]],"english-0069":["The sentences before list problems: mislabeled envelopes and seeds that don\'t sprout. The next sentence says the program is \\"worth the trouble.\\" That\'s a turn in the other direction, so you need a contrast word like \\"Still.\\" \\"For example\\" would need an example to follow, \\"As a result\\" says the problems caused the good opinion, and \\"Similarly\\" says the ideas match.",["Wrong logic: the librarians\' opinion isn\'t an example of seeds being mislabeled or not sprouting.","Wrong logic: problems with the seeds wouldn\'t cause the librarians to say the program is worth it.","Wrong logic: \\"Similarly\\" signals a matching idea, but this sentence turns away from the problems.","Correct: \\"Still\\" shows contrast; despite the problems, the librarians think the program is worth it."]],"english-0070":["The paragraph ends by explaining that saved seeds can become better suited to the town\'s short summers and rocky soil. A sentence about lending cake pans is true to the library but has nothing to do with seeds, so it would distract from the paragraph\'s point. The trap is the first choice: cake pans really are unusual, but \\"interesting and related to the library\\" isn\'t the same as fitting this paragraph. The last choice reaches the right decision for a false reason, since the essay never mentions cake pans before.",["Tempting but off-focus: cake pans are unusual, but this paragraph is about seeds adapting to Marlow Creek.","False claim: the sentence says nothing about how the seed program began.","Correct: the paragraph ends on seeds becoming suited to the town; cake pans pull the reader away from that.","Right decision, wrong reason: cake pans are never mentioned earlier, so nothing is repeated."]],"english-0071":["Many students learn \\"no comma between a title and a name,\\" as in \\"the conservator Mirela Danko.\\" That rule works when the title alone could fit many people, so the name is needed to say which one. Here the title says she is the county\'s ONLY paper conservator. There is just one, so the name adds extra (nonessential) information and gets a comma on each side, like \\"my only sister, Rosa, lives in Ohio.\\" The one-comma choices are half a pair; the last one also puts a comma between the subject and the verb \\"has spent.\\"",["Correct: \\"only\\" already identifies the person, so her name is extra information and needs a comma on both sides.","Rule misapplied: no commas is right when the title could fit many people (\\"the conservator Mirela Danko\\"), but \\"only\\" singles her out.","Half a pair: the comma before the name needs a matching comma after it, before the verb \\"has.\\"","Half a pair: a lone comma now separates the subject from its verb, \\"has spent.\\""]],"english-0072":["Nothing in the sentence itself tells you whether to use commas; you have to read the sentence before it. The paragraph contrasts two kinds of paper: rag paper, which \\"has held up well,\\" and wood-pulp paper, which is \\"the real problem.\\" So the clause about wood pulp is what identifies which paper is the problem. A clause that identifies is essential: no commas, and \\"that\\" is the natural choice. With commas (NO CHANGE), the clause becomes a side comment, and \\"The paper\\" would refer back to the rag paper, which makes the paragraph contradict itself. The other two choices break basic rules: \\"that\\" can\'t follow a comma this way, and a single comma can\'t separate a subject from its verb.",["Meaning error: with commas, \\"The paper\\" points back to the rag paper just described, as if that paper were made from wood pulp.","Correct: the clause is essential; it tells WHICH paper is the problem, so it takes \\"that\\" and no commas.","\\"That\\" never begins a clause set off by commas.","A lone comma now splits the subject from its verb, \\"is.\\""]],"english-0073":["Danko is saying a too-neat repair is the same as a lie. The word for \\"equivalent to\\" is \\"tantamount,\\" and it always takes \\"to.\\" \\"Paramount\\" sounds almost the same but means \\"of the highest importance,\\" so it\'s a sound-alike trap. \\"Amounts for\\" and \\"tantamount with\\" use the wrong preposition; the correct idioms are \\"amounts to\\" and \\"tantamount to.\\"",["Wrong word: \\"paramount\\" means \\"most important,\\" so \\"paramount to a lie\\" says nothing sensible.","Wrong preposition: the idiom is \\"amounts to,\\" not \\"amounts for.\\"","Correct: \\"tantamount to\\" means \\"equivalent to,\\" which is exactly Danko\'s point.","Wrong preposition: \\"tantamount\\" is always followed by \\"to.\\""]],"english-0074":["The new sentence starts with \\"Both,\\" so it must follow a sentence that names exactly two things, and those things must be removable parts of \\"her work.\\" At Point C, Danko has just brushed on paste and pressed on tissue: two materials she adds to the map, which a later conservator might want to remove. At Point A, the sentence before lists three tools, and only the paste ever touches the map. At Point B, \\"cotton and linen rags\\" is a pair, but rags in old paper aren\'t her work and can\'t be undone. Point D is tempting because the fourth paragraph is about honest repairs, but nothing right before it gives \\"Both\\" a pair to point to.",["\\"Both\\" needs two things; the sentence before Point A lists three tools, and a bone folder and brush aren\'t put on a map.","The only pair here is \\"cotton and linen rags,\\" which can\'t be \\"removed\\" from a map or \\"undo her work.\\"","Correct: \\"Both\\" refers to the paste and the tissue she has just applied, and \\"undo her work\\" fits a repair.","Same topic (careful repairs), but nothing in the sentence before Point D gives \\"Both\\" two things to refer to."]],"english-0075":["First find the reason the paragraph already gives: a too-neat repair \\"is tantamount to a lie, since it hides what the map has been through.\\" So the reason is honesty. Only the second choice repeats that idea; removing the marks would be \\"the same dishonesty\\" in a quieter form. Every other choice is about the marks and sounds reasonable, which is the trap. Each one adds a different reason (interest, value to collectors, risk of damage) instead of reinforcing the one already given.",["On topic, but it gives a new reason (the marks interest her), not the one stated earlier.","Correct: calling removal \\"dishonesty\\" ties back to the earlier point that a too-neat repair is \\"tantamount to a lie.\\"","A believable reason, but a different one (money), not the honesty reason given earlier.","A believable reason, but a new one (risk of damage), not the honesty reason given earlier."]],"english-0076":["The sentence says the footbridge is one of a group: \\"the few bridges in Carrow County that still carry foot traffic.\\" The clause \\"that still carry foot traffic\\" describes the group of bridges, not the one footbridge, so \\"that\\" stands for \\"bridges\\" and the verb must be plural. Test it by flipping the sentence: \\"Of the few bridges that still carry foot traffic, this is one.\\" The singular choices are tempting because the sentence\'s main subject, \\"footbridge,\\" is singular, but that subject goes with \\"is,\\" not with \\"carry.\\"",["Correct: \\"that\\" refers to \\"bridges\\" (plural), so the verb is plural \\"carry.\\"","Agreement trap: the verb seems to go with \\"one\\" or \\"footbridge,\\" but \\"that\\" stands for \\"bridges.\\"","Same singular-verb trap, plus an awkward shift to the progressive tense.","Same singular-verb trap, and \\"has still carried\\" is an unnatural tense here."]],"english-0077":["Look past the underlined part: after \\"1968\\" there is a dash, and then the sentence keeps going with \\"and twice, residents packed the courthouse.\\" That means \\"once in 1931 and again in 1968\\" is an interruption set off in the middle of the sentence, and an interruption needs matching marks on both sides. Since it closes with a dash, it must open with a dash. The colon is the strongest trap because a colon often introduces details, but a colon can\'t be paired with a closing dash, and the sentence continues after the details. The comma makes a mismatched pair, and the semicolon needs a complete sentence after it.",["Mismatched pair: a colon can\'t open an interruption that a dash closes, and a colon here would end the sentence\'s main idea too early.","Mismatched pair: the interruption opens with a comma but closes with a dash.","A semicolon must join two complete sentences; \\"once in 1931 and again in 1968\\" isn\'t one.","Correct: a dash opens the interruption to match the dash that closes it after \\"1968.\\""]],"english-0078":["The paragraph sets up a disagreement: the county\'s inspectors reported rotting trusses, but the residents\' engineer found only a few bad boards. The new sentence explains how that gap could happen: the inspectors looked from a boat and never climbed into the trusses. That fits the paragraph\'s point, so it should be added. The second choice reaches \\"Yes\\" but claims the inspectors lied on purpose, which the sentence doesn\'t show. The \\"shifts the focus\\" choice is the most tempting \\"No,\\" but the inspectors are already central to this paragraph. The \\"contradicts\\" choice forgets that the earlier sentence only says what the inspectors reported.",["Correct: inspecting from a boat suggests why they saw rot that a close look didn\'t confirm, which supports the residents\' side of the dispute.","Right decision, overstated reason: a careless inspection isn\'t proof that anyone lied on purpose.","The paragraph is about the clash between the county\'s report and the residents\' engineer, so the inspectors are already part of its focus.","Misreading: the paragraph says the inspectors REPORTED rot, not that the rot was real, so nothing is contradicted."]],"english-0079":["The goal has two parts: (1) go back to an idea from the first paragraph and (2) show that people kept the bridge alive. The first paragraph\'s main idea is that the builder expected it to last about forty years. The second choice uses that idea and says the neighbors, by refusing to give it up, gave it three times as long. The original covers only part 1. The \\"Twice its neighbors\\" choice covers only part 2, and it echoes the second paragraph. The last choice is the sneakiest: it repeats \\"the few... bridges\\" from the first paragraph, but it talks about the officials who wanted the bridge gone, not the people who defended it.",["Half the goal: it returns to the builder\'s forty-year estimate but doesn\'t credit the people who defended the bridge.","Correct: it returns to the forty-year estimate from the first paragraph and credits the neighbors who refused to give the bridge up.","Half the goal: it credits the defenders, but it recalls the second paragraph\'s protests, not an idea from the first paragraph.","Echoes \\"the few bridges\\" from the first paragraph, but it focuses on the officials who opposed the bridge, not the people who saved it."]],"english-0080":["Pull out the words \\"the county had assumed\\" for a moment: \\"The residents who would accept the new bridge.\\" Now you can see the pronoun is the subject of \\"would accept,\\" so it must be the subject form, \\"who.\\" The trap is that \\"the county had assumed\\" sits right after the pronoun and makes it look like an object (\\"the county had assumed whom\\"). Try the he/him test: \\"the county had assumed HE would accept,\\" not \\"HIM would accept.\\" \\"He\\" means \\"who.\\" \\"Which\\" is for things, and \\"whose\\" shows ownership.",["Case trap: \\"the county had assumed\\" is an aside; the pronoun is really the subject of \\"would accept,\\" so it needs \\"who.\\"","\\"Which\\" refers to things, not people like residents.","\\"Whose\\" shows possession, but nothing belongs to the residents here.","Correct: \\"who\\" is the subject of \\"would accept the new bridge.\\""]],"english-0081":["The necks belong to the parents, so you need a possessive pronoun that is plural: \\"their.\\" \\"There\\" is a place word and \\"they\'re\\" is short for \\"they are,\\" so both are sound-alike traps. \\"Its\\" is possessive, but it is singular, and \\"parents\\" is plural.",["\\"There\\" points to a place; it can\'t show that the necks belong to the parents.","\\"They\'re\\" means \\"they are\\"; \\"craned they are necks\\" makes no sense.","Number error: \\"its\\" is singular, but the necks belong to the parents (plural).","Correct: \\"their\\" is the plural possessive pronoun for \\"the parents.\\""]],"english-0082":["To \\"return\\" is to go back. So \\"returned back again\\" says the same thing three times. \\"Returned back\\" still doubles it, and \\"came back and returned\\" says it twice in a different way. Plain \\"returned\\" keeps the full meaning with no extra words.",["Redundant twice: \\"returned\\" already means \\"went back,\\" and \\"again\\" repeats the idea a third time.","Correct: \\"returned\\" says it once, with no repeated meaning.","Still redundant: \\"back\\" repeats what \\"returned\\" already means.","Redundant: \\"came back\\" and \\"returned\\" mean the same thing."]],"english-0083":["Ask what was in the crate: the plates. \\"For each team\\" only tells whose plates they were, so the subject is \\"plates,\\" which is plural. The verb must be plural too: \\"were stacked.\\" The word right before the verb is \\"team,\\" which is what makes \\"was\\" sound fine. \\"Has been\\" and \\"is\\" are singular as well, and they also knock the story out of the past tense.",["Agreement error: the subject is \\"plates\\" (plural), not \\"team.\\"","Two errors: \\"has\\" is singular, and it breaks the story\'s simple past tense.","Correct: plural \\"were\\" agrees with \\"plates\\" and keeps the past tense.","Two errors: \\"is\\" is singular, and it switches the story to the present."]],"english-0084":["This sentence has an aside inside an aside. The big one, about what the scoreboard was built from, is opened by a dash, so it has to be closed by a dash. Inside it, \\"my uncle claimed\\" is a small interruption that needs a comma on both sides. The original does all of that, so it is correct. The tempting choice ends with a comma: commas and dashes can each mark an aside, but you can\'t open with one and close with the other. The other two each drop one mark, so a piece of the aside runs into the words after it. When the original looks busy, check each pair before you change it.",["Correct: a dash opens and a dash closes the aside, and the commas inside set off \\"my uncle claimed.\\"","Mismatched pair: the aside opens with a dash, so it must close with a dash, not a comma.","Half a pair: \\"my uncle claimed\\" is opened by a comma but never closed, so it runs into \\"from the side panels.\\"","Missing closing mark: with no dash after \\"truck,\\" the aside runs straight into \\"had outlasted.\\""]],"english-0085":["The goal has two parts: (1) refer back to the opening and (2) show that the narrator\'s feelings changed. The opening says the narrator sulked, sure he was missing a better Saturday. The third choice turns that around: those Saturdays he thought he was wasting are now the ones he\'d least like to miss. That does both jobs. The original and the \\"reminded him\\" choice each echo the opening but say nothing about feelings. The \\"hang a new score\\" choice shows the narrator wants to stay, but it doesn\'t connect to the opening. On two-part goals, check each choice against both parts.",["Half the goal: it echoes \\"the booth behind home plate\\" from the opening, but it doesn\'t say how the narrator feels now.","Half the goal: it recalls the opening (signed up without asking) but reveals nothing about the narrator\'s feelings.","Correct: \\"the Saturdays I once thought I was wasting\\" recalls the sulking first game, and \\"the ones I would least like to miss\\" shows the change.","Half the goal: it shows the narrator now wants the job, but it points to the scoreboard work in the second paragraph, not the opening."]],"english-0086":["\\"Marguerite Osei has tuned pianos ... for almost thirty years\\" is a complete sentence, and so is \\"she still carries the same canvas tool roll.\\" Two complete sentences can\'t be joined by a comma alone (a comma splice) or by nothing (a run-on). A period fixes it. The last choice sounds smooth, but \\"and still carrying\\" can\'t team up with \\"has tuned\\"; it would need to be \\"and still carries.\\"",["Comma splice: two complete sentences joined by only a comma.","Correct: a period separates the two complete sentences.","Run-on: two complete sentences with no punctuation between them.","Faulty verb: \\"has tuned ... and still carrying\\" pairs a real verb with an -ing word, so the second half has no proper verb."]],"english-0087":["\\"A strong ___\\" needs a noun. \\"Effect\\" is the noun that means an influence or result, so humidity has a strong effect on pitch. \\"Affect\\" and \\"affects\\" are verb forms (humidity affects pitch), so they can\'t follow \\"a strong.\\" \\"Effects\\" is plural, but \\"a strong\\" calls for just one.",["Sound-alike: \\"affect\\" is usually a verb (to influence); after \\"a strong\\" you need a noun.","Verb, not noun: \\"affects\\" is what humidity does (humidity affects pitch), but after \\"a strong\\" you need a noun.","Number error: \\"a strong\\" needs one thing, so the plural \\"effects\\" doesn\'t fit.","Correct: \\"effect\\" is the noun meaning a result or influence."]],"english-0088":["Brooke didn\'t trust electronic tuning devices, so he taught Osei to tune by ear in place of them. \\"Instead\\" signals exactly that swap. \\"For example\\" needs an example to follow. \\"Similarly\\" needs two alike things. \\"Even so\\" means \\"despite that,\\" which would only make sense if teaching by ear were surprising for someone who distrusts devices. It isn\'t.",["Correct: Brooke rejected the devices and taught tuning by ear in their place.","No example follows: tuning by ear is not an example of distrusting devices.","Wrong logic: tuning by ear is the opposite of using devices, not something similar.","Wrong logic: \\"even so\\" signals a surprise, but teaching by ear is exactly what you\'d expect from someone who distrusts devices."]],"english-0089":["Read the sentence after the insertion point: \\"When those strings are even slightly out of tune with one another...\\" Which strings? Nothing before it mentions strings at all. The new sentence supplies them: one key strikes two or three strings, and those strings have to agree. So the sentence belongs. The big trap is \\"No, it shifts the focus\\": a fact about how pianos are built looks like a side trip, but this one sets up the very next sentence. The first choice has the right decision with a reason the sentence doesn\'t support, and the last choice invents a contradiction.",["Right decision, wrong reason: the sentence says how many strings a key strikes, not why notes are tuned one at a time.","Correct: the next sentence\'s \\"those strings\\" are the two or three strings a key strikes; without this sentence, the reader can\'t tell which strings are meant.","Tempting, but the fact isn\'t a detour: the next sentence\'s \\"those strings\\" depends on it.","No contradiction: tuning one note at a time works fine when each note has two or three strings."]],"english-0090":["The sentence says Brooke was \\"one of only three tuners\\" in the county. Since there were three, \\"the tuner\\" alone doesn\'t tell you which one; the name is essential, so no commas go around it (just like \\"the singer Aretha Franklin\\"). After the name, though, a comma is needed, because \\"one of only three tuners then working in the county\\" is an extra description of Brooke. The original does both things. The tempting \\"the tuner, Ansel Brooke,\\" looks neat, but those commas would mean the name is just a bonus detail, which only works if there were one tuner.",["Correct: there were three tuners, so the name is needed to say which one; no commas around it. The comma after the name opens the description that follows.","Wrong commas: setting off the name treats it as extra, as if he were the only tuner, but the sentence says there were three.","Half a pair and the wrong idea: a comma before the name with nothing after it also runs the name into \\"one of only three tuners.\\"","Missing comma: \\"one of only three tuners\\" is a description of Brooke and needs a comma to separate it from his name."]],"english-0091":["Two words to check. The trucks belong to Halden, which is one town, so the owner gets apostrophe + s: \\"town\'s.\\" The trucks themselves don\'t own anything, so \\"trucks\\" is a plain plural with no apostrophe. Only \\"town\'s salt trucks\\" gets both right. \\"Towns\\" with no apostrophe isn\'t possessive, \\"towns\'\\" means several towns, and \\"truck\'s\\" adds an apostrophe a plain plural doesn\'t need.",["Missing apostrophe: the trucks belong to the town, so \\"town\\" needs to be possessive.","Wrong number: \\"towns\'\\" is a plural possessive, but the sentence is about one town, Halden.","Correct: \\"town\'s\\" is the singular possessive, and \\"trucks\\" is a plain plural.","Extra apostrophe: \\"truck\'s\\" makes the trucks possessive, but they don\'t own anything here; they\'re just plural."]],"english-0092":["\\"Before a storm arrives\\" already tells you the brine goes down ahead of time. Adding \\"ahead of time\\" or \\"in advance\\" just repeats it, and \\"ahead of time, prior to\\" doubles it a different way. The shortest choice keeps all the meaning.",["Redundant: \\"ahead of time\\" repeats \\"before a storm arrives.\\"","Redundant: \\"in advance\\" repeats \\"before.\\"","Redundant: \\"ahead of time\\" and \\"prior to\\" say the same thing twice.","Correct: \\"before a storm arrives\\" says it once."]],"english-0093":["A helping verb like \\"has\\" or \\"had\\" must be followed by a past participle. For \\"freeze,\\" the forms are freeze, froze, frozen, so it\'s \\"has frozen.\\" \\"Froze\\" is the simple past (it froze), which can\'t follow \\"has\\" or \\"had.\\" \\"Have frozen\\" fixes the verb form but breaks agreement: the subject, \\"slush,\\" is singular.",["Wrong form: after \\"has\\" you need the past participle \\"frozen,\\" not the simple past \\"froze.\\"","Correct: \\"has frozen\\" uses the past participle after \\"has,\\" and \\"has\\" agrees with the singular subject \\"slush.\\"","Agreement error: the subject is \\"slush,\\" which is singular, so it takes \\"has,\\" not \\"have.\\"","Still the wrong form: \\"had\\" also needs the past participle \\"frozen\\"; \\"froze\\" is the simple past."]],"english-0094":["A good transition faces both ways. The paragraph before describes a careful way to use salt (spraying brine before storms). This paragraph is about where the salt ends up and the harm it does. \\"However carefully it is spread, all of that salt has to go somewhere\\" nods back to the careful spraying and sets up the trip into soil and lakes. It also gives \\"it\\" in the next sentence a clear meaning: the salt. The original only looks back. The \\"Road salt can harm\\" choice only looks forward, so the jump from the last paragraph feels sudden. The rock salt fact is true but connects to nothing.",["Looks back only: it repeats the plowing point and leaves \\"it\\" in the next sentence with nothing sensible to refer to.","Looks forward only: it introduces the harm, but nothing connects it to the brine spraying just described.","Off topic: a true fact about where salt comes from, but it links to neither paragraph.","Correct: \\"however carefully it is spread\\" looks back at the careful brine spraying, and \\"has to go somewhere\\" leads into where the salt ends up."]],"english-0095":["Strip the aside: \\"The salt itself ... eventually washes into nearby streams.\\" The subject is \\"salt,\\" which is singular, so \\"washes\\" is right. The trap is the stretch between the commas: \\"along with the sand and chemicals that crews spread on the roads.\\" It is full of plural words, and \\"roads\\" sits right before the verb, so \\"wash\\" sounds natural. But \\"along with,\\" like \\"as well as\\" or \\"together with,\\" never turns a singular subject plural. Only \\"and\\" does that. \\"Have eventually washed\\" is plural too, and \\"washing\\" leaves the sentence without a main verb.",["Correct: the subject is \\"salt\\" (singular); \\"along with\\" adds information without making the subject plural.","Agreement error: this treats \\"the sand and chemicals\\" (or \\"roads\\") as the subject, but they sit inside an \\"along with\\" aside.","Agreement error: \\"have\\" is plural, and the subject \\"salt\\" is singular.","Fragment: \\"washing\\" can\'t be the main verb of a sentence, so the sentence has no real verb."]],"english-0096":["The essay is a serious argument aimed at a town council, written in a clear, formal voice. \\"Well worth the cost\\" fits that voice and connects neatly to \\"would cost money\\" earlier in the sentence. The other three all use slang (\\"totally,\\" \\"no question,\\" \\"super,\\" \\"big time\\") that sounds like a text message, not a proposal.",["Too casual: \\"totally\\" and \\"no question\\" are chatty slang in a serious argument.","Too casual: \\"super\\" is slang that clashes with the essay\'s formal voice.","Correct: \\"well worth the cost\\" is clear, formal, and answers \\"would cost money.\\"","Too casual: \\"big time\\" is slang that doesn\'t fit an argument to a town council."]],"english-0097":["\\"Because light that shines upward is wasted\\" can\'t stand alone: \\"because\\" makes you wait for the rest of the thought, so the original has a fragment. Drop \\"because\\" and you have two complete sentences: \\"Light that shines upward is wasted\\" and \\"No one on the ground benefits from it.\\" A semicolon can join them. A comma alone can\'t (comma splice), and no punctuation at all makes a run-on.",["Fragment: \\"Because\\" turns the first part into an incomplete thought standing alone as a sentence.","Correct: a semicolon joins two complete, closely related sentences.","Comma splice: two complete sentences joined by only a comma.","Run-on: two complete sentences with no punctuation between them."]],"english-0098":["The light belongs to the fixture, so you need a possessive. For \\"it,\\" the possessive is \\"its,\\" with no apostrophe. \\"It\'s\\" always means \\"it is\\" or \\"it has.\\" \\"Their\\" is possessive but plural, and there is only one fixture in this sentence. \\"Its\'\\" is never correct.",["\\"It\'s\\" means \\"it is\\"; \\"nearly all of it is light\\" makes no sense here.","Number error: \\"their\\" is plural, but the light belongs to one fixture.","Not a word: the possessive of \\"it\\" never takes an apostrophe.","Correct: \\"its\\" is the possessive for one fixture."]],"english-0099":["A comparison has to put like things side by side. The sentence says \\"the sky over Lavell is now far darker than ...\\", so the other side must be a sky too: \\"the sky over Corrin Falls.\\" The original sounds fine because we know what it means, but it literally says a sky is darker than a town. \\"Corrin Falls is\\" and \\"the town of Corrin Falls\\" make the same mistake in different words.",["Faulty comparison: this compares the sky over Lavell to a town, not to another sky.","Faulty comparison: still compares the sky to the town itself (\\"darker than Corrin Falls is\\").","Correct: it compares the sky over Lavell to the sky over Corrin Falls, two like things.","Faulty comparison: \\"the town of Corrin Falls\\" is still a town, not a sky."]],"english-0100":["This one reverses a famous trick. Often, words like \\"the council expects\\" hide the fact that the pronoun is a subject, so \\"who\\" is right. Here, look at what comes right before the pronoun: \\"some of.\\" The pronoun is the object of \\"of,\\" so it must be \\"whom\\" (\\"some of them,\\" never \\"some of they\\"). The subject of \\"will object\\" is \\"some,\\" not the pronoun. Lift out \\"the council expects\\" to check: \\"some of whom will object to the cost.\\" \\"Whose\\" shows ownership, and \\"them\\" turns the end into a second sentence held on by just a comma.",["Correct: the pronoun is the object of the preposition \\"of,\\" so it takes the object form \\"whom\\"; \\"some\\" is the subject of \\"will object.\\"","Case trap: \\"the council expects\\" makes it look like the pronoun is the subject of \\"will object,\\" but the subject is \\"some\\"; after \\"of\\" you need \\"whom.\\"","\\"Whose\\" shows ownership, but nothing belongs to the shop owners here.","Comma splice: \\"some of them\\" starts a new complete thought joined to the sentence by only a comma."]],"english-0101":["The blade belongs to the oar, so you need a possessive pronoun for one thing: \\"its.\\" No apostrophe. \\"It\'s\\" is the contraction of \\"it is,\\" the classic sound-alike trap. \\"Their\\" is plural, and there is only one oar. \\"His\\" would point to Coach Pirelli, but the blade is part of the oar he handed over.",["Correct: \\"its\\" is the possessive for one thing, the oar, whose blade this is.","\\"It\'s\\" means \\"it is\\"; \\"It is blade was painted\\" makes no sense.","Number error: \\"their\\" is plural, but the blade belongs to one oar.","Wrong owner: \\"his\\" would make it Coach Pirelli\'s blade, but the blade is part of the oar."]],"english-0102":["\\"The river was still dark\\" on an early morning already tells you the sun hasn\'t risen. The original adds \\"since the sun had not yet risen,\\" which repeats that. The third choice says it three ways (dark, before sunrise, sun not up), and the last one adds \\"unlit,\\" which means the same as \\"dark.\\" The short version keeps all the meaning.",["Redundant: a river that is \\"still dark\\" first thing in the morning already tells you the sun isn\'t up.","Correct: \\"still dark\\" says it once, with no repeated idea.","Redundant twice: \\"before sunrise\\" and \\"when the sun had not come up\\" repeat each other and \\"still dark.\\"","Redundant: \\"unlit by any sun\\" just says \\"dark\\" a second time."]],"english-0103":["Look at each side of the bracket. \\"Only our coxswain, Lena, who sits in the stern facing forward, can see where the boat is going\\" is a full sentence. \\"She steers with a small rudder and calls out the rhythm\\" is another full sentence. Two full sentences need a period (or a semicolon, or a comma plus \\"and\\"). A comma alone makes a comma splice, and no punctuation makes a run-on. Adding \\"then\\" is a common fake fix: it\'s still two sentences joined by a comma.",["Comma splice: \\"Only our coxswain ... can see where the boat is going\\" and \\"she steers ...\\" are both full sentences, and a comma alone can\'t join them.","Run-on: two full sentences are jammed together with no punctuation at all.","Correct: the period ends the first full sentence, and \\"She steers...\\" starts the second.","Still a comma splice: adding \\"then\\" doesn\'t make the second sentence part of the first."]],"english-0104":["Ask what is being compared. The sentence starts with \\"they,\\" meaning the narrator\'s distances. Distances must be compared with distances, not with people. \\"Longer than the other novices\\" compares distances to novices, which sounds fine in a hurry but doesn\'t make sense. \\"Those of the other novices\\" means \\"the distances of the other novices,\\" so the comparison is logical. \\"That of\\" makes the same move, but \\"that\\" is singular and \\"distances\\" is plural. \\"The other novice\'s\\" is singular too: the narrator was one of seven, so there were six others.",["Faulty comparison: this compares the narrator\'s distances to the other novices themselves, not to their distances.","Number error: there were six other novices, so a singular possessive (\\"novice\'s\\") points to only one of them.","Correct: \\"those\\" stands for \\"distances,\\" so distances are compared with distances.","Number error: \\"that\\" is singular, but it has to stand for \\"distances,\\" which is plural."]],"english-0105":["The new sentence starts with \\"this kind of timing\\" and says crews chase it, so it has to follow a sentence that describes good timing. That\'s the sentence about all eight blades entering at the same instant and leaving together, so the new sentence goes right after it, at Point D. Point C is the trap: the sentence before it mentions timing too, but it describes a blade going in a split second early, which only makes the boat rock. Nobody chases that. The new sentence also sets up the last one: \\"when we get it right\\" then refers to swing.",["Wrong spot: nothing before Point A describes timing; the sentence there would follow the earlier paragraph about winning a seat.","Wrong spot: the sentence before Point B is about rowing a full practice without stopping, which is endurance, not timing.","Tempting, but the sentence before Point C describes a blade going in early, which is bad timing; crews don\'t chase that.","Correct: \\"this kind of timing\\" points back to all eight blades going in and coming out together, the timing crews want."]],"english-0106":["This is the \\"one of the ... that\\" pattern. Ask what \\"that\\" refers to. The fruits are the things that are native to North America, and the cranberry is one of them. Since \\"that\\" stands for \\"fruits\\" (plural), the verb after it is plural: \\"are.\\" Most people change it to \\"is\\" because the sentence begins with \\"one,\\" but \\"one\\" goes with \\"The cranberry is,\\" earlier in the sentence. \\"Has been\\" and \\"was\\" make the same singular mistake and also break the present tense of a general fact. The original is correct.",["Correct: \\"that\\" refers to \\"fruits,\\" which is plural, so the verb in the \\"that\\" clause is plural: \\"are.\\"","Agreement trap: \\"is\\" agrees with \\"one,\\" but the \\"that\\" clause describes the fruits, not the one.","Two errors: \\"has been\\" is singular, and it shifts the general fact out of the present tense.","Two errors: \\"was\\" is singular, and it suggests the cranberry is no longer native."]],"english-0107":["The subject comes right before the verb: \\"the loose berries.\\" Berries is plural, so the verb must be plural: \\"float.\\" The other three choices all use singular forms (\\"floats,\\" \\"is,\\" \\"has\\"). The paragraph is in the present tense, so plain \\"float\\" fits best.",["Agreement error: \\"floats\\" is singular, but the subject, \\"berries,\\" is plural.","Agreement error: \\"is\\" is singular; \\"berries\\" needs \\"are\\" or just \\"float.\\"","Correct: plural \\"float\\" agrees with \\"berries\\" and keeps the present tense.","Agreement error: \\"has\\" is singular, and the berries are plural."]],"english-0108":["The goal has two parts: the color and how tightly the berries are packed. Check each choice for both. \\"Crimson carpet ... packed so tightly that a gull could almost stand on it\\" does both. The \\"bright red lake\\" has color but no crowding. The \\"solid raft\\" has crowding but no color. The sneakiest choice is \\"a pond dotted with bright red berries\\": it has color and it mentions berries, but \\"dotted\\" means spread out, the opposite of packed together. When a goal has two parts, a choice that meets only one is wrong, however vivid it is.",["Correct: \\"crimson\\" gives the color, and \\"packed so tightly that a gull could almost stand on it\\" shows how crowded the berries are.","Half the goal: \\"bright red\\" gives the color, but a glowing lake says nothing about the berries being crowded together.","Color, but the opposite of crowded: \\"dotted\\" means scattered here and there.","Half the goal: it shows crowding vividly, but it never mentions the berries\' color."]],"english-0109":["Read the two sentences together. First: wet berries don\'t keep as well. Second: most wet-harvested berries become juice, sauce, or dried fruit. The second sentence is the result of the first, so you need a cause-and-effect transition: \\"For this reason.\\" \\"For example\\" would need an example to follow. \\"However\\" signals a contrast, and \\"Similarly\\" adds a parallel point, and neither fits.",["Wrong logic: the sentence isn\'t an example of berries not keeping well; it\'s a result of it.","Wrong logic: \\"however\\" signals a contrast, but the sentence follows from the one before it.","Wrong logic: \\"similarly\\" adds a matching point, but this sentence is a consequence.","Correct: berries from a flooded bog don\'t keep well, so they become juice, sauce, or dried fruit instead of being sold fresh."]],"english-0110":["This paragraph follows the berries step by step: booms sweep them to a corner, a pump lifts them into a truck, and the paragraph explains why most become juice and why fresh berries are picked dry. A sentence about sweetened juice breaks into that sequence with a fact about taste, so the answer is No. The \\"Yes\\" choices try to connect it to the paragraph, but the paragraph gives a different reason berries become juice (they don\'t keep well). The other \\"No\\" choice has a false reason: nothing about sweetening was said earlier.",["Misreading: the paragraph says the berries become juice because they don\'t keep well; their tartness isn\'t the reason.","Not what it does: the sentence is about how juice tastes, not about the next step in the harvest.","Right decision, wrong reason: nothing earlier in the paragraph mentions sweetening or tartness.","Correct: the paragraph follows the berries from the bog to the truck to the store; a sentence about the taste of juice breaks that sequence."]],"english-0111":["The sentence describes something Lindahl does every morning, and its second verb is \\"holds,\\" in the present tense. The rest of the paragraph stays in the present too (\\"softens,\\" \\"bends,\\" \\"kinks\\"). So the first verb should also be present: \\"lights.\\" \\"Lit,\\" \\"had lit,\\" and \\"was lighting\\" all jump into the past.",["Tense shift: \\"lit\\" is past, but the rest of the sentence (\\"holds\\") and the paragraph are in the present.","Correct: present \\"lights\\" matches \\"holds\\" and describes what she does every morning.","Tense shift: \\"had lit\\" points to a time before some other past event, but the paragraph is in the present.","Tense shift: \\"was lighting\\" is past, but she \\"holds\\" the tube in the present."]],"english-0112":["The essay uses clear, plain words (\\"softens,\\" \\"bends,\\" \\"kinks\\"). Three choices drop into slang: \\"a total no-go,\\" \\"big time,\\" \\"basically toast,\\" \\"totally stuck.\\" \\"Cools and hardens again\\" keeps the essay\'s voice and also says exactly what happens: the glass stiffens and can\'t be bent.",["Too slangy: \\"a total no-go\\" doesn\'t fit the essay\'s plain, informative voice.","Too slangy: \\"big time\\" and \\"basically toast\\" are casual talk.","Too casual: \\"totally stuck that way\\" sounds like chatting, and it is vaguer than the key.","Correct: plain, precise words that match the rest of the profile and say exactly what happens to the glass."]],"english-0113":["Two decisions. First, \\"a glassblower named Tomas Avila\\" is one unit: \\"named Tomas Avila\\" tells you which glassblower, so no commas break it up. Second, once a person is named, a \\"who\\" clause that follows just adds extra information, so it needs a comma before it. Only NO CHANGE gets both right. The other choices either split the name off with commas or drop the comma before \\"who.\\"",["Correct: \\"named Tomas Avila\\" is essential (no commas), and the comma before \\"who\\" sets off extra information about a specific, named person.","Comma error: a comma can\'t separate \\"named\\" from the name it introduces, and the pair of commas makes the name look like an aside.","Missing comma: Avila is one specific, named person, so the \\"who\\" clause adds extra information and needs a comma before it.","Mismatched commas: the comma after \\"glassblower\\" cuts the phrase in two, and the needed comma before \\"who\\" is missing."]],"english-0114":["Cover up \\"Lindahl believes\\" for a moment: \\"a diner owner ___ is still waiting for it.\\" The blank is the subject of \\"is,\\" so it takes the subject form, \\"who\\" (just as you\'d say \\"he is waiting,\\" not \\"him is waiting\\"). \\"Whom\\" sounds right because it comes right before \\"Lindahl believes,\\" and it looks like the object of \\"believes.\\" It isn\'t. What Lindahl believes is the whole idea that the owner is still waiting. \\"Which\\" is for things, and \\"whose\\" shows ownership, so neither works here.",["Case trap: \\"Lindahl believes\\" is an interruption; the pronoun is the subject of \\"is still waiting,\\" so it must be \\"who.\\"","Correct: take out \\"Lindahl believes\\" and you get \\"who is still waiting,\\" so the subject form is needed.","Wrong pronoun: \\"which\\" is for things, and a diner owner is a person.","Wrong pronoun: \\"whose\\" shows possession, but nothing here belongs to the owner; the pronoun needs to be the subject of \\"is.\\""]],"english-0115":["Read the rest of the paragraph first. Every sentence after the bracket is about color: neon glows red-orange, argon and mercury make blue, phosphor coatings make white, pink, or green. The best opening names that topic: the color depends on what\'s inside the tubes. The other choices are true, but they\'re about the shop\'s location, Avila\'s career, or bending glass, which aren\'t the subject of this paragraph.",["Off topic: where the shop is located has nothing to do with the paragraph, which is about how signs get their colors.","Correct: the rest of the paragraph explains how neon, argon with mercury, and phosphor coatings produce different colors.","Off topic: this points back to Avila\'s story in the previous paragraph, not to color.","Off topic: bending glass was the subject of an earlier paragraph; this one is about color."]],"english-0116":["A weekend is Saturday and Sunday. So \\"every weekend\\" already tells you the gates are locked on both days. The other choices all add words that repeat that idea. On redundancy questions, the shortest choice is often right, as long as it keeps the meaning, and here it does.",["Redundant: a weekend is Saturday and Sunday, so \\"on both Saturday and Sunday\\" repeats \\"every single weekend.\\"","Correct: \\"every weekend\\" already covers both days.","Redundant: \\"every Saturday and every Sunday\\" and \\"each weekend\\" say the same thing twice.","Redundant: \\"both days of it\\" repeats what \\"each weekend\\" already means."]],"english-0117":["The board is worried that the public would change (have an impact on) the track\'s surface. The verb for that is \\"affect.\\" \\"Effect\\" is usually a noun (\\"the effect of rain\\"); as a verb it means \\"to bring about,\\" which doesn\'t fit. After \\"would,\\" use the plain form of a verb: \\"would affect,\\" not \\"would affects.\\"",["Wrong word: as a verb, \\"effect\\" means \\"bring about,\\" so this would say the public would create the surface\'s condition.","Wrong word: \\"effects\\" is a noun (or the verb \\"bring about\\"), and \\"would\\" needs a plain verb.","Correct: \\"affect\\" is the verb meaning \\"have an impact on,\\" and its plain form follows \\"would.\\"","Wrong form: \\"would\\" is followed by a verb\'s plain form (\\"would affect\\"), not the -s form."]],"english-0118":["A colon is right when the words before it form a complete sentence and the words after it explain or list what was promised. \\"Other towns have handled the same worry with two rules\\" is complete, and the two rules follow, so the original is correct. The comma version reads smoothly but joins two complete sentences with just a comma, which is a comma splice. The last two choices use a colon in places it never goes: right after a verb like \\"are\\" and right after \\"such as.\\" \\"Such as\\" also changes the meaning, because these are the two rules, not examples of them.",["Correct: the colon follows a complete clause and introduces the two rules it promised.","Comma splice: \\"Other towns have handled the same worry with two rules\\" and \\"the track closes at dusk...\\" are both complete sentences.","Colon misuse: a colon can\'t come between a verb (\\"are\\") and what completes it.","Colon misuse: a colon can\'t follow \\"such as,\\" and \\"such as\\" wrongly suggests these are only examples of the rules."]],"english-0119":["The goal has two parts: different ages, and at the same time. Retirees are one age group. The cross-country team is together but all teenagers. The father and daughter are different ages, which makes that choice the trap, but one jogs at dawn and the other runs after school, so they aren\'t there at the same time. Only the grandmother and grandson meet both parts: two generations, and \\"while\\" puts them on the track together.",["Half the goal: retirees are one age group, and nothing shows anyone else there with them.","Half the goal: the runners are together, but they are all teenagers.","Tempting, but they\'re there at different times (dawn and after school), not at the same time.","Correct: a grandmother and her grandson are different ages, and \\"while\\" shows they use the track at the same time."]],"english-0120":["The whole essay argues one thing: the school board should open the track to the public. A conclusion that restates the argument has to say that, and only one choice does. The others are details about track surfaces, training partners, or when runners like to train. None of them states the essay\'s claim.",["Off topic: what tracks are made of has nothing to do with whether this one should be open.","Off topic: this is about how some people like to run, but it doesn\'t restate the essay\'s argument that the board should open it.","Correct: it restates the essay\'s main claim, that the board should open the track to the town.","Off topic: when runners like to train doesn\'t restate the argument for opening the track."]],"english-0121":["Find the subject by stripping away everything between it and the verb. \\"The string of letters and numbers that Ehman circled, 6EQUJ5, ___ how the signal\'s strength rose...\\" The core is \\"The string ... records.\\" \\"Of letters and numbers\\" is a prepositional phrase, \\"that Ehman circled\\" is a clause describing the string, and \\"6EQUJ5\\" renames it, so none of them can be the subject. The plural nouns right before the blank make \\"record\\" sound right, which is the trap. \\"Have recorded\\" is plural too, and \\"recording\\" leaves the sentence with no main verb.",["Correct: the subject is the singular \\"string,\\" so the verb is \\"records\\"; \\"that Ehman circled\\" and \\"6EQUJ5\\" sit between them.","Nearest-noun trap: \\"letters and numbers\\" is plural, but it only tells what the string is made of; the subject is \\"string.\\"","Plural verb with a tense change: the subject \\"string\\" is singular, so \\"have\\" doesn\'t agree.","Fragment: \\"recording\\" is not a main verb, so the sentence would have a subject and no verb."]],"english-0122":["Read both sentences around the transition. The paragraph first shows that the signal\'s timing matched a source far beyond Earth. The next sentence gives a different fact about the same signal: its frequency was near the natural frequency of hydrogen. That is a second piece of information on top of the first, so an adding transition fits: \\"In addition.\\" \\"As a result\\" is tempting because the sentence follows a conclusion, but the frequency didn\'t happen because of the drift. \\"Instead\\" and \\"Even so\\" both signal a turn the paragraph never makes.",["\\"Instead\\" signals that one thing replaces another, but the frequency doesn\'t replace the timing; both count as clues.","Correct: the frequency is a second, separate clue about the signal, added to the timing clue before it.","Cause trap: the signal\'s frequency was not caused by its 72-second drift through the beam; the two facts are independent.","Contrast trap: \\"Even so\\" means \\"despite that,\\" but the frequency doesn\'t go against the timing; it adds to the case."]],"english-0123":["The paragraph has one job: to show that searchers looked for the signal again and never found it, so no explanation can be confirmed. The fact about Big Ear\'s demolition is true and interesting, which is the bait, but it is about the telescope, not the search. It also doesn\'t explain anything: other telescopes did the later searching, so losing Big Ear didn\'t make the signal disappear. And there\'s no contradiction, since the weeks of repeat searches came two decades before 1998.",["Overreach: losing Big Ear doesn\'t stop anyone from studying that part of the sky; the paragraph says other telescopes searched it.","False cause: other, larger telescopes kept searching, so Big Ear\'s removal isn\'t why the signal never returned.","Correct: the paragraph is about repeated searches that never found the signal again; the telescope\'s fate is a side note.","No contradiction: the repeat searches happened in the weeks after 1977, long before the telescope came down in 1998."]],"english-0124":["Words like \\"the members believed\\" can hide what job a pronoun does. Take them out: \\"composers ___ would still move a listener.\\" Now it\'s clear the pronoun is the subject of \\"would still move,\\" and subjects take \\"who\\" (you\'d say \\"they would move,\\" not \\"them would move\\"). \\"Whom\\" sounds formal and correct, which is the trap: it would only be right if the pronoun were an object, as in \\"composers whom the members admired.\\" \\"Whose\\" shows ownership, and \\"which\\" is for things.",["Case trap: \\"the members believed\\" makes the pronoun look like the object of \\"believed,\\" but it is the subject of \\"would still move,\\" so it needs \\"who.\\"","Correct: lift out \\"the members believed\\" and you get \\"composers who would still move a listener\\"; the pronoun is a subject.","\\"Whose\\" shows ownership, but nothing belongs to the composers here; \\"whose would still move\\" has no noun for \\"whose\\" to modify.","\\"Which\\" refers to things, not people; composers are people."]],"english-0125":["Two decisions are hidden in this one. First, \\"astronomer Carl Sagan\\" is a title in front of a name, like \\"Senator Ruiz\\" or \\"author Toni Morrison\\"; no comma goes between them. (\\"The astronomer, Carl Sagan,\\" would need \\"the.\\") Second, \\"who had already helped design...\\" adds information about a person who is already named, so it is nonessential and needs a comma before it. Only NO CHANGE gets both right. The choice without any commas sounds smooth, which makes it the most tempting wrong answer.",["Correct: \\"astronomer\\" is a title used right before the name, so no comma separates them, and the comma before \\"who\\" sets off a clause that adds information about Sagan.","Splits a title from its name: with no \\"the\\" before \\"astronomer,\\" the title belongs to the name, so commas around \\"Carl Sagan\\" are wrong.","Missing comma: a clause that adds information about a named person must be set off with a comma; \\"Carl Sagan\\" is already identified.","Two errors: a comma wrongly splits the title from the name, and no comma sets off the \\"who\\" clause."]],"english-0126":["Ask what \\"who\\" refers to. It can\'t be \\"era\\" or \\"period\\" (\\"who\\" means people), and it isn\'t \\"one\\": the sentence picks Bazalgette out of a group, \\"the few engineers ... who were willing.\\" So \\"who\\" is plural and takes \\"were.\\" Flip the sentence to check: \\"Of the few Victorian engineers who were willing to design for a larger city, Bazalgette is one.\\" The trap is that every noun near the verb is singular (\\"one,\\" \\"era,\\" \\"period,\\" \\"growth\\"), and the appositive between them pushes \\"engineers\\" far from the verb. \\"Were being\\" has the right number, but the progressive doesn\'t fit a lasting quality like being willing. (Casual speech often uses \\"was\\" here, but standard written English, which the ACT tests, uses the plural.)",["Correct: \\"who\\" stands for \\"engineers\\" (the few who were willing), so it takes the plural, simple past \\"were.\\"","Agreement trap: the singular nouns nearby (\\"one,\\" \\"era,\\" \\"period,\\" \\"growth\\") pull toward \\"was,\\" but \\"who\\" refers to the plural \\"engineers.\\"","Singular, and the progressive \\"being\\" doesn\'t work with a state like \\"willing\\" here.","Plural, which is right, but the progressive \\"were being willing\\" describes a temporary act, not the lasting trait the sentence means."]],"english-0127":["Two punctuation decisions are packed into this span. First, the aside: \\"led by ... Sciences\\" interrupts \\"a Japanese team ... sent,\\" and it opens with a dash, so it has to close with a dash (Choices B and C break the pair). Second, the name inside it: in \\"the astrobiologist Akihiko Yamagishi,\\" the word \\"astrobiologist\\" describes thousands of people, and the name tells us which one. That makes the name essential, and essential names take no commas, as in \\"the poet Emily Dickinson.\\" Choice A adds commas that sound like natural pauses, which is the trap. Only Choice D gets both right.",["Commas around an essential name: \\"the astrobiologist\\" names a whole profession, so \\"Akihiko Yamagishi\\" is needed to say which one. Setting it off treats him as the only astrobiologist there is.","Mismatched pair: a comma opens the aside after \\"team\\" and a dash closes it.","The dash after \\"team\\" opens the aside and nothing closes it. The commas around \\"of Tokyo University...\\" make a second, smaller aside, so the reader never gets back to \\"sent.\\"","Correct: one dash pair wraps the whole aside, and the name inside it is essential (it tells which astrobiologist), so it takes no commas."]],"english-0128":["Every choice connects the experiment to panspermia, so the deciding question is which one reports the results accurately. The pellets were dried bacteria, not bacteria in rock, and they spent up to three years in orbit. They did not cross between planets. Choice C says only what that supports: for part of the journey, a crust of dead cells might do the job a rock was imagined to do. Choice A (NO CHANGE) stretches three years in orbit into \\"the whole trip.\\" Choice B puts a rock into an experiment that had none. Choice D turns \\"could\\" into \\"most likely did.\\"",["Overstates the results: the pellets spent up to three years outside the space station. That says nothing about a whole trip between planets, which would also mean being blasted off one world and falling onto another.","Misstates the experiment: no rock was involved. The shield was the bacteria\'s own dead outer cells.","Correct: it ties the actual finding (dead outer cells shielded living ones) to panspermia\'s picture of sheltering rocks, and claims only what three years in orbit can support.","Leaps from \\"could survive part of the trip\\" to \\"most likely already did\\"; the essay gives no evidence that any microbe has ever made the trip."]],"english-0129":["Three things hide the subject here. The sentence is inverted (\\"Beneath the new road runs one...\\"), so the subject comes after the verb. The subject is \\"one,\\" not the plural \\"sewers\\" inside \\"of the main intercepting sewers.\\" And \\"along with a tunnel\\" sounds like a second subject, but phrases like \\"along with,\\" \\"as well as\\" and \\"together with\\" don\'t make a subject plural the way \\"and\\" does. Put it in normal order: \\"One of the main intercepting sewers, along with a tunnel, runs beneath the new road.\\" \\"Ran\\" is singular but puts the sewer in the past, and the next sentence says it is still there.",["Correct: in this inverted sentence the subject comes after the verb, and it is \\"one\\" (of the sewers), which is singular. \\"Along with a tunnel\\" doesn\'t make it plural.","Compound-subject trap: \\"along with\\" adds information but doesn\'t join subjects the way \\"and\\" does, and the subject \\"one\\" is singular, not \\"sewers.\\"","Singular in form but past tense: the sewer is still under the road today, as the next sentence makes clear.","Plural, and the present perfect suggests something that ran for a while; the subject \\"one\\" is singular."]],"english-0130":["Some verbs come with their own fixed connecting words. \\"Consider\\" can be followed directly or by \\"to be\\": \\"they consider it one of the great works\\" or \\"consider it to be.\\" \\"Regard\\" works differently: you regard something as something. \\"Regard it to be\\" borrows the pattern of \\"consider,\\" and because the two verbs mean nearly the same thing, it sounds fine to a hurried ear. \\"Like\\" and \\"as being like\\" change the meaning from \\"is one of\\" to \\"resembles one of.\\"",["Idiom error: \\"consider\\" can take \\"to be\\" (\\"consider it to be\\"), but \\"regard\\" takes \\"as\\"; mixing the two verbs\' patterns is the trap.","Correct: the standard idiom is \\"regard X as Y.\\"","\\"Like\\" compares the system to something else; the sentence means the system is one of the great works, not that it resembles one.","Wordy and illogical: \\"as being like\\" turns a claim that the system is a great work into a claim that it resembles one."]],"english-0131":["Find the subject by flipping the sentence into normal order: \\"A small balance wheel and the coiled spring that controls its swing sit inside its case, behind a white dial.\\" The subject is two things joined by \\"and,\\" so the verb must be plural: \\"sit.\\" Two things hide this. First, the verb comes before the subject, so your ear checks it against the words in front of it (\\"case,\\" \\"dial,\\" \\"scrollwork\\"), which are all singular. Second, the part of the subject you reach first, \\"a small balance wheel,\\" is singular too. \\"Is sitting\\" has the same singular problem, and \\"sitting\\" leaves the sentence without a main verb.",["Agreement trap: the verb comes before its subject here. The subject is \\"a small balance wheel and the coiled spring,\\" two things joined by \\"and,\\" so it needs the plural \\"sit.\\"","\\"Sitting\\" is not a main verb, so the sentence has no verb at all and becomes a fragment.","Correct: the sentence is inverted, and its subject (\\"a small balance wheel and the coiled spring\\") is compound, so the verb is plural.","Still singular. Adding \\"is\\" changes the tense but keeps the agreement error with the compound subject."]],"english-0132":["When a subject is built with \\"neither ... nor\\" (or \\"either ... or\\"), the verb agrees with the part closest to it. Here that part is \\"Lamarr herself,\\" which is singular, so the verb is \\"was.\\" The trap is that the sentence opens with a plural (\\"the Navy\'s engineers\\") and then two long clauses, so by the time you reach the verb it feels like a group of people. Counting the people (\\"more than one person, so \'were\'\\") is the mistake the item tests. \\"Have been\\" repeats that plural error and changes the tense; \\"being\\" leaves the sentence without a verb.",["Agreement trap: with \\"neither ... nor,\\" the verb agrees with the nearer subject, \\"Lamarr herself,\\" which is singular. The plural \\"engineers\\" is farther away.","Correct: in a \\"neither ... nor\\" subject, the verb matches the part closest to it, and \\"Lamarr herself\\" is singular.","Plural, so it repeats the agreement error, and it also shifts out of the past tense the paragraph uses.","\\"Being\\" isn\'t a main verb, so the sentence becomes a fragment."]],"english-0133":["Many students learn a rule from sentences like \\"She is one of the novelists who were still publishing.\\" There, \\"who\\" refers to the plural \\"novelists,\\" so the verb is plural. Adding \\"the only\\" flips it. Lund isn\'t one member of a group of novelists who were still publishing; she is the only one who was. So \\"who\\" refers to \\"the only one,\\" and the verb is singular. Reorder the sentence to check: \\"Of the novelists, Lund was the only one who was still publishing.\\" The trap is the plural noun \\"novelists\\" sitting right before \\"who.\\" \\"Have been\\" and \\"are\\" are plural and also break the past tense of a sentence set in 1979.",["Agreement trap: in \\"one of the novelists who were,\\" \\"who\\" would mean the novelists, but \\"the only one\\" changes that. Here \\"who\\" means the one person, Lund, so the verb is singular.","Plural, and the wrong tense: the sentence describes 1979, so it needs the simple past, and \\"who\\" refers to one person.","Correct: \\"who\\" refers to \\"the only one\\" (Lund), so it takes the singular \\"was.\\"","Plural and present tense, but the sentence is set in 1979 and \\"who\\" refers to one person."]],"english-0134":["The usual test for commas around a name is to remove it and see whether the sentence still works. \\"Her sister was the model for the ferry pilot\\" is still a sentence, so it\'s tempting to add commas. But that test only checks the grammar, not the meaning. The paragraph says Lund had three sisters (\\"The other two sisters\\" confirms it), so \\"her sister\\" alone doesn\'t tell you which one. The name \\"Ines\\" is essential, and essential information takes no commas. Compare \\"her husband, Tom,\\" which takes commas because a person has only one husband. Choices B and C each use a single comma, which leaves an aside unclosed or splits the subject from its verb.",["Commas mark a name as extra information, which works only if Lund had one sister. She had three, so the name is needed to tell which sister.","One comma opens an aside that never closes. And the name shouldn\'t be set off at all, since Lund had three sisters.","A comma between the subject (\\"sister Ines\\") and its verb (\\"was\\") breaks the sentence.","Correct: Lund had three sisters, so \\"Ines\\" tells which one. A name that identifies which person is meant is essential and takes no commas."]],"english-0135":["Two different verbs are hiding here. \\"Lay\\" means to put something down, and it takes an object: lay, laid (\\"She laid it in a chest\\"). \\"Lie\\" means to rest somewhere, with no object: lie, lay (\\"It lay there for years\\"). The sentence has one of each. Lund put the manuscript away, so she \\"laid it\\" in the chest; then the manuscript rested there, so \\"it lay.\\" The confusing part is that \\"lay\\" belongs to both verbs: it\'s the present of the putting verb and the past of the resting verb. So the right answer uses \\"laid\\" and \\"lay\\" side by side, which looks wrong to anyone who half-remembers the rule. The original uses \\"laid\\" twice, which is what most people say.",["The first verb is right (she put the manuscript down, so \\"laid\\"), but the second is wrong: the manuscript was resting, which is \\"lie,\\" and its past is \\"lay.\\"","The second verb is right, but the first is wrong: Lund put the manuscript somewhere, which is \\"lay,\\" and its past is \\"laid,\\" not \\"lay.\\"","Both verbs are swapped: \\"lay\\" can\'t be the past of putting something down, and \\"laid\\" can\'t mean resting.","Correct: Lund laid (put) the manuscript in the chest, where it lay (rested). \\"Laid\\" is the past of \\"lay\\"; \\"lay\\" is the past of \\"lie.\\""]],"english-0136":["Test the pronoun by turning the clause into a sentence with \\"she\\" or \\"her\\": \\"Most readers believe her to be Lund herself.\\" It has to be \\"her\\" (no one says \\"believe she to be\\"), and \\"her\\" means \\"whom.\\" This is the reverse of a well-known trap. In \\"a mapmaker who readers believe is Lund,\\" the words \\"readers believe\\" are an interruption, and \\"who\\" is the subject of \\"is,\\" so \\"who\\" is right. Here the verb after \\"believe\\" is \\"to be,\\" not \\"is,\\" and the person in front of \\"to be\\" takes the object form, as in \\"believe her to be.\\" \\"Whose\\" shows ownership, and \\"which\\" is for things.",["Correct: \\"whom\\" is the object of \\"believe\\": readers believe her to be Lund. The person in front of \\"to be\\" takes the object form.","Case trap: \\"who\\" would be right in \\"a mapmaker who readers believe is Lund,\\" where it is the subject of \\"is.\\" Here there is no \\"is\\"; with \\"to be,\\" the pronoun is the object of \\"believe.\\"","\\"Whose\\" shows ownership, but nothing belongs to the mapmaker here.","\\"Which\\" refers to things, and the mapmaker is a person."]],"english-0137":["Usually, nouns joined by \\"and\\" take a plural verb: \\"A letter, a draft, and a notebook have been scanned.\\" But \\"every\\" (like \\"each\\") in front of the list changes that. It makes you think of the items one at a time: every letter has been scanned, every draft has been scanned, every notebook has been scanned. So the verb is singular, \\"has been.\\" The trap is the \\"and\\" rule most students know well. Don\'t be thrown by the plural \\"originals are\\" later in the sentence; that clause has its own plural subject.",["Agreement trap: \\"letter, draft, and notebook\\" looks like a plural subject, but \\"every\\" in front of the list makes you count the items one at a time, so the verb is singular.","Correct: when \\"every\\" comes before a compound subject joined by \\"and,\\" the verb is singular: \\"Every letter, draft, and notebook has been scanned.\\"","Plural: \\"every\\" makes the subject singular even though three nouns are joined by \\"and.\\"","Plural, for the same reason: \\"every\\" in front of the list calls for a singular verb."]],"english-0138":["Most students learn that \\"each\\" is singular: \\"Each founder keeps a notebook.\\" That\'s true when \\"each\\" is the subject or comes before the noun. Here, though, the subject is \\"Its two founders,\\" and \\"each\\" comes after it, as in \\"They each keep a notebook.\\" In that position \\"each\\" doesn\'t change the verb, so the plural subject takes the plural verb \\"keep.\\" To check, take out the description and \\"each\\": \\"Its two founders keep a notebook.\\" The traps are \\"each\\" itself and the singular nouns in the description (\\"a retired science teacher and her former student\\") right before the verb. \\"Is keeping\\" and \\"has kept\\" are singular too, and they also change the tense.",["Agreement trap: \\"each\\" and the singular nouns nearby (\\"teacher,\\" \\"student\\") pull toward a singular verb, but the subject is \\"founders.\\" When \\"each\\" follows a plural subject, the verb stays plural.","Correct: the subject is the plural \\"founders\\"; \\"each\\" comes after it, so it doesn\'t change the verb: \\"The founders each keep.\\"","Singular, and the progressive \\"is keeping\\" describes one ongoing act rather than a regular habit.","Singular, and \\"has kept\\" breaks the present tense the paragraph uses for the founders\' routine."]],"english-0139":["Two things push toward \\"whomever\\" here: it comes right after the preposition \\"to,\\" and \\"they think\\" follows it. But a pronoun\'s form depends on its job inside its own clause. Take out \\"they think\\": \\"whoever will return the most seeds.\\" The pronoun is the subject of \\"will return,\\" so it must be \\"whoever\\" (she will return, not her will return). The preposition \\"to\\" takes the whole clause as its object, not just the pronoun. \\"Whom\\" and \\"those whom\\" make the same mistake about the pronoun\'s job.",["Double trap: \\"to\\" and \\"they think\\" both make the pronoun look like an object, but it is the subject of \\"will return,\\" so it needs the subject form.","\\"Whom\\" is the object form, but the pronoun is the subject of \\"will return.\\"","Correct: the whole clause \\"whoever ... will return the most seeds\\" is the object of \\"to,\\" and inside that clause the pronoun is the subject of \\"will return.\\"","Adding \\"those\\" fixes nothing: \\"whom\\" is still the object form where the subject of \\"will return\\" is needed."]],"english-0140":["In this sentence the verb comes before the subject. To check agreement, put the sentence back in normal order: \\"A compact biology laboratory and an instrument that could identify carbon-containing compounds ... were tucked inside the body of each lander.\\" The subject is two nouns joined by \\"and,\\" so the verb must be plural: \\"were.\\" The trap is that the nouns just before the verb, \\"the body of each lander,\\" are singular, and \\"each\\" makes everything feel singular. But \\"each lander\\" is inside a prepositional phrase, and nouns in prepositional phrases are never the subject. \\"Sits\\" is singular and in the wrong tense, and \\"was set\\" keeps the singular \\"was.\\"",["Inverted-order trap: the verb comes before its subject, and \\"the body of each lander\\" sounds like the subject, but the real subject is \\"a compact biology laboratory and an instrument,\\" which is plural.","Correct: flip the sentence around: \\"a compact biology laboratory and an instrument ... were tucked inside.\\" Two things joined by \\"and\\" take a plural verb.","\\"Sits\\" is singular, like the original, and it also shifts the paragraph from past tense into present tense.","\\"Was set\\" changes the wording but keeps the singular \\"was,\\" so it repeats the original error with a plural subject."]],"english-0141":["An opening phrase like \\"Heated inside Viking\'s soil analyzer\\" describes the subject of the main clause, so ask: what was heated? The perchlorate. In the original, \\"researchers suggested\\" is an aside set off by commas. Lift it out and the sentence reads \\"Heated inside Viking\'s soil analyzer, perchlorate would have broken down any carbon compounds.\\" The subject is \\"perchlorate,\\" so the sentence is correct. The trap is that \\"researchers\\" comes right after the comma and looks like the subject, so many readers \\"fix\\" a sentence that isn\'t broken. The other choices really do make the error: they turn \\"researchers,\\" \\"perchlorate\'s breakdown,\\" or \\"the suggestion\\" into the subject, and none of those was heated.",["Correct: \\"researchers suggested\\" is set off by commas as an aside, so the subject of the main clause is \\"perchlorate,\\" and the opening phrase \\"Heated inside Viking\'s soil analyzer\\" correctly describes the perchlorate.","Dangling modifier: now \\"researchers\\" is the subject right after the opening phrase, so the sentence says the researchers were heated inside the soil analyzer.","Dangling modifier: the subject is now \\"perchlorate\'s breakdown,\\" but it was the perchlorate, not its breakdown, that was heated in the analyzer.","Dangling modifier: the subject is now \\"the suggestion,\\" so the sentence says a suggestion was heated inside the analyzer; it is also wordy."]],"english-0142":["The verb has to agree with its subject, and the subject here is \\"the smell,\\" not \\"peaches.\\" \\"Of the ripe peaches\\" is just a phrase describing the smell, so the verb must be singular: \\"was.\\" \\"Were\\" and \\"have been\\" agree with the nearby word \\"peaches,\\" which is the trap. \\"Are\\" is plural too, and it also switches the past-tense story into the present.",["Agreement error: the subject is \\"smell\\" (singular), not \\"peaches.\\"","Two errors: \\"are\\" is plural, and it shifts the past-tense story into the present.","Correct: the singular subject \\"smell\\" takes the singular verb \\"was,\\" and past tense fits the story.","Agreement error: \\"have been\\" is plural, and \\"smell\\" is singular."]],"english-0143":["The abbreviation \\"a.m.\\" means \\"before noon,\\" so it already tells you it is morning. Adding \\"in the morning\\" or \\"early in the morning\\" says the same thing twice. The last choice is even wordier. \\"Six a.m.\\" is the shortest choice, and it keeps all the meaning.",["Redundant: \\"a.m.\\" already means \\"in the morning.\\"","Correct: \\"six a.m.\\" gives the time once, with nothing repeated.","Redundant: \\"early in the morning\\" repeats what \\"a.m.\\" already says.","Redundant: \\"a.m.,\\" \\"morning,\\" and \\"hours\\" all pile up the same idea."]],"english-0144":["Read the sentence before and after the bracket. First, they sold every peach. Next, they packed up and went home early. The second event happens because of the first, so \\"As a result\\" is the logical link. \\"For example\\" would need an example to follow, \\"In contrast\\" would need an opposite idea, and \\"Similarly\\" would need a matching idea. None of those fit.",["Wrong logic: packing up early is not an example of selling every peach.","Wrong logic: going home early doesn\'t contrast with selling out; it follows from it.","Wrong logic: packing up isn\'t similar to selling out; it happens because of it.","Correct: they went home early because everything was sold, so a cause-and-effect transition fits."]],"english-0145":["The water pressure belongs to the fountain, so you need a possessive pronoun. The possessive of \\"it\\" is \\"its,\\" with no apostrophe. \\"It\'s\\" is a contraction of \\"it is,\\" which makes no sense here. \\"Its\'\\" is not a real word. \\"Their\\" is possessive, but it is plural, and the essay is talking about one fountain.",["\\"It\'s\\" means \\"it is\\"; \\"it is water pressure\\" makes no sense here.","Correct: \\"its\\" is the possessive form; the water pressure belongs to the fountain.","\\"Its\'\\" is never correct; the possessive of \\"it\\" has no apostrophe.","Number error: \\"their\\" is plural, but it refers to one fountain."]],"english-0146":["\\"Reusable\\" describes \\"bottles,\\" and a single describing word is never split from its noun with a comma. The phrase \\"to class\\" is part of the same idea (where the students carry the bottles), so no comma belongs after \\"bottles\\" either. The version with no commas is correct.",["Unneeded comma: nothing should separate \\"reusable\\" from the noun it describes.","Two unneeded commas: one splits \\"reusable\\" from \\"bottles,\\" and the other cuts off the phrase \\"to class.\\"","Unneeded comma: it separates the object \\"bottles\\" from \\"to class,\\" which tells where the bottles are carried.","Correct: \\"reusable bottles to class\\" needs no commas at all."]],"english-0147":["The essay is a serious argument aimed at a school board, and it uses clear, standard words (\\"tight budget,\\" \\"approve the plan\\"). The original sentence keeps that voice. The other three choices drop into slang (\\"no-brainer,\\" \\"super lame,\\" \\"hands down\\") that doesn\'t belong in a formal proposal.",["Correct: the plain, serious wording matches an argument written to a school board.","Too slangy: \\"for real\\" and \\"total no-brainer\\" sound like a text message.","Too slangy: \\"super lame\\" doesn\'t fit a serious proposal.","Too casual: \\"awesome\\" and \\"hands down\\" clash with the essay\'s formal voice."]],"english-0148":["The workbench belongs to Ruth, so you need the possessive form. For one person, add an apostrophe and s: \\"Ruth\'s.\\" \\"Ruths\\" has no apostrophe, so it doesn\'t show ownership. \\"Ruths\'\\" puts the apostrophe after the s, which you would only do for more than one Ruth. \\"Ruth is\\" turns the word into a subject and verb, which breaks the sentence.",["Missing apostrophe: without it, \\"Ruths\\" looks like a plural, not a word that shows ownership.","Correct: \\"Ruth\'s\\" shows that the workbench belongs to one person, Ruth.","Misplaced apostrophe: \\"Ruths\'\\" would be the possessive of several people named Ruth.","\\"Ruth is workbench\\" makes no sense; the sentence needs a possessive, not a verb."]],"english-0149":["The sentence lists three things Ruth does each morning: she \\"unlocks,\\" \\"sets out,\\" and ___. The third verb should match the first two, so it needs the present tense: \\"winds.\\" \\"Winded\\" is the wrong tense (and the past of \\"wind\\" is \\"wound\\" anyway). \\"Winding\\" breaks the pattern, and \\"will have wound\\" jumps to the future.",["Two errors: this sentence is in the present tense, and the past tense of \\"wind\\" is \\"wound,\\" not \\"winded.\\"","Not parallel: \\"winding\\" doesn\'t match the other verbs in the series, \\"unlocks\\" and \\"sets.\\"","Wrong tense: \\"will have wound\\" points to the future, but the routine is described in the present.","Correct: \\"winds\\" matches \\"unlocks\\" and \\"sets,\\" all present tense."]],"english-0150":["\\"Many of the clocks... are more than a hundred years old\\" is a complete sentence, and so is \\"they still keep good time.\\" Two complete sentences can\'t be joined by a comma alone (a comma splice) or by nothing at all (a run-on). A comma plus a joining word like \\"but\\" fixes it, and \\"but\\" also fits the meaning: the clocks are old, yet they still work. In the last choice the comma is on the wrong side of \\"but.\\"",["Comma splice: a comma alone can\'t join two complete sentences.","Run-on: two complete sentences run together with no punctuation or joining word.","Correct: a comma plus \\"but\\" properly joins the two sentences and shows the contrast between \\"old\\" and \\"still keep good time.\\"","Misplaced comma: the comma belongs before \\"but,\\" not after it."]],"english-0151":["When two nouns are joined by \\"and\\" (\\"the yeast and bacteria\\"), the subject is plural, so the verb must be plural: \\"feed.\\" The other three choices are all singular forms, which might sound right if you look only at the nearby word \\"jar.\\" \\"Feed\\" also matches the present tense of \\"give off\\" later in the sentence.",["Correct: \\"the yeast and bacteria\\" is a plural subject joined by \\"and,\\" so it takes the plural verb \\"feed.\\"","Agreement error: \\"feeds\\" is singular, but \\"the yeast and bacteria\\" is plural.","Agreement error: \\"is feeding\\" uses the singular helping verb \\"is,\\" but \\"the yeast and bacteria\\" is plural.","Agreement error: \\"has fed\\" uses the singular helping verb \\"has\\"; the plural form would be \\"have fed.\\""]],"english-0152":["The sentence before says the starter looked bad: flat, gray, and covered in liquid. The bracketed sentence says it was not ruined after all. That is a contrast, so a contrast word like \\"Still\\" fits. \\"Therefore\\" claims the bad look caused it to be fine, which makes no sense. \\"For example\\" and \\"Likewise\\" would need an example or a similar idea to follow.",["Correct: the starter looked ruined, yet it wasn\'t; \\"Still\\" signals that contrast.","Wrong logic: looking flat and gray is not a reason the starter was fine.","Wrong logic: \\"it was not ruined\\" isn\'t an example of the starter looking flat and gray.","Wrong logic: \\"Likewise\\" adds a similar idea, but this sentence says the opposite of what came before."]],"english-0153":["\\"Bubbly again\\" already tells you the starter is back to how it was. The other choices repeat that idea: \\"once again\\" plus \\"as it had been before,\\" \\"again\\" plus \\"once more,\\" and \\"again\\" plus \\"for a second time.\\" The shortest choice keeps all the meaning.",["Redundant: \\"once again\\" and \\"as bubbly as it had been before\\" say the same thing twice.","Redundant: \\"bubbly again\\" and \\"once more bubbly\\" repeat each other.","Correct: \\"bubbly again\\" says it once, with no repeated idea.","Redundant: \\"again\\" and \\"for a second time\\" mean the same thing."]],"english-0300":["Find the aside first. It starts with the dash after \\"resulted\\" and runs through \\"toward the horizon.\\" Take it out and the main sentence is \\"The lens that resulted was far thinner and lighter...\\" An aside that opens with a dash must close with a dash, so \\"—was\\" is right. The trap is the comma after \\"prisms\\": it is part of the aside (it sets off \\"each one angled...\\"), and it makes a closing comma look natural. But a dash and a comma never make a pair. Leaving out the mark leaves the aside open, and a semicolon splits the subject from its verb.",["Mismatched pair: the aside opens with a dash, so it must close with a dash. The comma after \\"prisms\\" belongs inside the aside and doesn\'t close it.","Correct: the dash closes the aside that the dash after \\"resulted\\" opened, so the main sentence reads \\"The lens that resulted was far thinner and lighter.\\"","Leaves the aside open: nothing marks where it ends, so \\"the horizon was far thinner\\" seems to be its own statement.","A semicolon can\'t close an aside, and it cuts the subject \\"The lens\\" off from its verb \\"was.\\""]],"english-0301":["An opening phrase like \\"Built from hundreds of separate prisms\\" describes whatever comes right after the comma. The thing built from prisms is the lens, so the lens must be the subject, and the original does that. The workers version and the -ing version put other subjects there, so the phrase seems to describe workers or an action. The subtle trap is \\"the lens\'s pieces\\": \\"lens\\" appears first, but it is only a possessive, and the real subject is \\"pieces.\\" That makes the sentence say the pieces were each built from hundreds of prisms.",["Correct: the opening phrase \\"Built from hundreds of separate prisms\\" describes the lens, and \\"the lens\\" comes right after it as the subject.","Possessive trap: the subject is \\"pieces,\\" not \\"lens,\\" so the sentence says the pieces were built from hundreds of prisms. A possessive like \\"the lens\'s\\" can\'t be what an opening phrase describes.","Dangling modifier: it says the workers were built from hundreds of separate prisms.","Dangling modifier: the subject is the act of taking the lens apart, which wasn\'t built from prisms."]],"english-0302":["To decide, find the claim the new sentence would support. The paragraph says that cleaning the lens \\"took the most care\\" of all the keeper\'s tasks, then lists what the keeper did. A detail about wearing linen aprons so that buttons wouldn\'t scratch the glass is a concrete example of that care, so it belongs. The first \\"Yes\\" reason is wrong: the aprons have nothing to do with why soot built up. The \\"No\\" choices misjudge it: clothing is mentioned only to show how the glass was protected, and the sentence describes keepers avoiding damage, not causing it.",["Right decision, wrong reason: the aprons protected the glass from scratches. They don\'t explain why soot had to be wiped off.","Correct: the paragraph says cleaning the lens \\"took the most care,\\" and the aprons are a specific example of that care.","The aprons matter only because of the lens: they show how carefully the glass was treated, which is the paragraph\'s point.","Misreads the sentence: it describes a way keepers avoided scratching the glass, not damage they caused."]],"english-0303":["Two choices pad the idea: \\"make estimates, approximate ones\\" and \\"estimate and guess at\\" both say the same thing twice. The shortest choice, \\"estimate the floor\'s shape,\\" looks best, but read the next sentence: \\"Those gaps were often dozens of miles wide.\\" \\"Those gaps\\" needs something to point back to, and the shortest choice cuts it out. So the best choice is the one that is concise but keeps the needed words: \\"estimate the shape of the floor in the gaps between those lines.\\" The most concise choice is only right if it keeps the meaning the paragraph needs.",["Redundant: an estimate is already approximate, so \\"approximate ones\\" repeats the idea.","Too short: it drops \\"the gaps between those lines,\\" so \\"Those gaps\\" in the next sentence no longer refers to anything.","Correct: it says once that she estimated the floor\'s shape and keeps \\"the gaps,\\" which the next sentence refers back to.","Redundant: \\"estimate\\" and \\"guess at\\" mean nearly the same thing here."]],"english-0304":["This goal has two parts, and the right choice must do both: show the notch in separate measurements (repetition) and show that it followed one path (a line). \\"In all six profiles, a notch cut into the crest\\" shows repetition but no path. \\"The notch she had found ran in a nearly straight line\\" shows a path but treats it as one find. The original shows only a single dramatic profile. Only the last choice does both: the notch shows up in all six, and on the map the notches line up.",["Shows neither part: it describes one striking profile, not a pattern across six, and says nothing about a path.","Meets only half the goal: it shows the notch in every profile but not that the notches lined up along one path.","Meets only half the goal: it shows a path, but it speaks of a single notch she had found, not one repeated in separate profiles.","Correct: \\"In all six profiles\\" shows the repetition, and \\"fell along one continuous line\\" shows the single path."]],"english-0305":["The clause \\"the two of them were publishing maps of the ocean floor that showed three features few people had pictured before\\" is complete, and what follows names those three features. A colon is the mark that introduces a list or explanation after a complete clause, so \\": a\\" is right. \\", these were a\\" adds a subject and verb, which makes a second complete sentence held on by only a comma (a comma splice). A semicolon needs a complete clause after it, and the list isn\'t one. With no mark at all, \\"before\\" seems to take \\"a long central ridge\\" as its object, which garbles the meaning.",["Comma splice: \\"these were a long central ridge...\\" is a complete sentence joined to another complete sentence by only a comma.","With no punctuation, \\"before a long central ridge\\" reads as if \\"before\\" were a preposition meaning \\"in front of the ridge.\\"","A semicolon must join two complete clauses, but \\"a long central ridge, a valley..., and fracture zones\\" is only a list.","Correct: the colon follows a complete clause and introduces the list that names the three features."]],"english-0170":["The goal has two parts: slow loss and sudden loss. The original names one of each: sudden (\\"to a war or a flood\\") and slow (\\"as a freezer breaks down and no one has the money to fix it\\"), and the next sentence, \\"The vault guards against both,\\" depends on that pair. Choice B gives two sudden disasters. Choice C is the sneaky one: it mentions a freezer, but one that fails \\"one night,\\" so it is sudden too. Choice D is only the slow half. Check every part of the goal, not just whether a choice sounds like the key.",["Correct: \\"suddenly\\" (a war or a flood) and \\"slowly\\" (a freezer no one can afford to fix) gives both kinds of loss, which the next sentence calls \\"both.\\"","Only sudden: a war and a flood are two disasters, but both happen fast, so nothing here is slow.","Only sudden: the freezer looks like the slow example, but it \\"stops working one night,\\" which is as sudden as the fire.","Only slow: this is a vivid gradual loss, but it leaves out sudden loss, so \\"both\\" in the next sentence has nothing to point to."]],"english-0171":["The new sentence begins \\"At that temperature,\\" so it must come right after a sentence that names a specific temperature. Only one sentence does: \\"Its seed rooms are kept at about −18°C.\\" That puts it at Point C. Point D is the trap: the sentence before it is also about cold, but \\"below freezing\\" describes what would happen if the power failed, not the temperature the seeds are stored at, and the new sentence would then end the paragraph on a detail about normal storage after the emergency. At Points A and B, no temperature has been mentioned yet.",["No temperature has been named yet, so \\"that temperature\\" has nothing to refer to, and it would split \\"both\\" from the two kinds of loss it points back to.","Too early: \\"that temperature\\" would come before −18°C is mentioned, so it has nothing to refer to.","Correct: \\"that temperature\\" points straight back to \\"about −18°C\\" in the sentence before it.","Tempting because the sentence before also talks about cold, but \\"below freezing\\" is a backup condition during a power failure, not the temperature the rooms are kept at."]],"english-0172":["The shortest choice isn\'t always right: a concise choice still has to keep every idea the passage needs. The next sentence says ICARDA had to \\"replace the collection it could no longer reach,\\" which only makes sense if we\'ve been told the war forced it to leave. Choice C cuts that, so it is too short. The original and choice D keep the idea but repeat themselves (Syria three times; \\"elsewhere, away from Syria\\"). Choice B says everything needed, once.",["Redundant: \\"in Syria,\\" \\"the Syrian war,\\" and \\"out of Syria\\" name the country three times, and \\"the genebank\'s work\\" repeats \\"genebank.\\"","Correct: it names the place once and keeps the war, which explains why ICARDA \\"could no longer reach\\" its collection in the next sentence.","Too short: it drops the war, so the next sentence\'s \\"the collection it could no longer reach\\" no longer has a reason behind it.","Redundant: \\"elsewhere\\" already means away from where it was; \\"away from Syria\\" says it again."]],"english-0173":["Read the sentence after the bracketed one: \\"The backup had done its job, and then it was a backup again.\\" The first half refers to the withdrawal. The second half, \\"a backup again,\\" is true only because ICARDA refilled the vault, which is what the bracketed sentence reports. Delete it and the ending has nothing to stand on, so it should stay. The \\"focus\\" reason in B sounds like a standard ACT answer, but here the paragraph\'s own last sentence is about what came after. Choice A gives a false reason (the sentence doesn\'t explain the choice of countries), and C misreads whose seeds were sent.",["Wrong reason: the sentence comes after the planting and says nothing about why ICARDA chose those two countries. The war explains that.","Sounds like a focus argument, but the last sentence of the paragraph is about what happened after the withdrawal, and it depends on the deposit.","Misreads it: the sentence says ICARDA sent its own fresh seeds back, which fits the essay\'s point that each genebank keeps the rights to its own seeds.","Correct: \\"then it was a backup again\\" is true only because ICARDA sent new seeds back; cut the sentence and that claim comes out of nowhere."]],"english-0320":["Find the aside. It starts with the comma after \\"seeds\\" and runs through \\"moisture\\": \\"sealed in foil packets that keep out moisture.\\" Take it out and the main sentence is \\"Many kinds of seeds can stay alive for decades.\\" An aside that opens with a comma must close with a comma, so \\"moisture, can\\" is right. The trap is the word \\"that\\": students learn that a \\"that\\" clause takes no commas, so they leave the comma out. That rule is about the \\"that\\" clause itself. Here the comma isn\'t setting off the \\"that\\" clause; it is closing the bigger aside that contains it. A dash can\'t close a comma-opened aside, and \\"they\\" gives \\"can stay\\" a second subject.",["Leaves the aside open: the comma after \\"seeds\\" starts an aside, and nothing ends it, so the sentence seems to say that the moisture can stay alive.","Correct: the comma closes the aside that the comma after \\"seeds\\" opened, so the main sentence reads \\"Many kinds of seeds ... can stay alive for decades.\\"","Mismatched pair: the aside opens with a comma, so it has to close with a comma, not a dash.","Double subject: \\"Many kinds of seeds\\" is already the subject of \\"can stay,\\" so adding \\"they\\" gives the verb two subjects."]],"english-0321":["This sentence is flipped: the place comes first (\\"Along the metal shelves inside the vault\\"), then the verb, then the subject. Turn it around to check: \\"Hundreds of sealed boxes sit along the metal shelves.\\" The subject is plural, so the verb must be \\"sit.\\" Two things pull toward the singular. The noun right before the verb, \\"the vault,\\" is singular, and \\"each one holding seeds\\" after the subject sounds singular too. But neither is the subject. \\"Has sat\\" is also singular, and \\"sitting\\" leaves the sentence without a main verb.",["Agreement trap: the subject comes after the verb here. It is \\"hundreds of sealed boxes,\\" which is plural, so the verb can\'t be the singular \\"sits.\\"","Fragment: with \\"sitting,\\" the sentence has no main verb at all.","\\"Has sat\\" is singular too, and the subject, \\"hundreds of sealed boxes,\\" is plural.","Correct: the sentence is inverted, and its subject, \\"hundreds of sealed boxes,\\" is plural, so it takes the plural verb \\"sit.\\""]],"english-0322":["An opening phrase like \\"Unlike an ordinary gene bank\\" describes the subject that comes right after the comma, so that subject must be something you can sensibly compare with a gene bank. Only \\"the Svalbard vault\\" works: a bank is compared with a vault. Every other choice sounds smooth, which is why this is hard. \\"The seeds at Svalbard\\" and \\"at Svalbard, seeds\\" both make \\"seeds\\" the subject, comparing a bank with seeds. (In the last one, \\"at Svalbard\\" is just a place phrase, not the subject.) \\"Svalbard\'s staff\\" compares a bank with people. Ask what the opening phrase is about, then check that the subject matches it.",["Faulty comparison: the sentence compares \\"an ordinary gene bank\\" with \\"the seeds at Svalbard,\\" a bank with seeds.","Faulty comparison: this compares a gene bank with the vault\'s staff, people rather than another seed store.","Correct: the opening phrase \\"Unlike an ordinary gene bank\\" now describes the vault itself, so a gene bank is compared with a gene-bank-like store.","Still a faulty comparison: \\"at Svalbard\\" is only a place phrase, so the subject is \\"seeds,\\" and a gene bank is again compared with seeds."]],"english-0323":["\\"Though\\" and \\"however\\" mean almost the same thing, but they follow different punctuation rules. \\"Though\\" is a joining word (like \\"although\\"): it turns \\"it never reached the rooms\\" into a dependent clause, and a comma attaches that clause to the main sentence. So \\", though it\\" is right. A semicolon needs a complete sentence on each side, so it can\'t come before a \\"though\\" clause. And a \\"though\\" clause can\'t stand alone after a period. \\"However\\" is the reverse case: it isn\'t a joining word, so \\", however, it\\" makes a comma splice. The trap is using the \\"; however,\\" rule for \\"though.\\"",["A semicolon has to have a complete sentence on each side, but \\"though it never reached the rooms ...\\" is a dependent clause.","Comma splice: \\"however\\" is not a joining word, so this joins two complete sentences with only a comma.","Correct: \\"though\\" is a joining word that makes the second part a dependent clause, and a comma can attach that clause to the sentence.","Fragment: \\"Though it never reached the rooms where the seeds are kept\\" can\'t stand alone as a sentence."]],"english-0324":["Find the real subject: \\"The Norwegian government.\\" The phrase \\"along with the Crop Trust and the Nordic Genetic Resource Center\\" sits between commas and adds information, but it isn\'t part of the subject. Only \\"and\\" joins subjects into a plural one. Phrases like \\"along with,\\" \\"as well as,\\" and \\"together with\\" don\'t. Lift the phrase out: \\"The Norwegian government manages it.\\" So the singular \\"manages\\" is right. The plural verbs (\\"manage,\\" \\"have managed\\") fall for the three names. \\"Managing\\" leaves the clause without a main verb.",["Correct: the subject is \\"The Norwegian government,\\" which is singular; \\"along with\\" adds partners without making the subject plural.","Agreement trap: \\"along with ...\\" seems to make three subjects, but it isn\'t \\"and.\\" The subject is still the singular \\"government.\\"","Fragment problem: \\"managing\\" is not a main verb, so the first half of the sentence has no verb.","\\"Have\\" is plural, but the subject, \\"The Norwegian government,\\" is singular."]],"english-0325":["This list comes after a colon and has three items: the egg, the nymph, and the adult. Each item has its own comma-set description, so commas alone can\'t show where one item ends and the next begins. That is why the writer put a semicolon between the egg and the nymph. The same mark has to separate the nymph from the adult: \\"; and the adult.\\" With a comma, \\"the nymph, which lives underground and feeds on fluid from tree roots, and the adult\\" can be misread as a single item. Leaving the mark out makes it worse, and a second colon can\'t be used inside a list. The trap: the usual rule is a comma before \\"and\\" in a list, but a list whose items contain commas uses semicolons all the way through.",["Inconsistent: the first two items are separated by a semicolon because they contain commas, so the last two need one as well. With a comma, the \\"which\\" clause about the nymph seems to keep going.","With no mark at all, the nymph\'s \\"which\\" clause runs straight into the next item, so it seems to say that the nymph feeds on fluid from tree roots and the adult.","A colon introduces a list or an explanation; it can\'t separate items inside a list that already began after a colon.","Correct: because the items contain commas, a semicolon separates each item from the next, including before \\"and\\" at the end of the list."]],"english-0326":["Strip the sentence down to its core. The subject is \\"The appearance.\\" Then comes \\"of so many insects at the same time,\\" and then an aside between commas, \\"in some places more than a million on a single acre.\\" Only after all that does the verb come. Plural words like \\"insects\\" and \\"a million\\" sit right before it and pull you toward \\"are.\\" But the appearance is what\'s thought to be a defense, so the verb must be singular: \\"The appearance ... is thought to be their main defense.\\" \\"Have been\\" is plural, and \\"being\\" leaves the sentence without a main verb.",["Agreement trap: \\"insects\\" and \\"a million\\" come right before the verb, but the subject is \\"The appearance,\\" which is singular.","\\"Have\\" is plural, but the subject, \\"The appearance,\\" is singular.","Fragment problem: \\"being\\" is not a main verb, so the sentence before the colon has no verb.","Correct: the subject is the singular noun \\"appearance\\"; \\"of so many insects\\" and the aside after it come between the subject and the verb."]],"english-0327":["The sentence lists three things the nymph does, so all three should take the same form and share the subject \\"a nymph\\": it \\"climbs ..., splits ..., and pumps its new wings full of fluid.\\" The original switches to the passive (\\"its new wings are pumped\\"), which drops the nymph as the one doing the action. \\"Pumping\\" changes the verb form. \\"It pumps\\" adds a new subject, so the third item becomes a full clause in a series of plain verbs. Each wrong choice sounds fine when read alone, so test the whole series.",["Not parallel: the first two actions (\\"climbs,\\" \\"splits\\") have the nymph as their subject, and this one switches to a passive clause about the wings.","Correct: \\"climbs ..., splits ..., and pumps\\" gives three present-tense verbs that share the subject \\"a nymph.\\"","Not parallel: \\"pumping\\" is an -ing form, but the other two actions in the series are \\"climbs\\" and \\"splits.\\"","Not parallel: adding \\"it\\" makes the third item a full clause, while the first two are verbs that share the subject \\"a nymph.\\""]],"english-0328":["The clue is \\"by the time they emerge in 2041.\\" That is a future deadline, and the seventeen years will be over by then. Use the future perfect for that: \\"will have spent.\\" \\"Had spent\\" is the past perfect, which is for something finished before a past moment. \\"Have spent\\" says the time is already used up today, which isn\'t true until 2041. \\"Would spend\\" is the trap because it sounds like it\'s about the future, but it looks ahead from a past moment and clashes with \\"by the time they emerge.\\"",["Correct: the future perfect \\"will have spent\\" describes time that will be finished by a future moment, \\"by the time they emerge in 2041.\\"","The past perfect \\"had spent\\" describes time finished before a past moment, but 2041 is in the future.","\\"Have spent\\" says the seventeen years are already over now, but the nymphs won\'t finish them until 2041.","\\"Would spend\\" is used for a future seen from the past (\\"In 2024, scientists said they would spend...\\"), and it doesn\'t fit with \\"by the time they emerge,\\" which looks ahead from now."]],"english-0329":["An opening -ing phrase describes the subject that comes right after the comma. Who is \\"sipping watery fluid from tree roots\\"? The nymphs. So the subject must be \\"the nymphs\\": \\"the nymphs grow slowly.\\" The original is the classic trap: \\"the nymphs\'\\" seems to put the nymphs right there, but the possessive only describes the real subject, \\"growth,\\" and growth can\'t sip anything. \\"The growth of the nymphs\\" and \\"slow growth\\" make the same mistake. Ask what the subject of the sentence is, not just which noun comes first.",["Dangling modifier: the subject is \\"growth,\\" so the sentence says the growth is sipping fluid from tree roots. The possessive \\"nymphs\'\\" only describes the subject; it isn\'t the subject.","Correct: the subject, \\"the nymphs,\\" comes right after the opening phrase, and the nymphs are the ones doing the sipping.","Dangling modifier: the subject is \\"slow growth,\\" so the opening phrase says that growth is sipping fluid.","Dangling modifier: the subject is \\"the growth,\\" not the nymphs. Moving \\"of the nymphs\\" after it doesn\'t change that."]],"english-0330":["The subject of the sentence is \\"the students in our school\'s robotics club,\\" and the verb is \\"built.\\" A subject and its verb should not be split by a comma, even when the subject is long. Moving the comma after \\"built\\" just splits the verb from what was built. A semicolon is wrong too, because the part before it can\'t stand alone as a sentence.",["A comma here cuts the subject (\\"the students in our school\'s robotics club\\") off from its verb, \\"built.\\"","This comma splits the verb \\"built\\" from its object, \\"a small rover.\\"","Correct: no comma belongs between a subject and its verb.","A semicolon joins two complete sentences, and the words before it aren\'t one."]],"english-0331":["Find the subject by skipping the phrase \\"of photos\\": the box is still full of pictures. \\"Box\\" is singular, so it takes \\"is.\\" The trap is to match the verb to \\"photos,\\" the noun closest to it. \\"Were\\" has the same problem and also shifts to the past, even though the sentence says \\"Today.\\" \\"Being\\" leaves the sentence without a real verb.",["Correct: the subject is \\"box,\\" which is singular, so the verb is \\"is.\\"","This verb agrees with \\"photos,\\" but \\"photos\\" sits inside the phrase \\"of photos.\\" The subject is \\"box.\\"","\\"Were\\" is plural, and it is past tense, which clashes with \\"Today.\\"","\\"Being\\" can\'t be the main verb of a sentence, so the sentence would have no verb."]],"english-0332":["The word \\"Yesterday\\" and the later verb \\"carried\\" both say this happened in the past. So the verb should be the simple past, \\"found.\\" \\"Finds\\" is present and \\"will find\\" is future. \\"Has found\\" is a form used for an unfinished or unnamed time, so it sounds wrong next to a specific past time like \\"yesterday.\\"",["\\"Finds\\" is present tense, but \\"Yesterday\\" and \\"carried\\" place the action in the past.","\\"Will find\\" is future tense, which can\'t describe something that happened yesterday.","\\"Has found\\" doesn\'t go with a finished time like \\"yesterday\\"; the simple past does.","Correct: \\"found\\" is simple past, matching \\"Yesterday\\" and \\"carried.\\""]],"english-0333":["The reading room belongs to the library, so you need a possessive pronoun. For \\"it,\\" the possessive is \\"its,\\" with no apostrophe. \\"It\'s\\" always means \\"it is\\" or \\"it has.\\" \\"Their\\" is possessive but plural, and there is only one library. \\"Its\'\\" isn\'t a real word.",["\\"It\'s\\" means \\"it is\\": \\"it is reading room\\" makes no sense.","Correct: \\"its\\" is the possessive form; the reading room belongs to the library.","Number error: \\"their\\" is plural, but \\"library\\" is singular.","\\"Its\'\\" is not a word; the possessive of \\"it\\" has no apostrophe."]],"english-0334":["This sentence compares two dogs, so it needs \\"than\\": \\"bigger than mine.\\" \\"Then\\" sounds almost the same but tells when something happens. Adding a comma doesn\'t fix the word, and \\"bigger from\\" is not a standard way to compare.",["Sound-alike trap: \\"then\\" is a time word (\\"first this, then that\\"), not a comparison word.","Still the time word \\"then,\\" and the comma breaks up the comparison.","Correct: \\"than\\" is the word used to compare two things: bigger than mine.","\\"Bigger from\\" isn\'t how English makes a comparison; comparisons use \\"than.\\""]],"english-0335":["Read both sentences together. Mateo practiced every night, and then he felt calm. The calm is a result of the practice, so \\"As a result\\" is the logical link. \\"However\\" would mean the second sentence is surprising, and it isn\'t. \\"For example\\" and \\"Instead\\" don\'t describe how the two ideas connect.",["\\"However\\" signals a contrast, but feeling calm is what you\'d expect after a week of practice.","No example follows; the second sentence tells what happened next.","\\"Instead\\" means one thing replaced another, but nothing was replaced.","Correct: his calm came from all that practice, so a cause-and-effect transition fits."]],"english-0336":["\\"Our bus broke down on the way to school this morning\\" is a complete sentence, and so is \\"we had to walk the last mile.\\" Two complete sentences need more than a comma between them. A comma plus a joining word like \\"so\\" works, and \\"so\\" also shows that the breakdown caused the walk. With no punctuation at all, you get a run-on. Starting the second part with \\"Because\\" makes it a fragment and gets the cause backward.",["Comma splice: a comma alone can\'t join two complete sentences.","Correct: the comma plus \\"so\\" joins the two sentences and shows cause and effect.","Run-on: the two complete sentences are jammed together with no punctuation.","Fragment: \\"Because we had to walk the last mile\\" can\'t stand alone, and it reverses the cause."]],"english-0337":["The sentence lists three things he likes to do: bake bread, fix old radios, and plant tomatoes. Each item in a list should have the same form. \\"Bake\\" and \\"fix\\" are plain verbs, so the last item should be the plain verb \\"plant.\\" The other choices switch to an -ing form, a new clause, or a wordy verb phrase.",["Correct: \\"plant\\" matches \\"bake\\" and \\"fix,\\" so the three activities follow the same pattern.","\\"Planting\\" is an -ing form, which breaks the pattern set by \\"bake\\" and \\"fix.\\"","Adding \\"he\\" turns the last item into a full clause, unlike the other two.","\\"To be planting\\" doesn\'t match the simple verbs \\"bake\\" and \\"fix.\\""]],"english-0338":["The nurse is a person, so the clause should start with \\"who\\" or \\"whom,\\" not \\"which.\\" To choose between those two, ask who did the action: the nurse gave the shot. The nurse is the subject, so the answer is \\"who.\\" \\"Whom\\" is for objects (\\"the nurse whom I thanked\\"), and \\"whose\\" shows ownership.",["\\"Which\\" is used for things and animals, not for people like the nurse.","\\"Whom\\" is for objects, but here the nurse is the one doing the giving (the subject).","Correct: \\"who\\" refers to a person and acts as the subject of \\"gave.\\"","\\"Whose\\" shows ownership (\\"whose bag\\"), which doesn\'t fit here."]],"english-0339":["\\"Our science teacher\\" is extra information about Mr. Lin. The sentence still works without it: \\"Mr. Lin keeps a pet tortoise.\\" Extra information like this needs a comma on both sides. The first comma is already there after \\"Mr. Lin,\\" so the answer adds the closing comma after \\"teacher.\\" Splitting \\"science teacher\\" or using a semicolon breaks the sentence.",["The comma after \\"Mr. Lin\\" opens an interruption, but nothing closes it.","This comma splits \\"science teacher,\\" a single title.","A semicolon would need a complete sentence on each side.","Correct: the second comma closes the extra phrase \\"our science teacher.\\""]],"english-0340":["To return is to go back, so \\"return back\\" says the same idea twice. Just \\"return\\" keeps the full meaning. The other choices pile on more repeated words: \\"back again\\" and \\"go back and return.\\"",["Redundant: \\"return\\" already means \\"go back,\\" so \\"back\\" says it twice.","Correct: \\"return\\" says it once, with nothing repeated.","Even more redundant: \\"back\\" and \\"again\\" both repeat the meaning of \\"return.\\"","Redundant: \\"go back\\" and \\"return\\" mean the same thing."]],"english-0341":["Try the sentence without \\"Priya and\\": \\"Coach Rivera thanked me.\\" The pronoun is the object of \\"thanked,\\" so it takes the object form, \\"me.\\" Many students pick \\"I\\" because \\"Priya and I\\" sounds formal, but \\"I\\" is only for subjects. \\"Myself\\" needs \\"I\\" earlier in the sentence, and \\"mine\\" shows ownership.",["Correct: \\"me\\" is the object form; the coach thanked me.","\\"I\\" is a subject form, but here the pronoun receives the thanks. Drop \\"Priya and\\": \\"thanked I\\" sounds wrong.","\\"Myself\\" is only for when \\"I\\" is already the subject (\\"I hurt myself\\").","\\"Mine\\" shows ownership, which doesn\'t fit after \\"thanked.\\""]],"english-0342":["Read the sentence right after the bracketed one: rescuers \\"found themselves stuck.\\" Why? Because, as the bracketed sentence says, the cold air made the molasses \\"thicker and stickier.\\" Without it, the paragraph never explains why wading in was so hard. The speed and the injury count are in other sentences, and nothing in the bracketed sentence says the cold made the tank burst. It only describes what happened to the molasses afterward.",["Correct: the molasses thickening in the cold is what made the rescuers get stuck, as the next sentence says.","The speed is given in the paragraph\'s first sentence, not in the bracketed one.","The sentence is about what the cold did to the molasses after the tank burst, not why the tank failed.","The injury count is in the last sentence and would still be there."]],"english-0343":["The next sentence says the official \\"rejected that argument,\\" so the bomb story was only a claim the company made in court. \\"Argued\\" fits exactly. \\"Proved\\" and \\"discovered\\" both treat the bomb as true, which the rest of the paragraph contradicts. \\"Admitted\\" has the wrong shade: people admit things that count against them, but blaming a bomber was the company\'s defense.",["Correct: \\"argued\\" presents the bomb story as the company\'s claim, which the official later rejected.","If the company had proved it, the official could not have rejected it in the next sentence.","\\"Discovered\\" treats the bomb as a real fact, but the paragraph goes on to show it wasn\'t.","You admit something that hurts your own side. Blaming a bomber helped the company, so it wasn\'t an admission."]],"english-0344":["The new sentence talks about \\"the claim,\\" so it must come after a claim has been made. The bomb claim appears in the sentence \\"The company argued that someone had planted a bomb in it.\\" Point C sits right after that sentence, so the order works: the company blames a bomb, the bomb idea sounded believable at the time, and then the official rejects it. At Points A and B no claim exists yet. Point D is the trap: it is also after the claim, but it wedges the sentence between the official\'s rejection and \\"He concluded,\\" so \\"He\\" no longer follows the official.",["No claim has been made yet, so \\"the claim\\" has nothing to refer to.","This comes right after the lawsuit but before the company says anything, so \\"the claim\\" still has no claim to point to.","Correct: it follows the bomb argument directly, so \\"the claim\\" clearly means the bomb story, and it explains why people took it seriously before it was rejected.","This splits the official\'s rejection from \\"He concluded,\\" and the pronoun \\"He\\" would then follow a sentence with no person in it."]],"english-0345":["The stem asks for two things: the official\'s findings (an untrained man built a weak tank) and the change in the last paragraph (plans must be signed by an architect or engineer). The second choice covers both: the hearings showed how easily an unsafe tank could go up, so the city changed its rules. NO CHANGE is a fine general opener but skips the findings. The hearings choice looks only backward, and the industrial-alcohol fact connects to neither paragraph.",["Points ahead to the new rules but says nothing about what the hearings found.","Correct: it links the findings (an unsafe tank built by an untrained man) to the new rule (qualified people must sign off on plans).","True but off track: it adds a fact about molasses and connects to neither the findings nor the new rules.","Looks back at the hearings only; it never reaches the change in the city\'s rules."]],"english-0346":["Trace the essay: the tank bursts (first two paragraphs), the hearings find it was too weak and built by an untrained man (third), and Boston then requires signed calculations for large structures (fourth). That is a clear path from one disaster to a rule change, so the answer is Yes. The rescue detail is real but isn\'t the reason. The lawsuit paragraph is part of the chain, not a detour. And why the tank stood by the harbor has nothing to do with this purpose.",["Correct: the essay moves from the disaster to its cause to the rule it produced.","Right answer, wrong reason: the rescue gets one sentence and has nothing to do with the building rules.","The lawsuit fills one paragraph, and it matters because its findings lead to the new rule.","That detail isn\'t needed for the stated purpose, which is the link between the disaster and the rules."]],"english-0347":["The bracketed sentence does one job: it names the process just described. The water a body makes when it breaks down food is called \\"metabolic water.\\" Delete it and the paragraph still describes the process but loses the name, which the last paragraph uses again (\\"Many animals make metabolic water\\"). The comparison with other animals is in the next sentence. Nothing here says how the animal finds seeds, and the paragraph says the seeds are dry.",["That comparison is in the last sentence, which would still be there.","The paragraph calls the seeds dry; the water comes from breaking them down, not from water stored in them.","Finding seeds isn\'t mentioned in this paragraph.","Correct: the sentence gives the term \\"metabolic water\\" for the water made when food is broken down."]],"english-0348":["The word \\"too\\" means the droppings are like something just mentioned. The only thing in the paragraph that holds little water is the kangaroo rat\'s concentrated urine, so the sentence belongs right after it, at Point B. Then the paragraph lists the body\'s water-saving tricks in order: urine, droppings, and \\"also\\" the burrow. At Point C, \\"too\\" would follow a sentence about humid air, the opposite idea. Points A and D have nothing for \\"too\\" to point back to.",["\\"Too\\" needs something earlier that also contains little water, and no such thing has come up yet.","Correct: right after the urine sentence, \\"too\\" pairs droppings with urine, two ways the body could lose water but doesn\'t.","This follows the burrow sentence, which describes air that is MORE humid, so \\"too\\" has nothing to match.","This follows the sentence about carrying seeds home, which has nothing to do with water leaving the body."]],"english-0349":["Check the paragraph\'s first sentence: \\"Holding on to that water matters just as much.\\" Everything after it shows how the kangaroo rat saves water: concentrated urine, a humid burrow, and nighttime activity. Foot drumming is an interesting and true fact, but it is about warning other animals, not about water. Adding it would pull the paragraph off its topic, so the answer is No for that reason. The first choice is the trap: drumming may help the animal survive, but \\"survival\\" is broader than this paragraph\'s focus.",["It is a survival behavior, but the paragraph is specifically about holding on to water, and foot drumming has nothing to do with water.","The sentence never mentions night or the burrow, so it explains nothing about the animal\'s schedule.","There\'s no contradiction: drumming could happen at any time, and the sentence doesn\'t say when.","Correct: the paragraph begins \\"Holding on to that water matters just as much,\\" and drumming is about communication, not water."]],"english-0350":["Look at how the series is built: \\"Many animals make metabolic water, many produce concentrated urine, and...\\" Each item is \\"many\\" plus a verb. The original, \\"many hide from the heat,\\" follows that pattern exactly. The other choices say the same thing but switch to an -ing phrase, a flipped order, or a \\"there are\\" clause, which breaks the rhythm the sentence set up.",["Correct: \\"many hide from the heat\\" matches \\"many animals make\\" and \\"many produce\\": \\"many\\" plus a verb.","Breaks the pattern: the first two items start with \\"many\\" plus a verb, not an -ing phrase.","Breaks the pattern by flipping the order, so \\"the heat\\" comes first instead of \\"many.\\"","Breaks the pattern: \\"there are others known for\\" is a different, wordier structure."]],"english-0351":["A comparison of several desert animals would describe how different animals survive and set them side by side. This essay stays on one animal from start to finish. Other animals appear only in one general sentence near the end (\\"Many animals make metabolic water...\\"). So the answer is No, and the reason is the essay\'s single focus. The third choice says No for a false reason: the essay does explain how the kangaroo rat avoids the heat, by staying in its burrow during the day.",["The essay mentions other animals in a single sentence; that\'s not a comparison of their strategies.","True of the essay, but that\'s about one animal, not several.","False: the third paragraph says it spends the hot daylight hours in a burrow.","Correct: the whole essay is about the kangaroo rat; other animals appear only in passing in the last paragraph."]],"english-0352":["The bracketed sentence reports the result of Laennec\'s experiment: the heartbeat \\"came through more clearly than he had ever heard it with his ear alone.\\" That is the evidence that the tube worked, and worked better than the usual method. Without it, the paragraph describes the experiment but never says whether it succeeded. The other choices describe things found in other sentences (why the usual method felt improper, how the tube was made, why he switched to wood).",["Correct: hearing the heartbeat \\"more clearly than... with his ear alone\\" shows the tube beat pressing an ear to the chest.","That reason is in the first paragraph, not in this sentence.","The rolled paper is described in the sentence before; this one reports what he heard.","The switch comes in the next paragraph, because the paper was flimsy, not because of anything said here."]],"english-0353":["The previous paragraph ends with the improved wooden instrument and its name. This paragraph is about using it: three years of listening to patients and comparing what he heard with later findings. The best opener bridges the two, and the third choice does both jobs. \\"A better instrument in hand\\" points back, and \\"learn what the sounds actually meant\\" points ahead. The naming choice is the trap. It connects to the previous paragraph, but only by repeating it, and it doesn\'t introduce anything new.",["Background about his training that connects to neither the new instrument nor the listening study that follows.","A fact about the hospital that doesn\'t link the instrument to the study.","Correct: \\"a better instrument\\" looks back at the wooden stethoscope, and \\"what the sounds actually meant\\" sets up the three years of listening and comparing.","Repeats the naming from the end of the previous paragraph and doesn\'t introduce the study."]],"english-0354":["The paragraph says the paper tube worked but was flimsy. The next sentence tells what Laennec did next: he switched to sturdier wooden cylinders. That is the next step in time, and it follows from the problem, so \\"Soon\\" fits. \\"Nevertheless\\" gets the logic backward, because the flimsiness is the reason for the switch, not an obstacle to it. \\"For instance\\" and \\"Similarly\\" don\'t fit a sentence that moves the story forward.",["Correct: the switch to wood followed the flimsy paper tube, and \\"Soon\\" marks that next step in time.","Signals \\"despite that,\\" but the switch to wood happened because the paper was flimsy, not in spite of it.","Signals an example, but switching to wood isn\'t an example of the paper being flimsy.","Signals a likeness, but the sturdy wooden tube is an improvement on the paper, not something like it."]],"english-0355":["The essay tells a history in clear, standard English. The original sentence keeps that voice. The other three choices say much the same thing in slang (\\"blew doctors\' minds,\\" \\"super into,\\" \\"no joke\\"), which doesn\'t belong in this essay.",["Correct: plain and serious, like the rest of the essay.","Slang (\\"totally blew... minds\\") that clashes with the essay\'s even voice.","\\"Like\\" and \\"super into\\" are far too casual.","\\"Game changer, no joke\\" is chatty slang."]],"english-0356":["The essay follows the stethoscope from the moment Laennec rolled up a sheet of paper, to the wooden version he named, to three years of using it to study heart and lung sounds. That covers both how the tool was invented and how it was first put to use, so the answer is Yes. The last choice is the trap. The essay does say Laennec relied on \\"a simple fact about sound,\\" but applying a known fact in a new way is still inventing.",["False: the essay never mentions modern stethoscopes.","Correct: the essay covers the invention (paper, then wood) and the first use (three years of listening, then a book).","False: his training is never described.","Using a known fact about sound doesn\'t make the tube any less his invention."]],"english-0357":["The paragraph says the books were heavy and each letter had to be traced slowly. The bracketed sentence drives that point home: a student \\"could spend a long time on a single sentence.\\" Without it, the paragraph loses its clearest picture of how slow this kind of reading was, which is the problem Braille later solves. The sentence doesn\'t explain why books were scarce, never mentions Braille, and doesn\'t describe the letters\' appearance.",["The sentence is about how slowly students read, not about why there were few books.","Braille isn\'t mentioned in the sentence, and no one\'s speed is compared.","The letters are described in the sentences before (\\"large raised letters\\"); this sentence is about time.","Correct: it shows the cost of tracing letters one by one, which sets up why a faster system was needed."]],"english-0358":["The new sentence does two things: it looks back at \\"the idea\\" and looks ahead to \\"a serious flaw.\\" So it has to come after Barbier\'s system is described and before the flaw is explained. That spot is Point C: the system of raised dots, then \\"The idea was clever, but it had a serious flaw,\\" then the flaw itself (too many dots for a fingertip). At A and B the idea hasn\'t been described. At D the flaw has already been named, so the sentence would announce something the reader just learned.",["No idea has been described yet, so \\"The idea\\" has nothing to refer to.","At this point Barbier has only been named; his system hasn\'t been described, so \\"The idea\\" is still unclear.","Correct: it follows the description of Barbier\'s dot system and sets up the next sentence, which names the flaw.","The flaw has already been explained by this point, so announcing \\"a serious flaw\\" afterward is backward."]],"english-0359":["The series describes the cell three ways. The first two items have the same shape: \\"small enough to feel...\\" and \\"simple enough to press....\\" The third should be \\"___ enough to ___\\" too, and \\"flexible enough to stand for every letter, number, and punctuation mark\\" is the only choice that is. The other choices say the same thing, but as a new clause, a \\"with\\" phrase, or an -ing phrase, which breaks the pattern.",["Breaks the pattern: after \\"small enough to\\" and \\"simple enough to,\\" a new clause with \\"it had\\" doesn\'t match.","Closer, but a \\"with\\" phrase still doesn\'t match the \\"___ enough to\\" form of the first two items.","Correct: \\"flexible enough to stand for\\" matches \\"small enough to feel\\" and \\"simple enough to press.\\"","An -ing phrase breaks the \\"___ enough to\\" pattern set by the first two items."]],"english-0360":["The first paragraph ends with \\"few people expected a blind child to learn to read.\\" The best conclusion picks up that idea, and the second choice does: the boy few expected to read created a system blind readers would use around the world. NO CHANGE is the trap, because it is a strong closing fact, but the stem asks for a return to the first paragraph, and it doesn\'t make one. The teacher detail and the Barbier comparison reach back only to the second and third paragraphs.",["A true and fitting fact, but it doesn\'t return to anything in the first paragraph.","Correct: it echoes the first paragraph\'s point that few people expected a blind child to read, and turns it around.","Returns to the school from the second paragraph, not to an idea from the first.","Looks back at the third paragraph\'s code, not at the first paragraph."]],"english-0361":["The essay shows a problem Braille lived with (slow, heavy raised-letter books at his school) and then his solution: a six-dot cell he worked out by fifteen. That is exactly an invention growing out of a young person\'s own problem, so the answer is Yes. Barbier\'s code is the trap for both Yes and No. It isn\'t the young person\'s invention, and the fact that Braille built on it doesn\'t cancel the purpose.",["Correct: the problem (slow books for blind readers) and the invention (the six-dot cell) are both Braille\'s own.","Barbier\'s code is part of the story, but it was Barbier\'s invention for soldiers, not the young person\'s answer to his own problem.","Building on someone else\'s idea doesn\'t stop an invention from being his own response to his own problem.","False: the accident fills only the first paragraph; most of the essay is about reading and the six-dot cell."]],"english-0362":["The goal is about the narrator, who isn\'t used to being up at four in the morning. Only the last choice tells us anything about the narrator\'s habits. The narrator has seen that hour only \\"at the end of a sleepover, never at the start of a day.\\" The original describes the town, not the narrator. The calendar detail is about Noor, and the owl detail explains the timing of the survey. All are reasonable details, but only one meets the goal.",["Shows how dark and empty the town was, but says nothing about the narrator\'s own habits.","Explains why the survey starts early, but not how the narrator feels about the hour.","Shows that Noor planned ahead, not that the narrator was unused to the hour.","Correct: the narrator has seen 4 a.m. only after staying up all night, never as the start of a day, so the hour is new to the narrator."]],"english-0363":["The bracketed sentence comes during the three minutes of listening, and it describes what the narrator heard: nothing but her own heartbeat. Its job is to show how silent the woods were. It doesn\'t explain why Noor turned off the recording, and it doesn\'t describe the owl call. The \\"frightened\\" choice reads too much into the heartbeat. Nothing in the paragraph says the narrator was scared.",["The sentence doesn\'t explain Noor\'s choice; it describes what the narrator heard afterward.","A heartbeat here shows how quiet it was, not fear; the narrator never says she was afraid.","Correct: hearing her own heartbeat shows just how complete the silence was.","The call isn\'t described in this sentence; the barred owl\'s call comes up in the next paragraph."]],"english-0364":["Sentence 3 is about \\"a second owl,\\" so it has to come after the first owl calls. That happens in Sentence 4, at the sixth stop. Sentence 5 then says Noor wrote down \\"both owls,\\" so the second owl must already have appeared. The only spot that works is between Sentences 4 and 5. Placing it at the end is the trap: it is after the first owl, but it comes too late for \\"both.\\"",["A \\"second\\" owl can\'t answer before the first owl has called.","This puts the second owl before the five silent stops, which makes no sense.","Correct: the first owl calls at the sixth stop, then the second one answers, and then Noor writes down \\"both.\\"","Noor writes down \\"both owls\\" in Sentence 5, so the second owl has to show up before that."]],"english-0365":["This is a personal story told in everyday language: \\"My toes went numb,\\" \\"I could see her grinning.\\" The bracketed portion should sound like the same person talking. \\"I started to wonder why anyone would do this for six years\\" does. The other choices go the wrong way, into stiff, formal wording (\\"commenced,\\" \\"rationale,\\" \\"individuals,\\" \\"reflect upon\\"). The trap is thinking the most formal choice is always best. The right choice is the one that matches the essay.",["Far too stiff: \\"commenced\\" and \\"rationale\\" sound like a report, not a personal story.","Still too formal: \\"individuals\\" and \\"persist in this activity\\" clash with the narrator\'s casual voice.","\\"Reflect upon\\" is more formal than the rest of the narrative.","Correct: plain, everyday words that match the narrator\'s voice (\\"My toes went numb\\")."]],"english-0366":["Earlier, with numb toes, the narrator \\"started to wonder why anyone would do this for six years.\\" A conclusion that shows the narrator\'s feelings have changed needs to answer that doubt. The second choice does: the narrator says yes to next year \\"before she finished the question.\\" The paragraph even sets it up with \\"Our two didn\'t sound like much,\\" and the \\"But\\" turns it around. The other choices are details about the survey, the cold, or the coffee, and none of them shows a change.",["A fact about the survey\'s history; it says nothing about how the narrator feels.","Correct: the narrator who once wondered \\"why anyone would do this\\" now says yes to next year right away.","Returns to the cold, but doesn\'t show any change in how the narrator feels about the survey.","A small sensory detail about the morning, not a change in attitude."]],"english-0367":["The essay argues a policy point with numbers and calm reasoning (\\"$1.50 each way,\\" \\"a 180-day school year\\"). Its thesis should sound the same way, and the original does. The other choices state the same position, but in slang (\\"seriously,\\" \\"like\\") or with a pleading exclamation, which would undercut a serious argument.",["Correct: a clear, serious statement of the essay\'s position, in the same voice as the rest.","Too casual: \\"seriously\\" and \\"already\\" sound like a complaint to a friend.","Talks to the county like a person and ends with an exclamation, which doesn\'t fit a formal argument.","\\"Like\\" and \\"the obvious move\\" are slang that clashes with the essay\'s voice."]],"english-0368":["The paragraph\'s first sentence makes a claim: free fares would mean fewer missed days. The bracketed sentence explains why. When a ride costs money, a student may skip the days the family can\'t afford. The teachers\' report then backs this up with a pattern. Without the bracketed sentence, the paragraph jumps from claim to evidence and never says how the fare causes the absences. The other choices describe numbers, budgets, or people the sentence never mentions.",["The sentence gives no figures at all.","The budget comes up in the last paragraph, not here.","No teacher is named, and the teachers in the paragraph report absences rather than take a side.","Correct: it spells out the link between the fare and missed days that the paragraph\'s first sentence claims."]],"english-0369":["The paragraph opens with \\"Free fares would also help the county itself.\\" Then it lists benefits: fewer cars in traffic, and future adult riders. The second benefit adds to the first, so \\"In addition\\" is right. \\"However\\" and \\"Even so\\" signal a turn against what came before, and there is none. \\"Instead\\" means the second idea replaces the first, but the writer wants both.",["Signals a contrast, but this sentence gives a second benefit, not an objection.","Correct: the sentence adds a second way free fares would help the county, after less traffic.","Suggests the new idea replaces the traffic benefit, when it adds to it.","Signals \\"despite that,\\" but nothing in the previous sentence is a drawback to push against."]],"english-0370":["The next sentence says \\"That is a real expense, but it is small compared with the system\'s $41 million budget.\\" \\"That\\" points to the bracketed sentence\'s $300,000. Delete it and the comparison has no number to compare, so the sentence should stay. The third choice confuses the $540 for one student with the $300,000 for the whole system. The fourth gets argument backward: naming the cost and then showing it is small is how a persuasive essay handles an objection.",["Correct: \\"That is a real expense... small compared with the system\'s $41 million budget\\" depends on knowing the cost is about $300,000.","Nothing in the paragraph says what the leaders expected.","The first paragraph gives the cost for one student ($540); this is the total for the bus system, a different number.","Facing the cost and then answering it makes the argument stronger, not weaker."]],"english-0371":["Ask what the essay mostly does. It states a position (\\"The county should let students ride its buses for free\\") and supports it: fewer absences, less traffic, future riders, and an affordable cost. It never explains how the system is run, who drives the buses, or how the budget is raised. The budget appears once, to show the cost is small. So the answer is No, because the essay argues for a policy. The first choice is the trap: the numbers are there, but they serve the argument.",["Those numbers appear, but only as evidence in an argument, not as a description of how the system works.","That\'s one benefit in the argument for free fares, not an account of how the system is run.","Correct: the essay is an argument for a policy; the budget figure supports that argument.","False: the first sentence says students pay $1.50 each way."]],"english-0372":["The goal is the amount of walking. The original and the second choice both say she walks every trail, but neither gives a sense of scale. The second only adds a time of day. The last choice puts a number on it (\\"all 140 miles\\") and adds that she walks many of them \\"several times.\\" That is the choice that emphasizes how much walking the job takes. The older-maps choice isn\'t about walking at all.",["Says she walks the trails, but gives no sense of how much ground that is.","Adds when she walks, not how much.","Describes research at a desk, not walking.","Correct: 140 miles, many walked several times, makes the amount of walking concrete and large."]],"english-0373":["The new sentence starts with \\"That,\\" so it has to follow something that could put a hiker on the wrong side of a stream. The only such thing is a GPS signal drifting \\"by dozens of feet.\\" Point B comes right after that sentence. It also sets up \\"So she also counts her steps\\": the drift is a real danger, so she double-checks. At the other points, \\"That\\" would refer to her distrust, her step counting, or her sketches, and none of them puts hikers in the wrong place.",["\\"That\\" would refer to Brandt not trusting her GPS, and distrust can\'t put a hiker anywhere.","Correct: \\"That\\" refers to the signal drifting by dozens of feet, and the next sentence (\\"So she also counts her steps\\") follows from the danger.","\\"That\\" would refer to counting steps and checking a compass, which are the fix, not the problem.","\\"That\\" would refer to her sketches of landmarks, which help hikers rather than mislead them."]],"english-0374":["The sentence explains why trails are red: red is easy to see against the forest\'s greens and browns. The phrase for \\"easy to notice\\" is \\"stands out.\\" Each of the others is a real phrase with a different meaning. \\"Stands up\\" means rises or holds up, \\"stands by\\" means waits or supports, and \\"stands in\\" means substitutes. None of them describes a color catching the eye.",["\\"Stands up\\" means rising to your feet or holding up under pressure, not being easy to see.","\\"Stands by\\" means waiting or staying loyal, which makes no sense for a color.","Correct: \\"stands out\\" means is easy to notice, which is why red is used against green and brown.","\\"Stands in\\" means substitutes for something, which doesn\'t fit here."]],"english-0375":["The last paragraph is about how Brandt judges her own work. She saves the cards from hikers who never got lost, not the ones that praise her maps\' looks. The opener should point to that idea, and \\"Her maps have won awards, but Brandt measures success differently\\" does. The cards then show what that different measure is. The other choices are true details about her office, the visitor center, and her childhood, but none of them introduces what follows.",["Returns to her office, which has nothing to do with the comment cards the paragraph is about.","About the visitor center\'s shop, not about Brandt or how she judges her work.","Correct: \\"measures success differently\\" sets up the comment cards and the kind of note she saves.","Background on Brandt\'s childhood, which the paragraph never comes back to."]],"english-0376":["Look at why Brandt does each thing in the essay. She sketches \\"what a hiker actually sees\\" because \\"People don\'t read coordinates.\\" She picks red because it stands out on the trail. She saves the cards from people who \\"never once wondered where they were.\\" Each choice is shaped by what hikers need, so the answer is Yes. The awards choice gives the wrong reason. The last choice is the subtle trap: the essay shows hikers\' needs through her methods, so it doesn\'t need hikers\' own words.",["The awards get half a sentence, and they show that others praise the maps, not that hikers\' needs shape her work.","Correct: almost every detail of her method is explained by what a hiker needs on the trail.","False: she doesn\'t fully trust her GPS, and the essay keeps returning to what hikers see and need.","The purpose is about how hikers\' needs shape her work, and her methods show exactly that; hiker quotes aren\'t required."]],"english-0377":["\\"The room\\" needs a room to refer back to, and the first one mentioned is \\"a converted storage room at the back of the community center.\\" Point C comes right after that sentence. Point D also comes after the room, but it lands between \\"Its signal reaches only about six miles\\" and \\"Inside that range, though....\\" \\"That range\\" means the six miles, so nothing can come between those two sentences. At Points A and B, no room has come up yet.",["No room has been mentioned yet, so \\"The room\\" has nothing to refer to.","This comes before the storage room is mentioned, so \\"The room\\" still has nothing to refer to.","Correct: it follows the sentence that introduces the converted storage room, so \\"The room\\" is clear.","This splits the six-mile signal from \\"Inside that range,\\" which needs to follow the signal sentence directly."]],"english-0378":["The goal has a time element: Bixby has stayed committed \\"since it began.\\" Every choice says he offered to train the students at the start. Only the third adds that he \\"still comes in to help three afternoons a week,\\" which shows he never left. The Chicago detail shows his experience, and the newspaper detail shows how he heard about it. Both are about the beginning, not about staying.",["Shows how Bixby helped at the start, but not that he has stayed involved.","Shows Bixby is qualified, not that he has stuck with the station.","Correct: \\"still comes in... three afternoons a week\\" shows he has kept helping long after the start.","Explains how Bixby got involved, not whether he stayed."]],"english-0379":["The two sentences before the bracket share a shape: a student (\\"A ninth grader,\\" \\"Two juniors\\"), then a present-tense verb (\\"plays,\\" \\"interview\\"), then what they do on the air. \\"A senior reads the high school\'s sports scores...\\" follows the same shape. The other choices keep the information but rearrange it. One adds a \\"There is... whose job is\\" frame, one turns passive, and one starts with an -ing phrase. Each of those breaks the series.",["Breaks the pattern: \\"There is also a senior whose job is...\\" is wordier and built differently from the two sentences before it.","Flips the pattern into the passive voice, so the student is no longer the subject.","Starts with an -ing phrase, so the student comes last instead of first.","Correct: like the two sentences before it, it names the student first and then says what that student does."]],"english-0380":["The paragraph before is about fun shows: polka records, business interviews, and loud sports scores. The new paragraph shifts to something serious, a storm in which the station became a lifeline. That is a contrast that grew over time, so \\"Over time, though\\" fits. \\"For instance\\" and \\"In other words\\" would treat the storm story as an example or a restatement of the shows. \\"Similarly\\" treats it as more of the same.",["Signals an example, but the station becoming \\"more than a hobby\\" is not an example of varied shows.","Correct: it marks a turn from the playful shows to the station\'s serious role, which developed later.","Signals a likeness, but the storm coverage is a different, more serious side of the station.","Signals a restatement, but the new sentence says something new rather than repeating the last paragraph."]],"english-0381":["The paragraph\'s point is that the station became \\"more than a hobby.\\" The bracketed sentence completes that point: for many residents, 91.7 FM was the only news that night. Without it, the paragraph says what the students did but not how much it mattered to listeners. The generator and the kinds of news are in the sentence before, and nothing here claims the signal reaches the whole county.",["Correct: the sentence shows that for many people, the station was the only news they had.","The backup generator is mentioned in the sentence before, which would still be there.","Road closures and shelter locations are listed in the previous sentence, not this one.","The sentence says \\"many residents,\\" not every home, and the first paragraph says the signal reaches only about six miles."]],"english-0382":["The narrator is describing what they expected before starting: they imagined something like the sink at home. \\"Pictured\\" means exactly that, seeing it in your mind. The other three words are close relatives with a different meaning. \\"Depicted,\\" \\"portrayed,\\" and \\"illustrated\\" all mean showing something to other people through art, words, or acting. That doesn\'t fit a private expectation that turned out \\"wrong.\\"",["Correct: \\"pictured\\" means imagined, which is what the narrator did before the first day.","\\"Depicted\\" means showed in a picture or a story for others, not imagined in your own mind.","\\"Portrayed\\" means presented or acted out for others, which the narrator didn\'t do.","\\"Illustrated\\" means drew pictures or gave examples, not imagined."]],"english-0383":["The sentence before says what Mr. Tavares did NOT do: give a speech or a list of rules. The next sentence says what he did in place of that: showed the narrator once. \\"Instead\\" is the word for one thing taking the place of another. \\"Likewise\\" signals similarity, \\"Therefore\\" signals a result, and \\"For example\\" signals an illustration of the previous sentence. None of those fits a sentence that swaps one approach for another.",["Signals a similarity, but showing the narrator the job is different from giving a speech, not like it.","Signals a result, but not giving a speech doesn\'t cause the one demonstration.","Signals an example, but the demonstration isn\'t an example of not giving a speech.","Correct: rather than a speech or rules, he gave one quick demonstration; \\"Instead\\" marks that replacement."]],"english-0384":["Look at what follows the bracketed sentence: the bell means a big group and mugs running out, and the loud grill means hash browns and a sheet pan soon. These are two examples of one skill, understanding the kitchen\'s signals. The bracketed sentence names that skill: \\"I had also learned to read the kitchen.\\" Without it, the examples arrive with no idea tying them together. It doesn\'t explain the tub speed or describe Mr. Tavares\'s cooking, and nothing suggests the narrator wanted to cook.",["The sentence doesn\'t explain the tub speed; it introduces a new skill.","Correct: the bell and the grill are examples of \\"reading the kitchen,\\" and this sentence names that skill.","Hash browns come up once, as part of an example; the sentence itself describes no dishes.","Nothing in the paragraph says the narrator wanted to cook."]],"english-0385":["The stem asks for two things: Mr. Tavares\'s words and an earlier detail about him. The earlier detail is in the second paragraph: he \\"rarely spoke above a mutter\\" and didn\'t give speeches. The last choice connects the two: from a man who rarely said anything, three words meant a lot. The roll, the school year, and the diner\'s winter repairs are all accurate, but none of them says anything about the compliment or the man who gave it.",["Tells us about the roll, not about Mr. Tavares\'s words, and links to nothing earlier about him.","Moves the story forward in time but makes no connection to Mr. Tavares.","A fact about the diner with no link to Mr. Tavares or his compliment.","Correct: it ties \\"You were quick\\" back to the earlier detail that Mr. Tavares \\"rarely spoke above a mutter.\\""]],"english-0386":["The narrator gets almost no instruction. Mr. Tavares \\"showed me once\\" and went back to the grill. Then, by working, the narrator learns to empty a tub in under four minutes and to read the kitchen\'s signals. That is learning mostly through practice, so the answer is Yes. The last choice is the careful trap: there was a little instruction, but the purpose says \\"mostly,\\" and one demonstration followed by a summer of work fits that.",["Correct: one brief lesson, then a month of work that teaches speed and how to \\"read the kitchen.\\"","False: the machine is mentioned only in passing, and the essay never explains how it works.","His nineteen years get half a sentence; the essay is about the narrator\'s summer.","One quick demonstration fits \\"mostly through practice\\"; it doesn\'t make the job something learned by instruction."]],"english-0387":["The paragraph builds to a risk: a nail at the wrong angle can reach the sensitive tissue inside the hoof. The best closing sentence follows from that risk, and the third choice does. Because of it, Marsh checks every nail\'s angle even after twenty-six years. The other choices are accurate, but each one jumps to a new topic (the history of horseshoes, unshod horses, her supply of shoes) and leaves the danger hanging.",["A general fact about horseshoes that drops the paragraph\'s point about nail angles.","Shifts to horses without shoes, which has nothing to do with the danger just described.","Correct: \\"That is why\\" follows from the danger of a badly angled nail and brings the paragraph back to Marsh.","A detail about her supplies that ignores the nail-angle problem the paragraph ends on."]],"english-0388":["Follow the time words. Sentence 1 announces a routine. Sentence 3 is the first step and names Marsh. Sentence 2 starts with \\"Then,\\" so it comes next. Sentence 4 starts with \\"Finally,\\" so it comes last. That gives 1, 3, 2, 4. Putting 3 first pushes the opening line into the middle. Putting 2 before 3 makes \\"Then\\" come before any first step, and putting 3 at the end comes after \\"Finally.\\"",["\\"Then she lifts each foot\\" can\'t come before the sentence that names Marsh and starts the visit.","Correct: the visit begins, she watches the horse walk, then lifts and trims, and finally shoes it.","\\"Each visit begins the same way\\" announces the routine, so it should open the paragraph, not follow the first step.","Watching the horse walk would come after it\'s already shod, and \\"Finally\\" would no longer be the last step."]],"english-0389":["The last sentence lists three things Marsh does: she \\"heats\\" a shoe, \\"shapes\\" it, and... The third item should be the same kind of verb, \\"nails it in place.\\" The original switches the subject to \\"the shoe\\" and uses the passive voice, and \\"then it gets nailed\\" does the same. \\"Nailing\\" is an -ing form that doesn\'t match the other two verbs.",["Breaks the pattern by switching to a new subject (\\"the shoe\\") and the passive voice.","An -ing form doesn\'t match \\"heats\\" and \\"shapes.\\"","Correct: \\"nails\\" matches \\"heats\\" and \\"shapes,\\" three present-tense verbs with Marsh as the subject.","Adds a new subject and a passive verb, which breaks the series of things Marsh does."]],"english-0390":["The paragraph opens with \\"The work is hard on the body\\" and goes on to mention two knee surgeries. The goal is to show that strain while she holds the foot. Only the last choice adds weight to the picture: a horse that may weigh more than half a ton leans on her. The other choices describe a calm voice, a watching owner, and a hoof check. They are all believable moments from a visit, but none of them is hard on her body.",["Shows her gentle manner with the horse, not the strain on her body.","Adds a bystander; nothing here is hard on Marsh\'s body.","Describes part of the job, but not a physical strain.","Correct: an animal that may weigh more than half a ton leaning on her makes the strain concrete."]],"english-0391":["\\"Bent over\\" already tells us her position. Adding \\"in a bent position\\" or \\"a bent posture,\\" or starting with \\"Bending over,\\" repeats that idea. The second choice says it once and keeps every bit of meaning.",["\\"In a bent position\\" repeats \\"bent over.\\"","Correct: \\"bent over\\" says it once, with nothing repeated.","Says \\"bent\\" twice: \\"bent over\\" and \\"a bent posture.\\"","\\"Bending over\\" and \\"bent\\" say the same thing twice."]],"english-0392":["The paragraph\'s first sentence sets its focus: \\"Trees would make recess safer.\\" The next sentence supports it with cooler ground and shade for overheated students. A fact about the lifespan of bristlecone pines is true and interesting, but it does nothing for the safety argument. It is a detour, so the writer shouldn\'t add it. The first choice is the trap: it sounds related to the essay, but the sentence is about one ancient kind of tree, not the trees a school would plant.",["The sentence is about bristlecone pines, not the trees the district would plant, so it shows nothing about this schoolyard.","The sentence is about how long one kind of tree lives; it says nothing about shade or temperature.","Misreads it: the sentence is about how long trees live, not how fast they grow.","Correct: the paragraph is about safety and shade at recess, and a fact about ancient pines doesn\'t support that."]],"english-0393":["The sentence says shade makes the ground cooler, so the verb has to mean \\"make lower.\\" \\"Lower\\" does exactly that. \\"Demote\\" and \\"humble\\" both mean bringing a person down in rank or pride. \\"Decline\\" can mean go down (\\"temperatures declined\\"), but with an object it means refuse, so \\"decline the temperature\\" says the tree refuses the temperature.",["Correct: to \\"lower\\" a temperature is to make it go down.","\\"Demote\\" means to move someone to a lower rank, not to cool something.","\\"Humble\\" means to make someone less proud, which doesn\'t apply to temperature.","Used with an object, \\"decline\\" means to refuse (\\"decline an offer\\"), so \\"decline the temperature\\" makes no sense."]],"english-0394":["The essay argues in clear, everyday standard English: \\"Trees would make recess safer,\\" \\"that is a small price.\\" The original sentence matches that voice. Two choices are too casual (\\"totally stressing,\\" \\"like, whoa\\"). The tricky one goes the other way: \\"harbor grave apprehensions regarding the fiscal burden of arboreal expenditure\\" sounds formal, but it is puffed up and doesn\'t match the essay\'s plain style. Matching the tone means matching the essay, not choosing the fanciest words.",["Correct: plain and serious, matching the essay\'s calm, reasoned voice.","Too slangy: \\"totally stressing\\" and \\"price tag\\" sound like casual talk.","Too inflated: \\"grave apprehensions\\" and \\"arboreal expenditure\\" are far stiffer than the rest of the essay.","Slang (\\"like, whoa\\") that clashes with a serious argument."]],"english-0395":["\\"This year\\" already says when, and \\"wait\\" already means putting it off. The second choice uses each idea once. The others double up: \\"this year\\" plus \\"in the current year,\\" \\"wait\\" plus \\"delay,\\" or \\"promptly\\" and \\"until later\\" added on top of words that already say the same thing.",["\\"In the current year\\" repeats \\"this year.\\"","Correct: says when to act, once, and keeps the contrast with waiting.","\\"Promptly\\" and \\"until later\\" repeat ideas already carried by \\"this year\\" and \\"wait.\\"","\\"Wait\\" and \\"delay\\" mean the same thing here."]],"english-0396":["The core sentence is \\"Mary Anning grew up searching those cliffs.\\" Everything between \\"Anning\\" and \\"grew\\" is extra information about her, so it needs a comma on each side. The \\"who\\" clause belongs inside that aside, because it describes the cabinetmaker (her father sold fossils), so no comma goes before \\"who.\\" The original does all of this. Choice B adds a comma before \\"who\\" and leaves that new aside unclosed. Choices C and D each keep only one of the two commas that the aside needs.",["Correct: one pair of commas sets off the whole appositive, including the \\"who\\" clause that describes the cabinetmaker.","The comma after \\"cabinetmaker\\" opens a new aside that is never closed, so \\"sideline grew\\" runs together.","Drops the opening comma, so the closing comma after \\"sideline\\" now splits the subject from its verb, \\"grew.\\"","Opens the aside with a comma but never closes it, so the appositive runs straight into the verb."]],"english-0397":["Two clues decide this. \\"The skull\\" means the sentence must come after Joseph finds the skull. \\"At first\\" means it describes an early reaction, before people knew more. The best spot is Point B: Joseph finds a skull, some people take it for a crocodile\'s, then Mary uncovers the rest of the skeleton, which shows what the animal really looked like. At Point C, the full skeleton is already out, so going back to a first guess about the skull alone breaks the timeline. Point D is worse, since by then the animal has been described.",["No skull has been found yet, so \\"the skull\\" has nothing to refer to.","Correct: right after Joseph finds the skull and before the rest of the skeleton turns up, \\"At first\\" fits the timeline.","By now the whole skeleton has been uncovered, so a first guess based on \\"the skull\\" alone is out of order.","By now the animal has been fully described, so a first guess based on the skull alone comes far too late."]],"english-0398":["Find the subject: \\"The skeleton.\\" The phrase \\"along with many of the other specimens she pulled from the cliffs\\" is set off by commas and doesn\'t make the subject plural. Only \\"and\\" does that. So the verb must be singular, and it must be past tense to match the paragraph: \\"was.\\" The original falls for the plural noun \\"specimens\\" sitting right before the verb. \\"Have been\\" and \\"are\\" are plural and also change the tense.",["Treats \\"along with...\\" as part of the subject, but the subject is just \\"The skeleton,\\" which is singular.","Correct: the singular subject \\"The skeleton\\" takes the singular verb \\"was.\\"","Plural, and the shift to present perfect clashes with the past-tense story.","Plural, and present tense in a paragraph about events in the 1820s."]],"english-0399":["The paragraph is about credit: \\"Anning received little credit in her lifetime.\\" The scientists described her fossils \\"without acknowledging her,\\" that is, without giving her credit. The other three words are close relatives meaning \\"to grant\\" or \\"to own up to,\\" but each one goes wrong here. \\"Admitting her\\" means letting her into a group, which the next clause shows is a different matter. \\"Confessing\\" suggests guilt. \\"Conceding\\" needs a point or a claim as its object, not a person.",["Correct: to \\"acknowledge\\" someone is to give them credit, which is what the scientists failed to do.","\\"Admitting her\\" means letting her in (as a member), not giving her credit for the fossils.","You confess a wrong you did; \\"confessing her\\" makes no sense and wrongly suggests guilt.","\\"Concede\\" means to give up a point or grant something reluctantly; it takes a claim as its object, not a person."]],"english-0400":["The essay describes Anning\'s discoveries (the ichthyosaur skeleton and the first nearly complete plesiosaur), notes that scientists bought and wrote about her fossils, and says the Geological Society never made her a member. That is a person without official standing contributing to science, so the answer is Yes. The trap is the third choice. The essay does stress that she lacked credit, but lack of credit is not lack of contribution. Her story shows exactly that gap.",["Right answer, wrong reason: her father\'s sideline is background, not her contribution to science.","Correct: her discoveries are the contribution, and her exclusion shows she had no official standing.","Tempting, but being denied credit doesn\'t erase the contribution; it is part of the point.","False: the animals get a phrase or two each, while every paragraph is built around Anning\'s work and treatment."]],"english-0401":["\\"The tern\'s journey follows the sun\\" is a complete sentence, and so is \\"it spends the northern summer nesting in the Arctic and the southern summer feeding... near Antarctica.\\" The second explains the first, so a colon joins them correctly. A comma alone is a comma splice. \\"; because\\" looks like a careful fix, but \\"because\\" turns the second half into a dependent clause, which can\'t follow a semicolon. \\"Which it\\" makes the sentence fall apart.",["Correct: a colon can join two complete sentences when the second explains the first, as it does here.","A comma splice: two complete sentences joined by only a comma.","A semicolon must join two complete sentences, but \\"because\\" makes the second part dependent.","Makes no sense: \\"which\\" would point to the sun, and \\"the sun, which it spends the northern summer nesting\\" is garbled."]],"english-0402":["Read the sentence: the researchers \\"recaptured some of them and removed them.\\" The first \\"them\\" is the birds. The second could be the birds again (removed the birds from somewhere?) or the geolocators. The writer means the devices, so the clearest choice names them. \\"Those\\" has the same problem as \\"them,\\" and \\"it\\" is singular with nothing singular to point to.",["\\"Them\\" could mean the birds or the devices; the sentence has just mentioned both.","Singular, but the researchers removed many devices, and \\"it\\" has no clear antecedent.","Correct: naming the devices makes it clear the researchers removed the geolocators, not the birds.","\\"Those\\" is just as unclear as \\"them\\": those birds or those devices?"]],"english-0403":["The goal has two parts: the device\'s size, and a comparison with the bird. Choices B and C describe how the geolocator worked and how its data was collected, which are true details but not about size. Choice D is the near miss. It gives the weight, 1.4 grams, but doesn\'t compare it with anything. Only the original does both. It gives the weight and calls it \\"a tiny fraction of the bird\'s own weight,\\" which ties back to the first paragraph\'s 100 grams.",["Correct: it compares the device\'s weight directly with the bird\'s, calling it \\"a tiny fraction.\\"","Explains how the device worked, not how small it was.","About collecting the data, not about size.","Gives the device\'s weight but doesn\'t compare it with the bird, so the reader must work out the contrast alone."]],"english-0404":["The sentence before the bracket makes a claim: \\"The terns did not fly straight south.\\" The bracketed sentence supports it with the first surprise, a stop of almost a month in the middle of the North Atlantic. Without it, the paragraph jumps from the claim to the two southern paths, and the reader never learns what made the route indirect at its start. The other choices describe things the sentence doesn\'t do: describe the southern summer, explain the devices, or compare nesting sites.",["The southern summer near Antarctica is described in the first paragraph, not here.","How the devices worked is never explained in this sentence.","The sentence doesn\'t separate the birds by where they nested.","Correct: a month-long stop in the middle of the North Atlantic shows exactly how the birds strayed from a direct route."]],"english-0405":["\\"In its lifetime\\" already means the whole of it. The original sounds careful and formal, but it stacks up three words for the same idea (\\"entire,\\" \\"span,\\" \\"whole\\"). Choice C adds \\"from birth to death,\\" which is just a definition of \\"lifetime.\\" Choice D is the subtle one: \\"of thirty years or more\\" looks like useful detail, but the sentence already said terns \\"can live for thirty years or more.\\"",["Sounds thorough, but \\"entire,\\" \\"span,\\" and \\"whole\\" all say the same thing.","Correct: says it once, and the sentence has already given the lifespan.","\\"From birth to death\\" is what a lifetime means.","Repeats \\"can live for thirty years or more\\" from earlier in the same sentence."]],"english-0406":["Ask what the opening phrase describes: what is \\"held together by grooved strips of lead\\"? The pieces of glass. Whatever comes right after the comma must be that thing, and in the original it is. The other choices all sound natural and keep the same facts, but each changes the subject to the lead, to Vance, or to an empty \\"it.\\" Each one then says the wrong thing is held together by lead. The original may look plain next to them, but it is the only one where the modifier fits.",["Correct: the pieces of glass are what the cames hold together, and they come right after the opening phrase.","Now the lead is the subject, so the sentence says the lead is held together by strips of lead.","Now Vance is the subject, so the sentence says Vance is held together by strips of lead.","\\"It\\" becomes the subject, so the opening phrase describes \\"it\\" instead of the glass."]],"english-0407":["The series describes how Vance got the glass: \\"salvaged from torn-down buildings, bought at estate sales, or ___.\\" The first two items are past participles, so the third must be too: \\"traded with other restorers.\\" The original adds a whole new clause (\\"she trades for them\\"). The other choices use \\"trading\\" or \\"by trading,\\" which don\'t match the pattern.",["Switches from a series of past participles (\\"salvaged,\\" \\"bought\\") to a full clause with a new subject.","Correct: \\"traded\\" matches \\"salvaged\\" and \\"bought,\\" three past participles describing the glass.","An -ing form doesn\'t match \\"salvaged\\" and \\"bought.\\"","\\"By trading\\" is a new kind of phrase that breaks the series of past participles."]],"english-0408":["The paragraph\'s first sentence makes a claim: \\"finding a match is the hardest part of the job.\\" The new sentence explains why. Old glass has bubbles and ripples that new glass lacks, so a modern piece can stand out even in the right color. It also sets up the next sentence, about why Vance keeps hundreds of sheets of old glass. That is support for the paragraph\'s point, not a detour, so the answer is Yes. Choice C is the trap: the sentence mentions how glass is made, but only to explain her problem.",["Correct: it supports the claim just before it, that finding a match is the hardest part, and explains why Vance saves old glass.","The sentence says nothing about strength; it is about bubbles, ripples, and appearance.","Tempting, but the sentence is about Vance\'s problem: why a modern piece won\'t match.","No contradiction: it explains why replacing a piece well is hard, not that it can\'t be done."]],"english-0409":["The previous paragraph is about repairing and replacing broken pieces. The last paragraph describes putting the window back together. The best opener bridges the two, and the last choice does: once every piece is \\"cleaned, matched, or replaced,\\" the window \\"is rebuilt.\\" The other choices are true but point elsewhere: her grandfather, the scenes in windows, or who makes lead cames.",["Background about her training that links to neither the glass matching before it nor the rebuilding after it.","A detail about the windows\' subjects; it doesn\'t lead into putting a window back together.","A side fact about cames that doesn\'t connect the two paragraphs.","Correct: it wraps up the cleaning and matching from the previous paragraph and introduces the rebuilding described next."]],"english-0410":["The essay describes Vance\'s work in clear, everyday standard English: \\"The first step is taking a window apart.\\" \\"She has done her job well\\" matches that. Two choices are too casual (\\"totally nailed it,\\" \\"super well\\"), and the original goes the other way, into stiff, inflated wording that sounds out of place. Matching tone means matching the essay, not picking the fanciest phrase.",["Too stiff: \\"her labors have attained their intended end\\" is far more formal than the essay\'s plain voice.","Too casual: \\"totally nailed it\\" is slang.","Correct: plain and direct, like the rest of the profile.","\\"Super well\\" is too casual for this essay."]],"english-0411":["The clause after \\"Gus\\" is interrupted by \\"my grandfather warned me,\\" which makes \\"whom\\" look right, since the grandfather seems to be warning someone. Take the interruption out: \\"a retired firefighter named Gus who sang louder than anyone in the county.\\" The pronoun is the subject of \\"sang,\\" so it must be \\"who.\\" \\"Whose\\" would need a noun to own, and \\"which\\" isn\'t used for people.",["\\"Whom\\" is for objects, but here the pronoun is the subject of \\"sang\\"; \\"my grandfather warned me\\" is only an interruption.","Correct: remove the aside and the clause reads \\"who sang louder than anyone,\\" so the subject form is needed.","\\"Whose\\" shows ownership (\\"whose voice\\"), but nothing here is owned; the clause needs a subject for \\"sang.\\"","\\"Which\\" refers to things, not to a person like Gus."]],"english-0413":["\\"By the time the December concert arrived\\" sets a point in the past. Learning every note happened before that point, and the verb for \\"before a past moment\\" is the past perfect: \\"had learned.\\" \\"Have learned\\" and \\"learn\\" are present forms that clash with the rest of the paragraph (\\"lost,\\" \\"wavered,\\" \\"leaned\\"). \\"Will have learned\\" points to the future, but the narrator is telling us about a concert that already happened.",["Present perfect doesn\'t fit a story told in the past; it can\'t follow \\"By the time... arrived.\\"","Correct: \\"By the time\\" plus the past tense \\"arrived\\" calls for the past perfect, for something finished before that moment.","Future perfect, but the concert has already happened in the story.","Present tense breaks the past-tense story."]],"english-0414":["The paragraph tells events in order: Gus starts early, the section wavers, and then the grandfather sings the right line and the others follow. \\"Then\\" marks that next step in time. \\"For example\\" and \\"In other words\\" would treat the sentence as an illustration or a rewording of the wavering. \\"Similarly\\" would say the grandfather\'s steady singing was like the wavering, when it is what fixed it.",["Signals an example, but the grandfather\'s action isn\'t an example of the section wavering.","Signals a likeness, but steady singing is the opposite of wavering, not like it.","Signals a restatement, but the sentence tells what happened next, not the same thing again.","Correct: the grandfather\'s steady singing comes next in time, right after the moment the section wavered."]],"english-0415":["The narrator\'s grandfather says almost nothing, so humming the tenor line all the way home is, for him, very generous praise. \\"Lavish\\" means generous and plentiful, and \\"lavish praise\\" is the standard phrase. The near-synonyms carry the wrong shade. \\"Excessive\\" means too much and suggests the praise wasn\'t deserved. \\"Luxurious\\" fits things like fabric or a hotel. \\"Wasteful\\" is purely negative.",["Correct: \\"lavish praise\\" means generous, abundant praise, and the humor is that humming counted as that for a quiet man.","\\"Excessive praise\\" means too much, more than deserved, which wrongly suggests the narrator didn\'t earn it.","\\"Luxurious\\" describes rich comforts like a hotel or fabric, not praise.","\\"Wasteful\\" means needlessly using something up, which is negative and makes no sense for praise."]],"english-0416":["This sentence is inverted: it starts with a place (\\"Along the greenhouse\'s single long wooden table\\") and puts the verb before the subject. Flip it to find the subject: \\"Dozens of trays of seedlings ___ along the table.\\" \\"Dozens of trays\\" is plural, so the verb is \\"sit.\\" The trap is the singular \\"table\\" right before the verb, which makes \\"sits\\" sound right.",["Agrees with \\"table,\\" which comes first, but in this inverted sentence the subject comes after the verb: \\"dozens of trays.\\"","Correct: the subject, \\"dozens of trays of seedlings,\\" is plural, so the verb is \\"sit.\\"","Singular, and the present perfect doesn\'t fit a description of what the greenhouse looks like now.","Singular; it still treats \\"table\\" as the subject."]],"english-0417":["The goal has two parts: scientific work, and work that is the students\' own. Only the last choice meets both. Students \\"design their own experiments\\" and test how light, water, and fertilizer change growth. Watering plants and learning plant names happen in a greenhouse, but they aren\'t experiments. Hanging posters isn\'t science at all. This choice also prepares the essay\'s ending, which calls the greenhouse a place where science is \\"something they do with their own hands.\\"",["Shows students caring for plants, but watering is a chore, not scientific work.","Learning names is studying facts, not doing science of their own.","Decoration, not scientific work.","Correct: designing experiments and testing light, water, and fertilizer is science that students carry out themselves."]],"english-0418":["The sentence has two complete thoughts: \\"Some board members argue that the greenhouse costs too much to repair\\" and \\"its roof is leaking, and two of its four heaters no longer work.\\" A semicolon can join them. A comma alone can\'t. That\'s the original\'s comma splice. \\"; since\\" looks like a fix, but \\"since\\" makes the second part dependent, and a semicolon must have a complete sentence on both sides. Removing the punctuation is the subtle trap: \\"repair its roof\\" seems to work, but then \\"is leaking\\" is left with no subject.",["A comma splice: \\"its roof is leaking\\" begins a second complete sentence, which a comma alone can\'t join.","Correct: a semicolon joins two complete sentences that are closely related.","\\"Since\\" makes the second part a dependent clause, and a semicolon can\'t introduce one.","Fused sentence: \\"repair its roof\\" now reads as one verb and object, so \\"is leaking\\" has no subject and the sentence falls apart."]],"english-0419":["An estimate is already approximate. The original sounds formal and careful, which makes it tempting, but \\"estimate,\\" \\"approximate,\\" and \\"roughly\\" all mean the same thing. The third choice says the contractor \\"has estimated\\" the cost at \\"about $18,000.\\" That keeps the meaning and drops the repeats. The other two choices also pile on repeated words (\\"approximately... about,\\" \\"estimated... estimated\\").",["Sounds official, but \\"estimate,\\" \\"approximate,\\" and \\"roughly\\" all say the same thing.","\\"Estimated,\\" \\"approximately,\\" and \\"about\\" repeat one idea three times.","Correct: \\"estimated\\" and \\"about\\" together are all the hedging the sentence needs.","Says \\"estimated\\" twice."]],"english-0420":["The essay does open with a bit of history (\\"built in 1974 and... used by science classes ever since\\"), which makes the first Yes tempting. But look at what the essay spends its paragraphs doing: it states a position (\\"The board should keep the greenhouse\\"), gives reasons, answers the cost objection, and ends with a value judgment (\\"That is worth $18,000\\"). That is persuasion, not history. So the answer is No, for the reason in the third choice. The fourth choice says No but gives a false reason.",["Those facts appear, but only in one opening sentence that sets up the argument.","Damage is mentioned to answer an objection about cost, not to tell the building\'s history.","Correct: the essay is an argument about a coming vote, not a history.","False: the parking lot\'s cost appears once, in a comparison."]],"english-0421":["\\"The prickly seed heads of a plant called burdock\\" explains what the burrs are. The sentence works without it (\\"The burrs had clung so tightly...\\"), so it is extra information and needs a matching pair of marks. Two dashes or two commas would both work, but you can\'t mix one of each. The original uses two dashes and nothing inside them. Choices B and C mix a dash with a comma. Choice D keeps the dashes but adds a comma that cuts a phrase in half.",["Correct: a matching pair of dashes sets off the explanation of what the burrs are.","Mismatched pair: an aside opened with a dash must close with a dash, not a comma.","Mixes a comma and a dash, and the extra comma splits \\"a plant\\" from its name.","The comma inside wrongly splits \\"seed heads\\" from \\"of a plant,\\" which tells whose seed heads they are."]],"english-0422":["\\"Convinced that the same trick could fasten clothing\\" describes a person, and that person has to be the subject right after the comma. Only the last choice puts \\"de Mestral\\" there. The other choices are fluent and even mention de Mestral, but their subjects are \\"a fastener,\\" \\"the idea,\\" or \\"his idea.\\" So each one says that a thing was convinced. The \\"his idea\\" choice is the sneakiest, because \\"his\\" points to de Mestral, but a possessive is not the subject.",["Dangling modifier: the fastener can\'t be \\"convinced\\"; the person who was convinced must come right after the comma.","Still dangling: now \\"the idea\\" seems to be convinced.","Still dangling: \\"his\\" names de Mestral, but the subject is \\"his idea,\\" and an idea can\'t be convinced.","Correct: \\"de Mestral\\" follows the opening phrase, so he is the one who was convinced."]],"english-0423":["The paragraph contrasts two materials. Cotton \\"wore out quickly,\\" and nylon \\"worked far better.\\" The bracketed phrase gives the reason: nylon \\"is strong and holds its shape.\\" Without it, the reader learns that nylon was better but not why. The other choices describe things that are in other paragraphs (the burrs, the name) or nowhere in the essay (cost).",["The burrs are in the first paragraph; this phrase is about nylon.","The name comes up in the next paragraph.","Cost is never mentioned.","Correct: cotton \\"wore out quickly,\\" and the phrase says nylon is strong and holds its shape."]],"english-0424":["The list has a clear shape: \\"sneakers for children, jackets for skiers, and ___.\\" Each item is an object, then \\"for,\\" then the people who use it. \\"Tool bags for astronauts\\" fits that shape. The other choices name the same idea but as a clause, a \\"things that\\" phrase, or a reversed order.",["Breaks the pattern: a full clause with a new subject doesn\'t match \\"sneakers for children\\" and \\"jackets for skiers.\\"","Drops the \\"___ for ___\\" form of the first two items.","Correct: \\"tool bags for astronauts\\" matches \\"sneakers for children\\" and \\"jackets for skiers.\\"","Flips the order, putting \\"for astronauts\\" first."]],"english-0425":["The stem is precise: the ending must return to the specific scene in the first paragraph, which is a walk in the Alps and a dog covered in burrs. Only the last choice names that scene. Choice B is the near miss. It connects to the burrs in a general way (learning from nature), but it is a broad lesson, not the scene. Choices A and C are accurate endings that point to the fastener\'s later history instead.",["Adds a new use for the fastener but doesn\'t return to the opening scene.","Tempting, since it gestures at the burrs, but it states a general lesson rather than returning to the specific walk and dog.","A true and fitting final fact, but it looks forward in time instead of back to the opening scene.","Correct: it brings back the exact opening scene, the walk in the Alps and the burr-covered dog."]],"english-0426":["\\"In winter... it carries mostly islanders\\" is a complete clause, and what follows is a list explaining which islanders. A colon is the right mark for that. A semicolon needs a full sentence after it. Pairing a colon with \\"such as\\" in either order doubles up two ways of introducing the same list. Use one or the other, not both.",["Correct: a colon after a complete clause introduces the list of who those islanders are.","A semicolon must be followed by a complete sentence, and the list that follows isn\'t one.","A colon shouldn\'t come before \\"such as\\"; the two do the same job.","A colon shouldn\'t come after \\"such as,\\" which already introduces the examples."]],"english-0427":["The series begins \\"she learned to coil lines, read the wind on the water, and ___.\\" The \\"to\\" before \\"coil\\" carries over to every item, which is why the second item is just \\"read.\\" The third item should match: \\"fix the engine.\\" Repeating \\"to\\" before only the last item makes the series uneven. You can repeat \\"to\\" before every item or before only the first, but not before some. \\"Fixing\\" and \\"how to fix\\" change the form altogether.",["Breaks the pattern: once \\"to\\" appears only before the first item (\\"to coil... read...\\"), adding it again to the last item makes the series uneven.","Correct: \\"to\\" covers the whole series, so \\"coil,\\" \\"read,\\" and \\"fix\\" match.","An -ing form doesn\'t match the plain verbs \\"coil\\" and \\"read.\\"","\\"How to fix\\" is a different kind of phrase from \\"coil\\" and \\"read.\\""]],"english-0428":["The paragraph\'s first sentence introduces storms, and the rest shows how Pelletier handles them: she checks the forecast, cancels crossings in high waves, and has earned the islanders\' trust. A sentence about where and when the boat was built has nothing to do with that. It belongs, if anywhere, in the first paragraph. The second choice is the trap. It is true that the boat appears earlier, but that doesn\'t make the detail fit here.",["The sentence gives the boat\'s age and engine history; it says nothing about how it handles waves.","Background on the boat may be interesting, but this paragraph is about weather and Pelletier\'s judgment.","There\'s no contradiction; the sentence isn\'t about the lake at all.","Correct: the paragraph moves from storms to her caution to the islanders\' trust, and the boat\'s history breaks that line."]],"english-0429":["The goal has two parts: a specific incident, and one that shows the islanders\' trust. Choices B and D show trust but are general statements, with no particular event. Choice C is a specific incident, but it is only about Pelletier, and we don\'t learn how the passengers reacted. Only the original has both: on one particular day she turned back, and \\"not one of the twelve passengers on board complained.\\"",["Correct: it is a specific incident, and the passengers\' silence when she turned back shows their trust.","Shows loyalty over time, but it\'s a general fact, not a specific incident.","A specific incident, but it shows only her caution; nothing in it shows the passengers\' trust.","Shows the islanders\' faith in her, but it\'s a general belief, not a specific incident."]],"english-0430":["The essay describes Pelletier in plain, standard English. \\"Has not been decided\\" says the same thing in that voice. \\"TBD\\" is a shorthand for texts and schedules, and \\"a total toss-up\\" is slang. The third choice goes too far the other way. It sounds formal, but it is stiff and wordy (\\"remains a question whose resolution is yet to be determined\\") in a way the rest of the essay never is.",["\\"TBD\\" is a texting or memo abbreviation, too casual for the essay\'s voice.","\\"A total toss-up\\" is slangy.","Too stiff and wordy; it also repeats itself (\\"remains... yet to be\\").","Correct: clear and plain, like the rest of the profile."]],"english-0431":["\\"Its riders\\" needs something that has riders. The only such thing is the Pony Express, and it isn\'t named until the paragraph\'s last sentence, \\"The Pony Express had begun.\\" So the new sentence must come after it, at Point D. \\"Would carry\\" fits there too: it looks ahead from the start of the service. At Point B, \\"its\\" would try to point back to \\"a rider,\\" and a rider can\'t have riders. At Point C, the nearest singular nouns are the trip and California.",["Nothing has been named yet, so \\"Its\\" has no antecedent.","\\"Its riders\\" can\'t refer to \\"a rider,\\" and the service hasn\'t been named yet.","\\"Its\\" would seem to refer to California or the trip; the Pony Express still hasn\'t been named.","Correct: \\"Its\\" refers to the Pony Express, named in the sentence just before, and \\"would carry\\" looks ahead from its beginning."]],"english-0432":["\\"A leather cover that fit over the saddle and had four pockets for letters\\" explains what a mochila is. It is extra information, so it needs commas on both sides. Here the closing comma is the same one that comes before \\"so,\\" which joins two complete clauses. The original has both commas. Choice B drops the opening one, and D drops the closing one. Choice C adds a comma before \\"that,\\" which splits the essential \\"that\\" clause from \\"a leather cover.\\"",["Correct: a pair of commas sets off the definition of \\"mochila,\\" and the second comma also comes before \\"so,\\" which joins the two clauses.","Missing the comma that opens the explanation of what a mochila is.","The comma before \\"that\\" cuts off the clause that tells which leather cover is meant.","Without a comma before \\"so,\\" the explanation is never closed, and the two clauses run together."]],"english-0433":["The next sentence tells us what the Pony Express lacked: it charged high prices but \\"still lost large sums of money.\\" The precise word is \\"profitable,\\" which means making more than it costs. The other choices are positive words that mean something broader. \\"Valuable\\" and \\"beneficial\\" would say it was worthless or helped no one, which the essay doesn\'t claim (it was fast and is still famous). \\"Rewarding\\" is about personal satisfaction, not money.",["\\"Never valuable\\" says the service was worthless, but it was fast and important; the problem was money.","Correct: \\"profitable\\" means it made more money than it spent, and the next sentence says it \\"lost large sums.\\"","\\"Never beneficial\\" claims it helped no one, which conflicts with its speed and fame.","\\"Rewarding\\" usually means personally satisfying, which isn\'t what the money-losing details describe."]],"english-0434":["The sentence contrasts ten days with the telegraph\'s speed. \\"Almost instantly\\" says that completely. The original looks formal and careful, which is the trap, but it turns two words into eight. The other two choices add a second phrase (\\"in very little time,\\" \\"very quickly\\") that repeats the first.",["Sounds formal, but \\"in a span of time that was nearly instantaneous\\" takes eight words to say \\"almost instantly.\\"","\\"In very little time\\" repeats \\"almost instantly.\\"","Correct: two words, with nothing repeated and nothing lost.","\\"Very quickly\\" repeats \\"almost instantly.\\""]],"english-0435":["To compare the Pony Express with other mail services, the essay would need to describe those services: stagecoaches, ships, or other routes. It never mentions any. It describes the Pony Express\'s start, how its relays worked, its money troubles, and its end, so the answer is No. The second choice is the strongest trap, because the telegraph is compared with the riders. But the telegraph carried messages, not mail, and it is what ended the Pony Express, not one of the services of its time.",["The essay gives the Pony Express\'s speed but never compares it with any other mail service.","The telegraph sent messages, not mail, and it came at the end; it\'s not one of the mail services of the time.","Correct: stagecoaches, ships, and other mail routes never come up.","False: the telegraph appears only in the last paragraph."]],"english-0436":["Ask whether the \\"who\\" clause is extra or needed. It is needed. The sentence isn\'t about workers in general; it is about the workers who came up too quickly, and the clause tells us which ones. An essential clause takes no commas, so the plain-looking original is right. Choice B is the trap. Paired commas look tidy, but they turn the clause into a side remark and change the meaning. Choice C puts a comma between the subject and its verb. Choice D opens a pair it never closes.",["Correct: \\"who came up too quickly\\" tells which workers got sick, so it is essential and takes no commas.","The commas make the clause extra information, so the sentence would claim that many workers got sick and, by the way, they all came up too quickly. The clause is what identifies them.","A single comma here splits the subject (\\"Many workers who came up too quickly from the caissons\\") from its verb, \\"suffered.\\"","Opens an aside with a comma but never closes it."]],"english-0437":["\\"From there\\" has to point to a place, and the place is \\"a window of his house in Brooklyn Heights.\\" Point B comes right after that sentence and before the paragraph turns to Emily. At Point C, it would interrupt Emily\'s part. \\"Became his link to the bridge\\" is explained by the next sentence (she carried his instructions), so nothing should come between them. At Point D, it would split two sentences about Emily, and \\"She also studied\\" needs to follow her other work. At Point A, \\"there\\" would point back to the site, which he could no longer visit.",["\\"From there\\" needs a place to point to, and the only place just mentioned is the site he could no longer visit.","Correct: \\"From there\\" points to the window of his house, named in the sentence just before.","This splits \\"became his link to the bridge\\" from the next sentence, which explains how she was that link.","This breaks up the account of Emily\'s work, which continues with \\"She also studied.\\""]],"english-0438":["The preposition \\"to\\" makes \\"whomever\\" sound right, but look at the clause that follows: \\"___ was running the work.\\" The pronoun is the subject of \\"was running,\\" so it takes the subject form, \\"whoever.\\" The whole clause, \\"whoever was running the work,\\" is what serves as the object of \\"to.\\" \\"Whom\\" and \\"those whom\\" make the same mistake as \\"whomever\\": an object form where a subject is needed.",["Correct: the whole clause \\"whoever was running the work\\" is the object of \\"to,\\" and inside it, \\"whoever\\" is the subject of \\"was running.\\"","\\"Whomever\\" looks right after \\"to,\\" but the pronoun is the subject of \\"was running,\\" so it must be \\"whoever.\\"","An object form, and it leaves \\"was running\\" with no subject: \\"to whom was running the work.\\"","\\"Whom\\" still can\'t be the subject of \\"was running.\\""]],"english-0439":["\\"The first carriage ride across the bridge\\" already says no one rode across before her. \\"The earliest one ever,\\" \\"and earliest,\\" and \\"before any other\\" just say it again.",["\\"The earliest one ever\\" repeats \\"first.\\"","Correct: says she took the first ride, once.","\\"First\\" and \\"earliest\\" mean the same thing.","\\"Very first\\" and \\"before any other\\" say the same thing twice."]],"english-0440":["The first Yes is tempting because it is accurate. The essay does explain the caissons. But one sentence of method doesn\'t make the essay about methods. Look at what each paragraph centers on: John Roebling\'s death, Washington\'s illness, Emily\'s role as his link, and her ride across the bridge. The essay is about a family seeing a project through, so the answer is No. The last choice gives a false reason, since the towers are mentioned twice.",["True, but the caissons get one sentence, and mainly to explain how Washington Roebling got sick.","That sentence is about Emily\'s knowledge, not about how the cables were actually built.","Correct: each paragraph centers on a Roebling, and the engineering appears only as background to their story.","False: the towers come up in the second and third paragraphs."]],"english-0441":["\\"The soil there is poor in nutrients, so the plant gets some of what it needs another way\\" is a complete sentence. \\"It catches insects\\" is another. A comma alone can\'t join them, and no punctuation is a run-on. A period fixes it.",["A comma splice: \\"It catches insects\\" is a complete sentence and can\'t be joined to the first with only a comma.","A run-on: the two sentences run together with no punctuation.","Correct: a period separates the two complete sentences.","Still a comma splice, and \\"is catching\\" is an awkward tense."]],"english-0442":["This sentence is inverted: it opens with a place (\\"On the inner surface of each lobe\\") and puts the verb before the subject. Flip it to find the subject: \\"Usually three tiny trigger hairs stand on the inner surface of each lobe.\\" The subject, \\"hairs,\\" is plural, so \\"stand\\" is right. The singular nouns at the front (\\"surface,\\" \\"lobe\\") make \\"stands\\" sound right, which is the trap.",["Correct: in this inverted sentence, the subject is \\"three tiny trigger hairs,\\" which is plural, so the verb is \\"stand.\\"","Agrees with \\"surface\\" or \\"lobe,\\" which come first, but the real subject comes after the verb.","Singular, and the present perfect doesn\'t fit a description of how the plant is built.","Singular; it still treats \\"surface\\" or \\"lobe\\" as the subject."]],"english-0443":["\\"Bent only once\\" describes a trigger hair, the thing the previous sentence introduced. So \\"a trigger hair\\" has to come right after the comma, and in the original it does. The other choices all report the same facts in natural-sounding sentences, but their subjects are the trap, the plant, or the signal. None of those gets bent. The original looks plain next to them, but it is the only one where the opening phrase describes the right thing.",["Correct: a trigger hair is what gets bent, and it comes right after the opening phrase.","Dangling: now the trap seems to be \\"bent only once,\\" but the hairs are what bend.","Dangling: the plant as a whole isn\'t bent; one of its hairs is.","Dangling: now the signal seems to be what was bent."]],"english-0444":["The goal asks for a reason: why does the trap wait for a second touch? Only the last choice gives one. A single touch could be a raindrop or falling debris, and closing on those would waste energy. Each other choice is accurate and related. The speed of closing and the plant\'s \\"counting\\" describe the behavior, not its purpose. The limit on how many times a trap can close is the near miss: it suggests closing is costly, but it never ties that to waiting for a second touch.",["About how fast the trap closes, not why it waits for a second touch.","Hints that closing is costly, but it doesn\'t connect that cost to the two-touch rule.","Names the behavior but doesn\'t explain the reason for it.","Correct: it gives the reason for waiting: one touch might be rain or debris, and a false alarm would waste energy."]],"english-0445":["The plant makes digestive juices to break down its prey. The word for producing and releasing a useful substance is \\"secrete.\\" \\"Excrete\\" is the near-twin trap: it also means releasing something from the body, but as waste. \\"Exhale\\" applies to breath, and \\"evacuate\\" means emptying out or leaving.",["\\"Excrete\\" means to get rid of waste, but the digestive juices are made for a purpose, not discarded.","Correct: to \\"secrete\\" is to produce and release a useful substance, as glands do with digestive juices.","\\"Exhale\\" means to breathe out, which applies to air, not liquids.","\\"Evacuate\\" means to empty out or move people away from danger, which doesn\'t fit making digestive juices."]],"english-0446":["There are two complete sentences here: \\"Most customers arrive expecting a repair\\" and \\"they leave having made one themselves.\\" \\"However\\" signals the contrast, but it is not a joining word like \\"but,\\" so commas alone around it make a comma splice. The fix is a semicolon before \\"however\\" and a comma after it. Choice B drops that comma, and choice D drops the mark before \\"however\\" and is still a comma splice.",["A comma splice: \\"however\\" is not a conjunction, so commas alone can\'t join the two complete sentences.","The semicolon is right, but \\"however\\" needs a comma after it when it begins a clause this way.","Correct: a semicolon joins the two complete sentences, and a comma follows \\"however.\\"","Still joins two complete sentences with only a comma, and it drops the needed mark before \\"however.\\""]],"english-0447":["The paragraph describes Hollis\'s method: questions first (what do you hear, where does the chain slip), then the part and the tool. The bracketed sentence explains why she starts with questions. Problems \\"announce themselves if you listen.\\" Without it, her questions have no stated reason. Choice A is the trap: it sounds close, but the sentence gives the reason for her questions; it never claims she can diagnose a bike by sound alone.",["Overstates it: the sentence never says Hollis diagnoses bikes by sound alone, and her questions cover more than sound (where the chain slips).","The sentence says nothing about how many problems a bike has.","No repair is described in the sentence.","Correct: problems \\"announce themselves if you listen,\\" which is why her first step is asking what they hear and feel."]],"english-0448":["The goal names a specific group: people beyond the shop\'s own customers. Every choice is about donated bikes, but only the original reaches outside the shop. Repaired bikes are \\"given to people who can\'t afford one,\\" including about sixty students. The parts count and the sorting bins show how the shop runs, and the bikes\' age is just a detail. None of them shows anyone outside the shop being helped.",["Correct: the bikes go to people who can\'t afford one, such as sixty students, who aren\'t shop customers fixing their own bikes.","About the bikes\' age, not about who they help.","Shows how much the shop gets from donations, not who else benefits.","Describes how the shop stays organized, not who benefits."]],"english-0449":["The essay is a clear, friendly profile in standard English. \\"Puts them at ease\\" fits that voice. Each wrong choice misses in a different way. \\"Talks them off the ledge\\" is a slangy exaggeration, \\"no big deal\\" is chatty, and \\"assuages their trepidation\\" swings the other way into stiff wording that no other sentence in the essay uses. Watch for the formal-sounding choice. Being formal is not the same as matching the essay.",["A slangy, exaggerated idiom: it treats a nervous beginner like someone in a crisis.","Formal-sounding, but stiffer than anything else in the essay\'s plain voice.","Too casual: \\"no big deal\\" reads like conversation, not like the rest of the profile.","Correct: a plain, standard phrase that fits the essay\'s voice and the gentle joke that follows."]],"english-0450":["This paragraph is about how Hollis helps nervous customers fix their own bikes. A fact about how Ferry Street got its name has nothing to do with that, so the writer should not add it.",["The shop\'s street is named in the first paragraph; the street\'s history doesn\'t belong here.","It\'s a true detail, but it has nothing to do with the paragraph.","There\'s no contradiction; it\'s just off topic.","Correct: the paragraph is about nervous customers learning to do repairs, and the street\'s name is off topic."]],"english-0451":["The subject is \\"The maples,\\" which is plural, so the verb must be plural too: \\"The maples grow.\\" Each of the other choices uses a singular verb (\\"grows,\\" \\"is,\\" \\"has\\").",["\\"Grows\\" is singular, but the subject, \\"The maples,\\" is plural.","Correct: the plural subject \\"maples\\" takes the plural verb \\"grow.\\"","Singular: \\"is\\" doesn\'t agree with \\"maples.\\"","Singular: \\"has\\" doesn\'t agree with \\"maples.\\""]],"english-0452":["\\"Beneath it\\" already says where the bucket hung: under the spout. Adding \\"under the spout,\\" \\"and under it,\\" or \\"underneath the spout\\" repeats the same idea.",["\\"Under the spout\\" repeats \\"beneath it.\\"","Correct: says where the bucket hung, once.","\\"Beneath\\" and \\"under\\" mean the same thing.","\\"Below it\\" and \\"underneath the spout\\" say the same thing twice."]],"english-0453":["\\"Not only... but also\\" sets up a pair, and the two halves must have the same form. The first half is a noun, \\"patience,\\" so the second half must be a noun too: \\"a strong back.\\" Read it whole: \\"The job required not only patience but also a strong back.\\" The other choices all make sense, but each one swaps in a clause, a phrase with \\"for someone,\\" or an -ing form. That breaks the pairing, even though they sound natural.",["Correct: \\"a strong back\\" is a noun phrase, just like \\"patience,\\" so the two halves match.","A long phrase that doesn\'t match the single noun \\"patience.\\"","An -ing phrase doesn\'t match the noun \\"patience.\\"","Breaks the \\"not only... but also\\" pairing: a noun (\\"patience\\") is paired with a full clause."]],"english-0454":["Track the process and the time clues. The paragraph should describe sap (1), then the boiling (2). Next comes the water steaming away \\"late in the evening\\" (4). Then \\"By then\\" (3) refers to that late-evening moment, when only syrup is left. The forty-to-one rule (5) sums it up. In the original order, the pan holds syrup before the water has steamed away. Starting with Sentence 2 leaves \\"it\\" without an antecedent, and putting 4 before 2 has the water boil off before the fire is lit.",["The thin layer of syrup comes before the water steams away, which reverses the process.","Correct: boil for hours, the water is gone by late evening, \\"By then\\" only syrup is left, and the general rule closes the paragraph.","\\"To turn it into syrup\\" comes before sap is named, so \\"it\\" has nothing to refer to.","The water steams away before any boiling begins."]],"english-0455":["The setup is \\"my grandfather handed me the drill and stood back.\\" A conclusion about a new role should show the narrator doing the job. Only the last choice does: \\"I drilled the first hole myself.\\" Its last words, \\"where he had once shown me how,\\" also reach back to the first year. The original shows the narrator still watching, which ignores the handoff. The family-history and harvest choices are relevant but don\'t show the narrator\'s new role.",["Shows the narrator still only watching, the opposite of a new role.","Adds family history, but it\'s about the grandfather, not a change in the narrator\'s role.","About the harvest, not about what the narrator now does.","Correct: the narrator now drills the first hole, the job the grandfather used to do, in the same tree where the narrator first learned."]],"english-0456":["\\"It has no bike lanes\\" is a complete sentence, and \\"Riders squeeze between parked cars and moving traffic\\" is another. A comma alone can\'t join them, and no punctuation at all is a run-on. A period fixes it. Adding \\"who\\" leaves the second part without a verb of its own, so it no longer says what the riders do.",["A comma splice: \\"It has no bike lanes\\" and \\"riders squeeze...\\" are both complete sentences.","Correct: a period separates the two complete sentences.","A run-on: the two sentences crash together with no punctuation.","Adding \\"who\\" leaves \\"riders who squeeze between parked cars\\" with no verb of its own, so it can only hang off \\"lanes\\" as if the riders were the lanes, which makes no sense."]],"english-0457":["Read both sentences together. \\"Protected lanes do cost more than painted ones\\" is a concession, the writer admitting a drawback. The next sentence answers it: the price is about $120,000, \\"less than a tenth\\" of a recent repaving. That\'s a turn against the concession, so the transition must show contrast: \\"Even so.\\" \\"For example\\" is the trap. A price could be an example of cost, but this one is offered to show the cost is small, not large.",["Treats the price as an example of lanes costing more, but the sentence shows the price is small.","Signals a result, but a higher cost doesn\'t lead to a low price.","Signals a likeness, but the low price pushes against the point before it.","Correct: the writer admits the lanes cost more, then shows that the actual price is still small."]],"english-0458":["Should \\"Maya Chen\\" be set off with commas? Only if the sentence already tells us exactly who is meant without it. \\"The eighth grader\\" doesn\'t. Dunmore Falls has two schools full of eighth graders, so the name is what identifies her. An essential name takes no commas, so the plain original is right. The paired commas in C look polished, which is the trap, but they would mean \\"the one eighth grader, who happens to be named Maya Chen.\\" B and D each use a single comma, which either splits the subject from its verb or opens an aside that never closes.",["Correct: there are many eighth graders, so the name is needed to say which one, and an essential name takes no commas.","A single comma splits the subject (\\"the eighth grader Maya Chen\\") from its verb, \\"collected.\\"","Paired commas make the name extra information, as if the town had only one eighth grader and the name were just a bonus detail.","Opens an aside with a comma but never closes it, so \\"Maya Chen collected\\" reads as a separate clause."]],"english-0459":["The paragraph\'s first sentence sets its topic: \\"Students have already asked for the lanes.\\" The next sentences describe their petition. A fact about when the library was built and its clock tower has nothing to do with that, so the writer should not add it.",["The library is mentioned earlier, but its age and clock tower have nothing to do with students asking for lanes.","Interesting isn\'t enough; it doesn\'t support the paragraph\'s point.","There\'s no contradiction; it\'s just off topic.","Correct: the paragraph is about the students\' petition, and the library\'s history doesn\'t connect to it."]],"english-0460":["The first Yes is tempting because the essay really does describe what a protected lane is: a curb or posts instead of paint. But describing what something is differs from explaining how it is built, and the essay never covers construction. Every paragraph pushes one argument: Main Street is dangerous, protected lanes work and are affordable, and students have asked for them. So the answer is No, because the description serves the argument. The last choice says No for a false reason.",["True, but that one description serves the argument; the essay never explains how a lane is actually built.","A price isn\'t a building method.","Correct: the essay is a case for one project, not a how-to explanation.","False: the crashes come up in one sentence."]],"english-0461":["\\"Luggage\\" is a singular noun (you say \\"my luggage is heavy,\\" not \\"are\\"). The sentence is about the past, so the verb is \\"was.\\" The other choices are plural, and two of them also have the wrong tense.",["\\"Were\\" is plural, but \\"luggage\\" is singular.","Correct: \\"luggage\\" is singular, so it takes \\"was.\\"","Plural, and present tense in a story set in 1889.","Plural, and the wrong tense for a past event."]],"english-0462":["\\"Traveling by steamship and train\\" describes Bly, so \\"Bly\\" must be the subject right after the comma, as it is in the original. Choice C is the sneaky one. \\"Bly\'s\\" appears right after the comma, but it is a possessive, and the subject is \\"route.\\" A route doesn\'t travel by train. Choices B and D make \\"the route\\" or \\"the direction\\" the subject. Only the original makes Bly the subject, and it also fits the rest of the sentence (\\"Bly headed east..., stopping in England, France...\\").",["Correct: \\"Bly\\" is the subject, so she is the one traveling by steamship and train.","Still dangling: the subject is \\"the route.\\"","Dangling: the subject is \\"route,\\" not Bly, so the route seems to be traveling by steamship and train. \\"Bly\'s\\" is only a possessive.","Still dangling: the subject is \\"the direction.\\""]],"english-0463":["The previous sentence says Bly learned in Hong Kong \\"that she was in a race.\\" The next sentence should explain how that race came about, and only the third choice does. A rival magazine sent a rival reporter, the same day, in the opposite direction. The other choices are all true and connected to the trip. The contest is about readers guessing, the book comes after the trip, and Hong Kong is only the place. None of them explains the race.",["A true detail about the trip\'s publicity, but a guessing contest isn\'t a race against anyone.","Mentions Bisland, but it never explains that she was racing Bly, or how the race began.","Correct: a rival reporter set out the same day in the other direction, which is what made it a race.","About where she learned the news, not why there was a race."]],"english-0464":["The sentence needs to say one thing: she got back seventy-two days after she left. The second choice does that plainly. The original is the trap, because it sounds formal and exact, but \\"total elapsed time... from the date of her departure\\" piles up words that all mean \\"since she left.\\" The other two choices also pad the idea (\\"first... at the start,\\" \\"a total of... had gone by since\\").",["Sounds official, but \\"total,\\" \\"elapsed,\\" and \\"from the date of her departure\\" just restate the time since she left.","Correct: gives the time and its starting point in seven words.","\\"First\\" and \\"at the start\\" repeat \\"set out.\\"","Wordy: \\"a total of\\" and \\"had gone by since the day of\\" stretch out a simple idea."]],"english-0465":["All four words come from joining things, but only \\"cemented\\" also has the figurative meaning the sentence needs: to make firm and lasting, as in \\"cemented a friendship\\" or \\"cemented her reputation.\\" \\"Fastened,\\" \\"glued,\\" and \\"welded\\" stay literal. You fasten a coat, glue paper, and weld steel, but none of them strengthens a reputation.",["\\"Fastened\\" means attached physically, as with a clip or a button; it has no sense of making fame secure.","\\"Glued\\" is literal, about sticking things together, so \\"glued Bly\'s fame\\" doesn\'t make sense.","Correct: \\"cemented\\" can mean made firm and lasting, which is what the trip did to her fame.","\\"Welded\\" means joined metal with heat; it isn\'t used to mean strengthening a reputation."]],"english-0466":["This sentence is inverted: it starts with a prepositional phrase (\\"Among the oil\'s drawbacks\\"), then the verb, then the subject. Flip it to find the subject: \\"Its thickness was among the oil\'s drawbacks.\\" The subject is \\"thickness,\\" which is singular, so the verb is \\"was.\\" The trap is that every noun before the verb is plural (\\"drawbacks,\\" \\"doctors,\\" \\"countries,\\" \\"years\\"), so \\"were\\" sounds right. \\"Have been\\" is also plural and shifts the tense, and \\"being\\" leaves the sentence with no main verb.",["\\"Were\\" agrees with the plural nouns before it (\\"drawbacks,\\" \\"doctors,\\" \\"countries\\"), but the subject comes after the verb: \\"its thickness.\\"","Correct: the sentence is inverted, and its subject, \\"its thickness,\\" is singular, so it takes \\"was.\\"","Plural, and the present perfect doesn\'t fit a sentence about the early 1900s.","\\"Being\\" is not a main verb, so the sentence becomes a fragment."]],"english-0467":["A comparison has to put like things side by side. The sentence compares how readily the esters were absorbed with how readily the raw oil had been absorbed, so the esters are compared with the raw oil. \\"Than that of the raw oil\\" sounds formal, but \\"that\\" must stand for a singular noun earlier in the sentence (as in \\"the cost of oil is higher than that of water\\"), and the only noun being compared is the plural \\"esters.\\" \\"The raw oil\'s\\" compares the esters with something belonging to the oil. \\"Is\\" switches to present tense. Only the original compares the right things in the right tense.",["Correct: it compares the esters with the raw oil, and \\"had been\\" correctly places the raw oil\'s use before the esters\'.","\\"That\\" has to stand for a singular noun already in the sentence, and there isn\'t one; the sentence ends up comparing the esters with \\"that of the raw oil.\\"","The possessive compares the esters with something that belongs to the raw oil, not with the oil itself.","Present tense in a passage about events in the 1910s; it also implies the raw oil is still being injected."]],"english-0468":["Read what comes right after Point A: \\"In 1922, however, Hollmann published a paper that called it the \'Ball method.\'\\" That \\"however\\" answers the sentence before it, Dean taking the credit. The paragraph is about who got credit for Ball\'s work. If the new sentence goes in, \\"however\\" would follow a sentence about sulfone drugs, and the paragraph would jump to the 1940s and back to 1922. The first choice is the strongest trap: the fact is true and on the general topic, but the paragraph doesn\'t need it, and here it breaks the link between the claim and its correction. The last choice has the right decision for a false reason, since being replaced in the 1940s doesn\'t mean the method failed.",["The detail is true and interesting, but at Point A it pulls the paragraph away from the credit dispute, and the \\"however\\" in the next sentence would then seem to answer the sulfone drugs.","The sentence says nothing about Dean or about how he claimed the method.","Correct: the paragraph moves from Dean\'s claim directly to Hollmann\'s correction (\\"however\\"); the sentence would sit between them and change what \\"however\\" seems to contrast with.","Right decision, wrong reason: a treatment replaced by better drugs decades later was still a success, so there\'s no contradiction."]],"english-0469":["The goal has two parts: the way Ball has been honored, and the situation from the first paragraph (patients sent to Kalaupapa with only chaulmoogra oil to treat them). The last choice does both. It begins with the plaque and explains that the tree beside it once supplied the remedy those patients were offered. The original mentions the tree but leaves the reader to make the connection. The Alice Ball Day choice covers only the honor, and the 1969 choice covers only the first paragraph\'s situation, with nothing about Ball.",["Names the tree, which hints at a link, but never connects it to the patients or the remedy described in the first paragraph.","Stays with how Ball is honored and never reaches back to the first paragraph.","Returns to the first paragraph\'s situation but drops Ball\'s recognition entirely.","Correct: it starts with the plaque (how she is honored) and ties the tree back to Kalaupapa and the only remedy patients had (the first paragraph)."]],"english-0470":["The shortest choice is usually right on concision questions, but not when it cuts something the passage needs. The next sentence says Dean \\"published them.\\" \\"Them\\" has to point back to a plural noun, and that noun is \\"her results.\\" \\"Before publishing\\" removes it, so \\"them\\" no longer refers to anything. \\"Before she could publish her results\\" keeps that noun and nothing extra. The original and the last choice say the same thing with padding (\\"get... published in print,\\" \\"published by her in a printed journal\\").",["Wordy: \\"had been able to get... published,\\" \\"her own,\\" and \\"in print\\" stretch a simple idea.","Shortest, but it drops \\"her results,\\" and the next sentence\'s \\"published them\\" then has nothing to refer to.","Correct: short and complete, and it gives \\"them\\" in the next sentence its antecedent, \\"her results.\\"","Wordy and passive: \\"could be published by her\\" and \\"printed journal\\" add nothing."]],"english-0471":["The subject here is the entire clause \\"That a wild bird... would learn to recognize the calls of human hunters.\\" A clause used as a subject counts as one thing, like \\"This fact strikes many biologists as remarkable.\\" So the verb is the singular \\"strikes.\\" The trap is that the words right before the verb, \\"calls of human hunters,\\" are plural. \\"Have struck\\" is plural too. \\"Striking\\" leaves the sentence without a main verb.",["\\"Strike\\" agrees with the plural nouns near it (\\"calls,\\" \\"hunters\\"), but the subject is the whole \\"That...\\" clause, which is singular.","Correct: a clause beginning with \\"That\\" acts as a single noun, so it takes the singular \\"strikes.\\"","Still plural; the tense change doesn\'t fix the agreement error.","\\"Striking\\" is not a main verb, so the long \\"That...\\" clause never gets a verb and the sentence is a fragment."]],"english-0472":["The new sentence begins with \\"This,\\" so it must follow the thing that let researchers test birds on an unfamiliar call. Sentence 3 describes that: the researchers played both the whistle and the trill in both countries, so the Mozambican birds heard a Hadza whistle and the Tanzanian birds heard a Yao trill. Point C puts the new sentence right after it, before the results in Sentence 4. Point B is tempting because Sentence 2 mentions both calls, but it describes what the Hadza do, not anything the researchers did. Point D puts an explanation of the method after its results.",["No researchers or calls have been mentioned yet, so \\"This\\" has nothing to point to.","Here \\"This\\" would refer to the Hadza using a whistle, which is a custom, not something the researchers did.","Correct: \\"This\\" refers to playing both calls in both countries, which is exactly what exposed birds to a call from the other region.","Sentence 4 is a result; a result can\'t \\"let\\" the researchers test the birds, and the method would be explained after its outcome."]],"english-0473":["The goal has two parts: the bird waits for the hunters, and it does so repeatedly. Only the last choice has both. \\"Perches, and calls until the hunters catch up\\" is the waiting, and \\"repeating the pattern\\" makes it happen more than once. The tail-feather choice is the strongest trap: it shows the bird paying attention to the hunters, but a bird can flash its feathers while flying steadily ahead. The original gives a distance, and the \\"winding route\\" choice gives a shape. Neither one shows the bird stopping.",["Tells how far the nest may be, but describes one flight with no waiting.","Shows the bird helping the hunters follow, but never shows it stopping to wait for them.","Describes the route\'s shape, not any pause for the hunters.","Correct: \\"perches, and calls until the hunters catch up\\" is waiting, and \\"repeating the pattern\\" shows that it happens again and again."]],"english-0474":["The sentence explains why the leftover wax is a good payment for the bird: almost no other animal can get food out of it. That is what \\"digest\\" means: break food down so the body can use it. \\"Ingest\\" is the close sound-alike trap, but it only means swallow, and swallowing wax is easy. \\"Devour\\" is about how eagerly something is eaten, and \\"dissolve\\" is about melting into a liquid.",["\\"Ingest\\" just means take into the body; any animal can swallow wax, so this doesn\'t explain why the wax is a reward only for the honeyguide.","Correct: \\"digest\\" means break down and use as food, which is what makes the wax worth having and leaves the bird without competition for it.","\\"Devour\\" means eat eagerly; it says nothing about whether the wax can be used as food.","\\"Dissolve\\" means break up in a liquid, which isn\'t something an animal does to its food in this sense."]],"english-0475":["Find where the interruption starts. After \\"one of three sounds,\\" a dash opens a list: the traditional call, a hunter speaking Yao words, or a dove\'s call. That list contains its own commas, including the one before \\"a bird common in the area,\\" so a comma after \\"area\\" looks natural. But a dash pair has to match, so the list must close with a dash. Without the second dash, the reader can\'t tell where the list ends. Read without the list, the sentence works: \\"the researchers used a loudspeaker to play one of three sounds and then watched...\\"",["The list of sounds opens with a dash, so it has to close with a dash; a comma leaves the dash unpaired.","With no closing mark, the dash still has no partner, and \\"area and then watched\\" runs the list into the rest of the sentence.","A semicolon can\'t close a dash, and what follows it (\\"and then watched...\\") is not an independent clause.","Correct: the dash after \\"sounds\\" opens the list, so a second dash must close it before the sentence continues with \\"and then watched.\\""]],"english-0476":["There are two goals: set the panel apart from the abandoned items, and show that it is still in use. The third choice does both. \\"Though\\" and \\"on purpose\\" contrast it with the things that were \\"simply abandoned,\\" and \\"it is still doing its job\\" shows it is in use. The original does only the first part. The second choice does only the second part, and it actually files the panel \\"among\\" the trash. The last choice tells how the panel was made, not whether it still works.",["Sets the object apart from the trash with \\"on purpose,\\" but says nothing about whether it still works.","Says the panel is still used, but \\"Among those objects\\" groups it with the abandoned trash instead of setting it apart.","Correct: \\"though... on purpose\\" sets it apart from the abandoned items, and \\"still doing its job\\" says it is in use.","Contrasts it with the trash in a way, but gives no hint that it is still working."]],"english-0477":["An opening phrase like \\"Placed on level ground near the lunar module\\" describes the subject that comes right after the comma. The thing that was placed was the panel, so the panel must be the subject. The second choice does that. It uses the passive voice (\\"was tilted by the astronauts\\"), which sounds weaker, and that is why many students skip it. The original and the third choice make the astronauts the subject. The last choice makes \\"job\\" the subject; \\"astronauts\'\\" is only a possessive.",["Dangling: the astronauts weren\'t \\"placed on level ground near the lunar module\\"; the panel was.","Correct: \\"the panel\\" comes right after the comma, so it is the thing placed on level ground.","Still dangling: the subject is again \\"the astronauts\\"; moving \\"the panel\\" later doesn\'t fix it.","Dangling: the subject is \\"job,\\" and a job can\'t be placed on the ground. \\"Astronauts\'\\" is only a possessive."]],"english-0478":["The bracketed phrase gives the reason for the claim just before it: timing the round trip \\"gives the distance to the Moon\\" because light always travels at the same known speed. If you know the speed and the time, you know the distance. The second choice is the main trap. It is about the same sentence and the same idea of time, but a fixed speed doesn\'t explain why the trip takes about two and a half seconds; the distance does. The other choices describe ideas from other sentences in the paragraph.",["The spreading is described later in the paragraph, and the light\'s speed doesn\'t explain it.","A fixed speed alone doesn\'t set the time; the distance does. The phrase explains the method, not the number.","The prisms\' job is described in the sentence before; the bracketed phrase is about light\'s speed, not reflection.","Correct: because light\'s speed is fixed and known, a measured time can be turned directly into a distance."]],"english-0479":["The sentence says the prisms send light \\"straight back toward the laser.\\" Sending light back is reflecting it, so \\"reflect\\" is the precise word, and the device\'s full name is a laser ranging retroreflector. \\"Refract\\" is the trap because prisms are known for bending light, but bending changes a beam\'s direction as it passes through; it doesn\'t return the beam to where it came from. \\"Deflect\\" means turn aside, and \\"diffuse\\" means scatter, which would spread the light out instead of returning it.",["\\"Refract\\" means bend light as it passes from one material into another; bending alone can\'t send light straight back to its source. The word \\"prisms\\" makes it tempting.","\\"Deflect\\" means turn something aside, the opposite of sending it straight back.","Correct: to \\"reflect\\" light is to send it back, and \\"straight back toward the laser\\" describes reflection.","\\"Diffuse\\" means spread light out in many directions, which would scatter it instead of returning it."]],"english-0480":["A colon is correct when the words before it form a complete sentence and the words after it explain or list. \\"Over more than fifty years, their measurements have helped settle questions that no one in 1969 could have answered\\" is complete, and the three \\"whether/how\\" questions follow. So the original is right. \\"Such as:\\" is a classic error, because a colon can\'t come right after \\"such as.\\" The semicolon needs a full clause after it, and the list isn\'t one. \\"These are whether...\\" adds a subject and verb, which makes a comma splice.",["Correct: the colon follows a complete clause (\\"their measurements have helped settle questions...\\") and introduces the list of those questions.","A colon can\'t follow \\"such as,\\" because \\"such as\\" leaves the words before the colon incomplete.","A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and the list that follows (\\"whether the Moon has a liquid core...\\") isn\'t one.","Comma splice: \\"these are whether...\\" is a new independent clause joined to the first with only a comma."]],"english-0481":["Read the sentences on both sides. Before: on its own, the pattern \\"might have meant little.\\" After: a star can look bright because it is powerful or just because it is close. The second sentence explains the first: if brightness can come from either cause, a link between brightness and cycle length proves nothing by itself. A transition that adds support is needed, and \\"Indeed\\" does that. \\"However\\" is the trap, because the paragraph does turn to a contrast, but that turn comes in the next sentence, with \\"But.\\" \\"As a result\\" gets the cause and effect backward, and \\"Afterward\\" treats a general fact as a later event.",["\\"However\\" signals a contrast, but this sentence supports the one before it: it explains why the pattern alone might have meant little.","Reverses the logic: the pattern meaning little doesn\'t cause stars to look bright for two reasons; it\'s the other way around.","A time word, but the sentence states a general fact, not a later event.","Correct: \\"Indeed\\" introduces support for the previous claim, and the sentence explains why the pattern alone might have meant little."]],"english-0482":["The sentence measures a span of time up to a moment in the past: from Leavitt\'s death to the 1925 announcement. When something had already been true for a while at a past moment, English uses the past perfect, \\"had been.\\" \\"By the time\\" plus \\"for more than three years\\" is the signal. The simple past, \\"was dead,\\" doesn\'t show that a state had already lasted a while when a later past event happened. \\"Has been\\" measures up to the present, not to 1925, and \\"would be\\" looks ahead from 1925 even though her death came first.",["Simple past can\'t express a state that had lasted \\"for more than three years\\" up to a past moment (\\"by the time... 1925\\").","Present perfect connects to the present, but the sentence measures up to 1925.","Correct: \\"by the time\\" a past event happened, with a length of time up to it, calls for the past perfect.","\\"Would be\\" points forward from 1925, but she had died before it."]],"english-0483":["\\"Extent\\" means \\"degree,\\" and we say something is true \\"to an extent\\" or \\"to what extent.\\" So the matching relative phrase is \\"the extent to which\\": \\"no one could tell the extent to which a faint star\'s dimness came from its distance.\\" That is the original. The other choices all sound formal, which makes them tempting, but each uses the wrong preposition or treats an extent like a place (\\"where,\\" \\"in which\\") or a measured object (\\"of which\\").",["Correct: the standard phrase is \\"the extent to which,\\" from \\"to an extent\\" (\\"to what degree\\").","\\"In which\\" goes with places or situations (\\"the case in which\\"), not with \\"extent.\\"","\\"Where\\" refers to a place; an extent is a degree, not a location.","\\"The extent of which\\" would mean the size of something, and it doesn\'t connect to the clause that follows (\\"a faint star\'s dimness came from...\\")."]],"english-0484":["The goal has two parts, painstaking and unrecognized, and two choices each do only one. The original shows care and effort (\\"star by star,\\" a magnifying eyepiece) but says nothing about credit. The second choice mentions credit but describes the work so plainly that it no longer sounds painstaking. The third is about the size of the plate collection. Only the last choice includes both \\"star by star... faint dots\\" and \\"seldom earned them credit.\\"",["Shows the work was painstaking (\\"star by star,\\" \\"magnifying eyepiece\\") but says nothing about recognition.","Shows the work went uncredited, but \\"examine photographic plates of the night sky\\" doesn\'t convey slow, careful labor.","Shows the size of the collection, not how careful the work was or how little credit it got.","Correct: \\"star by star\\" and \\"faint dots\\" show painstaking work, and \\"seldom earned them credit\\" shows it went unrecognized."]],"english-0485":["The paragraph depends on one contrast: how bright a star appears versus how much light it really gives off. The original states the second half once, with one word, \\"actually.\\" \\"Each star\'s light\\" is shorter but blurs the contrast, since the light we see from Earth is also \\"each star\'s light.\\" The other two choices sound scientific, but \\"true, inherent... really\\" and \\"in reality... true output\\" say the same thing two or three times.",["Correct: \\"actually\\" is the one word needed to separate a star\'s real output from how bright it appears.","Too short: \\"light\\" could mean the light we see, so it loses the contrast between how bright a star looks and how much light it really gives off.","\\"True,\\" \\"inherent,\\" and \\"really\\" all say the same thing; piling them up only sounds precise.","\\"In reality\\" and \\"its true output\\" repeat each other and the rest of the phrase."]],"english-0486":["Look ahead to the last paragraph. Ms. Dvorak offers to lighten \\"the stains,\\" the narrator says no, and \\"the spots\\" become a record of Ilse\'s cooking. Those words point back to this phrase, which is the only place the stains are introduced. Without it, the ending refers to something the reader has never heard of. The second choice is the strongest trap: it is true that the narrator doesn\'t fix the stains, but that is why they matter. The third choice keeps the phrase for a reason the essay never gives.",["The phrase describes marks on the pages, not the recipes, so it stays on the book\'s condition.","True that she doesn\'t repair them, but that is the point: the last paragraph\'s decision to keep them depends on the reader already knowing about them.","Right decision, wrong reason: the paragraph never says stains loosened the pages.","Correct: the last paragraph\'s \\"the stains\\" and \\"the spots\\" refer back to this detail, and the narrator\'s decision to keep them only makes sense if they were introduced here."]],"english-0487":["Both halves are complete sentences: \\"she showed us how to sew... onto strips of linen tape\\" and \\"she had us practice on stacks of scrap paper.\\" Two independent clauses need a period, a semicolon, or a comma plus a coordinating conjunction (and, but, so...). \\"Then\\" feels like a joining word, but it is an adverb, like \\"later,\\" so \\", then she\\" is a comma splice. That is why it sounds fine to many readers. The semicolon fixes it. Extra commas around \\"then\\" don\'t help, and no punctuation at all makes a run-on.",["Comma splice: \\"then\\" is an adverb, not a joining word, so a comma alone can\'t connect \\"she showed us...\\" and \\"she had us practice....\\"","Correct: a semicolon joins the two independent clauses, and \\"then\\" can follow it.","Still a comma splice; putting commas around \\"then\\" doesn\'t turn it into a conjunction.","A run-on: two complete clauses with no punctuation between them."]],"english-0488":["Finish the comparison in your head. The writer means \\"Walt enjoyed her stories far more than I enjoyed them,\\" so the pronoun is the subject form, \\"I,\\" and \\"I did\\" says it outright. \\"Me\\" is what many people say, but in formal writing it reads as the object of \\"enjoyed,\\" as if Walt enjoyed the stories more than he enjoyed the narrator. \\"Myself\\" is a reflexive pronoun with nothing in its clause to reflect, and \\"mine\\" compares the stories with something the narrator owns.",["The object pronoun \\"me\\" makes the comparison read as Walt enjoying the stories more than he enjoyed me.","A reflexive pronoun needs its own antecedent in the same clause (\\"I enjoyed myself\\"); \\"myself\\" can\'t replace \\"I\\" in a comparison.","Correct: the comparison is between Walt\'s enjoyment and the narrator\'s, and \\"I did\\" makes that clear.","\\"Mine\\" is possessive and would compare the stories with something of the narrator\'s."]],"english-0490":["A speaker or a text implies; a reader or listener infers. Here the narrator studies Ilse\'s pencil notes and concludes that she changed almost every recipe. That is inferring, so the original is right. \\"Implied\\" is the classic mix-up, since the notes imply the changes, but the narrator is the one drawing the conclusion. \\"Alluded\\" and \\"insinuated\\" both describe hinting, which is something the person communicating does.",["Correct: to \\"infer\\" is to draw a conclusion from evidence, and the narrator draws one from the pencil notes.","To \\"imply\\" is to hint at something to someone else; the notes imply, but the narrator, reading them, infers.","\\"Alluded\\" means mentioned indirectly and is followed by \\"to,\\" not \\"from... that\\"; it doesn\'t mean concluded.","\\"Insinuated\\" means hinted at something, usually unpleasant, which is the speaker\'s act, not the reader\'s."]],"english-0491":["The sentence begins \\"The summer I turned sixteen,\\" so the reader already knows the narrator\'s age. Every choice except DELETE says it again in different words. Deleting the phrase leaves a complete sentence that keeps all of its meaning: \\"Grandpa announced that I would learn to drive it.\\" When DELETE is offered, try the sentence without the words; if nothing is lost, DELETE is the answer.",["Redundant: \\"The summer I turned sixteen\\" already tells you how old the narrator was.","Redundant: this says the narrator\'s age a second time in the same sentence.","Redundant: \\"a sixteen-year-old\\" repeats \\"The summer I turned sixteen.\\"","Correct: the sentence already gives the narrator\'s age, so the phrase can go."]],"english-0492":["The word \\"both\\" is half of a pair: it must be followed by \\"X and Y.\\" The original sentence has that pair: \\"both how the truck worked and why it sometimes didn\'t.\\" DELETE looks tempting because it is shorter, but it leaves \\"both\\" with only one item, which is ungrammatical, and it drops the idea that the narrator also learned why the truck broke down. \\"As well as\\" sounds fine in speech, but in standard written English \\"both\\" goes with \\"and.\\" \\"Or\\" is wrong because \\"both\\" means two things together, not a choice. The original is correct.",["Correct: \\"both\\" must be paired with \\"and\\": \\"both how the truck worked and why it sometimes didn\'t.\\"","Idiom error: \\"both\\" pairs with \\"and,\\" not \\"as well as\\"; \\"both ... as well as\\" says the joining idea twice.","Idiom error: \\"both\\" can\'t be paired with \\"or\\"; \\"or\\" offers a choice, but Grandpa wanted the narrator to learn two things.","Deleting leaves \\"both\\" with only one item (\\"both how the truck worked\\"), and the sentence loses half of what Grandpa wanted taught."]],"english-0493":["Read both halves of the sentence: the narrator stalled the truck eleven more times, and Grandpa never raised his voice. Most people would expect the stalling to make him angry, so the right word shows contrast: \\"Although.\\" \\"Because\\" says the stalling caused his calm, which is backward, and \\"Unless\\" sets up a condition that doesn\'t fit. DELETE is the trap for students who see that \\"Because\\" is wrong and just cut it. Without a subordinating word, \\"I stalled it eleven more times that afternoon\\" is a complete sentence, and joining it to another complete sentence with only a comma makes a comma splice.",["Wrong logic: stalling the truck eleven times isn\'t the reason Grandpa stayed calm; you\'d expect it to make him lose patience.","Correct: \\"Although\\" shows the contrast between the repeated stalling and Grandpa\'s calm.","Wrong logic: \\"Unless\\" sets a condition, which makes no sense; Grandpa stayed calm while the stalling happened.","Comma splice: without the subordinating word, two complete sentences are joined by only a comma."]],"english-0495":["In the first paragraph, Grandpa explains why he wants the narrator to learn on a manual truck: \\"I want you to understand what the engine is doing.\\" The best conclusion shows that goal reached. Only the third choice does: the narrator stops thinking about the pedals and listens to the engine, knowing what it needs. NO CHANGE is the trap, because it also looks back to the first paragraph, but the radio is a detail about the truck, not Grandpa\'s idea. The second choice mentions Grandpa without his idea, and the last choice drifts to the fair itself.",["This echoes a detail from the first paragraph (the radio\'s two stations), but not Grandpa\'s idea about understanding the engine.","This ends with Grandpa, but it doesn\'t return to his idea that a driver should understand what the engine is doing.","Correct: the narrator now listens to the engine and knows what it needs, which is exactly what Grandpa wanted.","This describes the fair, which says nothing about Grandpa\'s idea and shifts the focus away from driving."]],"english-0496":["Two clues decide this. First, \\"These animals\\" must come right after a sentence that names animals, and only Sentence 1 does (a deer, a raccoon, or a cat). Second, the new sentence introduces the term \\"tapetum lucidum\\" and says what it is. Sentence 2 begins \\"The tapetum sits just behind the retina,\\" which only makes sense if the reader has already been told what the tapetum is. So the new sentence has to come between Sentence 1 and Sentence 2: Point A. Points B, C, and D are tempting because they sit next to sentences about the tapetum, but each one would name the tapetum after the paragraph has already been using the word.",["Correct: \\"These animals\\" points back to the deer, raccoon, and cat in Sentence 1, and the sentence names the tapetum before Sentence 2 starts describing it.","Too late: Sentence 2 already says \\"The tapetum\\" as if the reader knows what it is, and \\"These animals\\" would no longer follow the list of animals.","Too late: by this point the paragraph has described the tapetum twice without naming it, and the new sentence would break up the explanation of how light bounces.","Too late: the new sentence introduces the tapetum, so it can\'t come after the paragraph has already explained how the tapetum makes eyeshine."]],"english-0497":["The sentence already says that the tapetum gives the cells \\"a second chance to catch light.\\" Each of the first three choices says the same thing again in other words: \\"another opportunity,\\" \\"try once more,\\" \\"one more try.\\" Deleting the phrase loses nothing, and the next sentence (\\"That second chance helps an animal see ...\\") still has a clear idea to point back to. The answer is DELETE.",["Redundant: \\"another opportunity to absorb it\\" repeats \\"a second chance to catch light.\\"","Redundant: \\"try once more\\" says \\"second chance\\" again.","Redundant: \\"one more try\\" is the same idea as \\"a second chance.\\"","Correct: \\"a second chance to catch light\\" already says the cells get another try."]],"english-0498":["The essay explains how eyes work in clear, plain language, the way a science magazine would. \\"That is a trade worth making\\" keeps that voice and states the idea directly: a slightly blurrier image is worth the ability to see in dim light. The other choices sound like someone talking with friends. \\"Totally\\" and \\"no question,\\" \\"any day of the week,\\" and the filler \\"like\\" are all too casual for this essay.",["Too casual: \\"totally\\" and \\"no question\\" sound like conversation, not like the essay\'s clear, informative style.","Too casual: \\"any day of the week\\" is a chatty expression that doesn\'t fit an informative essay.","Correct: plain, clear wording that matches the essay\'s informative tone.","Too casual: the filler word \\"like\\" belongs in speech, not in this essay."]],"english-0499":["The original says the same thing twice: the tissue is \\"rich in blood vessels\\" and \\"has a large supply of blood.\\" DELETE looks like the cleanest fix, and the sentence would still be complete. But read the next sentence: \\"That blood is what gives the reflection its red color.\\" \\"That blood\\" has to point back to blood already mentioned, so the detail can\'t be cut entirely. The third choice has the same problem: it describes where the tissue is but never mentions blood. The best choice is the one that keeps the needed idea once: \\", which is rich in blood.\\" The shortest choice is only right if it keeps the meaning the paragraph needs.",["Redundant: \\"rich in blood vessels\\" and \\"a large supply of blood\\" say the same thing twice.","Correct: it keeps the one detail the next sentence needs (\\"That blood\\") and says it only once.","Drops the needed detail: with no mention of blood, \\"That blood\\" in the next sentence has nothing to refer to.","Shortest, but it leaves \\"That blood\\" in the next sentence pointing to blood that was never mentioned."]],"english-0500":["\\"Between\\" always needs two things joined by \\"and\\": \\"between Pruett and Calder Springs.\\" The original mixes two different patterns. \\"From ... to\\" is correct, and \\"between ... and\\" is correct, but \\"between ... to\\" is not. \\"Or\\" is wrong for the same reason. DELETE leaves \\"between Pruett\\" with only one place, and it would also drop Calder Springs, a town the essay mentions again later.",["Idiom error: \\"between\\" pairs with \\"and,\\" not \\"to\\" (\\"from\\" is the word that pairs with \\"to\\").","Idiom error: \\"between\\" needs \\"and\\" to join its two places; \\"or\\" offers a choice instead.","Correct: \\"between Pruett and Calder Springs\\" is the standard pairing.","Deleting leaves \\"between Pruett,\\" and \\"between\\" needs two things; the sentence also loses the railroad\'s second town."]],"english-0501":["The words \\"who cannot drive\\" describe \\"anyone,\\" and \\"who\\" is the subject of \\"cannot drive.\\" Subjects take the subject form, \\"who\\" (you\'d say \\"he cannot drive,\\" not \\"him cannot drive\\"). \\"Whom\\" is the object form, and \\"which\\" refers to things, not people. DELETE keeps the sentence grammatical, but it changes the meaning: \\"anyone must either find a ride or walk\\" would include people who own cars. The paragraph\'s argument is about residents without a car, so the phrase has to stay.",["Correct: \\"who\\" is the subject of \\"cannot drive,\\" so the subject form is right.","Case error: \\"whom\\" is the object form, but the pronoun is the subject of \\"cannot drive.\\"","\\"Which\\" is for things, and \\"anyone\\" means a person.","Loses needed meaning: \\"anyone must either find a ride or walk\\" would mean everyone, even people who drive, and the point of the paragraph is lost."]],"english-0502":["The trail belongs to Wexley County, so the sentence needs a possessive word: \\"whose trail.\\" \\"Who\'s\\" is a contraction of \\"who is\\" (or \\"who has\\"), and neither one fits before \\"trail.\\" \\"Which\\" can\'t show possession here. \\"Whose\\" also works for places and organizations, not only for people. Deleting the words leaves \\"Wexley County, in 2009 along a similar abandoned line,\\" which no longer mentions the trail at all.",["\\"Who\'s\\" means \\"who is,\\" and \\"Wexley County, who is trail opened in 2009\\" makes no sense.","\\"Which trail opened\\" doesn\'t show that the trail belongs to the county; this spot needs a possessive.","Correct: \\"whose\\" is the possessive form; the trail belongs to Wexley County.","Deleting leaves \\"Wexley County, in 2009 along a similar abandoned line,\\" a phrase that no longer says what happened in 2009."]],"english-0503":["The original joins two complete thoughts with a comma: \\"Building the trail would cost about $3 million\\" and \\"most of it a state recreation grant would cover\\" (that is, a state grant would cover most of it). That\'s a comma splice. Changing \\"it\\" to \\"which\\" fixes it, because \\"most of which a state recreation grant would cover\\" can\'t stand alone; it describes the $3 million. \\"And most of which\\" adds a conjunction that doesn\'t belong. DELETE is wrong too: it breaks the sentence and drops \\"most,\\" and the next sentence depends on that word, because the county\'s share is the part the grant doesn\'t cover.",["Comma splice: \\"most of it a state recreation grant would cover\\" is a complete thought joined to the sentence with only a comma.","Correct: \\"most of which\\" makes the second part a dependent clause describing the $3 million.","\\"And\\" plus \\"which\\" don\'t work together here; there is no earlier \\"which\\" clause for this one to join.","Deleting leaves \\"about $3 million, a state recreation grant would cover,\\" which is broken, and it drops the idea that the grant pays only most of the cost."]],"english-0504":["Look at Sentence 2: \\"These concerns are fair.\\" The paragraph never says what the concerns are, so \\"These concerns\\" has nothing to point back to. The new sentence supplies them. It also begins with \\"They,\\" which has to refer to the farmers in Sentence 1. Placed at Point A, it names the farmers\' worries right before Sentence 2 calls them fair and Sentence 3 calls them \\"the same objections.\\" Point D is the tempting trap because Sentence 5 also mentions farmers, but those Wexley farmers had no complaints, so \\"They worry\\" would contradict it. Points B and C come after the paragraph has already referred to the concerns.",["Correct: \\"They\\" refers to the farmers in Sentence 1, and the worries it names are the \\"concerns\\" Sentence 2 calls fair.","Too late: Sentence 2 already calls the worries \\"These concerns\\" before they\'ve been stated, and \\"They\\" would no longer follow the farmers.","Too late: Sentences 2 and 3 already refer to \\"these concerns\\" and \\"the same objections\\" as if the reader knows what they are.","Here \\"They\\" would seem to mean the Wexley farmers, who just said they had no complaints, so the sentence would contradict the one before it."]],"english-0505":["The sentence just before this one says Delgado works \\"entirely by hand, using no power tools.\\" An electric sander is a power tool, so \\"never with an electric sander\\" repeats that fact, and so do \\"not with any machines\\" and \\"without power equipment.\\" The redundancy is easy to miss because it crosses a sentence boundary. Always check the sentences around the underlined part, not just the sentence it\'s in. Deleting the phrase leaves a complete sentence that loses nothing.",["Redundant: the previous sentence already says Delgado uses no power tools, and an electric sander is a power tool.","Redundant: \\"not with any machines\\" repeats \\"using no power tools\\" from the sentence before.","Redundant: \\"without power equipment\\" says almost word for word what the previous sentence said.","Correct: the paragraph has just said she uses no power tools, so the reader already knows she doesn\'t use a sander."]],"english-0506":["This is the \\"so ... that\\" pattern: \\"a wood so soft that a carver can shape it with a pocketknife.\\" The \\"that\\" clause tells you how soft the wood is, so it is tied closely to \\"so soft.\\" No punctuation belongs between them. The comma is tempting because the sentence is long and seems to need a pause, but a pause in speech isn\'t a reason for a comma. A semicolon would need a complete sentence on each side, and a colon would need to introduce something such as a list or an explanation.",["Unneeded comma: in \\"so soft that,\\" the \\"that\\" clause finishes the \\"so\\" idea, so nothing should split them.","A semicolon must separate two complete sentences, and \\"that a carver can shape it with a pocketknife\\" isn\'t one.","Correct: \\"so soft that a carver can shape it\\" needs no punctuation between \\"soft\\" and \\"that.\\"","A colon cuts off the \\"that\\" clause from the \\"so\\" it completes; nothing is being introduced or listed."]],"english-0507":["The opening phrase \\"Sanded down to bare wood\\" describes the horse, so the subject of the main clause must be the horse. Only the second choice does that: \\"Sanded down to bare wood, the horse finally revealed the pencil lines.\\" NO CHANGE makes Delgado the one who was sanded. The third choice is the subtle trap. It mentions the horse, but only as the possessive \\"horse\'s.\\" The subject is \\"pencil lines,\\" so the sentence says the pencil lines were sanded down. A possessive can\'t be the noun an opening phrase describes. DELETE leaves \\"Sanded down to bare wood, the original carver had drawn to guide his chisel,\\" which says the carver was sanded and is missing what he drew.",["Dangling modifier: this says Delgado was \\"sanded down to bare wood,\\" but the horse was.","Correct: the horse is what was sanded down to bare wood, so \\"the horse\\" comes right after the opening phrase.","Possessive trap: \\"horse\'s\\" is only a describing word here; the subject is \\"pencil lines,\\" so the lines seem to be what was sanded down.","Deleting leaves \\"the original carver had drawn\\" as the main clause, which says the carver was sanded down and leaves \\"drawn\\" with no object."]],"english-0508":["The new sentence says Delgado will restore \\"every one of them,\\" even the ones so small that riders won\'t notice. \\"Them\\" has to be things on the horses that can be restored and that can be tiny. Sentence 4 names exactly those: details that later painters covered over, like a row of roses carved along a saddle. So the sentence belongs after Sentence 4, at Point D. Point B is the main trap, because Sentence 2 mentions a large number of things (two hundred photographs), but Delgado studies the photographs; she doesn\'t restore them. Point C would make \\"them\\" the colors, which aren\'t small objects.",["Here \\"them\\" would mean the old photographs, and Delgado doesn\'t restore photographs.","Here \\"them\\" would mean the two hundred photographs the historical society lent her, which she studies, not restores.","Here \\"them\\" would seem to mean the colors, but colors aren\'t \\"so small\\" that riders miss them, and no details have been mentioned yet.","Correct: \\"them\\" refers to the hidden carved details in Sentence 4, such as the tiny roses along a saddle\'s edge."]],"math-0001":["After a 20% discount you pay 80% of the original price, so 0.8 × original = $40, and original = $40 ÷ 0.8 = $50. Check: 20% of $50 is $10, and $50 − $10 = $40. The trap is $48, which adds 20% of $40 back on; that is wrong because the discount was 20% of the ORIGINAL price, not of $40. $32 takes another 20% off instead of undoing the discount.",["Took another 20% off $40 instead of undoing the discount.","Added 20% of $40 back on, but the discount was 20% of the original price, not of $40.","Correct: $40 is 80% of the original, and $40 ÷ 0.8 = $50.","Added 50% instead of working backward from 80%."]],"math-0002":["Use FOIL: 3·4 = 12, 3·i = 3i, −2i·4 = −8i, and −2i·i = −2i². Since i² = −1, −2i² = +2. Combine: 12 + 2 = 14 and 3i − 8i = −5i, so the product is 14 − 5i. The most common trap is 10 − 5i, which treats i² as +1. Dropping the −2i² term entirely gives 12 − 5i, and 14 + 11i comes from dropping the minus sign on −8i.",["Correct: 12 + 3i − 8i − 2i² = 12 − 5i + 2 = 14 − 5i.","Used i² = 1 instead of −1, so −2i² became −2 instead of +2.","Dropped the −2i² term; since i² = −1, it equals +2 and must be added to the 12.","Sign slip: wrote −2i·4 as +8i, so the i-terms added to 11i."]],"math-0003":["Two rules: a power raised to a power multiplies the exponents, so (x³)⁴ = x^(3·4) = x¹². Multiplying powers with the same base adds the exponents, so x¹² · x² = x¹⁴. x⁹ adds everything, x²⁴ multiplies everything, and x¹² stops after the first step.",["Added all three exponents (3 + 4 + 2); a power of a power multiplies.","Intermediate result: (x³)⁴ = x¹², but the x² was never included.","Correct: (x³)⁴ = x¹², and x¹² · x² = x¹⁴.","Multiplied all three exponents (3 · 4 · 2); when you multiply powers with the same base, you add."]],"math-0004":["Multiples of 6 below 200: 6, 12, ..., 198, which is 198 ÷ 6 = 33 numbers. Multiples of 8 below 200: 8, ..., 192, which is 24 numbers (200 is not less than 200). Numbers like 24 and 48 are in both lists. A number is a multiple of both 6 and 8 when it is a multiple of their least common multiple, 24: 24, ..., 192, which is 8 numbers. Count each number once: 33 + 24 − 8 = 49. Adding without subtracting gives 57, and including 200 gives 50.",["Subtracted the 8 multiples of 24 twice; they were only counted twice, so subtract them once.","Correct: 33 multiples of 6 + 24 multiples of 8 − 8 multiples of 24 = 49.","Off-by-one: counted 200 itself as a multiple of 8, but the numbers must be less than 200.","Added 33 + 24 without removing the numbers counted in both lists (the multiples of 24)."]],"math-0005":["Distribute first: 5(x − 2) = 5x − 10. Then 5x − 10 = 3x + 8. Subtract 3x from both sides: 2x − 10 = 8. Add 10: 2x = 18, so x = 9. Check: 5(9 − 2) = 35 and 3(9) + 8 = 35. The answer 5 comes from forgetting to multiply the 2 by 5, and −1 comes from moving the −10 across without changing its sign.",["Sign slip: moved −10 to the right as −10 instead of +10, giving 2x = −2.","Added 3x to the left side instead of subtracting it, giving 8x = 18.","Didn\'t distribute the 5 to the 2: wrote 5x − 2 instead of 5x − 10.","Correct: 5x − 10 = 3x + 8, so 2x = 18 and x = 9."]],"math-0006":["Let a = adult tickets and s = student tickets. Then a + s = 340 and 11a + 7s = 3,140. Substitute a = 340 − s: 11(340 − s) + 7s = 3,140, so 3,740 − 4s = 3,140 and 4s = 600. That gives s = 150 (and a = 190). Check: 11(190) + 7(150) = 2,090 + 1,050 = 3,140. Watch the question: it asks for student tickets, so 190 (the adult count) is a trap, and 600 is the step before dividing by 4.",["Correct: 11(340 − s) + 7s = 3,140 gives 4s = 600, so s = 150.","Assumed the tickets were split evenly (340 ÷ 2); the prices make that impossible.","Wrong piece: 190 is the number of ADULT tickets.","Intermediate result: 4s = 600, but you still need to divide by 4."]],"math-0007":["Subtract 7 from both sides: −3x > 12. Divide both sides by −3. Dividing (or multiplying) an inequality by a negative number reverses it, so x < −4. Check with x = −5: 7 − 3(−5) = 22, and 22 > 19, so it works. With x = 0 (which is greater than −4): 7 > 19 is false. The classic trap is x > −4, which forgets to flip the sign.",["Correct: −3x > 12, and dividing by −3 flips the sign: x < −4.","Forgot to flip the inequality sign when dividing by a negative number.","Sign slip: wrote −3x > −12 instead of −3x > 12, which flips the sign of the 4.","Got the wrong sign on 4 AND forgot to flip the inequality."]],"math-0008":["Look for (ax + b)(cx + d) with ac = 6, bd = −20, and ad + bc = 7. Try (3x − 4)(2x + 5): 3x·2x = 6x², 3x·5 = 15x, −4·2x = −8x, and −4·5 = −20. The middle terms give 15x − 8x = 7x, so 6x² + 7x − 20 = (3x − 4)(2x + 5), and 3x − 4 is a factor. Flipping both signs, (3x + 4)(2x − 5), gives −7x, so 3x + 4 and 2x − 5 are traps. A quick check: a factor 3x − 4 means x = 4/3 makes the polynomial 0, and 6(16/9) + 7(4/3) − 20 = 0.",["Correct: 6x² + 7x − 20 = (3x − 4)(2x + 5).","Right numbers, wrong sign: (3x + 4)(2x − 5) multiplies to 6x² − 7x − 20, so the middle term is negative.","Wrong sign: 2x + 5 is the other factor, not 2x − 5.","The first and last numbers can multiply to 6x² and −20, but no pairing with 6x − 5 gives the middle term 7x."]],"math-0009":["Set the two expressions equal: x² − 10x + c = 2x − 4. Move everything to one side: x² − 12x + (c + 4) = 0. A quadratic has exactly one solution when its discriminant is 0: (−12)² − 4(1)(c + 4) = 0, so 144 = 4(c + 4), c + 4 = 36, and c = 32. Check: x² − 12x + 36 = (x − 6)², which has only x = 6. The trap 25 makes the parabola touch the x-axis, not the line, and 36 stops one step early.",["Ignored the line and made x² − 10x + c a perfect square on its own.","Correct: x² − 12x + (c + 4) = 0 has one solution when 144 = 4(c + 4), so c = 32.","Intermediate result: 36 is c + 4, not c.","Sign slip: moved −4 across as c − 4 = 36."]],"math-0010":["Isolate 1/b: 1/b = 1/f − 1/a. Use a common denominator af: 1/f − 1/a = a/(af) − f/(af) = (a − f)/(af). So 1/b = (a − f)/(af), and flipping both sides gives b = af/(a − f). Check with a = 6 and f = 4: b = 24/2 = 12, and 1/6 + 1/12 = 3/12 = 1/4. The trap (a − f)/(af) is 1/b, not b, and af/(f − a) gets the subtraction backward.",["Treated reciprocals like ordinary numbers: 1/b = 1/f − 1/a does not mean b = f − a.","Subtracted in the wrong order: 1/f − 1/a = (a − f)/(af), not (f − a)/(af). This would make b negative.","Correct: 1/b = 1/f − 1/a = (a − f)/(af), so b = af/(a − f).","Intermediate result: this is 1/b. Flip it to get b."]],"math-0011":["Substitute x = −2 everywhere: f(−2) = 3(−2)² − 2(−2) + 1. Exponents first: (−2)² = 4, so 3(4) = 12. Next, −2(−2) = +4. Total: 12 + 4 + 1 = 17. Two traps: 3x² means 3 times x², not (3x)², and a negative times a negative is positive.",["Sign slip: −2(−2) is +4, not −4.","Correct: 3(4) − 2(−2) + 1 = 12 + 4 + 1 = 17.","Squared 3x instead of x (36 instead of 12) and also slipped on the sign of −2x.","Squared 3x instead of x: 3x² means 3 · x², so 3(−2)² = 12, not 36."]],"math-0012":["f⁻¹(5) asks: which input makes f give 5? Solve (2x + 7)/3 = 5: multiply by 3 to get 2x + 7 = 15, so 2x = 8 and x = 4. Check: f(4) = (8 + 7)/3 = 5. You can also find the inverse formula, f⁻¹(x) = (3x − 7)/2, and plug in 5: (15 − 7)/2 = 4. Traps: 17/3 is f(5), 3/17 is the reciprocal of f(5) (the −1 in f⁻¹ is not an exponent), and 11 comes from adding 7 instead of subtracting when undoing the function.",["Took 1/f(5). The inverse function is not the reciprocal.","Correct: f⁻¹(5) is the x with (2x + 7)/3 = 5, so 2x + 7 = 15 and x = 4.","Computed f(5) instead of f⁻¹(5).","Built the inverse with a sign error: (3x + 7)/2 instead of (3x − 7)/2."]],"math-0013":["First decide which rule each input uses. x = 2 satisfies x ≥ 2, so f(2) = 3(2) − 4 = 2. x = −3 satisfies x < 2, so f(−3) = (−3)² − 1 = 9 − 1 = 8. The sum is 2 + 8 = 10. Using the wrong piece at the boundary x = 2 gives 3 + 8 = 11, and using the second piece for −3 gives 2 + (−13) = −11.",["Used 3x − 4 for x = −3 as well, but −3 < 2 belongs to the first piece.","Sign slip: (−3)² is 9, not −9.","Correct: f(2) = 3(2) − 4 = 2 and f(−3) = 9 − 1 = 8, so the sum is 10.","Used x² − 1 for x = 2, but x = 2 belongs to the x ≥ 2 piece."]],"math-0014":["Inside the absolute value, changes act the opposite of how they look: |x + 3| moves the graph 3 units LEFT (its vertex goes from x = 0 to x = −3). Outside, changes act as they look: − 2 moves it 2 units down. So y = |x + 3| − 2, with vertex (−3, −2). The most common trap is |x − 3| − 2, which moves the graph right.",["Wrong direction: x − 3 shifts the graph 3 units to the RIGHT.","Correct: replacing x with x + 3 moves the graph left 3, and subtracting 2 moves it down 2.","Wrong vertical direction: + 2 moves the graph UP.","Swapped the two amounts: this moves the graph right 2 and down 3."]],"math-0015":["From the 3rd term to the 7th term is 4 steps, and the terms rise by 34 − 14 = 20, so the common difference is d = 20 ÷ 4 = 5. The 1st term is 2 steps before the 3rd: 14 − 2(5) = 4. The 20th term is 19 steps after the 1st: 4 + 19(5) = 99. (Or start from the 7th term: 34 + 13(5) = 99.) Count steps, not terms: that is where 82, 94 and 104 come from.",["Divided the difference 20 by 5 terms instead of by 4 steps, so the common difference came out 4 instead of 5.","Off-by-one: went back 3 steps from the 3rd term to find the 1st term (getting −1) instead of 2 steps.","Correct: d = 5, the 1st term is 4, and 4 + 19(5) = 99.","Off-by-one: used 4 + 20(5); the 20th term is 19 steps after the 1st term."]],"math-0016":["Adding logs with the same base multiplies their insides: log₃(x) + log₃(x − 6) = log₃(x(x − 6)). So log₃(x(x − 6)) = 3, which means x(x − 6) = 3³ = 27. Then x² − 6x − 27 = 0, which factors as (x − 9)(x + 3) = 0, so x = 9 or x = −3. Now check: a logarithm needs a positive input, and x = −3 gives log₃(−3), which is undefined. Only x = 9 works: log₃9 + log₃3 = 2 + 1 = 3.",["Correct: x(x − 6) = 27 gives x = 9 or x = −3, and only 9 keeps both logs defined.","Kept the wrong root: log₃(−3) is undefined, so −3 can\'t be a solution.","Forgot to check the roots: x = −3 makes both logarithms undefined, so it is extraneous.","Treated log₃(x) + log₃(x − 6) as log₃(x + x − 6); adding logs multiplies the insides."]],"math-0017":["The angles of a triangle add to 180°, so Q + R = 180° − 48° = 132°. Since Q = 2R, 2R + R = 3R = 132°, and R = 44°. Then Q = 88°. Check: 48 + 88 + 44 = 180. The question asks for angle R, so 88° (angle Q) is a trap, and 132° stops one step early.",["Correct: Q + R = 132°, and 2R + R = 132° gives R = 44°.","Split 132° evenly between Q and R, but Q is twice R.","Wrong piece: 88° is angle Q.","Intermediate result: 132° is Q + R together."]],"math-0018":["Because DE ∥ BC, triangle ADE is similar to triangle ABC. Matching sides: AD in the small triangle matches AB in the big one, and AB = AD + DB = 6 + 4 = 10. So the scale factor is 10/6, and BC = 9 × 10/6 = 15. The trap 13.5 uses DB (4) as if it were the whole side AB. Since the big triangle contains the small one, BC must be longer than DE = 9, which rules out 6.",["Reversed ratio: scaled 9 by 4/6, making the larger triangle\'s side smaller than DE.","Added the extra length 4 to 9; similar figures scale by multiplying, not adding.","Used AD/DB = 6/4 as the scale factor; the matching side of the big triangle is AB = 10, not DB.","Correct: AD/AB = DE/BC gives 6/10 = 9/BC, so BC = 15."]],"math-0019":["Complete the square. Group terms: (x² − 6x) + (y² + 10y) = 2. Add 9 to finish the x-square and 25 to finish the y-square, on both sides: (x − 3)² + (y + 5)² = 2 + 9 + 25 = 36. The standard form is (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r², so r² = 36 and r = 6. The center is (3, −5). Forgetting the 2 gives √34, and 36 is r² rather than r.",["Used only the constant term; you must complete the square first.","Completed the squares (+9 and +25) but forgot to move the −2 to the right side as +2.","Correct: (x − 3)² + (y + 5)² = 36, so r = 6.","36 is r², not r."]],"math-0020":["Tangent is opposite over adjacent. Stand at angle B: the side across from B is AC = 12, and the leg next to B is BC = 5. So tan B = 12/5. (The hypotenuse AB is √(12² + 5²) = 13, which you\'d need for sine or cosine.) The trap 5/12 is tan A: it uses the opposite and adjacent sides from the wrong angle.",["This is cos B (adjacent/hypotenuse), not tan B.","This is tan A. From angle B, the opposite side is AC = 12, not BC.","This is sin B (opposite/hypotenuse), not tan B.","Correct: tan B = opposite/adjacent = AC/BC = 12/5."]],"math-0021":["Cylinder volume: πr²h. Cone volume: (1/3)πR²H, and here R = 2r, so the cone\'s volume is (1/3)π(4r²)H = (4/3)πr²H. Set them equal: (4/3)πr²H = πr²h. Divide by πr²: (4/3)H = h, so H = 3h/4. Doubling the radius makes the base 4 times as big, and the cone\'s 1/3 factor makes it 3 times smaller, so the cone needs 3/4 of the height. Dropping the 1/3 gives h/4, and doubling the area instead of quadrupling it gives 3h/2.",["Forgot the 1/3 in the cone volume formula.","Correct: (1/3)π(2r)²H = πr²h gives (4/3)H = h, so H = 3h/4.","Doubled the radius but only doubled the base area; (2r)² = 4r², so the area is 4 times as large.","Inverted the ratio: multiplied by 4 and 3 instead of dividing."]],"math-0022":["The phrase \\"who plays a musical instrument\\" tells you the group you\'re choosing from. That group has 45 + 35 = 80 students. Of those 80, 45 play a sport, so the probability is 45/80 = 9/16. The traps use the wrong denominator: 45/200 uses everyone surveyed, and 45/120 flips the condition (it answers \\"given a sport player, what\'s the chance they play an instrument?\\").",["Wrong denominator: divided by all 200 students instead of the 80 instrument players.","Wrong condition: 45/120 is the chance a SPORT player plays an instrument.","Wrong group: 35/80 is the chance that a non-sport student plays an instrument.","Correct: 45 + 35 = 80 students play an instrument, and 45 of them play a sport: 45/80 = 9/16."]],"math-0023":["Total prize money: $300 + 2($100) + 5($20) = $300 + $200 + $100 = $600, spread over 500 tickets. Expected prize per ticket = $600 ÷ 500 = $1.20. Every ticket costs $2, so the expected net gain is $1.20 − $2.00 = −$0.80 (an average loss of 80 cents). Weight each prize by how many tickets win it, and remember the question asks for NET gain.",["Counted each prize amount only once ($300 + $100 + $20), ignoring that there are two $100 and five $20 prizes.","Correct: total prizes are $600, so the expected prize is $600/500 = $1.20, and $1.20 − $2.00 = −$0.80.","Counted each prize only once AND forgot to subtract the $2 ticket price.","Intermediate result: $1.20 is the expected prize; you still subtract the $2 cost."]],"math-0024":["With 25 scores, the median is the middle one, the 13th in order. Count up: scores of 6 fill positions 1-4, scores of 7 fill 5-12, and scores of 8 fill 13-17. So the 13th score is 8. The mean is (24 + 56 + 40 + 54 + 20) ÷ 25 = 7.76, which is a different measure. The mode (most common score) is 7, which is also the trap you get if you stop at the 12th score.",["Took the median of the frequency column (2, 4, 5, 6, 8) instead of the scores.","Off-by-one: the 12th score is 7, but the median of 25 scores is the 13th; 7 is also the mode.","This is the mean (194 ÷ 25), not the median.","Correct: the 13th score in order falls in the group with a score of 8."]],"math-0025":["There are 20 marbles. The first draw is blue with probability 6/20. After one blue is removed, 5 of the remaining 19 are blue, so the second draw is blue with probability 5/19. Multiply: (6/20)(5/19) = 30/380 = 3/38. Without replacement, BOTH the number of blue marbles and the total go down by 1; 9/100 changes neither, and 9/95 changes only the total.",["Correct: (6/20)(5/19) = 30/380 = 3/38.","Treated the draws as WITH replacement: (6/20)(6/20).","Lowered the total to 19 but forgot that only 5 blue marbles are left: (6/20)(6/19).","Intermediate result: 6/20 is only the probability that the first marble is blue."]],"math-0026":["Find the rate: 18 miles ÷ 1.5 hours = 12 miles per hour. Time for 30 miles: 30 ÷ 12 = 2.5 hours. Convert to minutes: 2.5 × 60 = 150 minutes. Always check the units the question asks for: 2.5 is right in hours but is not the answer.",["Unit slip: 2.5 is the time in HOURS; the question asks for minutes.","Copied the given time: 90 minutes is how long the first 18 miles took.","Treated 18 miles as taking 1 hour (30/18 × 60) and ignored the 1.5 hours.","Correct: 18 ÷ 1.5 = 12 mph, and 30 ÷ 12 = 2.5 hours = 150 minutes."]],"math-0027":["Convert step by step: 2.5 gallons × 4 = 10 quarts, and 10 quarts × 32 = 320 fluid ounces. Divide by the serving size: 320 ÷ 6 ≈ 53.3. Only 53 servings are full (the last third of a serving isn\'t a full one), so round down. Skipping the quarts step gives 13.",["Unit slip: skipped the gallons-to-quarts step (2.5 × 32 ÷ 6 ≈ 13.3).","Used 8 fluid ounces per serving (a cup) instead of the 6 given.","Correct: 2.5 × 4 × 32 = 320 fl oz, and 320 ÷ 6 ≈ 53.3, so 53 full servings.","Rounded 53.3 up; the 54th serving would not be full."]],"math-0028":["Work forward with the unknown cost C. Marked price: 1.60C. Sale price: 0.75 × 1.60C = 1.20C. So 1.20C = $54 and C = $45. Check: $45 × 1.6 = $72, and 25% off $72 is $54. Two traps: adding the percents (+35%) gives $40, and undoing a 25% discount by adding 25% back doesn\'t work, because 25% was taken off the larger marked price.",["Combined the changes as +60% − 25% = +35%; percent changes multiply, they don\'t add.","Undid the 25% discount by adding 25% back ($54 × 1.25) instead of dividing by 0.75.","Correct: sale price = 1.60 × 0.75 × cost = 1.2 × cost, so cost = $54 ÷ 1.2 = $45.","Intermediate result: $72 is the marked price before the sale ($54 ÷ 0.75)."]],"math-0029":["Turn means into totals. First 5 tests: 5 × 84 = 420 points. All 6 tests: 6 × 86 = 516 points. The 6th test supplied the difference: 516 − 420 = 96. Another way to see it: the 6th score has to be 86 itself plus 2 extra points for each of the first 5 tests (to raise them from 84 to 86): 86 + 10 = 96.",["Averaged the two means; the 6th score has to pull up all six tests, not just two numbers.","Copied the new mean as the 6th score.","Added the 2-point rise to the new mean; the 6th score must make up 2 points for each of the 6 tests.","Correct: 6 × 86 − 5 × 84 = 516 − 420 = 96."]],"math-0030":["The walkway goes around the garden, so the whole figure is (24 + 2w) by (15 + 2w). Walkway area = outer area − garden area: (24 + 2w)(15 + 2w) − 360 = 270. Expand: 360 + 78w + 4w² − 360 = 270, so 4w² + 78w − 270 = 0, or 2w² + 39w − 135 = 0. This factors as (2w + 45)(w − 3) = 0, and a width must be positive, so w = 3. Check: 30 × 21 = 630, and 630 − 360 = 270. Using only the side strips (78w = 270) misses the corners and gives about 3.5.",["Correct: (24 + 2w)(15 + 2w) − 360 = 270 gives 2w² + 39w − 135 = 0, so w = 3.","Treated the walkway as four strips along the sides (78w = 270) and left out the four corner squares.","Added the width only once to each dimension, (24 + w)(15 + w); the walkway is on both sides.","Used only half the garden\'s perimeter: 39w = 270."]],"math-0031":["To get the entry in row 2, column 2 of AB, multiply row 2 of A by column 2 of B, term by term, and add. Row 2 of A is (3, 1, k) and column 2 of B is (1, 4, 3). So the entry is 3·1 + 1·4 + k·3 = 7 + 3k. Set 7 + 3k = 1, so 3k = −6 and k = −2. The trap 5 comes from using column 1 of B instead of column 2. Always say \\"row of the first, column of the second\\" to yourself before you multiply.",["Forgot the last step: 3k = −6, so k is −6 ÷ 3, not −6.","Correct: row 2 of A times column 2 of B is 3(1) + 1(4) + k(3) = 7 + 3k, and 7 + 3k = 1 gives k = −2.","Sign slip: moved the 7 across without changing its sign, so 3k = 6.","Wrong column: used column 1 of B, getting 3(2) + 1(0) + k(−1) = 6 − k = 1."]],"math-0032":["Powers of i repeat every 4: i, −1, −i, 1. Each block of 4 in a row adds to 0. There are 50 terms, and 50 = 4·12 + 2, so the first 48 terms (12 full blocks) add to 0. The 2 terms left are i⁴⁹ and i⁵⁰. Since 49 = 48 + 1, i⁴⁹ = i, and since 50 = 48 + 2, i⁵⁰ = i² = −1. The sum is −1 + i. The trap 1 + i treats any even power of i as 1, but only multiples of 4 give 1.",["Correct: the first 48 terms form 12 groups of i − 1 − i + 1 = 0, leaving i⁴⁹ + i⁵⁰ = i + (−1).","Assumed every term cancels, but 50 is not a multiple of 4, so two terms are left over.","Grouped from the wrong end and kept i³ + i⁴ = −i + 1 as the leftover terms.","Took i⁵⁰ as 1 because 50 is even, but i² = −1, so i⁵⁰ = (i²)²⁵ = −1."]],"math-0033":["Write both bases as powers of 2. 4ˣ = (2²)ˣ = (2ˣ)², so (2ˣ)² = 9 and 2ˣ = 3 (2ˣ is always positive). Then 8ˣ = (2³)ˣ = (2ˣ)³ = 3³ = 27. You never need to find x itself. The traps come from treating the base like a factor: 8 is twice 4, but 8ˣ is not twice 4ˣ.",["Multiplied 9 by 3/2 because the exponent of 2 goes from 2 to 3, but exponents scale powers, not products.","Doubled 9 because 8 is twice 4, but doubling the base does not double the power.","Correct: 4ˣ = (2ˣ)² = 9, so 2ˣ = 3 and 8ˣ = (2ˣ)³ = 27.","Squared 9 as if 8 were 4², but 4² is 16."]],"math-0034":["Square both sides: 2x + 7 = x² + 4x + 4. Move everything to one side: x² + 2x − 3 = 0, which factors as (x + 3)(x − 1) = 0, so x = −3 or x = 1. Now check each in the original. x = 1: √9 = 3 and 1 + 2 = 3, so it works. x = −3: √1 = 1 but −3 + 2 = −1, so it fails. Squaring hides the sign of each side, which is why radical equations can pick up a false (extraneous) solution. Always check.",["Kept the wrong root: x = −3 makes the right side −1, but a square root can\'t equal a negative number.","Correct: squaring gives x² + 2x − 3 = 0, so x = 1 or x = −3, and only x = 1 checks (√9 = 3).","Forgot to check: squaring both sides can create a root that doesn\'t work in the original equation.","Threw out both roots; x = 1 works: √(2 + 7) = 3 and 1 + 2 = 3."]],"math-0035":["Growth by the same percent each year means the population is multiplied by the same factor over any 6-year span. From 2010 to 2016 that factor is 15,000 ÷ 12,000 = 1.25. From 2016 to 2028 is 12 years, which is two 6-year spans, so multiply by 1.25 twice: 15,000 × 1.25 × 1.25 = 23,437.5, or about 23,400. (Starting from 2010 works too: 12,000 × 1.25³ gives the same number.) The trap 21,000 adds the same amount each period, which is linear growth, not percent growth.",["Applied only one 6-year growth period: 15,000 × 1.25 = 18,750. From 2016 to 2028 is 12 years, which is two periods.","Linear growth: added 3,000 per 6 years, but the problem says the same percent each year.","Added 25% + 25% = 50% to 15,000 instead of multiplying by 1.25 twice.","Correct: every 6 years the population is multiplied by 15,000 ÷ 12,000 = 1.25, so 15,000 × 1.25² ≈ 23,400."]],"math-0036":["A system has no solution when the lines are parallel but different. The lines have the same slope when the x- and y-coefficients are in the same ratio: k/2 = 8/k, so k² = 16 and k = 4 or k = −4. Now check each. k = 4: the equations are 4x + 8y = 6 and 2x + 4y = 3, and the first is 2 times the second, so they are the same line (infinitely many solutions). k = −4: the equations are −4x + 8y = 6 and 2x − 4y = 3. Multiply the second by −2 to get −4x + 8y = −6, which has the same left side as the first but a different right side, so the lines are parallel and never meet. Only k = −4 works.",["Correct: k = −4 gives −4x + 8y = 6 and 2x − 4y = 3; doubling the second and changing signs gives −4x + 8y = −6, a parallel line, so there is no solution.","With k = 4, the first equation is exactly 2 times the second, so the lines are the same and there are infinitely many solutions.","Stopped at k² = 16. That only says the lines have the same slope; you still have to check whether they are the same line.","Thought both values of k make the lines identical, but only k = 4 does."]],"math-0037":["Make each part as large as possible on its own, since x and y are independent. x² is largest at the end of the x-range farthest from 0: x = −3 gives 9, while x = 2 gives only 4. The expression subtracts y, so it is largest when y is as small as possible: y = −1. The greatest value is 9 − (−1) = 10. The traps come from grabbing the biggest number in each range without thinking about squaring or subtracting.",["Plugged in the largest value of each variable (x = 2, y = 4), but subtracting y means you want y small.","Used x = 2 as the way to make x² largest, but (−3)² = 9 is bigger than 2² = 4.","Took y = 0, but y can be as low as −1, and subtracting −1 adds 1.","Correct: x² is largest at x = −3 (x² = 9) and −y is largest at y = −1, so 9 − (−1) = 10."]],"math-0038":["By the remainder theorem, the remainder when p(x) is divided by x − a is p(a). Here x + 2 = x − (−2), so the remainder is p(−2). Compute carefully: (−2)³ = −8, −2(−2)² = −8, k(−2) = −2k, plus 4. So p(−2) = −12 − 2k. Set −12 − 2k = −10: −2k = 2, so k = −1. The most common trap is plugging in x = 2, which gives k = −7.",["Evaluated p(2) instead of p(−2); dividing by x + 2 means plugging in x = −2.","Correct: the remainder is p(−2) = −8 − 8 − 2k + 4 = −12 − 2k, and −12 − 2k = −10 gives k = −1.","Sign slip at the end: −2k = 2 gives k = −1, not 1.","Took (−2)³ as +8, which gives 4 − 2k = −10."]],"math-0039":["Test with numbers first: a = −2 and b = −1. Then a² = 4 and b² = 1, so a² < b² fails. 1/a = −0.5 and 1/b = −1, so 1/a < 1/b fails. b/a = 0.5, so b/a > 1 fails. ab = 2 and b² = 1, so ab > b² holds. To be sure it must always hold, factor: ab − b² = b(a − b). Since b < 0 and a − b < 0 (a is less than b), the product of two negatives is positive, so ab > b² for every allowed a and b. Remember: with negatives, the number farther from 0 has the bigger square and the reciprocals switch order.",["Correct: ab − b² = b(a − b), and both b and a − b are negative, so the product is positive.","a is farther from 0 than b, so a² is larger: a = −2, b = −1 gives 4 > 1.","Taking reciprocals of two negative numbers flips the order: a = −2, b = −1 gives −1/2 > −1.","b is closer to 0 than a, so b/a is between 0 and 1: a = −2, b = −1 gives 1/2."]],"math-0040":["Work from the inside out for each x, and do both orders. x = 1: f(g(1)) = f(4) = 4 and g(f(1)) = g(1) = 4. Equal. x = 2: f(g(2)) = f(3) = 2 but g(f(2)) = g(5) = 1. x = 3: f(g(3)) = f(2) = 5 but g(f(3)) = g(2) = 3. x = 4: f(g(4)) = f(5) = 3 but g(f(4)) = g(4) = 5. (x = 5 gives 1 and 2.) Only x = 1 works, and it makes sense: f(1) = 1, so applying f before or after g(1) = 4 changes nothing. The traps are a value where f(x) = g(x), a value where f(g(x)) = x, and the output 4.",["Correct: f(g(1)) = f(4) = 4 and g(f(1)) = g(1) = 4, so they match; no other x works.","f(g(2)) = f(3) = 2, which equals x, but g(f(2)) = g(5) = 1.","f(3) = g(3) = 2, but that is f(x) = g(x), not f(g(x)) = g(f(x)): f(g(3)) = f(2) = 5 and g(f(3)) = g(2) = 3.","Gave the common output 4 instead of the input x = 1 that produces it."]],"math-0041":["Solve each piece, then keep only the solutions that fall in that piece\'s domain. First piece: x² − 4 = 0 gives x = 2 or x = −2, but this rule only applies for x < 1, so keep only x = −2. Second piece: 2x − 5 = 0 gives x = 2.5, and 2.5 ≥ 1, so keep it. The solutions are −2 and 2.5, and their sum is 0.5. The big trap is keeping x = 2: at x = 2 the second rule applies, and f(2) = −1, not 0.",["Found only the root from the first piece and forgot that the second piece can also equal 0.","Correct: x² − 4 = 0 gives x = −2 (x = 2 is not less than 1), and 2x − 5 = 0 gives x = 2.5 (which is at least 1); −2 + 2.5 = 0.5.","Kept x = 2 from the first piece: −2 + 2 + 2.5 = 2.5. But the first rule only applies when x < 1.","Used x = 2 instead of x = −2 from the first piece."]],"math-0042":["Factor the top and bottom: x² − 1 = (x − 1)(x + 1) and x² − 2x − 3 = (x − 3)(x + 1). The common factor x + 1 cancels, leaving y = (x − 1)/(x − 3), with x = −1 left out. A vertical asymptote happens where the denominator is 0 after canceling, so only x = 3. At x = −1 the graph has a hole (a single missing point), because the factor that made the bottom 0 there canceled. At x = 1 the top is 0, so the graph touches the x-axis there.",["Sign slip: x² − 2x − 3 = (x − 3)(x + 1), so the denominator is 0 at x = 3 and x = −1, not −3.","The factor x + 1 cancels from the top and bottom, so x = −1 is a hole in the graph, not an asymptote.","x = 1 makes the numerator 0, so it is where the graph crosses the x-axis.","Correct: y = (x − 1)(x + 1)/((x − 3)(x + 1)) = (x − 1)/(x − 3) for x ≠ −1, and the remaining factor x − 3 makes the bottom 0 at x = 3."]],"math-0043":["Set up the inequality: 10 − 4cos(πt/6) ≥ 12, so −4cos(πt/6) ≥ 2 and cos(πt/6) ≤ −1/2 (dividing by −4 flips the sign). On one cycle, cosine is at most −1/2 from 2π/3 to 4π/3. So πt/6 runs from 2π/3 to 4π/3, which means t runs from 4 to 8. The depth is at least 12 feet from 4 a.m. to 8 a.m., a span of 4 hours. Check: d(4) = 10 − 4(−1/2) = 12 and d(6) = 14, the maximum. The traps give a single moment (6 or 8) or the period (12) instead of a length of time.",["Correct: 10 − 4cos(πt/6) ≥ 12 means cos(πt/6) ≤ −1/2, which holds for 2π/3 ≤ πt/6 ≤ 4π/3, that is, 4 ≤ t ≤ 8.","Gave the time of the highest water (t = 6, depth 14 feet), not how long the depth stays at 12 feet or more.","Gave the time when the depth falls back to 12 feet (t = 8), not the length of the interval from t = 4 to t = 8.","Gave the period of the function (2π ÷ π/6 = 12 hours), not the time spent at 12 feet or deeper."]],"math-0044":["Break 72 into prime factors: 72 = 8 · 9 = 2³ · 3². The log of a product is the sum of the logs, and an exponent inside a log comes out front as a multiplier. So log_b(72) = log_b(2³) + log_b(3²) = 3log_b(2) + 2log_b(3) = 3p + 2q. The traps multiply logs (6pq), raise them to powers (p³q²), or factor 72 wrong (2p + 3q, which is the log of 108).",["Correct: 72 = 2³ · 3², so log_b(72) = 3log_b(2) + 2log_b(3) = 3p + 2q.","Swapped the exponents: 72 = 2³ · 3², not 2² · 3³ (which is 108).","Multiplied the logs together; the log of a product is the sum of the logs, not their product.","Applied the exponents to the logs themselves; log_b(2³) is 3log_b(2), not (log_b 2)³."]],"math-0045":["Think of |x − 3| + |x + 1| as the distance from x to 3 plus the distance from x to −1 on a number line. The points 3 and −1 are 4 apart. If x is anywhere between them, the two distances add to exactly 4. If x is outside, the sum is more than 4. So the graph of y = |x − 3| + |x + 1| is a flat segment at y = 4 from x = −1 to x = 3, with lines rising on both sides. A horizontal line y = k misses the graph when k < 4, touches the whole flat part when k = 4 (infinitely many solutions), and crosses it exactly twice when k > 4. From 1 through 10, the values 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 work: 6 values. Check k = 6: x = 4 gives 1 + 5 = 6 and x = −2 gives 5 + 1 = 6.",["Off by one: found that k must be greater than 4 but counted 10 − 5 = 5; the integers 5 through 10 are 6 values.","Correct: the sum of distances from x to 3 and to −1 is never less than 4, equals 4 for every x from −1 to 3, and equals k at exactly two points when k > 4, so k = 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10.","Included k = 4, but |x − 3| + |x + 1| = 4 for every x between −1 and 3, which is infinitely many solutions.","Assumed every equation with two absolute values has 2 solutions, but for k < 4 there are none."]],"math-0046":["For an ellipse x²/a² + y²/b² = 1 with a > b, the foci lie on the long (major) axis, c units from the center, where c² = a² − b². Here a² = 25 and b² = 9, so c² = 16 and c = 4. The foci are at (4, 0) and (−4, 0), which are 8 units apart. Don\'t stop at c = 4, and don\'t mix up the ellipse rule with the hyperbola rule c² = a² + b².",["Found c = 4, the distance from the center to one focus, and stopped.","Gave the length of the minor axis (2 · 3), not the distance between the foci.","Correct: c² = a² − b² = 25 − 9 = 16, so each focus is 4 from the center and they are 8 apart.","Added instead of subtracted: for an ellipse c² = a² − b², not a² + b² (that rule is for a hyperbola)."]],"math-0047":["Points 3 units from the line y = 2 lie on two lines: y = 5 and y = −1. Points 5 units from the origin lie on the circle x² + y² = 25. Now intersect. On y = 5: x² + 25 = 25, so x = 0, only one point, because y = 5 just touches the top of the circle. On y = −1: x² + 1 = 25, so x² = 24 and x = ±√24, two points. Total: 3 points. The trap 4 assumes both lines cut through the circle.",["Found only the point (0, 5) where the line y = 5 touches the circle, and missed the line y = −1.","Used only the line y = −1 (or assumed one line touching gives nothing), missing the point (0, 5).","Correct: the points 3 from y = 2 lie on y = 5 and y = −1; the circle x² + y² = 25 touches y = 5 once at (0, 5) and crosses y = −1 twice, at (±√24, −1).","Assumed each line crosses the circle twice, but y = 5 only touches it at the top."]],"math-0048":["The perpendicular bisector passes through the midpoint of AB and is perpendicular to AB. Midpoint: ((−2 + 6)/2, (1 + 7)/2) = (2, 4). Slope of AB: (7 − 1)/(6 − (−2)) = 6/8 = 3/4. A perpendicular slope is the negative reciprocal: −4/3. Line: y − 4 = −(4/3)(x − 2). At x = 0: y = 4 + 8/3 = 20/3. Each wrong choice comes from doing only part of \\"negative reciprocal\\" (or none of it).",["Flipped the slope to 4/3 but forgot to change its sign.","Used the slope of AB itself (3/4), which gives a line through the midpoint that is not perpendicular.","Changed the sign of the slope (−3/4) but forgot to flip it.","Correct: the midpoint is (2, 4), the slope of AB is 6/8 = 3/4, so the bisector has slope −4/3, and y = 4 − (4/3)(0 − 2) = 20/3 at x = 0."]],"math-0049":["The segment from the center perpendicular to a chord cuts the chord in half. So you get a right triangle with legs 8 (half of 16) and 6, and the hypotenuse is a radius. r² = 8² + 6² = 100, so r = 10 and the area is πr² = 100π. Draw it: the center, the midpoint of the chord, and one end of the chord form the triangle.",["Used the distance from the center to the chord (6) as the radius.","Used half the chord (8) as the radius; it is only one leg of the right triangle.","Correct: half the chord (8) and the distance (6) are legs of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is the radius: √(64 + 36) = 10, so the area is 100π.","Used the whole chord (16) as a leg: 16² + 6² = 292. The segment from the center meets the chord at its midpoint, so the leg is 8."]],"math-0050":["Place the cube with V at (0, 0, 0) and the cube filling 0 to 6 in each direction. The faces that don\'t contain V are the ones at x = 6, y = 6, and z = 6; by symmetry, all three centers are the same distance from V. The center of the face x = 6 is (6, 3, 3). The distance is √(6² + 3² + 3²) = √54 = 3√6, about 7.3 cm. Without coordinates: the face center is 6 cm across from V\'s face, and its shadow on V\'s face is half a face diagonal (√18) from V, so the distance is √(36 + 18) = √54. The traps are other famous cube distances: a near face center (3√2) and the space diagonal (6√3).",["Used the center of a face that does contain V; that center is half a face diagonal away.","Measured to the midpoint of an edge of the far face instead of the center of the face.","Correct: with V at (0, 0, 0), C is at (6, 3, 3), so VC = √(36 + 9 + 9) = √54 = 3√6.","Gave the space diagonal of the cube, which goes to the opposite vertex, not to a face center."]],"math-0051":["Count everything, then subtract what\'s not allowed. All orders: 6! = 720. Orders with Ana and Ben together: glue them into one block, so there are 5 items to arrange (5! = 120), and inside the block they can sit 2 ways, giving 240. Orders with them apart: 720 − 240 = 480. The trap 600 forgets the factor of 2 for the order inside the block.",["This is the number of orders where Ana and Ben DO sit together (5! × 2), not apart.","Correct: 6! = 720 orders in all, minus 2 × 5! = 240 orders with Ana and Ben together, leaves 480.","Subtracted only 5! = 120 for Ana and Ben together, forgetting they can sit as Ana–Ben or Ben–Ana.","Ignored the restriction: 720 is every possible order."]],"math-0052":["Ask two questions about any study. First, how were people chosen? At random from the city\'s voter list, so the results can be generalized to that list, not to the whole country. Second, were people assigned to a treatment? No: they reported their own habits. That makes it an observational study, which can show an association but cannot prove that coffee causes less sleep (for example, people who sleep less might drink more coffee to stay awake). The only choice that respects both limits is the association statement about the city\'s voters.",["Overclaims cause: people chose how much coffee to drink, so this was an observational study, not an experiment.","Correct: a random sample lets you generalize to the list it came from, and an observational study shows association, not cause.","Two overreaches: no random assignment (so no cause), and the sample came from one city\'s voters, not the whole country.","Generalizes too far: the sample was drawn from one city\'s voter list, so it represents only that group."]],"math-0053":["A residual is actual minus predicted. The line predicts y = 2.4(5) + 31 = 12 + 31 = 43. The student actually scored 38, so the residual is 38 − 43 = −5. A negative residual means the point is below the line of best fit. Watch the order of subtraction; reversing it gives +5.",["Correct: the predicted score is 2.4(5) + 31 = 43, and 38 − 43 = −5; the student scored below the line.","Subtracted in the wrong order (predicted minus actual).","Copied the student\'s actual score instead of comparing it with the prediction.","Gave the predicted score, the intermediate result, instead of the residual."]],"math-0054":["Standard deviation measures how far values typically are from the mean. All four sets have mean 30, so compare distances from 30. Set II\'s values are within 4 of 30, so it has the smallest spread. Sets I, III, and IV all have the same range (10 to 50), so the range can\'t decide. Look at the distances: Set I: 20, 10, 0, 10, 20. Set III: 20, 0, 0, 0, 20. Set IV: 20, 20, 0, 20, 20. Set IV has the most values far from the mean, so it has the greatest standard deviation. No calculation needed: same range does not mean same standard deviation.",["Set I has the same range as Set IV, but two of its values (20 and 40) are only 10 from the mean.","Set II has the least spread of all: every value is within 4 of the mean.","Set III has the same range, but three of its five values sit right at the mean.","Correct: four of Set IV\'s five values are 20 from the mean, more than in any other set."]],"math-0055":["Let the club have 3k boys and 5k girls. After 6 boys join, (3k + 6) : 5k = 3 : 4. Cross-multiply: 4(3k + 6) = 3(5k), so 12k + 24 = 15k and k = 8. Girls: 5k = 40 (and boys went from 24 to 30; check 30:40 = 3:4). The other choices are numbers you meet on the way: k, the old number of boys, and the new number of boys.",["Found the multiplier k = 8 in 3k and 5k, but the number of girls is 5k.","Gave the original number of boys (3k = 24), not the number of girls.","Gave the number of boys after 6 joined (24 + 6), not the number of girls.","Correct: with 3k boys and 5k girls, (3k + 6)/5k = 3/4 gives 12k + 24 = 15k, so k = 8 and there are 5(8) = 40 girls."]],"math-0056":["The block 428571 has 6 digits. Divide 45 by 6: 45 = 6 × 7 + 3. So 7 complete blocks fill digits 1 through 42, and digit 45 is the 3rd digit of the next block. The 3rd digit of 428571 is 8. The remainder tells you the position in the block (a remainder of 0 would mean the last digit, 1). The wrong choices are the digits right next to the answer or the first digit of the block.",["Off by one: 2 is the 44th digit (the 2nd digit of a block). Since 45 = 6 × 7 + 3, you need the 3rd digit.","Assumed a new block starts at digit 45, but blocks start at digits 1, 7, 13, …, 43.","Off by one the other way: 5 is the 46th digit (the 4th digit of a block).","Correct: 7 full blocks use 42 digits, so the 45th digit is the 3rd digit of the block 428571, which is 8."]],"math-0057":["Work in fractions of the tank per hour. Pump A fills 1/6 per hour and Pump B fills 1/9 per hour. In the first 2 hours they fill 2(1/6) + 2(1/9) = 1/3 + 2/9 = 5/9 of the tank. That leaves 4/9. Pump B alone fills 1/9 per hour, so it needs (4/9) ÷ (1/9) = 4 more hours. The trap 3.6 is the answer to a different question (both pumps the whole time).",["Took the time for both pumps together (3.6 hours) and subtracted the 2 hours, as if Pump A never broke.","Gave the time for both pumps working together the whole way: 1 ÷ (1/6 + 1/9) = 3.6 hours.","Correct: in 2 hours the pumps fill 2/6 + 2/9 = 5/9 of the tank; Pump B fills the other 4/9 at 1/9 per hour in 4 hours.","Subtracted the 2 hours from Pump B\'s 9 hours, ignoring the part Pump A filled."]],"math-0058":["Let the original price be 1. After a 25% raise it is 1.25. Cutting it by x% multiplies it by (1 − x/100), and the result is 10% below the original, or 0.90. So 1.25(1 − x/100) = 0.90, which gives 1 − x/100 = 0.72 and x = 28. Check with $100: up to $125, down 28% ($35) to $90. The trap 35 adds percents, and 72 stops one step early.",["Correct: 1.25(1 − x/100) = 0.90, so 1 − x/100 = 0.72 and x = 28.","Undid the 25% raise with a 20% cut, then took another 10% off. That last 10% is of the original price, which is only 8% of the raised price: 20 + 8 = 28.","Added the percents (25 + 10); percent changes multiply, they don\'t add.","Intermediate result: 0.72 is the fraction of the raised price that is kept, not the percent taken off."]],"math-0059":["The combined mean is a weighted average. Let Class B have n students. Total points: 18(78) + 88n = 82(18 + n). Then 1,404 + 88n = 1,476 + 82n, so 6n = 72 and n = 12. Quick check: the combined mean 82 is 4 above Class A and 6 below Class B. The class the mean sits closer to is the bigger one, and the sizes are in the ratio 6 : 4, so A : B = 18 : 12. The trap 27 flips that ratio.",["Correct: 18(78) + 88n = 82(18 + n) gives 1,404 + 88n = 1,476 + 82n, so 6n = 72 and n = 12.","Assumed the classes are the same size, but then the combined mean would be 83, halfway between 78 and 88.","Reversed the balance: the combined mean (82) is closer to Class A\'s mean, so Class A must be the bigger class, not Class B.","Gave the total number of students in both classes (18 + 12)."]],"math-0060":["Convert the floor to inches first: 18 ft = 216 in and 12 ft = 144 in. Tiles 8 in wide fit 216 ÷ 8 = 27 across and 144 ÷ 8 = 18 down, so 27 × 18 = 486 tiles. Boxes: 486 ÷ 25 = 19.44, and you can only buy whole boxes, so you need 20. The traps come from area conversion slips (1 ft² = 144 in², not 12) and from rounding down when you need to cover everything.",["Converted square feet to square inches by multiplying by 12 instead of 144 (there are 144 square inches in a square foot).","Divided 216 square feet by 2/3 foot, a length, instead of by the tile\'s area (4/9 square foot).","Rounded 486 ÷ 25 = 19.44 down; 19 boxes hold only 475 tiles, so you need a 20th box.","Correct: the floor is 216 inches by 144 inches, which is 27 × 18 = 486 tiles, and 486 ÷ 25 = 19.44 rounds up to 20 boxes."]],"math-0061":["To find 15% of $48.00, multiply by 0.15: 0.15 × 48 = 7.20, so the tip is $7.20. A quick check: 10% is $4.80 and 5% is half of that, $2.40, for $7.20 in all. Watch for two traps: dividing by 15 instead of taking 15%, and answering with the total bill ($55.20) when only the tip was asked for.",["Decimal slip: 15% is 0.15, not 0.015. This is 1.5% of the bill.","Divided 48 by 15 instead of multiplying 48 by 0.15.","Correct: 15% of $48.00 is 0.15 × 48 = $7.20.","This is the bill plus the tip. The question asks for the tip only."]],"math-0062":["Use only the three months named: April (3.1), May (3.6), and June (5.3). Their sum is 12.0, and 12.0 ÷ 3 = 4.0 inches. Leave March out; including it gives 14.8 ÷ 4 = 3.7, which isn\'t what was asked. The traps: 12.0 is the total before dividing, 3.6 is the median, and 4.2 averages only the smallest and largest values.",["This is the median of the three months (the middle value), not the mean.","Correct: (3.1 + 3.6 + 5.3) ÷ 3 = 12.0 ÷ 3 = 4.0 inches.","Averaged only the smallest and largest of the three values, (3.1 + 5.3) ÷ 2. The mean uses every value.","This is the total for the three months. Divide by 3 to get the mean."]],"math-0063":["Distribute first: 2x + 18 = 5x − 3. Subtract 2x from both sides and add 3 to both sides: 21 = 3x, so x = 7. Check: 2(7 + 9) = 32 and 5(7) − 3 = 32. The usual traps are multiplying only the x by 2 (which gives 4) and moving −3 to the other side without changing its sign (which gives 5).",["Sign slip: 3x = 21 gives x = 7, not −7.","Didn\'t distribute the 2 to the 9: 2x + 9 = 5x − 3 gives x = 4.","Sign slip moving −3 across: 18 − 3 = 15 instead of 18 + 3 = 21.","Correct: 2x + 18 = 5x − 3, so 21 = 3x and x = 7."]],"math-0064":["The perimeter counts two lengths and two widths: 2(14) + 2w = 46, so 2w = 18 and w = 9 feet. The area is 14 × 9 = 126 square feet. The trap answers come from using 18 as the width (forgetting there are two widths), using half the perimeter, 23, as the width, or multiplying by the perimeter itself.",["Correct: the width is (46 − 28) ÷ 2 = 9 feet, and 14 × 9 = 126 square feet.","Forgot to split the leftover 18 feet between the two widths, so used 18 as the width.","Used half the perimeter, 23, as the width instead of subtracting the length.","Multiplied the length by the whole perimeter (14 × 46). The perimeter is not a side length."]],"math-0065":["There are 5 + 3 + 4 = 12 socks. The socks that are not black are the 3 white and 4 gray ones, so 7 of 12. The probability is 7/12. Another way: P(black) = 5/12, so P(not black) = 1 − 5/12 = 7/12. The trap is answering with P(black) or counting only one of the other colors.",["This is the probability of white only (3 of 12). Gray socks are also not black.","This is the probability of gray only (4 of 12). White socks are also not black.","This is the probability that the sock is black, the opposite of what was asked.","Correct: 3 white + 4 gray = 7 socks that are not black, out of 12, so 7/12."]],"math-0066":["Each cup of sugar goes with 3/2 cups of flour. For 5 cups of sugar: 5 × 3/2 = 7.5, or 7 1/2 cups of flour. Set it up as a proportion to be safe: 3/2 = f/5. The traps are flipping the ratio and adding 3 to both amounts, which doesn\'t keep the ratio the same.",["Flipped the ratio: used 2 cups of flour per 3 cups of sugar (5 × 2/3).","Added instead of scaling: sugar went up by 3 cups, so flour went up by 3 cups. Ratios scale by multiplying.","Correct: 3/2 = f/5, so f = 5 × 3/2 = 7 1/2 cups.","Just added the two given amounts of flour and sugar (3 + 5)."]],"math-0067":["Find the rate first: 150 miles ÷ 6 gallons = 25 miles per gallon. Then 225 miles ÷ 25 miles per gallon = 9 gallons. Or scale: 225 is 1.5 times 150, so the gas is 1.5 × 6 = 9 gallons. Stopping at the rate (25) or flipping the proportion (4) are the common slips. Sanity check: the trip is 1.5 times as long, so the answer should be a bit more than 6.",["Set up the proportion upside down: 6 × 150/225 = 4. A longer trip needs more gas, not less.","Correct: the car gets 150 ÷ 6 = 25 miles per gallon, and 225 ÷ 25 = 9 gallons.","Intermediate result: 25 is the car\'s miles per gallon, not the gallons needed.","Divided the new distance by the old number of gallons (225 ÷ 6). That mixes up the rate."]],"math-0068":["Replace x with 3 and follow the order of operations: square first, then multiply, then subtract. 3² = 9, 2 × 9 = 18, and 18 − 3 = 15. The trap 33 comes from squaring 2 × 3 = 6; in 2x², only the x is squared.",["Correct: f(3) = 2(3²) − 3 = 2(9) − 3 = 15.","Forgot to subtract 3: 2(3²) = 18 is only the first part of f(3).","Sign slip: added 3 instead of subtracting it (18 + 3).","Squared 2x instead of x: (2 · 3)² − 3 = 33. Only x is squared."]],"math-0069":["The three angles of any triangle add to 180°. The two given angles add to 35 + 85 = 120°, so the third angle is 180 − 120 = 60°. The traps are stopping at the sum (120°) or subtracting only one of the given angles.",["Correct: the angles of a triangle add to 180°, so 180 − 35 − 85 = 60°.","Subtracted only 85° from 180°. Both given angles must be subtracted.","This is the sum of the two given angles, not the third angle.","Subtracted only 35° from 180°. Both given angles must be subtracted."]],"math-0070":["Sort first: 3, 5, 6, 8, 12, 14. With 6 values there are two middle values, 6 and 8, so the median is their average, 7. The traps: the mean is 48 ÷ 6 = 8, the range is 11, and taking the middle of the unsorted list gives 8.5.",["Correct: in order the values are 3, 5, 6, 8, 12, 14, and the two middle values average to (6 + 8) ÷ 2 = 7.","This is the mean (48 ÷ 6 = 8), not the median.","Averaged the two middle values of the list as written (12 and 5) without sorting it first.","This is the range (14 − 3), not the median."]],"math-0071":["f(g(x)) = √(4 − 1/(x − 1)). Two conditions: x ≠ 1, and 4 − 1/(x − 1) ≥ 0, so 1/(x − 1) ≤ 4. Split by the sign of x − 1. If x < 1, then 1/(x − 1) is negative, which is always ≤ 4, so every x < 1 works. If x > 1, multiply by the positive x − 1: 1 ≤ 4x − 4, so x ≥ 5/4. The domain is x < 1 or x ≥ 5/4. Check x = 1.1: g = 10, and √(4 − 10) is not real, so values just above 1 must be left out. The trap is multiplying an inequality by an expression without knowing its sign.",["Multiplied 1/(x − 1) ≤ 4 by (x − 1) as if it were always positive. That misses every x < 1, where g(x) is negative and the square root is fine.","Flipped the inequality for every x. When x > 1 the factor (x − 1) is positive, so the inequality does not flip there.","Correct: you need x ≠ 1 and 1/(x − 1) ≤ 4. That holds for all x < 1 (g is negative) and for x ≥ 5/4.","Only removed x = 1 for g. The square root also needs 4 − g(x) ≥ 0, which rules out 1 < x < 5/4."]],"math-0072":["The lines are parallel (or the same) when the coefficients are proportional: a(a + 1) = 3 · 2, so a² + a − 6 = 0 and a = 2 or a = −3. Now check each. a = 2: both equations become 2x + 3y = 6, the same line, so infinitely many solutions. a = −3: the equations become −3x + 3y = 6 (so y − x = 2) and 2x − 2y = 6 (so y − x = −3). These lines are parallel and different, so there is no solution. The trap is stopping at a² + a − 6 = 0: that equation finds both \'parallel\' and \'same line\' cases, and you have to test the constants to tell them apart.",["Correct: a = −3 makes the lines parallel and different (y − x = 2 and x − y = 3), so they never meet.","At a = 2 both equations become 2x + 3y = 6, the same line, so there are infinitely many solutions, not none.","These are the two values that make the lines parallel, but at a = 2 the lines are the same line, which has infinitely many solutions.","There is a value: a = −3 gives two parallel lines that never meet."]],"math-0073":["Count the complement. All committees of 4 from 11 students: C(11, 4) = 330. All-junior committees: C(6, 4) = 15. All-senior committees: C(5, 4) = 5. So 330 − 15 − 5 = 310. Check by cases: 3 juniors + 1 senior = 20 × 5 = 100, 2 + 2 = 15 × 10 = 150, 1 + 3 = 6 × 10 = 60, and 100 + 150 + 60 = 310. The tempting 1,080 comes from \'choose a junior, choose a senior, then fill the rest\': a committee with 2 juniors and 2 seniors gets counted 4 times that way.",["Correct: 330 total committees, minus 15 that are all juniors and 5 that are all seniors, is 310.","Subtracted the 15 all-junior committees but forgot the 5 all-senior ones.","This is every possible committee of 4 (11 choose 4). It still includes the all-junior and all-senior ones.","Picked 1 junior, then 1 senior, then any 2 others (6 × 5 × 36). This counts the same committee many times."]],"math-0074":["First find cos θ. Since sin²θ + cos²θ = 1, cos²θ = 1 − 9/25 = 16/25, so cos θ = ±4/5. The angle is between π/2 and π (Quadrant II), where cosine is negative, so cos θ = −4/5. Then sin(2θ) = 2(3/5)(−4/5) = −24/25. Quick check: 2θ is between π and 2π, where sine is negative. The main trap is the sign of cos θ; 6/5 is impossible because a sine can never be more than 1.",["Doubled sin θ: 2 × 3/5. The sine of a doubled angle is not double the sine, and no sine can be greater than 1.","Used cos θ = +4/5. Between π/2 and π, cosine is negative.","This is cos(2θ) = 1 − 2 sin²θ, not sin(2θ).","Correct: in Quadrant II, cos θ = −4/5, so sin(2θ) = 2(3/5)(−4/5) = −24/25."]],"math-0075":["List the rolls with a sum of at least 9: (3,6), (6,3), (4,5), (5,4), (4,6), (6,4), (5,5), (5,6), (6,5), (6,6). That is 10 equally likely rolls. The ones with a 6 are (3,6), (6,3), (4,6), (6,4), (5,6), (6,5), (6,6): 7 rolls. So the probability is 7/10. The condition shrinks the sample space to those 10 rolls, so dividing by 36 is the classic trap. Also count (6, 6): leaving it out gives 6/10 = 3/5.",["This is the chance of \'sum at least 9 and a 6 showing\' out of all 36 rolls. Given the sum is at least 9, divide by 10, not 36.","This is the chance of at least one 6 out of all 36 rolls. It ignores the condition on the sum.","Left out the roll (6, 6). It shows a 6 too, so 7 of the 10 rolls count, not 6.","Correct: 10 rolls have a sum of at least 9, and 7 of them include a 6."]],"math-0076":["Draw the heights from A and B down to DC. They cut off pieces x and y at the two ends, with x + y = 20 − 6 = 14. Both right triangles have the same height h: 13² − x² = h² = 15² − y². So y² − x² = 225 − 169 = 56, and since (y − x)(y + x) = 56 and y + x = 14, y − x = 4. That gives x = 5, y = 9, and h = √(169 − 25) = 12. Area = (6 + 20)/2 × 12 = 156. A shortcut: slide side BC over so it starts at A. That makes a triangle with sides 13, 14, 15, area 84, and height 12. The trapezoid is that triangle plus a 6-by-12 parallelogram: 84 + 72 = 156. The trap is guessing the height from a slanted side.",["This is only the area of the 13-14-15 triangle you get by sliding one leg over. You still need the 6-by-12 parallelogram.","Correct: the height is 12, so the area is (6 + 20)/2 × 12 = 156.","Used 14, the average of the two legs, as the height. A slanted side is not the height.","Found the height of 12 but forgot to halve the sum of the bases: (6 + 20) × 12."]],"math-0077":["Rewrite log₄(x) in base 2. Since 4 = 2², log₄(x) = log₂(x)/log₂(4) = ½ log₂(x). The equation becomes log₂(x) + ½ log₂(x) = 6, so (3/2) log₂(x) = 6 and log₂(x) = 4. Then x = 2⁴ = 16. Check: log₂(16) = 4 and log₄(16) = 2, and 4 + 2 = 6. The traps come from mixing up the base change (getting 4 or 8) or applying log rules across different bases (getting 512).",["Changed bases the wrong way: used log₄(x) = 2 log₂(x). Since 4 = 2², log₄(x) is half of log₂(x).","Treated log₄(x) as if it were log₂(x), so 2 log₂(x) = 6.","Correct: log₄(x) = ½ log₂(x), so (3/2) log₂(x) = 6, log₂(x) = 4, and x = 16.","Combined the logs as if the bases multiply: log₈(x²) = 6. The product rule only works when the bases are the same."]],"math-0078":["The sum is (a + bi) + (a − bi) = 2a = 6, so a = 3. The product is a² + b² = 25, so b² = 16 and b = 4 (since b > 0). Then z = 3 + 4i and z² = 9 + 2(3)(4i) + (4i)² = 9 + 24i − 16 = −7 + 24i. The traps are all squaring slips: dropping or halving the middle term, or forgetting that i² = −1.",["Forgot to double the middle term: (a + bi)² = a² + 2abi + b²i², and 2ab = 24, not 12.","Correct: a = 3 and b = 4, so z² = 9 + 24i + 16i² = 9 + 24i − 16 = −7 + 24i.","Squared each part on its own (a² and b²) and skipped the middle term. (a + bi)² is not a² + b²i.","Treated i² as +1, so 9 + 16 = 25. In fact i² = −1."]],"math-0079":["g(x) is defined only when the input to f, which is x/2 + 1, is in f\'s domain. Solve −2 ≤ x/2 + 1 ≤ 6: subtract 1 to get −3 ≤ x/2 ≤ 5, then multiply by 2 to get −6 ≤ x ≤ 10. The 3 − 2(...) part changes only the outputs (the range), not the domain. Traps: keeping f\'s domain, plugging the endpoints into x/2 + 1 (which gives 0 to 4), or undoing the steps in the wrong order (which gives −5 to 11).",["Plugged the endpoints −2 and 6 into x/2 + 1. That runs the transformation forward instead of solving for x.","Kept f\'s domain. The input x/2 + 1 changes which x-values are allowed.","Undid the steps in the wrong order (doubled, then subtracted 1). Subtract 1 first, then double.","Correct: g needs −2 ≤ x/2 + 1 ≤ 6, so −3 ≤ x/2 ≤ 5 and −6 ≤ x ≤ 10."]],"math-0080":["Don\'t solve for x (the roots are messy). Cube the given equation instead: (x + 1/x)³ = x³ + 3x + 3/x + 1/x³ = x³ + 1/x³ + 3(x + 1/x). So 5³ = x³ + 1/x³ + 3(5), and x³ + 1/x³ = 125 − 15 = 110. The traps come from forgetting the middle terms (125), handling them halfway (122), or using x² + 1/x² = 23 times 5 without subtracting the extra x + 1/x (115).",["Cubed both sides as if (x + 1/x)³ = x³ + 1/x³. The cube has middle terms too.","Remembered the middle terms but subtracted only 3 instead of 3(x + 1/x) = 15.","Found x² + 1/x² = 23 and multiplied by 5. That product equals x³ + 1/x³ + (x + 1/x), so 5 more must come off.","Correct: (x + 1/x)³ = x³ + 1/x³ + 3(x + 1/x), so 125 = x³ + 1/x³ + 15, giving 110."]],"math-0081":["Replace a with 5. The expression 4a means 4 times a, so 4a = 4 × 5 = 20. Then subtract 7: 20 − 7 = 13. The traps: reading 4a as the number 45 gives 38, adding 7 instead of subtracting gives 27, and stopping at 4 × 5 = 20 leaves out the − 7.",["Correct: 4(5) − 7 = 20 − 7 = 13.","Forgot to subtract 7: 4(5) = 20 is only the first step.","Sign slip: added 7 instead of subtracting it (20 + 7).","Wrote 4a as the two-digit number 45. 4a means 4 × 5 = 20."]],"math-0082":["The equation says x divided by 4 is 12. Undo the division by multiplying both sides by 4: x = 48. Check: 48 ÷ 4 = 12. The traps use the wrong operation: 12 ÷ 4 = 3, 12 − 4 = 8, and 12 + 4 = 16.",["Divided 12 by 4. To undo dividing by 4, multiply by 4.","Subtracted 4 from 12. The 4 divides x; it isn\'t subtracted from it.","Added 4 to 12. The 4 divides x, so multiply both sides by 4.","Correct: multiply both sides by 4: x = 12 × 4 = 48."]],"math-0083":["30% means 30 out of every 100, or 0.30. Multiply: 0.30 × 70 = 21 students. The tempting 49 is the other 70% of the class, the students who don\'t ride the bus. 40 and 100 come from subtracting or adding 30 as if it were a number of students.",["Correct: 30% of 70 is 0.30 × 70 = 21.","Subtracted 30 from 70. A percent is a part out of 100, not a number to subtract.","This is the number who do NOT ride the bus (70 − 21).","Added 70 and 30. A percent tells you to multiply, not add."]],"math-0084":["The mean is the total divided by how many values there are. The total is 5 + 8 + 9 + 14 = 36 minutes, and there are 4 days, so the mean is 36 ÷ 4 = 9 minutes. The traps: 36 is the total before dividing, 12 divides by 3 instead of 4, and 8.5 is the median.",["This is the median (the average of the middle values 8 and 9), not the mean.","Correct: (5 + 8 + 9 + 14) ÷ 4 = 36 ÷ 4 = 9.","Divided the total by 3 instead of by 4, the number of days.","This is the total time. Divide by the 4 days to get the mean."]],"math-0085":["Perimeter is the distance all the way around. A rectangle has two lengths and two widths: 12 + 12 + 7 + 7 = 38 feet. The traps: 84 is the area (length times width), 19 counts only one length and one width, and 26 skips one of the long sides.",["Added just one length and one width. A rectangle has 2 of each.","Left out one side: added 12 + 7 + 7, so one 12-foot side is missing.","Correct: 12 + 7 + 12 + 7 = 2(12 + 7) = 38 feet.","This is the area (12 × 7) in square feet, not the perimeter."]],"math-0086":["\\"Per minute\\" means divide by the number of minutes: 90 ÷ 5 = 18 pages per minute. Check: 18 pages each minute for 5 minutes is 90 pages. The wrong choices use the other operations: 90 − 5, 90 + 5, and 90 × 5.",["Correct: 90 pages ÷ 5 minutes = 18 pages per minute.","Subtracted 5 from 90. A rate per minute needs division.","Added 5 to 90. A rate per minute needs division.","Multiplied 90 by 5. Pages per minute means pages divided by minutes."]],"math-0087":["2⁵ means 2 multiplied by itself 5 times: 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 32. The common slips are 2 × 5 = 10 (multiplying the base by the exponent), 2 + 5 = 7, and 5² = 25 (swapping the base and the exponent).",["Added the base and the exponent (2 + 5).","Multiplied the base by the exponent (2 × 5). An exponent means repeated multiplication.","Switched the base and the exponent: 5² = 25.","Correct: 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 32."]],"math-0088":["Each foot is 12 inches, so 5 feet is 5 × 12 = 60 inches. The traps mix up units or operations: 5 + 12 = 17 adds instead of multiplying, 15 uses 3 (feet per yard), and 180 uses 36 (inches per yard).",["Used 3 per foot. There are 3 feet in a yard, but 12 inches in a foot.","Added 12 to 5. Each foot is 12 inches, so multiply.","Correct: 5 × 12 = 60 inches.","Used 36, the number of inches in a yard, instead of 12 inches in a foot."]],"math-0089":["Probability = favorable outcomes ÷ all equally likely outcomes. The 8 sections are equal, and 3 are red, so the probability is 3/8. The traps: 3/5 compares red to the non-red sections, 5/8 is the chance of not red, and 3/4 compares red only to blue.",["Correct: 3 of the 8 equal sections are red, so the probability is 3/8.","Compared red to the other sections (3 red to 5 not red). Probability compares red to ALL 8 sections.","This is the probability of NOT landing on red (5 of 8 sections).","Compared red to blue (3 red to 4 blue). Probability compares red to ALL 8 sections."]],"math-0090":["A prime number has exactly two factors: 1 and itself. 21 = 3 × 7, 27 = 3 × 9, and 33 = 3 × 11, so each has 3 as a factor. 29 isn\'t divisible by 2, 3, or 5 (and 6 × 6 = 36 is already past 29, so there is nothing else to check), so 29 is prime. Being odd isn\'t enough: 21, 27, and 33 are all odd.",["21 = 3 × 7, so it has factors other than 1 and itself.","27 = 3 × 9, so it isn\'t prime, even though it is odd.","Correct: 29 has no factors other than 1 and 29.","33 = 3 × 11, so it isn\'t prime."]],"math-0091":["Every inch on the map stands for 15 real miles. The towns are 6 inches apart, so the real distance is 6 × 15 = 90 miles. The wrong choices divide the numbers (15 ÷ 6 or 6 ÷ 15) or add them (15 + 6).",["Divided 6 by 15. Each map inch stands for 15 miles, so multiply.","Divided 15 by 6. Each map inch stands for 15 miles, so multiply.","Added 15 and 6. Each of the 6 inches stands for 15 miles.","Correct: 6 inches × 15 miles per inch = 90 miles."]],"math-0092":["The range is the greatest value minus the least value. Sorted, the numbers are 4, 7, 12, 15, 19, so the range is 19 − 4 = 15. The traps: 12 is the median, 11.4 is the mean, and 3 subtracts the first and last numbers in the order they were listed instead of the largest and smallest.",["Subtracted the first number listed from the last (15 − 12). Use the greatest and least values.","This is the mean (57 ÷ 5), a measure of center, not spread.","This is the median (the middle value after sorting), not the range.","Correct: greatest minus least = 19 − 4 = 15."]],"math-0093":["Fill the restricted places first: the last digit (it must be even) and the first digit (it can\'t be 0). Split into two cases. Case 1, last digit 0: the first digit can be 1-9 (9 ways), then 8 and 7 ways for the middle digits: 9 × 8 × 7 = 504. Case 2, last digit 2, 4, 6, or 8 (4 ways): the first digit can\'t be 0 or the last digit, so 8 ways, then 8 and 7 ways: 4 × 8 × 8 × 7 = 1,792. Total: 2,296. The trap 2,520 (5 × 9 × 8 × 7) gives the first digit 9 choices every time, which is only true when the last digit is 0. 2,268 assumes exactly half of the 4,536 different-digit numbers are even.",["Took half of 4,536. Even numbers aren\'t exactly half here, because 0 is even but can\'t be the first digit.","Correct: ending in 0 gives 9 × 8 × 7 = 504; ending in 2, 4, 6, or 8 gives 4 × 8 × 8 × 7 = 1,792; 504 + 1,792 = 2,296.","Used 5 × 9 × 8 × 7. When the last digit is 2, 4, 6, or 8, the first digit has only 8 choices (not 0 and not the last digit).","This counts every 4-digit number with different digits, odd ones included."]],"math-0094":["Notice that 9ˣ = (3²)ˣ = (3ˣ)². Let u = 3ˣ. The equation becomes u² − 4u + 3 = 0, which factors as (u − 1)(u − 3) = 0, so u = 1 or u = 3. Now go back to x: 3ˣ = 1 means x = 0, and 3ˣ = 3 means x = 1. Check x = 0: 1 − 4 + 3 = 0. Check x = 1: 9 − 12 + 3 = 0. The traps: stopping at u = 1 and 3 (those are values of 3ˣ, not x), and forgetting that 3⁰ = 1, which loses x = 0.",["Correct: with u = 3ˣ, u² − 4u + 3 = 0, so u = 1 or u = 3. Then 3ˣ = 1 gives x = 0, and 3ˣ = 3 gives x = 1.","Dropped 3ˣ = 1. It has a solution: 3⁰ = 1, so x = 0.","Stopped at u = 1 and u = 3. Those are values of 3ˣ, not of x.","Sign slip: factored as (u + 1)(u + 3), which gives impossible negative values of 3ˣ. The correct factors are (u − 1)(u − 3)."]],"math-0095":["Move everything to one side and factor: 2sin²θ − sin θ = 0, so sin θ(2sin θ − 1) = 0. Either sin θ = 0 or sin θ = 1/2. On 0 ≤ θ < 2π, sin θ = 0 at θ = 0 and π (not 2π, which is excluded). sin θ = 1/2 at θ = π/6 and 5π/6, since sine is positive in Quadrants I and II. That\'s 4 values. The big trap is dividing both sides by sin θ: it looks like simplifying, but it deletes the sin θ = 0 solutions and leaves only 2.",["Divided both sides by sin θ, which throws away every solution where sin θ = 0.","Found only one angle with sin θ = 1/2. Sine is positive in Quadrants I and II, so there are two.","Correct: sin θ = 0 gives θ = 0 and π; sin θ = 1/2 gives θ = π/6 and 5π/6.","Counted θ = 2π, which the interval excludes (θ < 2π)."]],"math-0096":["Write out a few terms: a₁ = 2, a₂ = 3(2) − 2 = 4, a₃ = 3(4) − 2 = 10, a₄ = 3(10) − 2 = 28. Each term is 1 more than a power of 3: 2 = 3⁰ + 1, 4 = 3¹ + 1, 10 = 3² + 1, 28 = 3³ + 1. The exponent is always 1 less than n, so aₙ = 3ⁿ⁻¹ + 1 and a₂₀ = 3¹⁹ + 1. Test any formula on a₁ before trusting it: 3²⁰ + 1 uses n instead of n − 1, and 2 · 3¹⁹ ignores the \\"− 2\\" in the rule.",["Sign slip in the pattern. Check n = 1: 3⁰ − 1 = 0, but a₁ = 2.","Correct: the terms are 2, 4, 10, 28, ..., which equal 3ⁿ⁻¹ + 1, so a₂₀ = 3¹⁹ + 1.","Ignored the \\"− 2\\" and treated it as a geometric sequence (2, 6, 18, ...). The real terms are 2, 4, 10, 28.","Off by one: used 3ⁿ + 1. Check n = 1: 3¹ + 1 = 4, but a₁ = 2."]],"math-0097":["Count unordered pairs of candies. There are C(9, 2) = 36 pairs. Pairs with no cherry (both lime): C(4, 2) = 6, so 30 pairs have at least one cherry. Pairs with both cherry: C(5, 2) = 10. Given at least one cherry, the probability is 10/30 = 1/3. The trap 1/2 treats \\"at least one is cherry\\" as \\"the first is cherry\\"; those aren\'t the same, because the cherry could be the second candy. 5/6 is the probability of the condition, not of both cherry given it.",["Correct: of the 36 equally likely pairs, 30 have at least one cherry and 10 are both cherry; 10/30 = 1/3.","Answered a different question: given the FIRST candy is cherry, the second is cherry with probability 4/8. \\"At least one\\" is a weaker condition.","This is the chance that one candy is cherry (5/9). It ignores the second candy and the condition.","This is the probability of the condition itself (at least one cherry), not of both being cherry given it."]],"math-0098":["Dividing by a quadratic leaves a remainder of degree less than 2, so call it mx + b: p(x) = (x − 2)(x + 1)q(x) + mx + b. At x = 2 the first part is 0, so p(2) = 2m + b. By the remainder theorem p(2) = 5. At x = −1, p(−1) = −m + b = −4. Subtract: 3m = 9, so m = 3 and b = −1. The remainder is 3x − 1. Check: 3(2) − 1 = 5 and 3(−1) − 1 = −4. The trap −3x − 1 plugs in x = −2 and x = 1, the opposite signs of the real roots.",["Multiplied the two remainders. The new remainder can be a linear expression, and it must give 5 at x = 2 and −4 at x = −1.","Added the two remainders. Test it: a constant 1 would give a remainder of 1 for both divisors, not 5 and −4.","Used the wrong inputs, x = −2 and x = 1. The remainder theorem for x − 2 uses x = 2, and for x + 1 uses x = −1.","Correct: the remainder is r(x) = mx + b with r(2) = 5 and r(−1) = −4, so m = 3 and b = −1."]],"math-0099":["Track each step. Adding 5 to every score adds 5 to the mean (75) but leaves the standard deviation at 8, because every score moves by the same amount and the gaps between scores don\'t change. Multiplying every score by 1.1 multiplies the mean (75 × 1.1 = 82.5) AND the standard deviation (8 × 1.1 = 8.8), because the gaps get 10% wider. The traps: treating the standard deviation like the mean (14.3), thinking a percent increase leaves spread alone (8.0), or applying the steps in the wrong order (82.0).",["Did the steps in the wrong order for the mean: 70 × 1.1 + 5 = 82. The 5 points were added first.","Right mean, but a 10% increase stretches the spread too, so the standard deviation becomes 8 × 1.1.","Correct: mean (70 + 5) × 1.1 = 82.5; standard deviation 8 × 1.1 = 8.8, since adding 5 doesn\'t change spread.","Added 5 to the standard deviation as well: (8 + 5) × 1.1. Adding the same amount to every score doesn\'t change the spread."]],"math-0100":["Complete the square: x² − 6x + 9 + y² + 8y + 16 = k + 9 + 16, so (x − 3)² + (y + 4)² = k + 25. The center is (3, −4). A circle is tangent to the y-axis when its radius equals the horizontal distance from the center to that axis, which is |3| = 3. So r² = k + 25 = 9, and k = −16. The trap −9 uses the distance to the x-axis (4); the positive choices forget that completing the square added 25 to the right side.",["Correct: (x − 3)² + (y + 4)² = k + 25. Tangent to the y-axis means the radius equals the distance 3 from the center to that axis, so k + 25 = 9.","Made the circle tangent to the x-axis: the center (3, −4) is 4 units from the x-axis, so this circle would cross the y-axis.","Set k equal to r² = 9 and forgot the 25 that completing the square adds to the right side.","Used the x-axis distance (4) and also forgot the 25 from completing the square."]],"math-0101":["Use the Pythagorean theorem: a² + b² = c². Here 9² + 12² = 81 + 144 = 225, so c = √225 = 15 inches. (9-12-15 is the 3-4-5 triangle scaled by 3.) The traps: 225 forgets the square root, 21 just adds the legs, and 108 multiplies them.",["Correct: 9² + 12² = 81 + 144 = 225, and √225 = 15.","Added the two legs. The Pythagorean theorem adds the SQUARES of the legs.","Multiplied the two legs. That isn\'t part of the Pythagorean theorem.","This is c², not c. Take the square root: √225 = 15."]],"math-0102":["The midpoint is the average of the endpoints: add the x-coordinates and divide by 2, then do the same for the y-coordinates. x: (2 + 8)/2 = 5. y: (7 + (−1))/2 = 6/2 = 3. The midpoint is (5, 3). The traps: (10, 6) forgets to divide by 2, and (6, −8) and (3, −4) subtract instead of add.",["Took half the DIFFERENCE of each pair of coordinates. The midpoint uses half the SUM.","Correct: ((2 + 8)/2, (7 + (−1))/2) = (10/2, 6/2) = (5, 3).","Subtracted the coordinates instead of averaging them.","Added the coordinates but forgot to divide by 2."]],"math-0103":["Slope = (change in y)/(change in x) = (12 − 4)/(5 − 1) = 8/4 = 2. Keep the order the same on top and bottom: if you start with the point (5, 12) on top, start with it on the bottom too. The traps: 1/2 is run over rise, 8 is the rise alone, and −2 mixes the order of subtraction.",["Subtracted in mixed order: (12 − 4)/(1 − 5). Subtract the y\'s and the x\'s in the SAME order.","Flipped the fraction: change in x over change in y. Slope is rise over run.","Correct: (12 − 4)/(5 − 1) = 8/4 = 2.","This is only the change in y (the rise). Divide it by the change in x."]],"math-0104":["The area of a circle is A = πr². With r = 5, A = π × 5² = 25π square centimeters. The traps: 10π is the circumference (2πr), 5π forgets to square the radius, and 20π doubles the diameter in the circumference formula.",["Forgot to square the radius: the area is πr², not πr.","This is the circumference (2πr), the distance around the circle, not the area.","Used the diameter in the circumference formula: 2π(10). Neither the formula nor the length fits.","Correct: A = πr² = π(5²) = 25π."]],"math-0105":["In an isosceles triangle, the angles opposite the equal sides are equal. AB and AC are equal, so angles C and B match. The angles add to 180°, so B + C = 180° − 40° = 140°, and each one is 140° ÷ 2 = 70°. The traps: 140° forgets to split the total, and 100° treats the 40° angle as one of the equal pair.",["Copied the given angle. Angle A is between the equal sides, so it isn\'t one of the matching base angles.","Correct: angles B and C are equal and share 180° − 40° = 140°, so each is 70°.","Treated 40° as a base angle (180° − 40° − 40°). The equal angles are B and C, opposite the equal sides.","This is the total of angles B and C together. Split it between the two equal angles."]],"math-0106":["Turn everything into a decimal and compare: 0.6 = 0.600, 5/8 = 0.625, 0.65 = 0.650, and 2/3 = 0.666... So 2/3 is the greatest. The trap is 0.65, which is the biggest of the numbers already written as decimals; you have to convert the fractions before comparing.",["0.6 = 0.600, the smallest of the four.","5/8 = 0.625, which is less than 0.65 and 2/3.","It looks largest among the decimals, but 2/3 = 0.666..., which is greater than 0.65.","Correct: 2/3 = 0.666..., the greatest of the four."]],"math-0107":["Multiply the front numbers and the powers of 10 separately. 3 × 2 = 6, and 10⁴ × 10³ = 10⁴⁺³ = 10⁷, so the product is 6 × 10⁷ (60,000,000). The traps: multiplying the exponents (10¹²), subtracting them (10¹), or adding the front numbers (5).",["Subtracted the exponents (4 − 3). When you multiply powers of 10, ADD the exponents.","Added 3 and 2. The front numbers get multiplied: 3 × 2 = 6.","Correct: (3 × 2) × 10⁴⁺³ = 6 × 10⁷.","Multiplied the exponents (4 × 3). When you multiply powers of 10, ADD the exponents."]],"math-0108":["Add vectors part by part. First parts: 3 + (−1) = 2. Second parts: −2 + 5 = 3. So u + v = ⟨2, 3⟩. The traps come from sign slips: losing the minus on −1 gives 4, losing the minus on −2 gives 7, and ⟨4, −7⟩ is u − v.",["Correct: ⟨3 + (−1), −2 + 5⟩ = ⟨2, 3⟩.","Dropped the negative sign on −2: 2 + 5 = 7. The second parts are −2 and 5.","This is u − v: ⟨3 − (−1), −2 − 5⟩.","Dropped the negative sign on −1: 3 + 1 = 4. The first parts are 3 and −1."]],"math-0109":["Subtract the real parts and the imaginary parts separately, and remember that the minus sign applies to the whole second number. Real parts: 5 − 3 = 2. Imaginary parts: 2i − (−4i) = 2i + 4i = 6i. The answer is 2 + 6i. The most common trap is 2 − 2i, which forgets that subtracting −4i is the same as adding 4i.",["Subtracted in the wrong order: this is (3 − 4i) − (5 + 2i).","Didn\'t distribute the minus sign to −4i. Subtracting −4i means adding 4i.","Correct: 5 − 3 = 2 and 2i − (−4i) = 6i, so 2 + 6i.","Added the real parts (5 + 3) instead of subtracting them."]],"math-0110":["An exponent of 1/3 means \\"cube root\\": 27^(1/3) is the number that, multiplied by itself 3 times, gives 27. Since 3 × 3 × 3 = 27, the answer is 3. The traps treat the exponent as a multiplier (27 × 1/3 = 9 or 27 × 3 = 81), or cube 27 instead of taking its cube root (27³ = 19,683).",["Correct: an exponent of 1/3 means the cube root, and 3 × 3 × 3 = 27.","Multiplied 27 by 1/3. A fractional exponent is a root, not a multiplier.","Multiplied 27 by 3. An exponent of 1/3 means the cube root.","Cubed 27 instead of taking its cube root. The 3 in 1/3 is in the denominator."]],"math-0111":["Undo the steps in reverse order. First subtract 5 from both sides: 3x = 21. Then divide by 3: x = 7. Check: 3(7) + 5 = 26. The traps: 21 stops before dividing, and 31 and 31/3 add the 5 instead of subtracting it.",["Correct: 3x = 26 − 5 = 21, so x = 21 ÷ 3 = 7.","Added 5 to both sides instead of subtracting it, then divided by 3.","This is 3x, not x. Divide by 3 to finish.","Added 5 instead of subtracting it, and never divided by 3."]],"math-0112":["First find the side length. A square\'s area is side × side, and 7 × 7 = 49, so each side is 7 meters. The fence goes around all 4 sides: 4 × 7 = 28 meters. The traps: 7 is just one side, 14 counts only two sides, and 12.25 divides the area by 4 as if the area were the perimeter.",["This is the length of ONE side. The fence goes around all 4 sides.","Divided the area by 4. Find the side length first (√49 = 7), then multiply by 4.","Counted only 2 sides. A square has 4 equal sides.","Correct: each side is √49 = 7 m, so the perimeter is 4 × 7 = 28 m."]],"math-0113":["The region is a circular segment: the sector minus the triangle formed by the two radii and the chord. Sector: with θ in radians, area = (1/2)r²θ = (1/2)(36)(2π/3) = 12π. (Check: 2π/3 is 1/3 of a full turn, and 1/3 of 36π is 12π.) Triangle: two sides of 6 with a 120° angle between them, so its area is (1/2)(6)(6)sin 120° = 18(√3/2) = 9√3. Segment: 12π − 9√3. The traps: dropping a 1/2 in either formula (24π or 18√3), or using 1/2 for sin 120°, which mixes it up with cosine.",["Forgot the 1/2 in the triangle\'s area: the triangle is (1/2)(6)(6)sin(2π/3) = 9√3, not 18√3.","Correct: sector (1/2)(6²)(2π/3) = 12π, minus triangle (1/2)(6)(6)sin(2π/3) = 9√3.","Used 1/2 for sin(2π/3). That\'s the size of cos(2π/3); sin(2π/3) = √3/2.","Forgot the 1/2 in the sector area formula (1/2)r²θ, so the sector came out as 24π."]],"math-0114":["The cut is 4 inches above the base, so the small cone on top has height 12 − 4 = 8 inches. It is similar to the whole cone with scale factor 8/12 = 2/3. Volumes of similar solids scale by the CUBE of the scale factor: (2/3)³ = 8/27. So the small cone\'s volume is 270 × 8/27 = 80, and the frustum is 270 − 80 = 190 cubic inches. The traps: using the scale factor itself (giving 90) or its square (giving 150) instead of its cube, and stopping at the small cone (80).",["This is the small cone on top, (2/3)³ × 270 = 80. The frustum is what\'s left.","Scaled the volume by the length ratio 2/3 instead of its cube: 270 − 180 = 90.","Scaled the volume by the area ratio (2/3)² instead of the cube: 270 − 120 = 150.","Correct: the small cone is (8/12)³ × 270 = 80, so the frustum is 270 − 80 = 190."]],"math-0115":["Two steps. First, find BC with the law of cosines: BC² = 5² + 8² − 2(5)(8)cos 60° = 25 + 64 − 40 = 49, so BC = 7. Second, find the area two ways. Using the sides around angle A: area = (1/2)(5)(8)sin 60° = 20(√3/2) = 10√3. Using base BC: area = (1/2)(7)h. Set them equal: h = 20√3/7 ≈ 4.95. The traps: treating the triangle as a right triangle (√89), finding the altitude to the wrong side (4√3 is the altitude from C to AB), and dropping the 1/2 in the area.",["Found BC with the Pythagorean theorem (√(25 + 64) = √89). Angle A is 60°, not 90°, so use the law of cosines.","Correct: BC = 7 and the area is 10√3, so the altitude is 2(10√3)/7 = 20√3/7.","This is the altitude from C to side AB (8 sin 60°), not the altitude from A to BC.","Forgot the 1/2 in the area formula, so the area came out as 20√3 instead of 10√3."]],"math-0116":["Count column by column with x² + y² < 25. x = 0: y² < 25, so y = −4 to 4 (9 points). x = ±1 or ±2: y² < 24 or y² < 21, so y = −4 to 4 (9 points each, 36 total). x = ±3: y² < 16, so y = −3 to 3 (7 each, 14 total). x = ±4: y² < 9, so y = −2 to 2 (5 each, 10 total). x = ±5: y² < 0, none. Total: 9 + 36 + 14 + 10 = 69. The traps: including the 12 points on the circle such as (±3, ±4), (±4, ±3), (0, ±5), and (±5, 0) gives 81; skipping the origin gives 68; skipping the axes gives 52.",["Counted only points with both coordinates nonzero (13 per quadrant × 4). The points on the axes, including the origin, count too.","Forgot the origin (0, 0). It is inside the circle.","Correct: 81 points satisfy x² + y² ≤ 25, and 12 of them lie on the circle, leaving 69.","This counts the 12 points ON the circle, such as (3, 4) and (5, 0). The question excludes them."]],"math-0117":["Split the hexagon into 6 equilateral triangles with side s, meeting at the center. The circumscribed circle reaches the vertices, so its radius is s. The inscribed circle reaches the middles of the sides, so its radius is a triangle\'s height, (√3/2)s. Ring area: π(s²) − π(3s²/4) = πs²/4. Hexagon area: 6 × (√3/4)s² = (3√3/2)s². Ratio: (πs²/4) ÷ (3√3s²/2) = π/(6√3) = π√3/18 ≈ 0.30. The traps: using s/2 as the inner radius (π√3/6), using the wrong triangle height (π/12), or dividing by the big circle instead of the hexagon (1/4).",["This compares the ring to the LARGE CIRCLE (πs²/4 ÷ πs²), not to the hexagon.","Gave each of the 6 triangles area s²/2, as if its height were s. Its height is (√3/2)s, so the hexagon is (3√3/2)s².","Correct: ring = π(s² − 3s²/4) = πs²/4 and hexagon = (3√3/2)s², so the ratio is π/(6√3) = π√3/18.","Used s/2 for the inscribed radius. That radius is the height of an equilateral triangle with side s: (√3/2)s."]],"math-0118":["Find the pattern. (1 + i)² = 1 + 2i + i² = 2i. So (1 + i)⁴ = (2i)² = −4, which is a negative real number, and (1 + i)⁸ = (−4)² = 16, which is positive. Every 8 steps the sign returns to positive, so (1 + i)ⁿ is a negative real only when n = 4, 12, 20, 28, 36, 44: 6 values (52 is past 50). Odd powers have both a real and an imaginary part, so they never count. The traps: assuming the powers of 1 + i cycle every 4 like the powers of i (13), and counting every real power, positive or negative (12).",["Correct: (1 + i)⁴ = −4 and (1 + i)⁸ = 16, so the powers are negative real exactly when n = 4, 12, 20, ..., 44: that\'s 6 values.","Counted every multiple of 4. Those powers are all real, but n = 8, 16, 24, ... give POSITIVE numbers (16, 256, ...).","Assumed the pattern repeats every 4 like the powers of i (n = 2, 6, 10, ...). But (1 + i)² = 2i is imaginary, not negative.","Counted every even n. Even powers are (2i)^(n/2), which is imaginary whenever n/2 is odd."]],"math-0119":["Find the inverse: write y = (3x − 1)/(x + 2) and solve for x. y(x + 2) = 3x − 1, so xy + 2y = 3x − 1, so xy − 3x = −1 − 2y, so x = (2y + 1)/(3 − y). Swap the letters: f⁻¹(x) = (2x + 1)/(3 − x), which is undefined at x = 3. Another way to see it: the inputs of f⁻¹ are the outputs of f, and f(x) never equals 3 (its graph has the horizontal asymptote y = 3). The trap −2 is the gap in f\'s domain; it becomes a gap in f⁻¹\'s RANGE, not its domain. The choices 1/3 and −1/2 are zeros, not undefined points.",["−2 is the x-value that f can\'t take. That makes −2 an output f⁻¹ never gives, not an input it can\'t take.","This is where f⁻¹(x) = 0, not where it is undefined.","This is where f(x) = 0, the x-intercept of f.","Correct: f⁻¹(x) = (2x + 1)/(3 − x), which is undefined at x = 3. Also, f never outputs 3, so f⁻¹ can\'t take 3 as an input."]],"math-0120":["Multiply both sides by x − 3: x = k + 2(x − 3) = k + 2x − 6, so x = 6 − k. This works for every k except when 6 − k = 3, because x = 3 makes the denominators 0. So k = 3 gives no solution. Check: with k = 3, the equation becomes (x − 3)/(x − 3) = 2, or 1 = 2 for every allowed x, which is never true. The traps: forgetting to multiply the 3 by 2 (k = 0), setting the solution equal to 0 instead of the excluded value (k = 6), and using x = −3 as the excluded value (k = 9).",["Multiplied only the x by 2 when clearing the fraction: 2(x − 3) is 2x − 6, not 2x − 3.","Correct: clearing the fraction gives x = 6 − k. That is never allowed when it equals 3, so k = 3 leaves no solution.","Made the solution x = 6 − k equal 0. The value x can\'t take is 3, where the denominators are 0.","Treated the forbidden value as x = −3. The denominator x − 3 is 0 when x = 3."]],"math-0121":["N is a product, so changes to its factors multiply. Lowering a factor by 20% multiplies it by 0.80, and lowering another by 75% multiplies it by 0.25. Together they multiply N by 0.80 × 0.25 = 0.20, so N is now one-fifth of what it was. To get back, the third factor must be multiplied by 1 ÷ 0.20 = 5. Multiplying by 5 means the new value is 500% of the old one, which is an increase of 400%. Watch the two traps: adding 20% and 75% to get 95%, and giving 500% (the new value as a percent of the old) instead of the increase.",["Subtracted the percents from 100% (100 − 95 = 5%) and then found the increase from 5% back to 100%; the changes multiply, they don\'t add.","This is the factor (×5) the third factor must be multiplied by, not the percent increase; ×5 is a 400% increase.","Correct: the two changes multiply N by 0.80 × 0.25 = 0.20, so the third factor must be multiplied by 5, a 400% increase.","Added the two decreases (20% + 75%); percent changes to factors of a product multiply rather than add."]],"math-0122":["Compare everything with the Sun, where L = 1 and d = 1 AU. The star\'s luminosity is R²T⁴ = (0.4)² × (0.5)⁴ = 0.16 × 0.0625 = 0.01 times the Sun\'s. For the planet to get Earth\'s energy per square meter, L/d² must equal 1/1² = 1, so d² = 0.01 and d = 0.1 AU. A dim star like this one keeps its warm zone very close in. The traps: stopping at 0.01 (the luminosity, not the distance), using T² instead of T⁴ (0.2), and dropping the radius (0.25).",["Forgot the last step: 0.01 is the star\'s luminosity compared with the Sun\'s; the distance is its square root, because d is squared.","Correct: L = 0.4² × 0.5⁴ = 0.16 × 0.0625 = 0.01 of the Sun\'s, and L/d² = 1 gives d = √0.01 = 0.1 AU.","Squared the temperature instead of raising it to the 4th power: √(0.16 × 0.25) = 0.2.","Ignored the radius: √(0.5⁴) = 0.25 uses only the temperature part of the luminosity."]],"math-0123":["A rectangle with r rows and c columns needs r × c = the length, with both r and c at least 2. That has exactly one answer when the length is a product of two primes. Test each choice. 1,151 is prime (no divisor up to its square root, about 33.9), so it has no rectangle at all. 1,155 ends in 5 and has digit sum 12, so it splits many ways. 1,161 has digit sum 9, so 9 divides it: 1,161 = 9 × 129 = 27 × 43, which gives three rectangles. 1,147 = 31 × 37, and both are prime, so it has exactly one. The trap is stopping at the first factor pair you find (27 × 43) without checking whether a factor can be split further.",["1,151 is prime, so it has no rectangle with at least 2 rows and 2 columns at all, not exactly one.","1,155 = 3 × 5 × 7 × 11 has many rectangles: 3 by 385, 5 by 231, 15 by 77, 33 by 35, and more.","1,161 = 27 × 43 looks like one product, but 27 = 3³, so 3 by 387 and 9 by 129 work too.","Correct: 1,147 = 31 × 37, a product of two different primes, so 31 by 37 is its only rectangle."]],"math-0124":["Work with fractions of all flagged candidates. Beyond the solar system and passing both checks: 0.02 × 0.90 × 0.90 = 0.0162. Interference passing both: 0.98 × 0.05 × 0.05 = 0.00245. Together, 0.01865 of all candidates pass both. The share from beyond is 0.0162 ÷ 0.01865 ≈ 0.869, about 87%. After one check the answer would be only about 27%, because interference is so common; the second check cuts interference by a factor of 20 but keeps 90% of the real ones. The traps flip the condition (81% is the chance a real one passes both) or ignore how common interference was to begin with (99%).",["This is the answer after only one check: 0.018 ÷ 0.067. A second check shrinks the interference share much more than the real share.","This is 0.90², the chance a beyond-the-solar-system candidate passes both checks, not the chance a candidate that passed both is from beyond.","Correct: passing both is 0.02 × 0.90² = 0.0162 (beyond) and 0.98 × 0.05² = 0.00245 (interference), and 0.0162 ÷ 0.01865 ≈ 0.87.","Treated passing both as ruling out interference (1 − 0.05² ≈ 99.75%), ignoring that interference started out 49 times as common."]],"math-0125":["Luminosity 9 times as great means √9 = 3 times as great for both distances. The ring\'s area is π(R² − r²), where R and r are the outer and inner distances. With both multiplied by 3, the area becomes π((3R)² − (3r)²) = 9π(R² − r²), which is 9 times as much. Shortcut: when every length in a flat shape is multiplied by k, the area is multiplied by k². The traps: stopping at 3 (the length scale), cubing it (27), and squaring the luminosity ratio instead of the length scale (81).",["Scaled the area like a length: both edges are 3 times as far out, but area grows with the square of length, so 3² = 9.","Correct: both radii are multiplied by √9 = 3, so the ring\'s area, π(outer² − inner²), is multiplied by 3² = 9.","Cubed the scale factor (3³ = 27); that is how a volume would grow, but the ring is flat, so its area grows by 3².","Squared the luminosity ratio (9² = 81); the radii grow by √9 = 3, not by 9, so the area grows by 9."]],"math-0126":["Each probe turns into itself plus 3 copies, so the number of probes is multiplied by 4 every cycle: after n cycles there are 4ⁿ probes. Solve 4ⁿ > 10¹¹. Taking logs: n > 11 ÷ log 4 ≈ 11 ÷ 0.602 ≈ 18.3. The count only changes at whole cycles, so the answer is 19. Check: 4¹⁸ ≈ 6.9 × 10¹⁰ (too few) and 4¹⁹ ≈ 2.7 × 10¹¹ (enough). The traps are forgetting that the original keeps going (3ⁿ, which gives 24) and rounding 18.3 down.",["Rounded down: 4¹⁸ ≈ 6.9 × 10¹⁰, which is still less than 10¹¹, so an 18th cycle isn\'t enough.","Correct: each cycle multiplies the count by 4 (the original plus 3 copies), and 4¹⁹ ≈ 2.7 × 10¹¹ is the first power of 4 above 10¹¹.","Multiplied by 3 each cycle; the original probe travels on too, so each probe becomes 4, not 3.","Doubled each cycle; each probe becomes 4 probes per cycle, not 2."]],"math-0127":["Count everything allowed by the no-repeat rule, then remove the codes that break the digit rule. With no repeats there are 36 × 35 × 34 × 33 × 32 = 45,239,040 codes. A code with no digit uses only letters: 26 × 25 × 24 × 23 × 22 = 7,893,600. So 45,239,040 − 7,893,600 = 37,345,440 codes contain at least one digit. Counting \\"at least one\\" directly (a digit in position 1, or 2, ...) double-counts codes with several digits, which is why the complement is the safe route. The traps forget the digit rule (45,239,040) or forget the no-repeat rule (36⁵ − 26⁵ or 36⁵).",["Correct: 36 × 35 × 34 × 33 × 32 = 45,239,040 codes with no repeats, minus the 26 × 25 × 24 × 23 × 22 = 7,893,600 with no digit at all.","Ignored the \\"at least one digit\\" rule: this counts every code with no repeated character.","Subtracted the all-letter codes correctly in spirit but allowed repeats: 36⁵ − 26⁵ breaks the no-repeat rule.","This is 36⁵, which allows repeated characters and ignores the digit rule."]],"math-0128":["A conditional probability is P(both events) ÷ P(condition). Here \\"exactly 1\\" already means \\"at least 1,\\" so the top is just P(exactly 1). Exactly 1 star: choose which star (3 ways), times (1/4) for it and (3/4)² for the other two: 3 × 1/4 × 9/16 = 27/64. At least 1: everything except \\"none,\\" so 1 − (3/4)³ = 1 − 27/64 = 37/64. Divide: (27/64) ÷ (37/64) = 27/37. The trap 27/64 forgets to divide by the condition, and 37/64 is the condition itself.",["That\'s the probability that EXACTLY 2 stars have such a planet, given at least 1: (9/64) ÷ (37/64).","This is the probability that exactly 1 star has such a planet, before using the condition; it still has to be divided by P(at least 1).","This is the probability of the condition itself (at least 1 star), not of exactly 1 given it.","Correct: P(exactly 1) = 3 × (1/4)(3/4)² = 27/64 and P(at least 1) = 1 − (3/4)³ = 37/64, so the answer is 27/37."]],"math-0129":["After t years the fraction left is (1/2)^(t/88). Set it equal to 0.8 and take logs: (t/88) × log(0.5) = log(0.8), so t = 88 × log(0.8) ÷ log(0.5) = 88 × (−0.0969) ÷ (−0.3010) ≈ 88 × 0.322 ≈ 28.3, or about 28 years. A quick sanity check: after 28 years less than a third of a half-life has passed, and 80% is between 100% and the 50% left after a full half-life, closer to the start. The traps all treat the decay as a straight line (18, 35) or scale the half-life by 0.8 (70).",["Treated the decay as linear at the start: 20% of a half-life (0.2 × 88 ≈ 18) assumes the same amount is lost each year, but losing 20% takes longer than that.","Correct: (1/2)^(t/88) = 0.8 gives t = 88 × log(0.8) ÷ log(0.5) ≈ 88 × 0.322 ≈ 28 years.","Assumed a steady loss of 50% per 88 years, so 20% takes 0.4 × 88 ≈ 35 years; exponential decay loses more in the early years.","Multiplied 0.8 × 88; the exponent must be solved with logarithms, not by scaling the half-life by 80%."]],"math-0130":["Start with one star. It has a planet and is missed with probability 0.2 × 0.5 = 0.1; it has no planet with probability 0.8. So it shows no detection with probability 0.9, and given no detection, the chance it has a planet is 0.1 ÷ 0.9 = 1/9. The stars are independent, so given no detections at all five, the chance that none has a planet is (8/9)⁵ ≈ 0.555. The chance that at least one does is 1 − 0.555 ≈ 0.445, about 45%. The traps ignore the survey result (67%), stop at one star (11%), or add the five chances (56%).",["This is the chance for one star (1/9), not for at least one of five.","Correct: for one star, P(planet | no detection) = 0.1 ÷ 0.9 = 1/9; so P(none of 5 has one) = (8/9)⁵ ≈ 0.555, and 1 − 0.555 ≈ 45%.","Added 1/9 five times. Adding probabilities double-counts the cases with two or more planets; use the complement instead.","This is 1 − 0.8⁵, the chance before the survey. Detecting nothing should lower it."]],"math-0131":["Write tan x as sin x / cos x and move everything to one side: sin x / cos x − 2 sin x = 0, so sin x (1/cos x − 2) = 0. Either sin x = 0 or cos x = 1/2. On 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π (both ends included), sin x = 0 at x = 0, π, and 2π, and cos x = 1/2 at x = π/3 and 5π/3. None of these makes cos x = 0, so tan x is defined at all five. The big trap is dividing both sides by sin x, which loses three solutions.",["Divided both sides by sin x. That throws away every solution with sin x = 0 (x = 0, π, and 2π).","Counted only the solutions with sin x = 0 and missed cos x = 1/2.","Missed one endpoint (2π is included) or one of the two angles with cos x = 1/2.","Correct: sin x(1/cos x − 2) = 0, so sin x = 0 (x = 0, π, 2π) or cos x = 1/2 (x = π/3, 5π/3), for 5 values."]],"math-0132":["Count all codes and remove the bad ones with inclusion-exclusion. All codes: 5⁵ = 3,125. Missing a given one of A, B, C: 4⁵ = 1,024, three ways, so subtract 3,072. Codes missing two given letters were subtracted twice, so add them back: 3 · 3⁵ = 729. Codes missing all three were added back once too often, so subtract 2⁵ = 32. The total is 3,125 − 3,072 + 729 − 32 = 750. Each trap stops one step early, or places the letters directly and so counts the same code several times.",["Subtracted the codes missing A, B, or C (3 × 4⁵) but never added back the codes missing two of them, which were subtracted twice.","Correct: by inclusion-exclusion, 5⁵ − 3 · 4⁵ + 3 · 3⁵ − 2⁵ = 3,125 − 3,072 + 729 − 32 = 750.","Added back the codes missing two letters but forgot to subtract the codes missing all three of A, B, and C (2⁵ = 32).","Placed A, B, and C first (5 · 4 · 3 ways) and filled the other two spots freely (5²). This counts a code like AABCD more than once."]],"math-0133":["Put the hypotenuse (length 10) at the bottom. The height of the triangle above it is the altitude: area = (1/2)(6)(8) = 24 = (1/2)(10)(h), so h = 4.8. The square, side s, sits on the hypotenuse. Above it is a smaller triangle whose base is the square\'s top side (length s) and whose height is 4.8 − s. It is similar to the whole triangle (its base is parallel to the hypotenuse), so s/10 = (4.8 − s)/4.8. Cross-multiply: 4.8s = 48 − 10s, so 14.8s = 48 and s = 120/37 ≈ 3.24. The answer 24/7 is the well-known square that sits in the right-angle corner, which is a different square.",["Half the altitude to the hypotenuse (4.8 ÷ 2). The square\'s side is not set by the altitude alone.","Correct: the altitude to the hypotenuse is 6 · 8 ÷ 10 = 4.8; the triangle above the square is similar, so s/10 = (4.8 − s)/4.8, which gives s = 48/14.8 = 120/37.","This is the square with a corner at the right angle and sides along both legs (6 · 8 ÷ 14), a different placement.","This is the altitude to the hypotenuse itself. The square must fit under it, so its side is smaller."]],"math-0134":["Order the list a ≤ b ≤ c ≤ d ≤ e ≤ f. The sum is 6 × 10 = 60, and the median is the average of the middle two, so c + d = 22. For 13 to be the mode it must appear at least twice, and both copies must be at least 11, so d = 13 and e = 13, which makes c = 9. Now a + b + 9 + 13 + 13 + f = 60, so a + b + f = 25. To make the range f − a as large as possible, make a and b as small as possible. They can\'t both be 1, because then 1 would tie 13 as a mode, so use a = 1 and b = 2. That gives f = 22, and the range is 22 − 1 = 21.",["Correct: the list 1, 2, 9, 13, 13, 22 meets every condition, and no valid list does better, so the greatest range is 22 − 1 = 21.","Either reports the largest value (22) instead of the range, or uses 1 twice (1, 1, 9, 13, 13, 23), which makes 1 a second mode.","The largest value when the two smallest are both 1 (1, 1, 9, 13, 13, 23). That list has two modes, 1 and 13.","Ignores the mode: 1, 1, 11, 11, 11, 25 has the right mean and median, but its mode is 11."]],"math-0135":["Compose step by step. f(f(x)) = a(ax + b) + b = a²x + ab + b. Then f(f(f(x))) = a(a²x + ab + b) + b = a³x + a²b + ab + b = a³x + (a² + a + 1)b. Match to 8x − 21: a³ = 8, so a = 2 (the only real cube root), and (4 + 2 + 1)b = 7b = −21, so b = −3. So f(x) = 2x − 3 and f(2) = 1. Check: f(2) = 1, f(1) = −1, f(−1) = −5, and 8(2) − 21 = −5. The common trap is writing the constant as 3b, which gives b = −7 and f(2) = −3.",["This is f(f(f(2))) = 16 − 21, the triple composition at 2, not f(2).","Uses 3b for the constant term (b = −7), as if each application of f just added b. The b\'s get multiplied by a as they pass through.","That\'s f(f(2)) = f(1) = −1, two applications of f. The question asks for f(2) itself.","Correct: f(f(f(x))) = a³x + (a² + a + 1)b, so a = 2 and 7b = −21, b = −3; f(2) = 4 − 3 = 1."]],"math-0136":["A line is tangent to a circle when its distance from the center equals the radius. The center is (6, 2) and the radius is 2. Write the line as mx − y = 0; its distance from (6, 2) is |6m − 2| / √(m² + 1). Set that equal to 2 and square: 36m² − 24m + 4 = 4m² + 4, so 32m² − 24m = 0, or 8m(4m − 3) = 0. So m = 0 or m = 3/4. The line y = 0 (the x-axis) really is tangent, touching at (6, 0), but m must be positive, so m = 3/4. The traps are slopes to points you can see on the circle, such as its center (1/3) or its top (2/3), which give lines that cut through the circle.",["That\'s the slope of the other tangent line, the x-axis (y = 0). The problem says m > 0.","This is the slope from the origin to the circle\'s center, (6, 2). That line cuts through the circle; it isn\'t tangent.","This is the slope to the circle\'s top point, (6, 4). The line through it crosses the circle, so it isn\'t tangent.","Correct: the distance from (6, 2) to the line mx − y = 0 must equal the radius 2: |6m − 2| = 2√(m² + 1), which gives 32m² − 24m = 0, so m = 3/4 (m = 0 is the other tangent)."]],"math-0137":["Rewrite each base. 4^x = 2^(2x) = 3, so 2^x = √3 and 8^x = (2^x)³ = 3√3. Likewise 9^y = 3^(2y) = 2, so 3^y = √2 and 27^y = (3^y)³ = 2√2. Multiply: 3√3 · 2√2 = 6√6 (about 14.7). Check: x = log₄ 3 ≈ 0.792 and y = log₉ 2 ≈ 0.315; 8^0.792 ≈ 5.20 and 27^0.315 ≈ 2.83, and 5.20 × 2.83 ≈ 14.7. The trap 36 treats 8^x as (4^x)², but 8 = 4^(3/2), so the right power is 3/2.",["Uses 8^x = 3 and 27^y = 2, as if changing the base didn\'t change the value.","Right values for 8^x and 27^y, but adds them instead of multiplying.","Correct: 8^x = (2^x)³ = (√3)³ = 3√3 and 27^y = (3^y)³ = (√2)³ = 2√2, so the product is 6√6.","Squares instead of raising to the 3/2 power: 8^x is (4^x)^(3/2), not (4^x)²."]],"math-0138":["Factor: 3,600 = 2⁴ · 3² · 5². A divisor 2^a · 3^b · 5^c is a perfect square when every exponent is even: a can be 0, 2, or 4, and b and c can each be 0 or 2, which gives 3 × 2 × 2 = 12 squares. It\'s a fourth power when every exponent is a multiple of 4: a can be 0 or 4, while b and c must be 0. That gives just 1 and 16. So 12 − 2 = 10. The trap 11 forgets that 1 counts as a fourth power (1 = 1⁴).",["Removes 4 fourth powers, as if 3 and 5 could each appear to the fourth power. In 3,600 they only reach the second power.","Correct: there are 12 square divisors (3 × 2 × 2 choices of even exponents), and the fourth powers among them are only 1 and 16, so 12 − 2 = 10.","Removes 16 but forgets that 1 = 1⁴ is also a fourth power.","Counts all the square divisors without removing the fourth powers."]],"math-0139":["A log base must be positive and not 1, so x > 0 and x ≠ 1 (and then x + 6 > 0 automatically). Split into two cases. If x > 1, log_x is increasing, so log_x(x + 6) > 2 means x + 6 > x², or x² − x − 6 < 0, which is (x − 3)(x + 2) < 0, so −2 < x < 3. Combined with x > 1: 1 < x < 3. If 0 < x < 1, log_x is decreasing, so the inequality flips: x + 6 < x². But on (0, 1), x² < 1 while x + 6 > 6, so this never holds. (You can also see it directly: x + 6 > 1 and the base is less than 1, so the log is negative, never more than 2.) The solution set is 1 < x < 3. The strong trap, 0 < x < 3 with x ≠ 1, forgets that the inequality flips for bases less than 1.",["Flips the inequality to x + 6 < x² for every base, which gives x > 3 (or x < −2). The inequality flips only when the base is between 0 and 1.","Solves x + 6 > x² but ignores that a base must be positive and not 1.","Uses x + 6 > x² for bases between 0 and 1 too. For those bases log_x is decreasing, so the inequality flips to x + 6 < x², which no x in (0, 1) satisfies.","Correct: for x > 1, log_x(x + 6) > 2 means x + 6 > x², so −2 < x < 3, giving 1 < x < 3; for 0 < x < 1 it would mean x + 6 < x², which never happens there."]],"math-0140":["Write √3 + i in polar form. Its length is √(3 + 1) = 2, and the point (√3, 1) makes an angle of 30° with the positive real axis (tan θ = 1/√3). Raising it to the nth power multiplies the angle by n, so (√3 + i)ⁿ has length 2ⁿ and angle 30n°. The result is a negative real number when the angle points along the negative real axis: 30n = 180, 540, 900, ... (180 plus a multiple of 360). That means n = 6, 18, 30, 42, ... and only 6, 18, and 30 are at most 40. Two traps: taking the angle as 60° (it\'s the angle whose tangent is 1/√3, not √3), and counting the multiples of 12, which give positive reals.",["Uses an angle of 60° for √3 + i and then counts every n that makes the power real. The angle is 30°, and only negative reals are asked for.","Uses an angle of 60° (tan 60° = √3 swaps the real and imaginary parts). The point (√3, 1) makes a 30° angle with the positive real axis.","Counts every n that makes the power real (multiples of 6), but every other one of those (n = 12, 24, 36) gives a positive real number.","Correct: √3 + i has angle 30°, so (√3 + i)ⁿ has angle 30n°. It is a negative real when 30n is 180, 540, or 900, so n = 6, 18, or 30."]],"math-0141":["Multiply rows by columns. A² has first row [a·a + 1·0, a·1 + 1·1] = [a², a + 1] and second row [0, 1]. The matrix 3A − 2I has first row [3a − 2, 3] and second row [0, 1]. Two matrices are equal only when every entry matches. Top-left: a² = 3a − 2, so a = 1 or a = 2. Top-right: a + 1 = 3, so a = 2. Both must hold, so a = 2 only. The common trap is to stop after the top-left entry and answer \\"1 and 2\\".",["a = 1 solves a² = 3a − 2 from the top-left entry, but then the top-right entry of A² is 1 + 1 = 2, not 3.","Correct: the top-right entries require a + 1 = 3, so a = 2, and a = 2 also satisfies the top-left condition a² = 3a − 2.","These are the solutions of the top-left condition only. The top-right entry gives a second condition that rules out a = 1.","Comes from squaring each entry separately, which makes the top-right entry 1² = 1 ≠ 3. Matrix multiplication mixes rows and columns."]],"math-0142":["Square both sides: sin²x − 2 sin x cos x + cos²x = 49/25. Since sin²x + cos²x = 1, this gives 1 − 2 sin x cos x = 49/25, so sin x cos x = −12/25. Two numbers whose squares add to 1 and whose product is −12/25 are ±3/5 and ∓4/5, and sin x − cos x = 7/5 leaves two pairs: sin x = 4/5, cos x = −3/5, or sin x = 3/5, cos x = −4/5. Both pairs are in Quadrant II, so the quadrant alone doesn\'t decide. The extra condition x < 3π/4 does: between 90° and 135°, the angle is closer to 90°, so sin x is larger than |cos x|. That picks sin x = 4/5 and cos x = −3/5, so tan x = (4/5)/(−3/5) = −4/3. The strong trap is −3/4, the other pair.",["Has the right size but the wrong sign. For π/2 < x < 3π/4, x is in Quadrant II, where cos x < 0, so tan x < 0.","Wrong sign, and it comes from the pair sin x = 3/5, cos x = −4/5, which puts x past 3π/4.","From sin x = 3/5 and cos x = −4/5. That pair also gives sin x − cos x = 7/5, but its angle (about 143°) is larger than 3π/4 = 135°.","Correct: the condition gives sin x cos x = −12/25, so (sin x, cos x) is (4/5, −3/5) or (3/5, −4/5); between 90° and 135°, sin x > |cos x|, so tan x = −4/3."]],"math-0143":["Draw a horizontal and a vertical line through P, parallel to the sides. They cut the rectangle into four smaller rectangles, and P\'s distances to the four sides are, say, p and q (to the left and right sides) and r and s (to the bottom and top). Each distance from P to a corner is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with one horizontal and one vertical leg: if A is the bottom-left corner, PA² = p² + r², PB² = q² + r², PC² = q² + s², and PD² = p² + s². Then PA² + PC² = p² + q² + r² + s² = PB² + PD². So 4 + 49 = 36 + PD², which gives PD² = 17 and PD = √17. Such a point really exists (for example p = 1, r = √3, q = √33, s = 4). The trap 9 pairs neighboring corners; the rule pairs opposite corners.",["Assumes the distances follow a linear rule, PA + PC = PB + PD. The rule works with squares of the distances, not the distances.","Correct: dropping perpendiculars from P to the sides splits each distance into two legs, and PA² + PC² = PB² + PD², so PD² = 4 + 49 − 36 = 17.","This is √(PA² + PC²), the right sum before subtracting PB².","Pairs the wrong vertices: PA² + PD² = PB² + PC² would pair neighboring corners. A and C are opposite corners, so PA² pairs with PC²."]],"math-0144":["The square has area 4. The condition |x − y| < 1 cuts off two corner triangles where y > x + 1 or y < x − 1. Each is a right triangle with legs 1, so together they have area 1, and the strip that is left has area 3. The condition x + y > 1 removes the triangle near the origin with vertices (0, 0), (1, 0), and (0, 1), area 1/2. Every point of that triangle already satisfies |x − y| ≤ 1, so the whole triangle comes out of the strip: 3 − 1/2 = 5/2. The probability is (5/2)/4 = 5/8. The trap 21/32 multiplies the two separate probabilities, which works only for independent events.",["Correct: the strip |x − y| < 1 has area 4 − 1 = 3, and the triangle x + y ≤ 1 (area 1/2) lies entirely inside that strip, so the region has area 5/2 out of 4.","Multiplies 3/4 by 7/8 as if the two conditions were independent. The triangle x + y ≤ 1 sits completely inside the strip, so they are not.","This is the probability of |x − y| < 1 alone; it ignores the condition x + y > 1.","This is the probability of x + y > 1 alone; it ignores the condition |x − y| < 1."]],"math-0145":["Compute a few terms. a₂ = (1 + 2)/(1 − 2) = −3. a₃ = (1 − 3)/(1 + 3) = −1/2. a₄ = (1/2)/(3/2) = 1/3. a₅ = (4/3)/(2/3) = 2, which is a₁ again, so the sequence repeats every 4 terms: 2, −3, −1/2, 1/3. The product of one block is 2 · (−3) · (−1/2) · (1/3) = 1. The first 48 terms are 12 complete blocks, with product 1. Terms 49 and 50 start a new block, so they are 2 and −3, and the total product is 1 · 2 · (−3) = −6. The trap is 1, from assuming the 50 terms end on a complete block.",["Correct: the terms repeat 2, −3, −1/2, 1/3 with a block product of 1, so the 12 blocks in the first 48 terms give 1, and a₄₉ · a₅₀ = 2 · (−3) = −6.","This is a₅₀ alone. The product still includes a₄₉ = 2 after the complete blocks.","The product of each block of 4 terms is 1, but 50 is not a multiple of 4: two terms are left over.","Stops one term short: 2 is the product of the first 49 terms. The 50th term, −3, still has to be multiplied in."]],"math-0146":["A number with strictly decreasing digits is fixed once you choose which digits it uses: they can be arranged in only one decreasing order. It is divisible by 5 when its last digit, its smallest digit, is 0 or 5. Last digit 0: choose the other three digits from 1 through 9, which gives C(9, 3) = 84 numbers, and none can start with 0 because 0 is smallest. Last digit 5: the other three digits must be larger than 5, chosen from 6, 7, 8, 9, which gives C(4, 3) = 4. The total is 84 + 4 = 88. Each trap keeps only one of the two cases or skips the divisibility condition.",["Counts only the numbers ending in 5. Numbers ending in 0 are also divisible by 5, and 0 can always be the last digit of a decreasing number.","Counts only the numbers ending in 0, the C(9, 3) ways to choose the other three digits. Numbers ending in 5, such as 9,765, also count.","Correct: ending in 0, any 3 of the digits 1-9 can go in front (C(9, 3) = 84); ending in 5, the other 3 digits must come from 6-9 (C(4, 3) = 4); 84 + 4 = 88.","This counts all 4-digit numbers with strictly decreasing digits, C(10, 4), without using the divisible-by-5 condition."]],"math-0147":["Let y = f(x). First solve f(y) = 0. On y < 1, y² − 4 = 0 gives y = ±2, but only y = −2 is less than 1. On y ≥ 1, 2y − 5 = 0 gives y = 5/2, which is at least 1. So f(x) = −2 or f(x) = 5/2. For f(x) = −2: x² − 4 = −2 gives x = ±√2, and only −√2 is less than 1; 2x − 5 = −2 gives x = 3/2, which is at least 1. For f(x) = 5/2: x² = 13/2 gives only x = −√(13/2) (the positive root is more than 1); 2x − 5 = 5/2 gives x = 15/4. That\'s 4 solutions: −√(13/2), −√2, 3/2, and 15/4. Each piece applies only on its own interval, so every root must be checked against it.",["Counts the values y with f(y) = 0 (y = −2 and y = 5/2) instead of the values of x.","Correct: f(y) = 0 only for y = −2 and y = 5/2, and each of f(x) = −2 and f(x) = 5/2 has exactly 2 solutions once each piece\'s domain is checked.","Keeps y = 2 as a solution of f(y) = 0, but y² − 4 applies only to y < 1. That adds 2 extra solutions (x = −√6 and x = 7/2).","Never checks the pieces\' domains: it keeps both square roots from x² − 4 every time and also keeps y = 2."]],"math-0148":["Write p(x) = (x² − 1)q(x) + 3x + 1. Multiply by x: x · p(x) = x(x² − 1)q(x) + 3x² + x. The first part is a multiple of x² − 1, but 3x² + x still has degree 2, so it isn\'t a remainder yet. Divide it once more: 3x² + x = 3(x² − 1) + x + 3. So x · p(x) = (x² − 1)(x·q(x) + 3) + x + 3, and the remainder is x + 3. Check with values: p(1) = 4 and p(−1) = −2, so x · p(x) is 4 at x = 1 and 2 at x = −1, and x + 3 gives 4 and 2. The trap 3x² + x stops before the remainder\'s degree is below 2.",["Multiplies the old remainder by x but doesn\'t finish dividing: a remainder after dividing by x² − 1 must have degree less than 2.","Assumes multiplying p(x) by x doesn\'t change the remainder. It does: x · p(x) has the value 4 at x = 1 but 2 at x = −1.","Correct: x · p(x) = x(x² − 1)q(x) + 3x² + x, and 3x² + x = 3(x² − 1) + x + 3, so the remainder is x + 3.","Sign slip when reducing: 3x² = 3(x² − 1) + 3, not 3(x² − 1) − 3."]],"math-0149":["Factor: f(x) = (x − 2)(x + 2)/((x − 2)(x + 1)). For x ≠ 2 and x ≠ −1, this simplifies to (x + 2)/(x + 1) = 1 + 1/(x + 1). The fraction 1/(x + 1) can be any real number except 0, so 1 + 1/(x + 1) can be any number except 1. But x = 2 is not in the domain, so the value the simplified expression would give at x = 2, (2 + 2)/(2 + 1) = 4/3, is also missing (each output of 1 + 1/(x + 1) comes from exactly one x). The range is all real numbers except 1 and 4/3. The common trap stops at the asymptote and misses the hole.",["Finds the horizontal asymptote y = 1 but misses the hole at x = 2, where f would equal 4/3 if it were defined.","Finds the value missing at the hole but forgets that the simplified function (x + 2)/(x + 1) = 1 + 1/(x + 1) never equals 1.","Correct: f(x) = (x + 2)/(x + 1) = 1 + 1/(x + 1) for x ≠ −1, 2, so it never equals 1, and the hole at x = 2 also removes the value 4/3.","These are the x-values left out of the domain (the zeros of the denominator), not y-values left out of the range."]],"math-0150":["Both sides are positive when x > 0, so take the base-10 log of each side. On the left, log(x^(log x)) = (log x)(log x) = (log x)². On the right, log(1,000x²) = log 1,000 + log x² = 3 + 2 log x. Let L = log x. Then L² = 3 + 2L, so L² − 2L − 3 = 0 and (L − 3)(L + 1) = 0. So L = 3 or L = −1, which means x = 1,000 or x = 0.1. Check the small one: 0.1^(−1) = 10, and 1,000(0.1)² = 10. The product is 1,000 × 0.1 = 100. Shortcut: the product of the solutions is 10 raised to the sum of the L-values, 10^(3 + (−1)) = 10². Traps: multiplying the L-values instead of adding them (0.001), dropping the 2 in log x² (10), and stopping at x = 1,000.",["Multiplies the two values of log x (3 × (−1) = −3) and raises 10 to that power. Since log(ab) = log a + log b, the product of the solutions is 10 raised to the sum of the logs.","Writes log(1,000x²) as 3 + log x, dropping the exponent 2. The right side is 3 + 2 log x.","Correct: taking the log of both sides gives (log x)² = 3 + 2 log x, so log x = 3 or log x = −1. The solutions are 1,000 and 0.1, and their product is 100.","This is only the larger solution, x = 1,000. There is a second one: log x = −1 also works, so x = 0.1 is a solution too."]],"math-0151":["Call the first term a and the ratio r, so a/(1 − r) = 12. Squaring the terms a, ar, ar², ... gives a², a²r², a²r⁴, ..., a geometric series with first term a² and ratio r². Its sum is a²/(1 − r²) = 48. Since 1 − r² = (1 − r)(1 + r), divide the second equation by the first: a/(1 + r) = 48/12 = 4. Now a = 12(1 − r) and a = 4(1 + r), so 12 − 12r = 4 + 4r, which gives r = 1/2 and a = 6. The series is 6 + 3 + 3/2 + ..., so the third term is 3/2. Check: 6/(1 − 1/2) = 12, and 36/(1 − 1/4) = 48. Traps: keeping the ratio r for the squared series (16/9), and picking the wrong term (6 is the first term and 3 is the second).",["Correct: the squares have first term a² and ratio r², so a/(1 − r) = 12 and a²/(1 − r²) = 48. Dividing gives a/(1 + r) = 4, so r = 1/2, a = 6, and the third term is 6(1/2)² = 3/2.","Uses a²/(1 − r) for the sum of the squares, keeping the ratio r. Squaring every term squares the ratio too, so that sum is a²/(1 − r²).","This is the second term, 6 × 1/2. The first term is 6, so the third term is 6 × (1/2)².","That\'s the first term, a. The third term is ar² = 6(1/2)² = 3/2."]],"math-0152":["From the parabola, x² = y + 2. Substitute into the circle: (y + 2) + y² = r², so y² + y + (2 − r²) = 0. Each root y gives two intersection points (x = ±√(y + 2)) if y > −2, one point (the vertex) if y = −2, and no points if y < −2. So 4 points need two different roots, both greater than −2. Two roots: the discriminant 1 − 4(2 − r²) must be positive, so r² > 7/4 and r > √7/2. Both above −2: the smaller root is (−1 − √(4r² − 7))/2, which is greater than −2 when √(4r² − 7) < 3, so 4r² − 7 < 9, r² < 4, and r < 2. Together: √7/2 < r < 2. Picture it: as r grows, the circle first touches the parabola at the two points nearest the origin, then crosses it 4 times, passes through the vertex at r = 2 (3 points), and after that crosses it only twice. Traps: stopping at the discriminant, assuming the vertex is the nearest point, and forgetting to take square roots.",["Correct: substituting x² = y + 2 gives y² + y + 2 − r² = 0. Four points need two different roots that are both greater than −2 (each then gives two x-values): the discriminant needs r² > 7/4, and the lower root stays above −2 only while r < 2.","These are the bounds on r², not on r. The condition 7/4 < r² < 4 means √7/2 < r < 2.","Uses only the discriminant. Once r ≥ 2, the lower root of y² + y + 2 − r² = 0 is at or below y = −2, so the circle meets the parabola at only 3 points (r = 2) or 2 points (r > 2).","Assumes the vertex (0, −2) is the point of the parabola closest to the origin. It isn\'t: the points with y = −1/2 are closer (√7/2 away), and once r > 2 the circle crosses the parabola only twice."]],"math-0153":["Count all orders, then remove the bad ones with inclusion-exclusion. All orders of R, R, B, B, G, G, Y: 7!/(2!·2!·2!) = 630. Orders with the two reds together: glue them into one block, leaving 6 items (RR, B, B, G, G, Y), so 6!/(2!·2!) = 180. Blue and green are the same, 180 each. Orders with reds together and blues together: 5 items (RR, BB, G, G, Y), so 5!/2! = 60, and there are 3 such pairs of colors. All three pairs together: 4 items, 4! = 24. Orders with at least one same-color pair together: 3(180) − 3(60) + 24 = 384. Good orders: 630 − 384 = 246. Traps: stopping after subtracting 3 × 180 (90), leaving off the last term (270), or adding it (294).",["Subtracts the 3 × 180 orders with some color pair together and stops. An order with two pairs together was subtracted twice, so those orders must be added back.","Correct: by inclusion-exclusion, 630 − 3(180) + 3(60) − 24 = 246.","Adds back the orders with two pairs together but forgets the last step. The 24 orders with all three pairs together were subtracted 3 times and added back 3 times, so they still need to be removed.","Adds the 24 orders with all three pairs together instead of subtracting them. In inclusion-exclusion the signs alternate: subtract, add, subtract."]],"math-0154":["For the mean to stay 40, the two new numbers must add to 80, so they are 40 − d and 40 + d. The standard deviation comes from the average squared distance from the mean, which is now 6² = 36. Adding two numbers keeps that average at 36 only if their own squared distances, d² and d², also average 36. So d² = 36 and d = 6: the numbers are 34 and 46. Check with totals: the 10 numbers have squared distances adding to 10 × 36 = 360, the 12 numbers need 12 × 36 = 432, and the new numbers add 2 × 36 = 72. (If the SD is computed by dividing by n − 1 instead, the totals are 324 and 396, and the new numbers must again add 72, so d = 6 either way.) The difference is 46 − 34 = 12. Traps: adding the mean twice (that shrinks the SD), and splitting 36 between the two numbers (6√2).",["Adding 40 twice keeps the mean at 40, but two numbers that sit right at the mean pull the standard deviation down.","Makes the two numbers one standard deviation apart (37 and 43). Each one must be a full standard deviation from the mean, so they are two standard deviations apart.","Makes the two new squared distances add up to 36. Each new number must have a squared distance of 36 by itself (72 in all) to keep the average squared distance at 36.","Correct: the mean stays 40 only if the numbers are 40 − d and 40 + d. The SD stays 6 only if their squared distances from the mean average 6² = 36, so d = 6. The numbers are 34 and 46, which differ by 12."]],"math-0155":["Put the square on axes with corners (0, 0) and (10, 10), and put circle P in the corner at the origin. A circle tangent to both sides at a corner has its center on the diagonal y = x, so P\'s center is (1, 1). Circle Q is tangent to the sides x = 10 and y = 10, so its center is (10 − r, 10 − r), also on the diagonal. The distance between the centers is √2 × (10 − r − 1) = (9 − r)√2. The circles touch from outside, so that distance is r + 1: (9 − r)√2 = r + 1. Then 9√2 − 1 = r(√2 + 1), and r = (9√2 − 1)/(√2 + 1) ≈ 11.728/2.414 ≈ 4.86 inches. Traps: measuring the center distance along a side (9 − r = r + 1 gives 4.00), using 10 for the diagonal (3.14), and ignoring the gaps between the circles and the corners (6.07).",["Treats the square\'s diagonal as 10 inches long. The diagonal of a 10-inch square is 10√2 ≈ 14.14 inches.","Measures the distance between the centers along a side instead of along the diagonal. The centers (1, 1) and (10 − r, 10 − r) are (9 − r)√2 apart, not 9 − r.","Correct: both centers lie on the square\'s diagonal, (9 − r)√2 apart. Tangent circles have centers r + 1 apart, and (9 − r)√2 = r + 1 gives r = (9√2 − 1)/(√2 + 1) ≈ 4.86.","Treats the diagonal as made up of only the two circles\' diameters (2 + 2r = 10√2). Each center is r√2 from its corner, so there is a gap between each circle and its corner. (A radius over 5 couldn\'t fit anyway.)"]],"math-0156":["The graph of f⁻¹ is the reflection of the graph of f across the line y = x. Any point where the graph of f crosses y = x is on both graphs: (x − 3)² + 2 = x gives x² − 7x + 11 = 0, so x = (7 ± √5)/2, and only (7 − √5)/2 ≈ 2.38 satisfies x ≤ 3. But f is decreasing, and a decreasing function can also meet its inverse at mirror-image pairs off the line y = x: points (a, b) and (b, a) with f(a) = b and f(b) = a. Try a = 2: f(2) = 1 + 2 = 3, and f(3) = 0 + 2 = 2. So (2, 3) and (3, 2) are both on the graph of f, and since each is the reflection of the other, both are on the graph of f⁻¹ too. (Algebra: subtracting (a − 3)² + 2 = b and (b − 3)² + 2 = a gives a + b = 5 when a ≠ b, and then a = 2 or 3.) The x-coordinates are 2, 3, and (7 − √5)/2, with sum (17 − √5)/2 ≈ 7.38. The trap is the rule \\"f and f⁻¹ meet only on y = x\\": it holds for increasing functions, not decreasing ones.",["Correct: the graphs meet at (2, 3), at (3, 2), and on the line y = x at x = (7 − √5)/2. The sum is 2 + 3 + (7 − √5)/2 = (17 − √5)/2.","Adds both roots of (x − 3)² + 2 = x. The root (7 + √5)/2 is greater than 3, outside the domain of f, and the points (2, 3) and (3, 2) off the line y = x are missing.","Finds the points (2, 3) and (3, 2) but misses the point on the line y = x, where (x − 3)² + 2 = x with x ≤ 3.","Finds only the point on the line y = x. Because f is decreasing, its graph and its inverse\'s graph also share pairs of mirror-image points off that line: (2, 3) and (3, 2)."]],"math-0157":["First the non-vertical lines. A line through (1, −3) with slope m is y = m(x − 1) − 3. It meets y = x² where x² − mx + (m + 3) = 0, and it meets the parabola at exactly one point when this equation has exactly one root: the discriminant m² − 4(m + 3) = 0, so m² − 4m − 12 = 0 and m = 6 or m = −2. The single root is x = m/2, so these two tangent lines touch the parabola at (3, 9) and (−1, 1). But there is a third line: the vertical line x = 1 passes through (1, −3), and it meets y = x² only at (1, 1). It isn\'t tangent, but it crosses the parabola exactly once, as every vertical line does. So the three points are at x = 3, x = −1, and x = 1, and the sum is 3. Traps: thinking only tangent lines meet a parabola once (2), and adding the slopes instead of the x-coordinates (4).",["Finds the two tangent lines (slopes 6 and −2, touching the parabola at x = 3 and x = −1) but misses the vertical line x = 1. It passes through (1, −3) and crosses the parabola only at (1, 1).","Correct: the tangent lines y = 6x − 9 and y = −2x − 1 touch the parabola at x = 3 and x = −1, and the vertical line x = 1 meets it only at (1, 1). The sum is 3 + (−1) + 1 = 3.","Adds the slopes of the two tangent lines, 6 + (−2), instead of the x-coordinates of the points (a line of slope m touches y = x² at x = m/2), and misses the vertical line.","Adds the y-coordinates of the three points, (3, 9), (−1, 1), and (1, 1), instead of their x-coordinates."]],"math-0158":["Write 2 as log₂ 4, so the right side is log₂ 4 + log₂(x + 2) = log₂(4(x + 2)). Then (x − 1)² = 4(x + 2): x² − 2x + 1 = 4x + 8, so x² − 6x − 7 = 0 and (x − 7)(x + 1) = 0. Check each root in the original equation, not in a rewritten one. For x = 7: log₂ 36 = 2 + log₂ 9, true because 4 × 9 = 36. For x = −1: log₂((−2)²) = log₂ 4 = 2, and 2 + log₂ 1 = 2 + 0 = 2, also true. The left side only needs (x − 1)² > 0, which is true for x = −1. The trap is the power rule: log₂((x − 1)²) = 2 log₂(x − 1) only when x − 1 > 0, so using it throws away a real solution. The sum is 7 + (−1) = 6.",["Comes from a sign slip in factoring x² − 6x − 7 as (x + 7)(x − 1). It factors as (x − 7)(x + 1).","Writes 2 + log₂(x + 2) as log₂(x + 4), adding the 2 inside the log. The 2 is log₂ 4, so the right side is log₂(4(x + 2)).","Correct: (x − 1)² = 4(x + 2) gives x² − 6x − 7 = 0, so x = 7 or x = −1. Both check in the original equation (for x = −1, log₂ 4 = 2 + log₂ 1), so the sum is 6.","Rejects x = −1 after rewriting log₂((x − 1)²) as 2 log₂(x − 1), which needs x > 1. The original equation needs only (x − 1)² > 0 and x + 2 > 0, and x = −1 meets both."]],"math-0159":["By the quadratic formula, the solutions are 3 ± √(9 − k). Nonreal case, k > 9: the solutions are 3 + i√(k − 9) and 3 − i√(k − 9). Each has absolute value √(9 + (k − 9)) = √k, so |z₁| + |z₂| = 2√k = 10, √k = 5, and k = 25 (solutions 3 ± 4i). Real case, k ≤ 9: the solutions add to 6. If both are 0 or positive, |z₁| + |z₂| = z₁ + z₂ = 6, not 10. So they must have opposite signs, and then |z₁| + |z₂| is the distance between them: 2√(9 − k) = 10, so 9 − k = 25 and k = −16 (solutions 8 and −2). Both values work. Traps: assuming the solutions must be nonreal (25 only) or must be real (−16 only). Real numbers are complex numbers too, with b = 0.",["Assumes the solutions are nonreal. For k = −16 the solutions are the real numbers 8 and −2, and |8| + |−2| = 10 too.","Correct: if k > 9, the solutions are 3 ± i√(k − 9), each with absolute value √k, so 2√k = 10 and k = 25. If k < 9, they are real and add to 6, so |z₁| + |z₂| = 10 needs opposite signs; then they are 10 apart, 2√(9 − k) = 10, and k = −16.","Assumes the solutions are real. For k = 25 the solutions are 3 + 4i and 3 − 4i, each with absolute value √(9 + 16) = 5, and 5 + 5 = 10.","Comes from a sign slip in the real case: the solutions are 2√(9 − k) apart, so 4(9 − k) = 100 gives k = −16, not 16. (For k = 16 the solutions aren\'t even real.)"]],"math-0160":["Let d be the common difference and r the common ratio. Equal second terms: 4 + d = 4r, so d = 4r − 4. Equal fourth terms: 4 + 3d = 4r³. Substitute: 4 + 12r − 12 = 4r³, so 4r³ − 12r + 8 = 0, or r³ − 3r + 2 = 0. Since r = 1 is a root, factor: (r − 1)(r² + r − 2) = (r − 1)(r − 1)(r + 2). The ratio isn\'t 1, so r = −2 and d = −12. The geometric sequence is 4, −8, 16, −32, ..., and the arithmetic sequence is 4, −8, −20, −32, .... The third term of the geometric sequence is 16. Traps: using r = 1 anyway (4), giving the arithmetic sequence\'s third term (−20), and giving the shared fourth term (−32).",["Correct: 4 + d = 4r and 4 + 3d = 4r³ give r³ − 3r + 2 = 0, which factors as (r − 1)²(r + 2) = 0. Since r ≠ 1, r = −2, and the third term is 4(−2)² = 16.","Uses r = 1, the easy-to-spot root of r³ − 3r + 2 = 0. The problem rules it out: with r = 1 both sequences are just 4, 4, 4, ....","This is the third term of the arithmetic sequence, 4 + 2(−12), not of the geometric sequence.","This is the fourth term, 4(−2)³, which the two sequences share."]],"math-0161":["For x²/25 + y²/9 = 1, a = 5 and b = 3, so the foci are (±c, 0) with c² = 25 − 9 = 16. So c = 4, and F₁F₂ = 8. Every point on an ellipse has distances to the two foci that add to 2a = 10, so PF₁ + PF₂ = 10. Let p = PF₁ · PF₂. The law of cosines with the 60° angle at P: 8² = PF₁² + PF₂² − 2 · PF₁ · PF₂ · cos 60° = PF₁² + PF₂² − p. Since PF₁² + PF₂² = (PF₁ + PF₂)² − 2p = 100 − 2p, this gives 64 = 100 − 3p, so p = 12. The area is ½ · PF₁ · PF₂ · sin 60° = ½ · 12 · (√3/2) = 3√3 ≈ 5.2. (Such a point exists: the two distances are 5 + √13 and 5 − √13.) Traps: leaving out sin 60° (6), forgetting the ½ (6√3), and assuming P is the top of the ellipse (12).",["This is the area when P is the top of the ellipse, (0, 3). There the angle at P is about 106°, not 60°: PF₁ = PF₂ = 5 and F₁F₂ = 8.","Finds PF₁ · PF₂ = 12 and multiplies by sin 60° but forgets the ½.","Finds PF₁ · PF₂ = 12 and takes half of it, leaving out sin 60°. The area of a triangle from two sides and the angle between them is ½ab sin C.","Correct: F₁F₂ = 8 and PF₁ + PF₂ = 10. The law of cosines gives 64 = (PF₁ + PF₂)² − 3 · PF₁ · PF₂, so PF₁ · PF₂ = 12, and the area is ½ · 12 · sin 60° = 3√3."]],"math-0162":["Work in three steps. First, decibels to a power ratio: 15 = 10·log₁₀(r), so r = 10^1.5 ≈ 31.6. The search can now detect signals about 31.6 times weaker. Second, power to distance: since power falls as 1/d², a signal 31.6 times weaker comes from √31.6 = 10^0.75 ≈ 5.6 times as far away. Third, distance to stars: if stars are spread evenly, the number within reach grows with the volume of a sphere, which grows with the radius cubed: (10^0.75)³ = 10^2.25 ≈ 178. Each wrong choice stops at one step (5.6 or 31.6) or skips the square root (31,600).",["This is how many times as far the search reaches (10^0.75 ≈ 5.6). The number of stars depends on the volume of space reached, so cube it.","This is the power ratio, 10^1.5 ≈ 31.6. Weaker signals can be detected, but the number of stars depends on distance cubed, not on power directly.","Correct: 15 dB is a power ratio of 10^1.5; power falls as 1/d², so the reach grows by √(10^1.5) = 10^0.75; the volume, and so the number of stars, grows by (10^0.75)³ = 10^2.25 ≈ 178.","This cubes the power ratio, treating distance as if it grew in proportion to power; but power falls with distance squared, so distance grows only by the square root of the power ratio."]],"math-0163":["Put the bottom of the dish (the vertex) at the origin with the dish opening upward. A parabola with its focus p units above the vertex is y = x²/(4p). The rim is 30 m from the center (half of 60) and 9 m up, so the point (30, 9) is on the parabola: 9 = 900/(4p), so 36p = 900 and p = 25. The focus is 25 m above the vertex. The question asks for the height above the rim, which is itself 9 m above the vertex: 25 − 9 = 16 m. Traps: stopping at 25, and using 60 instead of 30.",["Correct: with the vertex at the origin, y = x²/(4p) passes through (30, 9), so 9 = 900/(4p) and p = 25; the focus is 25 m above the bottom, which is 25 − 9 = 16 m above the rim.","This is the distance from the bottom of the dish (the vertex) to the focus. The rim is already 9 m above the vertex.","Uses the full width, 60, as if it were the distance from the center to the rim (p = 3,600/36 = 100), then subtracts 9.","Uses the full width, 60, instead of the half-width, 30, giving p = 100, and measures from the vertex."]],"math-0164":["The Sun, Earth, and Venus form a triangle with Sun-Earth = 1, Sun-Venus = 0.72, and a 30° angle at Earth. The law of sines gives sin V = (1 × sin 30°)/0.72 ≈ 0.694 for the angle at Venus. A calculator returns V ≈ 44°, but 180° − 44° = 136° has the same sine, and both fit, because two sides and an angle not between them can make two triangles. If V ≈ 44°, the angle at the Sun is 106° and Venus is about 1.38 AU from Earth, farther than the Sun. If V ≈ 136°, the angle at the Sun is 14° and Venus is about 0.35 AU away. (The law of cosines shows the same two cases: 0.72² = 1 + x² − 2x cos 30° has roots x ≈ 0.35 and x ≈ 1.38.) The stem says Venus is closer than the Sun, so the angle at the Sun is about 14°. The trap is trusting the calculator\'s single answer for the inverse sine, which leads to 106°.",["Correct: the law of cosines gives 0.72² = 1 + x² − 2x cos 30° for the Earth-Venus distance x, so x ≈ 0.35 or x ≈ 1.38; Venus is closer than the Sun, so x ≈ 0.35, and then the angle at Venus is obtuse (about 136°), leaving 180° − 30° − 136° ≈ 14° at the Sun.","This is the acute angle at Venus that the law of sines gives (sin V = 0.5/0.72), not the angle at the Sun.","This is the angle at the Sun for the other possible triangle, where Venus is 1.38 AU away, farther than the Sun. Taking the calculator\'s acute value for the angle at Venus leads here.","This is the angle at Venus in the correct triangle, not the angle at the Sun."]],"math-0165":["Find the chance that the first signal star is the 1st, 2nd, 3rd, ... star observed. First: 2/6 = 5/15. Second: the first is a miss (4/6), then a hit (2/5): 8/30 = 4/15. Third: 4/6 · 3/5 · 2/4 = 1/5 = 3/15. Fourth: 4/6 · 3/5 · 2/4 · 2/3 = 2/15. Fifth: after all 4 misses, the next star must be a hit: 1/15. These add to 15/15, as they should. The expected value is 1(5/15) + 2(4/15) + 3(3/15) + 4(2/15) + 5(1/15) = 35/15 = 7/3. The tempting shortcut, 1 ÷ (2/6) = 3, is the average wait when each try is independent with the same chance, like drawing with replacement. Here each miss removes a non-signal star, so the chance of a hit rises, and the average wait is shorter.",["Leaves out the last case: the team can observe as many as 5 stars (4 without the signal, then 1 with it), and that case adds 5 × 1/15 = 1/3.","Correct: P(1) = 2/6, P(2) = 4/6 · 2/5 = 4/15, P(3) = 1/5, P(4) = 2/15, P(5) = 1/15, so the expected value is 1/3 + 8/15 + 9/15 + 8/15 + 5/15 = 35/15 = 7/3.","Uses 1 ÷ p with p = 2/6, which is right only if the team could observe the same star again. Never repeating a star makes success more likely at each step, so fewer observations are needed on average.","This is the average position of a single star in a random order of 6, (1 + 6) ÷ 2, as if only 1 star gave off the signal."]],"math-0166":["Start with what the delay tells you: 20 microseconds × 0.3 km per microsecond = 6 km, so the transmitter is 6 km closer to A than to B. Points whose distances to A and B differ by 6 lie on a hyperbola with A and B as foci. Put C at the origin, A at (5, 0), and B at (−5, 0): c = 5, 2a = 6 so a = 3, and b² = 25 − 9 = 16, giving x²/9 − y²/16 = 1. The transmitter is nearer A, so x > 0. On the road y = 4: x² = 9(1 + 1) = 18, so x ≈ 4.243. Its distance to A is √((4.243 − 5)² + 16) ≈ 4.07 km, and its distance to C is √(18 + 16) ≈ 5.83 km. The extra 1.76 km takes 1.76 ÷ 0.3 ≈ 5.9 microseconds. The tempting shortcut, halfway between 0 and 20, gives 10.0, but the delay isn\'t proportional to position along AB.",["Correct: the burst travels 6 km farther to B than to A, so the transmitter is on A\'s branch of x²/9 − y²/16 = 1 (A at (5, 0), B at (−5, 0)); on the road y = 4, x = √18 ≈ 4.24, so it is about 4.07 km from A and √34 ≈ 5.83 km from C, and (5.83 − 4.07) ÷ 0.3 ≈ 5.9 microseconds.","Places the transmitter straight across the road from A, at (5, 4): then it is 4 km from A and √41 ≈ 6.40 km from C, giving 2.4 ÷ 0.3 = 8.0. But that point is about 10.8 km from B, which doesn\'t match the 20-microsecond delay.","Assumes C, halfway between A and B, hears the burst halfway between their times. Distances to the transmitter don\'t change in proportion to position along AB.","This is the travel time from the transmitter to C, 5.83 ÷ 0.3 ≈ 19.4, not the delay after A."]],"math-0167":["Let each material start at 1 unit (2 in all). After t years, P has (1/2)^(t/10) and Q has (1/2)^(t/20). Let u = (1/2)^(t/20); then P\'s amount is u², because 10-year half-lives pass twice as fast. We want the total to be 1/4 of 2: u² + u = 1/2, so u² + u − 1/2 = 0. The quadratic formula gives u = (−1 + √3)/2 ≈ 0.366 (the other root is negative). Then (1/2)^(t/20) = 0.366, so t = 20·log(0.366)/log(0.5) ≈ 29.0 years. The tempting shortcut is to treat the mixture as having one half-life of 15 years, which gives 30. That is close but wrong: the faster material dies out early, so the mixture\'s decay slows down over time and no single half-life describes it.",["This is when P alone is down to 1/4 (two half-lives of P), but at 20 years Q still has half its mass, so the total is (1/4 + 1/2) ÷ 2 = 3/8 of the start.","Correct: with u = (1/2)^(t/20), the total fraction is ½u² + ½u = 1/4, so u² + u − ½ = 0 and u = (√3 − 1)/2 ≈ 0.366; then t = 20·log₂(1/0.366) ≈ 29.0 years.","Averages the half-lives to 15 years and takes two of them. A mixture doesn\'t decay with the average half-life: at 30 years the total is (1/8 + 0.354) ÷ 2 ≈ 0.239, a bit less than 1/4.","This is when Q alone is down to 1/4 (two half-lives of Q), but by then P is nearly gone, so the total is below 1/4."]],"math-0168":["\\"Toward us\\" means negative velocity, so \\"toward us at more than 5 m/s\\" means v(t) < −5. Solve: 5 + 20 sin(πt/6) < −5, so 20 sin(πt/6) < −10 and sin(πt/6) < −1/2. On one cycle, sine is below −1/2 between 7π/6 and 11π/6. Set πt/6 equal to each: t = 7 and t = 11. That is 4 days. The traps come from mishandling the constant 5, which is the whole system\'s steady motion away from us: dropping it gives about 5 days, and solving v(t) < 5 instead of v(t) < −5 gives 6.",["Correct: moving toward us faster than 5 m/s means v(t) < −5, so 20 sin(πt/6) < −10 and sin(πt/6) < −1/2; that holds for 7π/6 < πt/6 < 11π/6, which is 7 < t < 11, or 4 days.","Drops the constant 5: it solves 20 sin(πt/6) < −5. That also happens to be about how long the star moves toward us at any speed (v < 0).","Solves v(t) < 5 instead of v(t) < −5. That reduces to sin(πt/6) < 0, which is half the cycle, but the star must be moving toward us, so v has to be below −5.","Counts the days when the star\'s speed is more than 5 m/s in either direction (4 days toward us plus 6 days away from us), but the question asks only about moving toward us."]],"math-0169":["50% means 50 out of 100, which is one half. Half of 64 is 64 ÷ 2 = 32. (Or 0.50 × 64 = 32.) Part of a group can\'t be bigger than the whole group, so 114 and 128 can\'t be right. 14 comes from treating 50% as \\"subtract 50.\\"",["Subtracted 50 from 64. 50% means half, not \\"minus 50.\\"","Correct: 50% is one half, and half of 64 is 32.","Added 50 to 64. A percent of a number is a part of it, so the answer must be less than 64.","Doubled 64 instead of halving it. 50% is one half."]],"math-0170":["Dani earns $12 for every hour, so 6 hours pays 6 × $12 = $72. Check: $12 + $12 + $12 + $12 + $12 + $12 = $72. The wrong choices use the other operations: 12 ÷ 6 = 2, 12 − 6 = 6, and 12 + 6 = 18.",["Divided 12 by 6. To find the pay for 6 hours, multiply the hourly pay by 6.","Subtracted 6 from 12. Each of the 6 hours earns $12, so multiply.","Added 12 and 6. Each of the 6 hours earns $12, so multiply.","Correct: $12 per hour × 6 hours = $72."]],"math-0171":["The equation says that 8 less than x is 15. Undo the subtraction by adding 8 to both sides: x = 15 + 8 = 23. Check: 23 − 8 = 15. The traps: 7 subtracts 8 instead of adding it, −7 is 8 − 15, and 15 just copies the right side.",["Computed 8 − 15. To undo subtracting 8, add 8 to both sides.","Subtracted 8 from 15. To undo subtracting 8, add 8 instead.","Copied the right side. x is 8 more than 15.","Correct: add 8 to both sides: x = 15 + 8 = 23."]],"math-0172":["Each cup holds 8 fluid ounces, so 3 cups hold 3 × 8 = 24 fluid ounces. The traps: 8 − 3 = 5 and 8 + 3 = 11 use the wrong operation, and 8 is only one cup.",["Subtracted 3 from 8. Each cup is 8 fluid ounces, so multiply.","That\'s one cup. The recipe needs 3 cups, so multiply 8 by 3.","Added 3 and 8. Each of the 3 cups is 8 fluid ounces, so multiply.","Correct: 3 cups × 8 fluid ounces per cup = 24 fluid ounces."]],"math-0173":["Undo the steps in reverse order. First add 4 to both sides: 2x = 14. Then divide both sides by 2: x = 7. Check: 2(7) − 4 = 14 − 4 = 10. The traps: 14 stops before dividing, 5 ignores the − 4, and 3 subtracts 4 when it should add it.",["Subtracted 4 instead of adding it: 10 − 4 = 6, and 6 ÷ 2 = 3.","Ignored the − 4 and just divided 10 by 2.","Correct: add 4 to both sides to get 2x = 14, then divide by 2: x = 7.","This is 2x, not x. Divide 14 by 2 to finish."]],"math-0174":["Replace x with 7. The term 2x means 2 times x, so 2x = 2 × 7 = 14. Then add 1: y = 15. The traps: reading 2x as 27 gives 28, adding 2 + 7 + 1 gives 10, and adding 1 before multiplying gives 16.",["Added all the numbers: 2 + 7 + 1. But 2x means 2 times x.","Correct: y = 2(7) + 1 = 14 + 1 = 15.","Added before multiplying: 2(7 + 1) = 16. Multiply 2 × 7 first, then add 1.","Read 2x as the two-digit number 27. 2x means 2 × 7 = 14."]],"math-0175":["One loaf takes 3 cups of flour. Four loaves take 4 times as much: 4 × 3 = 12 cups. The wrong choices divide the numbers (3 ÷ 4 or 4 ÷ 3) or add them (3 + 4). A quick check: more loaves need more flour, so the answer must be more than 3 cups.",["Divided 3 by 4. Each of the 4 loaves needs 3 cups, so multiply.","Divided 4 by 3. Each of the 4 loaves needs 3 cups, so multiply.","Added 3 and 4. Each of the 4 loaves needs 3 cups, so multiply.","Correct: 3 cups per loaf × 4 loaves = 12 cups."]],"math-0176":["\\"Per bottle\\" means divide the total cost by the number of bottles: $4.20 ÷ 6 = $0.70. Check: 6 × $0.70 = $4.20. The traps: 6 ÷ 4.20 divides the wrong way, $25.20 multiplies, and $10.20 adds. The price of one bottle has to be less than the price of the whole pack.",["Correct: $4.20 ÷ 6 bottles = $0.70 per bottle.","Divided the wrong way: 6 ÷ 4.20 ≈ 1.43. Price per bottle is dollars divided by bottles.","Added 6 to $4.20. A price per bottle needs division.","Multiplied $4.20 by 6. That would be the cost of 6 packs, not the price of 1 bottle."]],"math-0177":["5x means 5 times x. To undo multiplying by 5, divide both sides by 5: x = 45 ÷ 5 = 9. Check: 5 × 9 = 45. The other choices use the wrong operation: 45 − 5 = 40, 45 + 5 = 50, and 45 × 5 = 225.",["Correct: divide both sides by 5: x = 45 ÷ 5 = 9.","Subtracted 5 from 45. 5x means 5 times x, so divide by 5.","Added 5 to 45. 5x means 5 times x, so divide by 5.","Multiplied 45 by 5. To undo multiplying by 5, divide by 5."]],"math-0178":["Each hour has 60 minutes, so 2.5 hours is 2.5 × 60 = 150 minutes. Another way: 2 hours is 120 minutes, and half an hour is 30 more, for 150. The big trap is 125, which reads 2.5 hours as \\"2 hours and 5 minutes.\\" The decimal 0.5 means half of an hour.",["Added 60 to 2.5. Each hour is 60 minutes, so multiply.","Counted only the 2 whole hours. The 0.5 hour adds another 30 minutes.","Read 2.5 hours as 2 hours 5 minutes. The 0.5 means half an hour, which is 30 minutes.","Correct: 2.5 hours × 60 minutes per hour = 150 minutes."]],"math-0179":["Order of operations says to multiply before you subtract. So first 2 × 3 = 6, and then 12 − 6 = 6. The most common trap is 30, which comes from doing 12 − 2 first and then multiplying by 3. 7 and 17 ignore the multiplication sign.",["Correct: multiply first, 2 × 3 = 6, then subtract: 12 − 6 = 6.","Subtracted both numbers: 12 − 2 − 3. The × means multiply 2 by 3.","Added all three numbers: 12 + 2 + 3.","Worked left to right: (12 − 2) × 3 = 30. Multiplication comes before subtraction."]],"math-0180":["Each pen costs $1.25, so 4 pens cost 4 × $1.25 = $5.00. One way to see it: 4 × $1 = $4, and 4 × $0.25 = $1, so the total is $5. The trap $4.25 multiplies the dollars but forgets to multiply the cents, and $5.25 adds 4 instead of multiplying.",["Multiplied only the dollars (4 × $1) and kept the $0.25 once. All of the $1.25 is multiplied by 4.","Correct: 4 × $1.25 = $5.00.","Added 4 to $1.25. Each of the 4 pens costs $1.25, so multiply.","Decimal slip: 4 × 1.25 is 5.00, not 50.00."]],"math-0300":["Let t = log₂ x. Take log₂ of both sides. The left side is log₂(x^t) = t · log₂ x = t². The right side is log₂ 8 + log₂ x² = 3 + 2t. So t² − 2t − 3 = 0, which factors as (t − 3)(t + 1) = 0, and t = 3 or t = −1. That means x = 2³ = 8 or x = 2^(−1) = 1/2. Check: 8^3 = 512 and 8 · 64 = 512; (1/2)^(−1) = 2 and 8 · 1/4 = 2. Both work, so the sum is 8 + 1/2 = 17/2. Traps: throwing away the negative t (a negative logarithm still gives a positive x), forgetting that log₂ 8 = 3, or giving the product, 4.",["Correct: taking log₂ of both sides gives t² = 3 + 2t, where t = log₂ x, so t = 3 or t = −1. Then x = 8 or x = 1/2, and 8 + 1/2 = 17/2.","Keeps only t = 3 (x = 8). The root t = −1 is fine: it gives x = 1/2, which is positive, and (1/2)^(−1) = 2 = 8(1/2)².","Drops the 8 when taking logs (log₂ 8 = 3, not 0). Then t² = 2t gives x = 1 or 4, which add to 5, but neither one works in the original equation.","This is the product of the solutions, 8 × 1/2, not their sum."]],"math-0301":["Because g subtracts 2f(...), g is smallest exactly when f(5 − x/2) is largest. The largest value of f is 7, reached only when its input is −3. So set 5 − x/2 = −3: x/2 = 8, and x = 16. At that x, g = 4 − 2(7) = −10. The lowest point is (16, −10), so a + b = 6. Notice that f\'s maximum turns into g\'s minimum because of the minus sign, and the x/2 inside doubles horizontal distances. Common slips: solving 5 − 2x = −3 (giving 4), a sign error (−16), or dropping the factor 2 in front of f.",["Sign slip solving 5 − x/2 = −3: it gives x/2 = 8, so x = 16, not −16.","Solves 5 − 2x = −3 (x = 4) instead of 5 − x/2 = −3. Dividing x by 2 inside f stretches the graph horizontally, so a must be 16.","Correct: g is lowest where f is highest, at 5 − x/2 = −3, so a = 16, and b = 4 − 2(7) = −10. Then a + b = 6.","Forgets the factor 2: 4 − 7 = −3 gives 16 + (−3) = 13. The lowest value is 4 − 2(7) = −10."]],"math-0302":["Exactly 5 draws means the second red marble comes on draw 5. So the first 4 draws hold exactly 1 red and 3 blue, and draw 5 is red. Ways to choose the first 4 marbles with 1 red and 3 blue: 4 × C(6,3) = 4 × 20 = 80, out of C(10,4) = 210, so that part has probability 80/210 = 8/21. Then 6 marbles are left, 3 of them red, so the 5th draw is red with probability 3/6 = 1/2. The answer is 8/21 × 1/2 = 4/21. Check another way: the positions of the 4 reds among 10 draws are equally likely, C(10,4) = 210 sets. One red is in positions 1 to 4 (4 ways), one is in position 5, and two are in positions 6 to 10 (C(5,2) = 10 ways): 40/210 = 4/21. Traps: stopping after the first step (8/21), or counting one order only (1/21).",["This is the probability of one particular order, such as R, B, B, B, R. The single red marble among the first four could be in any of 4 positions.","Correct: the first 4 draws must hold exactly 1 red (probability 4 · 20 / 210 = 8/21), and then the 5th draw must be red (3 of the 6 left), so 8/21 × 1/2 = 4/21.","Off by one: it places the other 2 red marbles among 6 later draws, but only draws 6 through 10, five of them, come after the 5th.","This is the probability that the first 4 draws hold exactly 1 red. It leaves out the requirement that the 5th draw is the second red."]],"math-0303":["Group the terms in blocks of three. Each term in terms 4-6 is r³ times the matching term in terms 1-3, so the second block\'s sum is r³ times the first block\'s. The first block adds to 21. The second adds to −147 − 21 = −168. So r³ = −168/21 = −8 (and r = −2). The third block is r³ times the second: −168 × (−8) = 1,344. The sum of the first 9 terms is 21 + (−168) + 1,344 = 1,197. (Check: r = −2 and the first term is 7, giving 7, −14, 28, −56, 112, −224, 448, −896, 1,792, which add to 1,197.) Traps: −315 treats the sums as arithmetic, 1,029 multiplies the running total by −7, and 1,344 is only the last block.",["Treats the sums as growing by a fixed amount (−168 each time). In a geometric sequence, each block of 3 terms is r³ times the block before it.","Assumes each sum is −7 times the one before (−147 × −7). It\'s each block of 3 terms that is multiplied by r³ = −8, not each running total by −7.","Correct: terms 4-6 add to −147 − 21 = −168 = 21r³, so r³ = −8. Terms 7-9 add to −168 × (−8) = 1,344, and the total is 21 − 168 + 1,344 = 1,197.","This is the sum of terms 7 through 9 only. The question asks for the sum of all of the first 9 terms."]],"math-0304":["Complete the square: (x − 3)² + (y + 4)² = k + 25. The center is (3, −4), and the radius squared is k + 25. Write the line as 2x − y + 5 = 0. The distance from the center to the line is |2(3) − (−4) + 5| / √(2² + 1²) = 15/√5 = 3√5, so its square is 45. A circle crosses a line at 2 points when its radius is greater than that distance: k + 25 > 45, so k > 20. The least integer is 21. At k = 20 the circle only touches the line once (tangent). Traps: forgetting the 25 (46), stopping at the tangent case (20), or flipping the center\'s signs (−19).",["Forgets the 25 added when completing the square: the radius squared is k + 25, not k.","Correct: the center is (3, −4) and r² = k + 25. The center is |2(3) − (−4) + 5|/√5 = 3√5 from the line, so 2 points need k + 25 > 45, which means k > 20.","At k = 20 the radius equals the distance, 3√5, so the circle is tangent to the line: exactly 1 point, not 2.","Uses the center (−3, 4) instead of (3, −4). Completing the square on x² − 6x gives (x − 3)², so the center\'s x-coordinate is +3."]],"math-0305":["One way: multiply it out. A² has rows [5 3] and [3 2], and A³ = A² · A has rows [13 8] and [8 5]. Now pA + qI has rows [2p + q, p] and [p, p + q]. The top-right entries must match, so p = 8. The top-left entries give 2(8) + q = 13, so q = −3. Check the bottom-right: 8 + (−3) = 5. So p + q = 5. A faster way: A² = 3A − I (check: 3A − I has rows [5 3] and [3 2]). Multiply by A: A³ = 3A² − A = 3(3A − I) − A = 8A − 3I. Traps: stopping at A² (2), a sign slip on q (11), or adding entries of A³ (21).",["Adds the entries of the first row of A³, 13 + 8. The question asks for the numbers p and q that build A³ out of A and I.","Sign slip: A³ = 8A − 3I, so q is −3, not 3.","Correct: A³ has rows [13 8] and [8 5]. Matching the top-right entry gives p = 8, and then 13 = 2(8) + q gives q = −3, so p + q = 5.","These are the numbers for A², not A³: A² = 3A − I, and 3 + (−1) = 2."]],"math-0306":["The height is h = 22 − 20 cos θ, where θ is the angle the wheel has turned since the seat left the bottom (θ = 360° × t/6). The seat is above 35 meters when 22 − 20 cos θ > 35, so cos θ < −13/20 = −0.65. Since cos 130.5° ≈ −0.65, this is true from θ ≈ 130.5° to θ ≈ 229.5°, a span of about 98.9°. That is 98.9/360 of a turn, and 6 × 98.9/360 ≈ 1.65, about 1.6 minutes. Traps: counting only the way up (0.8), treating the height as rising at a steady rate (1.1), or placing the center at 20 meters (1.4).",["This counts only the time from 35 meters up to the top. The seat is above 35 meters on the way down too, for the same length of time.","Treats the height as changing at a steady rate (40 meters up in 3 minutes). On a wheel, the height changes slowly near the top, so the seat stays up there longer.","Puts the center at 20 meters, the radius, forgetting that the lowest point is 2 meters above the ground.","Correct: the height is 22 − 20 cos(2πt/6). It is above 35 when cos(2πt/6) < −0.65, which is true for about 98.9° of each 360° turn: 6 × 98.9/360 ≈ 1.6 minutes."]],"math-0307":["Let y = f(x). First solve f(y) = 32: y² − 4y − 32 = 0, so (y − 8)(y + 4) = 0, and y = 8 or y = −4. Now find every x with f(x) equal to each of those. f(x) = 8: x² − 4x − 8 = 0, discriminant 16 + 32 = 48 > 0, so 2 solutions (2 ± 2√3). f(x) = −4: x² − 4x + 4 = 0, which is (x − 2)² = 0, so exactly 1 solution, x = 2. The value −4 is the lowest point of the parabola, so only the vertex reaches it. Total: 3. Check: f(2) = −4 and f(−4) = 16 + 16 = 32. Traps: assuming every value gives 2 solutions (4), or discarding the negative value (2).",["Assumes each value of f(x) gives 2 values of x. The equation f(x) = −4 has only one solution, because −4 is the lowest value of f.","Correct: f(y) = 32 gives y = 8 or y = −4. Then f(x) = 8 has 2 solutions, and f(x) = −4, the vertex value, has just 1 (x = 2): 3 in all.","Counts only the solutions of f(x) = 8 and throws out f(x) = −4, but f(2) = 4 − 8 = −4, so x = 2 also works.","Counts one x for each value of f(x). The equation f(x) = 8, that is x² − 4x − 8 = 0, has two real solutions, 2 ± 2√3."]],"math-0308":["Picture 100,000 flagged candidates. 99.9% of them, 99,900, are interference; the other 100 are not. The filter passes 90% of the 100, which is 90, and 2% of the 99,900, which is 1,998. So 90 + 1,998 = 2,088 candidates pass, and only 90 of them are not interference: 90/2,088 ≈ 0.043, about 4%. Even a good filter leaves mostly interference, because there is so much more of it to start with. Note that \'not interference\' doesn\'t mean \'from aliens\': it could be a natural signal from space. Traps: reading the 90% pass rate as the answer, or comparing the rates without the group sizes.",["Compares the 90% pass rate with the 2% that leaks through as if the two groups were the same size (90 out of 92). Interference candidates outnumber the others 999 to 1.","This is the chance that a candidate that is not interference passes the filter, the reverse of what is asked. It ignores how rare those candidates are.","Correct: per 100,000 candidates, 100 are not interference and 90 of those pass; 99,900 are interference and 2% of those, 1,998, pass. 90/(90 + 1,998) ≈ 4%.","This is the share of interference that gets through the filter, 2%, not the share of passing candidates that are not interference."]],"math-0309":["Same starlight as Earth means L/d² = 1/1² = 1. So 0.25/d² = 1, d² = 0.25, and d = 0.5 AU. Now use the period rule with M = 0.5: P² = (0.5)³/0.5 = 0.125/0.5 = 0.25, so P = 0.5 year, or about 0.5 × 365 ≈ 183 days. Traps: forgetting the square root for d (65 days), multiplying by the mass instead of dividing (91 days), or ignoring the mass (129 days). Both rules are real: starlight spreads out with the square of distance, and Newton\'s version of Kepler\'s third law gives P² = d³/M in these units.",["Sets d = 0.25 AU, forgetting the square root: L/d² = 1 means d² = 0.25, so d = 0.5 AU.","Multiplies by M instead of dividing: P² = d³/M, not d³ × M.","Leaves out the star\'s mass, using P² = d³ as for the Sun. This star has half the Sun\'s mass, so the planet orbits more slowly.","Correct: 0.25/d² = 1 gives d = 0.5 AU. Then P² = 0.125/0.5 = 0.25, so P = 0.5 year, about 183 days."]],"math-0320":["Use the complement. \\"2 or more\\" is everything except \\"0 stars\\" and \\"exactly 1 star.\\" P(0) = 0.9¹² ≈ 0.2824. P(exactly 1): choose which star (12 ways), times 0.1 for it, times 0.9¹¹ for the other 11: 12 × 0.1 × 0.9¹¹ ≈ 0.3766. So P(2 or more) = 1 − 0.2824 − 0.3766 ≈ 0.341, or 0.34. The big trap is 0.72, which is \\"at least 1\\": it subtracts only the zero case. The other wrong choices are the exactly-1 and exactly-2 pieces.",["This is the probability of exactly 2 stars, 66(0.1)²(0.9)¹⁰ ≈ 0.23. \\"2 or more\\" also includes 3, 4, ... up to 12.","Correct: P(0) = 0.9¹² ≈ 0.282 and P(1) = 12(0.1)(0.9)¹¹ ≈ 0.377, so P(2 or more) = 1 − 0.282 − 0.377 ≈ 0.34.","This is the probability of exactly 1 star, 12(0.1)(0.9)¹¹ ≈ 0.38, the piece you subtract, not the answer.","This is the probability of at least 1 star, 1 − 0.9¹² ≈ 0.72. It subtracts only the \\"no stars\\" case and forgets the \\"exactly 1 star\\" case."]],"math-0321":["At the limit, the signal strength is exactly the weakest one the receiver can detect, so P/d² stays the same. That means d² is proportional to P, and d is proportional to √P. Raising power by 50% multiplies it by 1.5, so the distance is multiplied by √1.5 ≈ 1.2247: an increase of about 22% (from 40 to about 49 light-years). The traps: assuming distance grows as fast as power (50%), squaring instead of taking the root (125%), or treating the square root of a 50% increase as half of 50% (25%). The 40 light-years doesn\'t affect the percent.",["Reversed relationship: squaring 1.5 gives 2.25, a 125% increase, but distance grows with the square root of power, not the square.","This treats distance as directly proportional to power; with the inverse square, distance grows only as the square root of power.","Halving the 50% treats a square root like dividing by 2; √1.5 is about 1.22, not 1.25.","Correct: the signal is just detectable when P/d² is the same, so d is proportional to √P; √1.5 ≈ 1.225, an increase of about 22%."]],"math-0322":["An increase of 700% means the culture gained 7 times its starting size, so it ends at 1 + 7 = 8 times the start. Doubling 3 times gives 2³ = 8, so 3 doubling periods fit in 12 hours: d = 12 ÷ 3 = 4. The main trap is reading \\"increases by 700%\\" as \\"becomes 7 times as large\\"; solving 2^(12/d) = 7 gives d ≈ 4.3. Other slips treat each doubling as adding 100% (7 doublings, d ≈ 1.7), or count 8 doublings instead of 3.",["Treats the 8-fold growth as 8 doublings (12 ÷ 8 = 1.5); 8 = 2³, so it is 3 doublings.","Treats the 700% increase as 7 doublings (12 ÷ 7 ≈ 1.7); each doubling multiplies the number by 2, it doesn\'t add 100%.","Correct: an increase of 700% means the final number is 8 times the start; 8 = 2³, so 3 doublings took 12 hours and d = 4.","Percent trap: this uses 7 times the start (12 ÷ log₂7 ≈ 4.3), but an increase of 700% gives 100% + 700% = 800% of the start."]],"math-0323":["Let BC = x. Side AC = 5 is opposite angle B, so the law of cosines gives 5² = x² + 7² − 2(x)(7)cos 30°, or 25 = x² + 49 − 7√3x. That gives x² − 7√3x + 24 = 0. The discriminant is 147 − 96 = 51, which is positive, and the product of the roots, 24, is positive too, so both roots are positive: x = (7√3 ± √51)/2 ≈ 9.63 and 2.49. Each one gives a real triangle (this is the ambiguous case: the side opposite the given angle is shorter than the other given side). Their sum is 7√3 ≈ 12.12. Traps: keeping only one triangle, or mixing up the sum with the product (24) or with the difference (√51).",["This is the product of the two possible lengths (from x² − 7√3x + 24 = 0), not their sum.","Correct: with BC = x, 25 = 49 + x² − 7√3x, so x² − 7√3x + 24 = 0. Both roots are positive, so two triangles exist, and the sum of the roots is 7√3.","This is only the longer of the two possible lengths. Because the side opposite angle B (AC = 5) is shorter than AB = 7, two different triangles fit the given information.","This is the difference between the two possible lengths (the square root of the discriminant), not their sum."]],"math-0330":["First find the discount: 20% of $35.00 = 0.20 × 35 = $7.00. Then subtract it: $35.00 − $7.00 = $28.00. A quicker way: paying 80% of the price, 0.80 × 35 = $28.00. Traps: stopping at the discount ($7.00), taking off 20 dollars instead of 20% ($15.00), or adding the discount ($42.00).",["Intermediate result: $7.00 is the discount, not the sale price. Subtract it from $35.00.","Subtracted 20 dollars instead of 20 percent. 20% of $35.00 is $7.00.","Correct: 20% of $35.00 is $7.00, and $35.00 − $7.00 = $28.00.","Added the discount: $35.00 + $7.00. A sale lowers the price."]],"math-0331":["The mean is the total divided by the number of values. Total: 3 + 5 + 4 + 8 = 20 miles. Divide by 4 days: 20 ÷ 4 = 5 miles per day. Check: 5 is between the smallest and largest runs, as a mean must be. Traps: the median (4.5), the midpoint of the smallest and largest (5.5), and the total (20).",["That\'s the median (the middle of 3, 4, 5, 8), not the mean.","Correct: (3 + 5 + 4 + 8) ÷ 4 = 20 ÷ 4 = 5.","That\'s halfway between the smallest and largest values, (3 + 8) ÷ 2, not the mean.","Intermediate result: 20 is the total. Divide by the 4 days."]],"math-0332":["Fencing goes around the outside, so you need the perimeter. A rectangle has 2 lengths and 2 widths: 8 + 5 + 8 + 5 = 26 feet, or 2(8 + 5) = 26. Traps: adding just one length and one width (13), or leaving out one side (18 or 21).",["Added one length and one width. That covers only half of the way around.","Added only 3 sides: one length and both widths (8 + 5 + 5).","Added only 3 sides: both lengths and one width (8 + 8 + 5).","Correct: the perimeter is 8 + 5 + 8 + 5 = 26 feet."]],"math-0333":["The three angles of any triangle add up to 180°. The two given angles add to 50° + 60° = 110°, so the third angle is 180° − 110° = 70°. Check: 50 + 60 + 70 = 180. Traps: stopping at 110°, subtracting only one angle (130°), or using 360° (250°).",["Correct: the angles of a triangle add to 180°, and 180° − 50° − 60° = 70°.","Intermediate result: 110° is the sum of the two given angles. Subtract it from 180°.","Subtracted only one angle: 180° − 50° = 130°. Both given angles must be subtracted.","Used 360° (the sum for a four-sided figure) instead of 180°."]],"math-0334":["To find the median, put the numbers in order first: 3, 6, 8, 12, 15. The middle number of the 5 is the third one, 8. The big trap is taking the middle of the unsorted list, which is 15. 8.8 is the mean (sum 44 divided by 5), and 12 is the range.",["Correct: in order, the list is 3, 6, 8, 12, 15, and the middle number is 8.","That\'s the mean: 44 ÷ 5 = 8.8. The median is the middle value.","That\'s the range: 15 − 3 = 12.","15 is in the middle of the list as written, but the list must be put in order first."]],"math-0335":["Probability = (number of blue marbles) ÷ (total number of marbles). There are 6 blue marbles and 4 + 6 + 2 = 12 marbles in all, so the probability is 6/12 = 1/2. Traps: forgetting the green marbles (3/5) and treating each color as equally likely (1/3).",["Treated it as picking 1 of the 6 blue marbles. The question asks for any blue marble out of all 12.","Treated the 3 colors as equally likely. There are more blue marbles than red or green ones.","Correct: 6 of the 12 marbles are blue, and 6/12 = 1/2.","Left out the green marbles: 6 out of 10. The total is 4 + 6 + 2 = 12."]],"math-0336":["A fraction means division: 2/5 = 2 ÷ 5 = 0.4. Another way: multiply the top and bottom by 2 to get 4/10, which is 0.4. Traps: dividing the wrong way (2.5), writing the digits side by side (0.25), and putting the decimal point one place too far left (0.04).",["Flipped the fraction: 5 ÷ 2 = 2.5. The fraction 2/5 means 2 ÷ 5.","Correct: 2 ÷ 5 = 0.4 (2/5 = 4/10).","Wrote the digits 2 and 5 after the decimal point. That isn\'t how a fraction turns into a decimal.","Decimal point slip: 2/5 is 4 tenths, not 4 hundredths."]],"math-0337":["Volume of a rectangular box = length × width × height = 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 cubic inches. Traps: adding the measurements (9), and computing surface area (52) or half of it (26). Surface area is in square inches; volume is in cubic inches.",["Added the three measurements. Volume is length × width × height.","Correct: 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 cubic inches.","That\'s half of the surface area (12 + 8 + 6). Volume multiplies the three measurements.","That\'s the surface area, 2(12 + 8 + 6) = 52 square inches, which measures the outside, not the space inside."]],"math-0338":["A fraction of the whole is (part) ÷ (total). The total is 18 + 12 + 6 + 24 = 60 shirts, and 12 were blue, so the fraction is 12/60 = 1/5. Traps: comparing blue with just one other color (1/2 or 2/3), or assuming each color is one fourth.",["Correct: 18 + 12 + 6 + 24 = 60 shirts, and 12/60 = 1/5.","Treated blue as 1 of 4 equal colors. The colors didn\'t sell equal numbers.","Compared blue with black only (12/24). The fraction needs blue out of all shirts.","Compared blue with red only (12/18). The fraction needs blue out of all shirts."]],"math-0339":["Exponents come before addition. 3² = 3 × 3 = 9 and 4² = 4 × 4 = 16, so the sum is 9 + 16 = 25. Traps: squaring the sum, (3 + 4)² = 49; doubling instead of squaring (14); and skipping the exponents (7).",["Ignored the exponents and just added 3 + 4.","Doubled instead of squaring: 3 × 2 + 4 × 2 = 14. 3² means 3 × 3.","Correct: 3² + 4² = 9 + 16 = 25.","Added first and then squared: (3 + 4)² = 49. Square each number first, then add."]],"math-0340":["A multiple of a number is that number times a whole number. Check each choice against both 4 and 6. 16 works for 4 only, and 18 works for 6 only. 10 is the sum of 4 and 6, which works for neither. 24 = 4 × 6, so it divides evenly by both. (12 is the smallest number that works, but it isn\'t a choice.)",["10 is 4 + 6, the sum of the two numbers, not a multiple of them. 10 ÷ 4 is not a whole number.","16 is a multiple of 4 (4 × 4), but 16 ÷ 6 is not a whole number.","18 is a multiple of 6 (6 × 3), but 18 ÷ 4 is not a whole number.","Correct: 24 = 4 × 6 and 24 = 6 × 4, so 24 is a multiple of both."]],"math-0341":["Each liter is 1,000 milliliters, so multiply: 0.75 × 1,000 = 750 mL. A check: a milliliter is a much smaller unit than a liter, so the number of milliliters should be bigger than 0.75. Traps: dividing instead of multiplying (0.00075), or moving the decimal point too few places (7.5 or 75).",["Divided by 1,000 instead of multiplying. Milliliters are smaller, so there are more of them.","Multiplied by 10 instead of 1,000.","Multiplied by 100 instead of 1,000.","Correct: 0.75 × 1,000 = 750 milliliters."]],"math-0342":["Square both sides: sin²θ + 2 sin θ cos θ + cos²θ = 1/25. Since sin²θ + cos²θ = 1, this gives 2 sin θ cos θ = 1/25 − 1 = −24/25, so sin θ cos θ = −12/25. So sin θ and cos θ are two numbers whose sum is 1/5 and whose product is −12/25. They are the solutions of t² − (1/5)t − 12/25 = 0, or 25t² − 5t − 12 = 0, which factors as (5t − 4)(5t + 3) = 0: t = 4/5 or t = −3/5. For 0 < θ < π, sin θ is positive, so sin θ = 4/5 and cos θ = −3/5 (θ is in Quadrant II). Then tan θ = −4/3. Check: 4/5 + (−3/5) = 1/5. The tempting shortcut is to spot a 3-4-5 triangle and give a positive tangent; the negative product says the signs differ, and the interval decides which one is negative.",["Correct: sin θ cos θ = −12/25, so {sin θ, cos θ} = {4/5, −3/5}; sin θ > 0 on (0, π), so tan θ = (4/5)/(−3/5) = −4/3.","Swapped the values: sin θ = −3/5 and cos θ = 4/5 also add to 1/5, but sin θ is negative there, and sin θ > 0 when 0 < θ < π.","Positive tangent means sin θ and cos θ have the same sign, but their product, −12/25, is negative. This also swaps sine and cosine.","Right size, wrong sign. Because sin θ cos θ < 0, one of them is negative, so tan θ is negative."]],"math-0343":["Multiply both sides by z: z² + 25 = 6z, so z² − 6z + 25 = 0. The quadratic formula gives z = (6 ± √(36 − 100))/2 = 3 ± 4i. Take z = 3 + 4i. Then z² = 9 + 24i + 16i² = −7 + 24i, and z³ = (−7 + 24i)(3 + 4i) = −21 − 28i + 72i + 96i² = −117 + 44i. The other root, 3 − 4i, gives −117 − 44i, so the real part is −117 for both. Second way, with no complex arithmetic: z² = 6z − 25, so z³ = 6z² − 25z = 6(6z − 25) − 25z = 11z − 150. The real part of z is 3, so the real part of z³ is 33 − 150 = −117. Traps: cubing each part separately, or forgetting that i² = −1.",["Correct: z = 3 ± 4i, and (3 ± 4i)³ = −117 ± 44i, so the real part is −117 either way.","Cubed the two parts separately: 3³ + (4i)³, with (4i)³ treated as −64. But (a + b)³ ≠ a³ + b³, and (4i)³ = −64i is imaginary anyway.","Cubed only the real part, 3³ = 27. The imaginary part also adds to the real part of z³, through (4i)².","Treated i² as +1, so the term 3 · 3 · (4i)² became +144 instead of −144: 27 + 144 = 171."]],"math-0344":["From the hyperbola, y² = 16(x²/9 − 1) = (16/9)x² − 16. The distance condition is (x − 5)² + y² = 9. Substitute: x² − 10x + 25 + (16/9)x² − 16 = 9, so (25/9)x² − 10x = 0, or x((25/9)x − 10) = 0. That gives x = 0 or x = 90/25 = 18/5. Now check each. x = 0 would need y² = −16, which is impossible, so throw it out. x = 18/5 gives y² = 16(324/225 − 1) = 7.04, and (3.6 − 5)² + 7.04 = 1.96 + 7.04 = 9. So x = 18/5. Second way: c² = 9 + 16 = 25, so (5,0) is a focus. Every point of the left branch is at least 3 + 5 = 8 from it, so P is on the right branch, and the distances to the two foci differ by 2a = 6: P is 9 from (−5,0). Subtracting (x − 5)² + y² = 9 from (x + 5)² + y² = 81 gives 20x = 72, so x = 18/5. The trap is keeping x = 0, a solution of the combined equation that isn\'t on the hyperbola.",["Extraneous solution: the algebra gives x = 0 or x = 18/5, but no point of the hyperbola has x = 0 (it would need y² = −16).","3 is the x-coordinate of the vertex (3,0), which is only 2 units from (5,0), not 3.","Correct: substituting y² = 16(x²/9 − 1) into (x − 5)² + y² = 9 gives x = 0 or 18/5, and only 18/5 is on the hyperbola.","Assumed P is straight above (5,0). At x = 5, y² = 16(25/9 − 1) = 256/9, so that point is 16/3 from (5,0), not 3."]],"math-0345":["Because f(f(x)) = x, applying f twice gets you back where you started, so f is its own inverse. Since f(5) = 9, it follows that f(9) = f(f(5)) = 5. Now there are two equations. f(5) = 9: (5a + b)/2 = 9, so 5a + b = 18. f(9) = 5: (9a + b)/6 = 5, so 9a + b = 30. Subtracting gives 4a = 12, so a = 3 and b = 3, and a + b = 6. Check: f(x) = (3x + 3)/(x − 3). Then f(f(x)) = [3(3x + 3)/(x − 3) + 3] / [(3x + 3)/(x − 3) − 3] = [(9x + 9 + 3x − 9)/(x − 3)] / [(3x + 3 − 3x + 9)/(x − 3)] = 12x/12 = x. Second way: a function (ax + b)/(x + d) is its own inverse when a = −d, so here a = 3, and then 5a + b = 18 gives b = 3. The trap is having only one equation from f(5) = 9; the self-inverse condition supplies the second.",["Took a = −3, copying the −3 from the denominator. For f to undo itself, a must be the opposite of that −3, which is 3.","18 is 5a + b, from f(5) = (5a + b)/2 = 9. That is one equation in two unknowns, not the value of a + b.","Took a = 1, matching the coefficient of x in the denominator. Then b = 13, but that f does not undo itself.","Correct: f(f(5)) = 5 means f(9) = 5. With f(5) = 9, that gives 5a + b = 18 and 9a + b = 30, so a = 3, b = 3, and a + b = 6."]],"math-0346":["No digit can be 0, so look for four digits from 1 to 9 whose product is 24 = 2 · 2 · 2 · 3. Sort by how many 1s there are. Three 1s: the last digit would be 24, not a digit. Two 1s: the other two multiply to 24, so 3 and 8 or 4 and 6. One 1: three digits of at least 2 with product 24: 2, 2, 6 or 2, 3, 4. No 1s: 2, 2, 2, 3. Now count orders. A set with one pair of repeated digits has 4!/2! = 12 orders: {1,1,3,8}, {1,1,4,6}, {1,2,2,6} give 36. {1,2,3,4} has 4! = 24 orders. {2,2,2,3} has 4 orders (choose the spot for the 3). Total: 36 + 24 + 4 = 64. Traps: mishandling the repeated digits (72, 84, or 120).",["Correct: the digit sets are {1,1,3,8}, {1,1,4,6}, {1,2,2,6} (12 orders each), {1,2,3,4} (24 orders), and {2,2,2,3} (4 orders): 36 + 24 + 4 = 64.","Gave {2,2,2,3} 12 orders, like the sets with one repeated pair. With three 2s, the only choice is where the 3 goes: 4 orders.","Gave {2,2,2,3} 24 orders, as if its four digits were all different. With three 2s, only the spot for the 3 matters: 4 orders.","Counted 24 orders for each of the 5 digit sets, ignoring the repeated digits that cut down the number of different orders."]],"math-0347":["Start with the chance of two heads. Two-headed coin: 1/3 · 1 = 1/3. Fair coin: 2/3 · 1/4 = 1/6. Together that\'s 1/2. Given the two heads, P(two-headed coin) = (1/3)/(1/2) = 2/3 and P(fair coin) = (1/6)/(1/2) = 1/3. The third flip is heads for sure with the two-headed coin and half the time with a fair coin: 2/3 · 1 + 1/3 · 1/2 = 5/6. Second way: P(3 heads) ÷ P(2 heads). P(3 heads) = 1/3 · 1 + 2/3 · 1/8 = 5/12, and 5/12 ÷ 1/2 = 5/6. Traps: treating the flips as independent (1/2), stopping at the chance the coin is two-headed (2/3), or using only one of the heads (3/4).",["Treated the coin as fair, so each flip is a 50-50 chance. The two heads make it more likely that Ari has the two-headed coin.","2/3 is the chance that Ari has the two-headed coin after the two heads (and also the chance of heads on a first flip). Even a fair coin can still land heads.","Updated on only one head: after one head, the two-headed coin has probability 1/2, giving 1/2 + 1/2 · 1/2 = 3/4. Both heads count.","Correct: after two heads, the coin is two-headed with probability 2/3 and fair with probability 1/3, so P(heads) = 2/3 + 1/3 · 1/2 = 5/6."]],"math-0348":["Rewrite every log in base 2. Let a = log₂ x and b = log₂ y. Since 8 = 2³, log₈ y = b/3; since 4 = 2², log₄ x = a/2. The equations become a + b/3 = 5 and a/2 − b = −1. From the second, a = 2b − 2. Substitute: 2b − 2 + b/3 = 5, so 7b/3 = 7, b = 3, and a = 4. So x = 2⁴ = 16 and y = 2³ = 8. Check: log₂ 16 + log₈ 8 = 4 + 1 = 5, and log₄ 16 − log₂ 8 = 2 − 3 = −1. Then logₓ y = log₁₆ 8 = b/a = 3/4; check: 16^(3/4) = (2⁴)^(3/4) = 2³ = 8. Traps: flipping the ratio (4/3) and confusing a logarithm with a quotient of the numbers (1/2 or 2).",["That\'s y/x = 8/16. logₓ y is the exponent that turns x into y, not the quotient y/x.","Correct: x = 16 = 2⁴ and y = 8 = 2³, so logₓ y = 3/4 (16^(3/4) = 8).","Reciprocal: 4/3 is log_y x. logₓ y = (log₂ y)/(log₂ x) = 3/4.","That\'s x/y = 16/8, not a logarithm."]],"math-0349":["Let Sₙ = n² + 4n + 1 be the sum of the first n terms. The first term is the sum of just one term: a₁ = S₁ = 1 + 4 + 1 = 6. Any later term is the difference of two sums: a₁₀ = S₁₀ − S₉ = (100 + 40 + 1) − (81 + 36 + 1) = 141 − 118 = 23. So a₁ + a₁₀ = 6 + 23 = 29. Second way: for n ≥ 2, aₙ = Sₙ − Sₙ₋₁ = 2n + 3, so the terms are 6, 7, 9, 11, ..., 23. Check: 6 + 7 = 13 = S₂ = 4 + 8 + 1. The trap: the +1 in the formula makes the first term break the pattern, so 2n + 3 gives the wrong first term (5).",["Used 141, the sum of the first 10 terms, as the 10th term. The 10th term is the sum of 10 terms minus the sum of 9.","Used 2n + 4 for the nth term, as if the terms grew like the \\"slope\\" of n² + 4n. The 10th term is 141 − 118 = 23.","Correct: the 1st term is 1 + 4 + 1 = 6 and the 10th is 141 − 118 = 23, so the sum is 29.","Used the rule 2n + 3 for every term, giving a first term of 5. That rule holds only from the 2nd term on; the first term is 6."]],"math-0350":["Solve the orbit law for a: a³ = M · P² = 1.5 × 36 = 54, so a = ∛54 ≈ 3.78 AU. The planet appears about 3.78/d arcseconds from its star, and the telescope needs 3.78/d ≥ 0.1, so d ≤ 3.78/0.1 = 37.8. To the nearest parsec, that\'s 38. Check: at d = 37.8, the separation is 3.78/37.8 = 0.1 arcsecond, right at the limit; farther away it would look smaller. Traps: dividing by the mass (29), ignoring it (33), or taking a square root (73).",["Divided by the mass: a³ = 36/1.5 = 24. Solving P² = a³/M for a³ gives a³ = M · P², so a heavier star means a wider orbit.","Left out the star\'s mass: a³ = 6² = 36, so a ≈ 3.30 AU. The star\'s mass is 1.5, not 1.","Correct: a³ = 1.5 × 6² = 54, so a ≈ 3.78 AU, and 3.78/d ≥ 0.1 means d ≤ 37.8 parsecs.","Took a square root instead of a cube root: √54 ≈ 7.35 AU. The law has a³, so a is the cube root."]],"math-0351":["Measure time t in years from when the message is sent. The message reaches the planet at t = 12, and the reply leaves then. Probe\'s distance from Earth: 0.2(t − 3). Reply\'s distance from Earth: 12 − (t − 12) = 24 − t. They meet when 0.2(t − 3) = 24 − t, so 0.2t − 0.6 = 24 − t, 1.2t = 24.6, and t = 20.5. The probe is then 0.2 × 17.5 = 3.5 light-years from Earth. Second way: at t = 12 the probe is 0.2 × 9 = 1.8 light-years out, so the gap is 12 − 1.8 = 10.2 light-years. The reply and probe close that gap at 1 + 0.2 = 1.2 light-years per year, taking 8.5 years, and the probe moves 0.2 × 8.5 = 1.7 more: 1.8 + 1.7 = 3.5. Traps: stopping at year 12 (1.8), ignoring the delay (4.0), or forgetting it at the end (4.1).",["That\'s where the probe is when the reply is sent (after 9 years of travel). The probe keeps moving while the reply comes toward it.","Correct: the reply passes the probe 20.5 years after the message was sent, when the probe has traveled 17.5 years: 0.2 × 17.5 = 3.5.","Ignored the 3-year delay, as if the probe left with the message: then they meet in year 20, at 0.2 × 20 = 4.0.","Found the right meeting time, year 20.5, but multiplied all of it by 0.2. The probe was moving for only 20.5 − 3 = 17.5 years."]],"math-0352":["Distances first: d = 1/0.25 = 4 parsecs and d = 1/0.1 = 10 parsecs. The Sun and the two stars form a triangle with sides 4 and 10 and a 60° angle between them at the Sun. By the law of cosines, the third side s satisfies s² = 4² + 10² − 2(4)(10) cos 60° = 16 + 100 − 40 = 76, so s ≈ 8.72 parsecs. In light-years, 8.72 × 3.26 ≈ 28.4, and at 1 light-year per year the signal takes about 28.4 years. Second way: put the Sun at (0,0), one star at (4,0), and the other at (10 cos 60°, 10 sin 60°) = (5, 8.66). The distance is √(1² + 8.66²) = √76 ≈ 8.72 parsecs. Traps: skipping the conversion (8.7), subtracting the distances (19.6), or using the sine (22.3).",["8.7 is the distance between the stars in parsecs. Convert to light-years (× 3.26) to get the travel time in years.","Subtracted the distances, 10 − 4 = 6 parsecs, as if both stars were in the same direction. They are 60° apart.","Used sin 60° instead of cos 60° in the law of cosines: 116 − 80(0.866) ≈ 46.7, giving about 6.8 parsecs.","Correct: the stars are 4 and 10 parsecs away, so the distance between them is √(16 + 100 − 2 · 4 · 10 · cos 60°) = √76 ≈ 8.72 parsecs ≈ 28.4 light-years."]],"math-0353":["Work through the chain. Stars ∝ r³, so 5 times as many stars needs a reach 5^(1/3) ≈ 1.71 times as far. Signal strength ∝ 1/r², so signals from that far are (5^(1/3))² = 5^(2/3) ≈ 2.92 times weaker, and the dish must detect signals that much weaker. Weakest detectable signal ∝ 1/area, so the area must be 5^(2/3) times as big. Area ∝ diameter², so the diameter grows by √(5^(2/3)) = 5^(1/3) ≈ 1.71. Check: a dish 1.71 times as wide has 1.71² ≈ 2.92 times the area, which exactly makes up for signals from 1.71 times as far. Traps: stopping at the area (2.9), matching the area to the star count (2.2), or forgetting the cube (5.0).",["Correct: the reach must grow by 5^(1/3), so the dish must detect signals 5^(2/3) times weaker; the area grows by 5^(2/3), and the diameter by 5^(1/3) ≈ 1.71.","Assumed the dish\'s area must grow by the same factor as the number of stars, 5, giving a diameter factor of √5 ≈ 2.24. The area must grow by 5^(2/3).","Stopped at the factor for the area, 5^(2/3) ≈ 2.92. The diameter grows by the square root of that.","Treated the number of stars as proportional to distance instead of distance³: reach × 5, area × 25, diameter × 5."]],"math-0354":["Look for two numbers that multiply to −21 and add to −4. Those are −7 and 3, so x² − 4x − 21 = (x − 7)(x + 3). Set each factor equal to 0: x = 7 or x = −3. The positive solution is 7. Check: 49 − 28 − 21 = 0. Traps: reading the factor (x + 3) as the solution 3, giving the negative solution −3 when the question asks for the positive one, and copying the coefficient 4 from the equation.",["That\'s the other solution, from the factor (x + 3). It is negative, and the question asks for the positive one.","Sign slip: the factor (x + 3) gives x = −3, not 3. That solution is negative.","Copied the coefficient of x. The solutions come from factoring, not from the coefficients.","Correct: x² − 4x − 21 = (x − 7)(x + 3), so x = 7 or x = −3, and 7 is the positive one."]],"math-0355":["The ball hits the ground when h = 0. Factor out −16: −16(t² − 3t − 4) = 0, and t² − 3t − 4 = (t − 4)(t + 1). So t = 4 or t = −1. A time before the toss makes no sense, so the answer is 4 seconds. Check: −16(16) + 48(4) + 64 = −256 + 192 + 64 = 0. Traps: 1.5 seconds is the top of the flight, and 3 seconds is when the ball is back at roof height (you get it by dropping the 64), not on the ground.",["Sign slip: the factor (t + 1) gives t = −1, which is not a time after the toss.","That\'s when the ball is highest (t = −48 ÷ (2 × −16) = 1.5), not when it lands.","Solved −16t² + 48t = 0 by dropping the 64. That\'s when the ball falls back to roof height, 64 feet up.","Correct: −16t² + 48t + 64 = −16(t − 4)(t + 1) = 0 gives t = 4 (t = −1 is before the toss)."]],"math-0356":["Divide the equation by 3 to get x² − 4x + 5/3 = 0. For x² + px + q = 0, the two solutions add to −p and multiply to q. Here they add to 4 and multiply to 5/3. You can confirm with the quadratic formula: x = (12 ± √(144 − 60))/6 = (12 ± √84)/6. The ± parts cancel when you add, leaving 24/6 = 4. Traps: a sign slip (−4), forgetting to divide by 3 (12), and giving the product instead of the sum (5/3).",["Forgot to divide by the leading coefficient 3. First divide the equation by 3: x² − 4x + 5/3 = 0.","Correct: for ax² + bx + c = 0, the solutions add to −b/a = 12/3 = 4.","That\'s the product of the solutions, c/a = 5/3, not the sum.","Sign slip: the sum of the solutions is −b/a = −(−12)/3 = 4, not −4."]],"math-0357":["Start with P = 2l + 2w. Subtract 2l from both sides: P − 2l = 2w. Divide both sides by 2: w = (P − 2l)/2. Check with numbers: a 5-by-3 rectangle has P = 16, and (16 − 10)/2 = 3. Traps: dropping the 2 on l, halving only one term, and adding 2l instead of subtracting it.",["Correct: subtract 2l from both sides to get P − 2l = 2w, then divide by 2.","Dropped the 2 in front of l. You must subtract all of 2l.","Divided only P by 2. Dividing P − 2l by 2 halves both terms: P/2 − l.","Sign slip: to move 2l to the other side, subtract it; don\'t add it."]],"math-0358":["Start with A = (1/2)h(b₁ + b₂). Multiply both sides by 2: 2A = h(b₁ + b₂). Divide both sides by h: 2A/h = b₁ + b₂. Subtract b₁: b₂ = 2A/h − b₁. Check with numbers: h = 4, b₁ = 3, b₂ = 7 gives A = 20, and 40/4 − 3 = 7. Traps: dividing by 2 instead of multiplying, subtracting h instead of dividing by it, and subtracting b₁ before it has been multiplied by h.",["Correct: multiply both sides by 2/h to get 2A/h = b₁ + b₂, then subtract b₁.","Undid the 1/2 by dividing by 2. To undo a factor of 1/2, multiply by 2.","Subtracted h instead of dividing by it. h multiplies the sum, so you divide by h.","Divided by h too early: 2A = hb₁ + hb₂, so b₁ also gets multiplied by h before you subtract it. This version leaves b₁ unscaled."]],"math-0359":["Multiply both sides by (x − 2): y(x − 2) = 3x + k, so xy − 2y = 3x + k. Get the x terms on one side: xy − 3x = 2y + k. Factor out x: x(y − 3) = 2y + k. Divide: x = (2y + k)/(y − 3). Check with numbers: x = 4 and k = 1 give y = 13/2. Then (13 + 1)/(13/2 − 3) = 14/(7/2) = 4. The wrong choices each come from one sign slip while moving terms. The last one has the denominator flipped to 3 − y, which makes the whole answer the negative of the right one.",["Sign slip on k: k starts on the right side of xy − 2y = 3x + k and stays +k. Only the terms that cross the equals sign change sign.","Moved −2y to the right side without changing its sign. In xy − 2y = 3x + k, moving −2y across gives +2y.","Wrote the x terms as x(3 − y) but kept the right side as 2y + k. Flipping one side\'s signs means flipping the other side too. This is the negative of the correct answer.","Correct: y(x − 2) = 3x + k gives xy − 3x = 2y + k, so x(y − 3) = 2y + k."]],"math-0360":["Square both sides to undo the square root: x + 5 = 16. Subtract 5: x = 11. Check: √(11 + 5) = √16 = 4. Traps: skipping the squaring step (−1), doubling instead of squaring (3), and adding 5 instead of subtracting it (21).",["Added 5 instead of subtracting it: x + 5 = 16 means x = 16 − 5.","Correct: squaring both sides gives x + 5 = 16, so x = 11.","Doubled 4 instead of squaring it: x + 5 = 8. Squaring 4 gives 16.","Forgot to square: x + 5 = 4 gives −1. Undo the square root by squaring both sides."]],"math-0361":["Move one root to the other side first: √(x + 7) = 1 + √x. Square both sides: x + 7 = 1 + 2√x + x. The x terms cancel, leaving 6 = 2√x, so √x = 3 and x = 9. Check: √16 − √9 = 4 − 3 = 1. Traps: stopping at √x = 3 or 2√x = 6, and squaring 1 + √x as 1 + x. That last slip drops the middle term and makes it look like there is no solution.",["That\'s the value of √x. Square it to get x: 3² = 9.","That\'s 2√x. Divide by 2 to get √x = 3, then square: x = 9.","Correct: √(x + 7) = 1 + √x. Squaring gives x + 7 = 1 + 2√x + x, so √x = 3 and x = 9.","Squared as if (1 + √x)² = 1 + x, which leads to 7 = 1. The square of a sum also has the middle term 2√x."]],"math-0362":["Losing 15% each year means keeping 85% each year, so multiply by 0.85 once per year. After 3 years: 24,000 × 0.85 × 0.85 × 0.85 = 24,000 × 0.614125 = $14,739. Traps: taking 15% of the original price each year ($13,200, which treats the drop as linear), and stopping after 1 or 2 years ($20,400 or $17,340).",["Subtracted 3 × 15% = 45% of the original price. Each year\'s 15% comes off the current value, which keeps shrinking.","Correct: 24,000 × 0.85³ = 24,000 × 0.614125 = 14,739.","That\'s the value after 2 years (24,000 × 0.85²). One more year of decline is needed.","That\'s the value after 1 year only (24,000 × 0.85)."]],"math-0363":["There are 24 ÷ 6 = 4 half-life periods in 24 hours. Each one halves the amount: 400 → 200 → 100 → 50 → 25. That\'s 400 × (1/2)⁴ = 25 milligrams. Traps: dividing by 4 instead of by 2⁴ (100), and halving too few times (50 after 3 halvings, 200 after 1).",["Correct: 24 hours is 4 periods of 6 hours, so 400 × (1/2)⁴ = 400 ÷ 16 = 25.","Halved only 3 times (an off-by-one slip). After 6, 12, 18, and 24 hours there are 4 halvings.","Divided 400 by the number of halvings, 4. Each halving divides by 2, so 4 halvings divide by 2⁴ = 16.","Halved only once. The amount halves every 6 hours, and 24 hours holds 4 of those periods."]],"math-0364":["Rewrite the model with exponent t: (0.9)^(t/2) = (0.9^(1/2))^t ≈ (0.9487)^t. Each year the value is multiplied by about 0.9487, so it drops by about 1 − 0.9487 = 0.0513, or 5.1%. Check: two years of 5.13% drops give 0.9487² ≈ 0.9, a 10% drop in 2 years. Traps: reading 10% as the yearly rate, dividing 10% by 2 (4.5% ignores compounding), and squaring 0.9 instead of taking its square root (19%).",["Split the 10% drop evenly over the 2 years. Percent changes compound, so the yearly drop is 1 − √0.9 ≈ 5.1%.","Correct: each year V is multiplied by 0.9^(1/2) ≈ 0.9487, a drop of about 5.1%.","That\'s the drop every 2 years, since the exponent is t/2. The yearly drop is smaller.","Treated the exponent as 2t, so the yearly factor became 0.9² = 0.81. With t/2 the factor is √0.9."]],"math-0365":["The total cost is 25 + 0.10t, and it can be at most 40: 25 + 0.10t ≤ 40. Subtract 25: 0.10t ≤ 15. Divide by 0.10: t ≤ 150. So Jordan can send at most 150 texts. Check: 25 + 0.10(150) = 40. Traps: ignoring the $25 charge (400), adding it (650), or dividing the wrong amount (250).",["Correct: 25 + 0.10t ≤ 40 gives 0.10t ≤ 15, so t ≤ 150.","Divided the $25 monthly charge by $0.10. The money left for texts is 40 − 25 = $15.","Divided $40 by $0.10 and ignored the $25 monthly charge.","Added the $25 charge to the budget instead of subtracting it: (40 + 25) ÷ 0.10."]],"math-0366":["Subtract 3 from all three parts: −10 < 2x ≤ 8. Divide by 2: −5 < x ≤ 4. The integers are −4, −3, −2, −1, 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4, which is 9 integers. Watch the endpoints: −5 is out (strict <), and 4 is in (≤). Traps: getting an endpoint wrong (8 or 10), and forgetting to divide by 2 (18).",["Counted integers for −10 < 2x ≤ 8 without dividing by 2. That counts values of 2x, not x.","Included x = −5. The left side is strict (<), so −5 is not allowed: 2(−5) + 3 = −7 is not greater than −7.","Correct: the inequality simplifies to −5 < x ≤ 4, and the integers −4 through 4 are 9 numbers.","Left out x = 4. The right side is ≤, so 4 is included."]],"math-0367":["Add the two equations: (x + y) + (x − y) = 10 + 4, so 2x = 14 and x = 7. Then y = 10 − 7 = 3. Check: 7 − 3 = 4. Traps: giving y instead of x, splitting 10 evenly (5), and using 2y = 6 as if it were x.",["Correct: adding the equations gives 2x = 14, so x = 7.","Subtracted 10 − 4. Subtracting the equations gives 2y = 6, so 6 is 2y, not x.","Split the sum 10 evenly between x and y. But x − y = 4, so x is 4 more than y.","That\'s the value of y. Since x + y = 10, x = 10 − 3 = 7."]],"math-0368":["Add the two equations: 8x + 8y = 56. Divide by 8: x + y = 7. You don\'t even need x and y separately. (If you want them: subtracting gives 2x − 2y = 2, so x − y = 1, which means x = 4 and y = 3. Check: 5(4) + 3(3) = 29 and 3(4) + 5(3) = 27.) Traps: answering with x, with y, or with x − y.",["That\'s x − y. Subtracting the equations gives 2x − 2y = 2. Adding them gives x + y.","That\'s the value of y. The question asks for x + y = 4 + 3.","That\'s the value of x. The question asks for x + y = 4 + 3.","Correct: adding the equations gives 8x + 8y = 56, so x + y = 7."]],"math-0369":["Downstream speed: 36 ÷ 2 = 18 mph. Upstream speed: 36 ÷ 3 = 12 mph. If b is the boat\'s still-water speed and c is the current, then b + c = 18 and b − c = 12. Subtract: 2c = 6, so c = 3 mph (and b = 15). Traps: forgetting to halve the difference (6), and giving the upstream speed (12) or the boat\'s speed (15).",["Correct: b + c = 18 and b − c = 12, so 2c = 6 and c = 3.","That\'s the difference between the downstream and upstream speeds, 18 − 12. That difference is 2c, so halve it.","That\'s the upstream speed, 36 ÷ 3, which is the boat\'s speed minus the current.","That\'s the boat\'s speed in still water, b = (18 + 12) ÷ 2."]],"math-0370":["Multiply each term in the first parentheses by each term in the second: x · x = x², x · (−6) = −6x, 4 · x = 4x, and 4 · (−6) = −24. Combine: x² − 2x − 24. Check with x = 1: (5)(−5) = −25, and 1 − 2 − 24 = −25. Traps: dropping the middle terms, and sign slips on the middle term.",["Sign slip on the middle term: −6x + 4x is −2x, not +2x.","Used −4 instead of +4 for the middle term: −6x − 4x. The middle term is −6x + 4x.","Multiplied only the first terms and the last terms. The middle terms −6x + 4x = −2x are missing.","Correct: x² − 6x + 4x − 24 = x² − 2x − 24."]],"math-0371":["Expand each part. (2x − 3)² = 4x² − 12x + 9. (x + 1)(x − 1) = x² − 1. Subtract the whole second part: 4x² − 12x + 9 − x² + 1 = 3x² − 12x + 10. Check with x = 2: (1)² − (3)(1) = −2, and 12 − 24 + 10 = −2. Traps: squaring term by term, getting the wrong middle term, and not distributing the minus sign.",["Squared each term separately: (2x − 3)² is not 4x² + 9. It has a middle term −12x.","Middle term too small: (2x − 3)² has middle term 2(2x)(−3) = −12x, not −6x.","Subtracted (x² − 1) as x² + 1. The minus sign flips both terms: −(x² − 1) = −x² + 1.","Correct: (4x² − 12x + 9) − (x² − 1) = 3x² − 12x + 10."]],"math-0372":["The extra 3 hours (from 2 to 5) cost 370 − 190 = $180, so the hourly rate is 180 ÷ 3 = $60. The 2-hour job is fee + 2(60) = 190, so the fee is 190 − 120 = $70. Check: 70 + 5(60) = 370. Traps: giving the hourly rate (60), ignoring the fee (95), and subtracting only one hour (130).",["That\'s the hourly rate: (370 − 190) ÷ (5 − 2) = 60. The fixed fee is 190 − 2(60).","Correct: the rate is $60 per hour, so the fee is 190 − 2(60) = 70.","Divided $190 by 2 hours, which treats the whole cost as hourly charges.","Subtracted only 1 hour of work from the 2-hour job: 190 − 60."]],"math-0373":["Similar triangles have proportional sides. The longest sides are 9 and 27, so every side of the larger triangle is 27 ÷ 9 = 3 times as long. The shortest side is 4 × 3 = 12 centimeters. Traps: adding the difference instead of multiplying (22), and scaling the wrong side (18).",["Correct: the scale factor is 27 ÷ 9 = 3, so the shortest side is 4 × 3 = 12.","That\'s the middle side, 6 × 3. The shortest side matches the 4-centimeter side.","Added 27 − 9 = 18 to the side instead of multiplying. Similar figures scale by multiplying.","That\'s the given longest side. The shortest side is smaller."]],"math-0374":["The pole, its shadow, and the sun\'s rays form a right triangle, and the building forms a similar one, because the sun\'s rays hit both at the same angle. So height/shadow is the same: 1.8/1.2 = h/30. Then h = 30 × 1.5 = 45 meters. Traps: flipping the ratio (20), adding instead of multiplying (30.6), and skipping the division by 1.2 (54).",["Inverted the ratio: 30 × 1.2/1.8. The pole is taller than its shadow, so the building is taller than its shadow too.","Added the pole\'s height-minus-shadow difference (0.6 m) to 30. Similar triangles scale by multiplying, not adding.","Correct: height/shadow is 1.8/1.2 = 1.5 for both, so the building is 1.5 × 30 = 45 meters tall.","Multiplied 30 by 1.8 but forgot to divide by the pole\'s shadow, 1.2."]],"math-0375":["For similar solids with length ratio k, areas scale by k² and volumes scale by k³. Here k² = 4, so k = 2, and the volume ratio is k³ = 8. The larger box holds 40 × 8 = 320 cubic centimeters. Traps: using the area ratio for volume (160), treating 4 as the length ratio (2,560), and squaring the area ratio (640).",["Treated 4 as the length ratio and cubed it (4³ = 64). The 4 is the ratio of areas, not lengths.","Squared the area ratio (4² = 16). The length ratio is √4 = 2, and the volume ratio is 2³ = 8.","Correct: the area ratio 4 means a length ratio of 2, so the volume ratio is 2³ = 8 and 40 × 8 = 320.","Multiplied the volume by the area ratio, 4. Volume scales by the cube of the length ratio, which is 2³ = 8."]],"math-0376":["Use V = πr²h with r = 3 and h = 5: V = π × 9 × 5 = 45π ≈ 141.37, so about 141 cubic inches. Traps: leaving out π (45), not squaring the radius (47), and using the curved-surface formula 2πrh (94).",["Left out π: 3² × 5 = 45. The formula is πr²h.","Used r instead of r²: π × 3 × 5. The radius must be squared.","Computed 2πrh, the area of the can\'s curved side, not its volume.","Correct: π × 3² × 5 = 45π ≈ 141.4, which rounds to 141."]],"math-0377":["The rectangle\'s area is 10 × 6 = 60 square feet. The semicircle has diameter 6, so its radius is 3, and its area is half of π(3²): 4.5π ≈ 14.14. Total: 60 + 14.14 ≈ 74.1 square feet. Traps: using the full circle (88.3), using the diameter as the radius (116.5), and adding the arc length instead of the area (69.4).",["Used 6 as the radius. The 6-foot side is the diameter, so the radius is 3.","Added a whole circle of radius 3 (9π). Only half of the circle is attached.","Correct: 10 × 6 + (1/2)π(3²) = 60 + 4.5π ≈ 74.1.","Added the length of the semicircle\'s curved edge (πr = 3π) instead of its area."]],"math-0378":["A cube has 6 equal square faces, so each face has area 150 ÷ 6 = 25 square inches. Each edge is √25 = 5 inches. The volume is 5³ = 125 cubic inches. Traps: stopping at the face area (25), and treating the face area as the edge length (15,625).",["That\'s the area of one face (150 ÷ 6). Take its square root to get the edge, then cube the edge.","Correct: each face has area 150 ÷ 6 = 25, so each edge is 5 inches and the volume is 5³ = 125.","Copied the surface area. Surface area and volume are different measures.","Used 25, the area of one face, as the edge length: 25³. The edge is √25 = 5."]],"math-0379":["The given line has slope 2. A perpendicular line has slope −1/2 (the negative reciprocal). Through (−2, 5): y − 5 = −(1/2)(x + 2), so y = −(1/2)x − 1 + 5 = −(1/2)x + 4. The y-intercept is 4. Check: −(1/2)(−2) + 4 = 5. Traps: using the same slope (9), only the reciprocal (6), or only the opposite (1).",["Used slope 2, which makes a parallel line, not a perpendicular one.","Used slope 1/2. You took the reciprocal but kept the sign; perpendicular slopes have opposite signs.","Correct: the slope is −1/2, so y − 5 = −(1/2)(x + 2), which gives y = −(1/2)x + 4.","Used slope −2: perpendicular slopes are negative reciprocals, so the slope is −1/2, not −2."]],"math-0380":["Use the distance formula: (a − (−2))² + (3 − (−2))² = 13². That\'s (a + 2)² + 25 = 169, so (a + 2)² = 144 and a + 2 = ±12. Since a > 0, a + 2 = 12 and a = 10. Check: the horizontal distance is 12, the vertical distance is 5, and √(144 + 25) = 13. Traps: stopping at a + 2 = 12, a sign slip with −2 (14), and using the 13-unit distance as the horizontal leg (11).",["Correct: (a + 2)² + 5² = 13², so (a + 2)² = 144, a + 2 = 12, and a = 10.","Used 13 as the horizontal distance, so a + 2 = 13. The 13 is the slanted distance; the horizontal leg is √(169 − 25) = 12.","That\'s the horizontal distance, a + 2. Subtract 2 to get a.","Sign slip: subtracting −2 means adding 2, so the horizontal distance is a + 2, not a − 2."]],"math-0381":["In 6 hours the crew paints 3/8 of the fence, so it paints 1/8 every 2 hours. The rest of the fence is 5/8, which takes 5 × 2 = 10 hours. Another way: the whole job takes 6 ÷ (3/8) = 16 hours, and 16 − 6 = 10. Traps: giving the time for the whole fence (16), and multiplying by 3/8 instead of dividing (2.25).",["Multiplied 6 by 3/8. The crew paints 3/8 of the fence in 6 hours, so the whole fence takes 6 ÷ (3/8) = 16 hours.","Assumed the rest takes as long as the first part. But 5/8 of the fence is left, more than the 3/8 already done.","Correct: the whole fence takes 6 ÷ (3/8) = 16 hours, and 16 − 6 = 10 hours remain.","That\'s the time for the whole fence. The crew has already worked 6 of those hours."]],"math-0382":["First find who does not ride a bus: 100% − 35% = 65%, and 0.65 × 240 = 156 students. Then 25% of them ride a bike: 0.25 × 156 = 39 students. Traps: applying 25% to the whole group (60), stopping at the bus riders (84), and stopping at the 156 students who don\'t ride a bus. The key is to read which group each percent is \\"of\\".",["Intermediate result: 156 students don\'t ride a bus. Only 25% of them ride a bike.","That\'s the number of bus riders, 35% of 240.","Took 25% of all 240 students. The 25% is only of the students who don\'t ride a bus.","Correct: 65% of 240 is 156 students who don\'t ride a bus, and 25% of 156 is 39."]],"math-0383":["The rate is 2 mL per 5 seconds, which is 0.4 mL per second. A day has 24 × 60 × 60 = 86,400 seconds. So the faucet drips 0.4 × 86,400 = 34,560 milliliters, which is 34,560 ÷ 1,000 = 34.56 liters. Traps: ignoring the 5 seconds (172.8), dividing by 100 instead of 1,000 when changing to liters (345.6), and multiplying by 5 (864).",["Multiplied by 5 instead of dividing by 5. The rate is 2 mL per 5 seconds, or 0.4 mL per second.","Divided 34,560 mL by 100 instead of 1,000. There are 1,000 milliliters in a liter.","Used 2 mL per second and forgot that it takes 5 seconds to drip 2 mL.","Correct: 2 mL every 5 s is 0.4 mL/s, and 0.4 × 86,400 = 34,560 mL = 34.56 L."]],"math-0384":["The final grade is 0.60(tests) + 0.25(homework) + 0.15(exam). The first two parts give 0.60 × 82 + 0.25 × 94 = 49.2 + 23.5 = 72.7 points. To reach 85, the exam must add 85 − 72.7 = 12.3 points, so 0.15x = 12.3 and x = 82. Check: 49.2 + 23.5 + 12.3 = 85. Traps: an unweighted average (79), mixing up the weights (87, from 86.8), and just aiming for 85 on the exam.",["Took a plain average of the three scores. The weights (60%, 25%, 15%) matter.","Correct: 0.60(82) + 0.25(94) = 72.7, so the exam must add 12.3 points: 12.3 ÷ 0.15 = 82.","Assumed the exam score must match the target grade. Tests and homework already give 72.7 of the 85 points, so the exam needs less than 85.","Swapped the homework and exam weights (15% homework, 25% exam). Check which part counts for which percent."]],"math-0385":["Blue : yellow = 3 : 2. Since 15 cups of blue is 5 times 3, multiply both parts by 5: 15 cups blue to 10 cups yellow. Or solve 3/2 = 15/y, which gives y = 10. Traps: keeping the difference instead of the ratio (14), flipping the ratio (22.5), and giving the total (25).",["Correct: 15 is 3 × 5, so the yellow paint is 2 × 5 = 10 cups.","Kept the difference the same (blue is 1 more than yellow) instead of the ratio. Ratios scale by multiplying.","Flipped the ratio: 15 × 3/2. There is less yellow than blue, so the answer must be less than 15.","That\'s the total amount of paint, 15 + 10. The question asks for the yellow paint only."]],"math-0386":["Average speed is total distance divided by total time. Distance: 24 + 24 = 48 km. Time: 24 ÷ 16 = 1.5 hours, plus 0.5 hour resting, plus 24 ÷ 24 = 1 hour, for 3 hours in all. Average speed: 48 ÷ 3 = 16.0 km/h. Traps: averaging the speeds (20.0), leaving out the rest (19.2), and writing 30 minutes as 0.3 hour (17.1).",["Averaged the two speeds. She spends more time at the slower speed, and the rest also counts.","Left out the rest: 48 ÷ 2.5. The question says to include it.","Wrote 30 minutes as 0.3 hour. Half an hour is 0.5 hour.","Correct: riding takes 1.5 + 1 = 2.5 hours, plus 0.5 hour of rest, so 48 km ÷ 3 hours = 16.0 km/h."]],"math-0387":["A difference of logs with the same base is the log of a quotient: log₃((x + 6)/(x − 2)) = 2. Rewrite in exponential form: (x + 6)/(x − 2) = 3² = 9. Then x + 6 = 9(x − 2) = 9x − 18, so 24 = 8x and x = 3. Check: log₃ 9 − log₃ 1 = 2 − 0 = 2, and both x + 6 and x − 2 are positive. Traps: turning the difference of logs into the log of a difference, and computing 3² as 3 × 2 or as 2³.",["Correct: the equation means log₃((x + 6)/(x − 2)) = 2, so (x + 6)/(x − 2) = 3² = 9, which gives x + 6 = 9x − 18 and x = 3.","Used 2³ = 8 instead of 3² = 9. log₃(N) = 2 means N = 3², with the base raised to the power.","Used 3 × 2 = 6 instead of 3² = 9. log₃(N) = 2 means N is 3 squared, not 3 times 2.","Rewrote the left side as log₃((x + 6) − (x − 2)) = log₃ 8. A difference of logs is the log of a quotient, not of a difference."]],"math-0388":["Use log rules in base 2 first: log₂(a²b) = 2 log₂ a + log₂ b = 2(5) + 3 = 13. Now change to base 4. Since 4 = 2², log₄ N = (log₂ N)/(log₂ 4) = (log₂ N)/2. So log₄(a²b) = 13/2. Check: a = 2⁵ and b = 2³, so a²b = 2¹³ = 4^(13/2). Traps: stopping at 13, multiplying by 2 instead of dividing, and dropping the exponent on a (4).",["Multiplied by 2 to change base. Since 4 = 2², it takes half as many 4s as 2s: divide by 2.","That\'s log₂(a²b). The question asks for the base-4 log, and since 4 = 2², log₄ N = (log₂ N)/2.","Correct: log₂(a²b) = 2(5) + 3 = 13, and a base-4 log is half of a base-2 log, so the value is 13/2.","Left out the exponent 2 on a: (5 + 3) ÷ 2. log₂(a²) is 2 × 5 = 10."]],"math-0389":["7π/6 is π/6 past π, so it\'s in Quadrant III with reference angle π/6. Sine is negative there: sin(7π/6) = −1/2. 5π/3 is π/3 short of 2π, so it\'s in Quadrant IV with reference angle π/3. Cosine is positive there: cos(5π/3) = 1/2. The sum is −1/2 + 1/2 = 0. Traps: wrong signs for the quadrant (−1 or 1), and mixing up sine and cosine values.",["Gave sin(7π/6) a positive sign. The angle 7π/6 is in Quadrant III, where sine is negative.","Correct: sin(7π/6) = −1/2 (Quadrant III) and cos(5π/3) = 1/2 (Quadrant IV), so the sum is 0.","Gave cos(5π/3) a negative sign. The angle 5π/3 is in Quadrant IV, where cosine is positive.","Used sin(5π/3) = −√3/2 for the second term. The question asks for the cosine, and cos(5π/3) = 1/2."]],"math-0390":["The amplitude a is half the distance from the minimum to the maximum: a = (7 − (−1))/2 = 4. The midline c is their average: c = (7 + (−1))/2 = 3. The period of sin(bx) is 2π/b, so 2π/b = 3π and b = 2/3. Then a + b + c = 4 + 2/3 + 3 = 23/3. Traps: using 8 as the amplitude (35/3), confusing the midline with the amplitude (26/3), and flipping the period formula (17/2).",["Used the full distance from maximum to minimum, 8, as the amplitude. The amplitude is half of that, 4.","Used c = 4, the amplitude, for the midline. The midline is halfway between 7 and −1, which is 3.","Flipped the period formula: b = 3π/(2π) = 3/2. The period is 2π/b, so b = 2π/(period) = 2/3.","Correct: a = (7 − (−1))/2 = 4, c = (7 + (−1))/2 = 3, and b = 2π/(3π) = 2/3, so the sum is 4 + 3 + 2/3 = 23/3."]],"math-0391":["Work from the inside out. g(4) = 4² − 2 = 14. Then f⁻¹(14) is the input that makes f give 14: 3x + 4 = 14, so x = 10/3. (In general, f⁻¹(x) = (x − 4)/3.) Check: f(10/3) = 10 + 4 = 14. Traps: stopping at g(4), applying f instead of its inverse (46), and dividing by 3 without first subtracting 4 when undoing f (14/3).",["Used f instead of f⁻¹: f(14) = 3(14) + 4 = 46. The inverse undoes f: subtract 4, then divide by 3.","That\'s g(4). You still need to apply f⁻¹ to it.","Only divided by 3: 14 ÷ 3. The inverse undoes both steps of f: subtract 4, then divide by 3.","Correct: g(4) = 16 − 2 = 14, and f⁻¹(14) solves 3x + 4 = 14, so x = 10/3."]],"math-0392":["The common difference is 4. The 20th term is 7 + (20 − 1)(4) = 83. The sum of an arithmetic sequence is (number of terms) × (first + last)/2 = 20 × 90/2 = 900. Traps: multiplying the last term by 20 (1,660), an off-by-one last term (940), and forgetting to halve (1,800).",["Correct: the 20th term is 7 + 19(4) = 83, and the sum is 20(7 + 83)/2 = 900.","Used 7 + 20(4) = 87 as the 20th term. From the 1st term to the 20th there are only 19 steps of 4.","Multiplied the 20th term by 20, as if every term were 83. Use the average of the first and last terms, (7 + 83)/2 = 45.","Forgot to divide by 2 in the sum formula: the sum is the number of terms times the AVERAGE of the first and last terms."]],"math-0393":["You need an x-value where the input to f is 4, since you know f(4) = −2. That happens when x − 1 = 4, so x = 5. Then y = 3f(4) + 5 = 3(−2) + 5 = −1. The point is (5, −1). Traps: shifting left instead of right (3, −1), skipping the stretch (5, 3), and adding before multiplying (5, 9).",["Left out the vertical stretch: −2 + 5 = 3. The y-value is multiplied by 3 before adding 5.","Added 5 before multiplying by 3: 3(−2 + 5) = 9. The order is multiply by 3, then add 5.","Shifted the wrong way. In f(x − 1), the graph moves 1 unit RIGHT, so x = 4 becomes x = 5.","Correct: at x = 5, y = 3f(4) + 5 = 3(−2) + 5 = −1."]],"math-0394":["The center is the midpoint of the diameter: ((−3 + 5)/2, (4 + (−2))/2) = (1, 1). The diameter\'s length is √((5 − (−3))² + (−2 − 4)²) = √(64 + 36) = 10, so the radius is 5. A circle with center (h, k) and radius r is (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r², so the equation is (x − 1)² + (y − 1)² = 25. Check: (−3 − 1)² + (4 − 1)² = 16 + 9 = 25. Traps: the sign of the center, using the diameter as the radius, and forgetting to square the radius.",["Correct: the center is the midpoint (1, 1), and the radius is half the diameter\'s length of 10, so r² = 25.","Sign slip: a center at (1, 1) gives (x − 1)² + (y − 1)². The signs inside are opposite the center\'s coordinates.","Used the diameter, 10, as the radius. The radius is 5, so r² = 25.","Used r instead of r² on the right side. The radius is 5, so the right side is 5² = 25."]],"math-0395":["A sector with angle θ (in radians) is θ/(2π) of the whole circle, so its area is (θ/(2π))πr² = (1/2)r²θ. Here that\'s (1/2)(36)(5π/6) = 15π square centimeters. Check another way: 5π/6 is 5/12 of a full turn, and 5/12 of 36π is 15π. Traps: giving the arc length (5π), dropping the 1/2 (30π), and using the diameter (60π).",["Used the diameter, 12, as the radius: (1/2)(12²)(5π/6) = 60π.","Left out the 1/2: r²θ = 36 × 5π/6. The sector area formula is (1/2)r²θ.","Correct: (1/2)(6²)(5π/6) = 18 × 5π/6 = 15π.","That\'s the arc length, rθ = 6 × 5π/6. The area of a sector is (1/2)r²θ."]],"math-0396":["Side BC is opposite angle A, so use the law of cosines with the sides next to angle A: BC² = 7² + 10² − 2(7)(10)cos 60° = 49 + 100 − 140(1/2) = 79. So BC = √79 ≈ 8.9. That fits: it\'s between 3 and the right-triangle value √149 ≈ 12.2, as it should be for an angle smaller than 90°. Traps: dropping the cosine, using the Pythagorean theorem, and adding the last term.",["Added the cosine term instead of subtracting it: 149 + 70.","Treated the triangle as a right triangle. Angle A is 60°, so the cosine term must be subtracted.","Correct: BC² = 7² + 10² − 2(7)(10)(1/2) = 149 − 70 = 79.","Left out cos 60°: 49 + 100 − 140 = 9. The last term is 2(7)(10)(1/2) = 70."]],"math-0397":["Side AC is opposite angle B. By the law of sines, AC/sin 75° = BC/sin 40°, so AC = 12 sin 75°/sin 40° ≈ 12(0.9659)/0.6428 ≈ 18.03, or 18.0 inches. Make sure your calculator is in degree mode. Check: the longest side is opposite the largest angle, and 75° is the largest angle here (C = 180° − 40° − 75° = 65°). Traps: flipping the ratio (8.0), using angle C (16.9), and using the angles instead of their sines (22.5).",["Used the angles themselves instead of their sines: 12 × 75/40. The sides are proportional to the sines of the opposite angles.","Correct: AC/sin 75° = 12/sin 40°, so AC = 12 sin 75°/sin 40° ≈ 12(0.9659)/0.6428 ≈ 18.0.","Used angle C (65°) instead of angle B. Side AC is opposite angle B, so use sin 75°. This is side AB.","Flipped the ratio: 12 sin 40°/sin 75°. Side AC is opposite the larger angle, 75°, so it must be longer than 12."]],"math-0398":["Match y = (1/8)(x − 3)² + 2 to y = (1/(4p))(x − h)² + k: h = 3, k = 2, and 1/(4p) = 1/8. So 4p = 8 and p = 2. The focus is (h, k + p) = (3, 4). Traps: giving the vertex (3, 2), setting p equal to 8 instead of solving 4p = 8 (3, 10), and moving sideways instead of up (5, 2).",["Used p = 8. The coefficient 1/8 equals 1/(4p), so 4p = 8 and p = 2.","Added p to the x-coordinate. This parabola opens up, so the focus is directly above the vertex: (h, k + p).","That\'s the vertex (h, k). The focus is p units above the vertex.","Correct: 1/(4p) = 1/8, so 4p = 8 and p = 2. The focus is (3, 2 + 2) = (3, 4)."]],"math-0399":["The longest rod runs from one corner of the box to the opposite corner. The bottom\'s diagonal is √(3² + 4²) = 5. That diagonal and the 12-inch height form a right triangle, so the rod is √(5² + 12²) = 13 inches. In one step: √(3² + 4² + 12²) = √169 = 13. Traps: stopping at the bottom diagonal (5), using only two of the dimensions (√160 ≈ 12.6), and adding the dimensions (19).",["That\'s the diagonal of the 3-by-4 bottom only. The longest rod also rises the full 12 inches.","Used only the 4 and the 12: √(4² + 12²). The longest rod uses all three dimensions.","Correct: the space diagonal is √(3² + 4² + 12²) = √169 = 13.","Added the three dimensions. A diagonal uses the Pythagorean theorem, not a sum."]],"math-0400":["Multiply the top and bottom by the conjugate of the bottom, 1 + i. Top: (2 + 3i)(1 + i) = 2 + 2i + 3i + 3i² = 2 + 5i − 3 = −1 + 5i. Bottom: (1 − i)(1 + i) = 1 − i² = 2. So the result is (−1 + 5i)/2 = −1/2 + (5/2)i. Check: (1 − i)(−1/2 + (5/2)i) = −1/2 + (5/2)i + (1/2)i − (5/2)i² = 2 + 3i. Traps: i² = 1, the wrong conjugate, and not dividing by 2.",["Correct: multiply top and bottom by 1 + i. The top is 2 + 5i + 3i² = −1 + 5i and the bottom is 1 − i² = 2.","Used i² = 1 instead of −1 on top: 2 + 5i + 3 = 5 + 5i.","Multiplied the top by 1 − i instead of the conjugate 1 + i, getting (5 + i)/2. The conjugate of 1 − i is 1 + i, and the top must be multiplied by the same thing as the bottom.","Found the new numerator but forgot to divide by the new denominator, 2."]],"math-0401":["The entry in row 2, column 1 of AB comes from row 2 of A and column 1 of B. Row 2 of A is (3, 4). Column 1 of B is (2, 1). Multiply matching parts and add: 3 × 2 + 4 × 1 = 10. (The full product is AB = [4 −2; 10 −4].) Traps: computing BA instead (−2), using a row of B instead of a column (−1), and multiplying entries one by one (3).",["Multiplied in the wrong order: that\'s the entry of BA. For AB, use row 2 of A with column 1 of B.","Used row 2 of B instead of column 1 of B: 3(1) + 4(−1). Matrix products pair rows of A with columns of B.","Multiplied matching entries only: 3 × 1. Each entry of a product is a sum of products, row times column.","Correct: row 2 of A is (3, 4) and column 1 of B is (2, 1), so the entry is 3(2) + 4(1) = 10."]],"math-0402":["The determinant is ad − bc = k(k − 4) − (3)(4) = k² − 4k − 12. Set it equal to 0: k² − 4k − 12 = 0, which factors as (k − 6)(k + 2) = 0. So k = 6 or k = −2. Check k = −2: (−2)(−6) − 12 = 0. Traps: leaving out the 3 × 4 part (0 and 4), sign slips (2 and −6), and missing a root.",["Correct: k(k − 4) − 12 = 0 gives k² − 4k − 12 = (k − 6)(k + 2) = 0.","Sign slip in factoring: (k − 6)(k + 2) = 0 gives k = 6 and k = −2.","Found only one root of k² − 4k − 12 = 0. The factor (k + 2) gives k = −2 as well.","Set k(k − 4) = 0 and left out the bc part. The determinant is k(k − 4) − 3(4)."]],"math-0403":["First find the vector: 2u = ⟨6, −8⟩, and 2u − v = ⟨6 − 1, −8 − 2⟩ = ⟨5, −10⟩. Its magnitude is √(5² + (−10)²) = √125 = 5√5 ≈ 11.2. Traps: subtracting magnitudes instead of vectors (10 − √5 ≈ 7.8), adding v (√85 ≈ 9.2), and a sign slip in the y-part (√61 ≈ 7.8).",["Combined the magnitudes, 2|u| − |v| = 10 − √5. You have to find the vector 2u − v first, then its magnitude.","Sign slip in the y-part: −8 − 2 is −10, not −6. Subtracting 2 moves further below 0.","Added v instead of subtracting it: 2u + v = ⟨7, −6⟩, which has magnitude √85.","Correct: 2u − v = ⟨6 − 1, −8 − 2⟩ = ⟨5, −10⟩, and √(25 + 100) = √125 = 5√5."]],"math-0404":["For seniors, the probability of having a job is 42/120 = 0.35. For juniors, it\'s 18/80 = 0.225. Divide: 0.35 ÷ 0.225 ≈ 1.56, which rounds to 1.6. Traps: comparing raw counts (42/18 ≈ 2.3) when the classes are different sizes, comparing the class sizes (1.5), and using the wrong condition (42/60 = 0.7, which is the chance a worker is a senior).",["Compared the counts, 42/18. There are more seniors than juniors, so divide each count by its class total first.","Correct: P(job | senior) = 42/120 = 0.35 and P(job | junior) = 18/80 = 0.225, and 0.35/0.225 ≈ 1.6.","Compared the class sizes, 120/80. The question compares the share of each class that has a job.","That\'s the probability that a student with a job is a senior, 42/60. The question compares the job rates of the two classes."]],"math-0405":["Count the choices for each spot and multiply. Letters: 26 for the first, 25 for the second (it must differ), and 24 for the third, so 15,600. Digits: 9 for the first (1 through 9), then 10 and 10, so 900. Total: 15,600 × 900 = 14,040,000. Traps: each wrong choice ignores one or both of the two rules.",["Correct: 26 × 25 × 24 choices for the letters and 9 × 10 × 10 for the digits give 15,600 × 900 = 14,040,000.","Used 10 choices for the first digit. It can\'t be 0, so it has only 9 choices.","Let the letters repeat (26 × 26 × 26). The letters must all be different: 26 × 25 × 24.","Ignored both rules: 26³ × 10³. The letters can\'t repeat, and the first digit can\'t be 0."]],"math-0406":["Multiply each amount by its probability and add. A 6 has probability 1/6, a 4 or 5 has probability 2/6, and a 1, 2, or 3 has probability 3/6. Expected value = (1/6)(10) + (2/6)(2) + (3/6)(−3) = (10 + 4 − 9)/6 = 5/6 ≈ $0.83. Traps: averaging the amounts without weights ($3.00), leaving out the losses ($2.33), and counting the $3 loss for only one face ($1.83).",["Correct: (1/6)(10) + (2/6)(2) + (3/6)(−3) = (10 + 4 − 9)/6 = 5/6 ≈ $0.83.","Counted the $3 loss for only one face. It happens on a 1, 2, OR 3, which is 3 of the 6 faces.","Left out the losses: (10 + 4)/6. Losing $3 on half the rolls counts as −3.","Averaged the three amounts, (10 + 2 − 3)/3. Each amount must be weighted by its probability."]],"math-0407":["Use the complement. The chance that neither bulb is defective is (9/12)(8/11) = 72/132 = 6/11, because after one good bulb is taken, 8 of the remaining 11 are good. So the chance of at least one defective bulb is 1 − 6/11 = 5/11 ≈ 0.45. Check: P(exactly one) = 54/132 and P(both) = 6/132, which add to 60/132 = 5/11. Traps: exactly one instead of at least one (9/22), drawing with replacement (7/16), and both defective instead of at least one (1/22).",["Correct: P(neither defective) = (9/12)(8/11) = 6/11, so P(at least one) = 1 − 6/11 = 5/11.","Treated the draws as with replacement: 1 − (9/12)². After a good bulb is taken, only 8 of the 11 left are good.","That\'s the probability of EXACTLY one defective bulb, 2(3/12)(9/11). \\"At least one\\" also includes both being defective.","That\'s the probability that BOTH bulbs are defective, (3/12)(2/11). \\"At least one\\" also includes exactly one."]],"math-0408":["Infinitely many solutions means the two equations describe the same line, so one is a multiple of the other. The y-coefficients are 6 and 3, so the first equation must be 2 times the second: 2(2x + 3y) = 2b gives 4x + 6y = 2b. Matching terms: a = 4 and 2b = 12, so b = 6. Then a + b = 10. Traps: forgetting b (4), copying 12 as b (16), and scaling the wrong way (28).",["Multiplied 12 by 2 to get b = 24. The first equation is 2 times the second, so b is 12 ÷ 2 = 6.","Set b = 12 by matching the constants directly. The first equation is twice the second, so 12 = 2b and b = 6.","Correct: the first equation must be 2 times the second, so a = 4 and 12 = 2b, giving b = 6 and a + b = 10.","That\'s a alone. For infinitely many solutions, b must also match: b = 6."]],"math-0409":["By the factor theorem, x − 5 is a factor exactly when plugging in x = 5 gives 0. So 125 − 100 + 5k + 10 = 0, which simplifies to 35 + 5k = 0, and k = −7. Check: x³ − 4x² − 7x + 10 = (x − 5)(x² + x − 2). Traps: plugging in −5 instead of 5 (−43), dropping a term (−5), and a sign slip at the end (7).",["Substituted x = −5. If x − 5 is a factor, then x = 5 makes the polynomial 0.","Correct: 5³ − 4(5²) + 5k + 10 = 125 − 100 + 5k + 10 = 35 + 5k = 0, so k = −7.","Left out the constant term 10: 25 + 5k = 0. All four terms count.","Sign slip solving 35 + 5k = 0. Subtracting 35 gives 5k = −35."]],"math-0410":["Use the common denominator (x − 1)(x + 2). Rewrite each fraction: 2(x + 2)/((x − 1)(x + 2)) − 3(x − 1)/((x − 1)(x + 2)). The numerator is 2x + 4 − 3x + 3 = 7 − x. So the result is (7 − x)/((x − 1)(x + 2)). Check with x = 0: 2/(−1) − 3/2 = −3.5, and 7/(−2) = −3.5. Traps: not distributing the minus sign, adding instead of subtracting, and subtracting numerators without a common denominator.",["Correct: 2(x + 2) − 3(x − 1) = 2x + 4 − 3x + 3 = 7 − x, over the common denominator (x − 1)(x + 2).","Didn\'t distribute the minus sign to the −1: −3(x − 1) is −3x + 3, not −3x − 3.","Added the second fraction instead of subtracting it: 2x + 4 + 3x − 3.","Subtracted the numerators 2 − 3 without first rewriting each fraction over the common denominator."]],"math-0411":["|3x − 6| ≥ 9 means 3x − 6 is at least 9 away from 0. So either 3x − 6 ≥ 9, which gives x ≥ 5, or 3x − 6 ≤ −9, which gives 3x ≤ −3 and x ≤ −1. The answer is x ≤ −1 or x ≥ 5. Check: x = 0 gives |−6| = 6, which is less than 9, so 0 is correctly left out. Traps: the \\"between\\" answer (that\'s for ≤), keeping only one case, and splitting up the expression inside the bars.",["That\'s the solution of |3x − 6| ≤ 9. For \\"greater than or equal to,\\" the solutions lie outside the two endpoints.","Solved only 3x − 6 ≥ 9. The expression inside can also be 9 or more below 0: 3x − 6 ≤ −9.","Solved |3x| ≥ 15, as if the absolute value could be split apart. Keep 3x − 6 together inside the bars.","Correct: 3x − 6 ≥ 9 gives x ≥ 5, and 3x − 6 ≤ −9 gives x ≤ −1."]],"math-0412":["The first 5 GB are included, so the extra data is 8.3 − 5 = 3.3 GB. Because any part of a GB is charged as a full GB, that\'s 4 extra charges: 4 × $8 = $32. Total: $30 + $32 = $62.00. Traps: charging for a fraction of a GB ($56.40), rounding down ($54.00), and leaving out the $30 monthly fee ($32.00).",["Correct: 8.3 − 5 = 3.3 extra GB rounds up to 4, so the charge is 30 + 4(8) = $62.00.","Charged for exactly 3.3 extra GB. The plan charges for any part of a GB as a whole GB.","Charged for only 3 extra GB. The extra 0.3 GB counts as a 4th GB, since any part of a GB is charged.","That\'s only the charge for the 4 extra GB. Add the $30 monthly fee."]],"math-0413":["Gas car: 12,000 miles ÷ 32 miles per gallon = 375 gallons, and 375 × $3.60 = $1,350. Electric car: 12,000 miles × 0.30 kWh per mile = 3,600 kWh, and 3,600 × $0.15 = $540. The difference is $1,350 − $540 = $810. Traps: stopping at one car\'s cost, and adding the costs instead of subtracting.",["Added the two costs. \\"How much less\\" asks for the difference.","That\'s the gas car\'s cost. Subtract the electric car\'s $540.","Correct: the gas car costs (12,000 ÷ 32) × $3.60 = $1,350 and the electric car costs 12,000 × 0.30 × $0.15 = $540, a difference of $810.","That\'s the electric car\'s cost, 12,000 × 0.30 × $0.15. The question asks for the difference."]],"math-0414":["The solution starts with 0.30 × 12 = 3.6 liters of acid. After adding a liters of pure acid, there are 3.6 + a liters of acid in 12 + a liters in all. Set that equal to 40%: 3.6 + a = 0.40(12 + a) = 4.8 + 0.40a. So 0.60a = 1.2 and a = 2. Check: 5.6 liters of acid in 14 liters is 40%. Traps: forgetting that the total grows (1.2), answering a different question (remove 3 liters of water), and scaling by the change in percent (4).",["Scaled 12 liters by (40 − 30)/30. The percents don\'t scale the volume that way.","Found how much WATER to remove (12 − 3.6/0.40 = 3). The question asks how much acid to add.","Correct: 3.6 + a = 0.40(12 + a) gives 0.60a = 1.2, so a = 2.","Added acid equal to 10% of the original 12 liters. Adding acid also adds volume, so 40% must be taken of the NEW total."]],"math-0415":["The machine fills 30 bottles every minute, so in 15 minutes it fills 30 × 15 = 450 bottles. Traps: adding instead of multiplying (45), and dividing either way (0.5 or 2).",["Divided 15 by 30. The machine fills 30 bottles EACH minute, so multiply.","Divided 30 by 15. The machine fills 30 bottles EACH minute, so multiply.","Added 30 and 15. The machine fills 30 bottles in each of the 15 minutes, so multiply.","Correct: 30 bottles per minute × 15 minutes = 450 bottles."]],"math-0417":["Put the numbers in order: 6, 9, 14, 17, 21. With 5 numbers, the median is the 3rd one: 14. Traps: not sorting first (21), finding the mean (13.4), and finding the range (15).",["That\'s the mean: 67 ÷ 5. The median is the middle number after sorting.","Correct: in order the numbers are 6, 9, 14, 17, 21, and the middle one is 14.","That\'s the range, 21 − 6. The median is the middle value.","Took the middle of the list as written. Put the numbers in order first."]],"math-0418":["Each inch on the map stands for 25 miles, so 3.5 inches stands for 3.5 × 25 = 87.5 miles. Traps: dividing (7.1), adding (28.5), and leaving out the half inch (75).",["Correct: 3.5 × 25 = 87.5 miles.","Used only the 3 whole inches: 3 × 25 = 75. The extra half inch adds another 12.5 miles.","Added 25 and 3.5. Each inch stands for 25 miles, so multiply.","Divided 25 by 3.5. Each inch stands for 25 miles, so multiply."]],"math-0419":["The area of a rectangle is length × width: 8 × 5 = 40 square feet. Traps: adding (13), finding the perimeter (26), and halving the product as if the rug were a triangle (20).",["Added the length and width. Area is length times width.","Took half of 8 × 5, as for a triangle. A rectangle\'s area is the full length times width.","That\'s the perimeter, 2(8) + 2(5). Area is length times width.","Correct: 8 × 5 = 40 square feet."]],"math-0420":["Pay per hour = total pay ÷ hours: $96 ÷ 8 = $12. Check: 8 × 12 = 96. Traps: subtracting, adding, or multiplying instead of dividing.",["Multiplied 96 by 8. That would be the pay for 8 days of $96, not the hourly rate.","Added 8 to 96. To find the pay for each hour, divide.","Subtracted 8 from 96. To find the pay for each hour, divide.","Correct: $96 ÷ 8 hours = $12 per hour."]],"math-0421":["Add 7 to both sides: 4x = 28. Divide by 4: x = 7. Check: 4(7) − 7 = 21. Traps: subtracting 7 (3.5), ignoring the 7 (5.25), and stopping at 4x = 28.",["That\'s 4x. Divide by 4 to get x.","Correct: add 7 to get 4x = 28, then divide by 4.","Divided 21 by 4 and forgot about the −7.","Subtracted 7 instead of adding it: 4x = 14. To undo −7, add 7 to both sides."]],"math-0422":["To undo dividing by 3, multiply both sides by 3. Since 3 is positive, the inequality sign stays the same: x ≥ 12. Check: 12/3 = 4. Traps: flipping the sign, adding 3, and dividing by 3 again.",["Correct: multiply both sides by 3 to get x ≥ 12.","Flipped the inequality sign. You flip it only when multiplying or dividing by a negative number.","Added 3 to both sides. To undo dividing by 3, multiply by 3.","Divided 4 by 3. To undo dividing by 3, multiply by 3."]],"math-0423":["Angles that form a straight line are supplementary: they add to 180°. So the other angle is 180° − 47° = 133°. Traps: using 90° (43°), using 360° (313°), and repeating the given angle.",["Subtracted from 360°. That\'s for angles all the way around a point; a straight line is 180°.","Correct: angles that form a straight line add to 180°, and 180° − 47° = 133°.","Copied the given angle. The two angles are equal only if each is 90°.","That\'s the complement, 90° − 47°. Angles that form a straight line add to 180°."]],"math-0424":["The area of a square is side × side: 9 × 9 = 81 square inches. Traps: doubling the side (18), finding the perimeter (36), and repeating the side length (9).",["Correct: 9 × 9 = 81 square inches.","That\'s the perimeter, 4 × 9. Area is side × side.","Doubled the side, 9 × 2. Area is side × side, 9 × 9.","That\'s the side length. The area is the side length times itself."]],"math-0425":["The circumference is π times the diameter: C = π × 10 = 10π centimeters (about 31.4). Traps: using the radius (5π), and finding an area-style answer with a square (25π or 100π).",["Squared the diameter: π × 10². The circumference uses the diameter itself, not its square.","That\'s the area, πr² = π × 5². Circumference is the distance around.","Correct: circumference = π × diameter = π × 10 = 10π.","Used the radius, 5, in place of the diameter. The circumference is π × 10."]],"math-0426":["The volume of a cube is edge³: 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 cubic centimeters. Traps: stopping at one face\'s area (9), finding the surface area (54), and multiplying by 2 instead of cubing (6).",["Multiplied 3 by 2. The volume of a cube is edge × edge × edge.","That\'s 3 × 3, the area of one face. Multiply by 3 once more for volume.","Correct: 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 cubic centimeters.","That\'s the surface area, 6 faces × 9. Volume is 3 × 3 × 3."]],"math-0427":["Count the complement: 3-digit numbers with NO 7. The hundreds digit can be 1–9 except 7 (8 choices), and the tens and ones digits can be 0–9 except 7 (9 choices each). That\'s 8 × 9 × 9 = 648. There are 900 three-digit numbers (100 through 999), so 900 − 648 = 252 have at least one 7. Traps: adding the cases for each place, which counts numbers with two or three 7s more than once (280); giving the hundreds digit 9 choices (171); and starting from 1,000 numbers (271).",["Added 100 + 90 + 90 for a 7 in each place. That counts numbers like 777 and 707 more than once.","Subtracted from 1,000 instead of 900. The 3-digit numbers run from 100 to 999, which is 900 numbers.","Correct: there are 900 three-digit numbers, and 8 × 9 × 9 = 648 of them have no 7, so 900 − 648 = 252 have at least one.","Used 9 × 9 × 9 for the numbers with no 7. The first digit can\'t be 0 or 7, so it has only 8 choices: 900 − 8 × 9 × 9 = 252."]],"math-0428":["Use the complement. All three choose different numbers in 5 × 4 × 3 = 60 of the 5³ = 125 equally likely outcomes, so P(all different) = 60/125 = 12/25. Then P(at least two the same) = 1 − 12/25 = 13/25. Check by counting: exactly two match in 3 × 5 × 4 = 60 ways and all three match in 5 ways, and 65/125 = 13/25. Traps: forgetting to subtract from 1 (12/25), counting only the all-same case (1/25), and adding the three pair chances (3/5), which overlaps.",["Added 1/5 for each of the 3 pairs. That counts the case where all three match three times.","Correct: P(all different) = (5 × 4 × 3)/5³ = 60/125 = 12/25, so P(at least two match) = 1 − 12/25 = 13/25.","That\'s the probability that all three numbers are DIFFERENT, (5 × 4 × 3)/125. The question asks for the complement.","That\'s the probability that all three choose the same number, 5/125. \\"At least two\\" also includes exactly two matching."]],"math-0429":["The game takes 1 toss with probability 1/2 (heads first), 2 tosses with probability 1/4 (TH), and 3 tosses with probability 1/8 (TTH). It takes 4 tosses whenever the first three are tails, whatever the 4th toss shows, so that probability is 1/8. These add to 1. Expected value = 1(1/2) + 2(1/4) + 3(1/8) + 4(1/8) = 4/8 + 4/8 + 3/8 + 4/8 = 15/8. Traps: using 1/16 for the 4th toss (13/8), ignoring the limit (2), and treating the four outcomes as equally likely (5/2).",["Averaged 1, 2, 3, and 4 as if each were equally likely. Stopping early is much more likely than going to 4 tosses.","That\'s the expected number of tosses with no 4-toss limit. The limit cuts off the long runs of tails.","Correct: P(1) = 1/2, P(2) = 1/4, P(3) = 1/8, and P(4) = 1/8, so 1(1/2) + 2(1/4) + 3(1/8) + 4(1/8) = 15/8.","Gave the 4th toss a probability of 1/16, as if it only happens when it lands heads. The 4th toss happens whenever the first 3 are tails: probability 1/8."]],"math-0431":["The powers of i repeat every 4: i, −1, −i, 1. So the sum is i − 2 − 3i + 4 + 5i − 6 − 7i + 8. Real parts: −2 + 4 − 6 + 8 = 4. Imaginary parts: 1 − 3 + 5 − 7 = −4, so −4i. The total is 4 − 4i. Traps: assuming everything cancels (0), stopping after one cycle (2 − 2i), and using i³ = i (4 + 16i).",["Used i³ = i. Since i² = −1, i³ = i² · i = −i.","Assumed the powers of i cancel in every group of 4. They would without the coefficients, but here each term has a different coefficient.","Stopped after the first 4 terms. The next 4 terms add another 2 − 2i.","Correct: the first four terms give i − 2 − 3i + 4 = 2 − 2i, and the next four give 5i − 6 − 7i + 8 = 2 − 2i, for 4 − 4i."]],"math-0432":["Factor: n² + n = n(n + 1), a product of two consecutive integers. One of any two consecutive integers is even, so the product is always divisible by 2. It is divisible by 3 only when n or n + 1 is a multiple of 3. That fails when n is 1 more than a multiple of 3: n = 1, 4, 7, …, 100, which is 34 numbers. So 100 − 34 = 66 values work. Traps: counting only multiples of 3 (33) or of 6 (16), and counting the values that fail (34) instead of the ones that work.",["Counted only multiples of 6. n² + n = n(n + 1) is divisible by 6 for many other n, such as n = 2 (2 × 3 = 6).","Counted only multiples of 3. n(n + 1) is also divisible by 3 when n + 1 is a multiple of 3, such as n = 2 or n = 5.","That\'s the number of n that FAIL: the 34 values 1 more than a multiple of 3 (1, 4, 7, …, 100). Subtract them from 100.","Correct: n(n + 1) is always even, so it is divisible by 6 exactly when n or n + 1 is a multiple of 3. That rules out only the 34 values of n that are 1 more than a multiple of 3."]],"math-0433":["Expand: (a + bi)² = a² − b² + 2abi. Match parts with −5 + 12i: a² − b² = −5 and 2ab = 12, so b = 6/a. Substitute: a² − 36/a² = −5, so a⁴ + 5a² − 36 = 0, which factors as (a² + 9)(a² − 4) = 0. Since a is real, a² = 4, and a > 0 gives a = 2. Then b = 6/2 = 3. Check: (2 + 3i)² = 4 + 12i + 9i² = −5 + 12i. Traps: swapping a and b (2), taking the other root (−3), and giving the modulus (√13).",["That\'s |a + bi|, since |a + bi|² = |−5 + 12i| = 13. The question asks for the imaginary part b.","Correct: a² − b² = −5 and 2ab = 12. With a > 0 this gives a = 2 and b = 3, and (2 + 3i)² = 4 − 9 + 12i.","Swapped a and b. (3 + 2i)² = 9 − 4 + 12i = 5 + 12i, which has the wrong real part.","That goes with a = −2, the other square root of −5 + 12i. Since a > 0, a = 2 and b = 3."]],"math-0434":["For a 2 × 2 matrix [a b; c d], the inverse is (1/(ad − bc))[d −b; −c a]. Here ad − bc = 12 − 10 = 2, so M⁻¹ = (1/2)[4 −2; −5 3] = [2 −1; −2.5 1.5]. The entry in the first row, second column is −1. Check: the first row of M times the second column of M⁻¹ is 3(−1) + 2(1.5) = 0, as it should be for the identity. Traps: not dividing by the determinant (−2), putting −c in the wrong place (−5/2), and multiplying by the determinant instead of dividing (−4).",["Correct: the determinant is 3(4) − 2(5) = 2, and M⁻¹ = (1/2)[4 −2; −5 3], so the entry is −2/2 = −1.","Swapped a and d and changed the signs of b and c, but forgot to divide by the determinant, 3(4) − 2(5) = 2.","That\'s the entry in the second row, first column, −5 ÷ 2. The first row, second column holds −2 ÷ 2 = −1.","Multiplied by the determinant, 2, instead of dividing by it: 2 × (−2) = −4."]],"math-0435":["A quadratic has 2 distinct real solutions when its discriminant is positive: 4² − 4(k)(k) = 16 − 4k² > 0, so k² < 4 and −2 < k < 2. But the equation is only quadratic when k ≠ 0. At k = 0 it becomes 4x = 0, with just one solution, x = 0. So the answer is −2 < k < 2 with k ≠ 0. Traps: missing the k ≠ 0 case, flipping the inequality, and dropping the negative values.",["Flipped the inequality: 16 − 4k² > 0 means k² < 4, so k is BETWEEN −2 and 2.","Took only the positive square root of k² < 4. Negative k such as k = −1 also work: −x² + 4x − 1 = 0 has 2 real solutions.","Forgot that k = 0 makes the equation 4x = 0, which is linear and has only 1 solution.","Correct: the discriminant 16 − 4k² must be positive, so −2 < k < 2, and k ≠ 0 so that the equation stays quadratic."]],"math-0436":["Multiply both sides by (x − 2)(x + 1): x(x + 1) + 2(x − 2) = 6. That\'s x² + x + 2x − 4 = 6, so x² + 3x − 10 = 0, which factors as (x + 5)(x − 2) = 0. The candidates are x = −5 and x = 2. But x = 2 makes the denominators x − 2 zero, so it is extraneous. Check x = −5: −5/−7 + 2/−4 = 5/7 − 1/2 = 3/14, and 6/((−7)(−4)) = 6/28 = 3/14. Traps: keeping the extraneous root, sign slips in factoring, and throwing out both roots.",["Correct: clearing denominators gives x² + 3x − 10 = 0, so x = −5 or x = 2. But x = 2 makes a denominator 0, so only −5 works.","Kept x = 2. It makes x − 2 = 0, so the original equation is undefined there.","Sign slip in factoring: x² + 3x − 10 = (x + 5)(x − 2), so the candidates are −5 and 2.","Rejected both candidates. Only x = 2 and x = −1 make a denominator 0, and x = −5 checks in the original equation."]],"math-0437":["A fraction is negative when its top and bottom have opposite signs, and it is 0 when its top is 0. Here x − 3 ≤ 0 and x + 2 > 0 gives −2 < x ≤ 3. (The other sign pattern, x − 3 ≥ 0 and x + 2 < 0, is impossible.) The integers are −1, 0, 1, 2, and 3: five of them. Check x = −3: (−6)/(−1) = 6, which is positive, so it fails. Traps: multiplying through by x + 2 without knowing its sign (infinitely many), including x = −2 where the fraction is undefined (6), and excluding x = 3 (4).",["Left out x = 3. The inequality allows equality, and (3 − 3)/(3 + 2) = 0.","Correct: the fraction is negative or 0 when −2 < x ≤ 3, which holds for the integers −1, 0, 1, 2, and 3.","Included x = −2. That makes the denominator 0, so the fraction is undefined there.","Multiplied both sides by x + 2 and got x ≤ 3. That only works when x + 2 is positive; for x < −2 the fraction is positive."]],"math-0438":["Plug in the known zeros. x = 1: 1 + a + b − 6 = 0, so a + b = 5. x = −2: −8 + 4a − 2b − 6 = 0, so 2a − b = 7. Adding gives 3a = 12, so a = 4 and b = 1. The polynomial is x³ + 4x² + x − 6 = (x − 1)(x + 2)(x + 3), so the third zero is −3. Shortcut: the constant term −6 equals −(product of zeros), so the zeros multiply to 6, and 1 × (−2) × r = 6 gives r = −3. Traps: a sign slip in that shortcut (3), and answering with a (4) or the product (6).",["That\'s the product of all three zeros. Divide it by 1 × (−2) to get the third zero.","That\'s the value of a. Solving for a = 4 and b = 1 is a step along the way, not the zero.","Sign slip on the product of the zeros. For a cubic x³ + ax² + bx + c with leading coefficient 1, the product of the zeros is −c = 6, not −6.","Correct: the product of the three zeros of x³ + ax² + bx − 6 is 6, so 1(−2)(r) = 6 and r = −3."]],"math-0439":["Let t = |x|, so the equation is t² − 4t + k = 0. Each positive t gives two values of x (±t), t = 0 gives one, and negative t gives none. For exactly 4 solutions, t² − 4t + k = 0 needs two different positive roots. The roots add to 4 (positive) and multiply to k, so we need k > 0 and a positive discriminant, 16 − 4k > 0, so k < 4. The integers are k = 1, 2, 3. Check k = 3: t = 1 or 3, so x = ±1, ±3. Traps: k = 0 gives only 3 solutions, k = 4 gives 2, and negative k gives 2.",["Correct: with t = |x|, the equation t² − 4t + k = 0 needs 2 different POSITIVE solutions for t, which happens for 0 < k < 4: k = 1, 2, or 3.","Included k = 0. Then t = 0 or t = 4, which gives x = 0 and x = ±4: only 3 solutions, because t = 0 gives just one x.","Included both k = 0 and k = 4. At k = 4, t = 2 is a double root, giving only x = ±2.","Checked only that the discriminant 16 − 4k is positive (k < 4). For k < 0, one value of t is negative and can\'t equal |x|, leaving just 2 solutions."]],"math-0440":["Change base: log₄(x + 6) = log₂(x + 6)/log₂ 4 = (1/2)log₂(x + 6). Multiply both sides by 2: 2 log₂ x = log₂(x + 6), so log₂(x²) = log₂(x + 6) and x² = x + 6. Then x² − x − 6 = (x − 3)(x + 2) = 0, so x = 3 or x = −2. But log₂ x needs x > 0, so only x = 3 works. Check: log₂ 3 ≈ 1.585 and log₄ 9 = log₂ 3. Traps: keeping −2, writing 2 log₂ x as log₂(2x) (6), and ignoring the different bases (no solution).",["Correct: log₄(x + 6) = (1/2)log₂(x + 6), so 2 log₂ x = log₂(x + 6), x² = x + 6, and x = 3 (x = −2 is not in the domain).","Turned 2 log₂ x into log₂(2x) instead of log₂(x²), giving 2x = x + 6.","Kept x = −2. Log₂ x is defined only for x > 0, so −2 is extraneous.","Set x = x + 6 as if the bases were the same. The base-4 log must be changed to base 2 first."]],"math-0441":["tan θ = −5/12 with θ in Quadrant II means the reference triangle has legs 5 and 12, and its hypotenuse is √(25 + 144) = 13. In Quadrant II sine is positive, so sin θ = 5/13 (and cos θ = −12/13). Adding π moves to the opposite point on the unit circle, so sin(θ + π) = −sin θ = −5/13. Traps: stopping at sin θ (5/13), and using the 12 from the cosine (±12/13).",["Used the cosine\'s size, 12/13. The sine of θ comes from the side opposite θ, 5.","Correct: in Quadrant II, sin θ = 5/13, and adding π flips the sign: sin(θ + π) = −sin θ = −5/13.","That\'s sin θ. Adding π turns the angle halfway around the circle, which flips the sign of the sine.","Used 12/13, which is the size of cos θ, and kept it positive. The sine uses 5/13, and sin(θ + π) = −sin θ."]],"math-0442":["Let u = 2x. The smallest positive u with sin u = −√3/2 is 4π/3 (in Quadrant III, reference angle π/3). Then x = u/2 = 2π/3. Check: sin(4π/3) = −√3/2, and no smaller positive u has this sine, because sine is positive on (0, π) and −√3/2 is first reached at 4π/3. Traps: forgetting to divide by 2 (4π/3), the wrong sign (π/6), and using the second solution (5π/6).",["That\'s the value of 2x. Divide by 2 to get x.","That\'s from 2x = 5π/3, the second angle in [0, 2π) with sine −√3/2. The first one, 4π/3, gives a smaller x.","Correct: the first positive angle with sine −√3/2 is 4π/3, so 2x = 4π/3 and x = 2π/3.","Solved sin(2x) = +√3/2. The sine must be negative, which first happens past π."]],"math-0443":["The nth term is 5 + 4(n − 1) = 4n + 1, so the sum of the first n terms is n(5 + 4n + 1)/2 = n(2n + 3). Set n(2n + 3) > 500. Try n = 15: 15 × 33 = 495, not enough. n = 16: 16 × 35 = 560, which works. So n = 16. Traps: stopping at the last n below 500 (15), using a term instead of a sum (125), and dropping the 1/2 (11).",["Left out the 1/2 in the sum formula: n(a₁ + aₙ)/2. Without it, the sums double and 500 is passed too early.","The first 15 terms add to only 15(5 + 61)/2 = 495, just under 500. One more term is needed.","Correct: the sum of the first n terms is n(2n + 3), which is 495 for n = 15 and 560 for n = 16.","Found when a single TERM passes 500 (5 + 4(124) = 501). The question asks about the sum of the terms."]],"math-0444":["A radius drawn to a point of tangency is perpendicular to the tangent line, so triangles OAP and OBP have right angles at A and B. Each has hypotenuse OP = 10 and leg OA = OB = 6 (the radius), so the other leg is √(100 − 36) = 8. Each triangle\'s area is (1/2)(6)(8) = 24, and the quadrilateral is the two together: 48. Traps: skipping the 1/2 (96), treating the shape as a rectangle (80), and treating OP and the radius as the legs (60).",["Left out the 1/2 for each triangle: 2 × 6 × 8.","Multiplied OP = 10 by the tangent length 8, as if OAPB were a rectangle. It is two right triangles with legs 6 and 8.","Multiplied OP = 10 by the radius 6. The right angles are at A and B, so the triangles\' legs are 6 and 8.","Correct: OA = 6 is perpendicular to the tangent PA, so PA = √(10² − 6²) = 8. The two right triangles each have area (1/2)(6)(8) = 24, for 48 in all."]],"math-0445":["When the curved surface is flattened, the sector\'s radius is the cone\'s slant height, √(6² + 8²) = 10 inches. The sector\'s arc wraps around the base, so its length is the base circumference, 2π(6) = 12π. A full circle of radius 10 has circumference 20π, so the sector is 12π/20π = 3/5 of a circle, and 3/5 × 360° = 216°. Traps: using the height instead of the slant height (270°), using the height in the ratio (288°), and giving the leftover angle (144°).",["That\'s 360° − 216°, the gap that\'s left of the full circle, not the angle of the sector itself.","Correct: the sector\'s radius is the slant height, √(6² + 8²) = 10, and its arc is the base\'s circumference, 12π. So the angle is 360° × 12π/(20π) = 216°.","Used the height, 8, as the sector\'s radius: 360° × 6/8. The sector\'s radius is the slant height, 10.","Used 8/10 instead of 6/10. The arc of the sector is the base circle, whose radius is 6."]],"math-0446":["Group and complete the squares: 9(x² − 4x) + 4(y² + 2y) = 104. Add 9 × 4 = 36 and 4 × 1 = 4 to both sides: 9(x − 2)² + 4(y + 1)² = 144. Divide by 144: (x − 2)²/16 + (y + 1)²/36 = 1. The larger denominator, 36, is under the y-term, so the semi-major axis is √36 = 6 and the major axis is 12 long. Check: the points (2, 5) and (2, −7), 12 apart, satisfy the original equation. Traps: forgetting to balance the right side (2√26), choosing the wrong axis (8), and giving the semi-axis (6).",["Correct: completing the squares gives 9(x − 2)² + 4(y + 1)² = 144, or (x − 2)²/16 + (y + 1)²/36 = 1, so the major axis is 2√36 = 12.","Completed the squares without adding the same amounts to the right side. 9(x − 2)² adds 36 and 4(y + 1)² adds 4, so the right side is 104 + 36 + 4 = 144.","Used the x-direction: √16 = 4, doubled. The larger denominator, 36, is under the y-term, so the major axis is vertical.","That\'s the semi-major axis, √36. The full major axis is twice that, 12."]],"math-0447":["The altitude to the hypotenuse splits triangle ABC into two triangles similar to it, ACD and CBD. From their similarity, AD/CD = CD/DB, so CD² = 4 × 9 = 36 and CD = 6. Now use AB as the base and CD as the height: AB = 4 + 9 = 13, so the area is (1/2)(13)(6) = 39. Check with the legs: AC = √(16 + 36) = √52 and BC = √(81 + 36) = √117, and (1/2)√52 × √117 = (1/2)√6,084 = (1/2)(78) = 39. Traps: treating 4 and 9 as legs (18), finding only one small triangle (27), and forgetting the 1/2 (78).",["Multiplied 4 × 9 and halved, as if AD and DB were the legs. They are pieces of the hypotenuse.","That\'s the area of the smaller triangle BCD, (1/2)(9)(6). Triangle ABC uses the whole hypotenuse, 13.","Correct: CD² = AD × DB = 36, so CD = 6, and the area is (1/2)(AB)(CD) = (1/2)(13)(6) = 39.","Left out the 1/2: 13 × 6. The area of a triangle is half of base times height."]],"math-0448":["Each diagonal splits the parallelogram into two triangles with sides 6 and 10. The longer diagonal lies across from a 120° angle. By the law of cosines, d² = 6² + 10² − 2(6)(10)cos 120° = 36 + 100 − 120(−1/2) = 196, so d = 14. (The other diagonal, across from 60°, has d² = 136 − 60 = 76.) Traps: picking the shorter diagonal (√76), assuming a right angle (√136), and adding the sides (16).",["Added the sides. A diagonal is shorter than the two sides put end to end.","Correct: the longer diagonal is across from a 120° angle, so d² = 6² + 10² − 2(6)(10)cos 120° = 136 + 60 = 196.","Used the Pythagorean theorem as if the angle were 90°. The 120° angle makes the opposite diagonal longer than that.","That\'s the SHORTER diagonal, which lies across from the 60° angles: 36 + 100 − 2(6)(10)(1/2) = 76."]],"math-0449":["Work in tanks per hour. With everything open: 1/6 + 1/9 − 1/12 = 6/36 + 4/36 − 3/36 = 7/36. In 2 hours that\'s 14/36 of the tank, leaving 22/36. With Pipe A off: 1/9 − 1/12 = 1/36 per hour, so the rest takes 22 hours. Total: 2 + 22 = 24 hours. Traps: ignoring the shut-off (36/7), ignoring the drain afterward (15/2), and forgetting the first 2 hours (22).",["Correct: together they fill 1/6 + 1/9 − 1/12 = 7/36 per hour, so 14/36 in 2 hours. The other 22/36 fills at 1/9 − 1/12 = 1/36 per hour, taking 22 hours, for 24 in all.","That\'s the time AFTER Pipe A is shut off. Add the first 2 hours.","Forgot the drain after Pipe A is shut off: (11/18) ÷ (1/9) = 5.5 more hours. The drain is still removing water.","That\'s the time with all three open the whole way. Pipe A is shut off after 2 hours."]],"math-0450":["Let m be the number of men and w the number of women. The first mean gives 40m + 32w = 35(m + w), so 5m = 3w. After 6 women aged 30 join, the total age is 40m + 32w + 180, and 40m + 32w + 180 = 34(m + w + 6). That simplifies to 6m − 2w = 24. Substitute w = 5m/3: 6m − 10m/3 = 24, so 8m/3 = 24 and m = 9. Then w = 15, and the club had 9 + 15 = 24 members. Check: 40(9) + 32(15) = 840 = 35 × 24, and 840 + 180 = 1,020 = 34 × 30. Traps: answering with one group (9 or 15), and counting the new members (30). Shortcut: only the totals matter. With N members, 35N + 6(30) = 34(N + 6), so N = 204 − 180 = 24.",["That\'s the number of men. The club also had 15 women.","That\'s the number of women. The club also had 9 men.","Correct: 40m + 32w = 35(m + w) gives 5m = 3w, and 40m + 32w + 180 = 34(m + w + 6) gives 6m − 2w = 24. So m = 9, w = 15, and there were 24 members.","That\'s the total AFTER the 6 new women joined. The question asks for the number before."]],"math-0451":["The depth is (r/R)², so r/R = √0.0004 = 0.02. The star\'s radius is 0.5 Sun radii, so r = 0.02 × 0.5 = 0.01 Sun radii. Each Sun radius is about 109 Earth radii, so r ≈ 0.01 × 109 ≈ 1.09 Earth radii, close to Earth\'s size. Traps: skipping the square root (0.02), ignoring the star\'s size (2.18), and dividing by the star\'s size (4.36).",["Divided by 0.5 instead of multiplying. r = 0.02 × R, and R is 0.5 Sun radii.","Left out the star\'s size: 0.02 × 109. The star is only half as wide as the Sun, so the planet is half as big as that.","Correct: r/R = √0.0004 = 0.02, so r = 0.02 × 0.5 = 0.01 Sun radii, and 0.01 × 109 ≈ 1.09 Earth radii.","Used the depth without taking its square root: 0.0004 × 0.5 × 109 ≈ 0.02. The depth is (r/R)², so r/R = √0.0004."]],"math-0452":["From T² ∝ r³, the ratio of the radii is (T₂/T₁)^(2/3) = (27/8)^(2/3) = (3/2)² = 9/4. So the outer planet is 9/4 times as far from the star. Starlight per square meter is proportional to 1/r², so the inner planet receives (9/4)² = 81/16 ≈ 5.1 times as much. Traps: stopping at the radius ratio (9/4), treating the period ratio as the radius ratio (27/8), and squaring the period ratio (729/64).",["That\'s the ratio of the orbit radii, (27/8)^(2/3). The starlight depends on the SQUARE of the distance, so square it.","Used the ratio of the periods as the ratio of the distances. The radii follow from T² ∝ r³, so r ∝ T^(2/3).","Correct: r ∝ T^(2/3), so the outer orbit is (27/8)^(2/3) = 9/4 times as wide. Starlight ∝ 1/r², so the inner planet gets (9/4)² = 81/16 times as much.","Squared the period ratio, (27/8)². The distances first need T² ∝ r³: the radius ratio is 9/4, then square that."]],"math-0453":["The group is every candidate that is narrowband or unexplained. Narrowband: 240. Unexplained: 8. The 4 that are both are in both counts, so the group has 240 + 8 − 4 = 244 candidates. Of these, 4 are both narrowband and unexplained, so the probability is 4/244 = 1/61. Traps: double-counting the overlap (1/62), and using only one of the two groups (1/60 or 1/2).",["Used 240 + 8 = 248 for the group. The 4 candidates that are both narrowband and unexplained were counted twice; the group has 244 candidates.","Correct: the group has 240 + 8 − 4 = 244 candidates, and 4 of them are both, so the probability is 4/244 = 1/61.","Used only the 240 narrowband candidates. The group also includes the 4 broadband unexplained candidates.","Used only the 8 unexplained candidates as the group. The group is every candidate that is narrowband OR unexplained."]],"math-0454":["Use the complement. The number of ways to choose 3 of 12 stars is C(12, 3) = 220. The choices with no star known to have planets use only the other 8 stars: C(8, 3) = 56. So 220 − 56 = 164 choices include at least one. Check by cases: exactly 1 is 4 × 28 = 112, exactly 2 is 6 × 8 = 48, and exactly 3 is 4, and 112 + 48 + 4 = 164. Traps: counting only exactly 1 (112), forgetting to subtract (220), and counting in order (984).",["That counts choices with EXACTLY 1 star with planets, 4 × C(8, 2). \\"At least 1\\" also includes choices with 2 or 3 of them.","Correct: there are C(12, 3) = 220 choices in all, and C(8, 3) = 56 of them have no star with planets, so 220 − 56 = 164.","That\'s every possible choice of 3 stars, C(12, 3). Subtract the 56 choices that use none of the 4 stars with planets.","Counted ordered lists: 12 × 11 × 10 − 8 × 7 × 6. The order of the 3 stars doesn\'t matter."]],"math-0455":["During the transit, the light is 99% of normal, so F₂ = 0.99F₁ and F₁/F₂ = 1/0.99. The magnitude increases by 2.5 log₁₀(1/0.99) ≈ 2.5 × 0.004365 ≈ 0.0109, which is 0.011 to the nearest thousandth. Traps: the wrong sign (−0.011), dropping the 2.5 (0.004), and replacing the log with the percent (0.025).",["Sign slip: used log₁₀(0.99) instead of log₁₀(1/0.99). The star gets dimmer, so its magnitude goes UP.","Left out the factor 2.5: log₁₀(1/0.99) ≈ 0.0044.","Correct: F₁/F₂ = 1/0.99, and 2.5 log₁₀(1/0.99) ≈ 2.5 × 0.00436 ≈ 0.011.","Multiplied 2.5 by 0.01, treating the log of the ratio as the 1% itself. The log of 1/0.99 is about 0.0044, not 0.01."]],"math-0456":["N is a product, so each change multiplies N. Doubling L multiplies N by 2. Changing f_p from 0.5 to 0.4 multiplies N by 0.4/0.5 = 0.8. Together N is multiplied by 2 × 0.8 = 1.6, so it increases by 60%. Traps: adding or subtracting the percent changes (80% or 90%), and dividing by 0.8 (150%).",["Correct: N is multiplied by 2 × (0.4/0.5) = 2 × 0.8 = 1.6, which is a 60% increase.","Subtracted 20% from 100%. Percent changes in a product multiply: 2 × 0.8 = 1.6, not 2 − 0.2.","Treated the drop from 0.5 to 0.4 as 10%. It is a 0.1 drop out of 0.5, which is 20%.","Divided by 0.8 instead of multiplying: 2 ÷ 0.8 = 2.5. A smaller f_p makes N smaller, so multiply by 0.8."]],"math-0458":["The ocean is the space between two spheres, with radii 1,540 and 1,460 kilometers. The (4/3)π cancels in the ratio, so the fraction is (1,540³ − 1,460³)/1,560³ ≈ (3,652,264,000 − 3,112,136,000)/3,796,416,000 ≈ 0.142, or about 14%. That is far more than the layer\'s share of the radius (about 5%), because shells far from the center hold a lot of volume. Traps: comparing thicknesses (5%), using areas (10%), and cubing the thickness ratio (0.01%).",["Cubed the ratio of the thickness to the radius, (80/1,560)³. The ocean is a shell, so subtract two spheres\' volumes.","Divided the ocean\'s thickness by the radius, 80/1,560. Volume grows with the cube of the radius, so the outer layers hold much more volume.","Used squares instead of cubes: (1,540² − 1,460²)/1,560². Volume depends on r³.","Correct: (1,540³ − 1,460³)/1,560³ ≈ 0.142, or about 14%."]],"math-0459":["Sending time per day: 2 passes × 8 minutes × 60 seconds = 960 seconds. Data sent: 960 × 2 = 1,920 megabits. Convert to megabytes: 1,920 ÷ 8 = 240 megabytes. Pictures: 240 ÷ 4 = 60. Traps: counting one pass (30), stopping at megabytes (240), and skipping the bits-to-bytes step (480).",["Forgot to change megabits to megabytes: 1,920 ÷ 4. There are 8 bits in a byte, so 1,920 megabits is 240 megabytes.","That\'s the total data in megabytes. Each picture is 4 megabytes, so divide by 4.","Correct: 2 × 8 × 60 = 960 seconds, times 2 megabits per second = 1,920 megabits = 240 megabytes, and 240 ÷ 4 = 60 pictures.","Counted only one pass. There are 2 passes each day, so the sending time is 16 minutes."]],"math-0460":["The probability of a lined-up orbit is R/a ≈ 696,000/150,000,000 ≈ 0.00464, about 1 in 215. The expected number among 150,000 planets is 150,000 × 0.00464 ≈ 696, closest to 700. This is one reason transit surveys watch so many stars at once. Traps: squaring the ratio (3), a decimal slip (70), and using the wrong pair of radii (1,400).",["Squared the ratio R/a, as in a transit depth. The chance of a lined-up orbit is R/a itself, about 1/215.","Decimal slip: 696,000/150,000,000 is about 0.0046, not 0.00046.","Correct: R/a ≈ 696,000/150,000,000 ≈ 0.0046, and 150,000 × 0.0046 ≈ 700.","Used 1/109, the ratio of Earth\'s radius to the Sun\'s. The probability uses the Sun\'s radius over the orbit\'s radius."]],"math-0461":["Depth ∝ (planet radius)², so the radius ratio is √4 = 2. For the orbits, T² ∝ r³, so r ∝ T^(2/3), and the orbit-radius ratio is 8^(2/3) = (∛8)² = 2² = 4. Check: 4³ = 64 = 8². Traps: treating either ratio as if it were the answer directly, and inverting the exponent (8^(3/2)).",["Used the depth ratio as the radius ratio. Depth ∝ radius², so the radius ratio is √4 = 2.","Used the period ratio as the orbit-radius ratio. T² ∝ r³ gives r ∝ T^(2/3) = 8^(2/3) = 4.","Flipped the exponent: 8^(3/2) ≈ 22.6. Solving T² ∝ r³ for r gives r ∝ T^(2/3).","Correct: radius ∝ √(depth) = √4 = 2, and orbit radius ∝ period^(2/3) = 8^(2/3) = 4."]],"math-0462":["The probability is the ring\'s area divided by the disk\'s area. Ring: π(1.5)² − π(0.9)² = π(2.25 − 0.81) = 1.44π. Disk: π(2)² = 4π. Probability: 1.44π/4π = 0.36. Traps: subtracting the distances (0.60), forgetting to remove the inner disk (0.56), and dividing by the wrong disk (0.64).",["Correct: the ring\'s area is π(1.5² − 0.9²) = 1.44π, and the disk\'s area is π(2²) = 4π, so the probability is 1.44/4 = 0.36.","Left out the hole in the middle: π(1.5²)/π(2²) = 0.5625. Subtract the disk inside 0.9 AU.","Subtracted the radii, 1.5 − 0.9 = 0.6, and treated that as the probability.","Divided the ring\'s area by the area within 1.5 AU (1.44π/2.25π). The point is chosen from the whole disk within 2 AU."]],"math-0463":["Let k be the number of telescopes that detect the signal. P(k = 4) = 0.7⁴ = 0.2401. P(confirmed) = P(k ≥ 2) = 1 − P(k = 0) − P(k = 1) = 1 − 0.3⁴ − 4(0.7)(0.3)³ = 1 − 0.0081 − 0.0756 = 0.9163. Since k = 4 is part of being confirmed, P(k = 4 | confirmed) = 0.2401/0.9163 ≈ 0.262, or 0.26. Traps: ignoring the condition (0.24, which conditioning on \\"at least 1\\" also nearly gives), and answering for exactly 3 (0.41 or 0.45).",["That\'s 0.7⁴, the probability that all 4 detect it with no condition. Divide by the probability that it is confirmed, about 0.92.","Correct: P(all 4) = 0.7⁴ = 0.2401, and P(at least 2) = 1 − 0.3⁴ − 4(0.7)(0.3)³ = 0.9163, so 0.2401/0.9163 ≈ 0.26.","That\'s the probability that EXACTLY 3 detect it, 4(0.7)³(0.3), with no condition. The question asks about all 4.","That\'s the probability that exactly 3 detect it, given confirmation. The question asks about all 4."]],"math-0464":["Place star C first: it can go in positions 2, 3, 4, or 5, so 4 choices. The other 5 stars fill the remaining 5 spots in 5! = 120 ways, for 4 × 120 = 480 orders. In exactly half of them, A comes before B, since swapping A and B pairs up the orders and doesn\'t move C. So there are 480 ÷ 2 = 240 orders. Traps: ignoring both rules (720), and using only one rule (360 or 480).",["That\'s 6!, every order of the 6 stars. Both rules cut the number down.","Used only the rule about C: 4 × 5! = 480. Half of those have B before A.","Used only the rule about A and B: 720 ÷ 2. Star C also can\'t be first or last.","Correct: C has 4 allowed positions, the other 5 stars fill the rest in 5! = 120 ways, and A comes before B in exactly half of those: 4 × 120 ÷ 2 = 240."]],"math-0465":["For the survey to end on night 5, night 5 must be clear and be the 3rd clear night, so exactly 2 of nights 1–4 are clear. The number of ways to pick those 2 nights is C(4, 2) = 6. Each such order has probability (0.6)²(0.4)² × 0.6 = 0.03456. Total: 6 × 0.03456 ≈ 0.207, or 0.21. Traps: one order only (0.03), allowing the 3rd clear night to come before night 5 (0.35), and \\"by night 5\\" instead of \\"on night 5\\" (0.68).",["Found the probability of just one order, such as clear-clear-cloudy-cloudy-clear: 0.6³ × 0.4². There are 6 such orders.","Correct: the 5th night must be clear and exactly 2 of the first 4 must be clear: C(4, 2)(0.6)²(0.4)² × 0.6 ≈ 0.207.","Found the probability of exactly 3 clear nights among the 5, C(5, 3)(0.6)³(0.4)². That includes orders where the 3rd clear night comes before night 5.","Found the probability of at least 3 clear nights in 5, which is the chance the survey ends BY night 5, not ON night 5."]],"math-0466":["P transits on days that are multiples of 6. Q transits on days 2, 12, 22, …, 92. Check Q\'s days for multiples of 6: 12 works, 42 works, and 72 works. The pattern repeats every lcm(6, 10) = 30 days, so the next one would be day 102, past day 100. That\'s 3 days. Traps: assuming a shared start (4), deciding they never match (0), and counting every one of Q\'s transits (10).",["Counted all 10 of Q\'s transits (days 2, 12, …, 92). A shared day must also be a multiple of 6, since P transits only on those days.","Assumed both started on day 0, which gives days 0, 30, 60, and 90. Planet Q starts on day 2, so the shared days are 12, 42, and 72.","Correct: the shared days are 12, 42, and 72. After day 12 they repeat every 30 days, the least common multiple of 6 and 10, and day 102 is past day 100.","Decided the planets can never transit on the same day because they started on different days. P transits on days 0, 6, 12, … and Q on days 2, 12, …, so day 12 works."]],"math-0467":["Two events are independent when the chance of one is the same whether or not the other happens. Here that means the share of narrowband signals that repeated must equal the share of broadband signals that repeated, 10/50 = 0.2. So x/(x + 20) = 0.2, which gives x = 0.2x + 4, 0.8x = 4, and x = 5. Check with the full definition: the total is 75, P(narrowband) = 25/75 = 1/3, P(repeated) = 15/75 = 1/5, and P(both) = 5/75 = 1/15 = (1/3)(1/5). Traps: the wrong denominator (4), matching counts (10), and matching totals (30).",["Set x/20 = 0.2, using the other narrowband cell as the narrowband total. The narrowband total is x + 20.","Correct: independence means narrowband signals repeat at the same rate as broadband ones, 10/50 = 0.2. So x/(x + 20) = 0.2, and x = 5.","Made the two repeat counts equal. Independence compares rates within each row, not raw counts: 10 of 50 is 0.2, so the narrowband row also needs 0.2.","Made the two row totals equal (x + 20 = 50). Independence is about rates, not equal group sizes."]],"math-0468":["At the same speed, time is proportional to the length of the path. The first path is a diameter, 2R. The second path is 3.6/6 = 0.6 as long, so it is 1.2R long, with half-length 0.6R. The radius to the end of the chord, the half-chord, and the segment from the center to the chord form a right triangle: d² + (0.6R)² = R². So d² = 0.64R², and d = 0.8R. Traps: giving the length ratio (0.6), subtracting instead of using the Pythagorean theorem (0.4), and stopping at d² (0.64).",["Subtracted 0.6 from 1. The chord\'s half-length and its distance from the center make a right triangle with the radius, so use the Pythagorean theorem.","That\'s the ratio of the path lengths, 3.6/6. The question asks for the path\'s distance from the center.","Found 1 − 0.6² = 0.64, which is the SQUARE of the distance as a fraction of the radius. Take its square root: √0.64 = 0.8.","Correct: at the same speed, the chord is 3.6/6 = 0.6 of the diameter, so its half-length is 0.6R. Then the distance d satisfies d² + (0.6R)² = R², giving d = 0.8R."]],"math-0469":["A polynomial with real coefficients has nonreal zeros in conjugate pairs, so 2 + i is a zero too. Those two zeros give the factor (x − (2 − i))(x − (2 + i)) = x² − 4x + 5. Call the third zero r, so p(x) = (x² − 4x + 5)(x − r). The constant term is 5 · (−r) = −5r, and it must be 20, so r = −4. Multiply: (x² − 4x + 5)(x + 4) = x³ + 4x² − 4x² − 16x + 5x + 20 = x³ − 11x + 20. So c = −11 (and b = 0). Traps: forgetting the third zero\'s effect (5), a sign slip in the sum of products (11), or getting r = +4 from the product of the zeros (21).",["Correct: 2 + i is also a zero, and the third zero r satisfies 5r = −20, so r = −4. Then p(x) = (x² − 4x + 5)(x + 4) = x³ − 11x + 20.","Used only the quadratic factor x² − 4x + 5. The third zero, −4, also changes the x term: c = 5 + (−4)(4) = −11.","Sign slip: for x³ + bx² + cx + d, c equals the sum of the products of the zeros two at a time, with no minus sign. That sum is −11.","Took the third zero to be +4. The product of the three zeros is −20 (not 20), so 5r = −20 and r = −4; with r = 4 the constant term would be −20."]],"math-0470":["Let v = ⟨a, b⟩, so a² + b² = 25. Then u + v = ⟨3 + a, 4 + b⟩, and |u + v|² = (3 + a)² + (4 + b)² = 9 + 16 + (a² + b²) + 2(3a + 4b) = 50 + 2(3a + 4b). Setting this equal to 8² = 64 gives 3a + 4b = 7. Now u − v = ⟨3 − a, 4 − b⟩, so |u − v|² = 50 − 2(3a + 4b) = 50 − 14 = 36, and |u − v| = 6. (One such v is ⟨−3, 4⟩: u + v = ⟨0, 8⟩ and u − v = ⟨6, 0⟩.) Traps: assuming |u − v| = |u + v| (8), stopping at √14, or subtracting the magnitudes (2).",["Assumes u − v has the same magnitude as u + v. That is true only when u and v are perpendicular, and here they are not.","Correct: with v = ⟨a, b⟩, |u + v|² = 25 + 25 + 2(3a + 4b) = 64, so 3a + 4b = 7. Then |u − v|² = 50 − 2(7) = 36, so |u − v| = 6.","That\'s √(2(3a + 4b)) = √14, an intermediate result. Subtract 14 from 50 and then take the square root.","Treats magnitudes like plain numbers: (5 + 5) − 8 = 2. Magnitudes of vectors don\'t add or subtract that way."]],"math-0471":["Square both: z² = (a² − b²) + 2abi and (z̄)² = (a² − b²) − 2abi. Their sum is 2(a² − b²), so 2(a² − b²) = −14 and a² − b² = −7. Also |z| = 5 means a² + b² = 25. Adding the two equations gives 2a² = 18, so a² = 9 and b² = 16. Since a and b are positive, a = 3, b = 4, and z = 3 + 4i. Then z² = 9 − 16 + 24i = −7 + 24i. Traps: squaring the conjugate (−7 − 24i), swapping a and b (7 + 24i), or dropping the 2 in 2ab (−7 + 12i).",["That\'s (z̄)² = (3 − 4i)². Because b is positive, z = 3 + 4i, and the imaginary part of z², 2ab, is positive.","Swaps a and b: (4 + 3i)² = 7 + 24i. From 2(a² − b²) = −14, a² − b² = −7, so b is the larger part: a = 3 and b = 4.","Forgets the 2 in the middle term: (a + bi)² = a² − b² + 2abi, so the imaginary part is 2(3)(4) = 24, not 12.","Correct: z² + (z̄)² = 2(a² − b²) = −14, so a² − b² = −7. With a² + b² = 25, a² = 9 and b² = 16, so z = 3 + 4i and z² = −7 + 24i."]],"math-0472":["Don\'t multiply both sides by x − 1: it can be negative. Instead, move everything to one side. (x² − 4)/(x − 1) − (x + 1) = [x² − 4 − (x + 1)(x − 1)]/(x − 1) = [x² − 4 − x² + 1]/(x − 1) = −3/(x − 1). So the inequality becomes −3/(x − 1) ≥ 0. The numerator is negative, so the fraction is positive exactly when x − 1 < 0, that is, x < 1 (it can never equal 0). The integers from −10 through 0 work: 11 of them. Check x = 0: (−4)/(−1) = 4 ≥ 1. True. Traps: multiplying through by x − 1 (0), flipping the sign condition (9), or counting x = 1 (12).",["Counts x = 1, too. At x = 1 the left side has a denominator of 0, so it is undefined there.","Correct: subtracting x + 1 leaves −3/(x − 1) ≥ 0, which is true exactly when x − 1 < 0. The integers −10 through 0 make 11 values.","Gets the sign backward: −3/(x − 1) is positive when x − 1 is negative, so x < 1, not x > 1.","Multiplies both sides by x − 1 without thinking about its sign, gets −4 ≥ −1, and decides nothing works. When x − 1 is negative, the inequality sign must flip."]],"math-0473":["Isolate one radical: √(2x + 6) = 1 + √(x + 4). Square both sides: 2x + 6 = 1 + 2√(x + 4) + x + 4, so x + 1 = 2√(x + 4). Square again: x² + 2x + 1 = 4x + 16, so x² − 2x − 15 = 0 and (x − 5)(x + 3) = 0. Squaring can add false solutions, so check both. x = 5: √16 − √9 = 4 − 3 = 1. It works. x = −3: √0 − √1 = −1, not 1. (Also, x + 1 = 2√(x + 4) needs x + 1 ≥ 0, which rules out −3.) The only solution is 5, so the sum is 5. Traps: keeping both roots (2), dropping the middle term when squaring (−1), or keeping the wrong root (−3).",["Correct: squaring twice gives x² − 2x − 15 = 0, so x = 5 or x = −3. Only x = 5 checks: √16 − √9 = 1. At x = −3, √0 − √1 = −1.","Adds both roots of x² − 2x − 15 = 0. The root −3 is extraneous: it solves √(x + 4) − √(2x + 6) = 1, not the given equation.","Squares each side as if (√(x + 4) + 1)² were x + 4 + 1, dropping the middle term 2√(x + 4). That gives x = −1, which doesn\'t check.","Keeps only the extraneous root. At x = −3 the left side is 0 − 1 = −1, not 1."]],"math-0474":["Two things to handle. First, the domain: x − 1 > 0 and 5 − x > 0, so 1 < x < 5. Second, the direction: with base 1/2, the logarithm gets smaller as its input gets bigger (log_(1/2) 1 = 0, log_(1/2) 2 = −1, log_(1/2) 4 = −2). So log_(1/2)(A) > log_(1/2)(B) means A < B: x − 1 < 5 − x, so x < 3. Combine with the domain: 1 < x < 3. Check x = 2: log_(1/2)(1) = 0, and log_(1/2)(3) is negative, so 0 > log_(1/2)(3). True. Traps: not flipping (3 < x < 5 or x > 3) and skipping the domain (x < 3).",["Correct: base 1/2 is less than 1, so the log is decreasing and the inequality flips: x − 1 < 5 − x, so x < 3. Both logs must be defined, so 1 < x < 5. Together: 1 < x < 3.","Keeps the inequality direction (x − 1 > 5 − x). With a base between 0 and 1, a bigger log means a smaller input, so the sign must flip.","Flips the inequality correctly but forgets that x − 1 must be positive. For x ≤ 1, log_(1/2)(x − 1) is undefined.","Keeps the inequality direction and also skips the domain. The log with base 1/2 is decreasing, and 5 − x must be positive."]],"math-0475":["Two cases. If k − 1 = 0, the equation isn\'t a quadratic at all: k = 1 gives 4x + 1 = 0, which has exactly one solution, x = −1/4. If k ≠ 1, the equation is a quadratic with a = k − 1, b = 4, c = 1. It has exactly one real solution when the discriminant is 0: 16 − 4(k − 1) = 0, so k − 1 = 4 and k = 5. Check: 4x² + 4x + 1 = (2x + 1)², a double root at x = −1/2. So the values are 1 and 5. The trap is using the discriminant alone and missing the case where the x² term vanishes.",["Finds the double-root case but misses k = 1. Then the x² term disappears and 4x + 1 = 0 has exactly one solution, x = −1/4.","Sign slip in the discriminant: b² − 4ac = 16 − 4(k − 1), and setting it to 0 gives k = 5, not −3.","Catches the k = 1 case but makes a sign slip in the discriminant. 16 − 4(k − 1) = 0 gives k − 1 = 4, so k = 5.","Correct: if k = 1, the equation is 4x + 1 = 0, with one solution. Otherwise it\'s a quadratic, and one solution needs 16 − 4(k − 1) = 0, so k = 5."]],"math-0476":["Factor first. 2x² + 3x − 5 = (2x + 5)(x − 1) and x² − 1 = (x + 1)(x − 1). So f(x) = (2x + 5)/(x + 1) everywhere except x = 1, where the original denominator is 0 and the graph has a hole. Now set f(x) = 2: (2x + 5)/(x + 1) = 2 gives 2x + 5 = 2x + 2, so 5 = 2. No x works. If you skip the factoring and solve 2x² + 3x − 5 = 2x² − 2, you get x = 1, but that\'s exactly where f is undefined, so it\'s an extraneous solution. The graph never meets y = 2. (A graph CAN cross its horizontal asymptote in general; this one just doesn\'t.)",["That\'s the x-intercept, where f(x) = 0, not where f(x) = 2.","x = −1 is the vertical asymptote. The function isn\'t defined there, so the graph has no point at x = −1.","Solving 2x² + 3x − 5 = 2(x² − 1) does give x = 1, but f is undefined at x = 1 (the denominator is 0). The graph has a hole there, not a point.","Correct: f(x) = (2x + 5)(x − 1)/[(x + 1)(x − 1)] = (2x + 5)/(x + 1) for x ≠ 1. Setting (2x + 5)/(x + 1) = 2 gives 5 = 2, which is impossible."]],"math-0477":["Write 2x − 6 = 2(x − 3). Track a point (p, q) on the graph of f. On g, the input must satisfy 2x − 6 = p, so x = p/2 + 3, and the output is 1 − q. Choice D does exactly this: halve the x-coordinate (p/2), add 3 (p/2 + 3), negate the y-coordinate (−q), then add 1 (1 − q). Order matters. Choice A shifts 6 after the shrink, which lands at p/2 + 6. Choice C shifts 3 before the shrink, which lands at (p + 3)/2. Choice B gets the x-coordinate right ((p + 6)/2 = p/2 + 3) but shifts up before reflecting, so the y-coordinate becomes −(q + 1) = −q − 1 instead of 1 − q.",["After the shrink, a shift right 6 gives f(2(x − 6)) = f(2x − 12). Factor first: 2x − 6 = 2(x − 3), so the shift after the shrink is 3 units.","The horizontal part works (shift right 6, then shrink, gives f(2x − 6)), but the vertical steps are in the wrong order: shifting up 1 and then reflecting gives −f(2x − 6) − 1.","Shifting right 3 and then shrinking gives f(2x − 3). If you shift first, the shift has to be 6 units, because the shrink halves it.","Correct: the shrink gives f(2x), the shift right 3 gives f(2(x − 3)) = f(2x − 6), the reflection gives −f(2x − 6), and the shift up gives 1 − f(2x − 6)."]],"math-0478":["Find f⁻¹ first. If y = 2ˣ − 3, then 2ˣ = y + 3 and x = log₂(y + 3), so f⁻¹(x) = log₂(x + 3), defined for x > −3. For h(x) = f⁻¹(g(x)) to exist, two things must hold. First, g(x) must exist: x + 5 > 0, so x > −5. Second, g(x) must be a legal input for f⁻¹: log₂(x + 5) > −3, which means x + 5 > 2⁻³ = 1/8, so x > −5 + 1/8 = −39/8. The second condition is stricter, so the domain is x > −39/8. Traps: stopping at g\'s domain (x > −5), applying f⁻¹\'s domain to x (x > −3), or a sign slip in the inverse (x > 3).",["That\'s only the domain of g. The output g(x) must also be in the domain of f⁻¹, which is all inputs greater than −3.","Correct: f⁻¹(x) = log₂(x + 3), so h(x) = log₂(log₂(x + 5) + 3). This needs x + 5 > 0 and log₂(x + 5) > −3, that is, x + 5 > 1/8, so x > −39/8.","Applies f⁻¹\'s domain (inputs > −3) to x itself instead of to g(x). The condition is g(x) > −3.","Inverts f with the wrong sign, f⁻¹(x) = log₂(x − 3). Solving y = 2ˣ − 3 for x gives x = log₂(y + 3), so f⁻¹(x) = log₂(x + 3)."]],"math-0479":["Let y = f(x) and first solve f(y) = −3. On y < 3: y² − 4y = −3 gives y = 1 or y = 3; only y = 1 fits y < 3. On y ≥ 3: 2y − 9 = −3 gives y = 3, which fits. So f(x) = 1 or f(x) = 3. Solve f(x) = 1. On x < 3: x² − 4x − 1 = 0 gives x = 2 ± √5; only 2 − √5 ≈ −0.24 is less than 3. On x ≥ 3: 2x − 9 = 1 gives x = 5. Solve f(x) = 3. On x < 3: x² − 4x − 3 = 0 gives x = 2 ± √7; only 2 − √7 ≈ −0.65 fits. On x ≥ 3: 2x − 9 = 3 gives x = 6. Total: 4 solutions. Every solution must be checked against the piece it came from; skipping that gives 6, and missing y = 3 gives 2.",["Solves every equation with both pieces and never checks that each solution fits its piece\'s domain. Several of those solutions are on the wrong side of x = 3.","Correct: f(y) = −3 only for y = 1 and y = 3. Then f(x) = 1 gives x = 2 − √5 and x = 5, and f(x) = 3 gives x = 2 − √7 and x = 6. That\'s 4 solutions.","Finds f(y) = −3 only at y = 1 and then solves f(x) = 1 without checking domains. The value y = 3 also works (2(3) − 9 = −3), and 2 + √5 is not less than 3.","Misses y = 3, so solves only f(x) = 1, which has 2 solutions. The linear piece gives f(3) = 2(3) − 9 = −3, so f(x) = 3 must be solved too."]],"math-0480":["Complete the square: (x² − 6x + 9) + (y² + 4y + 4) = 3 + 9 + 4, so (x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 16. The center is (3, −2) and r = 4. The distance from the center to 3x + 4y − 11 = 0 is |3(3) + 4(−2) − 11|/√(3² + 4²) = |−10|/5 = 2. Draw radii to the two points where the line meets the circle. The perpendicular from the center to the chord splits the central angle in half, and each half has cosine 2/4 = 1/2, so each half is π/3 and the central angle is 2π/3. The shorter arc is rθ = 4(2π/3) = 8π/3. Traps: using the half-angle (4π/3), giving the angle instead of the length (2π/3), or finding the longer arc (16π/3).",["That\'s the longer arc: its central angle is 2π − 2π/3 = 4π/3, and 4(4π/3) = 16π/3.","Correct: the circle is (x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 16, so r = 4. The center is |9 − 8 − 11|/5 = 2 from the line, so half the central angle has cosine 2/4, giving π/3. The arc is 4(2π/3) = 8π/3.","Uses the half-angle π/3 as the whole central angle. The angle with cosine 2/4 is half of the angle that cuts off the arc.","That\'s the central angle, 2π/3, in radians. Multiply by the radius, 4, to get the arc length."]],"math-0481":["Let the radius be r and the central angle θ (in radians). The perimeter is two radii plus the arc: 2r + rθ = 20, so rθ = 20 − 2r. The area is (1/2)r²θ = (1/2)r(rθ) = (1/2)r(20 − 2r) = 10r − r². Set it equal to 16: r² − 10r + 16 = 0, so (r − 2)(r − 8) = 0. If r = 2, the arc is 16 and θ = 8 radians. That\'s more than a full circle (2π ≈ 6.28), so no sector has these measures. If r = 8, the arc is 4 and θ = 4/8 = 1/2. Check: area = (1/2)(64)(1/2) = 16. The answer is 1/2. Traps: taking the other root (8), giving the arc length (4), or giving a radius (2).",["Correct: with radius r and angle θ, 2r + rθ = 20 and (1/2)r²θ = 16 lead to r² − 10r + 16 = 0, so r = 2 or 8. r = 2 needs θ = 8 > 2π, impossible, so r = 8 and θ = 4/8 = 1/2.","That\'s a possible radius from r² − 10r + 16 = 0, not an angle, and r = 2 doesn\'t lead to a real sector anyway.","That\'s the arc length when r = 8: 20 − 2(8) = 4 centimeters. The angle is the arc length divided by the radius: 4/8.","Comes from r = 2: the arc would be 16 and θ = 16/2 = 8. But a central angle can\'t be more than 2π ≈ 6.28 radians, so this sector can\'t exist."]],"math-0482":["BC is opposite ∠A, so the law of cosines gives 7² = 8² + AC² − 2(8)(AC)cos 60°, that is, 49 = 64 + AC² − 8·AC. So AC² − 8·AC + 15 = 0 and AC = 3 or AC = 5. Both make real triangles, so use the extra fact. With AC = 3, the sides are 3, 7, 8, and 8² = 64 > 3² + 7² = 58, so the angle opposite the 8 is obtuse. With AC = 5, the sides are 5, 7, 8, and 64 < 25 + 49 = 74, so that triangle is acute. So AC = 3, and the area is (1/2)(AB)(AC) sin A = (1/2)(8)(3)(√3/2) = 6√3. Traps: the acute triangle (10√3), dropping the 1/2 (12√3), or using BC as a side of ∠A (14√3).",["Correct: 49 = 64 + AC² − 8·AC gives AC = 3 or AC = 5. Only AC = 3 makes an obtuse triangle (8² > 3² + 7²), so the area is (1/2)(8)(3)(sin 60°) = 6√3.","Uses AC = 5. That triangle (sides 5, 7, 8) is acute, because 8² = 64 is less than 5² + 7² = 74.","Uses AC = 3 but leaves out the 1/2 in (1/2)(AB)(AC) sin A.","Uses BC = 7 as one of the sides that form ∠A. The sides that form ∠A are AB and AC; BC is opposite ∠A."]],"math-0483":["Since DE ∥ BC, triangle ADE is similar to triangle ABC with ratio AD/AB = 2/5. Areas scale by the square of the ratio, so area ADE = (2/5)² × 75 = 12. Because DE ∥ BC, E divides AC in the same ratio: AE:EC = 2:3, so EC/AC = 3/5. Since EF ∥ AB, triangle EFC is similar to triangle ABC with ratio 3/5, so area EFC = (3/5)² × 75 = 27. Quadrilateral DEFB has both pairs of opposite sides parallel, so it\'s a parallelogram, and it fills the rest of triangle ABC: 75 − 12 − 27 = 36. Traps: giving one small triangle (12 or 27), or their sum (39) instead of what\'s left.",["That\'s the area of triangle ADE: (2/5)² × 75 = 12.","That\'s the area of triangle EFC: (3/5)² × 75 = 27.","Correct: triangle ADE has area (2/5)²(75) = 12 and triangle EFC has area (3/5)²(75) = 27. The parallelogram DEFB is what\'s left: 75 − 12 − 27 = 36.","That\'s the two small triangles together, 12 + 27. The question asks for the quadrilateral, which is the rest of the triangle."]],"math-0484":["The base has area 6² = 36, so the 4 triangular faces have total area 96 − 36 = 60, or 15 each. Each face is a triangle with base 6, so (1/2)(6)(l) = 15, and the slant height is l = 5. The slant height runs from the apex to the midpoint of a base edge. The pyramid\'s height, that midpoint, and the center of the base form a right triangle, with legs h and 3 (half of 6) and hypotenuse 5. So h = √(25 − 9) = 4. The volume is (1/3)(36)(4) = 48. Traps: using the slant height as the height (60), forgetting to remove the base (12√55), or leaving out the 1/3 (144).",["Leaves out the 1/3: 36 × 4 = 144.","Doesn\'t subtract the base area. Then each triangle seems to have area 96/4 = 24, the slant height becomes 8, and the height becomes √55.","Uses the slant height of a face, 5, as the pyramid\'s height. The height is a leg of a right triangle with the slant height as hypotenuse: √(5² − 3²) = 4.","Correct: the 4 triangular faces have area 96 − 36 = 60, so each is 15 = (1/2)(6)(slant height), giving slant height 5. The height is √(5² − 3²) = 4, and the volume is (1/3)(36)(4) = 48."]],"math-0486":["Conditional probability: P(first blue | exactly 2 red) = P(first blue and exactly 2 red) ÷ P(exactly 2 red). The top: blue, red, red has probability (4/9)(5/8)(4/7) = 80/504 = 10/63. The bottom: the 3 marbles are 2 red and 1 blue with probability C(5,2)·C(4,1)/C(9,3) = 40/84 = 10/21. So the answer is (10/63) ÷ (10/21) = 21/63 = 1/3. A quicker view: the three orders BRR, RBR, and RRB all have the same probability, (4·5·4)/(9·8·7), so the blue marble is equally likely to be in any of the 3 positions. Traps: stopping at the joint probability (10/63), ignoring the condition (4/9), or giving the probability of the condition (10/21).",["That\'s the probability of the condition itself, P(exactly 2 red) = 40/84 = 10/21, not the conditional probability asked for.","That\'s the probability that the first marble is blue with no condition: 4/9. The given information changes it.","Correct: once you know the draws are 2 red and 1 blue, the blue one is equally likely to be first, second, or third, so the probability is 1/3.","That\'s P(first blue AND exactly 2 red) = (4/9)(5/8)(4/7) = 10/63. Divide by P(exactly 2 red) = 10/21 to get the conditional probability."]],"math-0487":["Start with all committees: C(9,4) = 126. Remove those with no senior, which are chosen from the 5 juniors: C(5,4) = 5, leaving 121. Now remove committees that have both Ana and Bo and at least 1 senior. With Ana and Bo on it, choose 2 more from the other 7 people: C(7,2) = 21 ways, but C(3,2) = 3 of those add 2 more juniors and no senior, and they were already removed. So subtract 21 − 3 = 18: 121 − 18 = 103. Check another way: committees without both Ana and Bo number 126 − 21 = 105; of those, the all-junior ones number 5 − 3 = 2; 105 − 2 = 103. Traps: using one rule only (121 or 105), or subtracting the overlap twice (100).",["Uses only the senior rule: 126 − 5 = 121. It still counts committees with both Ana and Bo.","Uses only the Ana-and-Bo rule: 126 − 21 = 105. It still counts the 2 all-junior committees without both of them.","Correct: 126 total, minus 5 all-junior committees, gives 121. Committees with both Ana and Bo and at least 1 senior number 21 − 3 = 18, so 121 − 18 = 103.","Subtracts all 21 committees with Ana and Bo from 121. But 3 of those 21 are all-junior and were already removed, so they get subtracted twice."]],"math-0488":["f(x) = 0 means 4 sin(πx/6) = −2, so sin(πx/6) = −1/2. The sine is −1/2 at angles 7π/6 and 11π/6, plus any multiple of 2π. So πx/6 = 7π/6 + 2πk or 11π/6 + 2πk, which gives x = 7 + 12k or x = 11 + 12k. In 0 ≤ x ≤ 20: x = 7, 11, and 19 (−5 and −1 are below 0, and 23 is above 20). The sum is 7 + 11 + 19 = 37. Traps: using only one period (18), the wrong sign of the sine (36), or counting x = 23 (60).",["Uses only the first period, x = 7 and x = 11. The period is 12, so the interval from 0 to 20 also includes x = 19.","Solves sin(πx/6) = +1/2, which gives x = 1, 5, 13, and 17. f(x) = 0 means 4 sin(πx/6) = −2, so the sine is −1/2.","Correct: sin(πx/6) = −1/2 when πx/6 = 7π/6 or 11π/6 (plus multiples of 2π), so x = 7, 11, 19, 23, .... In 0 ≤ x ≤ 20 that\'s 7 + 11 + 19 = 37.","Includes x = 23, which is outside the interval: two full periods would reach x = 24, but the interval stops at 20."]],"reading-0001":["Early in the passage, Ezra is waiting for ten-thirty so he can lock the doors, holds his keys \\"already in his hand,\\" and calls the folding \\"the worst part.\\" Mr. Pell\'s visits keep him there until nearly eleven-thirty. All of that shows impatience. The sympathy choice is a timing trap: Ezra learns about Mr. Pell\'s wife only in the second-to-last paragraph, so he can\'t feel that sympathy at the beginning. Indifference is wrong because the visits clearly change his night.",["Ezra notices how little Mr. Pell washes, but nothing shows he wonders about it; he is focused on closing.","Correct: Ezra waits with \\"his keys already in his hand,\\" calls the folding \\"the worst part,\\" and wants to lock up at ten-thirty.","Wrong time: Ezra only learns about Mr. Pell\'s wife near the end of the passage.","The visits keep Ezra there until almost eleven-thirty, so they clearly affect his evening."]],"reading-0002":["The passage says Ezra left Mr. Pell\'s machine open \\"as though someone were still expected to use it.\\" He also stands \\"in the dark for a long minute\\" after finally getting the early closing he wanted. Together, these details hint that part of him was still waiting for Mr. Pell and missed him. The signaling choice goes too far: the doors are locked and the lights are off. The tired and cleaning choices have no support in the text. A good inference is a small, safe step from what the passage says.",["Correct: he leaves Mr. Pell\'s machine ready \\"as though someone were still expected to use it,\\" and he lingers in the dark.","Nothing suggests he was tired; he locks up exactly on time, as he always wanted to.","Too strong: he has already locked the doors and turned off the lights, so he isn\'t signaling anyone.","Not supported: the passage never mentions cleaning the machine."]],"reading-0003":["\\"Folded like a man wrapping a fish\\" is a comparison (notice \\"like\\"), so it is figurative. Picture someone wrapping a fish: they roll it up in paper quickly, with no care for neat corners. Mr. Pell says he is \\"trying to prove her wrong,\\" and the passage shows how he does that: he folds slowly and refolds a shirt four times. So his wife\'s comment must have meant his folding used to be careless. The last choice is the reversal trap; it describes his folding now, not what his wife complained about.",["The passage never mentions a job; the phrase is a comparison (\\"like\\"), not a fact about his past.","Nothing suggests a real fish was involved; \\"like\\" signals a comparison.","Correct: someone wrapping a fish bundles it up fast, and he is now \\"trying to prove her wrong\\" by folding with great care.","Reversed: the careful, slow folding is what he does NOW to prove her wrong, so her comment was about the opposite."]],"reading-0004":["In \\"Ezra ran the counter,\\" the sentence goes on to list his duties: sweeping, refilling the soap machine, locking the doors. So \\"ran\\" means he managed or was in charge of it. \\"Raced\\" is the most common meaning of \\"ran,\\" which is exactly why it\'s a trap. \\"Extended\\" and \\"flowed\\" are other real meanings of \\"ran\\" that fit roads and rivers, not jobs. Always plug your choice back into the sentence.",["Correct: Ezra was in charge of the counter, sweeping, refilling soap, and locking up.","The most common meaning (moving fast on foot) doesn\'t fit running a counter.","\\"Extended\\" fits \\"the road ran north,\\" not a person working a counter.","\\"Flowed\\" fits water or paint, not a person\'s job."]],"reading-0005":["The passage moves step by step: towns kept their own sun time, railroads ran into trouble because of it, the railroads created time zones in 1883, some people objected, and the law caught up in 1918. The last paragraph sums it up: the system \\"began... with a business problem.\\" That\'s an explanation of how railroad needs led to time zones. The first choice has the right topic but the wrong verb (\\"argue\\"). The farmer choice takes one detail and makes it the whole point. The comparison choice is broader than anything the passage covers.",["Wrong verb: the author explains what happened and never argues what Congress should have done.","Too narrow, and off: farmers appear in one sentence, which says the time differences hardly mattered to them.","Too broad: the passage covers only the United States and Canada, with no comparison of nations.","Correct: the passage traces the railroads\' time problem, their 1883 solution, and its legacy."]],"reading-0006":["The second paragraph states it directly: each railroad \\"ran its trains by its own chosen time, often the local time of a major city on its line.\\" That\'s why one station could show several clocks. The first choice describes the towns\' sun time. The Congress choice belongs to 1918, and the shared-time choice describes the zone system that started in 1883. Detail questions often borrow true facts from the wrong time or the wrong group.",["This is how towns kept time, not how railroads did; the passage says each railroad picked its own time.","Correct: the second paragraph says each railroad \\"ran its trains by its own chosen time, often the local time of a major city on its line.\\"","Wrong time period: Congress didn\'t make standard time law until 1918.","This describes the 1883 system, not what came before it."]],"reading-0007":["The third paragraph explains it: clocks were reset at midday, and \\"in places that had to turn their clocks back, noon came twice.\\" If it is noon by sun time and the town then sets its clocks back a few minutes, the clock soon reaches noon again. The Congress choice contradicts the fourth paragraph, which says \\"no law required the change.\\" The other two choices describe things the passage never mentions.",["Correct: the third paragraph says that \\"in places that had to turn their clocks back, noon came twice.\\"","The passage says nothing about two schedules; the railroads were moving to one shared time per zone.","Not stated: towns reset their clocks once, at midday.","The passage says no law required the change; Congress acted only in 1918."]],"reading-0008":["In the fourth paragraph, the author says \\"some people resented\\" railroad time. The detail that \\"a few cities held on to local time for years\\" backs up that claim: those cities kept resisting. The last choice is the closest trap because it sits in the same sentence, but cities that refused to switch are the opposite of towns that \\"soon adopted\\" railroad time. The other two choices are real claims from the passage, but from different paragraphs and about different ideas.",["This claim comes from the second paragraph and is about the railroads\' worries, not resistance to the new time.","This is about how sun time works; holding on to local time doesn\'t show anything about east versus west.","Correct: cities refusing to switch for years is evidence that some people resented and resisted railroad time.","Cities that held out for years are an exception to this claim, so they don\'t support it."]],"reading-0009":["The second paragraph says producing prints \\"required a team,\\" then describes each member\'s job: the artist draws, the carver cuts, the printer prints, and the publisher oversees and sells. The main point is the teamwork. The destroyed-drawing choice is a real detail promoted to main point. The publisher choice adds a ranking the passage never makes. The color choice stretches one sentence beyond what it says.",["A true detail from the paragraph, but only one step in the process, not the paragraph\'s main point.","Not supported: the publisher is one member of the team, and the paragraph never ranks the roles.","Only implied by one detail; the paragraph is about the whole team, not the difficulty of color prints.","Correct: the paragraph walks through the artist, carver, printer, and publisher in turn."]],"reading-0010":["The second paragraph says that for color prints, \\"the carver cut a separate block for each color, and the printer used small marks carved into each block to line up the paper exactly every time.\\" The first choice borrows a real detail (pasting the drawing) from the carver\'s step. The single-block choice is the opposite of what the passage says. The publisher choice gives the publisher a job the passage never describes.",["This mixes up steps: the carver pasted the drawing onto the wood block, not onto the printing paper.","Correct: the printer \\"used small marks carved into each block to line up the paper exactly every time.\\"","Reversed: the passage says each color had its own separate block.","The publisher chose subjects, paid workers, and sold prints; checking alignment isn\'t mentioned."]],"reading-0011":["The comparison says \\"the composer matters enormously, but we only hear the work through the performers.\\" In the print, Hokusai is the composer, and the carvers and printers are the performers: we only see his design through their lines and colors. So the comparison shows that those workers shaped the finished work. The second choice is the main trap. The author does want the workers remembered, but explicitly says the composer \\"matters enormously,\\" so the claim that they deserve MORE credit than Hokusai goes too far. The other two read the comparison literally.",["Correct: we \\"hear the work through the performers\\" just as we see a print through the carver\'s lines and the printer\'s colors.","Too strong: the author says \\"the composer matters enormously\\"; the point is shared credit, not more credit.","Takes the comparison literally; the passage never connects prints to concerts.","Takes the comparison literally; nothing in the passage says Hokusai was a musician."]],"reading-0012":["A fact can be checked; an opinion is a judgment someone could disagree with. Three choices describe what happened: how carvers worked, who bought prints, and when prints reached Europe. \\"We lose something when we forget them\\" is the author\'s view about what matters. Another reader could argue that forgetting the carvers\' names costs us nothing. That makes it the opinion.",["A fact about how prints were made; it could be checked against historical records.","A fact about who bought prints; it describes history rather than judging it.","Correct: whether we \\"lose something\\" by forgetting the workers is the author\'s judgment, which a reader could dispute.","A historical fact about when prints arrived in Europe; it could be checked."]],"reading-0013":["The passage first explains how color change works (chromatophores, reflecting cells, skin bumps), then turns to a puzzle: how a possibly colorblind animal matches colors. It ends by saying the machinery is \\"well mapped\\" but the decision-making is \\"still a mystery.\\" The last choice captures both halves. The first choice is the strongest trap because light-sensing skin IS mentioned, but only as a possibility, and \\"proved\\" goes far beyond \\"Neither idea has settled the question.\\" The diver choice confuses the hook with the whole passage.",["Half right: the passage does explain color change, but light-sensing skin is only a \\"possibility\\" and the question isn\'t settled; nothing was proved.","The three animals are named together, but the passage never compares their abilities.","Too narrow: the diver appears only in the opening sentences as a way into the topic.","Correct: the second and third paragraphs explain the skin\'s machinery, and the fourth and fifth raise the open puzzle."]],"reading-0014":["The second paragraph says: \\"When the muscles contract, they pull the sac open like a stretched balloon, spreading the pigment across a wider patch of skin.\\" The first choice is the reversal trap: shrinking to a speck happens when the muscles RELAX. The reflecting cells and skin bumps are real details, but from the next paragraph and about different parts of the skin.",["Reversed: this is what happens when the muscles relax.","Correct: \\"When the muscles contract, they pull the sac open like a stretched balloon.\\"","These reflecting cells are a separate layer and are not described as being worked by the chromatophore\'s muscles.","The bumps are a separate ability described in the third paragraph."]],"reading-0015":["These scientists say matching \\"brightness and pattern may be enough to fool many predators.\\" If that\'s true, an octopus whose colors are a little off could still hide successfully. The light-sensing skin choice is the strongest trap because it is in the same paragraph, but it belongs to a different group (\\"Others have found...\\"). The color-vision choice contradicts the passage, and the reflecting-cell choice isn\'t discussed by this group at all.",["Correct: if matching \\"brightness and pattern may be enough to fool many predators,\\" exact colors aren\'t required to hide.","Contradicts the passage\'s point that most cephalopods appear to be colorblind; these scientists are explaining around that.","This is the other group\'s idea (\\"Others have found...\\"), not this group\'s.","Not supported: this group says nothing about reflecting cells versus chromatophores."]],"reading-0016":["The sentence says that \\"by opening some sacs and closing others, an octopus can paint stripes, spots, or waves of color across its body.\\" Nothing liquid is applied; the octopus makes patterns appear by controlling its chromatophores. So \\"paint\\" means \\"display as a pattern.\\" \\"Coat with a liquid\\" is the everyday meaning and the main trap. \\"Describe in vivid detail\\" is a figurative meaning that fits writing, and \\"cover up\\" doesn\'t match the idea of creating stripes.",["The most common meaning, but the octopus uses no liquid; it opens and closes pigment sacs.","This meaning fits \\"paint a picture in words,\\" not color on skin.","Correct: by opening some sacs and closing others, the octopus makes stripes and spots appear.","The octopus is creating patterns here, not hiding something under a layer."]],"reading-0017":["The author lists the places where she was never alone or undisturbed: a bedroom shared with two sisters and a school where a bell was \\"always about to ring.\\" Then she says that in the reading room, \\"no one asked anything of me.\\" The bedroom detail sets up that contrast and explains why the room\'s silence mattered. Nothing suggests her family discouraged reading, and the teenagers belong to a different part of the story.",["Not supported: the passage shows home was crowded, not that anyone discouraged reading.","Reversed: the passage contrasts busy home with the reading room, where \\"no one asked anything of me.\\"","The teenagers appear years later and have no connection to her sisters.","Correct: a crowded bedroom and school bells contrast with the one place where no one asked anything of her."]],"reading-0018":["Passage B spends most of its length on what happens in a modern library on a typical afternoon: tutoring, a bicycle-repair class, story time, a résumé workshop. Passage A is a personal memory of one quiet room, with details about its windows and oak tables. The first and third choices describe Passage A, the classic \\"wrong passage\\" trap. The design-history choice fits neither passage.",["This describes Passage A, not Passage B.","Correct: Passage B lists tutoring, a bicycle class, story time, and a résumé workshop.","This also describes Passage A (the tall windows and oak tables).","Neither passage discusses the history of library design."]],"reading-0019":["Passage A\'s whole memory is about how valuable a quiet room was to a child. Passage B\'s author is in favor of noise but says, \\"I am not against quiet,\\" and keeps a quiet room that is \\"full most afternoons\\" because people need it. So both would agree that some users benefit from a quiet place. The first choice is an extreme claim that Passage B rejects and Passage A never makes. Passage A\'s author is glad for the teenagers, so she doesn\'t call for removing maker spaces, and Passage B treats noise as a good sign.",["Passage B explicitly rejects this, and Passage A never claims it.","Passage A\'s author is \\"glad for\\" the teenagers, and Passage B welcomes such activities.","Correct: Passage A shows how much quiet meant to her, and Passage B keeps a quiet room that is \\"full most afternoons.\\"","Passage B says the opposite: noise is \\"the sound of many kinds of people finding what they came for.\\""]],"reading-0020":["Passage A ends by wondering where a quiet-loving child can go now that the reading room is a maker space. Passage B has an answer: its branch \\"keeps one small room, with a door that closes, for anyone who needs it.\\" The author\'s view is that quiet should be \\"one of the things a library offers,\\" so the response would be to offer both. The second choice reverses Passage B\'s position, and the third and fourth aren\'t supported by anything in Passage B.",["Correct: Passage B\'s branch \\"keeps one small room, with a door that closes, for anyone who needs it.\\"","Passage B rejects silence as \\"the rule that governs everything else.\\"","Too extreme: Passage B says its quiet room is full most afternoons, so people clearly still need quiet.","Passage B never mentions reading at home; it wants the library to serve everyone."]],"reading-0021":["The first paragraph says that \\"a few owners collected signatures on a petition asking the city to reopen the street.\\" The first two choices describe things the CITY did in the second paragraph, a classic wrong-subject trap. Nothing in the passage says any business moved.",["It was the city, not the shop owners, that asked Ferrante\'s team to measure the results.","The city replaced parking with benches, planters, and room for tables.","Not mentioned: no business is described as moving.","Correct: the first paragraph says \\"a few owners collected signatures on a petition.\\""]],"reading-0022":["Ferrante limits his conclusion. He says one year of data from one street \\"cannot prove that closing streets to cars helps businesses everywhere,\\" but on Linden Street \\"the fears that shoppers would disappear did not come true.\\" The second choice captures that limit. The third choice reverses his caution. The first choice overreads it: he doesn\'t doubt the Linden Street results, only how far they can be stretched.",["Too far: he questions how widely the results apply, not whether the sales rose, and he never says the data prove nothing.","Correct: he says one year on one street \\"cannot prove\\" a general rule, but the fears \\"did not come true\\" there.","Reversed: he says the data \\"cannot prove that closing streets to cars helps businesses everywhere.\\"","Not supported: the paragraph never mentions future studies on other streets."]],"reading-0023":["The table\'s hardware store row shows −12%. The third paragraph explains the owner\'s view: the closure \\"hurt shops that depend on customers arriving by car,\\" since his customers buy heavy or bulky items. The first choice has the right number but borrows the outdoor seating detail, which the passage links to restaurants. The other two choices read the wrong rows of the table.",["Right number, wrong cause: the outdoor seating is credited with helping restaurants, not with hurting the hardware store.","Wrong row: +4% is the bookstore\'s change.","Correct: the table shows −12%, and the third paragraph says the owner blamed the loss of car access.","Wrong row and wrong direction: 18% is the restaurants\' increase."]],"reading-0024":["\\"Most kinds of businesses\\" means more than half of the types. The table lists four types, and three of them (restaurants and cafés, clothing shops, the bookstore) show increases. Only the hardware store fell. So the table supports the statement, and the reason is the three-out-of-four count. The second choice has the right answer with a reason that is true but doesn\'t prove \\"most.\\" The third choice treats one exception as if it disproved the claim. For Yes/No questions, both halves of the choice must be right.",["Correct: restaurants, clothing shops, and the bookstore all show increases; only the hardware store fell.","True, but having the largest increase doesn\'t show that MOST types rose.","One type falling doesn\'t contradict \\"most\\"; three of four still rose.","The claim is about kinds of businesses, and three of the four kinds rose regardless of how many restaurants there are."]],"reading-0025":["The narrator says \\"I\\" and calls Theo \\"my son,\\" so she is a parent telling her own story. Most of the passage happens in the present (\\"Now Theo wobbles forward\\"), and one paragraph is a memory of her father teaching her. The last choice covers both. The \\"outside observer\\" choice is wrong because the narrator is a character in the scene. The \\"only childhood\\" choice ignores the present-day frame.",["The narrator is the adult (Theo\'s parent); her own childhood ride is told as a memory.","The narrator uses \\"I\\" and takes part in the scene, so she is not an outside observer; we never get Theo\'s thoughts directly.","Too narrow: most of the passage is a present-day scene with Theo, told in the present tense.","Correct: she teaches Theo now (present tense) and remembers her father teaching her (past tense)."]],"reading-0026":["Every other paragraph is set in the present and written in the present tense (\\"Theo and I go,\\" \\"Now Theo wobbles\\"). This paragraph jumps back to \\"the summer I turned eight\\" and is told in the past tense. That is the shift: it is a flashback. It has the same narrator, other paragraphs have dialogue too, and Theo isn\'t in it, so the other choices don\'t work.",["The same narrator (\\"I\\") tells the whole passage.","Correct: this paragraph shifts to the past tense to tell a memory; the rest is a present-day scene.","Theo and the narrator also speak in the second and third paragraphs.","Theo doesn\'t appear in this paragraph at all; it is about the narrator and her father."]],"reading-0027":["As a child, the narrator felt betrayed. But at the end she does exactly what her father did: she lets go without a word and stands \\"with my arms in the air, the way my father did.\\" Choosing to repeat his method shows she now understands why he did it. The resentment choice is the trap; it describes her feelings at age eight, not at the end. The regret and disapproval choices add feelings the passage never states.",["Correct: she lets go silently and raises her arms \\"the way my father did,\\" repeating his choice on purpose.","\\"Betrayal\\" describes how she felt as a child; her actions at the end show that feeling has changed.","Not supported: she mentions the day of silence but never says she regrets it.","Nothing suggests she thinks he was wrong; she repeats his exact gestures by choice."]],"reading-0028":["The passage gives the rule: straight up the comb means \\"the same direction as the sun,\\" and an angle away from straight up means the same angle away from the sun. Straight down is the opposite of straight up, a half turn, so the flowers lie in the direction opposite the sun. The \\"underneath the hive\\" choice reads \\"down\\" literally. The \\"close to the hive\\" choice mixes up direction with distance, which comes from the length of the run.",["This is what a run straight UP the comb means.","Takes \\"down\\" literally; the angle on the comb stands for a direction relative to the sun, not up or down in space.","Correct: straight up means toward the sun, so straight down, the opposite angle, means away from the sun.","Distance is shown by the length of the run, not its direction."]],"reading-0029":["The scent theory says bees find food by smell. If a bee is moved somewhere new and still flies the direction and distance the dance gave, ending up in the wrong place rather than the flowers, it must be using the dance\'s instructions, not following a smell. That is why the passage says this \\"suggested that they were following the dance\'s directions rather than simply tracking a scent.\\" The flaw choice is backward: the radar work \\"gave strong support\\" to von Frisch. The other two choices aren\'t in the passage.",["Not supported: the passage never questions the tags; the bees flew as the dance indicated.","The passage says nothing about bees getting lost; they flew the direction and distance from the dance.","The radar studies supported von Frisch rather than finding a flaw in his work.","Correct: from a new spot, the bees still flew the dance\'s direction and distance, which a scent-following bee wouldn\'t do."]],"reading-0030":["The sentence reads: \\"some researchers argued that recruited bees found food mainly by smell, since a dancer carries the scent of the flowers she has visited.\\" The word \\"since\\" shows that the scent detail is the evidence for the smell theory. The other choices are real claims from the passage, but from other paragraphs or on the other side of the debate, which is the usual trap in evidence questions.",["This claim is about the length of the run, from the third paragraph; scent has nothing to do with it.","Correct: the passage says those researchers argued for smell \\"since a dancer carries the scent.\\"","This claim is from the second paragraph and concerns touch and vibration, not scent.","The scent detail is the reason for the doubt that the radar studies later answered, not support for von Frisch."]],"reading-0031":["When the narrator says the stand loses money, the grandfather\'s whole answer is about who set the price: \\"Your grandmother priced them. I haven\'t changed it.\\" The sign is still in her handwriting. That points to a reluctance to change something his wife decided. He never calls the price fair or teaches a lesson about profit, and his long pause before answering suggests he already knows the stand loses money.",["He never says the price is fair; his only explanation is who set it.","Correct: asked about money, he answers only that \\"your grandmother priced them\\" and \\"I haven\'t changed it,\\" and the sign is still in her handwriting.","He looks at the road \\"for a long time\\" before answering, which suggests he knows; nothing shows he hasn\'t noticed.","Sounds like a theme of the story, but he never makes a point about profit to the narrator."]],"reading-0032":["Earlier, the grandfather says \\"nothing at all\\" while customers taste his peaches. So when the narrator notes that his remark to the boy was the longest sentence she\'d heard him offer a stranger, the point is the contrast: he gives the boy free fruit and actual conversation. The detail doesn\'t explain the slow business, show resentment, or suggest a long friendship (the narrator calls the boy a stranger).",["Correct: with customers he says \\"nothing at all,\\" so a full sentence of advice to the boy stands out as unusual warmth.","Tempting cause-and-effect, but the narrator blames slow business on few cars, not on his silence.","She notices his silence, but nothing in the comment shows resentment.","Nothing suggests he knew the family; the narrator calls the boy a stranger."]],"reading-0033":["The narrator copies her grandfather\'s way of offering a slice but not his silence; she asks \\"Sweet, right?\\" His response is a sound she \\"eventually decided was a laugh,\\" and he \\"never once told me to stop.\\" A laugh at her chattiness, with no correction, suggests he enjoys it. Nothing shows him disapproving, worrying about customers, or planning for next summer.",["Nothing shows disapproval: he \\"never once told me to stop,\\" and the narrator decides his sound \\"was a laugh.\\"","This was never suggested; the narrator\'s only worry about customers was that too few came.","Correct: when she breaks his rule of silence, he answers with a sound she decides is a laugh and never tells her to stop.","Nothing in the passage looks ahead to next summer or to her running the stand alone."]],"reading-0034":["A sentence that someone \\"offers\\" a stranger is one they say to them, often freely. \\"Say\\" fits. \\"Propose\\" and \\"sell\\" borrow other meanings of \\"offer\\" (offering a plan or a price), and \\"sacrifice\\" is a religious meaning of \\"offering\\" that doesn\'t fit at all.",["\\"Propose\\" fits offers of plans or deals, not simply speaking a sentence.","Correct: \\"the longest sentence I had heard him offer a stranger\\" means the longest sentence he had said to one.","The stand sells peaches, but a sentence isn\'t sold.","\\"Offer\\" can mean to give up something precious, but nothing is given up here."]],"reading-0035":["Follow the passage\'s moves: the old view (paths as a nuisance to be stamped out), the opposite view (paths as information, shown at Varrow College), and then a limit (paths show where people want to go, not where they ought to go, so treat them \\"as evidence to be weighed\\"). The choice that captures both the value and the caution is the key. Each wrong choice fits only one or two paragraphs: the cost savings, the faculty complaints, or the old view of paths as a nuisance.",["Too narrow and too partisan: the faculty objections are one paragraph, and the author later questions following paths at all.","The author presents the opposite view but never attacks the old one; the fourth paragraph even supports caution.","Correct: the passage presents desire paths as information (the Varrow example) and then limits that idea (they show wants, not what is wise).","Too narrow: this is the Varrow example in the second and third paragraphs, not the purpose of the whole passage."]],"reading-0036":["The paragraph\'s central sentence is \\"They show where people want to go, not where they ought to go,\\" backed by two examples where the desired route is harmful or unsafe. The wrong choices either exaggerate (\\"usually damage,\\" \\"no reliable way\\") or reverse the paragraph, which explicitly rejects the \\"wisdom of crowds\\" fable.",["Too strong: the slope is one example of possible harm, and the paragraph says to weigh paths, not block them.","Correct: \\"They show where people want to go, not where they ought to go,\\" with the slope and parking-lot examples.","Reversed: the paragraph warns against turning the story into \\"a fable about the wisdom of crowds.\\"","Too strong: the paragraph says planners can \\"read desire paths well\\" by weighing them as evidence."]],"reading-0037":["In the first paragraph, the usual response (reseed, chains, hedges) fails: \\"The paths usually come back.\\" At Varrow, paving the worn routes meant the grass was \\"no longer being trampled,\\" so reseeding was barely needed. The budget detail therefore supports the idea that paving actual routes solves the trampling problem that blocking didn\'t. It says nothing about whether the faculty\'s taste was wrong, it doesn\'t mean fewer people crossed the quad, and it supports the opposite side of the passage from the fourth-paragraph warning.",["The \\"Yet\\" sets cost against looks, but saving money doesn\'t prove the quad looks good; the complaints are about appearance.","Misreads the cause: the grass recovered because people walked on pavement instead of grass, not because they walked less.","A real claim from the fourth paragraph, but the budget detail is about Varrow\'s success, not about the limits of desire paths.","Correct: reseeding and chains had failed (\\"the paths usually come back\\"), but once the worn routes were paved, the grass stopped being trampled."]],"reading-0038":["Planners should treat desire paths \\"as evidence to be weighed, not as instructions to be obeyed.\\" Weighing evidence means thinking it over and judging how much it counts. The literal meaning (measuring on a scale) is the trap, and \\"held down\\" and \\"balanced evenly\\" borrow other senses of the word.",["Correct: evidence \\"to be weighed\\" is evidence to be judged and thought over.","The most common meaning of \\"weigh,\\" but evidence isn\'t put on a scale.","That\'s \\"weighed down,\\" a different meaning.","Close to \\"weigh\\" in sound and image, but the point is judging, not making things equal."]],"reading-0039":["The passage moves from the problem (too many words, too little room), to how she chooses what to keep, to why she sees cutting as the craft, and finally to what is lost. A purpose that covers both the gain and the loss fits all four paragraphs. The \\"complete satisfaction\\" choice is the strongest trap because her attitude really does shift in the third paragraph, but the fourth paragraph shows she is still troubled by the cost.",["No viewers complain in the passage; the opening question is curiosity, and the author herself admits the losses.","Too narrow: the limits appear only in the first paragraph and set up the real subject.","Half right: her view did change, but the last paragraph\'s \\"There is a cost\\" rules out \\"complete satisfaction.\\"","Correct: the passage explains what she cuts and why, calls the cutting \\"the craft itself,\\" and then admits \\"there is a cost.\\""]],"reading-0040":["The comparison comes right after she says the job is not a word-for-word carrying-over, and right before \\"Each line forces a choice about what the scene cannot do without.\\" Like packing a small suitcase, subtitling means deciding what to leave behind. The regret choice is tempting because the last paragraph does discuss losses, but that\'s a different part of the essay; the suitcase image is about the act of choosing.",["Regret comes up later (\\"There is a cost\\"), but the suitcase image itself is about choosing, not regretting.","No other subtitlers are mentioned anywhere.","Correct: the next sentence spells it out: \\"Each line forces a choice about what the scene cannot do without.\\"","Nothing in the comparison suggests exhaustion or reluctance; she later calls the choosing \\"the craft itself.\\""]],"reading-0041":["In the last paragraph she names specific losses and tries to make up for them \\"with a word choice or a pause,\\" but she isn\'t always able to. Her final line, \\"I know exactly where,\\" shows the losses still bother her. Since she has also said that cutting is \\"the craft itself,\\" she accepts it. Accepting but uneasy covers both. Watch for tone pairs where one word fits and the other doesn\'t: she is proud elsewhere but not defensive here, and her calm sentences aren\'t the same as not caring.",["Correct: she accepts cutting as part of the craft, but she tries to \\"give some of it back\\" and knows \\"exactly where\\" each loss is.","Her pride shows in the third paragraph, but here she is admitting a cost, not defending herself.","Her plain sentences might look detached, but \\"I know exactly where\\" shows the losses stay with her.","Too strong: she calls the cutting \\"the craft itself\\" and never apologizes."]],"reading-0042":["The second paragraph ranks what survives: plot information \\"usually,\\" jokes \\"often,\\" and filler (false starts, \\"you know,\\" unfinished sentences) \\"almost never.\\" The old-fashioned phrasing is a trap from the last paragraph: she gives it as one example of a loss, not as something she almost never keeps.",["She says this is \\"usually\\" what she keeps.","Correct: \\"Almost never is it the filler that makes real speech sound real.\\"","She says this is \\"often\\" what she keeps.","She does sometimes smooth this away, but she never says \\"almost never\\" about it; that phrase is used only for filler."]],"reading-0043":["The third paragraph names two limits: growth rates vary with climate, and \\"the first years of growth are hard to measure.\\" The second limit means a rock exposed only a few years ago would give the shakiest estimate. Old rock is the trap, since \\"centuries\\" sounds like a stretch, but the passage says the method \\"can reach back centuries.\\" A known local growth rate makes the estimate better, not worse.",["The passage says the method \\"can reach back centuries,\\" so old surfaces are within its range.","Reversed: knowing the local growth rate is what makes the method work.","Nothing suggests that nearby rocks of the same age weaken the estimate.","Correct: the passage says \\"the first years of growth are hard to measure,\\" so a very young surface is where the method is weakest."]],"reading-0044":["The passage gives the cause directly in the last paragraph: with no roots, lichens absorb everything from rain and air, including pollutants. The other choices mix in true details from elsewhere (slow growth, the partner, drying out) that the passage never links to pollution, or reverse them.",["Slow growth is real, but the passage never connects it to pollution.","The passage says many lichen species can\'t tolerate sulfur dioxide; it never blames the partner.","Correct: \\"Because they have no roots, they take in water and dissolved substances directly from rain and air, along with whatever pollutants the air contains.\\"","Reversed: the passage says lichens can dry out and stop growing; it just doesn\'t link this to pollution."]],"reading-0045":["The phrase sets up a turn: something that looks like a drawback (growing a millimeter or less a year) turns out to make lichens useful for dating rock. The criticism choice is the strong trap, because the same paragraph does mention the method\'s limits. But the \\"weakness\\" is the lichens\' slowness, not a flaw in the method, and the author accepts the limits instead of arguing against them.",["Correct: the same sentence turns slowness into an advantage (\\"has made lichens useful to geologists\\"), and the dating method follows.","Nothing says slowness endangers lichens; the second paragraph shows them thriving on bare rock.","The paragraph does discuss the dating method\'s limits, but \\"weakness\\" refers to the lichens\' slowness, and the author accepts the limits rather than rejecting them.","No faster-growing organisms are mentioned."]],"reading-0046":["Ecologists survey lichens \\"as one rough measure of how clean its air has become,\\" meaning the lichens indicate air quality. Each wrong choice is a real meaning of \\"measure\\" in other contexts: a unit, an action taken, or an amount.",["A measure can be a unit (like an inch), but lichens aren\'t a unit of air quality.","Correct: the lichens on a city\'s trees serve as a sign, or indicator, of how clean its air is.","\\"Measures\\" can mean actions taken (\\"safety measures\\"), but surveying lichens isn\'t an action to clean the air.","\\"A measure of flour\\" means a portion, which doesn\'t fit here."]],"reading-0047":["The author describes the added sun gently (\\"small, careful\\") and right after it says he expected to feel protective but felt relief instead. He then insists the new mural not be his and lets residents choose the content and paint it. Together these suggest he sees the sun as an early sign of the community taking ownership. The vandalism choice clashes with his tone, and the other choices invent regrets or plans the passage never mentions.",["He calls the addition \\"small\\" and \\"careful\\" and feels no protectiveness, which doesn\'t fit vandalism.","Correct: he calls the sun \\"careful,\\" feels relief rather than protectiveness, and then lets residents shape and paint the new mural.","He notes the yellow is one he \\"never used,\\" but nothing says he regrets his own colors.","The new mural is built from residents\' ideas; nothing suggests he plans to remove anyone\'s work."]],"reading-0048":["Check each choice against both passages. Physical decay is described only in Passage A. A public process appears in both: Passage B names it, and Passage A shows it (three evenings at the library, residents helping paint). Admitting the old mural was more skillful happens only in Passage A. Only Passage B makes a general recommendation to cities: budget for replacement, not just restoration. The public-process choice is the most tempting, because Passage A never uses the phrase even though it describes one.",["Only in Passage A: chalky blues and a crack through the heron. Passage B speaks of fading only in general.","In both: Passage A\'s library evenings and Passage B\'s \\"public process\\" are the same idea in different words.","Only in Passage A: \\"The result is less skillful than my heron.\\" Passage B mentions loss but not skill.","Correct: only Passage B proposes that cities set aside money for replacement as well as restoration; Passage A tells one artist\'s story."]],"reading-0049":["Passage B says that for most murals the better question is \\"What should go here now?\\" and that new work should come from a public process. Passage A\'s author does exactly that: he listens to residents, draws what they describe, and lets them help paint. The flood trap mixes up the new mural\'s content with the old mural\'s subject. The restoration choices go against Passage B\'s main argument.",["Passage B saves conservators for murals that record events never to be forgotten; Passage A\'s river scene isn\'t described that way.","The flood comes from residents\' memories for the new mural; the original was a river scene, not a memorial.","Correct: Passage A\'s new mural asks \\"what should go here now\\" and is made through a public process, exactly what Passage B recommends.","Passage B argues against restoring most murals and wants money set aside for replacement instead."]],"reading-0050":["Find a claim both authors support. Passage A\'s author says the community-made mural is \\"more accurate\\" because it shows what the river means to people now. Passage B says a mural restored to its first day can become \\"a monument to a neighborhood that no longer exists.\\" Both agree a new mural can better reflect the present. The artist-feels-loss choice is the trap: Passage B says it, but Passage A\'s author reports relief, so he wouldn\'t share that view.",["Correct: Passage A calls the new mural \\"more accurate,\\" and Passage B warns that an endlessly restored mural can honor \\"a neighborhood that no longer exists.\\"","Too strong for Passage B, which says some murals deserve a conservator\'s care.","Passage B says this, but Passage A\'s author felt \\"relief,\\" not a sharp loss, so he would likely disagree.","Passage A\'s author did take part; he simply refused to make the new mural his own."]],"reading-0051":["The paragraph reports that basic tools (ladders and drills) were the most borrowed overall, then gives the director\'s explanation: many members were renters without basic tools or space to store them. Its last sentence states the point: the library was less about avoiding one expensive purchase than about avoiding owning tools at all. The renter choice is tempting but makes a comparison the passage never makes.",["Correct: ladders and drills led in total loans, and the director\'s explanation ends with \\"a way to avoid owning tools at all.\\"","Reversed: ladders and drills were borrowed \\"far more often in total.\\"","Overreach: the director suggests many members were renters but never compares joining rates of renters and homeowners.","Nothing in the paragraph recommends dropping any tools."]],"reading-0052":["Divide total loans by copies owned: ladder 270 ÷ 6 = 45, drill 288 ÷ 8 = 36, tile saw 74 ÷ 2 = 37, pressure washer 81 ÷ 2 = 40.5, carpet cleaner 45 ÷ 3 = 15. The ladder is highest. Two traps: the drill has the most total loans, and the passage\'s remark that pressure washers were borrowed often \\"relative to\\" their copies makes them sound like the leader, but the passage never says they were the highest.",["The drill had the most total loans (288), but with 8 copies that is only 36 per copy.","Correct: 270 loans ÷ 6 copies = 45 loans per copy, the highest of any item.","The passage says pressure washers were borrowed often relative to their copies, and 81 ÷ 2 = 40.5 is high, but the ladder\'s 45 is higher.","74 ÷ 2 = 37 loans per copy, lower than the ladder\'s 45."]],"reading-0053":["The organizers expected the expensive, rarely bought items, \\"such as tile saws and carpet cleaners,\\" to be the most popular. Together those two had 74 + 45 = 119 loans, while the ladder alone had 270 and the drill 288. So the data do not support the expectation, and the key gives a reason that is both true and relevant. Check each reason against the table: the carpet cleaner was not borrowed more per copy than the drill (15 vs. 36), and it did not have the shortest wait (the drill\'s 1 day is shorter). A long wait for the tile saw shows a shortage of copies, not overall popularity.",["A long wait reflects few copies (only 2), not overall popularity, and it ignores the carpet cleaner, the other item they expected to lead.","Half true: the tile saw\'s 37 per copy beats the drill\'s 36, but the carpet cleaner\'s 45 ÷ 3 = 15 is far below it.","Correct: 74 + 45 = 119 loans for the two expensive items, far fewer than the ladder\'s 270 or the drill\'s 288.","The right answer for a false reason: the drill\'s 1-day wait is shorter than the carpet cleaner\'s 2 days."]],"reading-0054":["A column of average waits shows that borrowers often had to wait for a copy, which supports the statement about waiting lists. The ladders-and-drills statement is also supported by the table, but by a different column (Total loans), so it doesn\'t answer this question. The table says nothing about who the members were or how long a typical drill is used.",["True, but it comes from the Total loans column, not the wait column.","The table has no information about who the members were.","This is the passage\'s opening joke; the table has no data on how long drills are used.","Correct: average waits of 4, 9, and 11 days show borrowers lining up for in-demand items."]],"reading-0055":["The narrator says it plainly: \\"I thought it was theater,\\" meaning a show for appearances. When Devin explains Mrs. Ferreira\'s reason, the narrator decides \\"that was theater too.\\" Her respect for the walking comes only later, after the rescue.",["This is closer to how the narrator feels by the end, not at the beginning.","Nothing mentions her age making the chair hard to climb.","Nothing suggests distrust; she walks the deck no matter who is on duty.","Correct: \\"I thought it was theater,\\" and she dismisses Mrs. Ferreira\'s explanation as \\"theater too.\\""]],"reading-0056":["Devin\'s remark explains the rescue: Mrs. Ferreira noticed the mother stop watching her child, so she was already moving toward that spot. That fits the earlier description of her walking past the narrator\'s chair \\"at the same unhurried pace\\" and then stepping straight into the water. The \\"happened to be passing\\" choice is the trap; it reads the rescue as luck, which is exactly what Devin\'s explanation rules out. And she couldn\'t have been assigned the deep end, because the narrator was.",["Correct: noticing a distracted parent explains why she was already heading toward the boy before anyone saw him slip.","The strongest trap: Devin\'s remark is offered as the explanation, so her timing wasn\'t luck.","She \\"did not mention it\\" afterward; nothing suggests blame or a report.","The narrator was the one assigned to the deep end."]],"reading-0057":["Until the end, the passage narrates one summer at Alder Street. The last paragraph jumps forward years (\\"four pools since\\") and shows how that summer changed the narrator: she still catches herself climbing down from the chair, as Mrs. Ferreira would. The head-guard choice is tempting because it sounds like the natural ending, but it adds facts (a promotion, never using the chair) that the passage doesn\'t give.",["Devin barely appears in the last paragraph; its focus is the narrator.","The boy was saved, and the paragraph shows a habit learned, not regret.","Correct: the earlier paragraphs tell one summer\'s events; the last jumps ahead (\\"four pools since\\") to a lasting habit.","Overreach: it never mentions head guard, and she only climbs down \\"every so often,\\" so she still uses chairs."]],"reading-0058":["The passage follows the wood frog from freezing, to how it survives (keeping ice outside its cells with glucose), to thawing, and finally to why scientists study it. The organ-storage choice is the trap: it takes the last sentence and turns a cautious hope into a confident prediction.",["Other frogs get one clause in the first paragraph; the passage is about the wood frog.","Correct: the passage describes the freezing, explains the glucose defense, and ends with why researchers care.","Too narrow: cell damage is background for the explanation in the third paragraph.","Overstated: the passage says only that \\"some researchers hope\\" this \\"might someday\\" help."]],"reading-0059":["The third paragraph says the glucose builds up inside the cells, lowers the freezing point of the fluid there, and helps cells keep their water as ice forms around them. The energy choice is the trap, using outside knowledge that glucose is fuel; but the passage says the heart stops. Ice still forms in the body, just not inside the cells.",["Ice forms beneath the skin and in the body cavity and stays frozen until spring; the glucose works inside the cells.","Ice does form, beneath the skin and around the organs; the glucose keeps it outside the cells.","Glucose is a fuel in general, but the passage says the frog\'s heart stops.","Correct: the glucose \\"lowers the freezing point of the fluid there and helps the cells hold on to water.\\""]],"reading-0060":["The sentence moves the danger from \\"cold\\" to \\"what ice does to cells.\\" That sets up the rest of the paragraph: the frog survives because it keeps ice outside its cells and uses glucose to protect them. The correct-a-belief choice is the closest trap, because the sentence does push back on a common idea, but the idea is about most animals; nobody in the passage thinks wood frogs die from the cold.",["Correct: once the danger is defined as damage to cells, the paragraph explains how the frog avoids that damage.","The sentence does correct a likely belief, but about most animals; wood frogs aren\'t killed by freezing at all.","The paragraph is about body chemistry (glucose); the leaf-litter shelter isn\'t mentioned again.","The heart stopping is in the second paragraph, and this sentence doesn\'t explain it."]],"reading-0061":["Each paragraph adds to one picture: what the bookmobile carries and where it goes, why people depend on it, what Okafor-Lind does besides lend books, and how residents fought to keep it. That\'s a description of the bookmobile and why it matters. The other choices either go further than the passage (arguing to replace buildings), zoom in on one detail (the notebook), or invent something it never does (compare costs).",["Correct: the passage describes the truck\'s route and work and shows how much residents depend on it.","Too strong: the passage never argues that library buildings should be replaced.","Too narrow: the request notebook is one detail in the third paragraph.","Not in the passage: the mail-order idea is mentioned, but no costs are compared."]],"reading-0062":["The first paragraph lists the stops: \\"a senior housing complex, two farm towns without a library of their own, and an elementary school at the end of a long gravel road.\\" Only the senior housing complex matches. The main branch is where the truck starts, the school is an elementary school, not a middle school, and the farm towns have no library.",["Wrong role: the truck starts from the main branch; it isn\'t one of the stops.","Twisted detail: the school on the gravel road is an elementary school.","Correct: the first paragraph lists a senior housing complex as a stop.","Contradicted: the farm towns it visits are described as having no library of their own."]],"reading-0063":["In the third paragraph, the older students choose from a shelf stocked with \\"the books they asked for the week before,\\" and \\"She keeps a notebook of those requests.\\" So the notebook tracks what students asked for. The wrong choices borrow real details from the passage (laptops, snow on the road) and attach them to the notebook.",["Not in the passage: overdue books are never mentioned.","Correct: the third paragraph says she keeps a notebook of the students\' requests.","Mixes in a detail from the last paragraph; the notebook isn\'t about the route.","Mixes in the laptops from the same paragraph; the notebook isn\'t about them."]],"reading-0064":["The last paragraph says the mail-order idea \\"was dropped after more than four hundred residents wrote to the county asking to keep the bookmobile.\\" The snow choice is tempting because snow is mentioned in the same paragraph, but there it causes problems for the truck, not a reason to keep it.",["Not in the passage: costs are never discussed.","Twisted detail: snow closes the road for the truck, not for mail.","Not in the passage: nothing is said about space at the main branch.","Correct: the last paragraph says the idea was dropped after more than four hundred residents wrote in."]],"reading-0065":["The sentence says some families \\"cannot spare the time or the gas money for a long trip.\\" Put each choice in its place: \\"cannot afford to give the time or the gas money\\" makes sense. \\"Keep in reserve\\" and \\"save from harm\\" are real meanings of \\"spare,\\" but they don\'t fit this sentence. That\'s the usual trap in these questions: the most familiar meaning of a word is often wrong in context.",["Correct: the families can\'t afford to give up the time or the gas money a long trip takes.","Common meaning trap: a \\"spare tire\\" is kept in reserve, but the families aren\'t saving time for later.","Another meaning of \\"spare\\" (\\"spare someone\'s feelings\\") that doesn\'t fit time and gas money.","Close to the opposite: the point is that they can\'t use up the time or money at all."]],"reading-0066":["The passage follows a single morning: the 3:41 alarm, the walk in the dark, shaping rolls, and the first customer. The other choices add things the passage never says (a future career, money trouble) or turn the doughnut joke into a comparison that isn\'t there.",["Unsupported: the passage never says what she did when she grew up.","Correct: the whole passage follows one early morning at the bakery, from the alarm to the first customer.","Not in the passage: nothing suggests the bakery is struggling; customers line up outside.","Twisted detail: she hoped for doughnuts, but no doughnut shops are described or compared."]],"reading-0067":["The first paragraph gives the reason directly: she volunteered \\"mostly because I thought it would mean free doughnuts.\\" Her parents\' double shifts explain why she was staying with Teo, not why she volunteered, so that choice mixes up two facts from the same sentence.",["Not stated: she learns to shape rolls, but that wasn\'t her reason for volunteering.","Not stated: her parents are working; nothing says they asked her to help.","Tempting because her parents were working double shifts, but the passage gives a different reason.","Correct: she says she volunteered \\"mostly because I thought it would mean free doughnuts.\\""]],"reading-0068":["Teo predicts \\"The fortieth one will be good,\\" but the narrator says he was \\"almost right\\" because \\"The thirty-fourth roll was good.\\" The fortieth is the trap: it\'s the number Teo said, not the one that happened. Six is the number of rolls the nurse bought.",["Contradicted: her first rolls came out lumpy, flat, and lopsided.","Wrong number: six is how many rolls the nurse bought.","Correct: \\"The thirty-fourth roll was good.\\"","Teo\'s prediction, not what happened; the narrator says he was \\"almost right.\\""]],"reading-0069":["When her rolls come out badly, Teo doesn\'t take over. He gives her a full tray and says, \\"Forty more. The fortieth one will be good.\\" That shows he thinks practice will fix the problem. The \\"too busy\\" choice is the main trap: he does go back to his own work, but only after showing her how and setting her up to practice. The last choice is contradicted when he puts her rolls in the window.",["Correct: giving her forty more and predicting the fortieth will be good shows he expects practice to work.","Misreads \\"without watching\\": he had already shown her, and leaving her alone shows trust, not lack of time.","No sign of disappointment: he calmly predicts she will succeed.","Contradicted: he later puts her rolls, even the lumpy ones, in the front window."]],"reading-0070":["The last two sentences answer this directly: \\"I did not say anything. I was too busy trying not to look as proud as I felt.\\" The other choices take details from the passage (lumpy rolls, her age, the early alarm) and turn them into reasons the passage never gives.",["Not supported: the lumpy rolls are in the window, but she says nothing about being embarrassed.","Not supported: her age is never a concern in this scene.","Not supported: the early alarm is mentioned, but tiredness isn\'t her reason for staying quiet.","Correct: she says she was \\"too busy trying not to look as proud as I felt.\\""]],"reading-0071":["Two parts of the passage set his attitude. In the third paragraph he expected to dislike the completion but was \\"disarmed\\" by how openly it separates itself from the original; that\'s real appreciation of its restraint. In the last paragraph he has a \\"reservation,\\" but he says it is \\"not with Lisowski\\": he worries that a recorded completion becomes \\"simply the piece\\" and listeners lose the silence. Only the second choice holds both halves. The skepticism choice describes how he felt before hearing it, and the \\"reluctant acceptance\\" choice is ruled out by \\"I no longer think anyone has to choose between the two versions.\\"",["Too strong: \\"My reservation\\" in the last paragraph shows his admiration has limits, and he never calls it an improvement.","Correct: he is \\"disarmed\\" by its plainness, yet worries a completion tends to become \\"simply the piece.\\"","Reversed timeline: he was skeptical BEFORE hearing it; afterward his only reservation is about how completions get used, not quality.","Contradicted: he says he no longer thinks anyone has to choose, so he doesn\'t rank it below the unfinished version."]],"reading-0072":["The last paragraph worries that a new listener \\"may never sit in that uncertain quiet.\\" The word \\"that\\" points back to a quiet the passage has already described: the first paragraph\'s silence after the players stop, which the author heard at nineteen and found \\"the most honest sound in the piece.\\" So his concern is that others will miss an experience he valued. You need both paragraphs to see this. The Lisowski quote and the \\"exaggerate\\" claim are real details, but they explain other parts of his argument.",["Not in the passage: no reviews of the completion are mentioned.","Real quote, wrong link: her statement explains why he admires the completion, not why he worries about new listeners.","Correct: \\"that uncertain quiet\\" a new listener may miss is the same silence he heard at nineteen and called \\"the most honest sound in the piece.\\"","A view from the second paragraph about OTHER completions; his worry about new listeners is about losing the silence, not about exaggeration."]],"reading-0073":["The author\'s worry is that a completion \\"has a way of becoming simply the piece.\\" The applause detail shows what that looks like: with a real final chord, the audience knows exactly when to clap, while for sixty years audiences of the unfinished version sat in uncertain silence. So the detail makes the change in the listener\'s experience concrete. The enjoyment choice is the near miss; applause here signals that people knew the piece was over, not that they liked it more.",["Correct: applause \\"the moment the final chord faded\\" replaces the uncertain silence of the unfinished version, which is the change the author worries about.","Near miss: the contrast is about knowing WHEN to applaud, not about enjoying one version more.","Misreading: the audience applauded after the final chord, at the real end; nothing is criticized.","Not supported: no other completions of this quartet are mentioned, and popularity isn\'t discussed."]],"reading-0074":["A footnote is extra material that sits visibly apart from the main text, so no reader confuses the two. That matches what the third paragraph describes: Lisowski\'s music \\"changes character at once\\" where the manuscript stops, and she wanted \\"no one to mistake my bars for his.\\" The trap choices each grab a different idea people connect with footnotes: being minor, being scholarly, or explaining. None of those fits what the passage says her music does.",["Everyday meaning trap: \\"just a footnote\\" can mean unimportant, but the author is won over (\\"disarmed\\") by it.","Associated idea: footnotes appear in scholarly writing, but the passage says nothing about her research.","Near miss: some footnotes explain, but her plain bars don\'t claim to say what he meant; she avoids sounding like him.","Correct: like a footnote, it is clearly marked off from the main text; she wanted \\"no one to mistake my bars for his.\\""]],"reading-0075":["The passage moves from the author\'s first experience of the unfinished quartet, to his expectation that he\'d dislike the completion, to being won over, to a closing request: keep playing both versions. The second choice covers that whole path. The third choice is the strongest trap because the passage does compare Lisowski\'s approach with most completions, but that comparison is a step along the way, not the point the essay builds to.",["Reversal: the author admires this completion and says no one has to choose between the versions.","Correct: it covers his change of mind (prepared to dislike it, then disarmed) and his closing request that quartets keep playing both.","Too narrow: the contrast with other completions is in the second and third paragraphs, but it isn\'t where the passage ends up.","Too narrow: audience reactions appear only in the first and last paragraphs, as support for his larger point."]],"reading-0076":["\\"Anyway\\" means \\"even so\\": the notes were useless as data, and she kept them regardless. The paragraph opens with \\"The volunteers taught me something, too,\\" which tells you she saw value in their new habit of listening to everything around them. The \\"future study\\" choice ignores \\"None of it was data I could use.\\" The \\"not discouraged\\" choice invents a motive; keeping the sheets is a private choice, and the passage never connects it to the volunteers\' feelings.",["Contradicted: she says plainly that none of it was data she could use.","Unsupported motive: nothing suggests the volunteers knew or cared whether she kept the sheets.","Correct: \\"The volunteers taught me something, too\\" frames the extra notes as something she valued, apart from the data.","Not supported: she never compares the reliability of the notes and the counts."]],"reading-0077":["The sentence before says volunteers \\"go where it is easy to go, when the weather is pleasant, and... report what excites them.\\" The map image sums up the first of these: sightings pile up where people already spend time, so the map partly shows human habits. The trap is the excitement choice; that bias is real and is mentioned right before, but a map of WHERE sightings happen illustrates the location problem, not the excitement problem.",["Too literal: dog walking stands for the places people usually go, not for pets bothering wildlife.","Too strong: the author says volunteers should not be discouraged.","Real claim, wrong detail: excitement is a separate habit in the same sentence; the map image is about WHERE people look.","Correct: it pictures the earlier claim that volunteers go where it is easy to go."]],"reading-0078":["In Passage A, first-year volunteers almost never turned in a sheet for a silent night, because it felt like failure. Passage B says the record of \\"looking and finding nothing\\" is what most corrections depend on, and that many volunteers don\'t think it worth reporting; it even says an empty night is worth more than a dozen excited sightings. So Passage B\'s author would see the habit as understandable but costly. The \\"minor problem\\" choice is the closest trap: location bias is one of Passage B\'s concerns, but Passage B ranks missing empty-night reports above it, and Passage A\'s volunteers didn\'t choose their ponds at all.",["Training appears only in Passage A, and it was about telling calls apart; Passage B says volunteers shouldn\'t be discouraged.","Correct: Passage A\'s volunteers skipped silent nights, and Passage B calls a reported empty night the most valuable record.","Reversal: Passage B argues a silent night tells an analyst whether frogs were absent.","Wrong ranking: Passage B says an empty night is worth more than a dozen sightings, and Passage A\'s ponds were assigned, not chosen."]],"reading-0079":["Look for an idea in both passages. Passage A: in the first year, \\"almost no one turned in a sheet for a night when nothing called,\\" and the author had to persuade them otherwise. Passage B: a record of finding nothing is what volunteers are \\"least likely to send in,\\" since many \\"do not think [it] worth reporting.\\" Both describe volunteers undervaluing empty results. The \\"choose where\\" choice is a classic one-passage trap: Passage B implies it, but Passage A\'s volunteers were assigned their ponds.",["Correct: Passage A\'s volunteers skipped silent nights; Passage B says many don\'t think empty records worth reporting.","Neither author says this; Passage B says volunteers shouldn\'t be discouraged, and Passage A uses their counts.","Only Passage B suggests this; in Passage A, each volunteer went to an assigned pond.","Passage B mentions excitement as a bias, but neither passage compares volunteers with professionals."]],"reading-0080":["Check each choice against both passages. Passage A opens with the routine: drive to an assigned pond, turn off the engine, stand for ten minutes, write down the calls. Passage B never describes what a volunteer actually does; it only recommends what forms should record. The \\"reluctance\\" choice is the trap: it is in Passage A, but Passage B covers it too. The other two choices are in Passage B only. Notice that Passage A\'s author persuades volunteers to report silent nights but never says why they matter; Passage B is the one that explains it.",["Backward: this is in Passage B (an analyst can\'t otherwise tell absence from no one looking); Passage A never explains why.","Backward: only Passage B makes this claim.","Correct: Passage A gives the step-by-step routine (drive, engine off, ten minutes, write it down); Passage B only says what forms should ask.","In both: Passage A\'s first-year volunteers almost never turned in a sheet for a silent night, and Passage B says many volunteers don\'t think an empty record worth reporting."]],"reading-0081":["The third paragraph lists three customers in order: Mrs. Abernathy with loafers, a boy with baseball cleats with a torn strap, and a stranger with black dress shoes. The wrong choices attach the other customers\' shoes to the boy, or borrow the word \\"rubber\\" from the last paragraph.",["Wrong customer: the loafers belonged to Mrs. Abernathy.","Wrong customer: the dress shoes came from the man the narrator didn\'t recognize.","Correct: the third paragraph says the boy dropped off baseball cleats with a torn strap.","Not in the passage: \\"rubber\\" appears only in the bits swept from under the bench."]],"reading-0082":["The grandfather\'s whole reason is in one line: \\"Print ... Nobody can read your cursive.\\" The other choices use real details from the story (his slanted hand, the back room, the ticket box) but none of them is the reason he gives.",["Correct: he says, \\"Nobody can read your cursive.\\"","Twisted detail: his handwriting is described as small and slanted, but no one complains about it.","Not in the passage: he works in the back room, but nothing says tickets are read from there.","Not in the passage: the box is mentioned, but not as a reason to print."]],"reading-0083":["The grandfather explains himself: \\"A man shouldn\'t lose his good shoes just because I\'m tired.\\" He\'s worried that a customer who doesn\'t pick up in time will lose his shoes when the shop closes, so he promises to deliver them. The strongest trap is the regret choice. The narrator does decide, at the very end, that the remark came close to saying he was sorry to leave, but that\'s the narrator\'s later reading, not the reason the grandfather wrote the line. The first choice ignores his nod, and the second turns \\"deliver\\" into a whole new repair business.",["Contradicted: the narrator checked the calendar before saying \\"Thursday,\\" and the grandfather nodded in approval.","Too far: he offers to deliver unclaimed shoes, not to keep doing repairs, and the man is a stranger, not a regular.","Confuses effect with motive: the narrator later reads regret into his words, but his stated reason is about the man\'s shoes.","Correct: he says, \\"A man shouldn\'t lose his good shoes just because I\'m tired.\\""]],"reading-0084":["The ending says the remark was the first time the narrator had heard the grandfather say he was tired, and \\"the closest he had ever come to saying he was sorry to leave.\\" So the point is that a small remark let out more feeling than he normally showed. The trap is the first choice, which goes too far: \\"the closest he had ever come to saying\\" means he never actually said it. The other choices either contradict the opening or focus on the delivery plan, which isn\'t what the last two sentences are about.",["Too strong: the narrator says it was \\"the closest he had ever come to saying\\" it, which means he didn\'t actually say it.","Correct: he had never before said he was tired, and his words came close to expressing a sadness he never stated outright.","Contradicted: the first sentence calls it \\"the last Saturday before the shop closed,\\" so the narrator knew.","Misplaced focus: the delivery offer comes earlier, and the last sentences are about his feelings, not his work plans."]],"reading-0085":["The end of the second paragraph gives the numbers: the share returned \\"has roughly doubled ... from about one in five to about two in five.\\" The first choice flips the direction. The \\"nineteen in twenty\\" choice twists the umbrella statistic from the third paragraph.",["Reversed: the share went up, not down.","Correct: the second paragraph says it roughly doubled, from about one in five to about two in five.","Wrong number: \\"one in twenty\\" is about umbrellas, and it describes how few are picked up.","Contradicted: the share roughly doubled."]],"reading-0086":["The third paragraph says it directly: \\"Umbrellas are the most commonly lost item and the least commonly retrieved.\\" Phones are the opposite case, since they\'re almost always claimed. Gloves and textbooks appear in the passage but not in this comparison.",["Opposite: phones are \\"almost always claimed within a day or two.\\"","Wrong detail: gloves are mentioned as a sign of cold weather, not as the most lost item.","Listed, but the passage says nothing about how often textbooks are lost or claimed.","Correct: the third paragraph says umbrellas are the most commonly lost and least commonly retrieved."]],"reading-0087":["The last paragraph begins, \\"Items that go unclaimed for ninety days are donated or auctioned.\\" The catalog and the ledger are real details from the second paragraph, but they describe how items are recorded, not what happens after ninety days.",["Not in the passage: nothing says items go back to stations.","Wrong time: photographing happens when items arrive, not after ninety days.","Correct: the last paragraph says unclaimed items are donated or auctioned.","Outdated: hand ledgers were the office\'s old method of recording items."]],"reading-0088":["Look at what each paragraph does: the first shows the office and its odd contents, the second its changing methods, the third which items people bother to claim, and the last what the manager thinks the office reveals about the city. Only the first choice covers that whole arc. The \\"explain\\" and \\"trace\\" choices are each true of one paragraph. The \\"argue\\" choice takes a fact (more items returned) and stretches it into a claim the passage never makes.",["Correct: the passage describes the office, its methods, and what riders claim, and ends with the manager\'s view that it records \\"how people live.\\"","Too strong: the catalog raised the share of items returned, but nothing says riders treat their things differently, and the passage doesn\'t argue.","Too narrow: this is the topic of the third paragraph only.","Too narrow: the change in methods is the second paragraph; the passage goes on to claiming habits and the office as a window into the city."]],"reading-0089":["The guard\'s report is in the third paragraph: visitors kept asking \\"why was the girl holding a dead bird?\\" Each wrong choice comes from somewhere else in the passage: the label writers (first paragraph), the painter\'s training (the old label), and the window (the new label\'s ending).",["Twisted detail: the first paragraph says most visitors never think about who writes the labels.","Correct: the third paragraph quotes their question: \\"why was the girl holding a dead bird?\\"","Wrong source: the painter\'s training was the subject of the author\'s first label, not the visitors\' question.","Wrong detail: the window is something the new label points out; most visitors had never looked at it."]],"reading-0090":["The third paragraph says the first label had \\"three careful sentences about the painter\'s training in a regional workshop.\\" Everything in the other choices (the bird, the girl\'s death, the window) belongs to the rewritten label. Keep track of which version of the label a detail comes from.",["Wrong version: the dead bird is what the rewritten label explains.","Wrong version: the girl\'s death appears in the rewritten label.","Correct: the first label was \\"three careful sentences about the painter\'s training in a regional workshop.\\"","Wrong version: the new label ends by pointing out the window."]],"reading-0091":["The third paragraph teaches a lesson: answer the visitor\'s real question. The fourth opens with \\"I don\'t mean that labels should only answer the obvious,\\" which is a warning not to take that lesson too far. Then it adds a second step (lead the eye somewhere new) and uses the window as the example. That\'s a qualification. The most tempting wrong choice treats the window as the explanation for why people linger, but the passage never draws that link, and the paragraph\'s opening words show its real job.",["Same example: the paragraph is still about the portrait of the merchant\'s daughter, not a new label.","A detail, not the job: the window may help hold attention, but the paragraph exists to refine the author\'s rule, as \\"I don\'t mean\\" signals.","Correct: \\"I don\'t mean that labels should only answer the obvious\\" limits the earlier lesson, and the window shows the second step.","Wrong paragraph and reversed: the colleagues appear in the last paragraph, where the author disagrees with them."]],"reading-0092":["In the last paragraph the author rejects the worry that her labels talk down to visitors. Writing for forty words of attention, she says, \\"is not simplifying the art. It is taking the visitor\'s question as seriously as the scholar\'s.\\" So a short label can be every bit as serious as scholarship. The trap is the \\"more valuable\\" choice: it points the right way but goes too far. \\"As seriously as\\" means equal, not greater. The \\"only simplify\\" choice is the very view she rejects.",["Too strong: she says the visitor\'s question is rarely the scholar\'s, not that scholars\' questions should be dropped.","Too strong: she says the visitor\'s question deserves to be taken \\"as seriously as\\" the scholar\'s, not more seriously.","Reversed: she says such writing \\"is not simplifying the art.\\"","Correct: she says writing for short attention is \\"not simplifying the art\\" but taking the visitor\'s question \\"as seriously as the scholar\'s.\\""]],"reading-0093":["The second paragraph opens, \\"A leaf is green because it is packed with chlorophyll.\\" Each wrong choice is a real term from the passage with a different role: carotenoids (yellow and orange), anthocyanins (red and purple), and nitrogen (a nutrient).",["Wrong pigment: carotenoids are the yellow and orange pigments.","Correct: the second paragraph says a leaf is green because it is packed with chlorophyll.","Wrong pigment: anthocyanins make reds and purples.","Wrong substance: nitrogen is a nutrient the tree pulls out of the leaf, not a color."]],"reading-0094":["The third paragraph explains it: as the green fades, the carotenoids show through, and \\"the yellow of a birch ... is mostly the color the leaf had beneath its green all summer.\\" The \\"new pigment from sugars\\" choice describes anthocyanins, which make reds. The nitrogen choice runs backward, since the tree takes nitrogen out of the leaf.",["Correct: the third paragraph says birch yellow is \\"mostly the color the leaf had beneath its green all summer.\\"","Wrong pigment: making new pigment from sugars is how reds and purples form.","Reversed: the tree pulls nitrogen out of the leaf.","Mixed-up details: sunlight does break down chlorophyll, but that isn\'t what makes a leaf yellow, and cool nights matter for reds."]],"reading-0095":["The third paragraph lists what the tree does: \\"It stops replacing chlorophyll and starts breaking it down, pulling valuable nutrients such as nitrogen back into its twigs and trunk.\\" The wrong choices reverse that (making more chlorophyll), move the sugars, or forget that carotenoids were there all along.",["Reversed: the tree stops replacing chlorophyll and breaks it down.","Not in the passage: the red pigment is made from sugars already in the leaf, not sugars sent from the trunk.","Contradicted: carotenoids are in the leaf all summer; they\'re only hidden.","Correct: the third paragraph says it stops replacing chlorophyll and pulls nutrients such as nitrogen back."]],"reading-0096":["To check the claim, you need two paragraphs. The third says the yellows and oranges come from carotenoids that were in the leaf all summer, hidden by chlorophyll. The fourth begins \\"Reds and purples are different\\": they come from anthocyanins that many trees make in the fall. So the claim holds for yellows and oranges but not for most reds. That\'s why the author wrote \\"much of that color,\\" not \\"all.\\" The \\"all of the fall colors\\" choice is the trap for readers who stop after the third paragraph.",["Reversed: reds and purples are the colors many trees make new in the fall.","Too broad: this ignores the fourth paragraph, where anthocyanins are newly made, which is why the author says \\"much\\" and not \\"all.\\"","Correct: carotenoids are hidden all summer, but the fourth paragraph says reds come from anthocyanins made in the fall.","Too strong: yellows and oranges were present all summer; they just weren\'t visible."]],"reading-0097":["Passage A says she \\"stripped seven layers of paint from the stair rail to find its first color, a dull green.\\" The other choices borrow nearby details (the porch boards, the modern kitchen, the year 1910) and attach them to the wrong task.",["Correct: she stripped the paint \\"to find its first color, a dull green.\\"","Mixed-up detail: the porch boards are a separate project, and nothing says the rail had to match them.","Reversed: she was restoring the original look, not modernizing.","Wrong detail: the passage gives 1910 as the year built, but she didn\'t learn it from the paint."]],"reading-0098":["The second paragraph of Passage B says many mills \\"have survived because someone turned them into apartments, offices, or schools.\\" The wrong choices describe the opposite approach, keeping buildings unchanged or unused, which the author says puts them at risk.",["Correct: Passage B says many mills survived \\"because someone turned them into apartments, offices, or schools.\\"","Wrong view: returning buildings to their original state is the tradition the author argues against.","Reversed: the author says buildings that can\'t change are unlikely to survive.","Contradicted: the author says a building no one uses is unlikely to survive for long."]],"reading-0099":["Check each choice against both passages. The two approaches and the claim that unchangeable buildings don\'t survive are both only in Passage B. Someone who questions the author shows up in both: the neighbor in A and the purists in B, so that one fails the \\"not in B\\" test. Only A uses a former occupant\'s own words, the grandmother\'s letters, to explain why the house was changed.",["Only in Passage B: its first paragraph lays out the two traditions.","In both: Passage A has the neighbor who asks about a modern kitchen, and Passage B has the purists who object.","Only in Passage B: this is its main argument.","Correct: the grandmother\'s letters explain the plumbing and the removed wall; Passage B quotes no occupant."]],"reading-0100":["Passage B\'s author sides with the view that \\"the changes a building has gone through are part of its history,\\" and he says authenticity lies in \\"the continuous life it has carried.\\" The grandmother knocked out the wall so her family could eat together, which is that kind of lived-in history, so he would approve. The trap is the third choice: it has the right verdict, but it borrows the idea of adaptive reuse, which is about giving a building a new purpose. The farmhouse is still a home, so that reason doesn\'t fit. The first choice is the purists\' view, and the last misreads what he says about walls and windows.",["Wrong view: this is the purists\' position, which the author of Passage B argues against.","Correct: B\'s author holds that a building\'s changes are part of its history and that authenticity lies in \\"the continuous life it has carried.\\"","Right verdict, wrong reason: the house is still a family home; keeping the wall open doesn\'t give it a new purpose like a mill turned into apartments.","Misreads B: the author says reuse keeps the walls and windows, not that authenticity lies in them; he places it in a building\'s continuous life."]],"reading-0101":["The first paragraph lists the week in order: Monday sanding, Tuesday taping, Wednesday rolling on a fresh coat. So on Tuesday he taped the boat. The other choices are real steps from the passage, just on the wrong day.",["Wrong day: Teo spent Monday sanding.","Correct: the first paragraph says Teo spent \\"Tuesday taping.\\"","Wrong day: he rolled on the fresh coat on Wednesday.","Wrong day: he drew the letters \\"the night before\\" name day, which was Thursday."]],"reading-0102":["Right after the narrator finishes painting, Teo \\"studied it for a long time\\" and then says \\"Good.\\" He is looking carefully at the name to judge how it turned out. The other choices are meanings of \\"study\\" that fit school, not this moment: he isn\'t memorizing, researching, or practicing anything.",["Correct: Teo is examining the newly painted name to see how well it came out.","Common meaning, wrong here: Teo isn\'t trying to learn the name by heart; he already knows it.","Common meaning, wrong here: there\'s nothing to look up; he is looking at the paint job in front of him.","Wrong meaning: the narrator did the painting; Teo is only looking at the result."]],"reading-0103":["The last paragraph moves ahead in time: \\"He left on Sunday. In the spring...\\" It is in the spring, when the salt has dulled the letters, that the narrator goes to the shed and finds the enamel and the wrapped brushes. The other choices are real moments from the story, but at each of them something else happens.",["Wrong time: the night before name day is when Teo drew the letters in pencil.","Wrong time: on Thursday she used the enamel on the dock; she found it in the shed later.","Wrong time: Sunday is when Teo left, not when she went to the shed.","Correct: the last paragraph says that in the spring, when the letters had dulled, she went to the shed and found the enamel."]],"reading-0104":["The narrator tells us right away that Teo\'s hands don\'t shake: she has watched him make free throws with the whole gym yelling. So \\"My hands shake\\" is an excuse, not the truth. The real reason comes at the end of the scene: \\"Now you know how. Next time it fades, it\'s yours.\\" He wanted her to learn the job because he\'s leaving, and the wrapped brushes in the shed show he left everything ready for her. The first choice is tempting because he is leaving, but he isn\'t nervous; he gives calm advice the whole morning. The last choice takes the excuse at face value.",["Unsupported: the narrator points out that his hands don\'t shake, and nothing shows he doubts himself; he even coaches her.","Wrong detail: the scrape is mentioned only to show the boat didn\'t need paint; nothing links it to his handing over the brush.","Correct: after she finishes, he says, \\"Now you know how. Next time it fades, it\'s yours,\\" and he leaves the supplies ready for her.","Takes him literally: the narrator says \\"They didn\'t\\" shake and recalls his steady free throws, so it\'s an excuse, not his real belief."]],"reading-0105":["The third paragraph describes the two clinics. The first has a bare room with no information. The second has a screen showing each patient\'s place in line and a nurse taking blood pressure readings. The trap is \\"a shorter wait\\": the passage says the waiting times are identical. That\'s the whole point of the example.",["Correct: the third paragraph says that at the second clinic, a screen shows each patient\'s place in line.","Contradicts the passage: the clinics have identical waiting times, and no one at the second clinic is seen sooner.","Not in the passage: reading is mentioned in Maister\'s first principle, but the clinics don\'t offer magazines.","Wrong clinic: the bare room with no information is the first clinic."]],"reading-0106":["Maister\'s two principles say a wait feels longer when it is unoccupied and when its length is unknown. Check each choice for those two conditions. Reading a magazine after being told \\"ten minutes\\" has neither, so it would feel shortest. Filling out a form with no time given has one (uncertain). Standing idle after being told \\"ten minutes\\" has one (unoccupied). Standing idle with no idea how long it will take has both, so it would feel longest.",["Feels shortest: this wait is both occupied and known, so both principles make it feel briefer.","Only half as bad: the wait is uncertain, but filling out a form keeps the person occupied.","Only half as bad: the person is unoccupied, but knowing the length makes the wait easier to bear.","Correct: this wait is both unoccupied and uncertain, the two conditions the principles say make a wait feel longer."]],"reading-0107":["Look at what each paragraph does. The first sets up the gap between how long a wait is and how long it feels. The second gives Maister\'s two principles that explain the gap. The third uses the clinics to show the principles in action. The fourth says organizations should pay attention to this. Together, that\'s explaining the gap and why it matters. The first choice grabs one idea from the last paragraph and turns the passage into an attack. The third is too broad, and the fourth gets the clinic example backward.",["Too narrow, wrong verb: the last paragraph warns that disguised waits are still long, but the passage as a whole explains rather than criticizes.","Correct: the passage presents Maister\'s principles about how waits feel, gives the clinic example, and ends by saying organizations miss something if they measure only the clock.","Too broad: the passage discusses only Maister\'s essay and two of its principles, not decades of research.","Misreads the example: the two clinics have identical waiting times; neither one reduced them."]],"reading-0108":["The last paragraph does two things. First it adds a limit: \\"None of this means that managers can ignore actual waiting times,\\" because a disguised long wait is still long. Then it comes back to the main point: the experience of waiting is \\"shaped by more than the minutes.\\" The key captures both moves. The first choice is the trap. It reads the warning as a rejection of Maister\'s ideas, but the paragraph keeps them and only says they aren\'t an excuse for long waits.",["Too strong: the paragraph warns that disguising a long wait doesn\'t make it short, but it still says the experience of waiting \\"is shaped by more than the minutes.\\"","Not in the passage: the paragraph adds a caution, not a new principle.","Correct: it says managers can\'t ignore real waiting times, then says organizations that measure only the clock miss part of the picture.","No example: the paragraph speaks in general terms; the only example in the passage is the two clinics."]],"reading-0109":["The first paragraph says Wynne \\"called it a \'word-cross.\'\\" The trap is \\"crossword,\\" the name everyone uses now, which flips the two parts. The other choices are phrases from later in the passage that describe clues, not the puzzle.",["Close but reversed: \\"crossword\\" is today\'s name; the passage says Wynne called it a \\"word-cross.\\"","Wrong phrase: \\"fair\\" is the author\'s word for a good misleading clue, not Wynne\'s name for the puzzle.","Correct: the first paragraph says Wynne \\"called it a \'word-cross.\'\\"","Wrong phrase: \\"plain definition\\" describes the kind of clue in early puzzles, not the puzzle\'s name."]],"reading-0110":["The author\'s favorite clues \\"tell the truth while pointing the solver in the wrong direction,\\" and the last paragraph says this makes a crossword \\"a small lesson in reading.\\" That is the main idea. The third and fourth choices take the side the author argues against. The second makes a comparison the passage never makes.",["Correct: the author praises clues that \\"tell the truth while pointing the solver in the wrong direction\\" and ends by calling a crossword \\"a small lesson in reading.\\"","Not in the passage: the author never compares how hard the first puzzle was.","Reverses the author\'s view: the author grants the critics \\"have a point\\" but argues that the best misleading clues do the opposite.","Reverses the author\'s view: the author says a misleading clue asks \\"a better question\\" than a plain definition."]],"reading-0111":["Right before the example, the author says the best clues \\"tell the truth while pointing the solver in the wrong direction.\\" DESK is that wrong direction: a sensible answer about a classroom pupil, when the real answer is about the pupil of an eye. So the example shows how an accurate clue leads to a reasonable mistake. The first choice is the strongest trap, but the author says right after that such a clue is \\"fair.\\"",["Reverses the author\'s view: the next paragraph calls this kind of clue \\"fair,\\" and the crossing words settle the answer.","Too broad: the example is about one tricky clue, not about how most solvers think.","Misreads the detail: the crossing words help the solver find IRIS; they aren\'t presented as the hard part.","Correct: DESK is a sensible guess for \\"Where a pupil sits,\\" which shows how a truthful clue points the solver the wrong way."]],"reading-0112":["The fourth paragraph has a turn in it. First the author concedes: \\"They have a point,\\" because a puzzle that reuses the same few jokes becomes \\"a test of membership rather than of wit.\\" Then comes \\"But\\": the best misleading clues \\"do the opposite.\\" So the author grants the complaint for some puzzles and defends the best clues. The first choice ignores the concession. The third accepts the concession but adds advice the author never gives and drops the \\"But.\\"",["Too strong: the author says the critics \\"have a point,\\" which is the opposite of dismissing them.","Correct: \\"They have a point\\" about puzzles that rely on the same few jokes, \\"But I think the best misleading clues do the opposite.\\"","Half right: the author does grant the worry about tricks, but says it applies to some puzzles, not to misleading clues in general, and never suggests using them less.","Wrong time: early puzzles used plain definitions, so the complaint about tricks can\'t be about them."]],"reading-0113":["The third paragraph says the 2000 team \\"filmed the snap with a high-speed camera while recording it with an underwater microphone.\\" Each wrong choice borrows a real word from another part of the passage: sonar from World War II, propellers from the engineers, and light and temperature from the later work.",["Wrong detail: sonar comes up in the first paragraph, about World War II, not the 2000 study.","Wrong detail: propellers are mentioned only to explain why engineers try to prevent cavitation.","Wrong time: the flash of light and the heat were found in later work, and the passage doesn\'t say how.","Correct: the third paragraph says the team filmed the snap with a high-speed camera while recording it with an underwater microphone."]],"reading-0114":["Engineers \\"work to prevent\\" cavitation because collapsing bubbles \\"can pit the metal of ship propellers.\\" So \\"pit\\" names a kind of damage: making small holes or dents in the surface. \\"Set against\\" and \\"remove the seeds from\\" are real meanings of \\"pit,\\" but they don\'t fit metal being damaged.",["Common meaning, wrong here: \\"pit one team against another\\" means \\"set against,\\" but nothing is being set against the metal.","Correct: collapsing bubbles damage the propeller\'s surface, leaving small dents and holes.","Common meaning, wrong here: you pit a cherry, but metal has no seeds.","Wrong meaning: \\"pit\\" can name a hole in the ground, but the bubbles aren\'t burying anything."]],"reading-0115":["The old idea was that the sound came from the claw striking itself, like a clap. If that were true, the sound would happen the instant the claw closed. The 2000 study found the sound matched the moment the bubble collapsed, a fraction of a millisecond later. That timing is what rules out the old idea. The other choices are all true facts from the passage, but none of them is about when the sound happens, so none of them tests the old idea directly.",["Correct: if the sound came from the claw striking itself, it would match the claw closing; it matched the bubble\'s collapse instead.","True but not proof: the jet explains how the bubble forms, but on its own it doesn\'t show where the sound comes from.","True but off target: the flash is a separate effect of the collapse and says nothing about the timing of the sound.","True but off target: this shows how loud the shrimp are, not what causes the sound."]],"reading-0116":["The final paragraph begins with \\"then,\\" a signal that it\'s summing up. Its last sentence puts the main finding in everyday words: the shrimp makes a bubble, and the bubble makes the noise. The first choice is the trap. It goes too far, because the claw still matters: it shoots out the jet that forms the bubble. The passage only says the sound doesn\'t come from the claw hitting itself.",["Too strong: the claw shoots out the jet that makes the bubble; it just isn\'t what makes the sound.","Wrong part of the passage: the contrast with engineers comes in the fourth paragraph, and engineers prevent cavitation rather than use it.","Correct: it sums up the 2000 finding, that the sound comes from the bubble the claw creates, in simple words.","Not in the passage: the sentence states a conclusion; it doesn\'t ask anything."]],"reading-0117":["The first paragraph of Passage A ends: \\"Under \'how much,\' she wrote, \'Enough.\'\\" The wrong choices are other words Halina writes or says: \\"if you have it\\" goes with the sour cream, \\"one egg\\" is an ingredient, and \\"Feel\\" is what she tells the narrator at the counter.",["Wrong line: \\"if you have it\\" is what she writes about the sour cream in the ingredient list.","Wrong moment: \\"Feel\\" is what she says out loud at the counter the next Sunday.","Wrong line: \\"one egg\\" is part of the ingredient list, not the \\"how much\\" line.","Correct: the first paragraph says, \\"Under \'how much,\' she wrote, \'Enough.\'\\""]],"reading-0118":["Check each choice against both passages. The Polanyi quotation and the cyclist are only in Passage B. Halina\'s card is only in Passage A. Only the phrase \\"soft but not sticky\\" shows up in both: Halina\'s dough in Passage A, and Passage B\'s example of a description that means little to a beginner.",["Only in Passage B: Polanyi is quoted in Passage B, not Passage A.","Only in Passage A: Passage B talks about written recipes in general, never a card.","Correct: Passage A says the dough was \\"soft but not sticky,\\" and Passage B uses the phrase \\"soft but not sticky\\" as an example.","Only in Passage B: the cyclist is Passage B\'s example of knowing more than you can tell."]],"reading-0119":["Passage A ends with the card being accurate but not \\"enough,\\" because the narrator had to learn the feel of the dough by hand. Passage B makes the general version of that point: a recipe is \\"the beginning of instruction, not the whole of it.\\" So both agree that a written recipe can\'t hold everything a cook needs to know. The third choice is the trap for readers who skim Passage B: it says photographs and descriptions \\"help,\\" but the next word is \\"Yet.\\" The last choice fits Passage A\'s story, but Passage B never talks about learning from family.",["The author of Passage A would disagree: she calls Halina\'s card \\"accurate, every word of it\\"; the problem is that it isn\'t enough.","Correct: Passage A\'s card \\"isn\'t enough,\\" and Passage B calls a recipe \\"the beginning of instruction, not the whole of it.\\"","Passage B rejects this: such details \\"help,\\" \\"yet\\" a description still means little to someone who has never felt the dough.","Only Passage A suggests this: Passage B never mentions relatives and says written recipes still have value."]],"reading-0120":["Passage B is built on Polanyi\'s line \\"we can know more than we can tell.\\" Halina knows exactly when to stop adding water, since she stops at a moment the narrator \\"could not see coming,\\" but she can\'t turn that into a number, so she writes \\"Enough.\\" That is knowledge that can\'t be fully told. The second choice gets it backward: Halina knows the amount, she just can\'t state it. The third choice reads her frown as stubbornness, but she spends the winter teaching the narrator. The last choice contradicts Passage B\'s own words.",["Correct: this is Polanyi\'s idea that \\"we can know more than we can tell\\": Halina knows when the dough is right, but she can\'t write it as an amount.","Reverses Passage B\'s idea: the point is that skilled people know more than they can say, not that they don\'t know.","Misreads Passage A: Halina frowns at the card, but she writes the recipe and then teaches the narrator by hand.","Too strong: Passage B says \\"This does not make written recipes useless.\\""]],"reading-0121":["Judge each half of a choice against its own passage. Passage A\'s author volunteers at the events and tells how a visitor who learned one repair came back to help others, so A presents them as help plus learning. Passage B opens by saying the events \\"deserve the praise they get,\\" then says it would be a mistake to treat them as a solution, because the real problem is how products are designed. The last choice is the strongest trap: its second half fits Passage B, but Passage A says nothing about laws, so it can\'t be comparing the events with laws.",["Reversed on both sides: A\'s author runs the events and clearly values them, and B\'s author says it would be \\"a mistake\\" to treat them as a solution to the design problem.","Neither passage makes cost the main point: A stresses learning to fix things, and B stresses landfills, skills, and design.","Correct: A shows people getting help and learning (Ruth), and B praises the events but says the real problem is design.","Half right: B does put laws first, but A never mentions laws, so it can\'t treat the events as a substitute for them."]],"reading-0122":["Go through B\'s claims and look for a matching example in A. B lists three design problems: sealed batteries, glue instead of screws, and fasteners that need special tools. A\'s third paragraph gives one example: phone cases that are \\"glued shut,\\" so opening one usually means breaking it. That matches glue, not special tools, which A never mentions. A never brings up laws, and a single event in Larch Falls can\'t show that events have spread to many countries.",["Passage A describes one monthly event in one town, which says nothing about events spreading to many countries.","Correct: A\'s phones, with cases glued shut so that opening one usually breaks it, are an example of this.","Passage A never mentions laws.","Close, but A\'s phones are hard to fix because of glue, not because of special tools."]],"reading-0123":["Passage B\'s second paragraph says the problem is \\"largely one of design,\\" lists parts joined with glue as an example, and says a volunteer can do a great deal \\"but not much\\" against such choices. The glued-shut phones in Passage A fit that exactly. The laws choice is tempting, but B says laws change \\"what the next toaster will be,\\" not what is already broken. B never says repair events are failing, and \\"a growing share\\" of hard-to-repair products is the opposite of rare exceptions.",["B says such laws \\"do not make a single broken toaster easier to fix today\\"; they change future products.","B says a \\"growing share\\" of products are built to resist repair, so it would not call these phones rare exceptions.","Too strong: B says the events \\"deserve the praise they get\\"; it wants laws as well, not instead.","Correct: B blames design, such as parts joined with glue, and says a volunteer can do \\"not much\\" against it."]],"reading-0124":["Track what each paragraph does. The first sets up the problem (traffic covers low notes). The second gives the obvious explanation (birds move their songs up). The third raises a complication (higher notes might be a side effect of singing loudly) and says how to tell the two ideas apart. The fourth describes an experiment that does that. The \\"argue that the obvious explanation is wrong\\" choice is the trap: the passage questions that explanation, but it never says it\'s wrong, and the data in Table 1 actually fit it.",["Correct: the passage gives the obvious explanation, raises the loudness complication, and ends with an experiment built to tell the two apart.","Wrong verb: the passage raises loudness as a possible cause but never rejects the obvious explanation, and the data in Table 1 actually fit it.","Mixes up details: the experiment compared kinds of played noise for birds of one species, not city birds and forest birds.","Too narrow: the first paragraph mentions going unheard by a mate, but the passage is about why songs get higher, not a warning."]],"reading-0125":["Use the test the third paragraph gives: if higher notes were only a side effect of loudness, any noise that makes birds sing equally loudly should raise their notes by the same amount. In Table 1, the birds sang at 78 decibels in both kinds of noise, yet the lowest note rose from 2.4 to 2.9 kHz in low-pitched noise and only to 2.5 kHz in high-pitched noise. Same loudness, different rises, so loudness alone can\'t explain it. The \\"Yes\\" choices state true facts from the table that don\'t settle the question.",["True of the table (78 vs. 70 decibels), but louder singing in noise fits both explanations, so it can\'t decide between them.","True (2.9 and 2.5 vs. 2.4), but the test in the third paragraph is whether the rise is the same in both noises, and it isn\'t.","Misreads the table: loudness was 78 decibels in both kinds of noise, so extra loudness can\'t be why the rise was bigger in low-pitched noise.","Correct: equal loudness should give equal rises if loudness were the only cause, but the rise was 0.5 kHz in low-pitched noise and only 0.1 kHz in high-pitched noise."]],"reading-0126":["The high-pitched condition works as a control for loudness. The birds sang just as loudly (78 decibels), but that noise was above their songs, so it covered none of their notes and gave them no reason to move their songs. There the lowest note rose only 0.1 kHz (2.4 to 2.5). By the assumption in the question, singing at 78 decibels adds the same 0.1 kHz in low-pitched noise. The total rise there was 0.5 kHz (2.4 to 2.9), so about 0.5 - 0.1 = 0.4 kHz came from something else, most likely the birds moving their songs away from the noise.",["That would need the same rise in both noises, but the rise was 0.5 kHz in low-pitched noise and only 0.1 kHz in high-pitched noise.","This is the part due to loudness alone, the rise in high-pitched noise (2.5 - 2.4). The question asks for the rest.","Correct: the total rise is 2.9 - 2.4 = 0.5 kHz, and loudness alone accounts for 0.1 kHz (the high-pitched condition), leaving 0.4 kHz.","This ignores that singing at 78 decibels raised the note 0.1 kHz even in high-pitched noise, where nothing pushed the songs up."]],"reading-0127":["The last paragraph says Bea was waiting for the narrator \\"to stop having to think about it,\\" not to get it right. So look for the moment when parking stops taking thought. On the sixth try she parks well, but Bea says \\"Again,\\" so that isn\'t it. Around the tenth try her hands loosen and she feels the car\'s corner the way you feel your own elbow in the dark, without looking or thinking. That is the change. \\"Enough\\" comes after it, and the drive home is when she understands it.",["This is when she first gets it right, and the last paragraph says Bea was never waiting for that; Bea answers it with another \\"Again.\\"","Correct: by about the tenth try she is driving by feel, the way you know where your elbow is, which is parking without having to think about it.","This is when Bea ends the lesson, a sign the change has already happened; it isn\'t the change itself.","This is when the narrator starts to understand what Bea wanted, not when the change in her driving happens."]],"reading-0128":["At the sixth try the narrator expected a verdict, got another \\"Again,\\" and wanted to protest that she had already done it. The last paragraph explains that moment: Bea \\"had never been waiting for me to get it right,\\" so a neat park wasn\'t the goal; she was studying whether it had become automatic yet. The first \\"Again\\" needs no new explanation, since that try put a tire on the curb. Bea\'s story about her own test isn\'t explained differently by the ending; it is what leads the narrator to understand.",["Correct: the narrator took this \\"Again\\" as a refusal to accept her success; she now sees that Bea wasn\'t judging success at all but waiting for parking to become automatic.","That \\"Again\\" makes sense either way, since that try failed, so the new understanding doesn\'t change its meaning.","This story leads to her understanding; it is the cause of her new view, not a moment the new view explains differently.","A detail about Bea\'s manner that the ending never comes back to."]],"reading-0129":["The first paragraph sets up an old assumption: life needs sunlight warming a surface, so icy moons far from the Sun don\'t count. The vents are \\"the first\\" of \\"two discoveries\\" that \\"changed that.\\" Their point is spelled out at the end of the paragraph: life \\"did not need a sun overhead,\\" just water, energy, and the right chemistry. That is why the icy moons come back into the picture. The other choices go beyond the text: the author never predicts what moon life would look like, never says the moons have vents, and never says why the spacecraft were launched.",["Overreach: the author never predicts what life on the moons would look like; the animals only show that vent ecosystems exist.","Unsupported cause: the passage never says why the spacecraft were sent, only what they later measured.","Wrong place: the vents described are on Earth; the passage never says the moons have vents.","Correct: the vents show life living on chemical energy without sunlight, which undoes the \\"warm places\\" thinking of the first paragraph."]],"reading-0130":["The last paragraph separates two ideas: a place being habitable (it has the conditions life needs) and a place being inhabited (life is really there). The open question, the author says, is \\"how often, given the ingredients, life actually begins.\\" Searching a habitable ocean carefully and finding nothing would be evidence that the start of life is rare, which is the \\"something\\" a null result \\"would tell us.\\" The strongest trap says the ocean isn\'t habitable, but the author warns against exactly that mix-up: an ocean can have every ingredient \\"and still be empty.\\"",["Correct: the author says a habitable ocean can still be empty, and that the moons matter because they test how often life begins once the ingredients are there.","Habitable-versus-inhabited trap: the author says \\"habitable\\" describes conditions, not residents, so an empty ocean could still be habitable.","Unsupported: the passage calls Earth the only place we know life began and never questions that.","Contradicts the passage: the second paragraph says vent microbes on Earth do live on chemical energy."]],"reading-0131":["The last paragraph says the moons matter because Earth gives us only one example of life beginning, and \\"one example cannot tell us whether the start of life is common or a rare accident.\\" What would answer it, the author says, is \\"a second, independent beginning.\\" Microbes that share Earth\'s origin would be exciting, but they would be the same beginning spread to a new place, so they\'d still leave us with one example. The tempting first choice skips the word \\"independent.\\" The last two choices misuse the habitable-versus-inhabited distinction: microbes living in the ocean would make it inhabited, which fits the author\'s point rather than contradicting it.",["Misses the key word: the author says only a \\"second, independent beginning\\" would answer the question; life with Earth\'s origin is the same beginning, found in a new place.","Correct: the author\'s question is whether life starting is common or a rare accident, and only an independent start would count as a second example.","No contradiction: finding residents in a habitable place fits the author\'s distinction between conditions and residents.","Contradicts the supposition: microbes living there would make the ocean inhabited."]],"reading-0132":["Look for the words that mark how sure the author is. Most statements in the second and third paragraphs are things people saw: the vents, the animals, the jets. The Europa ocean is different: \\"Measurements ... suggested\\" it lies beneath the ice. No one has seen it; it is a conclusion drawn from data, so the author presents it carefully. The Cassini choice is the trap, because the Enceladus ocean is also hedged in the passage (\\"appear to come from an ocean\\"), but this quotation stops before that part and describes only what Cassini spotted.",["Stated as plain fact: the author gives no hedge for the lack of sunlight at the vents\' depth.","A direct observation: scientists saw the vents themselves from the submersible.","A direct observation: Cassini \\"spotted\\" the jets; only the later claim about where its samples came from is hedged (\\"appear to come\\").","Correct: \\"suggested\\" marks the Europa ocean as an inference from measurements, not something anyone has seen."]],"reading-0133":["Notice what each passage is about. Passage B is about radio searches: directions, frequencies, signal strengths, a small sample of a huge haystack. Passage A\'s argument is about time and expansion. Given billions of years, a common kind of civilization should have spread everywhere, and its presence \\"should be hard to miss.\\" That argument doesn\'t depend on catching a signal, so saying the radio search is small doesn\'t answer it. Choice B fails because Passage A also uses estimates. Choice C has Passage A comment on searches it never mentions. Choice D gives Passage B an assumption it doesn\'t make.",["Correct: Passage A\'s case is about expansion; a civilization that \\"fill[ed] the galaxy many times over\\" would not have to be caught by a radio search to be noticed, so the size of the radio search doesn\'t answer it.","Passage A relies on estimates too (\\"a few million to a few hundred million years, depending on the assumptions\\"), so its author couldn\'t make this contrast.","Passage A never discusses searches or their growth, and Passage B says the larger search is \\"still a hot tub against an ocean.\\"","Passage B assumes no such thing; staying home is one of the explanations Passage A says believers in a crowded galaxy need."]],"reading-0134":["The last paragraph shows Dad went back and forth about the card: written, crossed out, written again. The passage gives one clue to why. Theo usually laughed at his father\'s careful habits, and Dad usually laughed along. A card holding a number Theo had known since he was four is more feeling than information, and Dad risked having it treated as a joke. That is why the narrator \\"waited for him to laugh.\\" Choice B is a believable worry for a parent, but nothing in the passage raises Theo\'s competence. Choice D gives a real line from the passage, \\"Phones die,\\" but that line explains making the card, not hesitating.",["Unsupported: nothing suggests the information was wrong; Dad wrote it in his careful fuse-box capitals.","Plausible in real life but not in this passage: Theo\'s competence is never questioned, and the passage points to a different risk, laughter.","Correct: Theo \\"laughed at everything Dad did that was careful,\\" and this card, holding a number Theo had known since he was four, was care meant seriously; Dad risked having it laughed off.","This is Dad\'s reason for making the card (\\"Phones die\\"), not a reason to hesitate over giving it."]],"reading-0135":["The key lines are the last two of Passage A: \\"Each such explanation can be made to work. That we need one at all is the point.\\" The first concedes something: the explanations are coherent. The second says that isn\'t what matters: a crowded galaxy needs a special excuse for its silence, and needing one counts against it. Choice B is the strongest trap because its first half is exactly right, but it draws the opposite conclusion. Choice D borrows the passage\'s words about cleverness and arithmetic, which describe a different argument.",["Sounds careful but has no basis: the author never weighs the explanations against one another or adds them up.","Half right: the author does grant that each \\"can be made to work,\\" but then says \\"That we need one at all is the point,\\" so the author does not leave the question open.","Correct: \\"Each such explanation can be made to work\\" concedes they are coherent; \\"That we need one at all is the point\\" says their coherence isn\'t what decides the matter.","Misreads the cleverness line: \\"too clever by half\\" is what critics say about Fermi\'s argument, and \\"arithmetic\\" is the author\'s defense of that argument, not a verdict on the explanations."]],"reading-0136":["Passage A treats \\"We see none\\" as a real result. A crowded galaxy would need a special explanation for it, so the silence counts as evidence that civilizations are rare. Passage B says the silence \\"has not yet been earned\\": the search is a hot tub against an ocean, so the quiet isn\'t a result yet. Choice A is the strongest trap. Its Passage B half is right, but it overstates Passage A, which argues that civilizations are rare, not that there are none. Choice B swaps the authors, and Choice C misdescribes both.",["Half right: Passage B fits, but Passage A argues only that civilizations are not \\"at all common\\" and grants that explanations of a crowded galaxy \\"can be made to work\\"; it never says none exist.","Swapped: \\"too little searching\\" is Passage B\'s view, and rarity is Passage A\'s.","Neither half fits: Passage A never calls for more searching, and Passage B says \\"the result is not yet in,\\" not that the silence is explained.","Correct: Passage A says a crowded galaxy needs an explanation for the silence (\\"That we need one at all is the point\\"); Passage B says the silence \\"has not yet been earned\\" because the search is a hot tub against an ocean."]],"reading-0137":["Passage B doesn\'t say anything has been found, and it doesn\'t say Passage A\'s observation is wrong. Its claim is about what the observation is worth: \\"our silence has not yet been earned,\\" and an empty glass of seawater \\"tells us something about the glass, and very little about whales.\\" So \\"We see none\\" is a true report that doesn\'t yet count as evidence. Choice D is a sharp observation about the two passages, which is why it tempts, but the question asks what Passage B\'s author would think, and that author never separates signals from spread.",["Too strong: Passage B never denies that nothing has been found (\\"None of this shows that anyone is out there\\"); it questions what that finding means.","Correct: Passage B accepts the silence as a fact but says it \\"has not yet been earned\\": the sample is a hot tub against an ocean.","Reversal: Passage B calls the larger estimate \\"still a hot tub against an ocean,\\" so the silence still isn\'t strong evidence.","This difference between the passages is real, but Passage B never notices it; its author treats all of today\'s quiet as one unearned result."]],"reading-0138":["The sentence explains why \\"the layer that would settle the matter no longer exists\\" leaves the dispute open. With the layer gone, the only firsthand evidence is the memory and records of the people who removed it, and they are the side claiming it was grime. Their account may be right, but it can\'t settle the question. Choice A is tempting because \\"best placed\\" sounds like a compliment. Choice D swings to the other extreme. Choice B moves the problem to the critics, though the real problem is that the evidence is gone.",["Reads \\"best placed\\" as praise: the author\'s point is that the best witnesses are also a party to the dispute, which is why the matter can\'t be settled, not that their side wins.","Shifts the problem onto the critics: what makes the claims untestable is that the layer \\"no longer exists,\\" not who took part in the cleaning.","Correct: the restorers alone saw the layer closely, but they are the side that called it grime, so their description can\'t serve as neutral proof.","Overreach: the author says the question can\'t be settled, not that the restorers\' view should be rejected, and the author leans toward it."]],"reading-0139":["Track what each paragraph does. The first describes the cleaning and its startling result. The second lays out the dispute. The third says the dispute can\'t be settled, whatever most scholars think. The last draws the lesson: the gloomy accounts of Michelangelo rested partly on soot, so we should be humble about the next account too. Choice A names that whole arc. Choice B is true, which makes it tempting, but it is only the example used in the last paragraph. Choice C has the right material but the wrong verb, since the author refuses to pick a winner.",["Correct: the passage moves from the dispute (first two paragraphs) to the limits of today\'s consensus (third) to a lesson in humility about any account, \\"including the bright one\\" (last).","Too narrow: this is the last paragraph\'s example, which the author uses to make the larger point about humility.","Wrong verb: the author does lay out both sides, but then says the matter can\'t be settled rather than deciding it.","Overreach: the author questions what the consensus proves but never says the critics were treated unfairly."]],"reading-0140":["Find what the author\'s claim depends on. The dispute can\'t be settled, the author says, because \\"the layer that would settle the matter no longer exists,\\" and the only witnesses are the restorers. So the finding that weakens this claim most directly is one that brings that layer back as evidence: surviving uncleaned patches. Choice B is tempting because it helps the restorers\' side, but the author already grants that most scholars side with them and says that agreement is not proof. Choice A shows how dark the ceiling looked, which nobody disputes. The question is what the darkness was made of.",["Shows how the ceiling looked, not what the dark layer was made of; a photo can\'t tell soot from a shadow Michelangelo painted on dry plaster.","Supports the brighter ceiling (the passage already reports this argument) but doesn\'t restore the missing evidence, so the question stays open in the author\'s terms.","Correct: the author\'s case rests on \\"the layer that would settle the matter no longer exists\\"; untouched samples of that layer would make the question testable again.","A judgment made at the time by people on one side of the question; it doesn\'t bring back the physical evidence the author says is missing."]],"reading-0141":["Passage A clears the mapmaker: the blank \\"simply declined to lie,\\" and if readers took it as a statement about the land, that\'s their fault. Passage B answers this directly. She says the blank \\"recorded a decision as well as a lack,\\" a decision that only surveyors\' knowledge would count, and that a decision like that \\"does not stay on the page\\": an empty space is \\"easy to read as a place that holds nothing.\\" Her closing suggestion, a label saying the land was known to its inhabitants, shows she thinks the mapmaker could have prevented the misreading. So she rejects A\'s claim because the misreading followed from the mapmaker\'s choice. The first choice is the strongest trap: B does treat the blank as being about the mapmaker, but as a choice that excluded knowledge, not as an innocent confession. The choice about a \\"longer confession\\" borrows her words but turns her objection into a quibble about length; her point is that the blank left out the inhabitants\' knowledge. And she never says mapmakers meant to make the land look empty, only that their choice made that reading easy.",["Half right: she does see the blank as saying something about the mapmaker, but as a choice that invites the misreading, not as a neutral statement that clears the mapmaker.","Borrows her phrase \\"a longer confession\\" but misses her point: the problem wasn\'t length but which knowledge counted, and she never grants that readers were to blame.","Overstates: she says the blank \\"recorded a decision\\" about which ways of knowing would count, not that mapmakers meant readers to see empty land.","Correct: Passage B says the blank \\"recorded a decision\\" about which knowledge counted and that such a decision \\"does not stay on the page,\\" so the mapmaker shares the blame."]],"reading-0142":["Look for the one idea both authors share, even though they draw opposite conclusions from it. Passage A: a blank is a statement about the mapmaker\'s knowledge (\\"Here my evidence ends\\"). Passage B: a blank \\"recorded a decision\\" about which ways of knowing would count. Either way, the blank tells you something about the mapmaker\'s side, not just about the land. Each wrong choice is a claim only one author makes: B thinks the mapmaker could have labeled the space and that maps left out inhabitants\' knowledge; A thinks the misreading was the readers\' fault.",["Only Passage B: Passage A treats the blank as already saying exactly what was needed (\\"Here my evidence ends\\") and calls it the most honest mark possible.","Only Passage A: Passage B argues the misreading grew out of the mapmaker\'s decision.","Correct: Passage A says a blank means \\"Here my evidence ends\\"; Passage B says it \\"recorded a decision\\" about which knowledge counted. Both read it as a sign of something on the mapmaker\'s side.","Only Passage B argues this; Passage A says only that d\'Anville removed guesswork, and never mentions knowledge from inhabitants."]],"reading-0143":["The narrator is \\"we\\": the regular swimmers, speaking as one. The harder question is whether they understand what they report. They decide the man is a coach timing them, but their own details point elsewhere. He presses the watch as he sits down and again as he stands up to swim, he never looks up when they touch the wall, and his rests get shorter over the winter. All of that fits a man timing his own rests. The tempting wrong answer is B. A careful reader sees the mistake, but the swimmers never admit it. In the last two paragraphs they still explain his leaving as wanting \\"a real team\\" and still sit on the bench \\"to see what he saw in us.\\" The irony works because the narrators never catch on.",["Correct: \\"we\\" speak for the group throughout, and details they report themselves (the watch pressed as he sits and again as he stands, his eyes on the clock, his shrinking rests) fit a man timing his own rests, not their swimming.","The reader can see the mistake, but the swimmers never do: at the end they still think he wanted \\"a pool with a real team,\\" and they sit on the bench \\"to see what he saw in us.\\"","No single narrator separates herself from the group: the passage never uses \\"I,\\" and every conclusion is shared (\\"we decided,\\" \\"we agreed\\").","The narrators are part of the group they describe (\\"we noticed,\\" \\"we swam accordingly\\")."]],"reading-0144":["First work out what the stopwatch measures. He presses it as he sits down, and later his thumb comes down on the button as he stands to swim again. So it times his own rests on the bench. Over the winter those rests shrink, from most of four of the swimmers\' lengths to less than two, which suggests he needed less time to recover. Choice B is the swimmers\' own explanation, and the passage keeps showing it\'s wrong: he never looks at them. Choice A is a subtle trap. The swimmers were going faster, but that would make a rest of the same time fill more of their lengths, not fewer, so it can\'t explain the change.",["Backward: the swimmers measured his rests in their own lengths, and they were swimming harder. Faster lengths would make a rest of the same time fill more of them, not fewer, so his rests really did get shorter.","This is the swimmers\' reading (\\"his standards were rising\\"), but he never watched them: he didn\'t look up when they touched the wall and kept his eyes on the clock even when Lorna finished in front of him.","Correct: the watch starts when he sits and stops when he stands to swim again, so it times his own rests. Rests that shrink over the winter suggest he was getting stronger.","He did leave in March, but nothing ties his shorter rests to leaving; shorter rests between lengths don\'t mean less time at the pool."]],"reading-0145":["Passage B\'s rule is not to keep things foreign. It is to keep a sentence about as strange as it was for its first readers, \\"no stranger and no plainer.\\" Apply that to each choice. The Brazilian saying was ordinary to Brazilians, so the plain \\"You make do\\" matches it, and B would approve. Keeping the dog and the cat (choice A) is the trap: it sounds like the \\"foreign\\" option B might seem to favor, but it makes an everyday sentence exotic. The comma-free chapter was strange even to Brazilian reviewers, so adding commas (choice C) removes the writer\'s own strangeness. And the reviewer\'s praise (choice D) is the compliment B says should worry a translator when the original was hard going, as the middle chapter was.",["Passage B favors strangeness only when it belongs to the writer. This saying was everyday Portuguese, so keeping the dog and the cat made a plain sentence exotic, which Passage B calls \\"its own kind of unfaithfulness.\\"","Correct: the saying belongs to the language, not the writer, and \\"You make do\\" is about as plain in English as the saying was to Brazilians: \\"no stranger and no plainer.\\"","The comma-free chapter was strange to its first readers too (one called it \\"unreadable\\"), so by Passage B\'s rule it should stay strange; the commas smooth away the writer\'s strangeness.","Passage B says this compliment should worry a translator when the original didn\'t read easily in its own language, and the middle chapter didn\'t."]],"reading-0146":["For a \\"both would agree\\" question, check each choice against both authors. Choice D is the only one both support. Passage A\'s author quietly doubts her own commas: the chapter \\"reads easily now. I am no longer certain it should.\\" Passage B\'s rule says a chapter that was strange to its first readers should be about as strange in English, so B would question the commas too. Choice A belongs to the editor and the reviewer, not to either author. Choice B is what the translator\'s first draft did and then dropped. Choice C states the view that Passage B sets out to correct.",["That\'s the view of Passage A\'s editor and reviewer. Passage A\'s author was pleased only \\"for about a week,\\" and Passage B says the compliment should sometimes worry a translator.","Passage A\'s author dropped exactly that first draft, and Passage B says carrying an idiom over word for word makes a plain sentence exotic.","Passage B says the fork \\"is drawn in the wrong place,\\" and Passage A never mentions Schleiermacher or describes her choices as loyalties.","Correct: Passage A\'s author says the chapter \\"reads easily now\\" and is \\"no longer certain it should\\"; by Passage B\'s rule, a chapter that was strange to its first readers should stay strange in English."]],"reading-0147":["Passage B\'s rule asks how strange a sentence was for its first readers. Here the novelist gives one character an old-fashioned way of speaking that Brazilian readers would notice. That strangeness is the writer\'s, not the language\'s. So the translator should keep it about as strange in English, with an English form of address that sounds dated in the same way. Choice A is the trap. Passage B does name \\"a common form of address\\" as something that belongs to the language, but this one isn\'t common. Choice C goes too far the other way: Brazilians understood the words, so leaving them in Portuguese would make them stranger in English than they were in the original.",["Trap: Passage B does list \\"a common form of address\\" as belonging to the language, but this one isn\'t common. It was odd to Brazilian readers too, so it belongs to the writer.","Correct: the form of address struck its first readers as old-fashioned, so by Passage B\'s rule it should be \\"about as strange in English as it was in the original, no stranger and no plainer.\\"","Brazilian readers understood the words; English readers wouldn\'t, so keeping the Portuguese makes the speech stranger than it was in the original.","Cutting it smooths away the writer\'s strangeness entirely, which Passage B says produces \\"a different book.\\""]],"reading-0148":["The claim has two parts: the seedlings respond to a sign of neighbors (a low red to far-red ratio), and they do it before being shaded (in full light). So the right pair changes only the ratio and keeps the light full: 40 mm versus 62 mm. The trap is choice B. It also changes only the ratio, which is the usual way to test one variable, but those seedlings get half the light, so they are already shaded. It can show that the ratio matters in shade, not that the response comes before shade. Choice C tests shading alone, and choice D changes two things at once.",["Correct: only the ratio changes, and both groups get full light, so the low ratio is a sign of neighbors with no shading. Stems grew from 40 to 62 mm.","Only the ratio changes here too, but these seedlings get half the light: they are already shaded, so this pair can\'t show a response that comes before shading.","This pair changes only the amount of light, so it tests the response to shading itself, not to an early signal.","Both the light and the ratio change at once, so this pair can\'t separate a response to the signal from a response to shade."]],"reading-0149":["To compare two causes, change one at a time from the same starting point. From full light and a high ratio (40 mm), lowering only the ratio gives 62 mm (+22), and halving only the light gives 52 mm (+12). The ratio wins. It also wins from the other starting points: in half light, lowering the ratio adds 14 mm (52 to 66), while at a low ratio, halving the light adds only 4 mm (62 to 66). Choice A gives the right decision for a bad reason, since the tallest seedlings had both changes. Choice D is the subtle trap: it is true that the ratio mattered less in half light, but that compares the ratio\'s effect with itself, not with the effect of light.",["Right decision, wrong reason: those seedlings had both changes at once, so their stems can\'t show which change mattered more.","Correct: from the same starting point, the ratio change alone added 22 mm (40 to 62) and the light change alone added 12 mm (40 to 52). The ratio\'s effect is larger from the other starting points too (14 mm versus 4 mm).","True, but it doesn\'t settle the question: both changes lengthened the stems, and the ratio change lengthened them more.","True numbers, wrong conclusion: this compares the ratio\'s effect with itself at two light levels. Even the smaller 14 mm beats the 4 mm that halving the light added at a low ratio."]],"reading-0150":["Translate the situation into Table 1\'s rows. Before: the neighbors reflect far-red light but cast no shade, so the seedlings get full light with a low ratio, 62 mm. After: the neighbors cut the light in half, and since that light now passes through or bounces off their leaves, the ratio stays low: half light with a low ratio, 66 mm. The difference is only about 4 mm. The trap is choice B, 12 mm, the effect of halving the light for seedlings that started with a high ratio. These seedlings had already stretched in response to the far-red signal, so being shaded added little.",["Correct: the seedlings start with full light and a low ratio (62 mm), since reflected far-red already lowers the ratio; shade from the taller neighbors keeps the ratio low and halves the light (66 mm). 66 - 62 = 4.","Wrong starting point: 52 - 40 is the effect of halving the light at a high ratio, but these seedlings already get a low ratio from their neighbors.","This is the effect of lowering the ratio in half light (66 - 52), but the ratio was already low before the neighbors grew; only the light changes.","Wrong baseline: 66 - 40 compares with seedlings in open sunlight, but these seedlings were already near neighbors that lowered the ratio."]],"reading-0151":["The author\'s test is to ask how someone could produce a measure without producing the result, which here is fewer rats in the city. Run each choice through it. Tails checked for death (choice A) and whole dead rats (choice B) both close the cutting-and-releasing loophole, which makes them tempting. But both can still be supplied by breeding rats, and the passage reports people raising rats on the city\'s outskirts. Hours of work (choice C) can rise with no change in rats at all. Only signs of rats in buildings the inspectors pick at random can\'t be produced without the city actually having fewer rats.",["This stops the cutting and releasing, but not the breeding: a rat farm can supply dead rats\' tails without the city having one fewer rat.","Same gap: whole dead rats can still come from rats bred for the purpose, so the count can rise while the city\'s rats don\'t fall.","Effort can be produced without results; hours in the sewers say nothing about how many rats are left.","Correct: signs of rats in randomly chosen buildings drop only if the city\'s rats really drop. Breeding rats or cutting off tails can\'t produce this measure."]],"reading-0152":["Check the claim against what the passage says each process leaves behind. Splitting carbon dioxide releases oxygen \\"along with carbon monoxide,\\" and Table 1 shows no carbon monoxide on Planet Y, which counts against the claim. Meanwhile Y has abundant oxygen and no water vapor at all, and the passage says such a planet \\"may be one whose oceans have already escaped\\": the water-splitting process, carried to its end. So the claim is not well supported. The trap is choice C. It treats the missing water vapor as proof that water was never split, when the passage presents a dry, oxygen-rich planet as what water splitting leaves behind once the oceans are gone.",["Correct: the passage says splitting carbon dioxide releases oxygen \\"along with carbon monoxide,\\" and Y shows none; it also says a planet with abundant oxygen but no water vapor \\"may be one whose oceans have already escaped,\\" which is the water-splitting process.","The passage says the opposite: models show a planet can build up oxygen, \\"in some models in enormous amounts,\\" with no life at all.","This is the main trap. Missing water vapor doesn\'t rule out water splitting; the passage says it fits a planet whose oceans have already been split and lost.","Missing methane weakens the case for life, but it doesn\'t point to carbon dioxide splitting; that process would leave carbon monoxide, which Y doesn\'t show."]],"reading-0153":["Compare the fifth planet with Planet X, which has the same oxygen, methane, and water vapor but no carbon monoxide. The passage treats oxygen and methane together as the strongest sign, but it also says \\"other gases count against life,\\" starting with plentiful carbon monoxide, which suggests carbon dioxide splitting and is a gas many microbes eat. It ends the paragraph by saying \\"a single missing or extra gas can change\\" the case. So the fifth planet is weaker than X. Choice A treats extra gases as extra evidence. Choice B ignores the methane, and choice C turns the author\'s \\"suggests\\" into proof.",["More gases are not more evidence: the passage says carbon monoxide counts against life, so the extra gas weakens the case.","This ignores the methane. The fifth planet isn\'t a case of \\"oxygen alone\\": like Planet X, it shows oxygen and methane together, which the passage treats as stronger evidence.","Too strong: the passage says plentiful carbon monoxide \\"suggests\\" carbon dioxide splitting; it never says it proves the planet lifeless.","Correct: the planet matches X in oxygen, methane, and water vapor, but the passage says plentiful carbon monoxide counts against life and that \\"a single ... extra gas can change\\" the case."]],"reading-0154":["The fourth paragraph tells you exactly what was wrong: \\"a paper clip had slipped between two keys and was keeping one of them from going down.\\" The other choices borrow details from other parts of the story. The open window and the dropped pencil belong to the Grange hall piano, and the piano no one had tuned in years is the narrator\'s own.",["Wrong piano: Bettina says this about the upright at the Grange hall, not Mrs. Abernathy\'s piano.","Correct: the fourth paragraph says a paper clip between two keys was keeping one from going down.","Wrong stop: the dropped pencil was inside the Grange hall piano, and the passage never says it blocked anything.","Mixes up details: drifting strings are explained at the Grange hall, and the untuned piano \\"in years\\" is the narrator\'s own."]],"reading-0155":["In the second paragraph, Bettina says someone left a window open all winter, and she explains that \\"cold and damp air... makes the wood swell and shrink, and the strings drift out of tune.\\" The chairs and the pencil are real details from the Grange hall visit, but the passage doesn\'t say either one caused the problem. Nothing is said about how long the piano went unplayed.",["Real detail, wrong cause: the chairs were on the piano, but Bettina never says they put it out of tune.","Real detail, wrong cause: the pencil was inside the piano, but the passage doesn\'t connect it to the tuning.","Correct: Bettina blames an open window and explains that cold and damp air makes the wood swell and shrink.","Not stated: the passage never says how long it had been since anyone played the piano."]],"reading-0156":["In the third paragraph, Bettina strikes a key, listens, turns a pin, and strikes the key again. She is playing a note so she can hear it, so \\"strike\\" means \\"press.\\" \\"Strike\\" has other meanings (to hit hard, to stop working, to cross something out), but none of them fit what a piano tuner does with a key.",["Too forceful: \\"attack\\" suggests violence, but Bettina is just playing a note to listen to it.","This is the meaning in \\"the workers went on strike,\\" which doesn\'t fit a piano key.","This is the meaning in \\"strike a word from a list,\\" which doesn\'t fit here.","Correct: Bettina presses a key to play a note, listens, and presses it again."]],"reading-0157":["On the drive home, Bettina says most of the job \\"was not tuning at all. It\'s paying attention... and then being honest about what you find.\\" The first choice restates that. The pin-turning choice is about tuning, which she says is the smaller part. Nothing in the passage mentions how many houses she visits or teaching customers to tune.",["Correct: she says the job is mostly \\"paying attention\\" and \\"being honest about what you find.\\"","Opposite of her point: she says most of the job is \\"not tuning at all.\\"","Not supported: the passage never mentions how many visits she tries to make.","Not supported: the passage never shows her teaching a customer to care for or tune a piano."]],"reading-0158":["The first paragraph says residents can borrow up to five tools \\"at a time, for one week, for free.\\" So the loan period is one week. The other numbers come from nearby details: five is the number of tools, three is how many evenings the library opens, and two months is how fast the first shelves filled.",["Number from the wrong detail: the library is open three evenings a week.","Number from the wrong detail: borrowers may take up to five tools at a time.","Correct: the first paragraph says tools can be borrowed \\"for one week.\\"","Number from the wrong detail: two months is how long it took to collect the first donations."]],"reading-0159":["The second paragraph explains how the library began: Castellanos \\"noticed how many of her neighbors owned the same expensive equipment and used it only once or twice a year,\\" like the unused tile saws on her street. The teenagers who had never held a screwdriver come up later, in Walter Osei\'s class. Late returns are described as rare, and the passage never says the book library stopped lending anything.",["Correct: the second paragraph says she saw neighbors owning the same expensive equipment and using it once or twice a year.","Not stated: the book library is mentioned only as the source of borrowers\' cards.","Wrong time and person: this is about Walter Osei\'s students, after the library was running.","Reversed: the passage says late returns are rare."]],"reading-0160":["The last paragraph says the sander was borrowed so rarely that the volunteers sold it \\"and used the money to buy three more cordless drills, the library\'s most requested item.\\" The other choices reuse words from the passage (the class, the shelves, the pressure washer), but none of them is what the money paid for.",["Contradicts the passage: Osei\'s class is free, and nothing says he is paid.","Not stated: the passage mentions the first set of shelves but no money spent on more.","Echo of the tool list: the library has a pressure washer, but the money didn\'t go to another one.","Correct: the last paragraph says the money bought three more cordless drills."]],"reading-0161":["Castellanos is explaining why they sold the sander and bought more drills: the library should keep on hand the tools people actually request. That is the store meaning of \\"stock,\\" as in \\"the shop stocks batteries.\\" The other choices come from other uses of the word (stocks and shares, soup stock, livestock), and none of them makes sense for a tool library.",["This is the meaning in \\"the stock market,\\" which doesn\'t fit tools on a shelf.","Correct: Castellanos means the library should keep on hand the tools people want.","This is the cooking meaning of \\"stock\\" (a broth), which doesn\'t fit here.","This borrows from \\"livestock\\" and pens for animals; it doesn\'t fit a library\'s shelves."]],"reading-0300":["The paragraph begins \\"That is also why I leave some things in,\\" which ties the cough to the rule in the paragraph before: she removes the hiss because no one in the recording room heard it, since the disc added it later. The cough is not music, and her software marked it as noise, but it \\"was in the room,\\" so by her rule it stays. The story shows her test is presence at the performance, not musical value. The software detail is true but isn\'t the point, and she never admits that disc noise belongs to the performance.",["The software did mark the cough as noise, but the point of the story is why she kept it, not a claim that the software is often wrong.","That is the concern of the first paragraph. The cough isn\'t hidden by the hiss; it is a sound she chooses to keep.","Correct: she keeps the cough because \\"that cough was in the room,\\" which matches her rule that she removes only what wasn\'t part of the performance, like the hiss.","Reverses her view: she says the hiss \\"was never part of the performance.\\" The cough was a sound in the room, not noise made by the disc."]],"reading-0301":["Passage B names two losses. Software can remove real sounds (a breath, high overtones) along with the hiss, and erasing the crackle wipes out a record of the disc\'s history that a future historian might want. The proposal is that every cleaned version should come with a flat transfer, \\"a copy made with nothing taken out, so that the next person can make a different choice.\\" That fixes exactly one problem: once something is removed, nobody later can get it back. The author doesn\'t say the software removes too little, defends listeners who want the music, and never makes Passage A\'s \\"too new\\" complaint.",["Reverses the passage: the worry is that the software removes too much, taking soft breaths and overtones along with the hiss.","That is the accusation mentioned in Passage A. Passage B never says cleaned records sound too new.","Reverses the passage: the author says most listeners \\"want the music, and they are right to want it.\\"","Correct: both losses in the passage are things that can\'t be gotten back once erased, and a flat transfer keeps them so \\"the next person can make a different choice.\\""]],"reading-0302":["Passage A defends restoration by saying it only subtracts: the hiss came from the disc, not the performance. Passage B doesn\'t fight that point. It never says software adds sounds. Instead it says that subtracting the hiss isn\'t clean: real parts of the performance, like a breath or a violin\'s overtones, share the hiss\'s frequencies \\"and when the hiss goes, some of them go with it.\\" So Passage B would grant the claim but say it misses the real loss. Passage B also values the crackle for historians, so it wouldn\'t say the hiss is worthless, and it never says the musicians heard the hiss.",["Correct: Passage B\'s complaint is about subtraction, not addition. The breath, overtones, and piano strings \\"sit among the same frequencies as surface hiss\\" and can go with it.","Half right: Passage B never says anything is added, but it says the crackle is \\"a record of the disc\\" that a historian of listening may care about.","Passage B never says the software adds anything. Its worry is what the software takes away.","Passage B doesn\'t claim the hiss was heard at the performance; it treats the crackle as a record of the disc\'s later life."]],"reading-0303":["At the time, the narrator felt Teodor let her \\"make a fool of myself\\" in front of the passengers. The phrase \\"I now understand\\" marks the adult looking back, and what she understands is that nobody noticed (the farmer \\"went on reading his newspaper\\"). Admitting that her humiliation was imagined is rueful (a bit regretful and wry about herself) and self-aware. She isn\'t bitter or blaming, she isn\'t nostalgic, and \\"dismissive\\" doesn\'t fit, because the next sentence treats Teodor\'s lesson with care.",["Correct: the adult narrator sees that her embarrassment was mostly in her own head, and she says so with a gentle look back at her younger self.","Her anger at the time was aimed at Teodor, and this comment undercuts it. The adult narrator isn\'t blaming anyone here.","She isn\'t longing for the past; she is correcting how she saw it.","There is some amusement at her younger self, but she isn\'t dismissing anyone or anything; she takes the lesson seriously in the next sentence."]],"reading-0304":["Teodor\'s answer is the key: \\"You\'d have watched for sandbars. I wanted you to watch the river.\\" A warning would have made her look only for sandbars. He wanted her to pay attention to everything the river was doing. The last sentence confirms it: a warning \\"would have taught me one shape of trouble.\\" The strongest wrong answer is that he wanted her to learn about sandbars by hitting one, but that keeps the lesson about sandbars, which is exactly what he says he didn\'t want. Nothing suggests he thought the sandbars were harmless or that his first-morning talk covered them.",["Too narrow: he says, \\"I wanted you to watch the river,\\" so his aim was her attention to the whole river, not a lesson about sandbars in particular.","He said nothing for six weeks, but the passage gives no sign he thought the first morning covered sandbars, which only appeared when the water dropped in August.","Nothing supports this: he stepped in at once when the hull dragged, which suggests he took it seriously.","Correct: \\"You\'d have watched for sandbars... I wanted you to watch the river\\" means a warning would have focused her on one danger instead of the river as a whole."]],"reading-0305":["Early on, the narrator says Teodor explained the ferry once \\"and then said nothing more about it for six weeks. I took his silence as confidence.\\" The words \\"I took\\" hint that the adult narrator sees it differently now. By the end, she understands that his not warning her about the sandbars was on purpose: a warning \\"would have taught me one shape of trouble,\\" and he wanted her to learn to sense trouble \\"before it has a shape.\\" So his silence turns out to be teaching. The other choices get her first view wrong or turn his care into indifference.",["Reversed: she first \\"took his silence as confidence,\\" not as disapproval.","Correct: she first \\"took his silence as confidence,\\" and by the end she understands that holding back the warning was his way of teaching her to watch the river.","Starts right but ends wrong: she comes to see his silence as a careful lesson, not as a lack of care.","She never treats his silence as a sign that the job was easy, and the ending is about how to watch for trouble, not about proving the job was dangerous."]],"reading-0306":["The sentence comes right after the 72-second detail. Big Ear didn\'t move; Earth\'s turning swept its view across the sky, so anything fixed among the stars would rise and fall over exactly that time. An airplane or a nearby transmitter moves differently from the stars, so it wouldn\'t match that timing. The sentence explains why the timing points to a source in the sky. It doesn\'t claim every human source is ruled out (the author only calls the signal \\"hard to dismiss\\"), and it isn\'t about the narrow frequencies.",["Correct: 72 seconds is the time Big Ear took to sweep past one point in the sky, so a source that rose and fell in that time behaved like something fixed among the stars.","Too strong: the author says only that these features made the signal \\"hard to dismiss,\\" not that every human source is ruled out.","Right passage, wrong detail: the narrow frequency range is a separate feature, mentioned earlier in the paragraph, and the author says only that natural sources \\"tend to\\" spread wider.","Nothing says Big Ear couldn\'t detect Earthly signals; the point is that such signals wouldn\'t follow the sky\'s timing."]],"reading-0307":["The last paragraph gives the author\'s view. It is \\"tempting\\" to treat the silence as a message, but the author would rather see a lesson: one observation \\"can tell us where to look\\" but \\"cannot tell us what we have found,\\" because knowledge needs \\"the second look.\\" So the signal\'s worth is in pointing the way, not in proving anything. The trickiest wrong answer says the features \\"show\\" the signal came from beyond Earth. The author does find the 72-second timing telling, but calls the features only \\"hard to dismiss,\\" and the main point is that without a second look nothing is settled.",["The author warns against reading \\"the mystery as a message\\"; the passage never says the signal was sent by anyone.","Too strong: the author lists features that make the signal \\"hard to dismiss,\\" but the last paragraph says one detection \\"cannot tell us what we have found,\\" which includes where it came from.","Correct: \\"A single detection can tell us where to look. It cannot tell us what we have found.\\"","Reverses the author\'s view: a single detection is valuable because it \\"can tell us where to look.\\""]],"reading-0170":["Ask what the whole passage is about, start to finish. The narrator starts the summer unable to answer children\'s questions, Mrs. Haldane gives her a notebook and a plan, and by August she has filled pages of her own. That is a summer of volunteering and the supervisor who helped her. College is never mentioned, there is no long disagreement (Mrs. Haldane is kind when the narrator expects anger), and the ice cream stand is only a detail in the first paragraph.",["Goes beyond the passage: it ends on her last day of the summer and never mentions college or what she studied later.","Correct: the passage follows one summer at the touch tank, from hiding in the closet to filling her own pages in Mrs. Haldane\'s notebook.","Reverses the story: she expected Mrs. Haldane to be angry, but Mrs. Haldane helped her instead, and the passage ends on a quiet, friendly moment.","Too narrow: the ice cream stand appears once, in the first paragraph, as the job she wanted. The passage never compares the two."]],"reading-0171":["Find Mrs. Haldane\'s actual instructions, in the fourth paragraph: \\"Write down what they ask you. Look it up at night. Then tell them the next time.\\" The line before it, \\"Nobody knows everything on the first day,\\" is said to the narrator, not meant for visitors. The notebook is full of answers, but she is told to add questions, not to read answers aloud, and she is never told to send visitors away.",["Not in the passage: Mrs. Haldane never offers to take the narrator\'s questions herself.","The notebook does hold answers, but Mrs. Haldane\'s advice is to write down new questions and look them up, not to read from her pages.","Wrong listener: Mrs. Haldane says \\"Nobody knows everything on the first day\\" to the narrator, to calm her. It isn\'t something to tell visitors.","Correct: \\"Write down what they ask you. Look it up at night. Then tell them the next time.\\""]],"reading-0172":["Compare the two points in time. At the start, \\"I didn\'t know any of the answers,\\" and she hides in the supply closet rather than answer a boy. In August she has filled eleven pages of her own and can tell anyone that Admiral is not really a crab. What changed is her ability to answer questions. Keeping children from lifting animals was always her job, with no sign she struggled with it. She says she never learned to like Admiral, and no aquarium job is ever offered.",["Correct: at first she \\"didn\'t know any of the answers\\" and hid from a question; by August she has filled eleven pages and \\"could tell anyone who asked\\" about Admiral.","This was part of her job from the start, but the passage never says she had trouble with it, so it shows no change.","Reverses the passage: she says, \\"I never did learn to like Admiral.\\"","Not in the passage: the paying job she wanted was at the ice cream stand, and no job is offered at the aquarium."]],"reading-0173":["Mrs. Haldane asks for the notebook, reads the narrator\'s pages, closes it, and gives it back, on the narrator\'s last day. The safe inference is that she means for the narrator to keep it, now that it holds the narrator\'s work too. She points out no mistakes, she was the one who told the narrator to write in it, and nothing says she is leaving the aquarium. The other choices each add something the passage never says.",["Unsupported: Mrs. Haldane reads \\"without saying a word\\" and points out no errors; she gives the notebook back instead of correcting it.","Unsupported: Mrs. Haldane handed her the notebook and told her to write down questions, so writing in it was what she asked for.","Correct: she reads the narrator\'s eleven pages and then hands the notebook back on the narrator\'s last day, a quiet way of letting her keep it.","Unsupported: nothing says Mrs. Haldane is leaving. Only the narrator\'s time at the aquarium is ending."]],"reading-0174":["The first paragraph sets up the question (a frozen lake \\"almost never\\" is solid all the way down; \\"The reason has to do with an unusual habit of water\\"), and the rest of the passage answers it: water is densest near 4°C, the lake mixes until it reaches that temperature, then ice forms on top and slows heat loss. That is an explanation of why the water stays liquid. Food, other liquids, and changes over the years are not what the passage is about.",["Touches on a detail: the first paragraph says fish and insects live under the ice, but the passage never discusses how they find food.","Too narrow: other liquids are mentioned in one sentence (\\"Most liquids shrink as they cool\\"), and Table 1 lists only water and ice.","Wrong verb and topic: the passage explains how lakes freeze; it never argues anything about how often they freeze.","Correct: the passage asks why a frozen lake isn\'t solid all the way down and answers with water\'s density, the fall mixing, and the ice acting as a lid."]],"reading-0175":["The second paragraph gives the rule and then the exception. Most liquids shrink (and get denser) as they cool, and water does too, \\"but only down to about 4°C.\\" Below that, water expands slightly, so 2°C water is a little less dense than 4°C water. The two choices about shrinking or sinking apply the general rule where the passage says it stops working. The 9 percent expansion is about freezing, which hasn\'t happened yet at 2°C.",["Correct: \\"Below that temperature, water expands slightly as it cools,\\" so water at 1°C or 2°C \\"is a little less dense than water at 4°C.\\"","This is what most liquids do, and what water does above 4°C, but the passage says water behaves the other way below 4°C.","Wrong step: the 9 percent growth happens when water freezes, at 0°C. Water at 2°C is still liquid.","Applies the general rule: most liquids shrink as they cool, but the passage says water does so \\"only down to about 4°C.\\""]],"reading-0176":["Read the liquid-water rows in the order of cooling: 20°C, 10°C, 4°C, 0°C. The densities are 0.9982, 0.9997, 1.0000, 0.9998. They rise until 4°C and then drop a little. That matches the passage: water shrinks as it cools \\"only down to about 4°C\\" and then expands slightly. The drop at the end is tiny, which is why \\"increases only\\" is tempting. Leave out the ice row, since it is not liquid water.",["Misses the last row: density rises from 0.9982 to 1.0000 g/cm³ at 4°C, but then falls to 0.9998 g/cm³ at 0°C.","Reverses the first part: from 20°C to 4°C, the density goes up, from 0.9982 to 1.0000 g/cm³.","Correct: 0.9982 (20°C) to 0.9997 (10°C) to 1.0000 (4°C), then down to 0.9998 (0°C), just as the passage says water is densest near 4°C.","Gets the order backward: the density rises first and then falls slightly. It never falls and then rises."]],"reading-0177":["Put the third paragraph\'s steps in order. The surface cools first; the cold water sinks and the lake mixes; this \\"goes on until the lake is close to 4°C from top to bottom.\\" After that, surface water that cools further is less dense, so it stays on top, keeps cooling, and freezes. So the sinking stops when the lake reaches about 4°C. Ice comes after that point, cold air is what starts the process, and the passage says the bottom stays near 4°C, not frozen.",["Too late in the sequence: the surface water stays on top before the ice forms; staying on top is what lets the surface cool until it freezes.","Correct: mixing \\"goes on until the lake is close to 4°C from top to bottom. After that,\\" colder surface water is less dense and stays on top.","Too early: colder air is what starts the cooling and sinking in the first place, not what stops it.","Contradicts the passage: the bottom water stays near 4°C, and a lake filling with ice from the bottom is what would happen only if ice sank."]],"reading-0178":["Passage A argues that a classroom map teaches sizes, not shapes, so an equal-area map is the better single choice. Passage B answers that point head-on in its second paragraph: a student who sees only the equal-area map will learn, without being told, \\"that Africa is long and thin.\\" So B would say shapes are taught too, and swapping maps just trades \\"one quiet error for another.\\" The tempting traps either give A\'s own view, give B a claim it never makes about Mercator\'s shapes, or stretch \\"no flat map keeps all four\\" into \\"no map keeps true areas,\\" which B denies.",["Correct: Passage B says a student who sees only the equal-area map \\"will also learn something without being told: that Africa is long and thin.\\" Shapes get taught too.","This is Passage A\'s position, not B\'s. Passage B warns that the equal-area map\'s shapes teach their own \\"quiet error.\\"","Passage B never says Mercator\'s map gets shapes wrong, and it refuses to name one map as the right choice for the wall.","Overstates B: B says no flat map keeps all four properties at once, not that none can keep areas. B grants that the equal-area map \\"keeps sizes right.\\""]],"reading-0179":["For an \\"agree\\" question, check every choice against both passages. Passage A: a student who sees the map daily \\"learns something from it whether a teacher says so or not,\\" a lesson \\"never taught on purpose.\\" Passage B: a student who sees only the equal-area map \\"will also learn something without being told.\\" Both authors make the same point about different maps. The equal-area recommendation is A\'s alone, the side-by-side display is B\'s alone (A is ready to pick one map), and A explicitly praises Mercator\'s map for navigation.",["Only Passage A argues this. Passage B distrusts the argument that one map should \\"simply replace another.\\"","Only Passage B says this. Passage A plans for a classroom that \\"gets only one map\\" and says which one it should be.","Neither author holds this: Passage A calls the map\'s virtue at sea \\"real,\\" and Passage B never dismisses it.","Correct: A says a student \\"learns something from it whether a teacher says so or not\\"; B says a student \\"will also learn something without being told.\\""]],"reading-0180":["Passage B\'s claim is that every flat map gives up at least one of shape, area, distance, and direction. The best example would show a real map keeping one of these and losing another. Passage A supplies one: Mercator\'s map keeps compass directions true (a straight line is a constant bearing) but badly enlarges lands near the poles, so Greenland looks as big as Africa. The date and the real size of South America don\'t show a trade-off, and the detail about what students learn backs a different claim, the one Passage A makes about wall maps teaching sizes.",["Gives the map\'s date and purpose, but it doesn\'t show the map keeping one property and losing another.","Correct: it shows one map keeping true directions while giving up true areas, exactly the trade-off B says every flat map must make.","A fact about the real continents. On its own it says nothing about what any map keeps or gives up.","This supports Passage A\'s claim about what wall maps teach, not B\'s claim about what flat maps must give up."]],"reading-0181":["Look at where the example sits. B\'s second paragraph opens by distrusting \\"the argument that one map should simply replace another,\\" then says the usual replacement stretches continents, then gives the student who sees only that map and learns \\"that Africa is long and thin.\\" The example\'s job is to show that a swap just trades \\"one quiet error for another.\\" It doesn\'t rank the maps (an even trade, not a worse one), it doesn\'t explain why the stretching happens, and its lesson is about shape, which cuts against Passage A rather than supporting it.",["Overreach: B says the student \\"trades one quiet error for another,\\" an even trade. B never says the equal-area map is worse.","The stretching is stated in the sentence before, but the student example doesn\'t explain its cause; it shows its effect on what students learn.","Correct: it backs B\'s distrust of \\"one map should simply replace another\\": the new map teaches its own quiet error, that \\"Africa is long and thin.\\"","Works against A: the lesson in B\'s example is about shape (\\"long and thin\\"), the very thing A says wall maps don\'t teach."]],"reading-0320":["Start from Ericsson\'s view in Passage B: what matters is not hours \\"of any kind\\" but deliberate practice, which targets \\"one weakness at a time\\" with \\"a teacher\'s guidance and quick feedback.\\" Before Mr. Adeyemi, the narrator played whole pieces, \\"backed up a measure and kept going\\" when she stumbled, and measured her practice by the clock. That is time spent without the features Ericsson cared about. The traps each borrow a real detail from Passage B: practicing alone (from the 1993 study\'s measure), the 10,000-hour threshold (which Ericsson rejected), and the 2014 study (which is about deliberate practice and states the critics\' view).",["Correct: she played pieces straight through and kept going past mistakes, with no one giving her feedback, which is the opposite of the deliberate practice Ericsson describes.","Practicing alone and daily matches how the 1993 study counted hours, but Ericsson\'s deliberate practice is defined by its focus and feedback, not its schedule.","This applies the \\"10,000-hour rule\\" as a threshold, which is exactly what Ericsson objected to; he said hours of any kind weren\'t what mattered.","The 2014 study measured deliberate practice, which hers was not, and its finding is the critics\' view, not Ericsson\'s."]],"reading-0321":["Connect the narrator\'s pride to the rules. In the first paragraph she is \\"proud of those hours\\" of playing pieces from beginning to end. Mr. Adeyemi\'s first rule is \\"Never play a passage through just to hear it,\\" the very thing she had done for six years, and she says practice \\"stopped feeling like something I was good at.\\" The rules stung because they implied her proud habit hadn\'t been worth much. The tempting wrong answer is the shorter sessions: she does practice less, but only later (\\"That spring\\"), and the passage doesn\'t connect that to why the rules felt insulting at first.",["Wrong time: she practiced less \\"that spring,\\" after she had followed the rules for a while; the passage never says the rules set a shorter time.","Correct: she was \\"proud of those hours,\\" and the first rule, \\"Never play a passage through just to hear it,\\" rejects the way she had spent them.","Unsupported: she kept working on the same Chopin waltz, only four bars at a time.","Too strong: Mr. Adeyemi criticizes how she practices, never her talent."]],"reading-0322":["The second paragraph of Passage B gives two objections, and it ranks them. The first is that 10,000 was an average, not a threshold. Then: \\"More important, the rule counted the wrong thing.\\" What mattered was deliberate practice, not hours of any kind. So his main objection is that the rule measured amount, not kind. The trap choice about the number being \\"too high\\" twists his first, lesser point: he didn\'t say the number was wrong, only that it wasn\'t a cutoff. The \\"overstated\\" choice is the 2014 critics\' view, and Passage B never says Ericsson objected to the students estimating their own hours.",["Wrong person: this is the 2014 critics\' position, and Ericsson took the other side of that debate.","Passage B does say the students estimated their hours, but it never says Ericsson objected to that.","Close but not his main point: he said 10,000 was an average and not a threshold, not that it was too high; then he said \\"More important, the rule counted the wrong thing.\\"","Correct: \\"More important, the rule counted the wrong thing\\": not hours of any kind, but deliberate practice."]],"reading-0323":["Find what each author claims for themselves. The narrator learned that how she practiced mattered more than how long. The author of Passage B describes Ericsson\'s view and his critics\' view but doesn\'t pick a side. The last paragraph says only that practice matters (\\"neither side doubts that it does\\") and that how much it explains is still being argued. So the safe common ground is the modest claim: practice helps determine skill. The hard trap is \\"kind matters far more than amount.\\" It fits Passage A and Ericsson perfectly, but the author of Passage B never endorses it. Don\'t mix up a view an author reports with a view the author holds. The other two choices are too strong for Passage B.",["Too strong for Passage B, whose 1993 study found that the best students had practiced thousands of hours more than others.","This is the narrator\'s lesson, and Ericsson\'s view, but the author of Passage B only reports Ericsson\'s view and ends by treating the question as still open.","Correct: the narrator improved through practice, and the author of Passage B says that neither side \\"doubts that it does\\" matter.","The author of Passage B says how much practice explains is exactly what\'s in dispute, and the 2014 study put it at about a fifth for musicians."]],"reading-0324":["Use the step-counting idea in two steps. On the way out, the ant has normal legs, and Table 1 gives a normal stride of 10.0 mm, or 0.010 m. So 8 m takes about 8 ÷ 0.010 = 800 steps. On the way home it takes the same 800 steps, but with trimmed legs each stride is 5.8 mm, so it covers 800 × 0.0058 ≈ 4.6 m before it starts searching. (Group 3 shows the same pattern for a 10 m trip: 1,000 steps × 5.8 mm ≈ 5.8 m, close to the 5.9 m measured.) The traps: copying Group 3\'s 5.9 m, assuming the ant gets it right (8.0 m), or flipping the ratio (13.8 m).",["Correct: with normal 10.0 mm strides, 8 m takes about 800 steps; 800 trimmed-leg strides of 5.8 mm cover about 4.6 m.","Copied from Group 3 in Table 1, but that group\'s feeder was 10 m away, not 8 m.","This assumes the ant judges the true distance; a step-counting ant with shorter strides would fall short.","Ratio flipped: this multiplies 8 m by 10.0/5.8 instead of by 5.8/10.0, as if shorter legs made the ant overshoot."]],"reading-0325":["Group 2 overshot (15.2 m) after getting stilts at the feeder. One explanation is step counting: the ant took the same number of steps home, but longer ones. Another is that stilts somehow confuse an ant, whatever it counts. Group 4 separates the two. Those ants wore stilts both ways, so their strides matched on both trips, and they walked about the right distance (10.2 m). So stilts by themselves don\'t cause the error; the change in stride between the two trips does. The trimmed-legs choice is the trap: it\'s the same kind of worry, but Group 4 didn\'t have trimmed legs, so it can\'t rule that out.",["Group 4 tells you nothing about Group 1\'s trip; Group 1 already walked home about the right distance.","Group 4 wore stilts, not trimmed legs, so it can\'t test whether trimming itself caused Group 3\'s error.","Correct: Group 4 wore stilts but had the same stride both ways and walked home about 10 m, so stilts alone don\'t cause the error. The mismatch between trips does.","No group in Table 1 tests ground motion; the second paragraph handles that idea separately."]],"reading-0326":["Put two facts together. Over the hills, the ants\' feet covered \\"the longer distance\\" going up and down. On the flat test ground, they walked home only the shorter, ground-level distance. A longer path takes more steps than a shorter one with strides of similar length, so the ants took fewer steps home than they took out. That\'s why the passage says step counting \\"cannot be the whole story\\": an ant that only matched its step count would have walked too far. The passage doesn\'t say how the ant adjusts for slope, so \\"ignored the uphill steps\\" claims more than it knows. The ground-motion choice brings back an idea the second paragraph played down.",["Too specific: the passage says only that the ants seem to take slope into account, not that they skip uphill steps.","Correct: going up and down hills covered a longer path than the flat trip home, so with strides like those in Table 1, the ants must have taken fewer steps going home.","Contradicted: they walked the distance measured along the flat ground, \\"not the longer distance their feet had covered.\\"","Unsupported: the passage says ground motion plays \\"at most a modest part,\\" and the hill studies don\'t mention it."]],"reading-0327":["The second paragraph gives two ideas about how ants judge distance. The first is that they gauge how far the ground slides past beneath them, \\"much as flying honeybees seem to judge distance\\" from moving scenery. The bees are there to show that another insect seems to use the method this first idea proposes, which makes the idea worth testing. The passage then says ground motion plays \\"at most a modest part\\" for walking ants. The direction choice is the trap: the bees are about distance, and the passage credits direction to polarized light.",["Unsupported: the passage never compares how accurate bees and ants are.","Mixes up direction and distance: the bees come up in the discussion of distance, and the passage says ants find direction from polarized light.","The third paragraph\'s study tests step counting in ants; it has nothing to do with bees.","Correct: bees \\"seem to judge distance from the motion of the scenery,\\" which is the ground-motion idea proposed for ants."]],"reading-0328":["Look for who says \\"I.\\" The narrator speaks of \\"my grandfather,\\" clamps the blanks, and makes the failed keys, so the story is told by the grandchild, looking back on \\"that summer.\\" The grandfather and the man in the suit are characters the narrator watches, and no one\'s private thoughts but the narrator\'s are given, which rules out an outside observer.",["Reversed roles: the grandfather is a character described by the narrator (\\"My grandfather kept the key machine\\"); he isn\'t the one telling the story.","The narrator uses \\"I\\" and \\"my,\\" so this is a first-person story, and the grandfather\'s thoughts are never revealed; he barely speaks.","The man in the suit is a customer the narrator describes, not the narrator.","Correct: the narrator says \\"my grandfather\\" and describes cutting keys \\"that summer\\" at his store."]],"reading-0329":["\\"As though\\" tells you this is a comparison, not a description of what actually happened, so the two literal choices are out. The question is which figurative meaning fits. Right after squinting, the grandfather names a specific flaw: \\"Burr\\" or \\"You rushed it.\\" So he is studying the edge for tiny mistakes, the way you\'d peer at very small print. The tempting wrong choice says he was only pretending, but he really does find what went wrong each time, and he shows no interest in soothing customers: he \\"never apologized\\" to them.",["\\"As though\\" signals a comparison: he is looking at the key the way someone reads tiny print, and the passage never mentions a code on it.","Nothing in the passage mentions glasses or receipts; this takes \\"print\\" literally and attaches it to something the story never shows.","Correct: after squinting he names a small flaw (\\"Burr\\" or \\"You rushed it\\"), so the comparison shows how carefully he inspects the key.","Overreach: he finds real flaws each time, and he \\"never apologized to the customers,\\" so there\'s no sign he was putting on a show for them."]],"reading-0330":["The grandfather never praises in words. Instead, on the day the narrator\'s key satisfies the man who watched \\"every pass of the wheel,\\" he quietly lets the narrator lock up, which in a hardware store means being trusted with the place itself. That\'s a small, safe step from the text: he trusts the narrator now. Regret, tiredness, and inheritance all go beyond anything the passage says.",["Correct: right after the narrator\'s key passes the doubtful customer\'s test, the grandfather trusts the narrator to lock up the store.","Reversal: nothing suggests regret; he \\"did not say anything,\\" and his strictness is what produced the narrator\'s skill.","Unsupported: the passage gives no hint that he is tired, and the timing after the man in the suit points to a reason tied to the narrator.","Too strong: letting someone lock up for one night says nothing about who will own the store."]],"reading-0331":["A judgment is a claim a reasonable person could disagree with. Three choices report checkable events: when the carts appeared, whom Goldman hired, and his patent and wealth. Only the last sentence of the passage weighs something: the author admits the claim is \\"an exaggeration\\" but argues it is only a slight one. That is opinion.",["Correct: \\"only a slight exaggeration\\" is the author\'s own weighing of how much the cart shaped supermarkets, a claim others could dispute.","A date and an event: this can be checked, so it is a fact, not a judgment.","This reports what Goldman did, which can be checked; it is a fact.","The spread of the cart, the patent, and the fortune are all matters of record, so this is a fact."]],"reading-0332":["The first four paragraphs tell one story in order: the basket problem, Goldman\'s idea, shoppers\' refusal, and his trick with hired shoppers. The last paragraph steps back: \\"The larger change was harder to see.\\" It describes what the cart did to shopping in general: people could buy more, and stores grew to sell more. The near miss is the choice about opinions. The last paragraph does contain an opinion, but it is about supermarkets, not Goldman\'s character, and so that choice gets the content wrong.",["Half right: the paragraph does include the author\'s opinion, but it is about the cart\'s effect on supermarkets; Goldman\'s character is never judged.","Unsupported: the passage never says Goldman doubted his invention; he responded to doubters with \\"theater.\\"","Correct: after telling how Goldman got shoppers to use the cart, the passage widens to \\"the larger change\\": shoppers buying more and stores built around the cart.","No rival inventor appears anywhere in the passage."]],"reading-0333":["The fourth paragraph answers this directly. Goldman\'s answer \\"was theater\\": he hired people to push carts around as if they were shopping, and posted a greeter to hand out carts. Real customers then followed. The other choices are plausible business moves, but the passage mentions none of them.",["Unsupported: the passage never mentions prices or discounts.","This sounds like a sensible fix for the baby-carriage complaint, but the passage never says he changed the design.","Unsupported: the passage never says the baskets were removed.","Correct: \\"He hired men and women of various ages to stroll the aisles pushing carts as if they were ordinary customers.\\""]],"reading-0334":["Every paragraph is about one question: how does a gecko stick, and how does it let go? The passage gives the old guesses, the structure of the toes, the 2000 and 2002 experiments, and the peeling motion, then ends with engineers\' copies. That\'s an explanation of what scientists learned. The wrong choices either pick a minor detail (Aristotle), invent an argument, or bring in animals the passage never mentions.",["Correct: the passage moves from the old puzzle, to the setae and spatulae, to the experiments on how they stick, to how geckos release.","Wrong verb and direction: the passage reports engineers\' adhesives without criticizing them.","Too broad: Aristotle is mentioned once, and the passage is only about geckos\' grip.","No other climbing animals are discussed."]],"reading-0335":["Ideas don\'t live or die, so the everyday meaning (\\"stayed alive\\") doesn\'t fit. The sentence says none of the guesses \\"survived a close look,\\" and the next sentence explains why: the toes are dry, leave no residue, and grip smooth glass. Each guess failed when examined. So \\"survived\\" means held up under.",["Outliving means lasting longer than someone else, which doesn\'t fit guesses being tested by \\"a close look.\\"","This is the most common meaning of the word, but guesses aren\'t alive.","Nothing in the sentence suggests the guesses were damaged and then got better.","Correct: none of the guesses held up when people examined them closely, as the next sentence shows."]],"reading-0336":["The first paragraph lists old guesses (suction cups, glue, hooks) and then says why they fail. The toes are dry (so no glue) and grip \\"polished glass, which offers nothing for a hook to catch\\" (so no hooks). The detail\'s local job is to knock down the hook explanation. The water-film choice is a trap: it borrows an idea from later in the passage, which the evidence actually went against.",["Correct: the detail answers the guess about \\"tiny hooks catching on invisible roughness\\": glass has no roughness to catch.","Unsupported: the passage never compares how well geckos climb smooth and rough surfaces.","Wrong idea: the water-film idea comes later, in the third paragraph, and the 2002 experiment argued against it.","Wrong part of the passage: the single-seta measurement is in the third paragraph and isn\'t connected to this detail."]],"reading-0337":["The test was set up as a prediction. If water were doing the work, the setae \\"should grip a water-attracting surface much better than a water-repelling one.\\" They \\"gripped both about equally well,\\" so the water idea lost support. The other choices are true statements from the passage, but each comes from a different finding or paragraph.",["Wrong experiment: this was the 2000 finding about a single seta\'s strength, not the test of what kind of force was at work.","Wrong part of the passage: the angle detail is in the fourth paragraph and explains how geckos let go.","Correct: a water film should have gripped water-attracting surfaces \\"much better,\\" but the setae \\"gripped both about equally well.\\"","This is from the first paragraph\'s list of old observations, not the experiment."]],"reading-0338":["The first three paragraphs are mostly facts: the dome\'s size, the dates, the church conversion, the concrete and pumice. The fourth paragraph starts with \\"I\\" and says the figures don\'t match what she feels. It\'s about her experience of standing under the open top. The first paragraph also describes being inside, but it talks about \\"most visitors\\" in general, not the author\'s own reaction. That difference is what makes the fourth paragraph stand out.",["Correct: after the dimensions, dates, and construction methods, the author turns to what she herself feels: \\"less a roof than a frame for the sky.\\"","Opposite: the author says \\"I believe them\\"; the paragraph gives no new measurements at all.","Wrong part of the passage: the move into ancient history happens in the second paragraph, not the fourth.","Other temples come up only in the second paragraph, and the fourth is entirely about the Pantheon."]],"reading-0339":["The last paragraph shows her feeling. She says the building is \\"less a roof than a frame for the sky\\" and points out that the Romans \\"could have closed the top\\" but \\"chose\\" not to, so that the most solid building in Rome answers to the weather. That\'s admiration. The rain is part of the wonder for her, and the drain holes show it has been handled for centuries, so worry and disappointment don\'t fit.",["Unsupported: she notes that drain holes have carried the rain away \\"for centuries,\\" with no sign of worry.","Opposite: she says the engineering figures don\'t move her; what moves her is the open top.","Correct: she calls the building \\"a frame for the sky\\" and stresses that the Romans \\"chose\\" to leave it open.","Wrong tone: the rain coming in is part of what she admires, not a complaint."]],"reading-0340":["The sentence sets two verbs against each other: \\"I believe them, but I do not feel them.\\" So \\"feel\\" can\'t mean believe; it means something like experiencing or sensing the force of the figures. She knows the numbers are true but doesn\'t sense them while standing there; what she senses instead is the sky. \\"Touch\\" is the everyday meaning trap.",["The most common meaning, but she can\'t touch numbers; she is talking about experiencing them.","She says she does \\"believe them,\\" and then contrasts that with not feeling them, so \\"feel\\" must mean something other than believe.","Correct: she accepts the figures in her mind but doesn\'t experience their meaning when she stands under the dome.","Suffering doesn\'t fit; nothing about the figures hurts her."]],"reading-0341":["The third paragraph gives two weight-saving methods: lighter material higher up (pumice near the opening, heavy stone near the base) and coffers cut into the underside. Only the pumice appears among the choices. The steel and thickness choices reverse what the passage says, and the marble is the floor.",["Correct: \\"near the opening, the builders mixed in light volcanic rock such as pumice.\\"","Opposite: the dome is \\"unreinforced concrete, with no steel rods inside.\\"","Opposite: the shell thins from about six meters to \\"little more than a meter\\" near the opening.","Marble is mentioned only as the floor, and the underside has coffers, not a marble covering."]],"reading-0342":["Read the last paragraph closely. The author refuses one question (\\"Whether 4\'33\\" is music... I have no wish to settle it\\") and then praises the piece\'s effect: ordinary sounds \\"arrive with the weight of notes.\\" So the author appreciates the piece without ruling on the music question. The hard trap is the choice that the author is \\"certain that the piece is music.\\" The author clearly likes it, but specifically declines to say whether it counts as music.",["Opposite: the author mentions that the piece is \\"often described as a joke or a stunt\\" only to push back (\\"but Cage had been moving toward it for years\\").","Too strong: the author says of the music question, \\"I have no wish to settle it.\\"","Unsupported: the author reports the walkouts and the angry artist without taking their side.","Correct: the author has \\"no wish to settle\\" whether it is music but praises how well it works \\"as an instrument for listening.\\""]],"reading-0343":["The paragraph opens with its own topic sentence: the piece is called a stunt, \\"but Cage had been moving toward it for years.\\" Then it gives two earlier influences, the silent room that wasn\'t silent and the white paintings. Its job is to show the piece\'s roots. The other choices either come from a different paragraph or blow up a small detail.",["Wrong paragraph: the audience\'s reaction is in the second paragraph.","Correct: it answers the \\"joke or a stunt\\" view by showing that \\"Cage had been moving toward it for years\\": the chamber visit and Rauschenberg\'s paintings.","Too narrow: the chamber is described in a few words only to set up what Cage heard there.","Too strong: the paintings \\"encouraged\\" Cage; the author never suggests shared credit."]],"reading-0344":["Sounds have no weight, so this is figurative. The question is what it means. The author praises the piece \\"as an instrument for listening\\": during it, a cough or a hum gets the attention you\'d normally give to notes. The trap is the figurative choice about wanting to leave. It borrows the walkouts from the second paragraph, but the author\'s tone here is admiring, not annoyed. The first literal choice is close to the last sentence (\\"the room seems... louder\\"), but \\"seems\\" shows that it\'s the listener\'s sense that changes, not the volume.",["The sounds don\'t actually change; the listener\'s attention does, which is why the author calls the experience \\"odd.\\"","Tudor \\"did not play a note,\\" so no notes are played to imitate anything.","Overreach: some people walked out of the 1952 premiere, but the author describes this effect as the piece working well \\"as an instrument for listening.\\"","Correct: sounds you\'d normally ignore carry the importance of musical notes because the piece makes you listen to them."]],"reading-0345":["The second paragraph begins with Cage\'s view: listeners heard sound, not silence, and \\"that... was the point.\\" The third paragraph supports it with Cage\'s conclusion that there\'s \\"no such thing as silence.\\" So the wind, the rain, and even the muttering were the piece doing its job. Treating them as interruptions gets Cage backward.",["Correct: \\"The audience did not hear silence, and that, Cage insisted, was the point.\\"","Opposite: Cage insisted that hearing those sounds \\"was the point.\\"","Unsupported: the open back of the hall let the forest in, but the passage never calls the hall unsuitable.","Unsupported: the wind and rain were weather, and nothing suggests anyone arranged them."]],"reading-0346":["The sentence lists damage done over \\"the last half century\\": a split leg, loose rungs, a crumbling seat. Rungs that have \\"worked loose\\" have gradually come loose through years of use and movement. The wrong choices use other common meanings of \\"worked\\" (labored, shaped, operated) that don\'t fit something that happened to the chair by itself.",["Correct: over the years, movement had slowly loosened the rungs in their sockets.","\\"Worked\\" often means labored, but rungs can\'t make an effort.","No one was shaping the rungs; they loosened on their own over time.","\\"Operated\\" is another common meaning of \\"worked,\\" but a rung isn\'t a machine being run."]],"reading-0347":["The detail is a setup. The chair looks perfect the next morning, and then \\"A week later, two of the joints had popped apart again.\\" Its job is to show the repair seemed to work before it failed. The other choices contradict the passage or add a detail it doesn\'t give.",["Opposite: a week later \\"two of the joints had popped apart again.\\"","Opposite: the repairman \\"was not impressed by my clamps.\\"","Correct: the chair looked fixed the next morning, which sets up the failure \\"a week later.\\"","Unsupported: the passage never says the chair had been crooked; it came \\"in pieces.\\""]],"reading-0348":["The adult narrator looks back at his nineteen-year-old self with clear eyes: he was \\"sure\\" a chair was simple, his repair fell apart, his glue held \\"nothing,\\" and in the end he thinks about \\"how wrong I was.\\" That\'s an admission of overconfidence, not pride, anger, or defensiveness.",["Opposite: he reports that the quick job failed within a week and calls his idea \\"wrong.\\"","Correct: he was \\"sure that a chair was a simple machine,\\" and later says \\"how wrong I was.\\"","Unsupported: the aunt is mentioned only as where the chair came from.","Opposite: he admits the glue \\"had been holding nothing.\\""]],"reading-0349":["The passage builds to its last lines: \\"I had thought it was force, applied all at once. It was patience, and the slow agreement of materials.\\" Everything before supports that: the rushed glue job fails, and the slow month-long rebuild works. The glue choice is tempting, but it\'s too strong. The passage says why his glue failed, not that glue never works.",["Too broad: the narrator learns from one repairman, but never argues that owners shouldn\'t do repairs; he did much of the work himself.","Too strong: his glue failed because it was spread \\"over a century of dust and old finish,\\" not because glue never works.","Correct: the last paragraph states the lesson: not \\"force, applied all at once\\" but \\"patience, and the slow agreement of materials.\\"","Off focus: the family history is a starting detail, but the passage is about what he learned from the repair."]],"reading-0350":["No one thinks wolves literally carry a valley, so the literal choices are out. The figurative meaning connects to the story she told on every tour: \\"one missing animal can set a broken landscape right.\\" Carrying the whole valley means holding up its entire recovery by themselves. \\"Half believed\\" and her later caution (\\"I tell the story more carefully now\\") show she now sees this as too much credit. The \\"worn out\\" choice reads the image as being about the wolves\' strain, which the passage never discusses.",["Nothing in Passage A describes wolves moving plants; this misreads a figure of speech.","No pups appear in the passage; this invents a literal scene to fit the words.","Correct: it captures the story she told, in which \\"one missing animal can set a broken landscape right.\\"","Overreach: being burdened suggests exhaustion here, but the sentence is about how much she credited the wolves, not their health."]],"reading-0351":["The second paragraph of Passage B gives the explanation step by step: elk browsing drove beavers away, streams without dams cut deeper channels, the water table dropped, and the willows lost water. The tempting wrong choice is the sampling criticism. It\'s in Passage B, but it questions how recovery was measured, not why the willows didn\'t grow.",["Reversed: the popular story in Passage A says the elk \\"stopped lingering along the rivers\\" once wolves returned.","Correct: without beaver dams, \\"the water table dropped, and willows along the banks lost easy access to water.\\"","Wrong passage and wrong idea: the songbirds come from Passage A\'s popular story, which says they nested in the recovering trees.","True of Passage B, but it is a criticism of the studies, not a reason the plants failed to grow."]],"reading-0352":["The sentence says the studies were challenged \\"both for where samples were taken and for how growth was measured,\\" and then mentions a correction lowering an estimate. Those are doubts about the research, so \\"challenged\\" means questioned. The other choices are real meanings of the word that don\'t fit studies.",["To dare someone is to challenge them to do something, but studies can\'t be dared.","A challenging task is a difficult one, but the sentence is about doubts raised over studies.","Correct: critics have raised doubts about \\"where samples were taken\\" and \\"how growth was measured.\\"","Being challenged to a contest doesn\'t fit; no competition is described."]],"reading-0353":["Passage A is built on the narrator\'s own experience: her first winter guiding, the faces of visitors, a pack crossing the snow, the willows she pointed to. Passage B makes the same basic point, that the wolf story is too simple, through research: other predators, beavers and water tables, and corrected studies. So A is more personal. The hard trap is \\"certain that the wolves restored the valley.\\" That was the story she used to tell, but her last paragraph pulls back from it, so it doesn\'t describe her approach in the passage as a whole.",["This describes Passage B, which discusses how growth was measured and a corrected estimate; Passage A gives no measurements.","Neither author says this: Passage A says \\"I still believe the wolves matter,\\" and Passage B says the wolves are not \\"unimportant.\\"","Too strong: she \\"half believed\\" that once, but now tells the story \\"more carefully.\\"","Correct: Passage A is a guide\'s memoir (\\"I told that story on every tour\\"), while Passage B analyzes the research."]],"reading-0354":["The story uses \\"she\\" for Nadia, so it\'s told in the third person. And it gets inside only her head: \\"She did not want the lamp. She wanted him to say something.\\" The father\'s \\"Huh\\" and Sami\'s silences are shown from outside, never explained from within. That\'s a narrator outside the story who mainly reveals one character, Nadia.",["The passage uses \\"she\\" and \\"her\\" for Nadia, never \\"I,\\" so it isn\'t a first-person story.","Correct: the narrator refers to Nadia as \\"she\\" and tells what she wants and remembers, but only guesses at others from outside.","Only Nadia\'s inner thoughts are given; Sami and the father are shown only through what they say and do.","Sami is described from the outside (\\"he said, without turning around\\"), so he isn\'t the narrator."]],"reading-0355":["The sentence says Sami works \\"in a fixed order\\" and then gives it: books, clothes, shelves. That\'s a set pattern, the way \\"he did everything.\\" So \\"fixed\\" means unchanging. The tempting wrong choice is \\"repaired,\\" the everyday meaning, which makes no sense for someone taking a room apart.",["\\"Repaired\\" is the most common meaning of \\"fixed,\\" but nothing is being repaired; he is taking things apart.","Correct: he always packs in the same order: \\"books first, then clothes, then the shelves.\\"","\\"Fastened\\" fits a fixed object, not an order of tasks.","A \\"fixed\\" game is rigged, but nothing dishonest is happening."]],"reading-0356":["Nadia wanted Sami to say something about leaving and the thin wall. Sami, who \\"had never been a talker,\\" finally does, in his own way. Hearing \\"when nobody\'s there\\" means hearing the quiet where his page-turning used to be. That\'s his way of saying he knows she\'ll miss him. Her reaction (\\"She laughed, and then she didn\'t\\") and his hand on her head confirm the moment is tender. The safety reading takes the words too literally, and nothing supports resentment or a return.",["Correct: \\"hear when nobody\'s there\\" points to the silence his absence will leave, an indirect admission from a brother who \\"had never been a talker.\\"","Literal misreading: \\"nobody\'s there\\" means his room will be empty, not that intruders might come.","Unsupported: nothing in the passage suggests he plans to return.","Wrong tone: he sits beside her and touches her head; nothing suggests resentment."]],"reading-0357":["Follow the paragraphs: a mail crisis, earlier fixes, how the five digits work, how people were persuaded to use them, and what the codes later became. That\'s an explanation of where ZIP codes came from and how they spread. The wrong choices take one paragraph\'s detail or add an argument the author never makes.",["Too narrow: Moon appears in one paragraph, and the passage tells nothing else about his career.","Wrong verb: the passage says his idea \\"went unused\\" but never argues it should have been adopted sooner.","Wrong verb and too narrow: marketers appear only in the last paragraph, and the author reports their use without criticizing it.","Correct: the passage covers the mail problem, the code\'s design, the campaign to get people to use it, and its later uses."]],"reading-0358":["The third paragraph breaks the code down in order: first digit, a broad region; next two, a central processing facility; last two, a local post office or delivery zone. Each wrong choice is the job of a different part of the code.",["Wrong digits: the last two digits identify a local post office or delivery zone.","Correct: \\"The first digit named a broad region of the country, from 0 in the Northeast to 9 in the West.\\"","Wrong digits: \\"the next two\\" point to a central facility.","Wrong digits: delivery zones are the job of the last two digits (and of the older wartime city zones)."]],"reading-0359":["In the sentence, each part of the code stands for something: the first digit \\"named\\" a region, the next two \\"pointed to\\" a facility, and the last two \\"identified\\" a post office. \\"Pointed to\\" works like the other two verbs: it means referred to. The other choices are real meanings of \\"point\\" that don\'t fit numbers.",["Gesturing with a finger is the most common meaning, but digits can\'t gesture.","A pointed stick is sharpened, but that meaning doesn\'t fit numbers.","Nothing in the sentence is moving; the digits stand for a place.","Correct: the two digits referred to, or identified, a central mail-processing facility."]],"reading-0360":["Look for wording that hands a claim to someone else. The last sentence says ZIP codes describe \\"where, and, some argue, who, a person is.\\" The phrase \\"some argue\\" marks the idea about who a person is as a view held by others. The author states the other three choices plainly as facts.",["The author states this directly in the first paragraph, as a fact.","The author states this flatly as a fact in the fourth paragraph.","Correct: the author marks this idea with \\"some argue,\\" attributing it to others.","The author reports this as a fact in the second paragraph."]],"reading-0361":["The paragraph goes on to show how each result hurt an older idea: a warning \\"means little\\" from a moth that tastes fine, and startled bats \\"should have gotten used to the noise.\\" So the results weakened those explanations. The other choices are real meanings of \\"undercut\\" (physical cutting, pricing) that don\'t fit evidence and ideas.",["This is the word\'s physical meaning, but results can\'t cut anything.","Correct: each result weakened an old explanation, as the rest of the paragraph shows.","To undercut a competitor is to charge less, but no prices are involved here.","Nothing is made shorter; the explanations are made less believable."]],"reading-0362":["The fourth paragraph matches each finding to the idea it weakens. The warning idea fails because this moth \\"does not taste bad\\": there\'s nothing to warn about. The strongest trap is the bats not improving over the nights. That result is real, but the passage uses it against the startle idea. Toxins in other tiger moths explain why the warning idea existed, not why it failed here.",["Wrong explanation: this is the result that argues against the startle idea, since startled bats \\"should have gotten used to the noise.\\"","True detail, but the passage never connects the clicking rate to the warning idea.","This is the reason the warning idea was proposed in the first place, not evidence against it.","Correct: \\"A warning means little coming from a moth that tastes fine.\\""]],"reading-0363":["The last paragraph says it plainly: \\"There is no final winner in this contest, and there may never be one.\\" Then it gives an example of a bat adapting in its turn. The author sees an ongoing back-and-forth, not a coming victory for either side.",["Opposite: the author says \\"there is no final winner,\\" and the barbastelle shows a bat that gets around moths\' hearing.","Correct: \\"There is no final winner in this contest, and there may never be one.\\"","Unsupported: the barbastelle is given as one example of a bat\'s trick, not as a sign that bats are winning.","Wrong tone: the author is interested enough to give a new example of the back-and-forth."]],"reading-0364":["Follow the whole passage\'s logic. The first two paragraphs set out the two roads and Venuti\'s case against domesticating. The third gives \\"the case against Venuti.\\" The fourth answers it: Venuti \\"never asked translators to be clumsy,\\" only for \\"a little resistance,\\" and the author\'s ideal translation is \\"fluent enough to carry me and strange enough\\" to feel foreign. That is a defense of a moderate Venuti against the charge that his approach makes books unreadable. The history choice describes only the setup, and the fluency choice flips the author\'s side.",["Too narrow and too neutral: the history fills the first two paragraphs, but only to set up the argument the author makes at the end.","Correct: the author states the objections (hard to read, readers want absorption), grants they are fair, and argues that Venuti asked only for \\"a little resistance.\\"","Reversed: the author grants that the objections are fair but says \\"neither... quite reaches the point.\\"","Venuti gave Schleiermacher\'s two roads \\"new names\\"; he built on them rather than rejecting them."]],"reading-0365":["The third paragraph opens \\"The case against Venuti is not hard to make,\\" so the statement belongs to the objection: strange, difficult translations get abandoned, and an abandoned book teaches nothing. The author states it in its strongest form and then answers it in the last paragraph: Venuti wanted resistance, not obstacles. Calling the objection \\"fair\\" is not the same as agreeing that fluency matters most, which is the trap in the last choice.",["Correct: it is part of \\"the case against Venuti,\\" and the next paragraph replies that \\"a little resistance is not the same as an obstacle.\\"","Reversed: publishers have preferred the domesticating road, translations that \\"read fluently.\\"","Wrong side: the statement comes from the case against Venuti, not from his argument.","Too far: the author calls the objections \\"fair\\" but says neither \\"quite reaches the point.\\""]],"reading-0366":["The key sentence has two halves. \\"Both objections are fair\\" shows respect, and \\"neither... quite reaches the point\\" shows the author isn\'t convinced. The rest of the paragraph explains what they miss: Venuti asked for resistance, not clumsiness. The undecided choice is the subtle trap. The author grants the objections some force but clearly comes down on Venuti\'s side.",["Contradicted: the author says \\"Both objections are fair.\\"","Reversed: the author goes on to defend Venuti and ends by describing the kind of translation Venuti wanted.","The author does decide: \\"neither, I think, quite reaches the point,\\" and the last paragraph takes a side.","Correct: \\"Both objections are fair, and neither, I think, quite reaches the point.\\""]],"reading-0367":["The second paragraph says it directly: \\"reviewers praise a translation by saying that one forgets it is a translation.\\" That is the fluent, domesticating ideal. The other choices describe what Venuti wanted, or something the passage never discusses.",["This is closer to what Venuti wanted; the passage never says reviewers praise it.","Reversed: Venuti\'s complaint is that fluent translations hide the translator\'s choices.","Correct: \\"reviewers praise a translation by saying that one forgets it is a translation.\\"","The passage never mentions word-for-word translation."]],"reading-0368":["Connect the second and third paragraphs. David\'s argument needs two things: QWERTY got locked in, and the locked-out design was better. For the second part he \\"pointed to a 1944 U.S. Navy study.\\" Liebowitz and Margolis then attack that very study, showing it was supervised by the patent\'s owner, with groups that weren\'t comparable. So their criticism removes David\'s evidence that Dvorak\'s design was better. The extreme choice goes beyond the last paragraph, which says only that people making the argument \\"must find another\\" example.",["Correct: \\"For evidence that the better design had lost, David pointed to a 1944 U.S. Navy study\\"; undermine the study, and his example loses its support.","Wrong person: the passage says Dvorak himself supervised the study; David only cited it.","Reversed: the Navy study favored Dvorak\'s keyboard; the 1956 study found no clear gain.","Too extreme: the passage is about this one example; it never says no other evidence of lock-in exists."]],"reading-0369":["The last paragraph lays out two cases with \\"If.\\" If Liebowitz and Margolis are right, QWERTY wasn\'t a trap, so it stops being evidence that markets lock in worse choices, and anyone who wants to argue that \\"must find another\\" example. That\'s a claim about this example, not about all markets, which is why \\"can never\\" is too strong. \\"No clear gain\\" for Dvorak\'s keyboard doesn\'t mean it is slower.",["Too strong: the author says only that the famous example \\"turns out to be no example at all\\"; other examples might exist.","Too strong: they concluded only that no reliable evidence showed Dvorak\'s design \\"to be much better.\\"","Unsupported: the passage never ties lock-in to the cost of retraining.","Correct: if QWERTY wasn\'t a trap, \\"the most famous example of such a trap turns out to be no example at all.\\""]],"reading-0370":["The first three paragraphs tell the history and the evidence: the layout, David\'s argument, and the economists\' attack on the Navy study. The last paragraph steps back: \\"The quarrel was never really about keyboards.\\" It explains the stakes for a broader idea about markets. The tempting wrong answer says the author declares a winner, but the paragraph is built on two \\"If\\" sentences that describe what follows from each side without choosing.",["No new evidence appears in the last paragraph; it discusses what the evidence implies.","The near miss: the author sets out both possibilities with \\"If... If\\" and never says which side was right.","Correct: \\"The quarrel was never really about keyboards\\"; it was about whether markets can lock in inferior choices.","Dvorak appears only in the first and third paragraphs; the last paragraph doesn\'t mention him."]],"reading-0371":["Only someone at the house could have put the radio, wrapped in the father\'s flannel shirt, in the narrator\'s car, and the narrator says \\"I had not put it there.\\" So the mother saved the one thing she had seemed to throw away \\"without looking up.\\" That changes the meaning of that moment: it wasn\'t coldness but a private decision to pass the radio on. The other moments either really happened as they seemed or were never in doubt.",["The lathe really was sold, as planned; nothing in the last paragraph changes what that moment meant.","Correct: the radio she marked Gone turns up wrapped in his shirt in the narrator\'s car, so her quick \\"Gone\\" was not indifference.","The pencils show the father\'s care, which the narrator already sees; the radio doesn\'t change that moment.","The bags are a closing image of the emptied workshop; the discovery doesn\'t revise them."]],"reading-0372":["Combine two clues. At noon, once she is alone, the mother is found sitting on a wheelbarrow, with an excuse the narrator \\"believed... or decided to.\\" At the end, the radio she marked Gone is hidden, wrapped in his shirt, in the narrator\'s car. Someone indifferent wouldn\'t do either. Her efficiency (\\"decided that the workshop would take one day\\") is how she gets through something that hurts. The first choice is the surface reading the story sets up and then overturns.",["This takes her manner at face value (\\"as if she were clearing a stranger\'s desk\\"), which the side yard and the radio contradict.","Unsupported: she never criticizes the narrator\'s pace, and the work finished on schedule.","Correct: alone at noon she sits in the yard with a \\"headache\\" the narrator half doubts, and she secretly saves his radio for the narrator.","Unsupported: the day\'s whole goal is to empty the workshop; the lathe is sold and the shelves are left bare."]],"reading-0373":["The narrator says little about feelings directly (\\"Instead I sorted\\"), but the details are chosen with care: each can \\"labeled in my father\'s square capitals,\\" blades \\"wrapped in oiled cloth,\\" pencils \\"every one to the same length.\\" The feeling is held back and carried by the objects. The trap is \\"detached and indifferent\\": that\'s how the mother appears in the same paragraph, not how the narrator writes.",["Correct: the narrator expected to cry but \\"Instead I sorted,\\" and the feeling shows in loving details: square capitals, oiled cloth, pencils of equal length.","Contradicted: the narrator \\"expected to cry\\" but didn\'t; there are no tears in the paragraph.","The narrator notes the mother\'s manner but never blames her; the story later suggests her coldness was a cover.","Half right: it describes how the mother seemed, but the narrator\'s careful attention to the father\'s things is far from indifferent."]],"reading-0374":["To rule out the horses themselves, you need a comparison on the same animal. The last paragraph gives one: on the coated horses\' \\"uncovered heads, flies landed as often as ever.\\" Same smell, same tail, same behavior, yet landings dropped only where the coat was. The fly-behavior choices are true, but they describe what happened at the coat, not why the horse can be ruled out.",["This shows the flies were drawn to the striped coats, not that the horses themselves weren\'t the cause.","Correct: the same horse got normal landings where it was uncovered, so whatever stopped the flies was the coat, not the horse.","This describes how the landings failed, but it doesn\'t rule out something about the horse as the cause.","The location tells nothing about what kept the flies off."]],"reading-0375":["The dispute in the fourth paragraph hinges on a tangle: warm places tend to have many biting flies, so a link between stripes and warmth fits either explanation. The supposed finding untangles them, with warmth but few flies, and the stripes are still bold. That favors heat, Larison\'s explanation. The first choice repeats Caro\'s reply, which works only when heat and flies occur together.",["This is Caro\'s argument in the fourth paragraph, but the supposed finding is a case where heat and flies don\'t go together, so that argument doesn\'t apply.","Unrelated: the finding is about heat and flies; it says nothing about grass.","The farm experiment tested fly landings directly; a map of stripes and flies doesn\'t confirm it.","Correct: Caro\'s reply was that heat and flies go together; a warm, fly-free place with bold stripes points to heat."]],"reading-0376":["The second and third paragraphs both describe surveys of where striping is strongest, matched against flies or temperature. The last paragraph is different: an experiment that watches flies land, or fail to land, on striped and plain coats. It tests what stripes do. Each wrong choice describes a different paragraph or contradicts the passage\'s own caution.",["True of the third paragraph (Larison\'s team), not the last.","That is the fourth paragraph, not the last.","Correct: the second and third paragraphs compare where stripes are strongest; the last watches flies landing on coats.","Contradicted: \\"The experiment cannot say why stripes first evolved.\\""]],"reading-0377":["The first paragraph gives the reason directly: no one can raise unstriped zebras to compare with striped ones. The other choices are either never mentioned or contradicted by the passage.",["Correct: \\"None has been easy to test, since no one can raise a herd of unstriped zebras for comparison.\\"","Contradicted: striping is strongest where biting flies thrive.","Unsupported: the passage never discusses lions on farms.","Unsupported: the passage never says stripes fade."]],"reading-0378":["The narrator\'s complaint is not that the typed notes were missing anything. Students \\"could find exactly what I had said.\\" The problem is that the notes were \\"transcripts\\" and the students \\"could not tell you what it meant.\\" Copying everything replaced understanding. The brevity choice gets it backward: short notes were the handwritten ones.",["Unsupported: the students \\"could find exactly what I had said\\"; the facts were there.","Correct: the notes were \\"transcripts,\\" and students could find what was said but \\"could not tell you what it meant.\\"","Reversed: typed notes were complete transcripts; it was the handwritten notes that were brief.","Unsupported: nothing suggests the notes were lost."]],"reading-0379":["Line up the explanations. In Passage A, pens slow students down, \\"so they had to choose,\\" and their notes became \\"theirs.\\" In Passage B, the authors proposed that \\"because handwriting is slow, it forces note takers to summarize.\\" Same idea: slowness forces processing. The review-time choice borrows A\'s \\"wrote less\\" but adds a reason neither author gives.",["Neither passage mentions memory for physical motion.","A common real-world argument, but neither passage makes it.","Passage A says students \\"wrote less,\\" but neither passage says shorter notes help because they\'re quicker to review.","Correct: A says students \\"could not keep up, so they had to choose\\"; B says slowness \\"forces note takers to summarize.\\""]],"reading-0380":["Passage B\'s last paragraph gives its view of rules like the narrator\'s: schools \\"wrote a rule on the strength of a result that has not held up well,\\" though note taking \\"may matter for some students or some material.\\" So the author would question the research basis without saying the narrator is wrong about her own class. The other choices either misstate a study\'s result or are far stronger than anything Passage B says.",["Passage B says the 2014 result \\"has not held up well,\\" so the author wouldn\'t call it proof.","Correct: B says such rules rest on \\"a result that has not held up well,\\" but that note taking \\"may matter for some students or some material.\\"","Wrong study result: the 2019 team found small, inconsistent differences, not that laptops were better.","Too extreme: B never discusses personal experience, and it allows that method may matter for some students."]],"reading-0381":["Passage A\'s evidence is personal: her students\' notes and essays one year, offered with a hedge (\\"I cannot prove the pens did it\\"). Passage B\'s evidence is research: the 2014 finding and the 2019 attempt to repeat it, which mostly failed. The first choice is the strongest trap because its first half fits A\'s hedge, but its second half is wrong about B.",["Half wrong: A does hedge (\\"I cannot prove the pens did it\\"), but B doesn\'t treat it as settled; it says the result \\"has not held up well.\\"","Reversed: A relies on a classroom, and B is the passage that discusses research.","A\'s example (\\"why the Congress of Vienna mattered\\") is about meaning, and B discusses both factual and conceptual questions.","Correct: A draws on one year with her students; B sets the 2014 study against the 2019 attempt to repeat it."]],"reading-0382":["The author explains what the front \\"said\\": the arched windows \\"were tall because the hall behind them rose two stories,\\" and the door \\"announced the weight of the vault.\\" The claim is that these signals now lie, and the next sentence gives the proof: \\"the windows open onto a ramp for cars.\\" The tower choice is the near miss. It shows something new was built, but not that a feature of the front now tells a false story.",["This describes the construction, not what the front now communicates.","Correct: the windows were tall because a two-story hall stood behind them; now they announce a hall that isn\'t there.","This shows the building changed, but the author\'s point is that specific features now mislead, as the windows do.","This is the defenders\' argument from the second paragraph, not support for the author\'s claim."]],"reading-0383":["The second paragraph is the defenders\' paragraph (\\"Its defenders make a reasonable case... the argument runs\\"). The sentence about the pedestrian shows their argument working on the author\'s own example: the part the public sees is still there. The author answers in the third paragraph (\\"But it rests on an assumption I do not share\\"). Mistaking the defenders\' voice for the author\'s leads to the second choice.",["Correct: it applies the defenders\' argument (\\"saves what the public actually sees\\") to the bank from the first paragraph.","Wrong voice: the paragraph presents the defenders\' case; the author\'s own view begins with \\"But\\" in the next paragraph.","Unsupported: the passage never says this owner was forbidden to change the bank.","Contradicted: the sentence assumes pedestrians do notice and value the front."]],"reading-0384":["The last sentence states the author\'s preference outright: \\"save fewer buildings whole than many as masks.\\" The author criticizes facadism, doesn\'t want old buildings simply replaced, and accepts that cities must change, so a total freeze is too strong.",["Too extreme: the author accepts the point that \\"a city cannot be preserved in amber\\" and accepts facadism \\"on some streets.\\"","The author wants old buildings saved; the objection is only to saving them as masks.","Correct: \\"Better, I think, to save fewer buildings whole than many as masks.\\"","This is facadism itself, the practice the author argues against."]],"reading-0385":["Find each year\'s count of late items. 2022: 9% of 610,000 = 54,900. 2024: 11% of 702,000 = 77,220. Difference: about 22,300. Both the share and the number of checkouts went up, so the change is bigger than either change alone suggests. Each wrong choice handles only one of those two changes.",["This applies the 2-point change in share only to the 92,000 extra checkouts.","This counts late returns only among the 92,000 extra checkouts (11% of 92,000).","Correct: 11% of 702,000 is about 77,200, and 9% of 610,000 is about 54,900; the difference is about 22,300.","This applies the 2-point rise to 2024\'s checkouts and ignores that checkouts also rose."]],"reading-0386":["The critics predicted borrowers would keep books longer. Two rows measure that: the share returned late (9% to 11%) and average days late (12 to 13). Both moved the predicted way, but only a little, which matches the author\'s \\"Late returns did rise slightly.\\" The blocked-cards row is a true change, but it answers a different question.",["Correct: the late share rose from 9% to 11% and average lateness from 12 to 13 days, small changes in the predicted direction.","Reversed: items never returned fell, from 2,900 to 2,300.","Reversed: the late share rose, from 9% to 11%.","True, but blocked cards don\'t measure how long borrowers keep books."]],"reading-0387":["The count fell from 2,900 to 2,300. Because checkouts rose (610,000 to 702,000), the share fell even more: 2,900 / 610,000 is about 0.48%, and 2,300 / 702,000 is about 0.33%. Fewer items, out of more checkouts, means a smaller share. Don\'t mix up the \\"returned late\\" row, which rose, with the \\"never returned\\" row.",["Miscalculation: the share fell too, from about 0.48% (2,900 of 610,000) to about 0.33% (2,300 of 702,000).","Correct: the number fell (2,900 to 2,300) while checkouts rose, so the share fell as well, from about 0.48% to about 0.33%.","This confuses late returns, which rose, with items never returned, which fell.","The rise in checkouts makes the drop in share larger, not smaller."]],"reading-0388":["The first paragraph lists the libraries\' reasons: fines raise little money, cost staff time, and fall hardest on poor families. Keeping books moving was what fines were meant to do, so that choice is on the wrong side.",["Correct: \\"the fines raise little money, cost staff time to collect, and fall hardest on poor families.\\"","Unsupported: the passage never says the fines were too small.","Wrong side: this was the original purpose of fines, not the case against them.","Contradicted: the fines \\"cost staff time to collect.\\""]],"reading-0389":["Follow the paragraph\'s turn. Tucker argued that adult European eels couldn\'t reach the Sargasso, so young eels in Europe must come from American eels. Genetics proved him wrong. \\"But his doubt pointed to a real gap\\": no one had followed an adult there. The next paragraph fills that gap with the 2022 tags. So the Tucker discussion presents an objection that failed but still showed what evidence was missing. The trap is the choice that repeats Tucker\'s own idea as if the passage endorsed it.",["Unsupported: Tucker doubted that adult eels could make the trip; he never said Schmidt\'s larvae came from the wrong place.","Reversed: this was Tucker\'s proposal, but \\"later genetic studies showed that European and American eels are separate species.\\"","Wrong paragraph: the satellite tags are described in the fourth paragraph.","Correct: Tucker\'s idea \\"was abandoned,\\" but \\"his doubt pointed to a real gap,\\" which the 2022 tracking study then filled."]],"reading-0390":["The last paragraph balances two things. The author calls the case \\"now strong,\\" and then adds \\"though\\": the evidence is young eels and tracked adults, not a sighting of spawning. That is confidence with a stated limit. The doubtful and certain choices each take one half of the paragraph and ignore the other, and both contradict specific facts in the passage.",["Contradicted: \\"Several of the eels had reached the Sargasso Sea.\\"","Contradicted: \\"no one has yet seen European eels spawn in the wild.\\"","Correct: \\"The case for the Sargasso is now strong,\\" but it rests on larvae and tracking, \\"not on anyone having watched the eels spawn.\\"","Wrong focus: Aristotle appears only in the first paragraph, as part of the history."]],"reading-0391":["The second paragraph explains Schmidt\'s reasoning: the larvae got smaller the farther west and south he sailed, and the smallest were in the Sargasso Sea, so that\'s where eels must be born. The tag choice describes the 2022 study, and the last paragraph says no one has watched eels spawn.",["Contradicted: no one has seen European eels spawn in the wild.","Wrong time: the satellite tags came in 2022, long after Schmidt.","Unsupported: Aristotle thought eels arose from mud.","Correct: \\"he had found the smallest of all in the Sargasso Sea.\\""]],"reading-0392":["The first paragraph says the feed \\"was kept in a metal can beside the back steps, with a brick on the lid.\\" The ramp and the garden are real places in the story, but neither is where the feed is kept.",["Wrong detail: the ramp is where the hens walk down each morning; the feed is somewhere else.","Correct: \\"Their feed was kept in a metal can beside the back steps.\\"","Unsupported: the narrator carries eggs inside, but the feed is never kept in the kitchen.","Wrong detail: the garden is where the hens got loose; no shed is mentioned."]],"reading-0393":["Mrs. Adeyemi\'s most serious rule was to latch the door \\"every night.\\" The next morning the door was \\"swinging open\\" and all six hens were in the garden, so the mistake was not latching the door. The other choices name things from the passage that never went wrong.",["Correct: the next morning \\"the coop door [was] swinging open,\\" and the hens were in the garden.","Reversed: the narrator carried the eggs \\"as if they were made of glass\\"; nothing broke.","Unsupported: the passage never says the narrator missed watering the hens.","Unsupported: the brick on the feed can is mentioned, but nothing happens to it."]],"reading-0394":["Mrs. Adeyemi says to latch the door \\"Every night. A raccoon only needs one chance.\\" One night with the door open is all the opening a raccoon needs, so \\"chance\\" means opportunity. The other choices are real meanings of \\"chance\\" from other phrases (\\"by chance,\\" \\"take a chance,\\" \\"a good chance of rain\\").",["\\"By chance\\" means by luck, but Mrs. Adeyemi means an opening the raccoon can use.","\\"Take a chance\\" means take a risk, but the raccoon isn\'t taking a risk; it is waiting for an opening.","Correct: one unlatched night gives a raccoon the opportunity it needs to get at the hens.","\\"A good chance of rain\\" means likelihood, but she isn\'t talking about how probable something is."]],"reading-0395":["The fourth paragraph begins \\"After that\\" and shows the change: checking the latch again and again, learning each hen, and by Friday knowing from the sounds whether something was wrong. Its job is to show the narrator becoming careful and skilled. Each wrong choice describes a different paragraph.",["Wrong paragraph: the hens get loose in the third paragraph.","Wrong paragraph: the rules are given in the second paragraph.","Wrong paragraph: Mrs. Adeyemi comes home in the fifth paragraph.","Correct: the narrator checks the latch three times and, by Friday, can tell \\"whether something was wrong\\" from the sounds."]],"reading-0396":["Each paragraph covers one way the sea otter copes with cold water: its fur, grooming, eating a lot (with rock tools), and sleeping wrapped in kelp. Together, that\'s how otters stay warm, fed, and safe. The rock-tool choice is a real detail, but only one part of one paragraph.",["Correct: the passage covers the otter\'s fur and grooming, its eating and tool use, and how it sleeps without drifting.","Too narrow, and the wrong animal: seals and whales are mentioned only in the first sentence, as a contrast.","Too narrow: rock tools are one detail in the third paragraph.","Unsupported: the passage never discusses where otters live or their numbers."]],"reading-0397":["The first paragraph explains it: the fur \\"traps a layer of air against the otter\'s skin, and that air keeps the cold water\\" off. The fat choice describes seals and whales, the animals the otter is being contrasted with.",["Wrong animal: fat (blubber) is how seals and whales stay warm; the otter \\"has none.\\"","Correct: \\"The fur traps a layer of air against the otter\'s skin.\\"","Reversed: the trapped air \\"keeps the cold water from ever touching\\" the skin.","Unsupported: grooming keeps the fur clean and fluffed; it doesn\'t make the fur grow."]],"reading-0398":["The third paragraph says some otters keep a favorite rock \\"tucked into a loose pouch of skin under one forearm.\\" The kelp choice borrows a detail from the last paragraph, about sleeping.",["Wrong detail: kelp is what otters wrap themselves in to keep from drifting.","Unsupported: the passage says otters carry the rock \\"from meal to meal.\\"","Correct: \\"Some otters even keep a favorite rock tucked into a loose pouch of skin under one forearm.\\"","Unsupported: otters use their paws for grooming and holding hands; the rock goes in a pouch."]],"reading-0399":["\\"A coat like that needs constant care,\\" and the paragraph goes on to explain the care: rubbing, combing, squeezing, and blowing air into the fur. That\'s upkeep. The other choices are real meanings of \\"care\\" that don\'t fit what the paragraph describes.",["\\"Care\\" can mean worry, but the sentence is about what the fur needs done to it.","\\"Care\\" can mean fondness, but the paragraph is about cleaning and fluffing, not affection.","\\"Caution\\" is a near miss: the otter isn\'t being careful; it is maintaining its coat.","Correct: the fur needs regular upkeep; the otter spends hours grooming it."]],"reading-0400":["The third paragraph gives the terms: forty shillings to join and ten shillings every year after that. The reversed choice swaps the two amounts, so check which number goes with which payment.",["Contradicted: members paid to join, and the money bought the books.","Correct: each agreed \\"to pay forty shillings to join and ten shillings every year after that.\\"","Unsupported: the passage describes a joining fee and yearly dues, not a charge per book.","Reversed: the larger amount, forty shillings, was the joining fee."]],"reading-0401":["The second paragraph says the shared-books plan lasted about a year, until \\"the members took their books home again.\\" The London order and the librarian both belong to the Library Company that came after, in the third paragraph.",["Correct: \\"The plan did not last; after about a year, the members took their books home again.\\"","Wrong plan: ordering books from London was part of the later Library Company.","Wrong plan, and reversed: the librarian belonged to the Library Company, whose members could take books home.","Unsupported: wealthy families are mentioned only as owners of private libraries."]],"reading-0402":["The first paragraph describes the problem: books were scarce and expensive, and few people outside wealthy families owned many. That sets up Franklin\'s solution. Each wrong choice describes a different paragraph.",["Wrong paragraph: the Junto is described in the second paragraph.","Wrong paragraph: the fees are explained in the third paragraph.","Correct: books were \\"scarce and expensive,\\" so a young worker \\"had few books to choose from,\\" which sets up the library.","Wrong paragraph: the spread to other towns is in the last paragraph."]],"reading-0403":["Every paragraph comes back to one idea: kintsugi repairs breaks with gold and makes them visible. The repair \\"does not pretend it was never broken.\\" The origin story and the drying time are details within that idea, and the point about value is hedged (\\"in the eyes of many collectors\\").",["Correct: the passage explains how kintsugi fills cracks with gold and why its makers show the break.","Too narrow: this is only a \\"popular story\\" in the second paragraph, and historians cannot confirm it.","Too narrow: the slowness of the work is the topic of one paragraph.","Too strong: the passage says only that \\"many collectors\\" value mended bowls more."]],"reading-0404":["The sentence says the goal is to \\"follow\\" the break \\"exactly, so that the gold traces the path the crack actually took.\\" The next words explain it: follow means trace along the line. The other choices are real meanings of \\"follow\\" that don\'t fit a crack.",["The most common meaning, but the repairer isn\'t coming after the crack in time.","\\"Follow the rules\\" means obey, but a crack gives no orders.","\\"Follow\\" can mean understand, as in following a story, but the repairer is filling the crack along its path.","Correct: the gold traces \\"the path the crack actually took.\\""]],"reading-0405":["The second paragraph says the bowl \\"returned held together with ugly metal staples,\\" and Japanese craftsmen then looked for a more beautiful way to mend pottery. The gold choice mixes up the result of the story with the craft it supposedly led to.",["This describes kintsugi, the craft that the story says came later.","Correct: \\"It returned held together with ugly metal staples.\\"","Unsupported: the passage says the same bowl came back, repaired with staples.","Contradicted: it came back mended, just with staples."]],"science-0001":["Read down the table: as height goes 10 → 20 → 30 → 40 cm, time goes 2.0 → 1.4 → 1.2 → 1.0 s, and every value is smaller than the one before. So time decreased only. \\"Increased only\\" mixes up the direction, and \\"increased, then decreased\\" would need the time to rise at some point, which never happens. Trend questions are about direction, not the size of each change.",["Reversed trend: the times get smaller as height increases, not larger.","Correct: every time in the table is lower than the one before it.","Non-monotonic choice: the time never goes up at any point in the table.","\\"Stayed the same\\" ignores the drop from 2.0 s to 1.0 s."]],"science-0002":["Find the 12 m row, then move across to the July DO column: 1.9 mg/L. The other choices are the values one row up (4.2), one row down (0.8), and the February column (11.5). With wide tables, put a finger on the row and check the column heading before you answer.",["Adjacent row: 0.8 mg/L is the July DO at 16 m.","Correct: the 12 m row, July DO column, reads 1.9 mg/L.","Adjacent row: 4.2 mg/L is the July DO at 8 m.","Wrong column: 11.5 mg/L is the February DO at 12 m."]],"science-0003":["Read only the February temperature column, top to bottom: 0.4, 2.9, 3.7, 4.0, 4.0°C. It rises through 12 m and then stays at 4.0°C. \\"Increased only\\" misses that the last two values are equal, and \\"decreased only\\" is the July pattern, not February\'s. (Under ice, the coldest water is at the top, just below the ice.)",["Close, but the temperature stops rising: it is 4.0°C at both 12 m and 16 m.","Reversed trend: in February the water gets warmer with depth (it is the July column that gets colder).","Order reversed: the rise comes first (0.4 to 4.0°C), and the steady part comes last.","Correct: 0.4 → 2.9 → 3.7 → 4.0°C, then 4.0°C again at 16 m."]],"science-0004":["In July, DO drops at every step down: 8.6, 8.1, 4.2, 1.9, 0.8 mg/L. A depth of 6 m lies between the 4 m and 8 m measurements, so its DO should lie between their values, 8.1 and 4.2 mg/L.",["This is below the 12 m value; 6 m lies between 4 m and 8 m.","Missed by one interval: this range belongs between 8 m and 12 m.","Correct: 6 m is between 4 m (8.1 mg/L) and 8 m (4.2 mg/L), and July DO falls steadily with depth.","Wrong direction: in July, DO decreases with depth, so 6 m should be lower than the 4 m value."]],"science-0005":["Compute each decrease. July: 8.6 − 0.8 = 7.8 mg/L. February: 12.4 − 11.2 = 1.2 mg/L. Five times the February decrease is 5 × 1.2 = 6.0 mg/L, and 7.8 > 6.0, so the prediction is supported. In Yes/No questions, both parts must be right: the decision AND the numbers that justify it.",["Correct: 8.6 − 0.8 = 7.8 and 12.4 − 11.2 = 1.2; 5 × 1.2 = 6.0, and 7.8 is more than that.","Misread number: 8.6 mg/L is the July DO at the surface, not the decrease from 0 m to 16 m.","Right numbers, wrong decision: 5 × 1.2 is only 6.0, so 7.8 IS more than 5 times as large.","Misread number: 11.2 mg/L is the February DO at 16 m, not the decrease."]],"science-0006":["Use the 40°C row: Salt X 61 g/100 g water, Salt Z 59 g/100 g water. Since 61 > 59, Salt X was (barely) more soluble. The close values are the trap: at 20°C Salt Z is far ahead, so it\'s easy to assume Z is still ahead at 40°C.",["Wrong row: these are the 20°C values.","Right numbers, wrong decision: 61 is greater than 59, so Salt X WAS more soluble.","Right decision, wrong row: these are the 60°C values.","Correct: at 40°C, Salt X dissolves 61 g and Salt Z 59 g per 100 g water, and 61 > 59."]],"science-0007":["The solubilities are equal where the two columns cross. At 20°C, Z > X (66 vs. 32). At 40°C, X > Z (61 vs. 59). Since the order flips between those rows, the crossing is between 20°C and 40°C (close to 40°C, because 61 and 59 are nearly equal).",["At both 0°C and 20°C, Salt Z is still more soluble than Salt X (74 vs. 14, 66 vs. 32).","Correct: at 20°C Salt Z is higher (66 vs. 32); at 40°C Salt X is higher (61 vs. 59), so they cross in between.","One interval too high: by 40°C Salt X has already passed Salt Z.","This is where Salt X pulls far ahead (106 vs. 52 and 168 vs. 47), long after they were equal."]],"science-0008":["At 80°C, up to 168 g of Salt X dissolves in 100 g of water, so all 150 g dissolves. At 40°C, only 61 g can stay dissolved. The rest must come out as crystals: 150 − 61 = 89 g. The trap 107 g uses 168 g, but the student only added 150 g.",["Subtracted 150 from the 80°C solubility (168 − 150); that is how much MORE could have dissolved at 80°C.","Wrong quantity: 61 g is the mass that stays dissolved at 40°C, not the mass that comes out.","Correct: all 150 g dissolves at 80°C, but only 61 g can stay dissolved at 40°C: 150 − 61 = 89 g.","Used the full 80°C solubility (168 g) instead of the 150 g actually added: 168 − 61."]],"science-0009":["The independent variable is the one the experimenter deliberately changes. In Experiment 1, the student moved the lamp to 10, 20, 30 and 40 cm. The number of bubbles is the dependent variable (the result), and the water temperature was kept at 22°C.",["Correct: the student chose and changed the lamp distance in Experiment 1.","Dependent variable: the bubble count is what was measured in response.","Wrong experiment: filter color was varied in Experiment 2, and no filter was used in Experiment 1.","Held constant: the water bath stayed at 22°C in every trial."]],"science-0010":["In Experiment 1, bubbles decrease as the lamp moves farther away: 58, 31, 17, 9. A distance of 15 cm lies between 10 cm and 20 cm, so the count should lie between their counts, 58 and 31.",["This is below the 30 cm result; a closer lamp gave more bubbles, not fewer.","Missed by one interval: this range matches distances between 20 cm and 30 cm.","Correct: 15 cm is between 10 cm (58 bubbles) and 20 cm (31 bubbles).","Even the closest lamp (10 cm) gave only 58 bubbles; 15 cm is farther away than that."]],"science-0011":["A control is the condition without the treatment being tested. Experiment 2 tests the effect of light color, so the clear filter (all colors, same intensity) is the baseline. Its result (30 bubbles) also closely matches the 20 cm trial in Experiment 1 (31 bubbles), as a control should. Don\'t pick the trial with the most extreme result.",["Blue light is one of the colors being tested, so it is a treatment, not the control.","Extreme result, not a control: green gave the fewest bubbles, but it is still a tested color.","Red light is one of the colors being tested, so it is a treatment.","Correct: the clear filter lets all colors through, giving a baseline to compare each colored filter with."]],"science-0012":["Photosynthesis takes in carbon dioxide and water and, using light energy, makes sugar and releases oxygen. The bubble count rose with light intensity, which fits a gas produced by photosynthesis: oxygen. The sodium bicarbonate was there to SUPPLY carbon dioxide, which the plant takes in. A reason can be a true fact and still not explain the answer, as in the last choice.",["Correct: photosynthesis uses CO₂ and water and releases O₂, and more light gave more bubbles.","Right gas, wrong reason: oxygen is released by photosynthesis, not used by it.","Wrong gas: carbon dioxide is used up in photosynthesis, not produced.","True fact, wrong conclusion: CO₂ is a reactant, so it is taken in, not given off as bubbles."]],"science-0013":["Use the Experiment 2 table (it\'s the one organized by temperature). The 35°C row shows 55 s. The 61 s trap is in the third row of the Experiment 1 table, which is about concentration, not temperature.",["Adjacent row: 30 s is the time at 45°C.","Correct: the 35°C row of Experiment 2 reads 55 s.","Wrong experiment: 61 s is from Experiment 1 (1.5 M HCl at 25°C).","Adjacent row: 97 s is the time at 25°C."]],"science-0014":["Experiment 1 kept the acid at 25°C and changed the concentration. Experiment 2 kept the concentration at 1.0 M and changed the temperature. So temperature is the one that was constant in Experiment 1 and varied in Experiment 2. Strip length and acid volume never changed, and concentration is the reverse case.",["Reversed: concentration was varied in Experiment 1 and held at 1.0 M in Experiment 2.","Held constant in BOTH experiments: every strip was 3.0 cm long.","Held constant in BOTH experiments: 50 mL of HCl was always used.","Correct: the acid was always at 25°C in Experiment 1 but was set to 15, 25, 35, and 45°C in Experiment 2."]],"science-0015":["Combine both experiments. From Experiment 1, 2.0 M HCl at 25°C took 44 s. Experiment 2 shows that lowering the temperature makes the reaction slower, so at 15°C the time should be longer than 44 s. From Experiment 2, 1.0 M HCl at 15°C took 171 s, and Experiment 1 shows that stronger acid is faster, so 2.0 M should take less than 171 s. Both limits together give a time between 44 s and 171 s.",["This is faster than the fastest trial in either experiment, but 15°C is the coldest condition.","Wrong direction for temperature: 2.0 M at 25°C took 44 s, and cooling to 15°C should make it slower, not faster.","Correct: colder than 25°C makes it slower than 44 s, and stronger acid than 1.0 M makes it faster than 171 s.","Wrong direction for concentration: 2.0 M acid reacts faster than the 1.0 M acid that took 171 s at 15°C."]],"science-0016":["The faster reaction makes the strip disappear in less time. 2.0 M HCl at 25°C is in Experiment 1: 44 s. 1.0 M HCl at 45°C is in Experiment 2: 30 s. Since 30 s < 44 s, the 45°C trial was faster. Watch the inverse relationship: in these tables, a bigger number means a slower reaction.",["Correct: 30 s (Experiment 2) is shorter than 44 s (Experiment 1), and a shorter time means a faster reaction.","Right condition, wrong value: 97 s is the 1.0 M time at 25°C, not 45°C.","Wrong decision: 44 s is LONGER than 30 s, so this reaction was slower.","Backward logic: a longer time to disappear means a slower reaction, not a faster one."]],"science-0017":["Every trial started at 80°C, so the biggest decrease goes with the LOWEST final temperature. Aluminum ended at 41°C, a drop of 39°C. Foam is the trap: its 68°C is the largest number in the table, but it means the water lost the least heat.",["Reversed: foam had the HIGHEST final temperature, so its water cooled the least (only 12°C).","Ceramic water dropped 28°C, less than aluminum\'s drop.","Glass water dropped 31°C, less than aluminum\'s drop.","Correct: every cup started at 80°C, and aluminum ended lowest (41°C), a drop of 39°C."]],"science-0018":["The independent variable is what the experimenters change on purpose. In Experiment 2 they changed the volume of water in the foam cups. The final temperature is the dependent variable, and the starting temperature (80°C) and cup material (foam) were held constant.",["Correct: the students chose 50, 100, 200, and 400 mL in Experiment 2.","Wrong experiment: cup material was varied in Experiment 1; Experiment 2 used only foam cups.","Held constant: every trial started with water at 80°C.","Dependent variable: the final temperature is what was measured."]],"science-0019":["Both samples started at 80°C and cooled for the same 20 min. The 200 mL sample fell to 68°C (a 12°C drop), and the 50 mL sample fell to 54°C (a 26°C drop). A smaller drop in the same time means the temperature decreased more slowly, so the claim is supported. Check both parts of each choice: the decision and the numbers.",["Right numbers, wrong decision: a smaller drop in the same 20 min means slower cooling, which supports the claim.","Wrong decision: a higher final temperature means the 200 mL sample cooled LESS, which supports the claim.","Swapped values: the 200 mL sample ended at 68°C (a 12°C drop), and the 50 mL sample ended at 54°C (a 26°C drop).","Correct: 80 − 68 = 12°C versus 80 − 54 = 26°C in the same 20 min, so the larger volume cooled more slowly."]],"science-0020":["Use each experiment for one limit. Experiment 2 shows that more water cools more slowly, so 400 mL in aluminum should end warmer than the 200 mL aluminum trial (41°C). Experiment 1 shows aluminum cools water much faster than foam, so it should end cooler than 400 mL in foam (72°C). The temperature should therefore be between 41°C and 72°C.",["Wrong direction for volume: Experiment 2 shows more water cools more slowly, so 400 mL should stay warmer than 200 mL did.","Correct: more water than the 200 mL aluminum trial (above 41°C), but a worse insulator than the 400 mL foam trial (below 72°C).","Wrong direction for material: aluminum let water cool much faster than foam, so it should end below foam\'s 72°C.","Impossible: the water started at 80°C in a 22°C room, so it can\'t get hotter."]],"science-0021":["Multiply every factor: Experiment 1 tested 5 NaCl concentrations (0, 50, 100, 150, 200 mM), each with 3 dishes, and each dish held 50 seeds. 5 × 3 × 50 = 750. Count-the-trials questions are built to catch a forgotten multiplier, so list all of them before multiplying.",["Forgot the number of NaCl concentrations: 150 seeds is one condition (3 dishes × 50).","Forgot the 3 dishes per condition: 5 concentrations × 50 seeds.","Correct: 5 concentrations × 3 dishes × 50 seeds = 750 seeds.","Counted both experiments (750 + 600); the question asks about Experiment 1 only."]],"science-0022":["Read down the Experiment 2 table: 22, 71, 93, 80%. Germination rose up to 25°C and then fell at 32°C, so it increased and then decreased. Always check the last row: one drop at the end changes the answer from \\"increased only\\".",["Correct: 22% → 71% → 93%, then down to 80% at 32°C.","Missed the last row: germination fell from 93% to 80% between 25°C and 32°C.","Reversed, and it\'s Experiment 1\'s pattern (with NaCl), not Experiment 2\'s.","Reversed order: the rise comes first and the drop comes last."]],"science-0023":["Each experiment shows one stress. Cold: at 10°C with no salt, only 22% germinated. Salt: at 25°C, 100 mM NaCl dropped germination from 94% to 63%. Seeds facing BOTH stresses should do worse than seeds facing only the cold, so germination should be below 22%. Averaging the two results is the trap: the effects add up rather than average out.",["Correct: 10°C alone gave only 22%, and adding 100 mM NaCl lowered germination, so the result should be below 22%.","Averaged the two results (22% and 63%); both conditions lower germination, so together they should push it lower than either one.","Ignored the cold: this range would fit 100 mM NaCl at a warmer temperature like 25°C.","Wrong direction: both salt and cold lowered germination compared with the best condition (0 mM, 25°C)."]],"science-0024":["Scientist 1 says the crater formed in seconds when a meteorite hit. Scientist 2 says the depression \\"also formed in a single, sudden event\\" (a steam explosion), so Scientist 2 agrees with Scientist 1 on timing while disagreeing on the cause. Scientist 3 says it formed slowly over thousands of years. So the answer is Scientists 1 and 2 only.",["One too few: Scientist 2 says the depression \\"also formed in a single, sudden event.\\"","One too few: Scientist 1\'s crater formed \\"in seconds,\\" which is also a single, sudden event.","Reversed: Scientist 3 is the only one who says it formed slowly, over thousands of years.","Correct: Scientist 1 (an impact, in seconds) and Scientist 2 (a steam explosion) both describe one sudden event."]],"science-0025":["Basalt is volcanic rock. Scientist 2 says drilling should find \\"a vertical pipe of broken volcanic rock (basalt) beneath the floor,\\" so this finding supports Scientist 2. Scientists 1 and 3 both predict no volcanic rock under the floor, so the finding weakens their viewpoints.",["Scientist 1 predicts that drilling will find NO volcanic rock, so this finding weakens Scientist 1.","Correct: Scientist 2 predicts a vertical pipe of broken basalt beneath the floor.","Scientist 3 predicts cavities in limestone and no volcanic rock, so this finding weakens Scientist 3.","Scientist 2 predicts exactly this finding, so it does support someone."]],"science-0026":["Check each scientist\'s claim about the edge layers. Scientist 1: pushed up and out, tilting away from the center. Scientist 2: pushed up, tilting away from the center. Scientist 3: sank, tilting down toward the center. A downward tilt toward the center fits only Scientist 3, so it is inconsistent with Scientists 1 and 2.",["One too few: Scientist 2 also says the edge layers were pushed up and tilt away from the center.","Reversed: Scientist 3 predicts exactly this tilt, so the finding fits Scientist 3.","Correct: Scientists 1 and 2 both say the layers tilt AWAY from the center, so a tilt toward the center contradicts both.","One too many: the finding matches Scientist 3\'s prediction."]],"science-0027":["Scientist 3 never mentions shocked quartz, so combine information. Scientist 1\'s paragraph explains that shocked quartz forms only under extreme shock pressure, like an impact. Scientist 3 describes a slow collapse over thousands of years, with layers that sank rather than being blasted out. That process creates no shock, so Scientist 3 would not expect shocked quartz. The steam-explosion choice has the right \\"No\\" but gives Scientist 2\'s mechanism.",["Wrong claim: Scientist 3 says the layers sank rather than being blasted out.","True statement about Scientist 3, but dissolving limestone has nothing to do with the extreme shock pressure shocked quartz needs.","Right decision, wrong scientist: the steam explosion is Scientist 2\'s explanation.","Correct: Scientist 1 explains that shocked quartz forms only under extreme shock pressure, and Scientist 3\'s slow collapse involves no such shock."]],"science-0028":["Student 2 begins by saying the island and mainland lizards \\"do not differ in the genes that control leg length,\\" and explains the difference by the surfaces each lizard grows up on. So the answer is No, for that reason. The other choices borrow claims from Students 1 and 3.",["Correct: Student 2 states that the genes do not differ and that growing conditions set leg length.","Right decision, wrong student: the \\"no survival advantage\\" claim is Student 3\'s.","Wrong student: the predator argument belongs to Student 1.","Wrong student: the few-colonists argument belongs to Student 3."]],"science-0029":["The finding says growing up on twigs did NOT shorten the island lizards\' legs. Student 2 claims each lizard\'s leg length is set by the surfaces it uses while growing, so twigs should have produced shorter legs: the finding weakens Student 2. Students 1 and 3 both say leg length is largely inherited, so island lizards should keep long legs wherever they grow up; the finding fits both of them.",["Student 1 says leg length is inherited, so island lizards keeping long legs on twigs fits Student 1.","Correct: Student 2 predicts that growing up on twigs would make the legs shorter, and they weren\'t.","Reversed: Students 1 and 3 both say leg length is inherited, which this finding supports.","One too many: the finding is consistent with Students 1 and 3, who say leg length is inherited."]],"science-0030":["Find a claim that appears in both views. Student 1: \\"Leg length is largely inherited\\" (then natural selection did the rest). Student 3: \\"Leg length is largely inherited\\" (then chance colonists did the rest). The survival-advantage claim is Student 1\'s alone (Student 3 denies it), and the few-colonists claim is Student 3\'s alone (Student 1 says many lizards arrived).",["Only Student 1 holds this; Student 3 says long legs give no survival advantage.","Only Student 3 holds this; Student 1 says the island was colonized by many lizards.","Correct: both Student 1 and Student 3 say, in the same words, that leg length is largely inherited.","This is Student 2\'s view, which both Students 1 and 3 reject."]],"science-0031":["Subtract the 0°C value from the 80°C value for each alloy. Alloy P: 56 − 40 = 16. Alloy Q: 52 − 20 = 32. Alloy R: 54 − 52 = 2. Alloy Q increased the most. The trap is Alloy R, which has the biggest numbers in the table; the question asks about the change, not the size.",["Alloy P rose from 40 to 56, an increase of 16, which is only half of Alloy Q\'s increase.","Correct: Alloy Q rose from 20 to 52, an increase of 32, the largest of the three.","Alloy R has the highest values, but it barely changed: from 52 to 54, an increase of 2.","The increases were 16, 32, and 2, so they were not the same."]],"science-0032":["In R = ρL/A, if L and A stay the same, R is directly proportional to ρ. Alloy Q\'s ρ goes from 28 at 20°C to 52 at 80°C, a factor of 52/28 = 13/7. So the new resistance is 7 Ω × 13/7 = 13 Ω. The traps either scale by the wrong numbers (the temperatures) or add a change in ρ to a resistance, which mixes different quantities.",["Correct: with L and A fixed, R is proportional to ρ, so R = 7 Ω × (52 ÷ 28) = 13 Ω.","Gave the change in resistivity (52 − 28 = 24) as if it were the resistance.","Scaled by the temperatures (80 ÷ 20 = 4, and 7 × 4 = 28), but R depends on ρ, not directly on temperature.","Added the increase in resistivity (24) to 7 Ω; R changes by the same factor as ρ, not by the same amount."]],"science-0033":["Extend both columns. Alloy Q rises by 8 every 20°C, so it would be about 60 at 100°C. Alloy R rises only about 0.5 every 20°C, so it would be about 54 to 55 at 100°C. At 80°C, Q (52) is still below R (54), and at 100°C, Q is above R, so they cross between 80°C and 100°C. The trap 100°C to 120°C mixes up Alloy R with Alloy P, which Alloy Q passes right at 100°C.",["Within the table, Alloy Q is always lower: at 80°C it is 52, still below Alloy R\'s 54.","Correct: Alloy Q gains 8 every 20°C and Alloy R gains about 0.5, so by 100°C Q is about 60 while R is only about 54 to 55.","This is when Alloy Q passes Alloy P (both reach 60 at 100°C), not Alloy R.","Too late: Alloy Q is already well above Alloy R by 100°C."]],"science-0034":["Use R = ρL/A with the 60°C values. Wire X: 53 × 3 ÷ 2 = 79.5. Wire Y: 44 × 2 ÷ 1 = 88. (Both are in the same units, so they can be compared directly.) Wire X\'s value is smaller, so yes, Wire X has the lower resistance, even though its alloy has the higher resistivity. The thicker cross section of Wire X more than makes up for its material and extra length. Choices that look only at ρ, or that multiply by A, miss this.",["Correct: dividing by the larger area brings Wire X\'s value (about 80) below Wire Y\'s (88).","Right answer, wrong reason: a greater resistivity would make the resistance higher, not lower.","Compared only resistivity and ignored the different lengths and areas in R = ρL/A.","Multiplied by the area instead of dividing; a thicker wire has less resistance."]],"science-0035":["Multiply every layer of the design. Each experiment had 4 conditions, so there were 12 conditions in all. Each condition was tested in 3 trials, and each trial used 40 kg of fresh soil. 12 × 3 × 40 = 1,440 kg. The bare, 15°, 50 mm/hr condition shows up in all three experiments, but the passage says each experiment used its own trays, so it was run separately each time.",["Counted only one condition: 3 trials × 40 kg.","Forgot the trials: 12 conditions × 40 kg, but each condition was run 3 times with fresh soil.","Counted the bare, 15°, 50 mm/hr condition only once, but each experiment used its own trays and trials.","Correct: 12 conditions (4 per experiment) × 3 trials × 40 kg = 1,440 kg."]],"science-0036":["Read each experiment\'s heading. Experiment 1 kept the rain rate at 50 mm/hr and changed the tilt. Experiment 3 kept the tilt at 15° and changed the rain rate. So rain rate is the factor varied in Experiment 3 but constant in Experiment 1. Tilt angle is the reverse case, which is the main trap.",["Reversed: tilt angle was varied in Experiment 1 and held at 15° in Experiment 3.","Soil cover was varied only in Experiment 2; it was bare in both Experiments 1 and 3.","Every tray in every experiment held 40 kg of soil, so this was never varied.","Correct: the rain rate was 50 mm/hr throughout Experiment 1 but ranged from 25 to 100 mm/hr in Experiment 3."]],"science-0037":["Use each experiment for the part it tested. Experiment 2: at 50 mm/hr, straw lowered soil loss from 450 g to 90 g, so straw kept 1/5 of the loss (an 80% reduction). Experiment 3: bare soil at 100 mm/hr lost 1,300 g. If straw cuts loss by the same percent, the straw trial would lose 1/5 × 1,300 = 260 g. Each wrong choice uses only one experiment or treats the straw effect as a fixed amount.",["This is the straw result at 50 mm/hr; it ignores the heavier rain.","Correct: straw cut soil loss to 90 ÷ 450 = 1/5 of bare soil, and 1/5 of the bare-soil loss at 100 mm/hr (1,300 g) is 260 g.","Added the bare-soil increase (1,300 − 450 = 850 g) to 90 g, which assumes straw removes the same amount, not the same percent.","This is bare soil at 100 mm/hr; it ignores the straw."]],"science-0038":["Check all four doublings. Tilt 5° to 10°: 120 g to 230 g, and 2 × 120 = 240, so this is less than double. Tilt 10° to 20°: 230 g to 700 g (more than double). Rain 25 to 50 mm/hr: 180 g to 450 g (more than double). Rain 50 to 100 mm/hr: 450 g to 1,300 g (more than double). One counterexample is enough to sink an \\"every time\\" claim, so the answer is No, and the reason must be the 5° to 10° case. Choice C says No but points to a case that actually fits the claim.",["One case that fits doesn\'t prove \\"every time\\"; the 5° to 10° case breaks the claim.","This case fits the claim, but the claim says every doubling, and 5° to 10° does not fit.","True numbers, wrong reason: 450 g is more than double 180 g, so this case agrees with the claim.","Correct: double 120 g is 240 g, and 230 g is less than that, so this doubling did not more than double the soil loss."]],"science-0039":["Find the scientist who made a prediction about riffles that this finding contradicts. Scientist 1 says oxygen ran out under the algae mats but riffles stayed better aerated, so riffle mussels should have done better. Equal death rates go against that. Scientist 2 predicted equal rates (riffles included), so the finding fits Scientist 2. Scientist 3\'s disease idea doesn\'t depend on where the water was aerated.",["Correct: Scientist 1 predicts that riffles, where water mixes with air, should have had fewer deaths than the low-DO areas under the mats.","Scientist 2 predicts exactly this: shallow, sunny areas, riffles included, should have died at similar rates.","Scientist 3 makes no prediction about riffles versus mats, so this finding neither helps nor hurts that explanation.","Scientist 1 explicitly predicts fewer deaths in riffles, so this finding does weaken one explanation."]],"science-0040":["Read each view for any role given to temperature, not just the main cause. Scientist 1: \\"Heat made this worse,\\" because warm water holds less oxygen. Scientist 2: heat is the cause. Scientist 3: the bacterium \\"multiplies rapidly in warm water.\\" All three give warm water a part in the deaths; they disagree about how it worked. The trap is to match each scientist only to their headline cause.",["Scientist 2 makes heat the whole cause, but the other two also give warm water a role.","Leaves out Scientist 3, who says the bacterium multiplies rapidly in warm water.","Leaves out Scientist 1, who says the heat made the oxygen shortage worse because warm water holds less dissolved gas.","Correct: heat is the direct cause for Scientist 2, it worsens the oxygen shortage for Scientist 1, and it speeds the bacterium\'s growth for Scientist 3."]],"science-0041":["Apply only Scientist 2\'s rule: water reaching 33°C kills these mussels within a few days, no matter the DO, and algae don\'t matter. Site X reaches 33°C, so its mussels should die. Sites Y and Z stay at 27°C, like the cooler water where mussels survived, so their mussels should mostly live, even at Site Y with its low DO. That is \\"most died at X; few at Y and Z.\\" The other choices are what Scientist 1, Scientists 1 and 2 together, or Scientist 3 would predict.",["This is Scientist 1\'s prediction: deaths where DO drops to near zero under the mats.","This mixes Scientists 1 and 2: it has deaths from low DO at Site Y as well as heat at Site X.","This is closer to what Scientist 3 would expect with the mussels spaced apart (no crowding to spread infection); Scientist 2 predicts deaths in the hottest water.","Correct: only Site X reached 33°C, the temperature Scientist 2 says kills mussels at any DO; Sites Y and Z stayed at 27°C."]],"science-0042":["The right finding has to do three jobs at once. It must fit Scientist 3 (bacterium in the dead, or spread from one area), go against Scientist 1 (deaths where DO stayed high and there were no algae), and go against Scientist 2 (deaths where the water stayed cooler). Only the deep, shaded pool finding does all three. Choice A is the closest trap: it supports Scientist 3, but deaths only above 32°C are exactly what Scientist 2 would expect, so it doesn\'t weaken Scientist 2.",["Supports Scientist 3, but deaths only in the hottest water also fit Scientist 2 (the bacterium could just invade mussels already dying from heat).","Correct: deaths in cool water rule out heat alone (Scientist 2), deaths with high DO and no algae rule out suffocation (Scientist 1), and the bacterium in the dead mussels supports Scientist 3.","This supports Scientist 1, not Scientist 3.","The spreading pattern supports Scientist 3, but the near-zero DO supports Scientist 1 rather than weakening it."]],"science-0043":["In Table 1, each extra 0.5 mol of Solute A lowers the freezing point by 2.0°C, which is 4.0°C per mole. To go from 12.0°C down to 0.0°C is a drop of 12.0°C, so you need 12.0 ÷ 4.0 = 3.0 mol. You can also count rows: 2.5 mol gives 2.0°C and 3.0 mol gives 0.0°C. The trap 6.0 counts 0.5-mol steps and forgets to convert them to moles.",["2.0 mol is the last amount tested, and that solution froze at 4.0°C, not 0.0°C.","Extended the table by only one step: 2.5 mol would freeze at 2.0°C.","Correct: each 0.5 mol lowers the freezing point by 2.0°C (4.0°C per mol), so a 12.0°C drop takes 3.0 mol.","Divided 12.0°C by 2.0°C to get 6 steps, but each step is 0.5 mol, so 6 steps is 3.0 mol."]],"science-0044":["First find p for Solute C. In Experiment 2, 0.5 mol of Solute C dropped the freezing point from 12.0°C to 6.0°C, a ΔT of 6.0°C. The model gives 6.0 = 4.0 × 0.5 × p, so p = 3. Now use n = 1.25: ΔT = 4.0 × 1.25 × 3 = 15.0°C. The solution freezes at 12.0 − 15.0 = −3.0°C. Two steps, two traps: using the wrong p (from Solute A or B), or stopping at ΔT.",["Correct: in Experiment 2, Solute C\'s 6.0°C drop at n = 0.5 means p = 3, so ΔT = 4.0 × 1.25 × 3 = 15.0°C and 12.0 − 15.0 = −3.0°C.","Used p = 2, which is the value for Solute B (its 4.0°C drop at 0.5 mol), not Solute C.","Used p = 1, as for Solute A, so the drop was only 5.0°C.","Gave the drop in freezing point (ΔT = 15.0°C) instead of the freezing point itself."]],"science-0045":["In the model, n is moles of solute per kilogram of solvent. Spreading 1.0 mol of Solute A through 2.0 kg of solvent makes n = 0.5 mol/kg. Solute A has p = 1 (0.5 mol in 1.0 kg dropped the freezing point by 2.0°C). So ΔT = 4.0 × 0.5 × 1 = 2.0°C, and the solution freezes at 12.0 − 2.0 = 10.0°C, the same as the 0.5 mol trial in Table 1. Adding solvent dilutes the solution, so the drop gets smaller, not bigger.",["Doubled the drop to 8.0°C, but more solvent dilutes the solute, so the drop gets smaller.","This is the result with 1.0 kg of solvent; it ignores the change.","Correct: n = 1.0 mol ÷ 2.0 kg = 0.5 mol/kg, so ΔT = 4.0 × 0.5 × 1 = 2.0°C and the freezing point is 10.0°C.","Assumed extra solvent cancels the solute\'s effect entirely, but the solute is still dissolved in it."]],"science-0046":["A trial with none of the factor being tested is a control. With 0.0 mol of Solute A, the trial measures the freezing point of pure Solvent S (12.0°C). Every other trial\'s drop is measured against it. It can\'t tell you anything about particles, solubility, or Solute B.",["Correct: with no solute, the trial gives the pure-solvent freezing point (12.0°C) that every other trial is compared with.","Finding p takes a trial with solute in it; a trial with none can\'t show how a solute splits.","No trial in Experiment 1 tested how much Solute A can dissolve.","Solute B appears only in Experiment 2, and comparing solutes takes trials with both solutes."]],"science-0047":["The number of orbits Moon C completes during one orbit of Moon D is Moon D\'s period divided by Moon C\'s period: 128 ÷ 54. Since 2 × 54 = 108 and 3 × 54 = 162, the answer is between 2 and 3. Use the period column; the radius and speed columns give different ratios.",["Reversed the comparison: Moon C has the shorter period, so it makes more than one orbit while Moon D makes one.","Compared the radii (1,600 ÷ 900 ≈ 1.8) or speeds (1.2 ÷ 0.9 ≈ 1.3) instead of the periods.","Correct: Moon D\'s period is 128 days and Moon C\'s is 54 days, and 128 ÷ 54 is about 2.4.","Squared the ratio of the radii ((16/9)² ≈ 3.2); the question is about periods, which are given directly."]],"science-0048":["Look for the pattern. The radii are 100 times 1, 4, 9, and 16 (perfect squares), and the periods are 2 times 1, 8, 27, and 64 (perfect cubes of 1, 2, 3, 4). So when the radius is 100 × n², the period is 2 × n³. For 2,500 = 100 × 25 = 100 × 5², the period is 2 × 5³ = 250 days. This is the passage\'s rule in action: period² is proportional to radius³. Check: 250² ÷ 2,500³ equals 2² ÷ 100³.",["Assumed the period is proportional to the radius (25 times Moon A\'s radius, so 25 × 2 days).","Added Moon D\'s last increase in period again (128 + 74); the periods grow faster than that.","Correct: the radii are 100 × 1, 4, 9, 16 and the periods are 2 × 1, 8, 27, 64; a radius of 100 × 25 gives a period of 2 × 125 = 250 days.","Assumed the period is proportional to the radius squared (2 × 25²); the period grows with radius to the 3/2 power."]],"science-0049":["Test each expression with two moons; one mismatch rules it out. With Moons A and B: speed × radius gives 360 and 720 (no). Speed × period gives 7.2 and 28.8 (no). Speed × radius² gives 36,000 and 288,000 (no). Speed² × radius gives 12.96 × 100 = 1,296 and 3.24 × 400 = 1,296 (yes), and Moons C and D also give 1,296. So the speed is inversely proportional to the square root of the radius: a moon 4 times as far moves half as fast.",["Moon A gives 3.6 × 100 = 360 but Moon B gives 1.8 × 400 = 720, so this is not constant.","Moon A gives 3.6 × 2 = 7.2 but Moon B gives 1.8 × 16 = 28.8, so this grows with distance.","Squaring the radius makes the product grow even faster: 36,000 for Moon A versus 288,000 for Moon B.","Correct: 3.6² × 100 = 1.8² × 400 = 1.2² × 900 = 0.9² × 1,600 = 1,296."]],"science-0050":["Each student predicts a different makeup for the ring particles. Student 1: the same mix as a whole moon, about half rock and half ice. Student 2: mostly rock. Student 3: nearly pure ice. A half-and-half finding matches only Student 1.",["Correct: Student 1 says the rings came from a whole moon, and the planet\'s moons are about half rock and half ice.","Student 2 predicts mostly rock, because heat drove off the ice near the young planet.","Student 3 predicts nearly pure ice, chipped from the icy surfaces of small moons.","Student 1\'s prediction matches this finding."]],"science-0051":["Look for an idea both students use, even if they word it differently. Student 1: near the planet, the planet pulls harder on a moon\'s near side than its far side, which tore the moon apart and keeps the pieces from regrouping. Student 2: close to the planet, the difference in pull across a clump stopped the material from ever building a moon. Both say uneven pull from the planet keeps material near it from collecting into one body. The other choices belong to only one of them, or to Students 1 and 3.",["Only Student 2 says this; Student 1 says the rings formed about 50 million years ago.","Correct: Student 1 says the stronger pull on a moon\'s near side keeps the pieces apart, and Student 2 says the difference in pull on a clump\'s edges stopped the material from building a moon.","Only Student 2 predicts mostly rock; Student 1 predicts half rock and half ice.","Students 1 and 3 say this; Student 2 says the material never formed a moon."]],"science-0052":["The planet is about 4 billion years old. Student 1: the particles came from a moon broken up about 50 million years ago, so they are young (compared with the planet). Student 2: the particles are leftovers from the planet\'s formation, so they are as old as the planet. Student 3: the rings themselves are old, but particles are lost and replaced, so most of today\'s particles are young. The trap is reading Student 3\'s \\"billions of years\\" and missing the sentence about the particles themselves.",["Leaves out Student 3, who says the rings are old but most of today\'s particles are young.","Leaves out Student 1, whose rings formed only about 50 million years ago, far less than 4 billion.","Correct: Student 1\'s particles date from a moon torn apart 50 million years ago, and Student 3\'s are constantly replaced by new debris.","Students 1 and 3 both describe young particles; only Student 2 says they are as old as the planet."]],"science-0053":["Ask which ring could still exist if material is being lost this fast. Student 1: the rings formed about 50 million years ago, less than the 100 million years it would take to lose today\'s material, so this fits (the rings were just bigger at first). Student 3: material is lost, but impacts keep adding new debris, so old rings can last. Student 2: the rings are about 4 billion years old and nothing is ever added, so at this rate they should have disappeared long ago. Only Student 2 has a problem. The trap is to flag every student whose rings are old (Students 2 and 3) without noticing that Student 3\'s rings are resupplied.",["Student 1\'s rings are only about 50 million years old, so they could still be here even if they lose material this fast.","Correct: Student 2\'s rings have been around for about 4 billion years with nothing added, but at this rate they would have vanished long ago.","Student 1\'s young rings fit the finding; only an old ring with no new material is a problem.","Student 3\'s rings are old, but new debris keeps arriving to replace what is lost, so they can last."]],"science-0054":["Experiment 2 had 5 temperatures and Experiment 3 had 4 rubbers: 9 conditions. Each condition used 3 balls, and each ball was dropped 4 times, so each condition took 12 drops. 9 × 12 = 108. Rubber 1 at 20°C from 100 cm appears in both experiments, but the passage says each experiment used its own drops, so count it twice.",["Counted balls but not drops: 9 conditions × 3 balls.","Counted drops per ball but not balls: 9 conditions × 4 drops.","Counted the Rubber 1, 20°C, 100 cm condition only once, but each experiment used its own set of drops.","Correct: (5 + 4) conditions × 3 balls × 4 drops per ball = 108 drops."]],"science-0055":["To test one factor, change only that factor and keep everything else the same. Here the factor is temperature, the material must be Rubber 3, and the drop height must stay fixed. Only the first set does that. The last set is the classic trap: it does vary temperature, but it varies drop height at the same time, so the effects are mixed together.",["Correct: only the temperature changes; the rubber and the drop height stay the same, like Experiment 2.","This changes the drop height, not the temperature, so it repeats Experiment 1 with Rubber 3.","This changes the rubber at one temperature, so it can\'t show an effect of temperature on Rubber 3.","Temperature and drop height change together, so you couldn\'t tell which one caused any change in rebound height."]],"science-0056":["Combine the two effects. Experiment 1: at 20°C, a drop from 200 cm rebounds 138 cm, roughly twice the 100 cm rebound. Experiment 2: from 100 cm, warming from 20°C to 50°C raised the rebound from 71 cm to 84 cm, about 1.2 times as high. So from 200 cm at 50°C, expect about 138 × 1.2, or roughly twice 84: about 165 cm. Each of the two smaller choices uses only one experiment, and 220 cm is impossible because a bouncing ball loses energy and can\'t rise above its drop height.",["This is the rebound from 100 cm at 50°C; it ignores the doubled drop height.","This is the rebound from 200 cm at 20°C; it ignores the warmer temperature.","Correct: from 200 cm at 20°C the rebound is 138 cm, and warming to 50°C raised the 100 cm rebound by about 18% (71 to 84 cm), giving about 165 cm.","A dropped ball can\'t bounce higher than it started; 220 cm is above the 200 cm drop height."]],"science-0057":["Get the target from Table 3: Rubber 3 at 20°C rebounds 83 cm. Then find 83 cm in Table 2 for Rubber 1. It is 80 cm at 35°C and 84 cm at 50°C, so Rubber 1 reaches 83 cm somewhere between 35°C and 50°C. The trap is 20°C to 35°C, because 80 cm is close to 83 cm, but it hasn\'t reached it yet.",["Rubber 1 rebounds only 58 to 71 cm in this range, well below 83 cm.","At 35°C Rubber 1 reaches only 80 cm, still below Rubber 3\'s 83 cm.","Correct: Rubber 3 rebounds 83 cm at 20°C, and Rubber 1\'s rebound rises from 80 cm at 35°C to 84 cm at 50°C, passing 83 cm in between.","Rubber 1 already reaches 84 cm at 50°C, which is above 83 cm, so it matches before 50°C."]],"science-0058":["Find the change in Enzyme K\'s column across each interval: +25 (pH 2 to 4), +50 (pH 4 to 6), +15 (pH 6 to 8), and −35 (pH 8 to 10). The biggest increase is from pH 4 to pH 6. Don\'t confuse the biggest values (near pH 8) with the biggest change.",["Enzyme K rose by 25 here (5 to 30), half as much as from pH 4 to pH 6.","Correct: Enzyme K rose from 30% to 80%, an increase of 50, the largest jump.","Enzyme K reaches its highest values here, but it rose by only 15 (80 to 95).","Enzyme K\'s activity fell over this interval, from 95% to 60%."]],"science-0059":["Two curves are equal where one passes the other. Compare K and L at each pH: K is higher at pH 2, 4, 6, and 8, but at pH 10, L (90) is higher than K (60). The switch happens between pH 8 and pH 10. Close values (like 5 and 0 at pH 2) are not a crossing; you need a change in which enzyme is ahead.",["The two enzymes are close at pH 2 (5% and 0%), but Enzyme K is still higher at pH 4, so their lines don\'t cross here.","This is where Enzyme J and Enzyme K cross, not Enzymes K and L.","Both enzymes rise here, and Enzyme K stays far ahead (80 vs. 15, then 95 vs. 55).","Correct: at pH 8 Enzyme K is higher (95 vs. 55), and at pH 10 Enzyme L is higher (90 vs. 60), so they are equal somewhere in between."]],"science-0060":["Average J and L at each pH: pH 2: 42.5%. pH 4: 31%. pH 6: 17.5%. pH 8: 30%. pH 10: 45%. The lowest is at pH 6, where both enzymes are working poorly at the same time. The traps are the pH values where one enzyme hits 0% or nearly 0%: the other enzyme is strong there, so the mixture still has plenty of activity.",["The average at pH 4 is (60 + 2) ÷ 2 = 31%, higher than at pH 6.","Correct: the average at pH 6 is (20 + 15) ÷ 2 = 17.5%, the lowest of all.","Enzyme J is nearly off here, but Enzyme L is at 55%, so the average is 30%.","Enzyme J is at 0% here, but Enzyme L is at 90%, so the average is 45%, the highest."]],"science-0061":["Find the row for 6 days, then move across to the column for Isotope Y: 28 g. The wrong answers come from reading the X column (10 g) or a neighboring row (40 g at 4 days, 57 g at 2 days).",["This is the mass of Isotope X at 6 days. Read the Y column.","Correct: the 6-day row shows 28 g in the Y column.","This is the mass of Y at 4 days, one row too early.","This is the mass of Y at 2 days."]],"science-0062":["Read down the Y column: 80, 57, 40, 28, 20 g. Each value is smaller than the one before, so the mass decreased only. The drops get smaller over time (23 g, then 17 g, then 12 g, then 8 g), but the mass still goes down every time, so \'decreased and then increased\' and the other choices don\'t fit.",["The mass never goes up; every value in the Y column is smaller than the one before it.","Correct: the Y column goes 80, 57, 40, 28, 20 g, falling at every step.","There is no rise at the start; the mass falls from the first measurement on.","There is no rise at the end; 20 g at 8 days is the smallest value."]],"science-0063":["A half-life is the time for a sample to drop to half its mass. X goes from 80 g to 40 g in 2 days (and from 40 g to 20 g in the next 2), so its half-life is 2 days. Y takes 4 days to go from 80 g to 40 g, so its half-life is 4 days. The shorter half-life belongs to X: 2 days. A common slip is picking Y because its numbers are bigger; a bigger mass remaining means slower decay, not faster.",["Correct: X drops from 80 g to 40 g in 2 days, while Y takes 4 days to fall from 80 g to 40 g.","Right isotope, but 4 days is the half-life of Y. X halves every 2 days (80 to 40 to 20).","Y keeps more of its mass at every time, so it decays more slowly and has the longer half-life.","4 days is Y\'s half-life, but that is the longer one. The question asks for the shorter half-life."]],"science-0064":["Both samples start at 80 g, so X/Y starts at 1. In each 4-day stretch, X halves twice (2-day half-life) and Y halves once (4-day half-life), so the ratio X/Y halves every 4 days. The table agrees: X/Y is 20/40 = 1/2 at 4 days and 5/20 = 1/4 at 8 days. Reaching 1/8 takes one more halving: 12 days. Check: at 12 days X = 80/64 = 1.25 g and Y = 80/8 = 10 g, and 1.25 is 1/8 of 10. The traps ignore that Y is decaying too (6 days, 10 days) or treat a halving pattern as a straight line (14 days).",["At 6 days, X is 10 g, which is 1/8 of the starting 80 g. But Y has also decayed to 28 g by then, so X is not 1/8 of Y.","Took 1/8 of Y\'s 8-day mass (2.5 g) and found when X reaches 2.5 g. Y keeps decaying after day 8, so the two times don\'t match.","Correct: X halves every 2 days and Y every 4 days, so the ratio X/Y halves every 4 days. Three halvings (1/8) take 12 days.","Extended the ratio as a straight line (1/2 at 4 days, 1/4 at 8 days). The ratio halves every 4 days; it doesn\'t fall by the same amount."]],"science-0065":["Scientist 2 says that wind carried fine dust that settled around the bases of shrubs, building the mounds. Burrowing animals belong to Scientist 1, and frost and cracks belong to Scientist 3. Rain is mentioned only by Scientist 1, and only to say the clay stays wet; no one says water carried the soil.",["This is Scientist 1\'s explanation, not Scientist 2\'s.","This is Scientist 3\'s explanation, not Scientist 2\'s.","None of the scientists says the soil was carried by water.","Correct: Scientist 2 says wind carried fine dust that settled around the base of each shrub."]],"science-0066":["The finding has to do two jobs at once. Soil-filled tunnels are what Scientist 1 predicts for mound soil, so they support Scientist 1. Scientist 3 says these mounds form only where the ground once froze deeply, so identical mounds where it never froze deeply weaken Scientist 3. The other choices each do only one job or the wrong job: gravel-filled cracks support Scientist 3; finer mound soil weakens Scientist 3 but supports Scientist 2; and the clay-layer finding supports Scientist 1 without touching Scientist 3, who never mentions the clay.",["This is what Scientist 3 predicts, so it supports Scientist 3 instead of weakening that view.","This weakens Scientist 3 (who says mound soil matches the surrounding soil), but it supports Scientist 2, not Scientist 1.","This supports Scientist 1, but Scientist 3 says nothing about the clay layer, so it doesn\'t weaken Scientist 3.","Correct: the filled tunnels fit Scientist 1, and mounds where the ground never froze contradict Scientist 3\'s claim that such mounds form only where it once froze deeply."]],"science-0067":["Check each scientist\'s timeline. Scientist 1: rodents are still building mounds, so steady growth up to today fits. Scientist 2: the mounds stopped growing when shrubs disappeared about 3,000 years ago, so growth until today contradicts this. Scientist 3: the mounds formed during a cold period that ended 10,000 years ago and haven\'t grown since, so growth that started 8,000 years ago and continues today contradicts this too. The answer is Scientists 2 and 3. The traps are noticing only one of the two contradictions.",["Scientist 2 is contradicted, but so is Scientist 3: the soil was added long after the cold period ended 10,000 years ago.","Scientist 3 is contradicted, but so is Scientist 2, who says the mounds stopped growing about 3,000 years ago.","Correct: Scientist 2 says growth stopped about 3,000 years ago, and Scientist 3 says the mounds formed before 10,000 years ago and haven\'t grown since. Only Scientist 1 says they are still growing.","Scientist 1 says rodents are still building the mounds today, so soil added up to the present fits Scientist 1."]],"science-0068":["Test each finding against each scientist. Finding (1), same grain size: Scientist 2 predicts finer soil in the mounds, so finding (1) is inconsistent with Scientist 2. Scientist 3 predicts the same soil in and between the mounds, and Scientist 1\'s rodents pile up the surrounding soil, so neither conflicts. Finding (2), mounds only over shallow clay: Scientist 1 predicts exactly this. Scientists 2 and 3 never mention the clay layer, and a fact an explanation doesn\'t address is not a contradiction. Only Scientist 2 is contradicted. The trap is treating \'not explained by\' as \'inconsistent with\'.",["Neither finding conflicts with Scientist 1: the rodents pile up the surrounding soil, and Scientist 1 predicts mounds only over shallow clay.","Correct: finding (1) contradicts Scientist 2, who predicts finer-grained soil in the mounds. Neither finding conflicts with Scientist 1 or Scientist 3.","Trap: Scientist 3 never explains the clay restriction, but saying nothing about the clay is not a contradiction. Scientist 3 even predicts the same soil in and between the mounds.","Neither finding conflicts with Scientist 1 or Scientist 3, so not all three are contradicted."]],"science-0069":["The independent variable is the one the experimenters change on purpose. In Experiment 2, the only thing that differed between trays was the concentration of H. Mean height was measured as the result (dependent variable), and the 50 mL volume and the 10 seedlings per tray were the same for every tray (held constant).",["Correct: the students chose different concentrations of H (0, 5, 10, and 20 mg/L) for the trays.","Mean height is what the students measured. It is the dependent variable.","Every seedling got 50 mL each day, so this was held constant.","Every tray held 10 seedlings, so this was held constant."]],"science-0070":["Use Table 1, which is Experiment 1, and find the 20 mg/L row: 12.1 cm. The traps are the same row in Table 2 (9.6 cm) and the neighboring row in Table 1 (11.6 cm).",["This is the 0 mg/L value, from the wrong row.","This is the 20 mg/L value from Table 2 (Experiment 2), not Table 1.","This is the 10 mg/L value, one row too early.","Correct: Table 1 shows 12.1 cm for 20 mg/L."]],"science-0071":["In Experiment 1, the 20 mg/L seedlings were 12.1 − 8.2 = 3.9 cm taller. But those seedlings also sat closer to the light, so two things changed at once. In Experiment 2, every tray had the same light, so the difference there, 9.6 − 8.4 = 1.2 cm, shows the effect of H alone. That leaves about 3.9 − 1.2 = 2.7 cm explained by something other than H, most likely the extra light on the upper shelf. Treating all 3.9 cm as the effect of H is exactly the mistake Experiment 2 was designed to catch.",["This is the difference between the two 0 mg/L trays (8.4 − 8.2). It compares the controls, not the effect of the shelf.","This is the part of the difference caused by H itself, measured in Experiment 2 where light was the same for every tray.","Correct: the total gap in Experiment 1 is 3.9 cm, and H alone accounts for about 1.2 cm (Experiment 2), so about 2.7 cm came from something else, most likely the closer light.","This is the whole difference in Experiment 1, which mixes the effect of H with the effect of the upper shelf."]],"science-0072":["To test one factor, change only that factor. Here the factor is shelf position, so H must be the same in every tray, and shelf position must differ. Putting 0 mg/L trays on both shelves does exactly that. The tempting wrong choice puts every tray on the upper shelf: it changes where the trays are, but because all trays are on the same shelf, there is nothing to compare. Adding seedlings or a new concentration doesn\'t separate H from light at all.",["Correct: with H held at 0 mg/L, shelf position is the only difference, so any height gap must come from the shelf.","This changes the amount of H, not the shelf, so it can\'t show what the shelf does.","More seedlings make each mean more reliable, but H and shelf position would still change together.","Here every tray is on the same shelf, so shelf position doesn\'t vary and its effect can\'t be measured."]],"science-0073":["Find 5 km in the altitude column of Table 1 and read across: 54 kPa. The other numbers come from the 4 km row (62 kPa), the top row (101 kPa), or from Table 2, which lists temperatures.",["Correct: the 5 km row of Table 1 shows 54 kPa.","This is the pressure at 4 km, one row too early.","This is a boiling point from Table 2 (81°C at 50 kPa), not a pressure.","This is the pressure at sea level (0 km), the other end of the table."]],"science-0074":["This time you start from the temperature. Find 97°C in the boiling-point column of Table 2, then read across to the pressure: 90 kPa. Lower pressures give lower boiling points, so 60, 70, and 80 kPa all give temperatures below 97°C.",["At 60 kPa, water boiled at 86°C.","At 70 kPa, water boiled at 90°C.","At 80 kPa, water boiled at 94°C, one row too low.","Correct: Table 2 shows a boiling point of 97°C at 90 kPa."]],"science-0075":["Chain the two tables. Table 1: at 3 km the air pressure is 70 kPa. Table 2: at 70 kPa water boils at 90°C. That is 10°C below the sea-level boiling point of 100°C, so by the rule the time doubles once: 10 × 2 = 20 minutes. The traps skip the tables and apply the rule to altitude directly (40 or 80 minutes), or use a different rule than the one given (11 minutes).",["Treated cooking time as inversely proportional to temperature (10 × 100/90). The given rule says the time doubles for each 10°C drop.","Correct: 3 km means 70 kPa (Table 1), where water boils at 90°C (Table 2). That\'s one 10°C drop, so the time doubles to 20 minutes.","Added 10 minutes for each kilometer of altitude. The rule depends on the boiling temperature, not on altitude directly.","Doubled the time once for each kilometer (2 × 2 × 2 × 10). The rule says to double for each 10°C drop, and the drop here is only 10°C."]],"science-0076":["A steady percent drop means the pressure is multiplied by the same factor for each kilometer, so it is also multiplied by the same factor for each 5 km. From 0 to 5 km the pressure went from 101 to 54 kPa, a factor of about 0.53. Applying the same factor from 5 to 10 km gives 54 × 0.53 ≈ 29 kPa. (Using about 0.88 per km gives the same: 54 × 0.88⁵ ≈ 28 kPa.) The trap is drawing a straight line: subtracting a fixed amount each kilometer gives 7 or 14 kPa, far too low.",["Extended the table as a straight line, dropping about 9.4 kPa per km (the average from 0 to 5 km). The pressure falls by a percent, not a fixed amount.","Extended the table as a straight line using the last 1 km drop (8 kPa per km). A steady percent drop gets smaller in kPa as the pressure falls.","Correct: from 0 to 5 km the pressure fell to 54/101, about 53%, of its value. The next 5 km should do the same: 54 × 0.53 ≈ 29 kPa.","Extended the table by only 1 more km (54 − 8). The question asks about 10 km, 5 km past the table."]],"science-0077":["Compare the H column: P = 400 W, Q = 120 W, R = 40 W, S = 16 W. The smallest is 16 W, for Material S. (Less heat flowing through means S is the best insulator of the four.) Picking P comes from mixing up \'smallest\' with \'largest\'.",["Material P had the largest H (400 W), not the smallest.","Material Q\'s H was 120 W, larger than R\'s or S\'s.","Material R\'s H was 40 W, but Material S\'s was smaller.","Correct: Material S had H = 16 W, the smallest value in Table 1."]],"science-0078":["\'How many more\' asks for a difference: Q\'s 120 W minus R\'s 40 W is 80 W. The ratio (3 times as much) answers a different question, and 160 W is the sum.",["This is how many times as much (120 ÷ 40), not how many more watts.","Correct: 120 W − 40 W = 80 W.","This is the value for Material Q alone, not the difference.","This is the sum of the two values (120 + 40), not the difference."]],"science-0079":["At 0.05 m, R lets through 40 W and S lets through 16 W. In H = kAΔT/d, H is inversely proportional to d when the material, A, and ΔT stay the same. To cut R\'s H from 40 W to 16 W, divide it by 2.5, so multiply the thickness by 2.5: 0.05 × 2.5 = 0.125 m. Check: R\'s k is 40 × 0.05/20 = 0.1, and 0.1 × 1 × 20/0.125 = 16 W. The traps flip the ratio (making R thinner) or square it.",["Flipped the ratio: made R thinner (0.05 × 16/40). R conducts heat better than S, so it must be thicker, not thinner.","Kept the same thickness. At 0.05 m, R lets through 40 W, not 16 W.","Correct: H is inversely proportional to d, so R must be 40/16 = 2.5 times as thick: 0.05 × 2.5 = 0.125 m.","Squared the ratio (0.05 × 2.5²) as if H depended on 1/d². In the model H depends on 1/d."]],"science-0080":["Use the given rule: 1/H_total = 1/400 + 1/16 = 1/400 + 25/400 = 26/400, so H_total = 400/26 ≈ 15.4 W. That is less than 16 W, the value for S alone. It makes sense: stacking a second layer onto S can only slow the heat further, so the pair lets through less heat than either layer by itself. Averaging (208 W) or adding (416 W) the values ignores the reciprocal form of the rule.",["Correct: 1/H_total = 1/400 + 1/16 = 26/400, so H_total ≈ 15.4 W, a bit less than S alone.","Assumed the result must land between the two layers\' values. Adding a second layer can only add resistance, so H_total is below both.","Averaged the two values (400 + 16) ÷ 2. The model adds the reciprocals, not the values.","Added the two values. The model adds the reciprocals (1/H), not H."]],"science-0081":["Read the CO₂ column of Table 1 and find the smallest number. Glucose gave 64 mL, fructose 61 mL, sucrose 58 mL, and lactose just 2 mL. Lactose is the smallest. Sucrose is a trap if you only compare the three similar values and skip the last row.",["Glucose gave the LARGEST volume (64 mL), not the smallest.","Fructose gave 61 mL, one of the larger volumes.","Sucrose gave 58 mL, the smallest of the three high values, but lactose was far lower.","Correct: lactose gave only 2 mL of CO₂."]],"science-0082":["The independent variable is the one the experimenters choose and change on purpose. Experiment 2 used glucose every time and ran it at 15, 25, 35, 45, and 55°C, so temperature was changed. The CO₂ volume was the result they measured. The sugar type was the changed variable in Experiment 1, not Experiment 2, and the water volume stayed at 100 mL in every trial.",["Correct: Experiment 2 tested glucose at five different temperatures.","The sugar type was changed in Experiment 1. Experiment 2 used only glucose.","Every trial used 100 mL of water; it was held constant.","This is what was measured (the dependent variable), not what the students changed."]],"science-0083":["Read Table 2 from top to bottom: 12, 35, 64, 50, 6 mL. The volume climbs until 35°C and then drops. So it increased and then decreased. \\"Increased only\\" fits just the first part of the table, which is the usual trap.",["Only true up to 35°C. After that the volume fell to 50 mL and then 6 mL.","The volume rose at first, from 12 mL to 64 mL.","Correct: the volume rose from 12 mL to a high of 64 mL at 35°C, then fell to 6 mL at 55°C.","This is the reverse pattern: the volume went up first, then down."]],"science-0084":["Experiment 3 is the test for lasting damage: it heats the yeast first, then measures it back at the normal 35°C. If 45°C had damaged the yeast, it would still make less CO₂ after cooling. It doesn\'t: 63 mL versus 64 mL. So the lower value at 45°C in Experiment 2 (50 mL) happened only while the yeast was hot. The claim is not supported. The 55°C result (8 mL after cooling) does show lasting harm, but at a different temperature. The tempting \\"No\\" choice about 45°C versus 55°C reaches the right decision for a reason that doesn\'t address the claim.",["True for 55°C, but the claim is about 45°C. Yeast pretreated at 45°C recovered almost fully.","This shows production was lower AT 45°C, but not that the drop lasted. Experiment 3 tests that, and the yeast recovered.","Right decision, wrong reason: comparing 45°C with 55°C says nothing about whether the 45°C drop was lasting.","Correct: after 10 min at 45°C, the yeast made 63 mL back at 35°C, almost the 64 mL of untreated yeast, so no lasting damage."]],"science-0085":["Look only at the migration rate column: 30, 15, 10, and 7.5 m/yr. The greatest is 30 m/yr, for Dune 1. Don\'t confuse the rate column with the height column: Dune 4 is the tallest but the slowest.",["Correct: Dune 1 migrated 30 m/yr, the largest rate in Table 1.","Dune 2 migrated 15 m/yr, half of Dune 1\'s rate.","Dune 3 migrated 10 m/yr.","Dune 4 is the TALLEST dune, but it had the smallest migration rate (7.5 m/yr)."]],"science-0086":["Line up the two columns. Heights go up: 4, 8, 12, 16 m. Rates go down every time: 30, 15, 10, 7.5 m/yr. So the migration rate decreased only. Taller dunes moved more slowly.",["This is the reverse: taller dunes had smaller rates.","The rates changed a lot, from 30 m/yr down to 7.5 m/yr.","The rate never went up; it fell with every taller dune.","Correct: as height went 4, 8, 12, 16 m, the rate went 30, 15, 10, 7.5 m/yr."]],"science-0087":["Subtract one year\'s distance from the next for Dune 1: 300 − 270 = 30, 270 − 240 = 30, 240 − 210 = 30. The distance fell 30 m each year, which matches Dune 1\'s rate in Table 1. 90 is the total over all 3 years, and 10 is Dune 3\'s yearly change.",["This is Dune 3\'s yearly change (160 to 150), not Dune 1\'s.","Correct: 300 to 270 to 240 to 210 is a drop of 30 m each year.","This is the drop over 2 years (2020 to 2022), not 1 year.","This is the total drop over 3 years (300 − 210), not the yearly drop."]],"science-0088":["In 2020, Dune 1 is 300 m from the highway and Dune 3 is 160 m away, a gap of 140 m. Each year Dune 1 gets 30 m closer and Dune 3 gets 10 m closer, so the gap shrinks by 30 − 10 = 20 m per year. 140 ÷ 20 = 7 years, so 2027. Check: Dune 1 is 300 − 7(30) = 90 m away and Dune 3 is 160 − 7(10) = 90 m away. The traps divide the gap by just one dune\'s rate (2025 or 2034), or find when Dune 1 reaches the highway (2030).",["Divided the 140 m gap by Dune 1\'s rate (30 m/yr). Dune 3 is also moving, so the gap closes only 20 m per year.","Correct: the 140 m gap in 2020 closes by 30 − 10 = 20 m per year, so it takes 7 years: both are 90 m away in 2027.","This is when Dune 1 alone reaches the highway (300 ÷ 30 = 10 years).","Divided the 140 m gap by Dune 3\'s rate (10 m/yr) instead of by the difference in rates."]],"science-0089":["Botanist 2 opens with \\"Cooling causes folding\\" and says the leaves fold when the air temperature drops. Botanist 2 also says light has no direct effect, so any choice that needs a change in light belongs to someone else: darkness is Botanist 1\'s cause, and the internal clock is Botanist 3\'s.",["Correct: Botanist 2 says cooling causes folding.","This is Botanist 1\'s explanation. Botanist 2 says light has no direct effect.","This is Botanist 3\'s explanation.","Botanist 2 says light has no direct effect, so a drop in light isn\'t required."]],"science-0090":["Test each view against what changes: light drops, temperature doesn\'t, and it\'s noon. Botanist 1: darkness causes folding within an hour, so yes. Botanist 2: only cooling causes folding, and there\'s no cooling, so no. Botanist 3: the clock decides, and noon isn\'t the plant\'s folding time; Botanist 3 says one change in light doesn\'t cause folding right away, so no. The trap is \\"Botanists 1 and 3\\": Botanist 3 agrees that light matters, but only as a slow reset of the clock.",["Correct: only Botanist 1 says darkness alone makes the leaves fold within an hour.","Botanist 3 says light matters, but only by resetting the clock over several days; a single change in light does not immediately cause folding.","Botanist 2 needs a temperature drop, and the temperature stays the same. Botanist 3 doesn\'t predict quick folding either.","Botanist 1 predicts exactly this: darkness, then folding within an hour."]],"science-0091":["Work out each botanist\'s prediction first. Botanist 1: the leaves fold within an hour of darkness, so from day 1 they fold soon after the lights go off. Botanist 3: on day 1 the clock still runs on the old schedule, so the leaves fold at their usual time; then the new light cycle resets the clock gradually, so folding moves earlier over several days until it matches lights-off. Only the third choice matches that, and it clashes with Botanist 1 because of day 1. The first choice is Botanist 1\'s prediction, the second is Botanist 2\'s, and the last describes a shift in the wrong direction.",["This is what Botanist 1 predicts, not Botanist 3: the clock wouldn\'t respond to one early lights-off right away.","This fits Botanist 2. Botanist 3 says the leaves still fold each day even when temperature is constant.","Correct: Botanist 3\'s clock keeps the old schedule at first, then is reset gradually by the new light cycle. Botanist 1 predicts folding within an hour of darkness on day 1.","Fits neither: Botanist 1 predicts folding soon after lights-off every day, and Botanist 3 predicts no quick folding on day 1."]],"science-0092":["This setup removes both dusk signals: it never gets dark and it never cools. Botanist 1 needs darkness, so the leaves stay open. Botanist 2 needs a temperature drop, so they stay open. Botanist 3 says the internal clock folds the leaves at about the same time every day, even with light and temperature held constant, so Botanist 3 predicts folding. The answer is Botanists 1 and 2 only. A common slip is to remember only one environmental trigger and pick Botanist 1 alone.",["Botanist 2 also predicts this: with no drop in temperature, nothing causes folding.","Correct: Botanist 1 needs darkness and Botanist 2 needs cooling to cause folding; neither happens.","Botanist 3 says the clock folds the leaves at the usual time even when light and temperature are held constant.","Botanists 1 and 2 both predict the leaves stay open, because neither of their triggers happens."]],"science-0093":["Find the 6 cm row in Table 1 and read across: 1.6 s. The other choices are the fall times for the other wing lengths, so check that you\'re on the right row.",["This is the fall time for 4 cm wings, the row above.","Correct: Table 1 lists 1.6 s for 6 cm wings.","This is the fall time for 8 cm wings, the row below.","This is the fall time for 10 cm wings."]],"science-0094":["Experiment 2 used one helicopter design, with 8 cm wings, and added 0, 1, 2, or 3 paper clips. So the wing length stayed the same. The number of clips was the variable they changed, the fall time was the result they measured, and the mass changed every time a clip was added.",["This is what the students changed in Experiment 2 (0 to 3 clips).","This is what was measured, and it changed from 2.0 s to 1.1 s.","Adding paper clips adds mass, so the mass changed from trial to trial.","Correct: every helicopter in Experiment 2 had 8 cm wings."]],"science-0095":["Read Table 2 down the fall-time column: 2.0, 1.6, 1.3, 1.1 s. Each value is smaller than the one before, so the fall time decreased only. More clips, faster fall, shorter time. Watch out for picking \\"increased only\\" because the number of clips increased.",["Correct: the fall time went 2.0, 1.6, 1.3, 1.1 s, dropping every time.","This is the reverse. More clips made the helicopter fall faster, so the time went down.","The time never went back up; the last value, 1.1 s, is the smallest.","The time changed from 2.0 s to 1.1 s."]],"science-0096":["The passage says each helicopter fell at a nearly constant speed for almost the whole drop. This one fell 2.0 m in 2.0 s, about 1.0 m/s. At a constant speed, twice the distance takes twice the time, so 4.0 m takes about 4.0 s. The strongest trap is 2.8 s: students who know that a dropped ball\'s time grows with the square root of the height apply it here, but that rule is for an object that keeps speeding up.",["Assumed the fall time doesn\'t depend on the height. Twice the distance at the same speed takes longer.","Used the free-fall rule that time grows with the square root of distance (2.0 × √2). That rule is for a speeding-up object; the helicopter falls at a nearly constant speed.","Correct: it fell at a nearly constant speed of about 2.0 m ÷ 2.0 s = 1.0 m/s, so 4.0 m takes about 4.0 s.","Squared the height ratio (2.0 × 2²). At a constant speed, time is simply proportional to distance."]],"science-0097":["Find 10°C in the temperature column of Table 1 and read across: d = 8 h. The other choices are the doubling times at the other temperatures.",["This is the doubling time at 30°C.","This is the doubling time at 20°C.","Correct: Table 1 lists d = 8 h at 10°C.","This is the doubling time at 4°C, the row above."]],"science-0098":["Read down Table 1: as the temperature rises from 4°C to 30°C, d falls from 20 h to 8 h to 2 h to 1 h. It goes down every time, so the doubling time decreased only. Warmer milk lets the bacteria double faster.",["This is the reverse: warmer milk had SHORTER doubling times.","The doubling time never went up.","The doubling time changed from 20 h to 1 h.","Correct: as temperature went 4, 10, 20, 30°C, d went 20, 8, 2, 1 h."]],"science-0099":["Spoiling means N grows from 1,000 to 1,024,000, which is 1,024 = 2¹⁰ times bigger: 10 doublings. At 20°C, d = 2 h, so 10 hours gives 10 ÷ 2 = 5 doublings. That leaves 5 doublings at 4°C, where d = 20 h: 5 × 20 = 100 h. Total time: 10 + 100 = 110 h. The traps: 200 h or 210 h start the 4°C clock from scratch, and 100 h forgets to add the first 10 hours.",["This is only the time spent at 4°C (5 doublings × 20 h). Add the first 10 h.","Correct: spoiling takes 10 doublings. 10 h at 20°C is 5 doublings; the other 5 at 4°C take 5 × 20 = 100 h; 10 + 100 = 110 h.","This is the time to spoil at 4°C from the start. The 10 h at 20°C already used up 5 doublings.","Added the 10 h at 20°C to the full 200 h at 4°C, ignoring the 5 doublings that already happened."]],"science-0100":["The sample now has to grow from 8,000 to 1,024,000 per mL, which is 128 times bigger. Since 128 = 2⁷, that takes 7 doublings. At 10°C each doubling takes 8 h, so 7 × 8 = 56 h. Starting 8 times higher saves only 3 doublings (8 = 2³), or 24 h, out of the original 80 h. Dividing 80 by 8 to get 10 h is the trap: multiplying the starting number by 8 doesn\'t divide the time by 8.",["Divided the 80 h spoilage time by 8. Starting 8 times higher removes only 3 doublings, because 8 = 2³.","This is the time SAVED (3 doublings × 8 h), not the time to spoil.","Correct: 1,024,000 ÷ 8,000 = 128 = 2⁷, so 7 doublings × 8 h = 56 h.","This is the time for a sample starting at 1,000 per mL (10 doublings × 8 h). The higher start changes it."]],"science-0101":["Find the 4 h row in Table 1, then move across to the water-rinsed column: 11 mL. The other choices come from the wrong column (1 mL for dry, 19 mL for vinegar-rinsed) or the wrong row (17 mL at 8 h).",["This is the 4 h value for the DRY steel wool, the column to the left.","Correct: the 4 h row of the water-rinsed column reads 11 mL.","This is the water-rinsed value at 8 h, the next row down.","This is the 4 h value for the VINEGAR-rinsed steel wool, the column to the right."]],"science-0102":["Experiment 2 used vinegar-rinsed steel wool every time and changed only the size of the tube: 50, 100, and 150 mL. The treatment was the variable in Experiment 1, and the temperature (22°C) and steel wool mass (2 g) were the same in every trial.",["Correct: Experiment 2 used tubes of 50, 100, and 150 mL.","The treatment was changed in Experiment 1. Experiment 2 used only vinegar-rinsed steel wool.","Every trial was done at 22°C.","Every trial used a 2 g wad of steel wool."]],"science-0103":["Go to the 2 h row of Table 1: dry 1 mL, water-rinsed 6 mL, vinegar-rinsed 15 mL. The largest is 15 mL, so the vinegar-rinsed steel wool had caused the greatest rise.",["Correct: at 2 h, vinegar-rinsed wool had raised the water 15 mL, more than 6 mL or 1 mL.","Water-rinsed wool had raised the water only 6 mL at 2 h.","Dry wool had raised the water only 1 mL at 2 h, the LEAST of the three.","The three values at 2 h are different: 1, 6, and 15 mL."]],"science-0104":["The water rises only as O₂ is used up, so the rise tells you how much O₂ has been used. When the O₂ in a 100 mL tube runs out, the rise stops at about 20 mL: both rinsed samples leveled off at 20 mL, and Experiment 2 shows a 100 mL tube gives a 20 mL rise. The dry steel wool raised the water only 3 mL in 24 h, so most of its O₂ was still there. The O₂ didn\'t run out; the dry steel wool simply rusted slowly. The tempting \\"Yes\\" choice notices that the dry wool\'s rise almost stopped, but a slow rise doesn\'t mean the O₂ was gone. The last choice says \\"No\\" for a reason that is true but has nothing to do with the dry steel wool.",["The rise did slow down, but slow rusting looks the same in the table. The rise shows how much O₂ was used: only 3 mL of about 20 mL.","True, but it doesn\'t show WHY the dry wool\'s rise was small. Running out of O₂ is one possible reason; the data rule it out.","Correct: the water rise equals the O₂ used. A 100 mL tube\'s O₂ supports about a 20 mL rise, so after a 3 mL rise most of the O₂ was still there.","True, but it doesn\'t address the dry steel wool at all. The right decision needs the right reason."]],"science-0105":["Find the 12:00 row and read across to the 10 cm column: 24°C. The 38°C choice is the surface temperature in the same row, and 29°C and 30°C are 10 cm readings from later rows.",["Correct: the 12:00 row of the 10 cm column reads 24°C.","This is the 10 cm reading at 15:00, the next row down.","This is the 10 cm reading at 18:00.","This is the SURFACE (0 cm) reading at 12:00, the column to the left."]],"science-0106":["For each column, subtract the lowest value from the highest. Surface: 42 − 12 = 30°C. 10 cm: 30 − 18 = 12°C. 40 cm: 22.8 − 22.0 = 0.8°C. The deepest soil barely changed, so 40 cm had the smallest difference.",["The surface changed the MOST: from 12°C to 42°C, a difference of 30°C.","At 10 cm the temperature went from 18°C to 30°C, a difference of 12°C.","Correct: at 40 cm the temperature stayed between 22.0°C and 22.8°C, a difference of less than 1°C.","The differences are 30°C, 12°C, and 0.8°C, so they aren\'t the same."]],"science-0107":["For heat to flow out of the 10 cm layer in both directions, the 10 cm soil must be warmer than BOTH the surface and the 40 cm soil. Check each time. 00:00: 22 is warmer than 16 but cooler than 22.6, so it fails. 12:00: 24 is cooler than 38, so it fails. 18:00: 30 equals 30, so no heat flows up; it fails. 21:00: 26 is warmer than 20 and warmer than 22.8, so it works. The trap at 00:00 is checking only the surface; the trap at 18:00 is missing that equal temperatures mean no heat flow.",["At 00:00 the 10 cm soil (22°C) was warmer than the surface (16°C) but COOLER than the 40 cm soil (22.6°C), so heat flowed up into it from below.","At 12:00 the 10 cm soil (24°C) was cooler than the surface (38°C), so heat flowed down into it from above.","At 18:00 the 10 cm soil and the surface were both 30°C. With no temperature difference, no heat flowed between them.","Correct: at 21:00 the 10 cm soil (26°C) was warmer than both the surface (20°C) and the 40 cm soil (22.8°C)."]],"science-0108":["Look at two patterns in Table 1. First, the daily high gets smaller with depth: 42°C at the surface, 30°C at 10 cm, and 22.8°C at 40 cm. Second, the high arrives later with depth: 15:00 at the surface, 18:00 at 10 cm, and 21:00 at 40 cm. A depth of 20 cm is between 10 cm and 40 cm, so its high should be between 22.8°C and 30°C and should come between 18:00 and 21:00. Only the last choice fits both patterns. Each trap fits one pattern but not the other, so check both parts of every choice.",["Wrong on both counts: deeper soil had a LOWER daily high, and its high came LATER than the surface\'s.","Right timing, but the daily high drops with depth: 42°C at 0 cm, 30°C at 10 cm, 22.8°C at 40 cm. At 20 cm it should be below 30°C.","Right temperature range, but the high comes later with depth (15:00 at 0 cm, 18:00 at 10 cm, 21:00 at 40 cm), not earlier.","Correct: 20 cm lies between 10 cm and 40 cm, so its high should fall between their highs (30°C and 22.8°C) and between their times (18:00 and 21:00)."]],"science-0109":["Student 1\'s key idea is use and disuse: parts that aren\'t used shrink during a fish\'s life, and those smaller eyes get passed on. Saving energy is Student 2\'s idea, and chance build-up of mutations is Student 3\'s.",["This is Student 2\'s explanation.","Correct: Student 1 says unused eyes shrank during each fish\'s life and the smaller eyes were passed on.","This is Student 3\'s explanation.","None of the students says this."]],"science-0110":["Student 2 says random inherited mutations made some fish grow smaller eyes. Student 3 opens by saying \\"Student 2 is correct that the eye changes came from random inherited mutations,\\" so Student 3 holds this view too. Student 1 credits lack of use, not mutations. So the answer is Students 2 and 3 only.",["Student 1 never mentions mutations; Student 1 says unused eyes shrank during each fish\'s life.","Correct: Student 2 names random inherited mutations, and Student 3 agrees with Student 2 on that point.","Student 3 does say this, but Student 3 is agreeing with Student 2, who said it first.","Student 1 explains the change by lack of use, not by mutations."]],"science-0111":["Match the finding to the claim it backs up. The researchers found that eye size makes no difference to survival or reproduction in the caves. Student 3 says exactly that: in total darkness, eye size did not affect survival or reproduction. So Student 3 would cite it. The trap is the last choice: it names Student 3, but its reason is about random mutations, and this finding says nothing about mutations. Student 2 can\'t use the finding at all, because Student 2\'s explanation needs small-eyed fish to survive and reproduce more often. And the finding doesn\'t touch Student 1\'s idea that eyes shrank from disuse.",["The finding is about survival and reproduction, not inheritance, and Student 1\'s explanation makes no claim about survival.","Backward: Student 2 needs small-eyed fish to survive and reproduce MORE often, so equal survival works against Student 2.","Correct: Student 3 says that in total darkness eye size did not affect survival or reproduction, which is exactly what the researchers found.","Right student, wrong reason: the finding is about survival, and it says nothing about where the eye changes came from."]],"science-0112":["This experiment tests whether eyes shrink during one fish\'s life when they aren\'t used. Student 1 says exactly that happens, so the result is inconsistent with Student 1. Students 2 and 3 both say the change came from inherited mutations over many generations; neither says a single fish\'s eyes shrink in the dark, so the result doesn\'t conflict with them. The trap is to think any no-shrink result hurts every explanation of eye loss.",["Correct: Student 1 says unused eyes shrink during a fish\'s own life, but these fish never used their eyes and their eyes didn\'t shrink.","Student 3 says the change came from mutations building up over many generations, not within one fish\'s life. One generation in the dark doesn\'t test that.","Students 2 and 3 both say the change took many generations of inherited mutations. Neither predicts shrinking within one lifetime.","Student 1\'s explanation predicts that eyes shrink during a life spent in the dark, which didn\'t happen."]],"science-0113":["Experiment 2\'s results are in Table 2. Find the 1.5 A row: 14 clips. The 13 comes from Table 1, which is a different experiment, and 9 and 18 are the rows just above and below.",["This is the result at 1.0 A, the row above.","This is from Table 1 (60 turns), not Table 2.","Correct: Table 2 lists 14 clips at 1.5 A.","This is the result at 2.0 A, the row below."]],"science-0114":["Experiment 1 kept the current at 1.0 A and changed the number of turns. Experiment 2 kept 40 turns and changed the current. So the current is the factor varied in Experiment 2 but held constant in Experiment 1. Turns are the reverse case, both used iron cores, and the clip count is what was measured.",["This is the reverse: turns were varied in Experiment 1 and held at 40 in Experiment 2.","Both experiments used iron cores; the core was varied only in Experiment 3.","This was measured in both experiments; it\'s the result, not a factor the students set.","Correct: the current was 1.0 A throughout Experiment 1 but ranged from 0.5 A to 2.0 A in Experiment 2."]],"science-0115":["The prediction is about ANY metal, so one metal that fails is enough to reject it. Aluminum is a metal, and the aluminum core lifted 0 clips, exactly the same as no core. So the answer is No, and the reason is the aluminum result. The tempting \\"Yes\\" choice only looks at the metals that worked. The other \\"No\\" choice gives a true fact, but steel lifting fewer clips than iron doesn\'t break the prediction, since steel still beat no core.",["Steel and iron did beat no core, but the prediction says ANY metal. Aluminum is a metal, and it lifted 0 clips.","False: the aluminum and wood cores both lifted 0 clips.","True, but steel still lifted more than no core. This fact doesn\'t contradict the prediction.","Correct: aluminum is a metal, but it lifted 0 clips, the same as no core, so not every metal helps."]],"science-0116":["Combine two results. From Experiment 3, at 40 turns and 1.0 A, steel lifted 7 clips and iron lifted 9, so steel is the weaker core. From Experiment 1, adding turns lifts more clips: iron went from 9 clips at 40 turns to 18 at 80 turns. A steel core with 80 turns should therefore beat a steel core with 40 turns (more than 7) but fall short of an iron core with 80 turns (less than 18). The traps use only one of the two results.",["More turns make a stronger magnet, so 80 turns should lift MORE than the 7 clips a 40-turn steel core lifted.","Correct: a 40-turn steel core lifted 7 clips, so 80 turns should lift more than 7; steel is weaker than iron, so fewer than iron\'s 18.","18 is the result for an IRON core with 80 turns. Steel lifted fewer clips than iron under the same conditions.","Steel did worse than iron in Experiment 3 (7 vs. 9), so it shouldn\'t beat iron\'s 18 clips."]],"science-0117":["All four values have the same number of decimal places, so compare the last digits: 12, 17, 23, and 9 millionths. Z\'s 0.000009 is the smallest. Y is the largest, which is the trap if you read the question too fast.",["W\'s α (0.000012) is larger than Z\'s (0.000009).","X\'s α (0.000017) is the second largest.","Y\'s α (0.000023) is the LARGEST, not the smallest.","Correct: Z\'s α, 0.000009, is the smallest."]],"science-0118":["Read down the ΔL column of Table 2: 0.24, 0.48, 0.72, 0.96 mm. Each value is larger than the one before, so ΔL increased only. Heating the rod more made it grow more.",["Correct: ΔL went 0.24, 0.48, 0.72, 0.96 mm, rising every time.","This is the reverse trend.","ΔL never went down; the last value, 0.96 mm, is the largest.","ΔL changed from 0.24 mm to 0.96 mm."]],"science-0119":["50°C falls halfway between the 40°C and 60°C rows. ΔL goes up by 0.24 mm every 20°C, so halfway between 0.48 mm and 0.72 mm is 0.60 mm. You can also use the equation: 0.000012 × 1,000 × 50 = 0.60 mm. The traps are the values at 40°C and 60°C themselves.",["This is ΔL for 40°C. At 50°C the rod grows more.","Correct: 50°C is halfway between 40°C and 60°C, so ΔL is halfway between 0.48 mm and 0.72 mm.","This is ΔL for 60°C. At 50°C the rod grows less.","This is ΔL for 80°C."]],"science-0120":["Solve the equation for α: α = ΔL ÷ (L₀ × ΔT). The temperature went from 20°C to 35°C, so ΔT = 15°C, not 20 or 35. Then α = 0.69 ÷ (2,000 × 15) = 0.69 ÷ 30,000 = 0.000023, which is Metal Y. Check: 0.000023 × 2,000 × 15 = 0.69 mm. The traps come from using one of the temperatures instead of the change: ΔT = 20 gives about 0.000017 (Metal X), and ΔT = 35 gives about 0.0000099 (closest to Metal Z).",["With W\'s α, the rod would grow only 0.000012 × 2,000 × 15 = 0.36 mm.","Used ΔT = 20, the starting temperature. ΔT is the CHANGE: 35 − 20 = 15°C.","Correct: α = 0.69 ÷ (2,000 × 15) = 0.69 ÷ 30,000 = 0.000023, which matches Y.","Used ΔT = 35, the final temperature. ΔT is the CHANGE: 35 − 20 = 15°C."]],"science-0121":["For each scientist, ask whether the thing they blame was present. Scientist 1 blames drought, but the cages were watered. Scientist 2 blames fire, but no fires occurred. Scientist 3 blames mice, but the cages kept them out. With every proposed cause removed, all three explanations predict that the seedlings would survive, so the result fits all three. It can\'t tell them apart, which is why a good test removes only one cause at a time.",["Scientist 1 fits (watering prevents drought), but so do the others: the cages kept out mice and there were no fires.","Leaves out Scientist 2: with no fires, Scientist 2 also expects the young pines to survive.","Leaves out Scientist 1: watering removed the drought that Scientist 1 blames, so Scientist 1 also expects survival.","Correct: each scientist\'s cause (drought, fire, mice) was absent, so all three predict that the pines would survive."]],"science-0122":["The finding must hurt Scientist 3 and leave Scientist 1 untouched. Scientist 3\'s key claim is that mice eat nearly all the pine seeds on the south slope before they sprout. If seeds sprout just as well with or without protection from mice, the mice aren\'t stopping them, so Scientist 3 is weakened. Scientist 1 blames drought killing seedlings after they sprout, so this finding doesn\'t touch that idea. The equal-soil-water finding is the trap: it does weaken Scientist 3, who agrees the soil is drier, but it weakens Scientist 1 even more.",["This weakens both: Scientists 1 and 3 both say the south slope\'s soil is drier, and Scientist 1\'s whole explanation rests on it.","Correct: Scientist 3 says mice eat nearly all seeds on the south slope before they sprout; this contradicts that and says nothing about drought.","This weakens Scientist 2, who blames fire; it doesn\'t bear on drought or mice.","This supports Scientist 1 (seedlings need moist soil) and doesn\'t conflict with Scientist 3, who agrees the south slope is drier."]],"science-0123":["Ask whether each scientist\'s cause depends on the grass. Scientist 1: seedlings die because sunlight dries the soil; removing grass doesn\'t change the sunlight, so Scientist 1 predicts the pines would still mostly die. Scientist 2: dry grass is what burns, and fires kill young pines; with no grass, fires rarely spread, so more pines would survive. Scientist 3: mice hide in the grass; with no grass they become rare, as on the north slope, and more seeds sprout. So Scientists 2 and 3 predict many more pines. The common miss is to link the grass only to the mice and forget that it is also the fuel in Scientist 2\'s explanation.",["Forgets Scientist 2: without grass, there is nothing to carry fires, so Scientist 2 also expects more pines.","Scientist 1 blames soil dried by direct sunlight, and removing the grass doesn\'t change the sunlight.","Correct: no grass means no fuel for fires (Scientist 2) and no cover for mice (Scientist 3), but the sun would still dry the soil (Scientist 1).","Includes Scientist 1, whose cause, soil dried by strong sun, would still be there."]],"science-0124":["First find R\'s density: 30 g ÷ 40 cm³ = 0.75 g/cm³. That is less than oil\'s density (0.80), so R still floats. Then use the passage\'s rule: the fraction below the surface equals the block\'s density divided by the liquid\'s density. In water that is 0.75 ÷ 1.00 = 75%; in oil it is 0.75 ÷ 0.80, about 94%. So R floats lower in oil. The \\"sink\\" choice compares oil with water instead of with R, and the \\"smaller fraction\\" choice gets the division backward.",["R\'s density, 30 ÷ 40 = 0.75 g/cm³, is still less than oil\'s 0.80 g/cm³, so R floats; it only has to be less dense than the liquid it is in.","Reversed: dividing R\'s density by a smaller liquid density gives a larger fraction, so R sits lower in oil.","R\'s density is unchanged, but the fraction below the surface also depends on the liquid\'s density, which is lower for oil.","Correct: the fraction below the surface is 0.75 ÷ 1.00 = 75% in water but 0.75 ÷ 0.80, about 94%, in oil."]],"science-0125":["Run the passage\'s rule backward. The fraction below the surface (0.90) equals V\'s density divided by salt water\'s density (1.20), so V\'s density is 0.90 × 1.20 = 1.08 g/cm³. A block floats only in liquids denser than itself. Salt water (1.20) qualifies; water (1.00) and oil (0.80) don\'t, so V floats only in salt water. Two traps: reading the 90% as a density of 0.90 (which is true only in water), and getting 1.08 right but forgetting that it is more than 1.00.",["Correct: V\'s density is 0.90 × 1.20 = 1.08 g/cm³, which is less than salt water\'s 1.20 but more than water\'s 1.00 and oil\'s 0.80.","Treats the 90% as if V were floating in water; in salt water the fraction must be multiplied by 1.20.","Divides instead of multiplying: 0.90 ÷ 1.20 = 0.75, but the fraction equals V\'s density divided by 1.20, so V\'s density is 0.90 × 1.20.","Right density, wrong decision: 1.08 g/cm³ is more than water\'s 1.00 g/cm³, so V sinks in water."]],"science-0126":["Check the claim against trials with the same bob mass. From Trial 1 to Trial 3, the length went from 0.25 m to 1.00 m, which is 4 times as long, and the period went from 1.0 s to 2.0 s, only twice as long. If doubling the length doubled the period, making it 4 times as long would have made the period 4 times as long. Trials 3 and 5 agree: the length more than doubled (1.00 m to 2.25 m), but the period rose only 1.5 times. The tempting \\"Yes\\" choice sees the period double and misses that the length quadrupled.",["The period did double, but the length went from 0.25 m to 1.00 m, which is 4 times as long, not twice.","The claim is about length, not mass; Trials 1 and 2 show only that the bob\'s mass doesn\'t affect the period.","Correct: if doubling the length doubled the period, 4 times the length would give 4 times the period, but going from 0.25 m to 1.00 m only doubled it (1.0 s to 2.0 s).","False: the period rose with length, from 1.0 s at 0.25 m to 3.0 s at 2.25 m."]],"science-0127":["The periods do not rise in a straight line. Compare trials with the same mass: going from 0.25 m to 1.00 m (4 times as long) doubled the period, from 1.0 s to 2.0 s. In fact the lengths are 0.5², 1², and 1.5², and the periods are 2 × 0.5, 2 × 1, and 2 × 1.5 seconds, so the period is 2 times the square root of the length. A 9.00 m pendulum is 4 times as long as the 2.25 m one, so its period should be about twice 3.0 s, or 6.0 s. A straight line through the last two trials gives 8.4 s, too high, because each added meter adds less time than the one before.",["This is the period for a 4.00 m pendulum, the next length in the pattern 0.25, 1.00, 2.25, 4.00, not for 9.00 m.","Correct: making a pendulum 4 times as long doubles its period (0.25 m to 1.00 m: 1.0 s to 2.0 s), and 9.00 m is 4 times 2.25 m, so the period is 2 × 3.0 = 6.0 s.","Extends the last two trials in a straight line (0.8 s more per meter); each added meter adds less time than the one before, so this overshoots.","Assumes the period is proportional to the length (2.0 s per meter), but quadrupling the length only doubled the period."]],"science-0128":["Link the two experiments through the drying time. The tuns in Experiment 1 were dried for 8 hours, and at 80°C they survived at 64%, the same as the 8-hour row of Table 2. Tuns dried for 4 hours survived at 30%, and half of 64% is 32%, so 30% is less than half. The other choices misread the tables. Survival barely changes after 8 hours of drying (64%, 66%, 64%), so longer drying doesn\'t \\"greatly increase\\" it and gives no reason to expect more than 50% at 100°C, where 8-hour tuns managed only 22%. And 1-hour tuns at 80°C (8%) did far worse than active tardigrades at 40°C (50%).",["Backwards: 1-hour tuns survived 80°C at 8%, much lower than the 50% for active tardigrades at 40°C.","Survival leveled off after 8 hours: 64% at 8 hours, 66% at 24, and 64% at 48. That is no big increase.","Correct: the Experiment 1 tuns were dried 8 hours and survived 80°C at 64%; half of that is 32%, and 4-hour tuns survived at 30%.","Unsupported: 8-hour tuns survived 100°C at only 22%, and drying past 8 hours barely changed survival at 80°C, so above 50% is not expected."]],"science-0129":["The proposal makes two predictions about Experiment 2: survival should rise with drying time at first (while the molecules are being built), and then stop rising (once there are enough). Table 2 shows both: 8%, 30%, 64% for 1, 4, and 8 hours, then 66% and 64% for 24 and 48 hours. So the answer is Yes, for that reason. The other Yes reason comes from Experiment 1 and isn\'t even true, since the two groups tied at 20°C. The No reasons treat a 2-point wobble as a real drop, or require the molecules to give perfect protection, which the proposal never claims.",["Correct: survival climbs from 8% to 64% over the first 8 hours (protection building up) and then levels off at about 64-66% (more time adds nothing).","Wrong experiment, and false: this uses Table 1, not Experiment 2, and at 20°C tuns and active tardigrades both survived at 98%.","Overreads a tiny dip: 66% versus 64% is a 2-point difference, which fits \\"stayed about the same,\\" not a real drop.","The proposal never says protection is complete; it only says it stops increasing, so survival below 100% doesn\'t go against it."]],"science-0130":["Combine two trends. Table 1 (8-hour tuns) shows survival falling as the temperature rises: 64% at 80°C and 22% at 100°C. Table 2 shows that at 80°C, 4-hour tuns survived much less often than 8-hour tuns (30% versus 64%). So 4-hour tuns at 100°C face both disadvantages: a higher temperature than in Table 2 and less drying than in Table 1. Their survival should be below the 22% that 8-hour tuns managed at 100°C. The trap is the 22-30% range, which treats the two tables as upper and lower limits instead of noticing that 22% is already a ceiling.",["Above every tun result at 80°C or hotter; heating to 100°C and drying less both lower survival.","This range is between the 4-hour and 8-hour results at 80°C; at 100°C survival should be lower than at 80°C.","Used only one table at a time: below 30% (4-hour tuns at 80°C) and above 22% (8-hour tuns at 100°C), but 4-hour tuns should do worse than 8-hour tuns at 100°C too.","Correct: 8-hour tuns survived 100°C at 22% (Table 1), and 4-hour tuns are less protected than 8-hour tuns (30% versus 64% at 80°C in Table 2), so they should survive 100°C at less than 22%."]],"science-0131":["Check the finding against each viewpoint separately. Scientist 1 says simple life \\"may be common wherever there is liquid water\\" and that civilizations are rare because of later unlikely steps. A planet with simple life and no civilization is exactly what Scientist 1 would expect. Scientist 2 says civilizations \\"may arise fairly often\\" over the galaxy\'s history. That doesn\'t mean every planet with life has one, so one planet without a civilization doesn\'t go against Scientist 2. The finding fits both. The common trap is remembering Scientist 1 as \\"life is rare,\\" when the passage says only the steps to technology are rare.",["Neither scientist claims simple life is rare or that every life-bearing planet has a civilization, so the finding contradicts no one.","Fits Scientist 1, who expects simple life to be common and civilizations rare, but it doesn\'t contradict Scientist 2 either.","Trap: Scientist 1 doesn\'t say life is rare; Scientist 1 says simple life may be common and only the later steps are rare.","Correct: Scientist 1 expects simple life to be common, and Scientist 2 says civilizations arise only \\"fairly often,\\" not on every planet, so neither view is contradicted."]],"science-0132":["Find what each viewpoint depends on. Scientist 1 depends on the steps to technology, including complex cells and many-celled bodies, being rare. Scientist 2 depends on short broadcast times and incomplete searches. Finding many-celled life that evolved independently on several nearby planets would show one of Scientist 1\'s \\"rare\\" steps happening often, which weakens Scientist 1; Scientist 2 says nothing about those steps. A thorough search with no detections hurts Scientist 2 instead. Simple life in an icy moon\'s ocean is something Scientist 1 already allows, and short broadcast times support Scientist 2.",["Correct: Scientist 1 rests on steps like many-celled bodies being rare; several independent cases nearby would undercut that, while Scientist 2 makes no claim about those steps.","This weakens Scientist 2, whose argument depends on searches having covered only a tiny fraction of stars and frequencies; it fits Scientist 1.","Scientist 1 already expects simple life to be common, so this weakens neither viewpoint.","This supports Scientist 2\'s claim that each civilization is detectable only briefly, and it doesn\'t go against Scientist 1."]],"science-0133":["Each scientist explains why the signal was never heard again, so each predicts no repeat a month later, just for a different reason. Scientist 1: the transmitter moved away or went quiet. Scientist 2: the cloud brightened only briefly and was faint again. Scientist 3: the comet drifted to a different part of the sky. It\'s easy to pick only the scientists whose source \\"moves\\" (1 and 3) and forget that Scientist 2\'s source stays put but stops shining brightly.",["Scientist 1 does predict no repeat, but so do the other two, each for a different reason.","Leaves out Scientist 1, who says the transmitter would have moved away or stopped broadcasting.","Leaves out Scientist 2, who says the cloud\'s brightening was rare and short, so it would be faint again.","Correct: the transmitter would be gone (1), the cloud would have faded (2), and the comet would have moved on (3)."]],"science-0134":["List what each explanation needs. Scientist 3 needs a comet, with its hydrogen cloud, in the part of the sky Big Ear was watching. Scientist 2 needs a distant hydrogen cloud that brightened briefly. Both need the signal to be at hydrogen\'s frequency. So a finding that no comet was there knocks out Scientist 3 and leaves Scientist 2 untouched. A frequency far from 1420 MHz would hurt both hydrogen explanations, not just one. The other two findings each target a different scientist.",["This weakens both explanations, because Scientists 2 and 3 both say the signal came from hydrogen, which gives off radio waves near 1420 MHz.","Correct: Scientist 3 needs a comet in that part of the sky; Scientist 2\'s cloud is far beyond the solar system and needs no comet.","This weakens Scientist 1, who blames a transmitter on a satellite or aircraft; it says nothing against Scientist 3.","This weakens Scientist 2, whose explanation depends on such a cloud brightening briefly; it leaves Scientist 3 alone."]],"science-0135":["Scientist 2 says a hydrogen cloud far beyond the solar system brightened. Scientist 3 says the hydrogen gas around a comet produced the signal. The common ground is hydrogen gas. The tempting wrong answer is \\"beyond the solar system,\\" because both sources sound far away, but a comet that passes near the Sun is part of our solar system, so Scientist 3 would not agree. Neither of them thinks the source still shines brightly, and the human-made transmitter is Scientist 1\'s idea.",["Only Scientist 2 places the source beyond the solar system; a comet passing near the Sun is inside the solar system.","Neither says this: Scientist 2\'s cloud faded again, and Scientist 3\'s comet moved on.","Correct: Scientist 2 credits a distant hydrogen cloud and Scientist 3 the hydrogen gas around a comet.","This is Scientist 1\'s explanation; Scientists 2 and 3 both describe natural sources."]],"science-0136":["Two patterns, both nonlinear. Table 2: each time the concentration doubles, the clock time is divided by 4 (640 → 160 → 40), so the time is proportional to 1/(concentration)². A concentration of 0.30 is 1.5 times 0.20, so at 20°C the time is about 160 ÷ 1.5² = 160 ÷ 2.25 ≈ 71 s, well below the 100 s that a straight line between 160 s and 40 s would give. Table 1: each 10°C rise halves the clock time. So at 30°C the time is about 71 ÷ 2 ≈ 36 s.",["Correct: in Table 2, doubling the concentration divides the time by 4, so the time goes as 1/(concentration)²; 0.30 is 1.5 times 0.20, so the time is 160 ÷ 1.5² ≈ 71 s at 20°C, and 10°C warmer halves it to about 36 s.","Halved the straight-line midpoint of 160 s and 40 s. The time does not change in a straight line with concentration.","This is the right estimate at 20°C, but it forgets that raising the temperature by 10°C halves the clock time.","This is the straight-line midpoint between 160 s (0.20 mol/L) and 40 s (0.40 mol/L) at 20°C, ignoring both the curve and the temperature."]],"science-0137":["Table 2 shows the rule: each sheet passes the same fraction of the light reaching it, so the fractions multiply (one X: 80%; two X: 0.8 × 0.8 = 64%). Work backward from the stack: 0.80 × (W\'s fraction) = 0.16, so W passes 0.20 of red light. Two W sheets pass 0.20 × 0.20 = 0.04, or 4%. The strongest trap is 2.6%, which squares the stack\'s 16%; that treats the X sheet as if it were doubled too. Stopping at 20% gives one W sheet, not two.",["Squares the whole stack\'s 16% (0.16 × 0.16), which counts the X sheet twice. Only W\'s own fraction should be squared.","Correct: X passes 80% of red, so W passes 0.16 ÷ 0.80 = 0.20; two W sheets pass 0.20 × 0.20 = 0.04, or 4%.","Halves 16%, as if each W sheet took away a fixed share. Table 2 shows sheets multiply fractions; they don\'t split a total.","This is one sheet of W (0.16 ÷ 0.80), not two."]],"science-0138":["Put every tube on the same footing using f × L = constant from Experiment 1. A 0.25 m tube of carbon dioxide: 130 Hz at 0.50 m, doubled for half the length, is 260 Hz. A 0.40 m tube of air at 0°C: 165 Hz at 0.50 m, times 0.50/0.40 = 1.25, is about 206 Hz. A 0.50 m tube of air at 60°C: 181 Hz, read straight from Experiment 3. A 1.00 m tube of helium: 500 Hz at 0.50 m, halved for twice the length, is 250 Hz. The lowest is 181 Hz. Each wrong choice has one feature that pulls f down (the gas with the lowest f, the coldest air, the longest tube), but each also has a second feature that pushes f back up.",["Carbon dioxide gives the lowest frequency at 0.50 m, but halving the tube doubles it: about 2 × 130 = 260 Hz.","Cold air lowers f, but the shorter tube raises it more: 165 × (0.50 ÷ 0.40) is about 206 Hz.","Correct: this one can be read straight from Experiment 3 (181 Hz), and every other tube works out higher (260, about 206, and 250 Hz).","The longest tube, but helium\'s high frequency more than makes up for it: 500 ÷ 2 = 250 Hz."]],"science-0139":["Experiment 3 used 0.50 m tubes, but this tube is 0.40 m. From Experiment 1, f × L is constant, so a 0.40 m tube\'s frequency is 0.50/0.40 = 1.25 times the 0.50 m value (check: 170 × 1.25 = 212.5, matching the 213 Hz in Experiment 1). Scale Experiment 3: 0°C about 206 Hz, 20°C about 213 Hz, 40°C about 220 Hz, 60°C about 226 Hz. The target, 225 Hz, falls between 40°C and 60°C. The strongest trap is \\"above 60°C\\": if you compare 225 Hz with the unscaled numbers in Experiment 3, it\'s higher than all of them.",["Wrong direction: at 20°C the 0.40 m tube gives only 213 Hz, and colder air lowers f further.","At 40°C the 0.40 m tube gives about 176 × 1.25 = 220 Hz, still below 225 Hz.","Correct: scaled to 0.40 m (× 1.25), Experiment 3 gives about 220 Hz at 40°C and about 226 Hz at 60°C, so 225 Hz falls between them.","Uses Experiment 3\'s 0.50 m values directly (225 Hz is above even 181 Hz). The 0.40 m tube\'s frequencies are 1.25 times higher."]],"science-0140":["Work through intensity, not current. In Study 3 the current was 56 mA, and Table 2 shows 56 mA at 800 W/m², so Lamp Q gives 800 W/m² at 30 cm. Table 1 shows that intensity times distance squared is constant (900 × 20² = 400 × 30² = 225 × 40² = 360,000), so moving from 30 cm to 45 cm multiplies the intensity by (30/45)² = 4/9: 800 × 4/9 ≈ 356 W/m². Up to 400 W/m², Table 2 rises a steady 8 mA per 100 W/m², so 356 W/m² gives about 28 mA. The strong trap is 25 mA (56 × 4/9): above 400 W/m² the current grows more slowly than the intensity, so scaling the current itself undercounts.",["This is about the current 45 cm from Lamp P (178 W/m²). Lamp Q is brighter: at 30 cm it gives 56 mA, while Lamp P gives only 32 mA.","Multiplies the 56 mA current by (30/45)² = 4/9. The intensity scales that way, but Table 2 shows the current is not proportional to intensity above 400 W/m².","Correct: 56 mA means 800 W/m² (Table 2); at 45 cm the intensity is 800 × (30/45)² ≈ 356 W/m², and Table 2\'s steady 8 mA per 100 W/m² up to 400 W/m² gives about 28 mA.","Scales the current by 30/45 = 2/3, as if intensity fell in proportion to distance. Table 1 shows it falls with the square of the distance."]],"science-0141":["Find each lamp\'s intensity at the cell, add them, then use Table 2. Lamp P at 30 cm gives 400 W/m² (Table 1). Lamp Q gives 800 W/m² at 30 cm (56 mA in Study 3 matches 800 W/m² in Table 2), and twice as far it gives 1/4 as much, 200 W/m². Together that\'s 600 W/m², and Table 2 shows 45 mA at 600 W/m². The tempting 48 mA adds the currents each lamp would give alone (32 + 16). That only works if the current is proportional to intensity, and Table 2 shows it isn\'t above 400 W/m²: 600 W/m² gives 45 mA, not 48 mA.",["Averages the two currents (32 mA and 16 mA). The intensities add; the currents are not averaged.","Correct: Lamp P gives 400 W/m² at 30 cm and Lamp Q gives 200 W/m² at 60 cm, so the cell gets 600 W/m², which Table 2 pairs with 45 mA.","Adds the two currents (32 mA + 16 mA). The passage says the intensities add, and Table 2 shows the current at 600 W/m² is 45 mA, less than 48 mA.","Assumes Lamp Q\'s intensity halves when the distance doubles (800 to 400 W/m²), giving 800 W/m² in all. Table 1 shows it drops to 1/4."]],"science-0142":["The same current means the same intensity, 100 W/m² (Lamp P at 60 cm, Table 1). Next, compare the lamps. At 30 cm, Lamp Q gives 800 W/m² (56 mA in Table 2) and Lamp P gives 400 W/m², so Lamp Q is twice as bright at any distance. Table 1 shows that intensity times distance squared stays the same (900 × 20² = 400 × 30² = 360,000), so for Lamp Q it\'s 800 × 30² = 720,000. Setting 100 × d² = 720,000 gives d² = 7,200, so d ≈ 85 cm (that\'s 60√2). The trap 79 cm compares currents instead of intensities (56 mA is only 1.75 times 32 mA, because the cell\'s current grows slowly at high intensity).",["Doubles the distance because Lamp Q gives twice the intensity. Intensity falls with the square of the distance, so the distance grows only by √2.","Correct: 60 cm from Lamp P the cell gets 100 W/m². Lamp Q gives twice Lamp P\'s intensity at any distance (800 versus 400 W/m² at 30 cm), so it gives 100 W/m² at 60 × √2 ≈ 85 cm.","Treats Lamp Q as 56/32 = 1.75 times as bright by comparing currents, but the current is not proportional to intensity above 400 W/m². Comparing intensities gives a factor of 2.","Divides by √2 instead of multiplying. A brighter lamp has to be farther away, not closer, to give the same intensity."]],"science-0143":["Test each explanation against each trial. Student 1 (evaporation) predicts the 70°C cup wins only when the cups are uncovered, so Trial 2 (lids, 70°C first) and Trial 3 (uncovered, 30°C first) both contradict it. Student 2 (dissolved air) predicts the 70°C cup wins with tap water no matter the surface, so Trial 3 contradicts it; and in Trial 4 both cups had been boiled, so neither had an air advantage, yet the 70°C cup still won. Student 3 (melted frost) predicts the 70°C cup wins only on a frosty shelf, which matches all four trials. Student 4 says either evaporation or melted frost alone is enough, so it predicts the 70°C cup wins in Trial 3, where the cups were uncovered. It didn\'t, so only Student 3 fits all four.",["Correct: Student 3\'s explanation depends only on the frosty shelf, and the 70°C cup froze first in exactly the three trials with a frosty shelf.","Student 2\'s explanation fails Trial 3 (tap water, yet the 30°C cup froze first) and Trial 4 (both cups had lost their air, yet the 70°C cup still froze first).","Student 4 says evaporation alone is enough, but in Trial 3 the cups were uncovered and the 30°C cup froze first.","Student 1\'s explanation fails Trial 2 (lids stopped evaporation, yet the 70°C cup froze first), and Student 4\'s fails Trial 3."]],"science-0144":["At 75 mL and 27°C, Table 1 gives 80 kPa, so the pressure must rise by a factor of 100/80 = 1.25. The air at 75 mL is the 60 mL air spread into 1.25 times the space, so at any temperature its pressure is 0.8 times what Table 2 shows. For 100 kPa at 75 mL, Table 2\'s air would need 100 ÷ 0.8 = 125 kPa. Table 2 rises 9 kPa per 27°C, so 125 kPa is 7 kPa above 118 kPa (81°C): 81 + (7/9)(27) = 81 + 21 = 102°C. (Another way: Table 2\'s pressures point back to 0 kPa near −273°C, so pressure is proportional to temperature + 273. Then 300 × 1.25 = 375, and 375 − 273 = 102°C.) The trap at 87°C borrows Table 2\'s 9 kPa per 27°C directly: that rate is for the 60 mL air, and the 75 mL air rises only 0.8 as fast.",["Table 2 shows 100 kPa at 27°C, but that was at 60 mL. At 75 mL and 27°C, the pressure is only 80 kPa (Table 1).","Multiplies 27°C by 100/80 = 1.25, treating pressure as proportional to the Celsius temperature. Table 2 shows it isn\'t: doubling the temperature from 27°C to 54°C raised the pressure only from 100 kPa to 109 kPa.","Uses Table 2\'s rise of 9 kPa per 27°C to add 20 kPa. That rise was measured at 60 mL. At 75 mL the pressure is only 0.8 times as large at every temperature, so it rises only about 7 kPa per 27°C.","Correct: at 75 mL the pressure is 80/100 = 0.8 times the 60 mL pressure at any temperature, so 100 kPa at 75 mL matches 125 kPa at 60 mL. In Table 2, 125 kPa is 7/9 of the way from 81°C (118 kPa) to 108°C (127 kPa), about 102°C."]],"science-0145":["Compare the X column with the no-inhibitor column. The X rates (11, 20, 33, 50, 67) are the no-inhibitor rates (20, 33, 50, 67, 80) moved down one row, and each row doubles the concentration of S. So with X present, the enzyme works as fast as it does without inhibitor at half the concentration: X at 2 mM matches no inhibitor at 1 mM, and X at 16 mM matches no inhibitor at 8 mM. With no inhibitor, the rate is 80 μmol/min at 16 mM, so with X it should be 80 μmol/min at 2 × 16 = 32 mM. The trap is a straight-line extension of X\'s last two rows (about 22 mM): each doubling of S adds less than the one before. Y is different: its rates are half the no-inhibitor rates at every concentration, so adding S never makes up for Y.",["This is where the rate is 80 μmol/min with no inhibitor. With X present, the rate at 16 mM is only 67 μmol/min.","Extends X\'s last two rates in a straight line (17 μmol/min more per 8 mM). The rates rise more slowly as S increases, so a straight line reaches 80 μmol/min too soon.","Correct: with X, each rate equals the no-inhibitor rate at half the concentration (X at 8 mM gives 50, as no inhibitor does at 4 mM). With no inhibitor the rate is 80 at 16 mM, so with X it is 80 at 32 mM.","This fits Y, whose rates are half the no-inhibitor rates at every concentration. X\'s rates keep catching up: they repeat the no-inhibitor rates at twice the concentration."]],"science-0146":["First find what Y does. In every row, the rate with Y is half the no-inhibitor rate (10 of 20, 25 of 50, 40 of 80), so under this model Y stops half of the enzyme molecules. The model says Y does the same with X present: half the enzyme molecules stop, and the other half work just as they would with X alone. With X alone at 16 mM, the rate is 67 μmol/min, so with both inhibitors it is about ½ × 67 ≈ 33 μmol/min. (The same answer another way: X makes 16 mM act like 8 mM, and Y at 8 mM gives 33.) The trap is subtracting both effects from 80 (27 μmol/min). Each drop was measured by itself; once Y has stopped half the enzyme, it removes half of whatever rate X leaves, not a fixed 40.",["This is the rate with Y alone at 16 mM. Adding X lowers the rate further.","Correct: Y\'s rates are half the no-inhibitor rates in every row, so Y stops half of the enzyme molecules. The working half, with X present, gives half of X\'s rate at 16 mM: ½ × 67 ≈ 33 μmol/min.","Subtracts both drops from 80: X lowers the 16 mM rate by 13 and Y by 40, and 80 − 13 − 40 = 27. Y stops a fraction of the enzyme, so it cuts X\'s rate in half rather than by a fixed 40.","Halves the rate for Y and then halves it again for X. X doesn\'t halve the rate; at 16 mM it lowers the rate only from 80 to 67 μmol/min."]],"science-0147":["Track actual numbers of atoms. Suppose Q formed with 100 parent atoms and 100 daughter atoms (D/P = 1). If P parent atoms remain today, then 100 − P have decayed, each into one daughter, so there are 100 + (100 − P) = 200 − P daughters. D/P = 4 means 200 − P = 4P, so 5P = 200 and P = 40: 40% of the parent atoms remain (check: 160 daughters and 40 parents gives 4). Table 1 has 50% at 1.5 b.y. and 25% at 3.0 b.y., so 40% falls between them, at about 2.0 b.y. Traps: subtracting the starting ratio (4 − 1 = 3) and reading 3 as if Q were like Mineral L (3.0 b.y.), and ignoring the starting daughters altogether (1 parent in 5 left, about 3.5 b.y.). The starting daughters are a fixed number, while the parent atoms they are compared with keep shrinking.",["At 1.5 b.y., half the parent atoms remain, so Q would hold 100 + 50 = 150 daughters for every 50 parents, a D/P of 3. A ratio of 4 means more time has passed.","Correct: if Q started with 100 parent and 100 daughter atoms, then when P parent atoms remain there are 200 − P daughters. D/P = 4 means 200 − P = 4P, so P = 40: 40% remain, between Table 1\'s 50% (1.5 b.y.) and 25% (3.0 b.y.), at about 2.0 b.y.","Subtracts Q\'s starting ratio (4 − 1 = 3) and then dates Q like Mineral L, at 3.0 b.y. But Q\'s starting daughters are compared with fewer and fewer parent atoms: at 3.0 b.y. Q would have (100 + 75)/25 = 7 daughters per parent, not 4.","Ignores the daughters Q started with. If all 4 daughters per parent came from decay, 1 parent in 5 (20%) would remain, about 3.5 b.y., but some of Q\'s daughters were there from the start."]],"science-0148":["Start with 100 parent atoms and 200 daughter atoms (D/P = 2). Table 1 shows that after 3.0 b.y., 25% of the parent atoms remain: 25 atoms. The other 75 have decayed into daughters, so there are 200 + 75 = 275 daughters. D/P = 275/25 = 11. Traps: adding ratios (2 + 3 = 5), dividing only the starting daughters by the remaining parents (200/25 = 8), and forgetting to divide by the shrunken number of parents (2.75). Both kinds of daughters, the starting ones and the new ones, are compared with the same small number of parent atoms.",["This is the number of daughter atoms per original parent atom (2 + 0.75). Only 25% of the parent atoms remain, so the ratio is 2.75 ÷ 0.25.","Adds Mineral L\'s ratio (3, the D/P of a mineral that formed with no daughters 3.0 b.y. ago) to R\'s starting ratio of 2. Ratios don\'t add: R\'s starting daughters are now compared with far fewer parent atoms.","Divides R\'s starting daughters by the remaining parent atoms (2 ÷ 0.25) but leaves out the new daughters made by decay.","Correct: for every 100 original parent atoms, R started with 200 daughters. After 3.0 b.y., 25 parents remain and 75 have become daughters, so D/P = (200 + 75)/25 = 11."]],"science-0149":["Use Trial 1 to compare M with water. There, 100 g of M cooled 72°C (100°C to 28°C) while 100 g of water warmed 8°C (20°C to 28°C). The heat lost equals the heat gained, so 100 × c_M × 72 = 100 × c_water × 8, and M\'s specific heat is 8/72 = 1/9 of water\'s. Now use 300 g of M and call the final temperature T. Heat lost by the metal: 300 × (c_water/9) × (100 − T). Heat gained by the water: 100 × c_water × (T − 20). Setting them equal gives (100 − T)/3 = T − 20, so 100 − T = 3T − 60 and T = 40°C. Check: the metal cools 60°C and the water warms 20°C, and 300 × (1/9) × 60 = 2,000 = 100 × 20. The trap is tripling the 8°C rise (44°C): as the water warms, the metal stops cooling sooner, so the rise is less than three times as big.",["Flips the ratio, so that the water warms 3 times as much as the metal cools. Here the water has the larger heat capacity (mass × specific heat), so the water changes temperature less.","Triples Trial 1\'s 8°C rise. That would be right only if all 300 g of metal still cooled to 28°C, but the warmer water stops the metal sooner, so each gram gives up less heat.","Correct: in Trial 1, M cooled 72°C while the water warmed 8°C, so 100 g of M takes 1/9 as much heat per degree as 100 g of water. Then 300 g of M takes 1/3 as much, so the metal cools 3 times as far as the water warms: 100 − T = 3(T − 20), and T = 40°C.","This is Trial 1\'s result for 100 g of M. Three times as much hot metal gives up more heat, so the water ends up warmer."]],"science-0150":["For each trial, heat lost by the metal = heat gained by the water: (metal mass) × c_metal × (metal\'s drop) = 100 × c_water × (water\'s rise). So c_metal/c_water = (100 × rise)/(mass × drop). Trial 1 (M): (100 × 8)/(100 × 72) = 1/9. Trial 2 (N): (100 × 16)/(100 × 64) = 1/4. Trial 3 (P): (100 × 10)/(50 × 70) = 1,000/3,500 = 2/7. Since 2/7 ≈ 0.29 is more than 1/4 = 0.25, P has the greatest specific heat. The trap is N: it gave the warmest water, but only because Trial 2 used twice as much metal as Trial 3. Note that you also can\'t just compare the water\'s rise: the metal\'s drop changes from trial to trial as well.",["Correct: specific heat compared with water\'s = (100 g × the water\'s rise) ÷ (metal mass × the metal\'s drop). M: 800/7,200 = 1/9. N: 1,600/6,400 = 1/4. P: 1,000/3,500 = 2/7, the largest.","N warmed the water the most (to 36°C), but Trial 3 used only 50 g of P. Per gram, P carries more heat: 2/7 of water\'s specific heat, compared with 1/4 for N.","M warmed the water the least, so it has the smallest specific heat of the three: 1/9 of water\'s.","The passage\'s equation includes the mass, so a different mass can be accounted for: P\'s specific heat works out to 2/7 of water\'s, more than N\'s 1/4."]],"science-0151":["Read each culture\'s row. Culture A (Table 1, 10°C): lag 24 h, doubling 12 h. Culture B (Table 2, 3%): lag 30 h, doubling 9 h. Both stay at 1,000 until A starts at 24 h, so A pulls ahead first. Now list the doubling times. A reaches 2,000 at 36 h, 4,000 at 48 h, and 8,000 at 60 h. B reaches 2,000 at 39 h, 4,000 at 48 h, and 8,000 at 57 h. The cultures are equal at 48 h. After that B\'s faster doubling keeps it ahead: at 72 h, A has 16,000 while B has about 25,000. So B leads only after 48 hours. The trap is judging by the lag time alone (\\"at no time\\"), or calling B ahead as soon as it first doubles, at 39 h, without checking where A is.",["B\'s longer lag puts it behind at first, but its shorter doubling time (9 h versus 12 h) lets it catch up: both reach 4,000 at 48 h, and B leads after that.","At 39 h, B has just doubled to 2,000, but A doubled to 2,000 at 36 h and has about 2,400 by 39 h, so A is still ahead.","Correct: A grows from 24 h, doubling every 12 h (2,000 at 36 h, 4,000 at 48 h, 8,000 at 60 h); B grows from 30 h, doubling every 9 h (2,000 at 39 h, 4,000 at 48 h, 8,000 at 57 h). They tie at 48 h, and B leads from then on.","Reversed: between 30 h and 48 h, A is ahead, since it started growing 6 hours earlier. B leads only after 48 h."]],"science-0152":["Find 15°C in the temperature column and read across: the average distance is 31 cm. The other choices are real values from the table, but from the 10°C, 30°C, and 20°C rows.",["Wrong row: 18 cm is the distance at 10°C.","Correct: the 15°C row shows an average distance of 31 cm.","Wrong row: 39 cm is the distance at 30°C.","Wrong row: 44 cm is the distance at 20°C."]],"science-0153":["Read the distances in order of temperature: 18, 31, 44, 52, then 39 cm. They go up through 25°C and then drop at 30°C. So the distance increased and then decreased. \\"Increased only\\" is the trap for anyone who stops reading before the last row.",["Correct: the distance rose from 18 cm to 52 cm at 25°C, then fell to 39 cm at 30°C.","Misses the last row: the distance dropped from 52 cm to 39 cm between 25°C and 30°C.","Reversed: from 10°C to 25°C, the distance went up, not down.","The distance changed a lot, from 18 cm to as much as 52 cm."]],"science-0154":["Look down the distance column for the largest value: 52 cm. Its temperature is 25°C. The trap is 30°C: it is the highest temperature tested, but the distance there (39 cm) is lower than at 20°C or 25°C.",["At 15°C the snails crawled 31 cm, less than at 20°C or 25°C.","At 20°C the snails crawled 44 cm, less than the 52 cm at 25°C.","Correct: 52 cm, at 25°C, is the largest distance in the table.","Highest temperature, not the longest distance: at 30°C the snails crawled only 39 cm."]],"science-0155":["The dependent variable is what the scientist measures to see the effect of the change. Here the student changed the air temperature (the independent variable) and measured how far the snails crawled (the dependent variable). The number of snails (6) and the trial length (10 minutes) stayed the same in every trial, so they were held constant.",["This is the independent variable: the student set the temperature for each trial.","This was held constant: the same 6 snails were used in every trial.","This was held constant: every trial lasted 10 minutes.","Correct: the distance was what the student measured to see how temperature affected it."]],"science-0156":["In Table 1, find the 15 cm row and read across: 73 mL of water remained. Be careful not to read Table 2 instead. Its Medium row (70 mL) sits in the same spot on a different table.",["Wrong row: 52 mL remained in the 20 cm dish.","Wrong table: 70 mL is the Medium fan setting in Table 2.","Correct: Table 1 shows 73 mL remaining in the 15 cm dish.","Wrong row: 88 mL remained in the 10 cm dish."]],"science-0157":["Each dish started with 100 mL. With the fan on High, 58 mL remained, so 100 − 58 = 42 mL evaporated. The biggest trap is 58 mL, which is the amount left in the dish, not the amount that evaporated. The other two choices do the right subtraction with a value from the wrong row or the wrong table.",["Wrong row: 100 − 70 = 30 mL evaporated with the fan on Medium, not High.","Correct: 100 mL − 58 mL remaining = 42 mL evaporated.","Wrong experiment: 100 − 52 = 48 mL evaporated from the 20 cm dish in Experiment 1.","This is the water remaining, not the water that evaporated."]],"science-0158":["In Experiment 2, every dish was 10 cm wide, and the fan setting changed (Off, Low, Medium, High). In Experiment 1, no fan was used, and the dish diameter changed instead. So the fan setting is the factor changed only in Experiment 2. The starting volume (100 mL) and the room temperature (22°C) were the same in every trial.",["Correct: the fan setting went from Off to High in Experiment 2; no fan was used in Experiment 1.","Reversed: the diameter changed in Experiment 1; every dish in Experiment 2 was 10 cm wide.","Held constant: every trial in both experiments started with 100 mL of water.","Held constant: the room was kept at 22°C for every trial."]],"science-0300":["Check what each student predicts about night. Student 1: the extra evaporation comes from sunlight heating the dark mud, so it \\"happens almost entirely during daylight.\\" Student 3: willows release water \\"mainly in daylight\\" and \\"very little at night.\\" Student 2: seepage depends on water depth, not sunlight, so it is steady day and night. A night loss equal to the day loss fits only Student 2, and weakens Students 1 and 3. The common slip is to remember the daylight point for the plants but forget that Student 1 makes it too.",["Reversed: Student 2 says seepage goes on \\"at a nearly steady rate day and night,\\" so this finding fits Student 2.","Misses Student 1, who also says the extra loss \\"happens almost entirely during daylight\\" because sunlight heats the shallow edges.","Correct: Students 1 and 3 both tie the extra loss to daylight (sun-heated water and leaf pores open in daylight), so an equal loss at night weakens both.","Student 2 predicts the same loss day and night, so the finding supports Student 2 instead of weakening it."]],"science-0301":["Each choice pairs a willow result with a soil result. For Student 3 to be supported, cutting back the willows must reduce the extra loss, which leaves the first two choices. For Student 2 to be weakened, the soil below the pond must not let water seep out, as dense clay would not. Only the second choice does both. Loose sand is what Student 2 expects, so the first choice supports both students.",["Half right: the willow result supports Student 3, but loose sand below the pond fits Student 2\'s seepage idea instead of weakening it.","Correct: a smaller loss without the willows supports Student 3, and dense clay instead of sand makes Student 2\'s seepage unlikely.","Reversed: this supports Student 2 (sand that water seeps through) and weakens Student 3 (removing the willows changed nothing).","Weakens both: removing the willows didn\'t help, and dense clay makes seepage unlikely."]],"science-0302":["The pan is watertight and has no plants, so its only way to lose water is evaporation. Student 1 says the pan reads low because it isn\'t heated like the pond\'s dark, muddy edges. Put it in that mud in full sun and it loses 9 mm per day, exactly the pond\'s drop. So evaporation from sun-heated water can account for all of the pond\'s loss, as Student 1 claims, with no need for seepage (Student 2) or willows (Student 3). Students 1 and 3 do agree the water leaves as vapor, but the pan result shows vapor from evaporation, not from leaves.",["Correct: a watertight pan with no plants can lose water only by evaporation, so if heating it like the pond\'s edges makes it lose all 9 mm, evaporation alone can explain the pond\'s loss.","The pan is watertight, so it can\'t lose water by seepage at all. Its matching the pond suggests seepage isn\'t needed.","The pan holds no willows, so its 9 mm loss happened without any plants; that makes the willows unnecessary.","Student 3\'s vapor comes from plants, and the pan has none. The result shows plain evaporation is enough, which is Student 1\'s claim only."]],"science-0303":["To see whether one relationship (diameter versus cooling time) depends on a second variable (airflow speed), you need the same diameter series at another airflow speed. Then you can compare the two patterns. Only the second choice does that. A single extra trial can\'t show a pattern, repeating Experiment 2 keeps the airflow the same, and changing the starting temperature changes the wrong variable.",["This adds one more airflow speed for a single diameter, so it can\'t show how the diameter pattern changes with airflow.","This changes the starting temperature, not the airflow speed, so it tests a different question.","Repeating trials checks that the 2 m/s results are reliable but never changes airflow, so it can\'t show whether the pattern depends on it.","Correct: repeating the whole diameter series at a different airflow speed lets the students compare the diameter pattern at 0 m/s with the one at 2 m/s."]],"science-0304":["Start from Table 1: a 2 cm cylinder at 1 m/s takes 24 min. Directly proportional means that multiplying the diameter by some factor multiplies the time by the same factor. Going from 2 cm to 3 cm multiplies the diameter by 3/2, so the time is 24 × 3/2 = 36 min. The trap is to borrow Table 2\'s step of 9 min per centimeter and get 24 + 9 = 33 min. At 2 m/s that step works because the time is 9 min per cm of diameter, but at 1 m/s the time per centimeter is 24/2 = 12 min. The other choices read a single table and miss one of the two changes.",["This is the time for a 2 cm cylinder at 1 m/s (Table 1). It leaves out the larger diameter.","This is the time for a 3 cm cylinder at 2 m/s (Table 2), the wrong airflow speed.","Adds 9 min for the extra centimeter, the step in Table 2. That step is 9 min only at 2 m/s; proportional means multiplying the 1 m/s time by 3/2.","Correct: a 2 cm cylinder takes 24 min at 1 m/s, and proportional to diameter means a 3 cm cylinder takes 24 × 3/2 = 36 min."]],"science-0305":["Two steps. First, airflow: in Table 1, a 2 cm cylinder takes 18 min at 2 m/s and 13 min at 4 m/s, so at 3 m/s it should take between 13 and 18 min. Second, diameter: 4 cm is twice 2 cm, and with time proportional to diameter, the time doubles, giving between 26 and 36 min. Check the ends: 36 min is the Table 2 time for 4 cm at 2 m/s, and more airflow should make it shorter. Traps: stopping after the first step, flipping the diameter effect, or the airflow effect.",["Treats a wider cylinder as cooling faster. Table 2 shows the opposite: doubling the diameter doubles the time.","This is the range for a 2 cm cylinder at 3 m/s. A 4 cm cylinder takes twice as long.","Correct: at 3 m/s a 2 cm cylinder takes between 13 min (4 m/s) and 18 min (2 m/s), and doubling the diameter doubles that to between 26 and 36 min.","Reads the airflow trend backward: 3 m/s is faster than 2 m/s, so the time must be less than the 36 min a 4 cm cylinder takes at 2 m/s."]],"science-0306":["Find who relies on volcanoes. Scientist 3 says a few ppb of phosphine is present and that volcanic compounds could make it, so a limit of 0.1 ppb, far below a few ppb, undercuts Scientist 3. Scientist 1 claims the opposite about volcanoes (they could make only a tiny fraction), so the result fits Scientist 1. Scientist 2 says the signal is SO₂, so how much phosphine volcanoes can make doesn\'t touch that view. The trap is grouping Scientists 1 and 3 because both say phosphine is there; the result is about where phosphine comes from, not whether it is there.",["Scientist 2 doesn\'t rely on volcanoes at all; the signal is SO₂, so the amount volcanoes can make doesn\'t matter to that view.","Correct: Scientist 3 says volcanoes could make the few ppb of phosphine present. If they can make at most 0.1 ppb, that explanation fails.","Scientist 1 already says non-living processes like volcanoes make \\"only a tiny fraction\\" of the phosphine, so this result supports Scientist 1.","Scientist 2\'s argument is about SO₂ and never depends on what volcanoes can do."]],"science-0307":["Scientists 2 and 3 disagree about what the signal is: SO₂ for Scientist 2, phosphine for Scientist 3. But both reject the 20 ppb figure. Scientist 2 says there may be \\"little or no phosphine at all,\\" and Scientist 3 says the true amount is \\"only a few ppb.\\" Either way, the amount is less than 20 ppb. Presence of phosphine is Scientist 3\'s claim only, SO₂ is Scientist 2\'s only, and the claim that no known non-living process works is Scientist 1\'s; the other two each name one.",["Only Scientist 3 says this; Scientist 2 says there may be \\"little or no phosphine at all.\\"","Only Scientist 2 says this; Scientist 3 says the signal is phosphine.","Correct: Scientist 2 says there may be little or no phosphine, and Scientist 3 says only a few ppb. Both put the amount below 20 ppb.","That is Scientist 1\'s view. Scientist 2 credits SO₂, and Scientist 3 credits volcanoes, both non-living."]],"science-0170":["An independent variable is something the experimenters change on purpose. Here they changed two things: the chamber temperature and whether the fan was on. Only the fan appears in the choices. The mass of water lost is what they measured (the dependent variable), and the dish size and the 24 h time were kept the same in every trial so they wouldn\'t affect the results.",["Correct: the students chose whether each dish had the fan on or off, and Table 1 shows results for both settings.","This is the dependent variable: it was measured to see the effect of temperature and the fan.","Held constant: \\"All the dishes were the same size.\\"","Held constant: every dish sat in the chamber for 24 h."]],"science-0171":["Find the 20°C row: 6 g with no fan and 13 g with the fan on. \\"How many more\\" means subtract: 13 − 6 = 7 g. The traps are the two numbers in the row by themselves and their sum.",["Copied value: 6 g is the water lost with no fan at 20°C, not the difference.","Correct: 13 g − 6 g = 7 g.","Copied value: 13 g is the water lost with the fan on at 20°C, not the difference.","Wrong operation: 13 g + 6 g = 19 g is the total of the two dishes, not how many more."]],"science-0172":["In the no-fan column, water loss goes up steadily with temperature. 27°C falls between the 25°C row (9 g) and the 30°C row (13 g), so the loss should be between 9 g and 13 g. Watch the column: the fan-on values for the same temperatures are 18 g and 25 g.",["Too low: that is less than the 20°C value, but water loss rises with temperature.","Wrong interval: 6 g to 9 g covers 20°C to 25°C, and 27°C is above 25°C.","Correct: 27°C is between 25°C (9 g) and 30°C (13 g) in the no-fan column.","Wrong column: 18 g to 25 g is the range for 25°C to 30°C with the fan on."]],"science-0173":["Compare the two columns row by row: 9 vs. 4, 13 vs. 6, 18 vs. 9, 25 vs. 13. Each fan-on value is about double the no-fan value (exactly double at 25°C). So the fan made the dish lose about 2 times as much water. One-half is the same comparison turned upside down.",["Too big: even the largest gap, 9 g vs. 4 g at 15°C, is only about 2.25 times.","Correct: 9/4 ≈ 2.3, 13/6 ≈ 2.2, 18/9 = 2, and 25/13 ≈ 1.9, all close to 2.","Ignores the fan\'s effect: at every temperature, the fan-on dish lost far more water.","Reversed: this is the no-fan loss compared with the fan-on loss, the ratio upside down."]],"science-0174":["Add the damp-half column of Table 1: 8 + 9 + 7 = 24. The dry half had 6 in all, the damp half averaged 8 per trial, and 30 is every pill bug in the experiment, on both halves.",["Wrong half: 2 + 1 + 3 = 6 is the total on the dry half.","Average, not total: 24 ÷ 3 = 8 is the damp-half count per trial (and also Trial 1\'s count).","Correct: 8 + 9 + 7 = 24.","Total of all pill bugs used: 3 trials × 10 = 30, counting both halves."]],"science-0175":["Experiment 1 tests moisture: damp towel on one half, dry on the other. If one half were lit and the other dark, a difference in counts could come from light instead of moisture. Covering both halves keeps light the same on both sides, so moisture is the only difference. Testing light is what Experiment 2 does, by covering just one half.",["That is the purpose of Experiment 2, where only one half is covered. In Experiment 1, both halves were dark, so light could not be tested.","Not stated, and covering both halves would not help test moisture, which is the point of Experiment 1.","Only one half had damp towel, and the dry half was covered too, so the cover was not about keeping the towel damp.","Correct: with both halves dark, any difference in where the pill bugs went came from damp vs. dry, not from light."]],"science-0176":["Add each column. Experiment 1, damp half: 8 + 9 + 7 = 24 of 30. Experiment 2, dark half: 6 + 5 + 6 = 17 of 30. So yes, more pill bugs went to the damp half (24) than to the dark half (17). Choice B adds the light-half column (13) by mistake. Both experiments did show a preference, but that doesn\'t answer which drew more.",["Correct: damp half 8 + 9 + 7 = 24; dark half 6 + 5 + 6 = 17.","Wrong column: 4 + 5 + 4 = 13 is the light half in Experiment 2, not the dark half.","Wrong comparison: it sets the dark half of Experiment 2 against the dry half of Experiment 1, and 17 is still less than 24.","True but beside the point: both experiments showed a preference, but the question asks which one drew more pill bugs."]],"science-0177":["First find each pond\'s N = M × C ÷ R: W = 40 × 50 ÷ 10 = 200; X = 60 × 80 ÷ 12 = 400; Y = 30 × 90 ÷ 6 = 450; Z = 100 × 40 ÷ 5 = 800. Then divide by area to get snails per m²: W 1.0, X 1.0, Y 1.8, Z 1.6. Pond Y is the most crowded, so a 50 m² section there would hold about 1.8 × 50 = 90 snails. Z has the most snails in all, but it is also the biggest pond. Don\'t confuse the largest population with the most crowded one.",["W\'s estimate is 200 snails in 200 m², or 1.0 per m² (50 in 50 m²), but Y and Z are more crowded.","Correct: N for Y = 30 × 90 ÷ 6 = 450 snails; 450 ÷ 250 m² = 1.8 per m²; 1.8 × 50 = 90, the most of any pond.","Z\'s estimate is right for Z (800 ÷ 500 = 1.6 per m², so 80 in 50 m²), but Y is more crowded at 1.8 per m².","Largest population, not most crowded: 800 is Z\'s whole estimate, and it is spread over the biggest pond."]],"science-0178":["Losing a dot doesn\'t change how many snails were caught (C) or how many were marked on Day 1 (M). It only means some marked snails get counted as unmarked, so R comes out too small. R is in the denominator of N = M × C ÷ R, so dividing by a smaller number gives a bigger N: the estimate would be too high. For example, if R had been counted as 10 instead of 12 in Pond X, N would be 60 × 80 ÷ 10 = 480 instead of 400. The \\"too low\\" choice catches students who miss that R is in the denominator.",["Right cause, wrong direction: R is in the denominator, so a smaller R makes N larger, not smaller.","C is the total number caught in the second sample. Losing paint changes which snails look marked, not how many were caught.","C doesn\'t change: the same snails were caught whether or not their dots wore off.","Correct: snails that lost their dots weren\'t counted in R, and since N = M × C ÷ R, a smaller R gives a larger N."]],"science-0179":["Use the equation with each row of Table 2 (M = 40, C = 50): 1 h gives 2,000 ÷ 25 = 80, 6 h gives 2,000 ÷ 16 = 125, and 24 h and 48 h both give 200. Three hours is between 1 h and 6 h, so R would be between 25 and 16 and the estimate between 80 and 125. Why so low? The passage says the equation works only if the marked snails have mixed evenly with the rest. Right after release they are still bunched at the spot where the sample is taken, so too many are recaught, R is too big, and N comes out too small. The estimate settles at 200 once they have spread out.",["Correct: at 1 h, N = 40 × 50 ÷ 25 = 80; at 6 h, N = 40 × 50 ÷ 16 = 125; 3 h falls between them, and the low estimates come from marked snails still crowded near the release spot.","Right range, wrong reason: the estimates were still rising at 3 h, so the marked snails had not finished spreading. They level off only by 24 h.","Right reason, wrong range: 125 to 200 covers 6 h to 24 h, and 3 h is earlier than 6 h.","Ignores Table 2: the estimate rose from 80 at 1 h to 200 by 24 h, so the timing clearly mattered."]],"science-0320":["The table gives percents, but the question asks for seeds per dish. Each dish held 50 seeds. At 80°C, 58% germinated: 0.58 × 50 = 29 seeds. Without heating (25°C), 6% germinated: 0.06 × 50 = 3 seeds. The difference is 29 − 3 = 26 seeds per dish. The traps: stopping at 29, using the 52 percentage points as if each dish held 100 seeds, or multiplying by the 4 dishes (104) when the question asks per dish.",["Correct: 58% of 50 seeds is 29 and 6% of 50 is 3, so 29 − 3 = 26 more seeds per dish.","Intermediate result: 29 is the number that germinated per dish at 80°C, before subtracting the 3 that germinated without heating.","Unit slip: 58 − 6 = 52 is the difference in percentage points, not in seeds. Each dish held 50 seeds, not 100.","Wrong scope: 104 is the extra seeds across all 4 dishes together, not per dish."]],"science-0321":["Look at what each cue does to Species K by itself. In Table 2, heating at 80°C gave 6%, almost the same as the 4% for unheated seeds, so 80°C neither helps nor harms these seeds much. In Table 1, 10% smoke water raised germination to 46%. Heat at 80°C has almost no effect, so seeds that get both should behave about like seeds that get only the smoke water: about 46%. Adding 46% + 6% = 52% is the trap. The 6% is mostly seeds that would have germinated anyway, so adding it counts them twice.",["Double counting: adding 46% and 6% counts the baseline germination (about 4-6%) twice; heating at 80°C added almost nothing for Species K.","Correct: heating at 80°C barely changed Species K\'s germination (4% to 6%), so the seeds should respond to 10% smoke water as in Experiment 1, about 46%.","Averaging the two results (46% and 6%) treats the heat as if it cancels half of the smoke effect, but nothing in the data shows heat at 80°C harming Species K.","This is Species K after heating alone. It ignores the 10% smoke water, which raised Species K\'s germination to 46% in Experiment 1."]],"science-0322":["Combine the soil facts with both tables. At 1 cm, seeds are heated to about 120°C, which gave Species K 0% and Species M 12%. At 3 cm, seeds reach about 80°C and still get smoke chemicals (they reach down to about 5 cm, at about 10% strength). Species K doesn\'t respond to 80°C but does respond to 10% smoke water, so it should reach about 46%. Species M doesn\'t respond to smoke (Table 1 stays at 5-8%), but 80°C gave it 58%. At 6 cm, 40°C would give Species M somewhere between 6% and 22%. So Species M at 3 cm is highest. The traps: picking Species K at 3 cm because it gets both cues, or picking 1 cm because it is hottest, even though 120°C hurts both species.",["Species K seeds at 1 cm get smoke chemicals but are heated to about 120°C, and no Species K seeds germinated after 120°C in Table 2.","Close, but lower: at 3 cm Species K seeds reach about 80°C (little effect) and get about 10% smoke strength, so about 46% is expected, less than Species M\'s 58%.","Correct: at 3 cm the soil reaches about 80°C, which gave Species M its highest germination, 58%; smoke chemicals don\'t change Species M\'s germination much.","At 6 cm the soil reaches only about 40°C, between the 25°C (6%) and 60°C (22%) results, so Species M would germinate far less than at 3 cm."]],"science-0323":["To tell a dead seed from a dormant one, give the same seeds a cue that is known to make living seeds germinate, and see whether they respond. For Species K, that cue is 10% smoke water (46% in Table 1). If the rinsed seeds then germinate at a good rate, the 100% smoke water had only kept them dormant; if almost none do, it had most likely killed them. The heat test is the trap: heat is a good cue for Species M, but Species K barely responds to it, so a poor result would mean nothing. More dishes or a longer soak never test the same seeds again.",["More dishes would give a more reliable average for the same trial, but every seed that didn\'t germinate could still be either dead or dormant.","A longer soak in full-strength smoke water changes the treatment; it still can\'t tell a dead seed from a dormant one.","Species K barely responds to 80°C (6% in Table 2), so even living, dormant seeds would mostly fail to germinate, and the test couldn\'t tell the two apart.","Correct: 10% smoke water is the cue that best wakes living Species K seeds (46%). If many of the rinsed seeds then germinate, they were alive; if almost none do, they were most likely killed."]],"science-0324":["The transit depth measures the planet\'s size compared with its star\'s: D ≈ (Rp/Rs)². If two planets have the same depth, they have the same ratio Rp/Rs, so the planet around the bigger star is bigger. From the formula Rp = 109 × Rs × √D, with D the same, Rp is proportional to Rs. Planet X\'s star (1.00) is twice the size of Planet W\'s (0.50), so Planet X is about twice as large: 109 × 1.00 × √0.00034 ≈ 2.0 Earth radii, versus about 1.0 for Planet W. Traps: assuming same depth means same size, or squaring the factor of 2.",["Same depth means the same planet-to-star size ratio, not the same planet size; Planet X\'s star is twice as large.","Correct: with the same D, Rp is proportional to Rs, and Planet X\'s star (1.00 solar radii) is 2 times Planet W\'s (0.50), so Planet X is about 2 times as large (about 2.0 vs. 1.0 Earth radii).","Squaring trap: the depth depends on the square of the size ratio, but Rp itself is proportional to Rs, not Rs².","Reversed: the planet around the larger star must be larger to block the same fraction of light."]],"science-0325":["Use F = L / d² for each planet. Planet W: 0.040 / (0.20)² = 0.040 / 0.040 = 1.0. Planet X: 1.00 / (0.80)² ≈ 1.6. Planet Y: 0.0050 / (0.050)² = 2.0. Planet Z: 0.40 / (0.90)² ≈ 0.49. Only Planet W gets about what Earth gets. Each wrong choice looks Earth-like in one way: Planet X has a Sun-like star, Planet Z is about 1 AU from its star, and Planet Y\'s tiny star seems to cancel its tiny distance. Remember to square the distance.",["Correct: F = 0.040 / 0.20² = 0.040 / 0.040 = 1.0, the same as Earth.","Planet X\'s star is as bright as the Sun, but the planet is closer (0.80 AU), so F = 1.00 / 0.64 ≈ 1.6.","Planet Y\'s star is very dim, but the planet is very close: F = 0.0050 / 0.050² = 0.0050 / 0.0025 = 2.0.","Planet Z is about Earth\'s distance from its star, but the star is dimmer: F = 0.40 / 0.81 ≈ 0.49."]],"science-0326":["Each planet has to pass two tests, so work out both numbers for each. Radii, from Rp = 109 × Rs × √D: W ≈ 109 × 0.50 × 0.0184 ≈ 1.0; X ≈ 2.0; Y ≈ 109 × 0.20 × 0.0548 ≈ 1.2; Z ≈ 109 × 0.80 × 0.0173 ≈ 1.5. Starlight, from F = L / d²: W = 1.0, X ≈ 1.6, Y = 2.0, Z ≈ 0.49. Planet W passes both tests, and so does Planet Z (1.5 is under 1.6, and 0.49 is in the 0.35-1.1 range). X fails both, and Y fails the starlight test. The traps: checking only one condition (which lets Y in), or leaving out Z because its radius is close to the limit.",["Misses Planet Z: its radius is about 1.5 Earth radii (under 1.6) and it gets about 0.49 times Earth\'s starlight, inside the range.","Correct: Planet W (about 1.0 Earth radii, F = 1.0) and Planet Z (about 1.5 Earth radii, F ≈ 0.49) meet both conditions; X is too large and Y gets too much light.","Planet X fails both tests: about 2.0 Earth radii and F ≈ 1.6.","Planet Y is small enough (about 1.2 Earth radii) but gets about 2.0 times Earth\'s starlight, more than 1.1."]],"science-0327":["An Earth-sized planet has Rp = 1 Earth radius, and Planet Y\'s star has a radius of 0.20 × 109 ≈ 21.8 Earth radii. So D ≈ (1/21.8)² ≈ 0.0021, which is 0.21%. Another way: around the Sun, Earth\'s depth would be (1/109)² ≈ 0.000084, or 0.0084%. A star 0.20 times as wide has 0.20² = 0.04 times the area, so the same planet blocks 1/0.04 = 25 times as large a fraction: 0.0084% × 25 = 0.21%. This is one reason small, dim stars are good places to look for Earth-sized planets. Traps: dividing by 0.20 only once (0.042%), or multiplying instead of dividing.",["This multiplies Earth\'s depth around the Sun (0.0084%) by 0.20², but a smaller star makes a planet block a larger fraction of its light, not a smaller one.","This is the depth for an Earth-sized planet crossing the Sun; Planet Y\'s star is only 0.20 times as wide as the Sun.","This divides by 0.20 once instead of by 0.20²: the depth depends on the square of the size ratio.","Correct: D ≈ (Rp/Rs)² = (1 / (109 × 0.20))² = (1/21.8)² ≈ 0.0021, or 0.21%."]],"science-0328":["The independent variable is the one the experimenters change on purpose. In Experiment 1, they dissolved different masses of NaCl: 0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, and 4.0 g. Conductivity is what they measured (the dependent variable). The water volume (100 mL) and temperature (25°C) stayed the same in every trial, so those were held constant.",["Temperature was kept at 25°C for every solution, so it was held constant, not changed.","Every solution used 100 mL of distilled water, so the volume was held constant.","Conductivity is what the meter measured. It is the dependent variable, the result.","Correct: the students chose different masses of NaCl (0 to 4.0 g) on purpose, so this is the variable they changed."]],"science-0329":["A trial with none of the tested substance is a control. Distilled water alone gave 2 µS/cm. Every solution with NaCl gave thousands of µS/cm, so the comparison shows the high conductivity came from the dissolved NaCl, not from the water. Sucrose belongs to Experiment 2, and the water volume was the same in every trial anyway.",["Correct: with no NaCl, the reading (2 µS/cm) shows how well the water alone conducts. That baseline shows how much the NaCl itself raised the conductivity.","The 0 g trial has the lowest conductivity, not the highest, so it can\'t help find the greatest value.","Sucrose was tested in Experiment 2, not in Experiment 1. The 0 g trial contains no sucrose.","Every solution already used 100 mL of water. Leaving out the NaCl doesn\'t keep the volume the same."]],"science-0330":["A solution conducts electricity when it contains ions, charged particles that can move. Salts like NaCl, KCl, and CaCl₂ break apart into ions when they dissolve, so their solutions conducted well (about 16,000 to 17,000 µS/cm). Sucrose dissolves too, but its molecules stay whole and have no charge, so the solution conducted only about as well as distilled water (3 µS/cm vs. 2 µS/cm). The trap choices either claim sucrose forms ions or claim it doesn\'t dissolve.",["If sucrose formed ions, its solution would conduct well, like the salts. Its reading of 3 µS/cm shows it doesn\'t.","Correct: sugar dissolves as neutral molecules. Without ions, the solution conducts about as poorly as pure water.","The passage says each substance was dissolved, and table sugar dissolves easily in water. Dissolving isn\'t the issue; forming ions is.","Wrong reason: moving ions are exactly what carry current in a solution. That is why the salt solutions conduct so well."]],"science-0331":["Use Experiment 2 for the starting point and Experiment 1 for the pattern. KCl at 1.0 g gave 16,900 µS/cm, close to NaCl\'s 17,400. In Experiment 1, doubling NaCl from 1.0 g to 2.0 g raised conductivity from 17,400 to 32,600, a little less than double. If KCl behaves the same way, 2.0 g of KCl should give a little less than 2 × 16,900 = 33,800, so about 32,000 µS/cm. The traps are keeping the 1.0 g value, halving it, or quadrupling it.",["Wrong direction: this halves the 1.0 g value, but adding more salt raised conductivity in Experiment 1.","This is the 1.0 g KCl result from Table 2. It ignores that doubling the salt mass nearly doubled conductivity in Experiment 1.","Correct: in Experiment 1, going from 1.0 g to 2.0 g of NaCl raised conductivity from 17,400 to 32,600, a little less than double. KCl at 1.0 g gave 16,900, so 2.0 g should give a little less than 33,800, about 32,000.","Too large: this is four times the 1.0 g value. Even quadrupling NaCl from 1.0 g to 4.0 g raised conductivity only about 3.4 times, and here the mass only doubles."]],"science-0332":["Scan the force column of Table 2 for the smallest value: waxed wood, 1.2 N. Glass (2.0 N) is close but larger. Sandpaper (6.4 N) is the greatest, the opposite of what the question asks.",["Correct: waxed wood needed only 1.2 N, the smallest force in Table 2.","Glass needed 2.0 N, the second-smallest force, more than waxed wood\'s 1.2 N.","Bare wood needed 3.0 N, more than both waxed wood and glass.","Reversed: sandpaper needed 6.4 N, the greatest force, not the least."]],"science-0333":["Scientists repeat trials and average them because any single measurement can be off a little by chance, for example if the block jerks or the scale is read at a slightly wrong moment. An average of several trials is more reliable. Repeating doesn\'t change the force needed, the surface, or the mass.",["Repeating a measurement doesn\'t change the block or the surface, so it can\'t change the force needed.","Each set of 3 trials used the same surface. Different surfaces were compared across rows of Table 2, not within one average.","The mass was set by the masses stacked on the block. Repeating a trial doesn\'t control it.","Correct: one reading might be a bit high or low by chance. Averaging 3 trials gives a more reliable value."]],"science-0334":["Combine the two experiments. Table 2 says a 1.0 kg block needs 6.4 N on sandpaper. Table 1 says that, on one surface, doubling the mass from 1.0 kg to 2.0 kg about doubles the force (3.0 N to 5.9 N). So a 2.0 kg block on sandpaper should need about 2 × 6.4 = 12.8 N. Each wrong choice uses only one experiment: 5.9 N changes the mass but not the surface, and 6.4 N changes the surface but not the mass.",["This is the bare-wood force at 1.0 kg. It ignores both the rougher surface and the larger mass.","This is the 2.0 kg force on bare wood from Table 1. It ignores that sandpaper needed about twice the force of bare wood in Experiment 2.","This is the sandpaper force at 1.0 kg from Table 2. It ignores that doubling the mass about doubled the force in Experiment 1.","Correct: at 1.0 kg, sandpaper needed 6.4 N. In Experiment 1, doubling the mass from 1.0 kg to 2.0 kg about doubled the force (3.0 N to 5.9 N), so 2.0 kg on sandpaper needs about 2 × 6.4 = 12.8 N."]],"science-0335":["Check pairs of masses where one is double the other. From 0.5 kg to 1.0 kg, the force went from 1.5 N to 3.0 N, exactly double. From 1.0 kg to 2.0 kg, it went from 3.0 N to 5.9 N, about double. So yes, Experiment 1 supports the claim. Choice C has the right numbers but the wrong decision. Choice D is about the surface, which the claim never mentions.",["Misread row: 4.4 N is the force at 1.5 kg, not at 2.0 kg. And 4.4 N is not double 3.0 N, so these numbers wouldn\'t support the claim.","Correct: 5.9 N is about twice 3.0 N. Likewise, 0.5 kg needed 1.5 N and 1.0 kg needed 3.0 N, exactly double.","Right numbers, wrong decision: 5.9 N is about twice 3.0 N, so the data agree with the claim.","That describes Experiment 2, and it doesn\'t test what happens when the mass doubles. The claim is about mass, not surface."]],"science-0336":["In Experiment 1, all cups were under the lamp 12 hours per day, so light was held constant there. In Experiment 2, light was the variable: 0, 6, 12, or 18 hours. Nitrate is the reverse (varied in Experiment 1, held at 10 mg/L in Experiment 2). Water volume never changed, and the plant count is the result being measured.",["Reversed: nitrate varied in Experiment 1 (0 to 40 mg/L) and was held at 10 mg/L in Experiment 2.","Correct: every cup in Experiment 1 got 12 hours of light per day, while Experiment 2 tested 0, 6, 12, and 18 hours.","Every cup in both experiments held 200 mL, so the volume never varied.","This was measured in both experiments. It is the dependent variable, not a condition the students set."]],"science-0337":["A control is the group that doesn\'t get the treatment being tested. Here the treatment is added nitrate, so the cup with 0 mg/L is the control. Its 18 plants show the growth without added nitrate. Every other cup can then be compared with it to see how much the nitrate helped.",["Correct: the cup with no added nitrate shows how much the duckweed grows without the treatment, so the other cups can be compared with it.","10 mg/L was the level used in every cup of Experiment 2, but in Experiment 1 it was one of the treatments.","20 mg/L gave the most plants, but the greatest result isn\'t what makes a control.","This is the most extreme treatment, the opposite of a control."]],"science-0338":["The word \\"always\\" means a single counterexample is enough to disprove the claim. Up to 20 mg/L, more nitrate did give more plants (18, 31, 44, 52). But at 40 mg/L there were 49 plants, fewer than the 52 at 20 mg/L. So the results don\'t support the conclusion. Choice A reads only part of the table, and choice C misreads the 0 mg/L result.",["True for 0 to 20 mg/L, but the word \\"always\\" fails at the next step: 40 mg/L gave fewer plants (49) than 20 mg/L (52).","True, but growth in every cup doesn\'t mean that more nitrate always means more growth.","False: the 0 mg/L cup ended with 18 plants, more than the 10 it started with.","Correct: going from 20 to 40 mg/L lowered the count from 52 to 49, so more nitrate did not always mean more growth."]],"science-0339":["Work out the conditions of the new cup: no nitrate, 12 hours of light, and everything else the same. Experiment 1 already tested this combination. Its cups were all under the lamp for 12 hours, and the cup with 0 mg/L nitrate ended with 18 plants. So the best prediction is about 18. 44 is the 12-hour cup that had 10 mg/L nitrate, and 9 is the cup that had no light.",["This is the 0 hours of light result from Table 2. The question changes the nitrate, not the light.","Correct: a cup with 0 mg/L nitrate and 12 hours of light is exactly the 0 mg/L cup of Experiment 1, which had 18 plants.","This is the 12-hour result with 10 mg/L nitrate. Removing the nitrate should lower it.","This is the 18-hour result from Table 2, the most light, not 12 hours."]],"science-0340":["Find the clay row in Table 1: 620 s. The other values come from the loam row (150 s) or from the footpaths in Study 2 (410 s and 1,150 s).",["This is the loam soil in Table 1, the row above clay.","This is the lightly used path from Table 2, not the clay soil in Study 1.","Correct: Table 1 lists 620 s for clay.","This is the heavily used path in Study 2, not the clay soil."]],"science-0341":["A dependent variable is what is measured as the result. In both studies, the students timed how long 250 mL of water took to soak in. Soil type (Study 1) and foot traffic (Study 2) were the variables they chose to change, and the 250 mL volume was kept the same throughout.",["Soil type was the independent variable in Study 1, and it was held constant (loam) in Study 2.","Foot traffic was the independent variable in Study 2, and it was not varied in Study 1.","Every ring got 250 mL, so the volume was held constant, not measured as a result.","Correct: in both studies, the students measured the time it took the water to soak in."]],"science-0342":["The claim compares two specific places: the heavily used loam path (Study 2) and untrampled clay (Study 1). A longer time means water soaked in more slowly. The path took 1,150 s and the clay took 620 s, so water soaked into the path more slowly, and the claim is supported. Choice C uses the lightly used path by mistake, and choice B compares the wrong pair of soils.",["Correct: a longer time means slower infiltration, and 1,150 s on the heavily used loam path is longer than 620 s in untrampled clay.","Wrong comparison: this compares untrampled clay with untrampled loam. The claim is about the heavily used path.","Wrong row: 410 s is the lightly used path. The claim is about the heavily used path, which took 1,150 s.","True only for untrampled soil. Study 2 shows that trampling can make loam even slower than clay."]],"science-0343":["Without the ring, poured water would run off across the surface, and how far it spread would change from place to place. Pushing the ring into the ground keeps all 250 mL over the same area of soil, so the time measures how fast that soil takes in water. That makes the times from different sites fair to compare.",["The ring doesn\'t speed up soaking; it keeps the water in one spot so the time reflects that spot\'s soil.","Correct: the ring holds the 250 mL over the same 25 cm wide patch of soil in every trial, so the times can be compared fairly.","The ring has nothing to do with temperature, and nothing in the procedure waits for the water to warm.","The ring doesn\'t mix anything. The water simply sits inside it and soaks in."]],"science-0344":["Student 2 says water vapor, a gas, turns into liquid water on the cold glass. A gas changing into a liquid is condensation. Evaporation is the reverse (liquid to gas), melting is solid to liquid, and freezing is liquid to solid.",["Reversed: evaporation is liquid turning into gas, the opposite of what Student 2 describes.","Melting is a solid turning into a liquid, like the ice in the glass, not vapor turning into liquid.","Correct: condensation is a gas (water vapor) changing into a liquid.","Freezing is a liquid turning into a solid. The drops on the glass are liquid, not ice."]],"science-0345":["Ask where each student\'s water comes from and whether it could carry the coloring. Student 1: the blue water itself seeps through the glass, so the drops should be blue. Colorless drops contradict Student 1. Student 3: the water first evaporates, and when water evaporates, dissolved substances stay behind, so the drops would be colorless. Student 2: the water comes from the air, so the drops would be colorless. Only Student 1\'s explanation is contradicted. The trap is to group Student 3 with Student 1 because both say the water starts in the glass.",["Correct: Student 1 says the liquid water itself seeps through the glass, and the dissolved coloring would come with it. Colorless drops contradict this.","Student 3 says the water evaporates first. Evaporation leaves dissolved coloring behind, so Student 3 would also expect colorless drops.","One too many: Student 3\'s water travels as vapor, which leaves the coloring behind, so colorless drops fit Student 3.","Student 2 says the drops come from vapor in the room\'s air, which has no coloring, so colorless drops fit Student 2. They fit Student 3 too."]],"science-0346":["The dry chamber removes the one source Student 2 depends on: water vapor in the outside air. Student 2 predicts no drops, which matches the result. Student 1 says water seeps through the glass from inside, and Student 3 says water from the melting ice evaporates and comes down the outside. Neither depends on vapor already in the air, so both predict drops even in dry air. Since no drops formed, Students 1 and 3 are weakened.",["Reversed: Student 2 says the drops come from water vapor in the air, so dry air should give no drops. The result supports Student 2.","One too few: Student 1\'s water seeps through the glass and has nothing to do with the air, so Student 1 is weakened too.","Correct: Students 1 and 3 both say the water comes from inside the glass, so dry air outside shouldn\'t stop the drops. Only Student 2 predicts no drops.","Student 2\'s explanation predicts exactly this result, so it is supported, not weakened."]],"science-0347":["Student 1 and Student 3 disagree about how the water gets out (seeping liquid through pores vs. vapor out of the open top), but they agree on where it starts: inside the glass. Student 2 is the only one who says the water comes from the room\'s air. The wrong choices each describe a claim made by only one student.",["Only Student 1 says this. Student 3 says the water leaves through the open top of the glass.","Correct: Student 1 says water from inside seeps out, and Student 3 says water from the melting ice evaporates. Both trace the drops to the glass\'s contents.","This is Student 2\'s view, not Student 1\'s or Student 3\'s.","Only Student 3 says this. Student 1 says the water seeps out as liquid."]],"science-0348":["Scientist 1 says the larvae cause the deformities. Scientist 3 begins by agreeing that the larvae disrupt leg growth, then adds that the pesticide lets more larvae survive. Scientist 2 says parasites play no role. So Scientists 1 and 3 both hold that the larvae are involved. The trap is skipping Scientist 3 because Scientist 3 also mentions the pesticide.",["One too few: Scientist 3 also says the larvae disrupt leg growth. Scientist 3 just adds a role for the pesticide.","Correct: Scientist 1 blames the larvae, and Scientist 3 agrees that the larvae disrupt leg growth.","Scientist 2 says parasites play no role, so Scientist 2 doesn\'t belong in this list.","Scientist 2 rejects the parasite idea completely."]],"science-0349":["Filtering took out the larvae but would leave a dissolved pesticide in the water. Scientists 1 and 3 both say the larvae are what disrupt leg growth, so both predict few deformities without larvae. Scientist 2 blames only the pesticide, which was still there. The same 30% deformity rate without larvae best supports Scientist 2.",["Scientist 1 says the larvae cause the deformities. With all the larvae removed, Scientist 1 would expect almost none.","Scientist 3 says the larvae disrupt leg growth and the pesticide only helps them survive. Without larvae, Scientist 3 would expect few deformities.","Both of these scientists need the larvae, and the filtered water had none.","Correct: filtering removes living things but not a dissolved chemical. Deformities without any larvae point to something in the water itself, like the pesticide."]],"science-0350":["This pond has the larvae but no pesticide, and the deformity rate is still high (28%). Scientist 1 says the larvae alone cause the deformities, so this fits Scientist 1. Scientist 2 says the pesticide causes them, but there was none, so Scientist 2 is weakened. Scientist 3 says that without the pesticide the parasite would cause few deformities, but it caused many, so Scientist 3 is weakened too. The trap is to see the parasite in Scientist 3\'s explanation and assume Scientist 3 is supported.",["Reversed: this result supports Scientist 1, who says the larvae alone cause the deformities.","One too few: Scientist 3 says that without the pesticide the parasite would cause few deformities, so Scientist 3 is weakened too.","Correct: Scientist 2 is weakened because there was no pesticide, and Scientist 3 is weakened because the parasite caused many deformities without the pesticide\'s help.","Scientist 1 is supported, not weakened: larvae without pesticide still gave a high rate."]],"science-0351":["To tell two explanations apart, test a condition where they predict different results. Scientists 1 and 3 agree that larvae disrupt leg growth. They disagree only about whether the pesticide matters. So give two groups the same number of larvae and change only the pesticide. If the pesticide group has far more deformities, Scientist 3 is supported. If the rates are about the same, Scientist 1 is supported. The other designs give results that both scientists would predict.",["Correct: Scientist 3 predicts many more deformities in the pesticide group; Scientist 1 predicts about the same rate in both. The result tells the two apart.","Both Scientist 1 and Scientist 3 predict few deformities with no larvae, so this can\'t tell them apart. It tests Scientist 2.","Both Scientists 1 and 3 say the larvae come from snails, so both expect more snails in the affected pond.","All three scientists expect almost no deformities here, so this control alone can\'t separate any of them."]],"science-0352":["Go to the 6 min row and read across to the Exp. 2 column: 47°C. The 74°C in the same row belongs to Experiment 1, and 38°C and 56°C come from the rows above and below.",["This is Experiment 2 at 4 min, one row too early.","Correct: the 6 min row shows 47°C in the Experiment 2 column.","This is Experiment 2 at 8 min, one row too late (and Experiment 1 at 4 min).","Wrong column: 74°C is Experiment 1 at 6 min."]],"science-0353":["The only change between the experiments was the mass of water: 200 g in Experiment 1 and 400 g in Experiment 2. The hot plate setting, the 20°C starting temperature, and the 2 min between readings were the same in both. Keeping everything else the same is what lets the students say the slower warming in Experiment 2 was caused by the extra water.",["The hot plate was kept at the same setting throughout, so it was held constant.","The water started at 20°C in both experiments.","The temperature was measured every 2 min in both experiments.","Correct: Experiment 1 heated 200 g of water and Experiment 2 heated 400 g."]],"science-0354":["Pure water at sea level boils at 100°C, and the lab was near sea level. Once the water reaches its boiling point, its temperature stops rising. The hot plate is still adding heat, but that energy goes into turning liquid water into water vapor instead of warming it further. Choice A has the right idea (boiling) with a wrong reason: the water doesn\'t stop taking in heat. Choice C contradicts the passage.",["Wrong reason: boiling water keeps taking in heat from the hot plate. That heat goes into turning liquid into vapor.","An empty beaker on a hot plate would keep getting hotter, not stay at exactly 100°C. And 200 g of water would take much longer than a few minutes to boil away.","The passage says the hot plate stayed at the same setting throughout.","Correct: at sea level, water boils at 100°C. While it boils, the heat goes into changing liquid to gas, so the temperature stays flat."]],"science-0355":["In Experiment 1, the water reached 56°C at 4 min. It was 91°C at 8 min and 100°C at 10 min, so it first reached 100°C sometime between 8 and 10 min. Water starting at 56°C is where the original water was at 4 min, so it needs the rest of that time: between 8 − 4 = 4 min and 10 − 4 = 6 min. The trap is to use the whole 8-10 min, which starts from 20°C.",["Too fast: in Experiment 1, the water rose only about 18°C every 2 min, so going from 56°C to 100°C takes more than 3 min.","Correct: in Experiment 1, the water was at 56°C at 4 min and first reached 100°C between 8 and 10 min, so starting at 56°C saves about 4 min, leaving 4 to 6 min.","This is when the original 20°C water first reached 100°C. Starting at 56°C skips the first 4 min of heating.","This is the time range for Experiment 2\'s 400 g of water, which is still below 100°C at 14 min."]],"science-0356":["The independent variable is the condition the students changed on purpose. In Experiment 2, they tested 15, 20, 25, 30, and 35°C, all at 3% salinity. The number of eggs that hatched is the dependent variable, and salinity and water volume were held constant.",["Salinity was the variable in Experiment 1. In Experiment 2 it was held at 3%.","This is what the students counted, the dependent variable.","Correct: Experiment 2 tested five temperatures, from 15°C to 35°C.","Every dish held 50 mL, so the volume was held constant."]],"science-0357":["Multiply all three parts: Experiment 1 tested 6 salinities, each in 2 dishes, with 100 eggs per dish. 6 × 2 × 100 = 1,200 eggs. The common slips are forgetting the 2 dishes (600) or adding the 5 temperatures of Experiment 2 (2,200).",["This is the number of eggs in one dish.","Forgets that each condition used 2 dishes: 6 salinities × 100 eggs counts only one dish per salinity.","Correct: 6 salinities × 2 dishes × 100 eggs = 1,200 eggs.","This counts both experiments: 11 conditions × 2 dishes × 100 eggs. The question asks about Experiment 1 only."]],"science-0358":["Look for the largest value across both tables. Table 1\'s best is 74 (3% at 25°C). Table 2\'s best is 81 (3% at 30°C). Since 81 is the highest of all, the claim is consistent with the results. Choice A has the right answer for the wrong reason: 74 eggs hatched at 25°C, not 30°C.",["Right decision, wrong data: 74 is the result at 3% and 25°C, not at 30°C.","Experiment 1 was done at 25°C, not 30°C. The 3%, 30°C condition is in Experiment 2, where 81 hatched.","False: at 35°C only 30 eggs per dish hatched, far fewer than at 30°C.","Correct: 81 is the largest number in either table, and it came from 3% salinity at 30°C (Table 2)."]],"science-0359":["In Experiment 1, the students used one 28 g ball and changed only the height. So the ball\'s mass was held constant. Drop height was the independent variable, crater diameter was the dependent variable, and the fall time changed along with the height.",["Drop height was the variable the students changed in Experiment 1 (25 cm to 200 cm).","Crater diameter was the result being measured, so it is the dependent variable.","A ball dropped from higher takes longer to fall, so this changed along with the drop height.","Correct: every drop in Experiment 1 used the same 28 g ball."]],"science-0360":["Read down Table 1: 5.0, 6.0, 7.1, 7.8, 8.4 cm. Every value is larger than the one before it, so the diameter increased only. The increases get smaller as the height grows, but the trend never reverses.",["Correct: each greater height gave a wider crater: 5.0, 6.0, 7.1, 7.8, and 8.4 cm.","Reversed: the diameters got larger, not smaller, as height increased.","There was no drop at the end: the widest crater (8.4 cm) came from the greatest height.","The diameter grew from 5.0 cm to 8.4 cm, so it didn\'t stay the same, even though each increase was smaller than the last."]],"science-0361":["The change is to the ball\'s mass, so look at Experiment 2, which tested mass at a fixed height. At 100 cm, the 64 g ball made an 8.6 cm crater and the 28 g ball made a 7.1 cm crater. So swapping in the 64 g ball should make wider craters. Choice D predicts \\"wider\\" too, but its reason is about height, which isn\'t what changed.",["False: in Experiment 2, heavier balls made wider craters (5.0, 7.1, and 8.6 cm).","Experiment 2 shows that mass matters too: at the same height, the 64 g ball made a crater 1.5 cm wider than the 28 g ball did.","Correct: at 100 cm, the 64 g ball made an 8.6 cm crater and the 28 g ball made a 7.1 cm crater, so a heavier ball should make wider craters at every height.","Right prediction, wrong reason: Experiment 1 shows the effect of height, but the change here is to the ball\'s mass, which only Experiment 2 tested."]],"science-0362":["Pick a pair of heights where one is double the other. From 50 cm to 100 cm, the diameter went from 6.0 cm to 7.1 cm, not to 12.0 cm. From 100 cm to 200 cm, it went from 7.1 cm to 8.4 cm, also far short of doubling. So the results do not support the claim. Choice B is true but beside the point: increasing isn\'t the same as doubling.",["Misread data: the crater at 100 cm was 7.1 cm, not 12.0 cm.","True, but getting wider is not the same as doubling. The claim needs the diameter to become twice as large.","Correct: if the claim were true, the diameter at 100 cm would be 12.0 cm, but it was only 7.1 cm.","False: the diameter increased with height."]],"science-0363":["The geologists disagree about what moved the boulders (ice vs. a flood), but both say the boulders traveled south from the mountains, where the nearest granite is. Each of the wrong choices is a claim made by only one geologist, or by neither.",["Only Geologist 1 says the boulders were carried by a glacier.","Correct: Geologist 1\'s glacier and Geologist 2\'s flood both moved the boulders south from the mountains.","Only Geologist 2 says a flood carried the boulders.","Neither geologist says this. The boulders are granite, not limestone, and both geologists say they were carried in from the north."]],"science-0364":["Geologist 1 says the glacier dropped the boulders along with clay, sand, and gravel, all mixed together, which is exactly what the diggers found. Geologist 2 says the flood sorted sediment by size, so a jumbled, unsorted mixture goes against Geologist 2. The finding supports Geologist 1 only.",["Correct: Geologist 1 says the melting glacier dropped the boulders with clay, sand, and gravel all mixed together.","Reversed: Geologist 2 says fast water sorts sediment by size, so an unsorted jumble goes against Geologist 2.","Geologist 2 expects sorted sediment, so the jumbled mixture fits only Geologist 1.","Geologist 1 describes exactly this kind of unsorted mixture."]],"science-0365":["Look for a finding that matches a prediction only Geologist 2 makes. Geologist 2 says the slowing flood dropped the largest boulders first, so boulders should get smaller toward the south. Geologist 1 says the glacier dropped everything mixed together, which wouldn\'t produce a steady north-to-south size pattern. So a steady decrease in boulder size supports Geologist 2 and weakens Geologist 1. Grooves in the bedrock would support Geologist 1, and the granite\'s source fits both.",["Reversed: Geologist 1 says rocks under the glacier scraped grooves into the bedrock, so this supports Geologist 1.","Both geologists say the boulders came from those mountains, so this doesn\'t favor either one.","Correct: Geologist 2 predicts boulders that shrink toward the south as the flood slowed. Geologist 1\'s glacier dropped everything mixed together, with no sorting by size.","This is already given in the passage, and both explanations are about moving very large boulders, so it favors neither."]],"science-0366":["In Experiment 3, both springs stretched 6.0 cm. Use Table 1 to find how much weight gives each spring that stretch. Spring A stretches 2.0 cm per 100 g, so 6.0 cm means 300 g. Spring B stretches 5.0 cm per 100 g, so 6.0 cm means 120 g. Check: 300 g + 120 g = 420 g, the full mass. Equal stretch doesn\'t mean equal shares, so 210 g is the trap. The stiffer Spring A carries most of the weight.",["Correct: each spring stretched 6.0 cm. In Table 1, Spring B stretches 5.0 cm per 100 g, so 6.0 cm means it held 120 g. Spring A, at 2.0 cm per 100 g, held 300 g, and 120 + 300 = 420.","Wrong split: this gives Spring B 2/5 of the weight, using the ratio of the springs\' stretches per 100 g (2.0 to 5.0). But the stiffer spring carries more weight, not less.","This assumes the springs shared the weight equally. They stretched equally, but Spring A needs far more weight than Spring B to stretch the same amount.","This is Spring A\'s share. A stretch of 6.0 cm takes 300 g on Spring A but only 120 g on Spring B."]],"science-0367":["Break the arrangement into its parts and use the rules from Experiments 2 and 3. Spring A is at the top, end to end with the pair, so it carries the whole 400 g. From Table 1, it stretches 8.0 cm. The two B springs hang side by side and are identical, so they share the 400 g equally: 200 g each. From Table 1, each stretches 10.0 cm, and because they are side by side, the pair stretches 10.0 cm in all. Total: 8.0 + 10.0 = 18.0 cm.",["This halves Spring A\'s load as well. Spring A is above the pair, so it carries all 400 g (8.0 cm); only the two B springs share the weight.","Correct: Spring A carries the full 400 g and stretches 8.0 cm. The two identical B springs share the weight equally, 200 g each, so each stretches 10.0 cm. Total: 8.0 + 10.0 = 18.0 cm.","This treats the B pair like a single Spring B carrying all 400 g (20.0 cm). Side by side, the two springs share the weight, so each carries only 200 g.","This adds both B springs as if they hung end to end, each carrying 400 g. Side by side, they stretch together, not one after the other."]],"science-0368":["Compare the two setups at the same mass. Table 3 gives 4.0 cm for the pair with 280 g. Spring A alone stretches 2.0 cm per 100 g (Table 1), so with 280 g it would stretch about 5.6 cm. Because 4.0 cm is less than 5.6 cm, the claim is consistent with the results. The same holds at 140 g (2.0 cm vs. about 2.8 cm) and at 420 g (6.0 cm vs. about 8.4 cm). The traps compare different masses, the wrong spring, or the wrong experiment.",["Right verdict, wrong evidence: this compares the pair with Spring B, not Spring A, and with different masses.","Mismatched masses: the side-by-side pair stretched 4.0 cm with 280 g, not 200 g. With 280 g, Spring A alone would stretch more than 4.0 cm.","This describes the end-to-end setup of Experiment 2. The claim is about the side-by-side setup of Experiment 3.","Correct: Spring A stretches 2.0 cm per 100 g, so 280 g would stretch it about 5.6 cm, more than the pair\'s 4.0 cm."]],"science-0369":["First find a station\'s distance. An S wave takes 1/4 s per km and a P wave takes 1/6 s per km, so the delay grows by 1/4 − 1/6 = 1/12 s for every km. Station X\'s delay was 16 − 12 = 4 s, so X is 4 × 12 = 48 km from the origin. P waves cover 48 km in 48 ÷ 6 = 8 s, and they reached X at 12 s, so the quake began at 12 − 8 = 4 s, or 08:00:04. Check with Station Z: delay 12 s, so 144 km; P travel time 144 ÷ 6 = 24 s; 28 − 24 = 4 s.",["Correct: Station X\'s delay was 4 s. Each km adds 1/4 − 1/6 = 1/12 s of delay, so X is 48 km away. P waves take 48 ÷ 6 = 8 s to go 48 km, so the quake began at 12 − 8 = 4 s.","This is the P waves\' travel time to Station X (8 s), not the time the quake began.","This is when the P waves reached Station X. The waves had already been traveling for 8 s by then.","This is when the S waves reached Station X, even later than the P waves."]],"science-0370":["Two facts are needed: the S wave\'s travel time and the quake\'s start time. S waves travel 4 km/s, so 120 km takes 30 s. To find the start time, use Station X. Its delay of 4 s means it is 48 km away (the delay grows 1/12 s per km), and P waves take 8 s to cover 48 km. They arrived at 12 s, so the quake began at 4 s. The S waves reach W at 4 + 30 = 34 s. A quick check: W (120 km) lies between Y (96 km, S at 28 s) and Z (144 km, S at 40 s), and 34 s is between those times.",["This is when the P waves would arrive (120 ÷ 6 = 20 s of travel, plus the 4 s start). The question asks about the S waves.","This is the S waves\' travel time, 120 ÷ 4 = 30 s. It forgets that the quake began 4 s after 08:00:00.","Correct: S waves take 120 ÷ 4 = 30 s to travel 120 km, and the quake began at 4 s (from Station X: 48 km away, P waves arrived at 12 s after 8 s of travel), so 4 + 30 = 34 s.","This is when the S waves reached Station Z, which is 144 km away, farther than W."]],"science-0371":["In the new region, an S wave takes 1/2 s per km and a P wave takes 1/6 s per km. The delay grows by 1/2 − 1/6 = 1/3 s for every km. A 12 s delay means 12 ÷ (1/3) = 36 km. Check: P waves take 36 ÷ 6 = 6 s and S waves take 36 ÷ 2 = 18 s, and 18 − 6 = 12 s. The 144 km trap uses the old region\'s 1/12 s per km.",["This multiplies the 12 s delay by the S-wave speed (2 km/s). The delay is a difference between two travel times, not the S wave\'s travel time.","Correct: each km now adds 1/2 − 1/6 = 1/3 s of delay, so a 12 s delay means 12 × 3 = 36 km.","This multiplies the 12 s delay by the P-wave speed (6 km/s), treating the delay as if it were the P wave\'s travel time.","This uses the original region\'s rule (1/12 s of delay per km). With slower S waves, the delay grows much faster with distance."]],"science-0372":["The delay is the S travel time minus the P travel time. Over 300 km, S waves take 300 ÷ 4 = 75 s and P waves take 300 ÷ 6 = 50 s, so the delay is 25 s and the prediction is right. The table agrees: Station X (48 km) had a 4 s delay and Station Z (144 km) had a 12 s delay, which is 1 s for every 12 km.",["Correct: P waves take 300 ÷ 6 = 50 s and S waves take 300 ÷ 4 = 75 s, so the delay is 75 − 50 = 25 s, which is 1 s for every 12 km.","Right verdict, wrong reason: P waves would take 300 ÷ 6 = 50 s, not 25 s.","75 s is the S waves\' travel time. The delay is the difference between the S and P travel times: 75 − 50 = 25 s.","This uses only the P-wave speed. The delay depends on both speeds: 1/4 − 1/6 = 1/12 s per km, so 300 km gives 25 s."]],"science-0373":["All three scientists agree that the birds arrive earlier, but they disagree about why. Scientists 1 and 3 say the birds leave earlier and the trip takes as long as ever. Only Scientist 2 says the birds leave on the same dates and arrive early because the trip itself is shorter.",["Scientist 1 says travel time has not changed. The birds simply leave earlier.","Correct: Scientist 2 says the birds leave on the same dates but refuel faster and finish the trip in fewer days.","One too many: Scientist 3 says travel time has not changed. Under Scientist 3\'s view, the birds leave earlier.","Scientists 1 and 3 both say travel time has not changed."]],"science-0374":["Ask what each scientist says about departure dates. Scientist 1: birds leave earlier because winters are warmer. Scientist 3: birds leave earlier because early-leaving birds have become more common. Scientist 2: birds leave on the same dates, and the trip is shorter. Unchanged departure dates contradict Scientists 1 and 3 and match Scientist 2. The trap is to forget that Scientist 3\'s evolutionary explanation also predicts earlier departures for the population.",["One too few: Scientist 3 also says the birds leave earlier now, because early-leaving birds have become more common.","One too few: Scientist 1 says warmer winters make the birds leave earlier, so unchanged dates weaken Scientist 1 too.","Correct: both Scientist 1 (warmth makes birds leave earlier) and Scientist 3 (early-leaving birds are more common) require earlier departures.","Scientist 2 says the birds leave on the same dates as before, so this finding fits Scientist 2."]],"science-0375":["Scientists 1 and 3 agree that birds now leave earlier. They differ on whether one bird\'s departure date changes with the weather. Scientist 1 says it does: a warm year means an earlier departure. Scientist 3 says it doesn\'t: each bird\'s date is inherited and fixed. Following the same birds over several years tests exactly this. If each bird leaves earlier in warm years, Scientist 1 is supported and Scientist 3 is weakened. The population\'s earlier average fits both, and inherited timing in chicks supports Scientist 3.",["Both Scientist 1 and Scientist 3 predict earlier departures for the population today, so this finding can\'t tell them apart.","Both Scientist 1 and Scientist 3 say travel time hasn\'t changed, so this fits both. It would weaken Scientist 2 instead.","This supports Scientist 3, who says departure dates are inherited. It says nothing in favor of Scientist 1.","Correct: Scientist 1 says each bird leaves earlier in warm years. Scientist 3 says each bird\'s date stays the same whatever the weather."]],"science-0376":["Scientists 1 and 2 disagree about the departure cue: temperature (Scientist 1) or day length (Scientist 2). A good test changes one cue while holding the other constant. The caged-bird study gives both groups the same day lengths and changes only the temperature. If the warm group becomes restless earlier, temperature matters, as Scientist 1 says. If both groups become restless together, day length is the cue, as Scientist 2 says. The other studies give results that both scientists could accept.",["Correct: Scientist 1 predicts the warm-room birds will become restless earlier; Scientist 2 predicts both groups will become restless at the same time, since day length is the same.","This checks part of Scientist 2\'s reasoning, but Scientist 1 says nothing about insects, so the result can\'t decide between them.","This tests Scientist 3\'s claim that early arrivals raise more chicks. It doesn\'t tell Scientist 1\'s cue from Scientist 2\'s.","All three scientists predict that arrival dates will stay early, so more years of arrival dates can\'t separate them."]],"science-0377":["If the Na₂CO₃ had run out, adding more of it would make more solid. But after 50 mL, more Na₂CO₃ made no more solid. So the other reactant, the calcium from the CaCl₂, must have been used up. Experiment 2 confirms this: with twice as much calcium, the solid kept increasing up to 1.00 g. Choice A has the right data but the wrong verdict.",["Right data, wrong conclusion: the mass did level off, but fresh Na₂CO₃ was being added the whole time, so the Na₂CO₃ can\'t be what ran out.","This actually points the other way: doubling the calcium let more solid form, which means calcium was what limited Experiment 1.","Correct: once the calcium from the CaCl₂ is used up, extra carbonate has nothing to combine with, so the mass stays at 0.50 g.","False: Table 1 shows 0.50 g at 50, 60, and 80 mL, so the mass stopped increasing at 50 mL."]],"science-0378":["In Experiment 1, the calcium ran out after 50 mL of 0.10 M Na₂CO₃. In the new trial, the amount of calcium is unchanged, so the same amount of carbonate is needed to use it up. Each mL of 0.20 M Na₂CO₃ holds twice as much carbonate as each mL of 0.10 M, so half the volume, 25 mL, delivers the same amount. The trap is to copy Experiment 2, where the CaCl₂, not the Na₂CO₃, was doubled and the volume needed doubled to 100 mL.",["Halves the volume twice: the calcium is unchanged, so only the carbonate\'s doubled strength matters, and that halves the volume once.","Correct: the calcium is the same as in Experiment 1, which needed 50 mL of 0.10 M Na₂CO₃. Solution that is twice as concentrated supplies the same carbonate in half the volume.","This is the Experiment 1 result. It ignores that each mL of the stronger solution carries twice as much carbonate.","Wrong direction: this is what doubling the CaCl₂ did in Experiment 2. Doubling the Na₂CO₃ concentration means less volume is needed, not more."]],"science-0379":["Find which reactant runs out. The most solid each CaCl₂ solution can make is proportional to its calcium: 0.10 M gave a maximum of 0.50 g, and 0.20 M gave 1.00 g, so 0.15 M allows 0.75 g. Reaching that takes 75 mL of 0.10 M Na₂CO₃ (Table 1 and Table 2 both show 0.10 g of solid per 10 mL until the calcium runs out). 100 mL is more than 75 mL, so the calcium runs out first, and the mass is about 0.75 g.",["This is the most solid that 0.10 M CaCl₂ could make. A 0.15 M solution holds 1.5 times as much calcium.","Correct: the maximum mass doubled from 0.50 g to 1.00 g when the CaCl₂ went from 0.10 M to 0.20 M, so 0.15 M allows 0.75 g. That takes only 75 mL of Na₂CO₃, and 100 mL is more than enough.","This is Experiment 2\'s result at 100 mL, where the CaCl₂ was 0.20 M. With 0.15 M CaCl₂, the calcium runs out first, at 0.75 g.","This is 1.5 times Experiment 2\'s 1.00 g. But 0.15 M is 1.5 times 0.10 M (Experiment 1), not 1.5 times 0.20 M, so the maximum is 1.5 × 0.50 = 0.75 g."]],"science-0380":["After filtering, the solid is still wet. The students wanted the mass of calcium carbonate alone, and water left in the solid would add extra mass. Drying removes that water, so the masses in the tables reflect only the solid that formed.",["The reaction was over once the solid was filtered out. Drying removes water; it can\'t make more product.","The solid settled out of the liquid before it was filtered. Drying came after filtering.","The passage doesn\'t say any sodium chloride was collected or removed by drying. The purpose of drying is to remove water.","Correct: the solid is weighed to find how much formed. Any water left in it would make it weigh more than the solid alone."]],"science-0381":["Coating a surface blocks its stomata, so the drop in water loss shows how much water left through that surface. Coating the lower surfaces cut the loss from 4.0 mL to 0.8 mL, a drop of 3.2 mL. Coating the upper surfaces cut it only from 4.0 mL to 3.5 mL, a drop of 0.5 mL. Much more water leaves through the lower surfaces, which supports the idea that they have more stomata. Choice C has the right numbers but reads them backward: a high loss after coating the upper surfaces means the upper surfaces weren\'t losing much.",["Correct: blocking the lower surfaces stopped far more water loss (4.0 − 0.8 = 3.2 mL) than blocking the upper surfaces (4.0 − 3.5 = 0.5 mL), so most of the water escapes through the lower surfaces.","Right verdict, weak evidence: this comparison shows how much water leaves through the upper surfaces (0.5 mL), not that the lower surfaces lose more.","Right numbers, wrong verdict: coating the upper surfaces barely mattered, while coating the lower surfaces stopped most of the water loss. That supports the hypothesis.","This shows that a little water escapes another way, such as through the stems. It doesn\'t compare the two leaf surfaces."]],"science-0382":["The water that leaves through the lower surfaces is what coating them stops: 4.0 − 0.8 = 3.2 mL. As a share of the uncoated shoot\'s 4.0 mL, that is 3.2 ÷ 4.0 = 0.80, or 80%. The numbers fit together: 0.5 mL leaves through the upper surfaces, 3.2 mL through the lower surfaces, and 0.3 mL by other routes, 4.0 mL in all. The trap is dividing 0.8 by 4.0, which gives the share that does not pass through the lower surfaces.",["This is the share lost through the upper surfaces: (4.0 − 3.5) ÷ 4.0 ≈ 13%.","This is 0.8 ÷ 4.0. But 0.8 mL is what the shoot still lost after the lower surfaces were blocked, so it is the water that did not go through them.","Correct: blocking the lower surfaces cut the loss from 4.0 mL to 0.8 mL, so 3.2 mL of 4.0 mL went through them: 3.2 ÷ 4.0 = 80%.","This is 3.5 ÷ 4.0, using the upper-coated shoot. That is the share that did not go through the upper surfaces, which includes the stems."]],"science-0383":["Start from what is known. In light, a shoot with its lower surfaces coated lost 0.8 mL: water from the upper surfaces plus other routes. In darkness the stomata close, which cut an uncoated shoot\'s loss from 4.0 mL to 0.9 mL. So darkness should cut this shoot\'s upper-surface loss too, and the total should be at most 0.8 mL. It shouldn\'t fall below 0.3 mL, the amount that leaves even when both leaf surfaces are blocked. So the loss would be between 0.3 mL and 0.8 mL.",["Even with both surfaces coated, a shoot in light lost 0.3 mL by other routes, so this shoot should lose at least about that much.","Correct: in light, this shoot would lose 0.8 mL. Darkness closes the stomata left open on the upper surfaces, so the loss can only shrink, but not below the 0.3 mL that escapes by other routes.","This adds the dark result (0.9 mL) to the coated result (0.8 mL), but darkness and coating both reduce water loss; neither adds to it.","This is more than an uncoated shoot in light lost. Coating and darkness both cut water loss."]],"science-0384":["Coating both surfaces blocks all of the leaf stomata. The 0.3 mL that these shoots still lost must have escaped some other way, for example through the stems. Knowing that amount lets the students separate water lost through the leaf surfaces from water lost by other routes. Air speed and darkness belong to Experiments 2 and 3.",["Moving air was tested in Experiment 2, with uncoated shoots. All the Experiment 1 shoots were in still air.","Darkness was tested in Experiment 3. The Experiment 1 shoots were all in light.","Leaf area was matched when the shoots were chosen. Coating the leaves doesn\'t change their area.","Correct: with both leaf surfaces blocked, any water lost (0.3 mL) must have left some other way, such as through the stems."]],"science-0385":["Look up both waters in Table 1. Water A (20°C, 37 g/kg) has a density of 1026.3 kg/m³. Water B (10°C, 35 g/kg) has a density of 1026.9 kg/m³. Water B is denser, so it sinks beneath Water A. Here Water B\'s lower temperature outweighs Water A\'s higher salinity.",["Salinity is only half the story. Water A is also 10°C warmer, which lowers its density more than its extra salt raises it.","Wrong comparison: this sets Water A against a third water, not against Water B.","Correct: Table 1 gives 1026.3 kg/m³ for Water A and 1026.9 kg/m³ for Water B. The denser water, B, sinks.","The densities aren\'t equal: 1026.9 kg/m³ is greater than 1026.3 kg/m³."]],"science-0386":["Find the salinity 35 g/kg column. At 10°C the density is 1026.9 kg/m³ and at 20°C it is 1024.8 kg/m³. 15°C is halfway between, so the density should be about halfway between: (1026.9 + 1024.8) ÷ 2 ≈ 1025.9 kg/m³. 1025.4 is a value from the wrong column.",["This is the density at 20°C. Water at 15°C is colder, so it should be denser than this.","Wrong column: this is the density at 10°C with a salinity of 33 g/kg.","Correct: 15°C is halfway between 10°C (1026.9) and 20°C (1024.8), so the density should be about halfway between them, near 1025.9 kg/m³.","This is the density at 10°C. Water at 15°C is warmer, so it should be less dense than this."]],"science-0387":["Compare the effects per single unit. Cooling: at 35 g/kg, going from 20°C to 10°C raised the density from 1024.8 to 1026.9 kg/m³, an increase of 2.1 over 10°C, or about 0.2 kg/m³ per °C. Below 10°C the effect is even smaller: from 10°C to 0°C, about 0.12 kg/m³ per °C. Salt: at 10°C, going from 33 to 35 g/kg raised the density from 1025.4 to 1026.9 kg/m³, an increase of 1.5 over 2 g/kg, or about 0.75 kg/m³ per g/kg. A 1 g/kg rise in salinity raises density more than 1°C of cooling, so the claim is not consistent with the table. Choice A compares unequal steps, 10°C against 2 g/kg.",["Unfair comparison: this compares a 10°C change with a 2 g/kg change. Per unit, that is about 0.21 kg/m³ per °C against about 0.75 kg/m³ per g/kg.","False: 2.1 kg/m³ is the change for 10°C of cooling (20°C to 10°C), not for 1°C.","False: every column of Table 1 shows density rising as temperature falls.","Correct: from 20°C to 10°C, density rose 2.1 kg/m³, about 0.2 per °C (and from 10°C to 0°C, only about 0.12 per °C). At 10°C, from 33 to 35 g/kg, it rose 1.5 kg/m³, about 0.75 per g/kg."]],"science-0388":["At sea level, water boils at about 100°C. Several of the tested temperatures (106°C to 124°C) are above that, so in an open flask the broth would boil instead of staying liquid. The passage notes that on the seafloor, high pressure keeps hot water from boiling. The sealed, pressurized vessels do the same job in the lab.",["Correct: at sea level, water boils at about 100°C. Tests at 106°C to 124°C need high pressure to keep the broth liquid, as on the seafloor.","The lowest temperature tested was 85°C, far above the temperature at which water freezes.","At sea level the broth would boil above 100°C, so it would not stay liquid at every temperature tested.","An open flask stays at about 1 atm, the pressure of the air. It can\'t build up hundreds of atmospheres."]],"science-0389":["Count the doublings in 6 hours. At 106°C the doubling time is 1.0 h, so there are 6 doublings: 1,000 × 2⁶ = 64,000 cells. At 112°C the doubling time is 1.5 h, so there are 4 doublings: 1,000 × 2⁴ = 16,000 cells. 64,000 ÷ 16,000 = 4. The culture at 106°C has 2 more doublings, and each one doubles the count again, so it has 2 × 2 = 4 times as many cells. The traps use the doubling-time ratio (1.5), the extra doublings (2), or the difference in cells (48,000).",["This is the ratio of the doubling times (1.5 h ÷ 1.0 h). Cell numbers grow by doubling, so the gap is much bigger than that.","This is the difference in the number of doublings (6 − 4 = 2), not the ratio of the cell counts. Two extra doublings means 2 × 2 = 4 times as many cells.","Correct: at 106°C the culture doubles 6 times (6 h ÷ 1.0 h), reaching 64,000 cells. At 112°C it doubles 4 times (6 h ÷ 1.5 h), reaching 16,000 cells. 64,000 ÷ 16,000 = 4.","This is the difference in thousands of cells (64,000 − 16,000 = 48,000). The question asks how many times as many, a ratio."]],"science-0390":["A shorter doubling time means faster growth. From 20 atm to 200 atm, the doubling time fell from 2.5 h to 1.0 h, so growth sped up. But from 200 atm to 400 atm it rose to 1.2 h, so growth slowed. The claim says growth speeds up over the whole range, so it is not consistent with the results. Choice D says \\"No\\" for a reason that actually agrees with the claim.",["Correct: a longer doubling time means slower growth, so the microbe grew more slowly at 400 atm than at 200 atm.","False: 2.5 h was at the lowest pressure tested (20 atm), not the highest.","True for 20 to 200 atm, but the claim covers the whole range. From 200 to 400 atm, the doubling time rose, so growth slowed.","Right verdict, wrong reason: a shrinking doubling time means faster growth, which agrees with the claim for that part of the range."]],"science-0391":["Start from the closest tested condition: 112°C and 200 atm, with a doubling time of 1.5 h (Table 1). Then apply the pressure change. In Experiment 2, lowering the pressure from 200 atm to 20 atm raised the doubling time from 1.0 h to 2.5 h. So lowering the pressure at 112°C should also slow growth, making the doubling time greater than 1.5 h. Choice C keeps the 200 atm value, and choices A and B move in the wrong direction.",["This is faster than the microbe grew under any condition tested. Both 112°C and 20 atm slowed growth compared with the best conditions (106°C and 200 atm).","This would be faster than at 112°C and 200 atm (1.5 h), but lowering the pressure to 20 atm slowed growth in Experiment 2.","This is the result at 112°C and 200 atm. It ignores the change in pressure from 200 atm to 20 atm.","Correct: at 112°C and 200 atm the doubling time was 1.5 h. In Experiment 2, dropping from 200 atm to 20 atm lengthened the doubling time (1.0 h to 2.5 h), so at 20 atm it should be longer than 1.5 h."]],"science-0392":["Rearrange the equation: production rate = amount ÷ lifetime. Planet P: 500 ÷ 5,000 = 0.1 ppm per year. Planet Q: 40 ÷ 200 = 0.2. Planet R: 1.8 ÷ 10 = 0.18. Planet S: 0.2 ÷ 10 = 0.02. Planet Q needs the greatest production. The trap is Planet P: it has by far the most methane, but its methane lasts so long that only a small supply keeps it steady.",["Planet P has the most methane (500 ppm), but its methane lasts 5,000 years, so it needs only 500 ÷ 5,000 = 0.1 ppm per year.","Correct: production rate = amount ÷ lifetime. Planet Q needs 40 ÷ 200 = 0.2 ppm per year, the most of the four.","Close, but lower: Planet R needs 1.8 ÷ 10 = 0.18 ppm per year, a little less than Planet Q\'s 0.2.","Planet S needs only 0.2 ÷ 10 = 0.02 ppm per year, the least of the four."]],"science-0393":["First find which planets need more than 0.05 ppm per year (production = amount ÷ lifetime): P needs 0.1, Q needs 0.2, R needs 0.18, and S needs only 0.02. So P, Q, and R all need more than non-living sources could supply. The argument says all of these have O₂. Q and R do, but P has none. So the argument is not consistent with the data. Choice C is a common trap: a planet with O₂ that needs little methane doesn\'t break the argument, because the argument only makes a claim about the planets that need a lot.",["True for Planets Q and R, but the argument covers every planet that needs more than 0.05 ppm per year, and Planet P is one of them.","False: steady methane always needs a source. Planet P needs 500 ÷ 5,000 = 0.1 ppm per year, more than non-living sources could supply.","True, but beside the point: the argument is about planets that need more than 0.05 ppm per year. Planet S needs less, so it doesn\'t test the argument.","Correct: Planet P needs 500 ÷ 5,000 = 0.1 ppm per year, more than 0.05, yet it has no O₂. One such planet is enough to contradict the argument."]],"science-0394":["Find Planet R\'s production first: 1.8 ppm ÷ 10 years = 0.18 ppm per year. Production doesn\'t change, so the new steady amount is 0.18 × 5 = 0.9 ppm. Halving the lifetime halves the amount. The trap is to double it: more O₂ destroys methane faster, so less of it can build up.",["This divides by 10 twice. Production stays at 0.18 ppm per year, so the amount is 0.18 × 5 = 0.9 ppm.","Correct: production is 1.8 ÷ 10 = 0.18 ppm per year. With a 5-year lifetime, the steady amount is 0.18 × 5 = 0.9 ppm, half the original.","This is the original amount. It ignores that methane now lasts only half as long, so half as much builds up.","Wrong direction: this doubles the amount. A shorter lifetime means methane is destroyed faster, so less builds up."]],"science-0395":["Use production = amount ÷ lifetime: 4 ppm ÷ 50 years = 0.08 ppm per year. Non-living sources could supply at most about 0.05 ppm per year, so they couldn\'t keep Planet T\'s methane steady by themselves. Comparing Planet T with Planets R or Q is beside the point. Choice D reaches \\"No\\" by multiplying instead of dividing.",["True that Planet T needs less than Planet R (0.08 vs. 0.18 ppm per year), but the question is whether non-living sources could supply it, and 0.08 is more than 0.05.","The amount alone doesn\'t settle it. What matters is amount ÷ lifetime: 4 ÷ 50 = 0.08 ppm per year, more than 0.05.","Correct: production = 4 ÷ 50 = 0.08 ppm per year, which is more than the 0.05 ppm per year non-living sources could supply.","Right verdict, wrong math: this multiplies 4 × 50 instead of dividing. The production rate is 4 ÷ 50 = 0.08 ppm per year."]],"science-0396":["Lamp 2 gives 4 times as much light, and light falls off with the square of distance. A cup twice as far from Lamp 2 gets 4 × 1/4 = the same light as a cup at the original distance from Lamp 1. The tables agree: Lamp 2 at 40, 60, and 80 cm matched Lamp 1 at 20, 30, and 40 cm. So 120 cm from Lamp 2 matches 60 cm from Lamp 1, where the algae grew 12%.",["0% came from the cup closest to Lamp 1, where the water reached 46°C. A cup 120 cm from Lamp 2 gets far less light.","Correct: Lamp 2 gives 4 times Lamp 1\'s light, so Lamp 2 at twice the distance matches Lamp 1. 120 cm from Lamp 2 matches 60 cm from Lamp 1, where growth was 12%.","This is Lamp 1 at 40 cm (or Lamp 2 at 80 cm). 120 cm from Lamp 2 is farther and gets less light.","This is Lamp 2 at 60 cm, only half as far away as 120 cm."]],"science-0397":["Light falls off with the square of distance, so a lamp with 9 times the light gives the same light at 3 times the distance (because 3² = 9). Lamp 1 gave the most growth at 20 cm, so Lamp 3 should give the most at 3 × 20 = 60 cm. The trap is multiplying by 9 (180 cm). Experiment 2 shows the same rule: Lamp 2, with 4 times the light, matched Lamp 1 at twice the distance. This is also why brighter stars have habitable zones farther out.",["At 20 cm, Lamp 3 gives as much light as Lamp 1 at about 7 cm, even closer than the 10 cm cup, where the water reached 46°C and no algae grew.","30 cm from Lamp 3 matches 10 cm from Lamp 1 (30 ÷ 3), where the water reached 46°C and no algae grew.","Correct: with 9 times the light, Lamp 3 at 3 times the distance matches Lamp 1 (9 × 1/9 = 1). So 60 cm from Lamp 3 matches 20 cm from Lamp 1, where growth was greatest (85%).","This multiplies the best distance by 9 instead of 3. 180 cm from Lamp 3 matches 60 cm from Lamp 1, where growth was only 12%."]],"science-0398":["To test whether heat or light is to blame, keep one and remove the other. A cooling bath keeps the water at 25°C while the cup still gets the strong light 10 cm from Lamp 1. If the algae grow, the heat was the problem. If they still fail, the light itself was. In every other choice, strong light and high temperature still come together, so the two causes stay mixed up.",["20 cm from Lamp 2 gives the same light as 10 cm from Lamp 1, and most likely the same heating, so both causes would still be present together.","This only measures the heat more often. It doesn\'t show whether the algae would grow under the strong light without it.","Moving closer makes the light stronger and the water hotter at the same time, so the result can\'t separate the two causes.","Correct: this keeps the strong light but removes the heat. If the algae grow well, heat was the cause; if they still don\'t grow, the strong light is to blame."]],"science-0399":["Combine the two tables. Table 1: water with 100 g/kg of salt freezes at −6.6°C. Table 2: the ice warms from −8°C at 18 km to −3°C at 20 km, so it reaches −6.6°C between 18 km and 20 km, a bit past 18 km. Of the choices, 19 km is closest. 22 km would be right only for fresh water, which freezes at 0°C.",["At 15 km the ice is −15°C, colder than the −6.6°C freezing point of 100 g/kg water. The ice must warm further before it meets the ocean.","Correct: water with 100 g/kg of salt freezes at −6.6°C. The ice is −8°C at 18 km and −3°C at 20 km, so it reaches −6.6°C between them, at about 19 km.","At 21 km the ice would be about −1.5°C, the freezing point of water with only about 25 g/kg of salt.","At 22 km the ice reaches 0°C, the freezing point of fresh water. Salt lowers the freezing point, so the ocean begins higher up."]],"science-0400":["Follow the chain. Table 1: saltier water freezes at a lower temperature. Table 2: the ice is coldest at the surface and warms with depth, so a lower temperature is reached at a shallower depth. The shell ends where the ice reaches the ocean\'s freezing point, so a saltier ocean begins higher up, under a thinner shell. For example, fresh water would begin at about 22 km, but water with 100 g/kg of salt at about 19 km.",["Correct: in Table 1, more salt means a lower freezing point. In Table 2, the ice is colder closer to the surface, so the shell\'s bottom moves up and the shell gets thinner.","False fact: Table 1 shows saltier water freezing at lower temperatures, not higher ones.","Right fact, wrong direction: Table 2 shows the ice getting warmer with depth, so lower temperatures are found closer to the surface.","False: Table 1 shows the freezing point falling from 0.0°C to −16.5°C as salinity rises."]],"science-0401":["Work backward through the tables. Table 2: at 15 km, the ice is −15°C. The shell ends there, so −15°C must be the ocean\'s freezing point. Table 1: −15°C falls between −10.9°C (150 g/kg) and −16.5°C (200 g/kg), so the salinity is between 150 and 200 g/kg. The common slip is stopping at 150 g/kg, whose freezing point, −10.9°C, is still warmer than −15°C.",["Water this fresh freezes at −3.0°C or warmer, which the ice reaches only at about 20 km or deeper, not at 15 km.","Water with 50 to 100 g/kg of salt freezes between −3.0°C and −6.6°C, which the ice reaches only at about 19 to 20 km.","Close, but not salty enough: 150 g/kg freezes at −10.9°C, which is still warmer than the −15°C ice at 15 km.","Correct: the ice at 15 km is −15°C, so the ocean must freeze at −15°C. That lies between −10.9°C (150 g/kg) and −16.5°C (200 g/kg)."]],"science-0402":["Scientist 3 begins with \\"Scientist 2 is right that life needs a long-lasting ocean that touches rock.\\" That is their shared ground. They disagree about which moon is the better target, and only Scientist 3 brings up the jets, the hydrogen gas, and sampling without drilling.",["Only Scientist 3 says this. Scientist 2 favors Europa.","Only Scientist 3 mentions hydrogen gas.","Scientist 3 says the opposite: Enceladus\'s ocean could be sampled without drilling, from its jets.","Correct: Scientist 2 stresses Europa\'s long-lasting ocean on a rocky seafloor, and Scientist 3 opens by saying Scientist 2 is right about this."]],"science-0403":["The finding removes the water-rock contact that Scientist 2 uses as a reason to pick Europa. So it weakens Scientist 2. Scientist 3 agrees that an ocean touching rock is needed, but argues for Enceladus, so the finding actually helps Scientist 3\'s case against Europa. Scientist 1\'s argument about Mars is untouched. The trap is grouping Scientists 2 and 3 because they share the ocean-on-rock idea.",["Correct: Scientist 2\'s case for Europa depends on the ocean touching a rocky seafloor. Scientist 3 argues for Enceladus, so a problem with Europa only strengthens Scientist 3\'s choice.","Scientist 3 favors Enceladus over Europa. A finding that makes Europa less suitable supports Scientist 3\'s choice instead of weakening it.","One too many: both value an ocean on rock, but only Scientist 2 relies on Europa having one. The finding says nothing about Enceladus.","Scientist 1 argues for Mars, and the finding is only about Europa, so Scientist 1\'s argument is unaffected."]],"science-0404":["Scientist 3 agrees with Scientist 2 about what life needs, so Scientist 3\'s reasons for preferring Enceladus are the extra things it offers: jets that a spacecraft has flown through, salts, organic molecules, and hydrogen gas, and an ocean that can be sampled without drilling. If Europa were found to have all of these too, Enceladus would lose its edge. The other findings either support Scientist 3 or concern Mars.",["This supports Scientist 3, who says a long-lasting ocean that touches rock is needed. It gives Enceladus one more point in its favor.","Correct: Scientist 3\'s advantages for Enceladus are its sampled jets, their chemistry, and sampling without drilling. If Europa had the same, those advantages would vanish.","This bears on Scientist 1\'s argument about Mars, not on the choice between Enceladus and Europa.","Scientist 3 already relies on this fact. It is a reason the hydrogen on Enceladus matters, so it doesn\'t weaken Scientist 3."]],"science-0405":["Scientist 1\'s claim has two parts: microbes could survive deep underground, and not at the surface. A good test looks for living microbes in both places and compares them. Only the drilling study samples both the surface and rock several meters down. Photographs of riverbeds and ultraviolet measurements don\'t look for microbes, and surface soil alone tests only half the claim.",["This confirms that Mars once had water, which Scientist 1 already accepts. It says nothing about microbes living there today.","This checks one reason Scientist 1 gives, but it doesn\'t look for microbes, so it can\'t show where they survive.","Correct: comparing deep rock with surface rock tests both parts of the claim: signs of life deep down but none at the surface would support Scientist 1.","This tests only the surface. Without deep samples to compare, it can\'t test the claim that microbes survive underground."]]}')}]);